// The Night Shelver — the text of every book. Generated by tools/build-stories.js from content/stories/*.json; edit those, not this.
window.TH.STORIES = [[["A Light in the Hedge","On the Lowlands road, a mile out of Emberwick, a carrier's cart went over a rut one windy evening and something fell off the back. It rolled into the hawthorn hedge and lay there, glowing.\n\nRusset the fox found it at moonrise. He was a young fox, proud of his nose, and his nose told him nothing useful: hot tin, lamp oil, and a faint smell of horse. He circled it twice. The thing did not run away, so he decided it was his.\n\nHe took the wire handle in his teeth and carried it home across the stubble, with his own shadow leaping about in front of him like a second, sillier fox. All night he lay awake in his den watching the small flame and wondering what it ate. A found thing, he was learning, comes with questions attached."],["The Moths' Complaint","Russet the fox had owned his lantern for three nights and had carried it everywhere. He went along the hedge by the Lowlands road with the handle in his teeth and his chin held high, and he felt very grand indeed.\n\nHe also felt hungry. Every vole in the field saw him coming from forty yards off and went below. The rabbits simply sat on the bank and watched him pass, as you might watch a parade. Only the moths came close, dozens of them, bumping against the glass and complaining that it was hot.\n\n\"You may shine,\" said the oldest moth, settling on his ear, \"or you may sneak. I have never met anyone who managed both.\" Russet went supperless to bed. The next night he left the lantern at home, and caught a beetle, and thought it over."],["Badger Trims the Wick","On the fifth night the lantern began to smoke. Its flame went tall and yellow and dirty, and the glass turned black on one side. Russet the fox carried it up the bank to Old Bramble the badger, who had lived near the Lowlands road for many years and knew something about the belongings of people.\n\nBramble looked at it for a long while. Then, with one careful claw, he nipped the burnt end off the wick and turned the little wheel down until the flame sat small and blue-edged, like a crocus.\n\n\"It was asking for too much at once,\" he said. \"So it got soot.\" He wiped the glass with a dock leaf and showed Russet the dark line where the oil stood, already lower than it had been. \"A light is not a thing you have, young fox. It is a thing you keep.\""],["Dormouse on the Stepping Stones","Below the hedge where Russet the fox had his den, a brook ran down to join the river Ember, and seven stepping stones crossed it. On a night of thick fog Russet heard a small voice calling from the far bank. It was Pip the dormouse, who had gone over at dusk for blackberries and could no longer see the first stone, let alone the seventh.\n\nRusset fetched his lantern, the one he had found on the road, and set it on the middle stone. The fog turned gold. Each stone showed its wet back, one after another.\n\nPip came across in seven careful hops and was too polite to mention that he had been rescued by a fox. He bowed, and left a blackberry on the bank. Russet ate it, which surprised them both. It was the first time the lantern had been of use to anybody."],["The Price of a Glow","Word went round the hedge that Russet the fox had a lantern and would light the stepping stones on dark nights. Russet, who was clever, saw a chance. He set a flat stone by the brook and announced the fee: one hazelnut for each crossing, payable in advance.\n\nThe creatures paid. Mice paid, and voles, and Mrs. Quill the hedgehog, who grumbled and paid twice because she was going there and back. By the end of the week Russet had a heap of hazelnuts as high as his shoulder.\n\nHe sat and looked at it. Foxes do not care for hazelnuts. He could not eat his fortune, and nobody had left him a blackberry, or said good evening, since the day the fee began. He had made himself rich in the one thing he had no use for."],["The Night It Went Out","It happened on a gusty night at the stepping stones. The lantern that Russet the fox had carried all autumn gave a small cough, shrank to a red thread, and went out. The oil was gone. He shook it, and it only rattled.\n\nThe dark came down like a sack. Russet stood very still on the bank with his heart thumping, and it was some time before he understood why he was afraid. He was a fox. He had been born into the night and had run about in it happily for a whole year before any lantern came.\n\nBut he had looked at a flame every evening since, and his eyes had grown lazy. He crept home slowly, bumping into a thistle he had known all his life. Lean on a stick long enough, Old Bramble the badger used to say, and you forget your legs."],["Oil from the Hazel Heap","Russet the fox had a lantern with no oil in it and a heap of hazelnuts he could not eat. They had sat side by side in his den for a week before Mrs. Quill the hedgehog, calling to complain about the dark at the stepping stones, looked from one to the other and sighed. \"Nuts have oil in them,\" she said. \"Anyone knows that. Fetch two flat stones.\"\n\nSo they pressed them. The mice cracked the shells, Pip the dormouse sorted the kernels, and Russet turned the top stone round and round until a thin gold thread ran into an acorn cup. It took three evenings to fill the lantern. Nobody mentioned the fee, and nobody was asked for one again.\n\nThe flame, when it came, smelled faintly of toast. Every creature who had once paid a nut could say truthfully that a little of that light was theirs."],["The Owl Objects","Not everyone along the Lowlands hedge loved the lantern. Mistle the owl flew down one evening and landed on the handle, which made the whole thing swing.\n\n\"I hunt by dark,\" she told Russet the fox. \"I have done so since before you had a tail. Now there is a yellow smear in the middle of my night, and every mouse can see me against it. The dark is not an emptiness, fox. Some of us live in it.\"\n\nRusset had not thought of that. He had supposed that what was good for the dormouse must be good for all. They talked until the moon set and came to terms: the lantern would burn at the stepping stones in fog and on moonless nights, and stay shuttered otherwise. Mistle got her dark back. The crossers kept their light. Neither had everything, and both slept well."],["The Hare's Enormous Shadow","A leveret named Hazel came to the stepping stones one moonless night, saw the lantern burning on the middle stone, and would not cross. Something huge and black with ears like fence posts was crouching on the far bank, she said. It moved whenever she moved.\n\nRusset the fox, who kept the lantern, looked where she pointed. Then he lifted one paw. On the far bank a monstrous paw rose too.\n\n\"That one is mine,\" he said. \"The long-eared one is yours. The light is behind us, so we are thrown large.\" He made a goose with his tail and a tree with his whole self. Hazel, after a moment, tried a rabbit, which was not difficult. They played until the oil ran low. She crossed at last, hopping straight through the place where the monster had been, and it did her no harm at all."],["The Carrier Comes Back","Late in the autumn the carrier's cart came creaking along the Lowlands road again, the same cart that had dropped the lantern months before. Russet the fox watched from the hedge. The carrier, an old man in a sacking hood, climbed down at the rut and poked about in the hawthorn with a stick.\n\n\"It was here I lost her,\" he told the horse. \"Thirty years I had that lantern.\" He found nothing, of course. He drove on toward Emberwick in the dusk with no light at all, the horse feeling for the road with its feet.\n\nRusset went home and looked at the lantern for a long time. He had trimmed it and fed it. He had earned it, surely. But he knew the smell of that hood and that horse. It had been on the tin since the first night, and he had always known what it meant."],["A Fox at the Milestone","There is a milestone on the Lowlands road that says Emberwick, one mile. On a cold evening Russet the fox carried his lantern there, the lantern he had found in the hedge and kept all autumn. He trimmed the wick as Old Bramble the badger had taught him, set it burning on top of the stone, and backed away into the bracken.\n\nThe carrier's cart came by at dusk. The old man pulled up so sharply that the horse snorted. He got down, turned the lantern round in his hands, and found it clean, full, and lit.\n\nHe looked at the hedge for some while. Then he took off his hood to it, quite seriously, as one does to a neighbour, and left half a cheese rind on the milestone. The light went swinging off down the road. Russet watched until it was the size of a star."],["Seeing in the Dark","Fog lay on the brook again, as thick as on the night Russet the fox first lit the stepping stones. A small voice called from the far bank. It was Pip the dormouse, late home, with no lantern to hope for now that it had gone back to the carrier.\n\nRusset waded to the middle stone and stood there. His eyes, after a month of honest darkness, had come back to him. \"Follow the white tip of my tail,\" he called. \"It is the palest thing out here. Seven hops.\"\n\nPip counted them aloud and arrived safely. After that Russet stood on the middle stone whenever the fog came down, and the creatures crossed by his tail. Some evenings a small gold light went by on the Lowlands road, and a hood was raised toward the hedge, and a fox, unseen, raised his chin in reply."]],[["A Room in the Roots","The hollow oak stands where the Lowlands fields give way to the marsh, and it is older than the oldest hedge in Emberwick. On a night of hard autumn rain a woodmouse named Thimble came to it with everything she owned, which was a wet tail and a seed.\n\nThe marsh had risen into her burrow. She had walked since dusk. Between two roots she found a dry hollow and, sitting in it, a large toad who introduced himself as Mudge.\n\n\"There is room,\" he said, moving over an inch, which for a toad is generous. \"There is one rule. Nobody eats anybody under these branches. Beyond the drip of the leaves, the world goes on as usual. In here, we are neighbours.\" Thimble agreed at once. She did not yet know who lived upstairs, or she might have asked a question or two first."],["The Squirrel Who Counted","Halfway up the hollow oak lived a squirrel named Pocket, and in autumn Pocket counted. He counted acorns into the fork of the oak, acorns into a knothole, acorns into forty small holes he dug between the roots. Three hundred and twelve, he told anyone who passed, and not one to spare.\n\nThimble the woodmouse, new to the tree, asked him for a single acorn against the winter. Pocket said he was very sorry. Every one was counted.\n\nBy the first frost he had forgotten where a good third of them were. He dug up the same empty hole four times and accused a jay. Old Mudge the toad, watching from the roots, remarked that a thing kept too carefully is as good as lost; but the oak, he added, would not mind. Oaks have always relied on a squirrel's poor memory."],["The Owl Upstairs","The top of the hollow oak belonged to Dame Sorrel, a tawny owl. Thimble the woodmouse, who lived among the roots, learned this on her third evening when a soft brown shape the size of a thundercloud dropped past her door without a sound.\n\nShe did not go out for two days. On the third, hunger sent her up the trunk for a beechnut lodged in the bark, and there was the owl on a branch, looking at her with eyes like two lamps.\n\n\"Good evening,\" said Dame Sorrel. \"You are the new mouse. I hunt beyond the far hedge. It is a long flight on an empty stomach, and you look a good deal closer, but a rule is a rule.\" Thimble said good evening back, in a squeak. It struck her afterwards that the owl's half of the bargain was much the harder to keep."],["The Woodpecker's Early Drum","Burr the woodpecker drummed on the hollow oak at first light, every morning, on the loudest dead branch he could find. The bats, who had only just hung themselves up to sleep, sent a complaint. So did Dame Sorrel the owl. Thimble the woodmouse, down in the roots, signed it with a muddy paw.\n\nBurr was hurt, but he stopped. The oak was beautifully quiet for a week.\n\nIt was also late for everything. Pocket the squirrel slept until noon and missed the best of the beechnuts. Thimble woke in the dark, not knowing if it was evening or morning. The bats went out too early and met a hawk. In the end a second letter went up the trunk, a politer one. Burr now gives three knocks at dawn, no more, and does the rest of his drumming at midday on the old elm across the field."],["First Frost Supper","On the night of the first frost the tenants of the hollow oak hold a supper inside the trunk, where the floor is soft with a hundred years of leaf dust. Everyone brings something. That is the custom.\n\nDame Sorrel the owl brought a tuft of sheep's wool for sitting on. Burr the woodpecker brought beetle grubs, which only he enjoyed. Thimble the woodmouse brought her one seed, carried all the way from the marsh, and cut it into five. Pocket the squirrel, after a long struggle, brought an acorn. One.\n\nMudge the toad brought nothing he could carry. He was sleepy with the cold and due under the mud until spring. Instead he told the story of the winter the snow reached the first branch, and told it well. They agreed afterwards that it had been the best dish on the table."],["The Night of the Gale","At midwinter a gale came down off the Northern Fells and leaned on the hollow oak all night. Towards morning the great west limb gave a crack like a gate slamming and fell into the field.\n\nNobody was hurt. But Dame Sorrel's ledge had gone with it, and Burr the woodpecker's hole, and half of Pocket the squirrel's knothole store. The three of them sat in the wreckage in the grey light, looking smaller than usual.\n\nThimble the woodmouse offered her root room to the owl, who thanked her gravely and did not fit. So everyone shifted. Burr took the bats' spare crack. Pocket squeezed his acorns into the fork and himself into the knothole. The owl had the whole inside of the trunk, standing up, like an umbrella in a stand. Nobody was comfortable, exactly. Everybody was indoors."],["The Jay's Bad News","After the gale a jay named Jabber stopped at the hollow oak on his way somewhere else. He looked at the fallen limb, and at the great dark hole in the trunk, and whistled.\n\n\"Hollow,\" he said. \"Rotten right through. A hollow tree is a dead tree, friends. I would be out of it by spring. There is a nice young beech by the road.\" Then he flew off, having done his duty.\n\nThe oak was in an uproar by dusk. Pocket the squirrel began carrying acorns to the beech, two at a time, and losing them on the way. Thimble the woodmouse cried into her tail. Only Dame Sorrel the owl stayed where she was. \"That bird,\" she said, \"has known this tree for the length of one conversation. I should like a second opinion from somebody who lives here.\""],["What the Bats Heard","Was the hollow oak dying? A passing jay had said so, and the tenants had talked of little else for a week. Nobody had thought to ask the bats. Nobody ever did. They hung in a row inside the trunk all winter, eleven of them, and said nothing.\n\nThimble the woodmouse climbed up and asked. The oldest bat opened one eye.\n\n\"We sleep with our ears against the wood,\" she said. \"The middle is empty, yes. It has been empty since before your grandmother. But on mild days we hear the outside drinking. Go and listen.\" Thimble went down and pressed her ear to the rough bark in the weak noon sun. She heard nothing for a long time. Then, very faint and very slow, came something like a river running under a floor. She called the others, one by one, to hear it."],["Thaw and Toad","The thaw came to the hollow oak in a single night. By morning the snow had gone into the ground, and a good deal of it had come out again in Thimble the woodmouse's root room, where she stood bailing with a nutshell.\n\nA lump of mud in the corner yawned. It was Mudge the toad, awake a month early and not pleased. He listened to the winter's news: the gale, the fallen limb, the jay who said the tree was finished.\n\n\"Hollow,\" said Mudge. \"Of course it is hollow. It was hollow when my grandfather wintered here, and he complained of the draught. Where did they suppose we all lived, in a solid tree? The hollow is the room, child.\" He climbed onto a root to watch the water go down. It went down by noon, as he said it would, and he went back to bed."],["Buds on the Broken Side","In April the hollow oak came into leaf, late as oaks are, and all over. There were buds even on the west side, round the pale scar where the great limb had torn away in the winter gale.\n\nThe tenants went about their repairs. Burr the woodpecker began a new hole a yard above his old one and pronounced the wood sounder there. Pocket the squirrel found the knothole pleasantly roomy now that half his acorns were eaten.\n\nDame Sorrel the owl had the best of it. The stump of the fallen limb had split into a deep, dry cup, sheltered from the north, with a view of the whole field. She turned round in it three times and settled. It was, she admitted to Thimble the woodmouse, a better ledge than the one she had lost; though she would not have chosen the way of getting it."],["The Forgotten Acorn","Pocket the squirrel spent a spring morning digging between the roots of the hollow oak for acorns he had buried in autumn and mislaid. He found three empty holes, a snail, and then a thing he did not expect: a red stem as long as his paw, with two crumpled leaves on top.\n\nAt the bottom of the stem, half split, sat one of his acorns. He reached for it.\n\n\"Leave it,\" said Thimble the woodmouse, from her doorway. \"It is busy.\" Pocket looked at the little oak, and up at the enormous one, and down again. He did the sum slowly. Then he fetched twigs and built a fence round it against rabbits, and from that day he visited every morning to count its leaves. Two. Then four. It was the first counting he had ever done that made the number go up."],["Another Wet Traveller","Autumn came round to the hollow oak again, and with it a night of hard rain off the marsh. Thimble the woodmouse was sweeping her root room when a shrew appeared between the roots, soaked to the skin and carrying nothing at all.\n\n\"There is room,\" said Thimble, moving over. She heard herself say it in old Mudge's voice, and the toad, from his corner, shut one eye at her. \"There is one rule. Nobody eats anybody under these branches.\"\n\nShe showed the shrew where the dry leaves were kept. She pointed out the little oak by the door, six leaves tall inside its fence of twigs, and explained that the squirrel would want it admired. Far overhead something soft and brown went out to hunt beyond the far hedge. \"That is the owl upstairs,\" said Thimble. \"Say good evening. She likes to be asked.\""]],[["What the River Left","When the spring flood went down, the river Ember left a number of things in the reeds below Emberwick: a boot, a gatepost, and a tin kettle lying on its side with the lid still on.\n\nMrs. Dabble the water vole found the kettle. The same flood had filled her burrow with silt, and she was looking for somewhere to dry her whiskers. She walked round it twice. She knocked on it, and it rang. She pushed up the lid with her nose and looked in.\n\nIt was dry inside, and round, and smelled only a little of rust. The spout pointed at the sky like a chimney. By evening she had carried in a bed of reed fluff and was sitting in the doorway, well pleased. Somebody upstream had thrown away a kettle. Somebody downstream had been given a house."],["The First Cup of Mint","Mrs. Dabble the water vole had lived in the tin kettle among the reeds for a week when she discovered what it was good at. On a sunny day the tin grew warm, and rainwater left standing in the upturned lid grew warm with it. She dropped in a sprig of water mint to see what would happen.\n\nWhat happened was tea, of a kind. It was pale green and smelled like the riverbank after a shower.\n\nTwo young frogs, Plip and Plop, followed the smell to her door and stood there being obvious. Mrs. Dabble owned exactly one acorn cup. She filled it and handed it out, and the frogs passed it between them, sip and sip about, until it was empty. They came back the next day with a friend. A door that opens once, she found, is expected to open again."],["The Heron Will Not Stoop","Every creature in the marsh below Emberwick had taken a cup of mint tea at the tin kettle, except one. Grimsby the heron stood in the shallows twenty yards off, grey and narrow as a fence rail, and looked the other way.\n\n\"A kettle,\" he was heard to say, \"is not a house. And I do not bend to doorways.\" He was a good deal too tall for this one, which may have been the trouble.\n\nMrs. Dabble the water vole did not argue. Each evening she climbed the handle and set a full acorn cup on the very top of the kettle, where a tall bird might find it without stooping an inch. For nine evenings it stood untouched and went cold. On the tenth morning the cup was empty and turned neatly upside down. Nobody said a word about it, the heron least of all."],["Otter Takes Too Much","Ripple the otter discovered the tin kettle in May. He arrived like a wave, drank the lid dry, ate eleven seed cakes, and told a story about a pike that lasted until the moon was up. The next day he did it again.\n\nBy the end of the week the frogs had stopped calling. There was no tea left for them, and no silence to put a word into. Mrs. Dabble the water vole looked at her bare shelf and at her large, cheerful guest.\n\nShe did not send him away. She gave him a rush basket. The best mint, she explained, grew a mile upstream past the weir, too far for a vole. Ripple was back within the hour, soaked and proud, with enough mint for a month. A big appetite, she had noticed, usually comes with big legs. He has been the kettle's carrier ever since."],["The Newt Who Listened","Fig the newt came to the tin kettle every evening, sat under the spout, and said nothing. The frogs thought him dull. Ripple the otter, who filled every silence he met, once asked Mrs. Dabble the water vole whether the newt was quite awake.\n\nThen, at midsummer, the moorhen Mrs. Coot came splashing through the reeds in a terrible state. Her smallest chick was missing. Everyone talked at once and nobody knew anything.\n\nFig cleared his throat. It took a moment to be noticed. \"On Tuesday,\" he said, \"the chick told the frogs she wished to see the boot the flood left by the alder. Nobody answered her. I expect she has gone to look.\" She had. She was sitting inside the boot, very pleased with herself. After that, when Fig sat quiet under the spout, the others understood that he was keeping the minutes."],["A Hole in the Bottom","Tin rusts. Mrs. Dabble the water vole knew it, and still it was a shock, one wet June morning, to find a small brown hole in the floor of her kettle and the marsh coming up through it.\n\nShe was ashamed. She stuffed the hole with moss, put the lid down, and told callers that the tea house was closed for cleaning. It stayed closed five days, while the moss went soggy and the bed went damp.\n\nIt was the frogs, Plip and Plop, who peered down the spout and saw her bailing. Within the hour the whole marsh knew, and within two the whole marsh had come. Ripple the otter brought blue clay from the weir. Fig the newt remembered that pine pitch keeps water out. They patched it together, badly at first and then well. A trouble behind a closed lid, said Mrs. Dabble, only gets wetter."],["Mrs. Coot Hears Something","It began when Mrs. Dabble the water vole remarked, over the mint, that she was thinking of moving the lid. It stuck in wet weather, and she wanted it an inch to the left.\n\nMrs. Coot the moorhen caught the word \"moving\" and nothing else. By noon she had told the reeds that Mrs. Dabble was moving. By dusk the alder had it that the tin kettle was closing and its keeper leaving for the Lowlands, heartbroken, on account of the damp.\n\nNext evening the whole marsh arrived with parting gifts: snail shells, a feather, and a speech from Ripple the otter that made him cry. Mrs. Dabble stood in her doorway holding a farewell beetle and understood nothing. It was Fig the newt who walked the story backwards, mouth by mouth, to one inch of lid. The gifts were kept. So was the speech, for a later date."],["A Summer Without Rain","No rain fell on the marsh below Emberwick for six weeks that summer. The river Ember shrank between its banks. The reeds stood in cracked mud, and the tin kettle, which had been at the water's edge, now sat a long hot walk from it. By noon the tin was too warm to touch.\n\nMrs. Dabble the water vole moved her tea to the evenings. But the lid was dry, the mint was limp, and Ripple the otter came back from the weir with an empty basket.\n\nGrimsby the heron spoke, from his usual distance. He alone stood high enough to see over the reeds. \"There is deep water under the third alder,\" he said, as if it bored him. \"And green mint beside it. I have been standing in it all week.\" He led them there, stalking ahead, not once looking back to see whether they followed."],["The Frogs Fall Out","Plip and Plop, the two young frogs of the tin kettle, quarrelled in August over who should sit on the spout. Each said it had always been his place. By evening Plip was sulking on the east bank and Plop on the west, and each had told Mrs. Dabble the water vole that he would take his tea alone, thank you.\n\nMrs. Dabble said that was quite all right. She set out the tea as usual.\n\nThere was, as there had been since the first day, one acorn cup. She filled it and put it on the flat stone exactly halfway between them. The frogs looked at the cup, and at each other, and at the cup. Mint tea does not keep. Plip took a sip and, from long habit, passed it on. Plop drank and passed it back. Nobody sat on the spout that night."],["Mending Day","When the alder leaves begin to yellow, the creatures of the marsh get the tin kettle ready for winter. They have a day for it. Ripple the otter fetches fresh pitch for the patch in the floor. The frogs, Plip and Plop, pile dry reed over the top for thatch. Mrs. Coot the moorhen supervises, which is to say she talks.\n\nThat autumn Fig the newt, who rarely suggested anything, suggested a rope. The river Ember had brought the kettle, he said, and might one day wish to take it on.\n\nSo they plaited one out of rushes, sitting in a row along the bank. It was as thick as an otter's tail when they finished. Mrs. Dabble the water vole tied one end to the kettle's handle and the other round an alder root. Grimsby the heron watched from the shallows and said it would never hold."],["The Night the Ember Rose","The flood came in December, in the dark. Mrs. Dabble the water vole woke to find her tin kettle rocking, and the frogs and Fig the newt scrambling in through the lid, and the river Ember going by on every side, brown and loud.\n\nThe kettle floated. It swung out on its rush rope until the rope hummed. One strand parted, then another. Inside, four small creatures held on to each other and to the reed-fluff bed.\n\nThen the swinging stopped. Something had taken hold of the handle. Mrs. Dabble looked up the spout and saw, against the racing clouds, a long grey neck bent right down to her roof. Grimsby the heron stood thigh-deep in the flood with the kettle's handle in his beak. He stood there until morning. He had said the rope would not hold, and he did not care to be proved right."],["Tea on Higher Ground","After the flood the creatures of the marsh rolled the tin kettle up the bank, a hand's breadth at a time, and set it under the alder, above the highest line of driftwood. Ripple the otter pushed. The frogs steered. Mrs. Coot the moorhen told everybody how.\n\nMrs. Dabble the water vole put the lid on straight, an inch to the left, and waited for a sunny day. It came. The mint was last summer's, dried, and the tea was the colour of pond water, and nobody minded.\n\nShe filled the acorn cup for the frogs to share. She set a second one on top of the kettle. Grimsby the heron came pacing up the bank, and bent his long neck, all the way down, until one yellow eye was level with her door. \"Good evening,\" he said. The kettle has never once boiled. It has not needed to."]],[["The Smallest Egg","Once upon a wren, there was an egg. It lay with five others in a ball of moss tucked into the ivy on the old orchard wall at Emberwick, and it was the smallest of the six, no bigger than a hazelnut and speckled like a wall in the rain.\n\nThe other five hatched on a Monday. The sixth took until Wednesday, and when it finally cracked, what came out was mostly beak. Her mother named her Tuppence, on the grounds that she was not worth much more to look at.\n\nA wren is the smallest bird in the orchard, and Tuppence was the smallest wren. Her brothers sat on her. The wind nearly had her out of the nest twice. But she opened that beak at the sky and made a noise that stopped a passing bee in mid-air. It was a start."],["A Voice Too Big","Tuppence the wren was three weeks old, the size of a walnut, and louder than the orchard gate. When she sang, her whole body shook with it and her tail stuck straight up.\n\nHer brothers begged her to hush. Marlow the rook, who ruled the tall elms and liked his mornings dignified, flew down to say that small birds ought to make small noises. So Tuppence tried. For a whole day she whispered, and felt like a kettle with the lid held on.\n\nThat evening Marmalade the orchard cat came along the top of the wall, slow as honey, toward the ivy where the wren brood sat dozing. Tuppence saw her first. She forgot about whispering. The alarm she let out sent five wrens into the brambles, woke the rooks, and so startled Marmalade that she fell off the wall. Nobody asked for quiet again."],["A Very Poor Rook","Tuppence the wren admired the rooks. They were large and black and walked about the orchard at Emberwick as though they owned the grass. She decided to be one.\n\nShe puffed out every feather until she was nearly the size of a plum. She practised walking instead of hopping, and tripped. She tried to caw, and produced a squeak. Last of all she chose a stick for her nest, a proper rook's stick as long as a rake handle, and it pinned her to the ground until a kind beetle lifted the end.\n\nMrs. Dunnock, the brown hedge-bird who nested low in the quince, watched without laughing. \"You make a very poor rook,\" she said. \"Have you looked at the wall? There are forty cracks in it with a spider at the back of each. No rook alive can fit his beak in one.\" Tuppence looked. It was true."],["Through the Gap","Mrs. Dunnock's youngest fledgling went missing on a warm afternoon. He was found by his cheeping, deep inside the woodpile behind the orchard wall, where he had hopped after a woodlouse and could not find the way back.\n\nThe whole orchard came to help. Marlow the rook heaved at a log, and the pile shifted in a way nobody liked. Brisk the robin tried the gap and got as far as his shoulders. The cheeping grew smaller.\n\nTuppence the wren did not puff herself up. She pulled every feather flat and went in like a mouse, along a tunnel no wider than a thumb, round two corners in the dark. She came out backwards, scolding, with the fledgling hopping after the sound of her. Mrs. Dunnock said thank you eleven times. Marlow said nothing, but he looked at the gap for a long while."],["The Robin's Line","Brisk the robin had sung from the orchard wall at Emberwick for three summers, and he considered every stone of it his. When Tuppence the wren began singing from the same wall, he flew at her with his red chest out.\n\nThey held a shouting match. It lasted two days. He sang from the gatepost and she sang from the ivy, louder and louder, until both were hoarse and Marlow the rook complained from the elms that nobody could hear themselves think.\n\nIt was the hoarseness that settled it. Unable to shout, they were obliged to talk. Brisk, it turned out, wanted the top of the wall, the spade handles and the open ground where worms came up. Tuppence wanted the cracks, the ivy and the dark inside of things. They had been fighting over a wall that could be divided without cutting it anywhere."],["The Cuckoo's Tall Tales","A cuckoo rested in the orchard at Emberwick one July evening on her way south. She told Tuppence the wren about deserts, and rivers a mile wide, and trees full of fruit in January. She said a wall was a poor sort of world, and that only dull birds stayed.\n\nTuppence did not sleep. At dawn she set off after the cuckoo, flying low from hedge to hedge as wrens do, and by afternoon she had come farther than any wren of her family: to the cliffs of the Grey Coast, where the land simply stopped.\n\nShe sat on a gorse bush and looked at the sea. It was very large and had no cracks in it. She looked for an hour, glad to have seen it, and then she turned round. There is no shame in going to the edge of the map and deciding you prefer the middle."],["The Knothole in the Hayloft","In August the orchard at Emberwick was all plenty. There were spiders in every crack and flies on every plum, and Tuppence the wren grew round and idle.\n\nMrs. Dunnock tried to teach her the winter places: which stones kept beetles under them in frost, where the moss stayed soft. \"You will want this,\" she said. Tuppence said yes, yes, and chased a moth instead. Winter was only a word the old birds used.\n\nShe did learn one thing that month, and it was not from prudence. She was simply nosy. High in the wall of the hayloft across the lane was a knothole the size of an acorn cup, and behind it a space lined with old hay, dry and still and smelling of summer. She went in, turned round, came out, and forgot about it until the nights turned cold."],["Feathers on the Ground","At summer's end Tuppence the wren began to fall apart. First one tail feather dropped out, then the other five. Her head came up in tufts. She looked, she thought, like something the cat had considered and declined.\n\nShe hid in the thickest part of the bramble by the orchard wall and resolved to stay there until spring. The bramble, she discovered, was already occupied. Far at the back, hunched and silent, sat Marlow the rook, lord of the elms. He was bald about the face, and one wing was showing its quills like a broken fan.\n\nThey looked at each other for some time. \"It is called the moult,\" said Marlow at last, stiffly. \"It happens to the best of us. I should be obliged if you did not mention it.\" They shared the bramble for a fortnight, and a great many blackberries, and neither has mentioned it since."],["The First Cold Night","The first frost came to the orchard at Emberwick in November, and Tuppence the wren met it alone. She had quarrelled with her five brothers in the summer over her singing, and had told them she could do very well without them.\n\nBy day this was true. By night it was not. A wren weighs about as much as a large coin, and so small a body cannot keep its warmth till morning. She tried the ivy, and the wind came through. She tried a crack in the wall, and the stone was like ice.\n\nShe sat with her feathers fluffed out and her feet tucked up and counted the hours. Across the orchard she could hear, faintly, other wrens settling down in other cold places, each one alone, each one too proud or too far away to say so. It was a long night, and she spent it thinking."],["Nine in a Knothole","On the second night of frost Tuppence the wren remembered the hayloft. She remembered the knothole high in its wall and the dry, hay-lined space behind it, which she had found in August out of pure nosiness.\n\nThere was room in it for more than one. So she sat on the hayloft roof at dusk and did the thing she was best at. She sang, with her tail straight up, loud enough to be heard from the orchard wall to the lane's end, and what the song said was: here, here, warm, here.\n\nThey came. Her five brothers came first, pretending they had been passing. Then three strangers arrived from the hedges. Nine wrens went through the knothole one after another and packed themselves into a ball, heads in and tails out. Tuppence was in the middle. It was the warmest she had ever been, and the quietest."],["Snow and the Rook","Snow lay a foot deep on the orchard at Emberwick, and the rooks were hungry. The ground they dug in was buried and frozen. Marlow, lord of the elms, sat on the hayloft roof with his feathers out and his dignity mostly gone.\n\nTuppence the wren came out of her knothole and saw him. She said nothing about the size of rooks. She went back inside, along the beam, and down to the hayloft floor, where the farmer's sacks had leaked oats into every gap between the boards.\n\nOne oat is a fair load for a wren. She carried it to the knothole and pushed it out, and it fell on the snow. Then she fetched another. By midday there was a small gold patch under the wall and seven rooks standing round it. Marlow ate last. Before he flew, he left one black feather on the sill."],["A Nest of Her Own","Spring came back to the orchard wall at Emberwick. A cock wren with a fine opinion of himself built three nests for Tuppence to choose from: one in the woodpile, one in the quince, and one in the ivy, in the very place where she had hatched.\n\nShe chose the ivy. She lined it herself, as wren hens do, with moss and hair, and with one black rook's feather that she had kept all winter. Brisk the robin sang from the top of the wall and left the cracks to her.\n\nShe laid six eggs. Five hatched on a Monday. The sixth, the smallest, took until Wednesday, and what came out was mostly beak. It opened that beak at the sky and made a noise that stopped a bee. Tuppence looked at it with great satisfaction. Once upon a wren, she thought, and tucked it under her wing."]],[["January: Cool Cream, Patience","January. My aunt Maud has left me three things: the dairy at Sedge Farm, two miles out of Emberwick; a brown cow named Dumpling, who dislikes me; and a wooden churn with a handle worn pale by her hands. I am Nell Harrow. I have never made butter, and mean to keep an almanac of how I learn.\n\nLet the cream stand a day until it smells faintly sharp, and have it cool, not cold. Churn steadily. For twenty minutes nothing happens and your arm aches. Then the sound changes from a slosh to a thump, and there are yellow grains swimming in thin buttermilk.\n\nPour off the buttermilk and keep it. Wash the grains in cold water, pressing with a wooden paddle, until the water runs clear. Work in a pinch of salt. I made half a pound, and ate a good deal of it standing up."],["February: Snowed-In Shortbread","February. Snow to the windowsills at Sedge Farm, and no getting to Emberwick. Dumpling the cow and I have reached an understanding: I warm my hands before milking, and she does not kick the pail.\n\nWith butter in the crock and nowhere to sell it, I made my aunt Maud's shortbread, which she wrote inside the lid of the flour bin as three numbers: one, two, three. One part sugar, two parts butter, three parts flour, by weight. Rub them together with cool fingers until the crumbs hold when squeezed. Press into a tin a finger deep, prick all over with a fork, and bake in a slow oven until pale gold at the edges, about three quarters of an hour. Cut it while warm.\n\nIt is plain as a snowfield and tastes entirely of butter. I begin to see why people care whose butter it is."],["March: What the Churn Leaves","March. Mud. Every churning at Sedge Farm leaves me a jug of buttermilk, thin and sharp, and for two months I gave it to the pig. Mrs. Pettigrew of Emberwick caught me at it. She has won the butter ribbon at the Harvest Fair twelve years running, and she looked at that jug as though I were pouring away silver.\n\nButtermilk scones, as she told them to me over the gate. Half a pound of flour, a teaspoon of baking soda, a pinch of salt, a walnut of butter rubbed in. Stir in buttermilk with a knife until it just holds together; it will take about a teacupful. Do not knead. Pat it out thick, cut in rounds, and bake hot for a quarter of an hour.\n\nThey rose like hats. A dairy wastes nothing, she said, and went off before I could thank her."],["April: Grass Butter","April. Dumpling the cow went out to the new grass at Sedge Farm on the ninth, and by the twelfth my butter had changed colour. Winter butter is pale as candle wax. This is yellow as a primrose, and softer, and smells of the meadow. I did nothing to deserve it. The grass did.\n\nTo honour it, a green butter. Chop a handful of chives and parsley from the garden bed, very fine, with a few leaves of mint. Beat them into a quarter pound of soft butter with a squeeze of lemon and a pinch of salt. Roll it in a damp cloth into a log, chill it in the dairy, and cut coins from it as wanted.\n\nA coin of it melting on a boiled egg, or on hot bread, is my whole supper tonight. I have written \"April\" on the cloth so I shall remember."],["May: The Butter Print","May. The best butter of the year comes now, when the Lowlands grass is tall, and everyone in Emberwick knows it. On market day I sold nine pounds from Sedge Farm before ten o'clock.\n\nMrs. Pettigrew's stall was beside mine. Every pat of hers carried a print, a thistle pressed in with a carved wooden stamp. Mine were bare. In my aunt Maud's dresser I have since found hers: a little cow, much worn, lying under a tree. Soak the stamp in cold water first or the butter will stick.\n\nFor supper, new potatoes, the first of the year. Scrub them, do not peel. Boil in salted water with a sprig of mint until a knife slides in, about fifteen minutes. Drain, return to the warm pan, and add a generous knob of May butter and a little chopped mint. Shake. Eat them from the pan."],["June: A Crock in the Stream","June. Heat. The cream at Sedge Farm turns before I can churn it, and what butter I make goes to oil on the dairy shelf. My aunt Maud's answer, I learned from a neighbour, was the stream. I now churn at five in the morning, pack the butter in a lidded stone crock, and stand the crock in the shallows under the alder with a flat rock on top. By noon it is firm as January.\n\nWith eggs plentiful, a weighing cake. Weigh three eggs in their shells. Take the same weight each of butter, sugar and flour. Beat butter and sugar until pale, add the eggs one at a time, and fold in the flour with a teaspoon of baking powder. Bake in two tins in a moderate oven for twenty-five minutes.\n\nSandwich it with jam. Dumpling watched me eat it over the gate, unimpressed."],["July: Clear Gold","July. Mrs. Pettigrew of Emberwick stopped at my stall and asked, not unkindly, how I meant to keep butter through the hot weeks. I had no answer. She told me to clarify it, and then looked as though she regretted giving away so much.\n\nMelt a pound of butter in a heavy pan over the lowest heat. Do not stir. A white foam rises; skim it off. Milky dregs sink to the bottom. When the middle is clear as amber, pour it gently through a clean cloth into a warm jar, leaving the dregs behind.\n\nWhat you have is butter with the parts that burn and spoil taken out. It keeps for weeks in the cool, and it will fry a pancake or an egg at a heat that would blacken ordinary butter. I fried mushrooms from the market in it, and wrote \"clear gold\" on the jar."],["August: Brown Butter, Burnt Butter","August. The Harvest Fair at Emberwick is five weeks off, and I have entered a pound of Sedge Farm butter against Mrs. Pettigrew's. I think of little else, which is how I came to burn a pan of it.\n\nBrown butter is butter cooked a minute past melting. Put a good lump in a pale pan, so that you can see the colour. It foams, then quietens, and the specks at the bottom turn the brown of a hazelnut shell. It smells of nuts and toast. Take it off the heat at that moment and pour it out, because the pan goes on cooking.\n\nMine went black while I stared out of the window, composing a speech of thanks. The second lot I watched. I poured it over runner beans with a squeeze of lemon, and it was better than any speech. Attend to the pan."],["September: The Fair","September. The Harvest Fair. Twenty pounds of butter sat on the long table in the Emberwick market hall, each under a numbered cloth. The judge tasted them with a horn spoon, and spent a long time over two.\n\nMrs. Pettigrew took the ribbon, her thirteenth. I had a card that said Second. I had expected to mind more than I did.\n\nShe found me afterwards by the cake stall and told me what she has told nobody. Wash it once more than you think it needs, because any buttermilk left in will sour the butter within the week. Then salt it properly: a small level teaspoon of fine salt to the pound, worked through until no streaks show. Press out every bead of water. \"Yours was the better cream,\" she said. \"Mine was the better washed.\" Her hands, I noticed, were stiff on the spoon."],["October: Apples and Cold Hands","October. Mrs. Pettigrew's hands are too stiff now to turn a churn. I walk into Emberwick on Tuesdays and Fridays and churn for her, and she sits by and corrects me. I learn more in an hour there than in a month alone at Sedge Farm.\n\nShe says butter pastry wants what butter wants: cold, and not much handling. Half a pound of flour, a quarter pound of cold butter cut small, a pinch of salt. Rub in with the fingertips only, lifting, until it looks like crumbs. Add cold water a spoonful at a time until it gathers. Rest it somewhere cool for half an hour.\n\nRoll it out, cut rounds, and fold each over a spoonful of sliced apple, sugar and a little cinnamon. Pinch shut, brush with milk, bake hot for twenty minutes. We ate four turnovers between us, and she allowed that they would do."],["November: The Lean Month","November. Dumpling the cow is to calve at midwinter, and I have stopped milking her so that she may rest. There will be no cream at Sedge Farm for weeks. I have six pounds of salted butter in crocks and one jar of clarified, and I count them as a miser counts.\n\nMrs. Pettigrew of Emberwick says that butter you cannot make must be made to go further, and showed me a spiced butter for cold evenings. Beat a quarter pound of soft butter with two spoonfuls of brown sugar, a teaspoon of cinnamon and a scrape of nutmeg until light. Keep it in a small pot.\n\nA little goes a long way. Spread it thin on hot toast or oatcakes by the fire, and it melts into something like a pudding. I took her a pot of it. I find I have stopped counting what I give her."],["December: The Year Turns","December. Dumpling calved on the shortest day, by lantern light: a brown heifer with a white star, already cross. I have named her Crumb. There is milk in the pail again at Sedge Farm.\n\nFor the midwinter market Mrs. Pettigrew and I shared one stall in Emberwick, her thistle print and my aunt Maud's little cow side by side. We sold butter biscuits. Rub six ounces of butter into half a pound of flour, stir in four ounces of sugar, the grated rind of an orange and one egg yolk, and knead lightly to a dough. Roll, cut, press the wet stamp on each, and bake in a moderate oven until just gold, about twelve minutes.\n\nA year ago I could not make butter. Now my hands know when the churn is about to turn. For whoever keeps this almanac next: begin with cool cream, and patience."]],[["The First Wet Evening","First day of rain. I am Hester Lumb, cook at the Ferry House, where the Emberwick road meets the river Ember and stops. It began at noon. By dusk Corwen the ferryman had tied up the boat and said the river was not to be argued with. One traveller is stranded already: a wool merchant, Mr. Garrick, who takes the weather personally.\n\nSo, onion soup, which wants only time. Slice four large onions thin. Cook them in butter in a heavy pot over a low fire for the better part of an hour, stirring now and then, until they are soft and brown as a saddle. Add a spoonful of flour, then a quart of stock and a bay leaf. Simmer twenty minutes.\n\nLadle it over toast with cheese on top. Mr. Garrick said it would do. I shall write down a soup each day until the ferry runs."],["Leeks for Wet Travellers","Second day of rain at the Ferry House. The river Ember is over the lower landing step. Three more came in off the road, dripping: Mr. Pell, a clock-mender with a case of small wheels, and two sisters, the Misses Finch, bound for market with a goose named Duchess on a ribbon. The goose has the wash-house. The rest have my kitchen fire.\n\nLeek and potato soup is the quickest comfort I know. Wash three leeks well, for they hold grit, and slice them. Soften them in butter with the lid on. Add four potatoes cut small, cover with stock, salt it, and simmer until the potato breaks under a spoon, about twenty minutes. Mash it roughly in the pot and stir in a little milk.\n\nFive bowls came back wiped clean with bread. Even Mr. Garrick, the wool merchant, had seconds, though he did not say so."],["Pease and Complaints","Third day of rain. The ferry has not crossed the Ember since Monday, and my four stranded guests at the Ferry House are beginning to know each other's faults. Mr. Garrick the wool merchant has explained to everyone what the delay is costing him. Mr. Pell the clock-mender has taken the kitchen clock apart on my table, to be useful.\n\nSplit pea soup. Soak a pound of dried split peas overnight. Drain them and simmer in fresh water with a chopped onion, a carrot and a sprig of thyme. Skim the froth. In an hour and a half the peas fall to a thick green porridge; thin it with hot water, salt it at the end, and stir in chopped mint.\n\nIt sticks to the ribs. Mr. Garrick complained that it was green. Tam, my pot-boy, ate three bowls and fell asleep in the settle."],["Barley Broth and a Clock","Fourth day of rain at the Ferry House, and the river Ember has taken the whole landing. Corwen the ferryman sits at the window watching his boat tug at her rope. In my kitchen the clock is back on the wall. Mr. Pell, the stranded clock-mender, has made it strike the hours, which it has not done since my mother's time.\n\nFor that he had first bowl of the barley broth. Rinse a teacup of pearl barley. Put it in the pot with two carrots, a turnip, an onion and a stick of celery, all cut small, and three pints of stock. Simmer gently for an hour, until the barley is fat and soft. Salt, pepper, and a handful of chopped parsley at the last.\n\nIt is plain, and nobody was ever sorry to see it. The clock struck six as I served. We all jumped."],["Mushrooms, Not from the Wood","Fifth day of rain. Tam, the pot-boy here at the Ferry House, came in from the wet wood with a hatful of mushrooms and a hopeful face. I thanked him and put them on the compost. I do not trust my eye with wild ones, and no soup is worth a wrong guess. The mushrooms in this pot came from the grower's trays at Emberwick market before the river rose.\n\nWipe a pound of them, do not wash, and slice. Cook an onion soft in butter, add the mushrooms and a pinch of thyme, and let them give up their juice and take it back again. Stir in a spoonful of flour, then a pint and a half of stock. Simmer fifteen minutes, add a cup of milk, and warm it through without boiling.\n\nThe Misses Finch pronounced it the best yet. Their goose, Duchess, got the bread crusts."],["Dumplings When Bread Runs Out","Sixth day of rain, and no bread. The baker's cart cannot reach the Ferry House, for the Emberwick road is under water at the dip, and my guests have eaten the last loaf. Mr. Garrick, the stranded wool merchant, asked what a soup was without bread. I said he would find out at six.\n\nDumplings. Mix four heaped spoonfuls of flour with a teaspoon of baking powder, a pinch of salt and chopped parsley. Rub in two spoonfuls of cold butter. Add just enough cold water to make a soft dough, and roll it into balls the size of a walnut with floured hands.\n\nDrop them into any simmering broth; I used yesterday's vegetable. Put the lid on and leave it on for fifteen minutes, however curious you feel. They come up twice the size and light as pillows. Mr. Garrick counted his, and then counted Mr. Pell's."],["Beans and a Leaking Roof","Seventh day of rain. The roof of the Ferry House has begun to leak above the stairs, and Tam the pot-boy has set my second-best pan under the drip. It rings like a slow bell all night. A week now, and the river Ember still too high for the ferry. The stranded guests have stopped asking Corwen when.\n\nWhite bean soup. Soak half a pound of dried white beans overnight in plenty of water. Drain, cover with fresh water, and boil them hard for ten full minutes before turning down to a simmer; dried beans must always have that hard boil. Cook until tender, an hour or more. Add a softened onion, a carrot, a bay leaf, and salt only at the end.\n\nCrush some of the beans against the side of the pot to thicken it. It is a patient soup for people who need patience."],["The Goose Pays Her Way","Eighth day of rain, and at breakfast Mr. Garrick the wool merchant looked through the wash-house door at Duchess, the Misses Finch's white goose, and wondered aloud how many she would feed. The elder Miss Finch stood up. I have not seen a man so thoroughly looked at. Nothing more was said.\n\nDuchess, perhaps hearing, laid an egg by noon. It was a large one, and the sisters presented it to my kitchen at the Ferry House with some ceremony.\n\nSo tonight, egg-thread broth. Bring a quart of clear, well-seasoned stock to a gentle simmer. Beat the egg in a cup with a spoonful of cold water. Stir the broth slowly in one direction and pour the egg in a thin stream; it sets at once into soft gold threads. Take it off the heat, add chopped chives, and serve directly. Mr. Garrick ate his very quietly."],["Parsnip, Apple, Almost Nothing","Ninth day of rain at the Ferry House. I took a candle down to the larder and came up with what there was: five parsnips, three wrinkled apples from the loft, one onion, and the heel of a pot of cream. The river Ember has kept the ferry tied up since the first day, and nothing has come in.\n\nIt makes a better soup than it sounds. Peel and chop the parsnips and onion and soften them in butter. Add the apples, peeled and cored, and a quart of water with a pinch of salt. Simmer until everything is tender, half an hour. Mash it smooth with a wooden spoon, or push it through a sieve if you have a willing pot-boy. Stir in the cream.\n\nIt is sweet and pale. Mr. Pell the clock-mender asked for the recipe, and wrote it on the back of a watch paper."],["Mr. Garrick's Bread Soup","Tenth day of rain, and nothing to put in a pot but water, garlic, and the dry ends of last week's loaves. I said as much aloud. It was Mr. Garrick, the wool merchant, stranded here at the Ferry House since the first evening and cross for every hour of it, who got up from the settle.\n\nHis mother made bread soup, he said. He rolled up his sleeves. Warm three sliced cloves of garlic in a little butter, slowly, without browning. Add a quart of water, a bay leaf, thyme, and salt, and simmer ten minutes. Tear in stale bread, two good handfuls, and let it swell until the soup is thick. Beat it with a spoon. A grating of hard cheese on top, if there is any.\n\nThere was a rind. He stirred the pot himself and served everyone before he sat down. Nobody mentioned it."],["Everybody's Pot","Eleventh day. The rain stopped in the night; I woke because the pan under the leak had gone quiet. But the river Ember is still brown and wide, and Corwen the ferryman says tomorrow, not today. One more supper, then, for my stranded guests at the Ferry House, and the larder bare.\n\nSo everyone turned out their bags. The Misses Finch had a twist of dried sage and six carrots meant for market. Mr. Pell the clock-mender produced an onion from his coat, unexplained. Mr. Garrick gave a paper of peppercorns he had been saving. Tam found barley at the bottom of the bin.\n\nInto one pot: the onion softened, carrots sliced, a handful of barley, water to cover well, sage, salt. An hour's gentle simmer. Pepper ground over at the table. It was not the best soup of the eleven, but it was the one we all leaned over."],["The Ferry Runs","Twelfth day, and sun on the wet slates. Corwen took the ferry across the Ember at eight with all of them aboard: the Misses Finch and their goose Duchess, Mr. Pell with his case of wheels, and Mr. Garrick the wool merchant, who shook my hand and left his peppercorns on the dresser. The Ferry House is very quiet. The mended clock strikes into an empty kitchen.\n\nThe last recipe is the one all the others began with. Keep a stockpot. Into it go onion skins, carrot ends, parsley stalks, celery tops and any clean bones, covered with cold water and simmered very gently for two hours, then strained and kept cold. Use it within two days.\n\nMine is on the fire now. I have copied out the twelve soups fair and put this book above the flour bin. It will rain again. We shall be ready."]],[["Four in the Morning","I am Wilf Aske, fourteen, and since Monday I am apprenticed to Mrs. Marrable at the bakehouse on Oven Lane in Emberwick. She has given me this book and told me to write down each bake once I have truly learned it. She says there are a dozen worth knowing. \"A baker's dozen,\" she added, and would not explain.\n\nFirst, the plain loaf. We begin at four, in the dark. A pound and a half of strong flour, two teaspoons of salt, a teaspoon of yeast, and warm water, about three quarters of a pint. Mix, then knead for ten minutes, until the dough is smooth and springs back from a thumb.\n\nCover it and leave it somewhere warm until doubled. Knock it down, shape it, let it rise again in the tin, and bake hot for thirty-five minutes. A done loaf sounds hollow when you knock underneath."],["Thirteen to the Dozen","My second month at Mrs. Marrable's bakehouse on Oven Lane, and she has put me on rolls. It is the plain loaf dough, cut into pieces the weight of a hen's egg. Cup your hand over a piece and move it in small circles until it tightens into a ball with a smooth skin. My first forty looked like knuckles.\n\nSet them on a tray, not quite touching. Let them rise until they lean on each other, brush with milk, and bake hot for fifteen minutes.\n\nFor every dozen, Mrs. Marrable has me shape a thirteenth. The dozen go to the shop. The thirteenth goes on a tin plate by the back door, and by the time I have swept the plate is empty. I asked who it was for. \"The dozen is for the shop,\" she said. I am Wilf, her apprentice, and that is no answer."],["Oatcakes on the Griddle","Third bake in my apprentice book. Mrs. Marrable says a baker in Emberwick who cannot make an oatcake will be laughed at as far as the Northern Fells, where they eat little else.\n\nNo yeast, and no oven. Half a pound of fine oatmeal, a pinch each of salt and baking soda, and a spoonful of melted butter. Pour in hot water, a little at a time, and mix to a stiff paste. Work fast, for it sets as it cools. Roll it thin on an oatmeal-dusted board and cut rounds, then quarters.\n\nBake them on a hot dry griddle, three minutes a side, until the edges curl. They should snap. Mine bent, the first day, and went to the hens. I, Wilf, counted twelve of the second batch for the shop and put a thirteenth on the plate by the back door. It was gone by six."],["Buns with Currants","Wilf Aske, apprentice, fourth bake. This week Mrs. Marrable let me make the currant buns that sell out by nine every Saturday at the bakehouse on Oven Lane.\n\nIt is an enriched dough, which means it is spoiled like a favourite child. To a pound of flour, a teaspoon each of salt and yeast, add two ounces of sugar, two of soft butter, one egg, and warm milk in place of water. It is sticky and slow. Knead it anyway, and work in a handful of currants.\n\nShape into buns, let them rise until puffy, and bake fairly hot for fifteen minutes. Brush them at once with sugar melted in a spoonful of milk, so they shine. I asked again who takes the thirteenth from the plate by the back door. \"Who is in the lane before we open?\" she said. Nobody, I said. \"Look harder.\""],["Gingerbread, Melted and Stirred","Fifth bake. Half a year at the bakehouse on Oven Lane, and Mrs. Marrable says I may be trusted with treacle.\n\nGingerbread is made backwards from bread: nothing is kneaded and everything is melted. Gently warm four ounces of butter with four of dark sugar and six of black treacle until it runs. Off the heat, stir in half a pound of flour sifted with two teaspoons of ground ginger and one of baking soda. Beat in an egg and a quarter pint of warm milk. It will be thin as paint. Bake it in a lined tin in a slow oven for an hour.\n\nIt is better on the third day than the first. I, Wilf, have been watching the back door, where each morning's thirteenth roll vanishes from its plate. This morning, at half past five, there was a shadow on the glass."],["Jam Tarts and Restraint","Sixth bake in the apprentice book of Wilf Aske, Oven Lane, Emberwick. Mrs. Marrable says pastry is the opposite of bread. Bread likes warm hands and hard work. Pastry likes cold hands and to be left alone.\n\nHalf a pound of plain flour, four ounces of cold butter, a pinch of salt. Rub the butter in with fingertips until it looks like breadcrumbs, and bring it together with very little cold water. Rest it. Roll it thin, cut rounds, and press them into the tart tin.\n\nNow the lesson: one small teaspoon of jam in each. Jam boils up. Bake them hot for twelve minutes and let them cool before eating, for hot jam bites. The thirteenth roll we leave by the back door each morning goes, I have learned, to Mr. Lunn the lamplighter, on his way home as we begin. He touches his cap to the oven."],["The Loaf That Fell","Seventh entry, and it is a failure, written down by order of Mrs. Marrable. I, Wilf Aske, apprentice, left forty loaves rising in the warm corner of the bakehouse and went to watch the carrier's horse being shod. I came back to dough risen over the tins, slack and full of bubbles. In the oven the whole batch sank like tired hats.\n\nThe test: press a floured finger into the dough. If the dent springs back at once, wait. If it fills slowly, bake now. If it does not fill at all, you have been watching a horse.\n\nWe did not waste it. Slice and butter a fallen loaf, layer it in a dish with currants and sugar, and pour over a pint of milk beaten with two eggs. Let it soak half an hour and bake until set and brown. It sold out. I was not praised."],["Grandmother in the Crock","Eighth bake. On the cold shelf of the bakehouse on Oven Lane sits a brown crock under a cloth, and in it lives something Mrs. Marrable calls Grandmother. It is old dough. Every day she saves a fistful from the morning's batch, and next day it goes into the new dough, as it did in her mother's time.\n\nTo use it: break a piece of yesterday's dough the size of an apple into the warm water, squeeze it to a milk, then mix the plain loaf as usual, with half the yeast. Give it longer to rise. The loaf comes out with a chewier crumb, a darker crust and a faint sourness, and it keeps two days longer.\n\nBefore you shape the loaves, save a fistful back. That is the whole rule. I, Wilf, the apprentice, forgot once. Mrs. Marrable had kept a second crock, and said nothing."],["The Harvest Plait","Ninth bake. For the Harvest Fair every bakehouse in Emberwick sends a plaited loaf to the market hall, and this year Mrs. Marrable said the one from Oven Lane would be mine. I am Wilf, her apprentice. I did not sleep much.\n\nUse the currant bun dough without the currants, a little stiffer. Divide it in three and roll each piece into a rope as long as your forearm, fatter in the middle. Pinch them together at the top. Then right over middle, left over middle, not pulling, until you reach the end. Pinch and tuck under.\n\nLet it rise until puffy but not doubled, or the plait will blur. Brush it with beaten egg and bake fairly hot for half an hour, until deep gold. Mine leaned to the left. Mrs. Marrable carried it to the hall herself, at arm's length, as though it were a crown."],["Scones and a Light Hand","Tenth bake. Mrs. Marrable's sister is poorly on the Grey Coast, and she talks of going to her for a week. First, she says, I must learn the scone, for the Thursday customers at Oven Lane forgive a baker anything but a flat one.\n\nHalf a pound of self-raising flour, a pinch of salt, two ounces of cold butter rubbed in, an ounce of sugar. Make a well and pour in a quarter pint of milk. Mix with a knife in a few strokes, only until it holds. Pat it an inch thick with your hand; no rolling pin.\n\nCut straight down with a floured cutter and lift straight up. Never twist: a twisted scone rises crooked. Brush the tops with milk and bake hot for twelve minutes. I, Wilf, twisted the first six out of habit. They came out leaning, like men in a wind."],["A Loaf Soaked in Tea","Eleventh bake, for midwinter. Mrs. Marrable leaves tomorrow for her sister's on the Grey Coast, and for one week the bakehouse on Oven Lane will be in my hands. Today she taught me the tea loaf, the kindest thing in the book: it cannot be hurried and cannot easily go wrong.\n\nThe night before, put three quarters of a pound of mixed dried fruit with six ounces of brown sugar and pour over half a pint of cold tea. By morning the fruit is fat. Stir in one beaten egg and half a pound of self-raising flour with a teaspoon of mixed spice. Bake in a lined tin in a slow oven for an hour and a half.\n\nWrap it and keep it two days before cutting. She has left me, Wilf, the keys. She has also left the tin plate by the back door, washed, for the thirteenth roll."],["The Thirteenth","I am Wilf Aske, and for a week I have run the bakehouse on Oven Lane while Mrs. Marrable was away. Each morning I put the thirteenth roll on the plate by the back door, and this week I watched. Mr. Lunn the lamplighter came, going home cold. So did the night shelver from Emberwick Library, yawning, and the carrier's boy. They had been up so the town could sleep. The first bread is theirs.\n\nThis book holds eleven bakes and one failure, which Mrs. Marrable counts as the twelfth. The thirteenth is my own. Take the roll dough, knead in a spoonful of honey and a handful of oats, shape, brush with milk, and press the tops in more oats. Bake hot for fifteen minutes.\n\nShe came home, ate one standing, and said nothing. Next morning there was a second name on the board, small, under hers."]],[["January: An Empty Shelf","January. The pantry shelf at my house on Marsh Lane, Emberwick, held forty jars every winter for thirty years. This winter it holds two. My wife Marjorie made the pickles, and I, Ambrose Tull, retired schoolmaster, only ate them. I have found her receipt book and mean to work through the year with it.\n\nOne page is nearly blank. It is headed Garden Pickle, the yellow one everybody asked for, and underneath she wrote only: \"You know how.\" I do not.\n\nI began with something she did write down. Red cabbage: shred a firm head finely, layer it in a bowl with a good handful of salt, and leave it overnight. Next day rinse and drain it well, pack it into clean jars, and cover it with cold spiced vinegar. It is ready in a week and best eaten within two months, while it still has its crunch."],["February: The Vinegar Itself","February. My wife Marjorie's receipt book says \"spiced vinegar\" on every page and explains it on none. I, Ambrose Tull, taught arithmetic for forty years and expect a sum to show its working. Mrs. Abbot next door on Marsh Lane showed me.\n\nTo a quart of malt vinegar add a spoonful each of black peppercorns and mustard seed, six cloves, a few allspice berries, two bay leaves and a piece of dried ginger. Warm it gently in a covered pan until it just steams; do not boil it or the kitchen will make you weep. Leave it covered until cold, and strain it into a bottle. It keeps for months and is the start of everything.\n\nI asked Mrs. Abbot about the Garden Pickle, the one Marjorie never wrote down. She shut her eyes. \"Yellow,\" she said. \"Bright as a daffodil. That is all I am sure of.\""],["March: Beetroot and a Clue","March. The last stored beetroot came up from the cellar at Marsh Lane looking like old boots. I am Ambrose Tull, a year into widowhood and three months into pickling, and I have learned not to judge by the outside.\n\nWash the beets without breaking the skin, or they bleed. Boil them whole until a skewer goes in easily, an hour for large ones. Cool them, rub off the skins with your thumbs, and slice. Pack the slices in jars. Warm a pint of spiced vinegar with two spoonfuls of sugar until it dissolves, let it cool, and pour it over. Ready in three days; eat within two months.\n\nThe carrier, taking a jar, said my wife Marjorie's Garden Pickle had been sweet as well as sharp. \"Not sugar only. Something from the orchard in it.\" I have written that beside her blank page: yellow; sweet; orchard."],["April: Rhubarb Takes Its Turn","April. Nothing is ready in the garden at Marsh Lane except rhubarb, which is ready all at once. My late wife Marjorie's book has a chutney for it, with a note: \"Ambrose will say it is too sharp. He will be wrong.\"\n\nCut two pounds of rhubarb short and put it in a wide pan with two chopped onions, half a pound of brown sugar, half a pint of vinegar, a spoonful of grated ginger, a handful of raisins and a teaspoon of salt. Simmer it gently, stirring often, for about an hour. It is done when a spoon drawn across the pan leaves a path that stays open a moment.\n\nJar it hot in clean warm jars, cover, and leave it a month to mellow. I tasted it from the spoon and thought it too sharp. I, Ambrose Tull, expect to be wrong."],["May: Radishes in an Hour","May. The radishes at Marsh Lane came up thick as a hedge, because I, Ambrose Tull, do not yet know how to sow thinly. My wife Marjorie's receipt book calls this one a quick pickle, for the impatient.\n\nSlice a bunch of radishes thin. Warm a teacup of vinegar with a teacup of water, a spoonful of sugar and a teaspoon of salt, until they dissolve. Pour it over the radishes. In an hour the liquid is pink and the radishes crisp and sharp. Keep them cold and eat within the week.\n\nMrs. Abbot, my neighbour, ate half the bowl and gave me another clue to the Garden Pickle that she never wrote down. \"Hers had a bite to it. The vegetables were never cooked soft. She salted them overnight in the blue bowl; I saw her do it.\" I have fetched the blue bowl down from the top shelf."],["June: Yellow, but Not Hers","June. The first small cucumbers, and a pickle in my wife Marjorie's book that comes out yellow. I, Ambrose Tull, thought I had found her unwritten Garden Pickle hiding under another name.\n\nSlice four cucumbers and two onions thin. Toss them with two spoonfuls of salt and leave them for two hours; then rinse and drain well. Warm half a pint of vinegar with six ounces of sugar, a spoonful of mustard seed and a teaspoon of turmeric until the sugar dissolves. Add the vegetables, bring it just to the simmer, and jar it at once. Keep it cool and eat within two months.\n\nI carried a jar next door to Mrs. Abbot in triumph. She tasted it. \"The right colour,\" she said. \"That is the turmeric, then. But hers had more in it, Mr. Tull. A whole garden.\" So: one spice found. The rest still missing."],["July: Nasturtium Seeds, a Plan","July. The nasturtiums have run over the fence at Marsh Lane and set fat green seeds, which my wife Marjorie pickled and called her capers. Pick them young. Soak them overnight in salted water, a spoonful of salt to a pint. Drain, dry on a cloth, and cover in a small jar with cold spiced vinegar. Leave them a month. They are peppery, and excellent chopped over fish from the Grey Coast.\n\nWhile hunting for a small jar I, Ambrose Tull, found something better. Inside the back cover of her book she had drawn the kitchen garden, bed by bed. One bed is marked with a circled P, and in it she wrote: cauliflower, French beans, small onions, one marrow.\n\nShe never wrote out her Garden Pickle. She planted it. It is too late to sow cauliflower this year, but Mr. Oakes the grocer sells them."],["August: The Marrow Problem","August. I, Ambrose Tull, planted six marrow seeds in the garden at Marsh Lane, in case some failed. None failed. I have left marrows on every doorstep in the lane, and found two returned to mine after dark.\n\nMy wife Marjorie's book has the answer, under the heading Glut. Peel a large marrow, take out the seeds, and cut the flesh into small dice. Salt it, leave it overnight to draw the water, and drain. Simmer it with a pound of chopped onions, a pound of chopped apples, a pound of sugar, a pint of vinegar, a spoonful of ground ginger and a few peppercorns, for an hour and a half, until thick and golden.\n\nJar it hot and keep it six weeks before opening. I shall need one marrow more, a small one, in October, for the Garden Pickle she never wrote down. I have dozens."],["September: Onions and Tears","September. Three pounds of pickling onions from Mr. Oakes the grocer in Emberwick. Pour boiling water over them, leave a minute, and the skins slip off. You will cry regardless. Leave them overnight in a bowl with a good handful of salt; rinse and dry them next day. Pack into jars and cover with cold spiced vinegar. They want six weeks at least.\n\nMr. Oakes weighed the onions and eyed me over his spectacles. Every September for thirty years, he said, Mrs. Tull bought the same things: mustard powder, turmeric, ground ginger. \"And she always asked had I a windfall apple or two, for the yellow pickle.\"\n\nSo there is the orchard. I, Ambrose Tull, went home to Marsh Lane and added it to the list beside my wife Marjorie's blank page: yellow, sweet, crisp, a whole garden, mustard, ginger, apple. It looks nearly like a recipe."],["October: The Attempt","October. Today I, Ambrose Tull, made my wife Marjorie's unwritten Garden Pickle from nine months of other people's memories.\n\nA small cauliflower in florets, a handful of French beans, a dozen small onions and half a small marrow, all cut thumbnail-small. Salt them overnight in the blue bowl, then rinse and drain. For the sauce, mix two spoonfuls of mustard powder, one of flour, a teaspoon each of turmeric and ground ginger, and four ounces of sugar to a paste with a little vinegar. Stir in the rest of a pint of vinegar and simmer until thick. Add the vegetables and one grated windfall apple, and cook five minutes only, to keep the bite. Jar it hot.\n\nIt is yellow as a daffodil. I tasted it from the spoon in the kitchen at Marsh Lane. It is close. It is harsh, too, and something is not there."],["November: Windfalls and Waiting","November. Six jars of yellow Garden Pickle sit on the shelf at Marsh Lane, and I, Ambrose Tull, do not much like them. It was my attempt at the receipt my wife Marjorie never wrote down. I told Mrs. Abbot next door that I had failed. She asked when I made it. Three weeks ago, I said.\n\n\"Then you have not tasted it yet,\" she said. \"Marjorie never opened hers before midwinter. Go and do something else.\"\n\nSo, apple chutney from the last windfalls. Two pounds of apples, peeled and chopped, a pound of onions, half a pound of sultanas, a pound of brown sugar, a pint of vinegar, a teaspoon each of ginger and salt. Simmer for an hour and a half until thick, stirring often, and jar it hot. It, too, wants a month. I am learning to shut the pantry door and walk away."],["December: You Know How","December. On midwinter eve I, Ambrose Tull, asked the neighbours of Marsh Lane to supper: bread, cheese, cold ham, and a jar of my yellow Garden Pickle, two months old. I opened it at the table. The harshness had gone out of it and the mustard had come round, as a cross child does.\n\nMrs. Abbot tasted it twice. \"It is not Marjorie's,\" she said. \"It is very good. Hers was never the same two years running, you know. She put in whatever the bed gave her. That is why she never wrote it down.\"\n\nThere are forty jars on the pantry shelf again, each labelled with what it is and the month it was made. After supper I opened my wife's book to the page that says only \"You know how,\" and wrote my receipt beneath, with the year. The facing page I have left blank."]],[["The Key and the Notebook","January. The garden at Number Nine, Tanner's Row came to me with a rusty key, a wheelbarrow with a flat tyre, and my great-uncle Corwin's notebook. Thirty years I sold ferry tickets at Ember Quay. I know tides and timetables. I do not know gardens.\n\nThe notebook's first page carries one rule, underlined twice: Wait a full year before you dig up anything you cannot name. Below it, in smaller writing: This will be harder than it sounds.\n\nSo today I only walked the paths. Everything is brown and flattened. By the south wall stands a bare grey stick, tall as my shoulder, with no label and no sign of life. My new neighbour, Mr. Hask, looked over the wall and said it had been dead for years. I cannot name it. By the rule, it stays."],["Snowdrops Under the Hedge","February. I came out this morning meaning to rake the dead leaves from under the hawthorn hedge, because a tidy garden seemed the least I could manage. Uncle Corwin's notebook stopped me. His February page says only: Leave the leaves. Look under the hedge on a mild day.\n\nI looked. Snowdrops, hundreds of them, pushing up through the brown litter like small white lanterns. I would have dragged a rake straight across their necks.\n\nSo I fetched a kneeling mat instead and counted, as I used to count passengers on the Ember ferry. I gave up at two hundred and forty. Mr. Hask, next door, says his own snowdrops are finished already, and looked a little put out. The grey stick by the south wall has done nothing whatever. I checked it twice, which I suspect is once more than a patient person would."],["Sweet Peas on the Sill","March. At last the notebook lets me do something. Sow sweet peas indoors, it says, one to a pot, and then go away. I filled twelve pots on the kitchen table at Number Nine, pushed a seed into each to the depth of my first knuckle, and lined them along the sill.\n\nThat was nine days ago. I have looked at them, by my own reckoning, some sixty times. Nothing. A ferry at least keeps to a timetable.\n\nMr. Hask leaned over the wall while I was sweeping the path and nodded at the grey stick. “You'll want that out before spring,” he said. “I've a good spade.” I thanked him and said it was not yet a year. This morning, the tenth day, one pot showed a pale green hook pushing up the soil. I told nobody. I simply stood there while the kettle went cold."],["The Weeds That Weren't","April. The gravel path was thick with seedlings, grey-green and hairy, and I had the hoe in my hand. Uncle Corwin's rule held me back: nothing dug up that I cannot name. I could not name these. I put the hoe away with poor grace and weeded the things I did know, which were dandelions and more dandelions.\n\nThis week the seedlings opened. Forget-me-nots, a blue river of them running the whole length of the path to the gate on Tanner's Row. People have stopped to look. The baker's girl asked if I had planned it.\n\nI said the garden had planned it and I had merely kept out of the way, which is the most honest sentence I have spoken about gardening so far. The sweet peas are out against the fence, eight of the twelve. The stick by the south wall is still a stick."],["Too Soon for Beans","May. I have learned what impatience costs, and it costs fourteen runner beans. The first week of the month was warm as a bakehouse, so I planted them out along their poles, a fortnight ahead of the date in Uncle Corwin's notebook. I thought the notebook was being fussy.\n\nOn the ninth night a frost came down off the Northern Fells. By morning every bean hung black and limp. Mr. Hask, whose own beans were still safe on his windowsill, had the kindness to say nothing at all, which was worse.\n\nI have sown fourteen more. They sit indoors at Number Nine until the date written down, and not an hour before. One good thing came of my sulking round the garden: the grey stick by the south wall has buds on it. Small, tight, green ones. So it is alive. I still cannot name it."],["Peonies and Plain Leaves","June. The bed beside the water butt looked so empty in winter that I nearly gave it over to cabbages. Now it holds eleven peonies, each bloom the size of a soup plate and the pink of the inside of a shell. Uncle Corwin's notebook says they sulked four years before flowering, and that he moved them once and was punished with three more.\n\nI cut one for the kitchen table at Number Nine and felt like a thief.\n\nThe stick by the south wall has come into leaf. I had hoped for something grand. The leaves are long, plain and rather rough, the sort you would not look at twice in a hedge. Mr. Hask examined them over the wall and pronounced it a weed with ideas above its station. He may be right. But I waited for the forget-me-nots, and I can wait for a weed."],["The Work of Sitting Still","July. No rain for three weeks. Every evening I carry twenty cans from the water butt, ten to the beans and ten to the sweet peas, and my arms have opinions about it. The notebook's advice for the month is brief: Water the roots, not the leaves. Then sit on the bench. Sitting is part of the work.\n\nI did not believe that last part. I am a person who ticked off ferry sailings for thirty years. But I tried it, with a cup of tea, at the hour when the swifts come screaming down Tanner's Row.\n\nFrom the bench I saw things I had walked past all summer. A blackbird has a path of its own under the currants. The sweet peas want tying in. And the plain-leaved shrub by the south wall, which I no longer call a stick, has grown a full hand's width since June."],["A Glut of Beans","August. The second sowing of runner beans, the one I made after the May frost took the first, has repaid me beyond all reason. I picked four pounds on Monday and four more on Thursday, and they are hanging there again today, long as rulers.\n\nI have eaten beans buttered, beans in soup and beans cold with vinegar. I have left bags of them on doorsteps along Tanner's Row like a burglar working backwards. Mr. Hask accepted his with dignity and returned the bag an hour later full of plums.\n\nUncle Corwin's notebook says to leave the six fattest pods on the plant until they rattle, then shell them and keep the seed dry for spring. So some of this summer is to be put away in a jam jar. I find I like that. A year ago I did not own a single thing that needed waiting for."],["Eleven Apples","September. There is an old apple tree in the corner by the shed, bent like a man looking for a dropped coin. Mr. Hask told me it had not fruited in his time. I had it marked in my mind for firewood, once my year of waiting was up.\n\nOn Saturday I went to prop the shed door and found eleven apples in the top of the tree, small, striped and red. I got them down with a walking stick and a colander.\n\nThey are sharp enough to make the eyes water, and in a pie they are the best I ever tasted. Uncle Corwin's notebook calls the tree Old Crooked and says it bears every second year, when it chooses. I took a slice round to Mr. Hask. He ate it standing at the wall and asked, quite humbly, for the name of the apple."],["Leaves and a Paper Bag","October. The garden at Number Nine is coming apart gently, like a parcel being unwrapped. I rake the leaves into a wire pen behind the shed, where Uncle Corwin's notebook says they will turn to good black leaf mould in two years. Two years! In January I would have laughed. Now I only wrote the date on a slate and propped it against the wire.\n\nA paper bag came from the seedsman in Emberwick: fifty daffodils and thirty tulips, looking like so many small onions.\n\nI planted them on my knees all Tuesday afternoon, each one in the dark with its nose up, and when I had finished there was nothing whatever to see. It is the strangest work I know, burying things on trust. The shrub by the south wall is turning yellow. Its leaves are dropping one by one, and it has still told me nothing."],["Mr. Hask's Good Spade","November. The shrub by the south wall is a bare grey stick again, exactly as I found it in January. Ten months of looking, and all it gave me was a summer of plain leaves. Mr. Hask came round with his good spade over his shoulder. “Nearly your year,” he said. “Shall I?”\n\nI will be truthful: I almost said yes. The spot would suit a rose.\n\nInstead I asked him in out of the wind and went through Uncle Corwin's notebook once more, page by page. Tucked in the back cover was a seedsman's receipt, seven years old, and on it in his writing: Wintersweet, for the south wall. Flowers on bare wood in the dark of the year. They say seven winters before the first. Do not let anyone talk you out of it. Mr. Hask read it twice and carried his spade home."],["Wintersweet at Last","December. I went out to the south wall this morning in my coat and slippers, with frost on every path at Number Nine, and smelled it before I saw it. Something like honey and spice, in a month when nothing has any business smelling of anything.\n\nThe grey stick is in flower. Small waxy bells, pale yellow with a stain of dark red inside, strung all along the bare wood. Uncle Corwin planted it seven winters ago and never saw this. I stood there until my feet went numb, and then I fetched Mr. Hask, who took his hat off.\n\nTonight there is a sprig in a jug on the kitchen table and the whole house is sweet with it. I have opened the notebook to the first clean page and written my own line under his rule, for whoever comes after me: He was right. Wait."]],[["A Mile of Hedge","January. These are the notes of Wilf Tarrant, hedger to the parish of Emberwick, kept for one year on the Long Hedge. It runs a mile along the Lowlands road, from the last house in town to Sallow Gate, and it has been let go for twenty years. It is tall, thin at the bottom and full of holes a sheep could stroll through.\n\nThe parish has given me the winter to lay it, and a boy to help. Pim Oakes is twelve and has never held a billhook. Today I showed him the cut: three parts through the stem, low and slanting, so the stem bends over but still lives. We call the laid stem a pleacher.\n\nHe watched, and said it seemed unkind to the tree. I told him to come back and look in May. We laid forty yards before the light went."],["Forty Whips","February. Halfway along the Long Hedge, by the stile, there is a gap eleven paces wide where the old thorns died out and nothing but nettle grows. You cannot lay what is not there. So the parish sent to the nursery at Emberwick for forty whips, and Pim Oakes and I planted them today in a double row.\n\nA whip is a young tree no thicker than a pencil and about as impressive. We put in twenty-four hawthorn, eight hazel, four field maple and four dog rose, each one heeled in firm with a boot.\n\nPim wanted to know how long before they made a hedge. I said seven years to be useful and fifteen to be handsome. He worked out aloud that he would be twenty-seven. I said I would be eighty-one, and that we had better both keep an eye on them."],["Blackthorn Winter","March. The blackthorn is out along the Long Hedge, white blossom on black wood before a single leaf has shown. From the Emberwick end it looks as though somebody has thrown a bedsheet over the far half-mile. With it comes the cold snap the old people call the blackthorn winter. It comes every year, and every year the town is surprised.\n\nPim Oakes and I laid the last stretch to Sallow Gate with numb fingers, and drove the final stake on the nineteenth. One mile, laid, staked and bound along the top with hazel rods.\n\nIt looks raw. The cuts show pale and the whole line leans one way like grass after wind. Pim stood back and said nothing, which is his way of saying it looks a ruin. We put the billhooks away oiled. From now until autumn the hedge belongs to the birds."],["Nests, and Hands Off","April. No hedger cuts in April. The rule is older than the parish of Emberwick, and the reason for it is sitting on five eggs. So Pim Oakes and I walk the Long Hedge with our hands in our pockets and a notebook, and call it work.\n\nWe have found nine nests in the mile. Two blackbirds, a song thrush, three dunnocks, a pair of long-tailed tits with a ball of moss and cobweb like a knitted purse, and two wrens. One wren has built inside the pleachers we laid in January, a yard from the road, bold as a toll-keeper.\n\nPim found that one himself and has told nobody but me. Every laid stem is breaking into leaf along its length, just as I promised him. He admits now that the cut was not unkind. Of the forty whips by the stile, thirty-nine show green. One hawthorn never woke."],["May Blossom","May. The hawthorn has flowered, and the Long Hedge is white from the last house in Emberwick to Sallow Gate. The scent is heavy, sweet, and not quite pleasant up close, which never stops anyone from burying a nose in it. Carters slow their horses to look. The stems Pim Oakes and I laid in winter are flowering as freely as the uncut ones, lying on their sides.\n\nUnder the hedge the bank has come up in stitchwort, red campion and the frothy white stuff children call lady's lace. Pim has started a list of flowers on the back page of my notebook. He is at twenty-two and spells most of them his own way.\n\nBad news by the stile. A rabbit has bitten two of the hazel whips clean through. Thirty-seven left of the forty, all in leaf. We fenced the gap with wire netting, a month late."],["Roses in the Dry","June. Dog rose and honeysuckle have taken over from the hawthorn. The roses are pale pink, flat as saucers and gone in two days; the honeysuckle smells strongest at dusk, when the big moths come to it. Walking the Long Hedge home to Emberwick in the evening is the best part of my wage.\n\nBut there has been no rain for a month, and the new whips by the stile are feeling it. Their leaves hang like wet washing. Old hedge has roots down to the cool; a whip planted in February has roots about the length of my hand.\n\nSo Pim Oakes and I carry water from the Sallow Gate trough, two buckets each on a yoke, three hundred paces there and three hundred back. Pim has given several of the whips names, which I advised against. It only makes the counting harder when one goes."],["Who Lives Here","July. Rain at last, two whole days of it, and the Long Hedge drank it standing. It came too late for two of the hawthorn whips by the stile, which had gone brown and brittle. Thirty-five of the forty remain. Pim Oakes took it hard. One of the two was called Gerald.\n\nTo cheer him I set him a census: every creature seen in or under the hedge in one week. He has filled four pages. A hedgehog that crosses the road at the same spot each dusk. Bank voles. A slow-worm under a flat stone. Yellowhammers, whitethroats, and the wren's second brood in the pleachers. Eleven sorts of butterfly, of which he could name seven.\n\nHe asked me how many lived in the mile altogether. I said nobody had ever finished counting, and that a hedge is a town with one long, thin street. He wrote that down too."],["Harvest Carts","August. The corn is coming in off the Lowlands, and the wagons go by the Long Hedge so heaped that the hawthorn combs straw off the top of every load. By evening the whole mile wears a yellow fringe, and the sparrows of Emberwick come out to pick it over.\n\nThe hedge has turned from flower to fruit. Haws are still green, sloes hard and dusty blue, hazelnuts pale in their frilled cups. The first blackberries are ripe at the sunny end by the town. Pim Oakes eats them faster than he picks and arrives each morning with a purple chin.\n\nWe mend the stile and scythe the nettles from around the whips. Thirty-five still, and the best hazel has put on a foot of growth. The wren in the pleachers has gone quiet. Her young are out somewhere along the street she built on."],["Blackberry Sunday","September. By old custom the second Sunday of the month is Blackberry Sunday, and half of Emberwick walks out along the Long Hedge with baskets, jugs, basins and one hat-box. I counted sixty people in the mile. The lower berries were stripped by noon; after that it was walking sticks to hook the high sprays down, and small children lifted on shoulders.\n\nSeveral stopped to look at the laying and said the hedge seemed thicker. It is: every pleacher has thrown up a row of new shoots, straight as candles, and you cannot see through it anywhere now. Pim Oakes explained the cut to the baker at some length. I let him.\n\nHis mother sent me a jar of bramble jelly, still warm. The label says Long Hedge in Pim's spelling. The sloes are ripening, and the fieldfares will be here soon for the haws."],["The Reckoning by the Stile","October. Hazelnuts are down, and so are the squirrels, the jays and Pim Oakes, all after the same nuts. The spindle bushes near Sallow Gate have opened their pink berries to show the orange seed, a combination no sensible person would choose and which looks splendid. Spiders have slung webs across every gap in the Long Hedge.\n\nToday we made the autumn count of the whips we planted by the stile in February. Forty went in. Frost, rabbits and the June drought took seven. Thirty-three stand, and the tallest hazel is up to Pim's belt.\n\nPim thought thirty-three a poor score. I told him it was a good one, and that a hedger who expects forty out of forty should take up carpentry. Next month we plant seven more in the spaces. A hedge is never planted once. It is planted, and then kept."],["Pim's First Pleacher","November. The leaves have come off the Long Hedge, and the work Pim Oakes and I did last winter stands plain: a mile of stems lying slantwise, stakes upright, the hazel binding running along the top like the rim of a basket. It looks like a thing made on purpose. In January it looked like an accident.\n\nThe parish has given us the next stretch, the far side of Sallow Gate. I handed Pim the billhook and stood back with my hands behind me, which was the hard part.\n\nHe took nine cuts where I would take three. He went too deep on one stem and it snapped, and he looked at me as if he had broken a window. But the next stem bent over sweetly and lay down on its neighbour, alive. We planted the seven new whips before dinner. He counted them in himself."],["Fieldfares on the Haws","December. Hard frost on the Lowlands road, and every twig of the Long Hedge furred white. The fieldfares have come down from the north in their hundreds. They work along the mile from the Emberwick end to Sallow Gate, stripping the haws and chattering like a drawer of spoons. What they drop, the mice have.\n\nSo ends my year of notes. One mile laid. Nine nests that I know of. Forty whips planted, thirty-three kept, seven more put in. One apprentice, who can now lay a stem in four cuts and has stopped apologising to the tree.\n\nI am sixty-seven next month, and my shoulder tells me about it. Today I gave Pim Oakes my second billhook, the light one, with his initials burnt into the handle. He will see those whips made into a hedge. I have asked him to keep the notebook going, in his own spelling."]],[["A Rose on a Broken Wall","July. I have taken the gatehouse at Brindle Hall, three miles up the Ember from Emberwick, on a seven-year lease and a great deal of optimism. The Hall itself lost its roof in a storm forty years ago. What the agent's letter called a rose garden is a square of brambles between fallen walls, with a sundial lying on its face.\n\nI am Ottoline Brack, lately a teacher of arithmetic. I have grown roses in four pots on a windowsill. I mention this so that what follows is understood.\n\nOn my first evening I climbed the rubble to see what I had done. High on the broken east wall, out of reach, one rose was in flower: white, with a blush at the heart, and a scent of apples that carried down to me in the dusk. Nobody can tell me its name. I intend to find out."],["Under the Brambles","August. Three weeks of clearing brambles at Brindle Hall, and I have the scratches of a woman who keeps forty cats. I had a billhook, leather gauntlets and a bonfire that sulked. The brambles had forty years' start.\n\nUnder them, though, the rose garden is still there. I have uncovered stone edging round four long beds, a path of worn brick laid in herringbone, and the plinth where the sundial stood. I also found eleven rose stumps, dead as doornails, and one rusted label that crumbled as I picked it up.\n\nThe ruin part of the bargain is plain enough. The west wall leans. The south wall is a heap. Only the east wall stands to its full height, and the white rose rides along the top of it where nobody can prune it, feed it or spoil it. I am beginning to think that is how it survived."],["What Mrs. Fenn Remembers","September. Old Mrs. Fenn of Mill Lane was the daughter of the last head gardener at Brindle Hall, and she came up in the carrier's cart. She is eighty-three. She walked the brick path with a stick and named every dead stump from memory.\n\nWhen I pointed to the white rose on the east wall she stood a long while. Her father, she said, raised a rose at the Hall called the Ember Maid, white with a blush, and it was never grown anywhere else. Everyone believes it went when the roof did. She would not say this was it until she could smell it in flower.\n\nIt is past flowering now. So I leaned a ladder on the wall, cut thirteen shoots, and set twelve in a trench of sandy soil behind the gatehouse. The thirteenth, an afterthought, went in a cracked pot by the pump."],["The Duchess Pays a Call","October. I write this in a temper. Mr. Dunmow, who farms the land beside Brindle Hall, keeps a white goat named Duchess. She has a beard, yellow eyes and no respect for a hurdle fence.\n\nYesterday I walked to Emberwick for lamp oil. When I came back, Duchess was standing in my cutting trench with the air of a guest who has enjoyed her dinner. Of the twelve cuttings from the white rose on the east wall, eleven were eaten to the ground. She was chewing the twelfth as I arrived, like someone tasting soup.\n\nMr. Dunmow came for her at dusk and promised a cartload of manure by way of apology. It is too late in the year to take more cuttings. All I have left is one thin afterthought in a cracked pot by the pump, which I do not expect to live."],["Twelve Roses by Carrier","November. The carrier from Emberwick brought a bundle of sticks wrapped in wet sacking, and with it most of my savings in another form. Twelve bare-root roses, ordered from a nursery in the Lowlands to fill the four old beds at Brindle Hall. I chose them from a catalogue by lamplight, which is how all foolish things are chosen.\n\nThere are three each of Widow Harrowby, a deep crimson; Marsh Lantern, yellow; Captain Thrale, striped pink and white like a boiled sweet; and Fellside Pearl, cream. So the catalogue says. At present they are twelve thorny twigs.\n\nI planted them in driving rain with Mr. Dunmow's apology manure under each, spreading the roots like fingers on a table. His goat watched from behind a newly mended fence. The one cutting in the cracked pot by the pump has kept its two leaves, for what that is worth."],["The East Wall Comes Down","December. On the night of the fourteenth a gale came off the Northern Fells and blew till morning. Some time after three there was a long, low roar from the garden, like a cart of coals being tipped.\n\nAt first light I went out. The top third of the east wall at Brindle Hall had fallen into the nettles, and the white rose with it. Forty years it rode up there, out of everyone's reach. Now it lay under a ton of stone, with one snapped cane sticking up like a hand.\n\nI am not ashamed to say I sat down on the sundial plinth and cried. Then I counted. The twelve new roses in the beds were untouched. The pot by the pump had blown over, but the cutting was still in it. I stood it up again. One does what one can reach."],["The Head Gardener's Ledger","January. The ground at Brindle Hall is iron, so I have been gardening at a table in Emberwick Library instead. They hold the Hall's garden ledgers there, forty years of them, in the hand of Mrs. Fenn's father. He wrote small and wasted nothing.\n\nI found his rose in the eleventh ledger. Ember Maid, raised here from seed. White, blushed at the centre like a child come in from the cold. Scent of apples. Seven leaflets to the leaf, not five. Slow from cuttings: one in a dozen strikes, if the gardener is lucky and leaves it alone.\n\nOne in a dozen. I copied the page out twice. Then I walked the three miles home in the frost, went straight to the cracked pot by the pump, and counted the leaflets on my cutting's last leaf. Seven. It proves very little. I counted them again anyway."],["A Lesson in Pruning","February. Every book agrees that roses are pruned in late winter and disagrees about everything else. Cut hard, says one. Cut lightly, says another. A third has diagrams like a geometry problem, and I taught geometry, and I still could not follow it.\n\nSo I knelt in the four beds at Brindle Hall with new secateurs and did my best with the twelve young roses. Mr. Dunmow, passing with his goat on a rope, watched me take Captain Thrale down to the height of an egg cup. “Bit severe,” he said. He was right. I had cut two of the three below every bud.\n\nThat afternoon he and his boy barrowed the fallen stone of the east wall off the buried white rose. The old root is there, thick as my wrist, split but not torn out. Whether it lives is not for us to say."],["Red Shoots","March. Good news, and I hardly dare write it down in case it notices. At the foot of the broken east wall at Brindle Hall, the old white rose has put up three shoots from its split root. They are dark red, fat as asparagus, and have come through the rubble as though stone were a small matter.\n\nAnd the cutting in the cracked pot by the pump, the afterthought, the only one the goat missed: I tipped it out today and found white roots circling the soil. One in a dozen, the old ledger said. It was one in thirteen, but I shall not quibble.\n\nTen of my twelve bought roses are in bud. The two Captain Thrales I pruned to nothing remain nothing. I have moved the cutting into the gatehouse porch and built a fence round the porch. Duchess has been seen looking at it."],["Greenfly and a Late Frost","April. I had not understood that everything on earth eats roses. This month it was greenfly, packed along every new shoot in the four beds like passengers on a market-day cart. I rubbed them off with finger and thumb for an hour each morning, and each morning they were back, with relations.\n\nThen, on the twentieth, a frost. The tender tips of the Marsh Lanterns went brown and limp overnight. I walked round the garden at Brindle Hall saying words I never used in a schoolroom.\n\nMrs. Fenn sent advice by the carrier: leave the greenfly and wait for the ladybirds. Within the week they came, red and spotted, and the blue tits with them, and the shoots were clean. The frosted tips have broken again lower down. The three red shoots on the old wall rose are knee high. I measure them daily, which helps nobody."],["Buds in the Rain","May. Rain every day for a fortnight, and the garden at Brindle Hall has gone soft and green and slightly mouldy, like a cheese. There is mildew on the Widow Harrowby and black spots on the leaves of Fellside Pearl. The sundial is upright on its plinth again, though it has had no sun to tell.\n\nBut there are buds. I have counted sixty-one on the ten bought roses, tight and green, each with a thread of colour at the tip.\n\nOn the old white rose by the east wall, one of the three new shoots carries a cluster of five. Whether they will open white and smell of apples, I cannot know for three weeks. I have asked Mrs. Fenn of Mill Lane to come at midsummer. She replied on a postcard: I have waited forty years. I can manage three weeks."],["The Ember Maid","June. Midsummer Eve at Brindle Hall. Half the garden is still a ruin: the west wall leans, the south wall is a heap of stone, and two Captain Thrales never came back from my pruning. But ten roses are in flower in the four old beds, crimson, yellow and cream.\n\nMrs. Fenn came up in the carrier's cart at four. She went straight past all of it to the foot of the east wall, where the old rose had opened three of its five buds that morning. White, blushed at the heart. She bent, with my arm to steady her, and breathed in. “Apples,” she said. “That's my father's rose.” Then she asked for a chair.\n\nSo the Ember Maid is not lost. There are two of her now, counting the cutting in the porch. Duchess the goat looked on over the fence. She was not invited."]],[["The Wager","January. I am Mrs. Dab, cook at the Copper Kettle inn on Emberwick market square, and I have made a wager I may regret. At last month's Midwinter Supper the sprouts came from the greengrocer and tasted of the cart. I said so. Mr. Ost, the landlord, said I was welcome to do better.\n\nSo it is agreed, in writing. Next Midwinter I am to feed forty guests with every vegetable and fruit on the table grown in the kitchen plot behind the inn. If I manage it, he buys me a new copper preserving pan. If I fail, I never mention the greengrocer again.\n\nThe plot is twenty paces by twelve and mostly dock. The gardener, Tob Wren, is seventy-one and speaks about twice a day. He looked at the paper, then at the plot. “Parsnips,” he said. I take that as a yes."],["Beans and Buckets","February. The kitchen plot behind the Copper Kettle is dug, and Tob Wren did most of the digging at the speed of a man winding a clock. I carried away dock roots in a coal scuttle. If I am to win my wager with Mr. Ost and serve the Midwinter Supper from this ground, I had better know what is in it.\n\nToday we sowed broad beans, two rows, each bean pushed in to the depth of a thumb. Seed potatoes are sitting in egg trays on my pantry windowsill, growing stubby purple shoots. Tob calls this chitting. He has put an upturned bucket over the old rhubarb crown to draw it up pale and early.\n\nI asked what else I could do. He said, “Wait.” A cook is used to things being ready in forty minutes. This is going to be a long year."],["Parsnips Teach Patience","March. We sowed the parsnips today, the first crop meant for the Midwinter table itself. Nine months in the ground, Tob Wren says, and three weeks before they so much as show. The seed is like flakes of brown paper and blows away if you breathe. I sowed one row and lost half of it to a gust across the Copper Kettle's yard.\n\nOnion sets went in too, two hundred of them, each pressed into the soil with its tip just showing. By next morning the blackbirds had pulled out thirty and left them lying on the path. I pushed them back. They pulled them out again.\n\nMr. Ost strolled down the plot with his hands behind him and asked whether I had ordered from the greengrocer yet, just in case. I said the only thing I required from him was the price of a preserving pan."],["The Landlord's Old Coat","April. War in the kitchen plot. The peas came up in a neat green line on Monday, and by Wednesday the pigeons from the Copper Kettle's stable roof had eaten a third of them. On Thursday night the slugs took the first carrots and left a silver signature.\n\nTob Wren and I have answered. He stretched black thread on sticks over the peas. I go out after dark with a lantern and a jam jar, collecting slugs, and what the kitchen maids think of their cook I do not ask. We resowed the carrots and ringed the row with ashes from my range.\n\nWe have also put up a scarecrow. It wears a coat Mr. Ost threw out and a pudding basin for a hat. From the taproom window the likeness is remarkable. Two customers have greeted it. The pigeons are less convinced, but the thread holds."],["Paper Hats for Beans","May. The kitchen plot is filling. Potatoes are earthed up in ridges, onions stand like green quills, and today Tob Wren and I planted out the leeks. You make a hole with a dibber, drop the seedling in, fill the hole with water and walk away. It looks like neglect. Tob says it is the proper way, and Tob has been right about everything since February, which is wearing.\n\nWe sowed the winter crops too, in a seedbed by the wall: sprouts, red cabbage and kale. These are for my wager, the Midwinter Supper for forty.\n\nOn the twelfth the sky cleared at dusk and Tob sniffed the air. “Frost,” he said. We covered every runner bean with a cone of newspaper from the Copper Kettle's taproom, forty-six paper hats in two rows. By morning the grass was white. The beans had not noticed."],["What a Cook May Eat","June. The first new potatoes came out of the kitchen plot today, pale as eggs, and I boiled them with mint within the half hour. Tob Wren ate nine. Mr. Ost, the landlord, ate eleven and said they were tolerable, which from him is poetry.\n\nHere is my difficulty. Everything in this plot wants eating now, and my wager is about December. If I serve the Copper Kettle's guests every pea and carrot as it ripens, there will be nothing for the Midwinter Supper but good intentions.\n\nSo Tob has run a length of twine down the middle of the plot. East of it is the summer kitchen: peas, lettuces, early potatoes, broad beans. West of it is the store ground: maincrop potatoes, onions, parsnips, leeks, carrots, the winter greens. Nobody picks west of the twine. I have told the kitchen maids. I have also, with more effort, told myself."],["Forty Jars","July. The kitchen at the Copper Kettle smells of vinegar, and my hands are stained the colour of a brown boot. The plot has given more than forty guests could eat in a month, all in the same fortnight, so I am putting summer into jars against the Midwinter Supper.\n\nSo far: twelve jars of pickled shallots, nine of beetroot, eight of runner beans laid down in salt, six of blackcurrant jam, and five of a green tomato relish I invented in a panic. Forty jars. One for each guest, Tob Wren pointed out, which is the longest remark he has made since spring.\n\nI am doing all this in the old preserving pan, the one with the dent and the loose handle. It boiled over twice. I mentioned this to Mr. Ost. He said a pan was a pan, and December a long way off."],["Onions on a String","August. The onions have fallen over, which alarmed me until Tob Wren explained that it is what onions do when they are finished. We lifted them and laid them on the path behind the Copper Kettle to dry in the sun, like a street paved in gold cobbles. Tob showed me how to plait them onto string. Mine look like a child's first knitting, but they hold.\n\nThe winter cabbages are another matter. The white butterflies found them, and now every leaf carries a crew of green caterpillars eating my wager.\n\nThere is no clever cure. Tob and I pick them off by hand each evening and drop them over the wall for the hens next door, who think very well of us. I counted two hundred and six in one night. Mr. Ost watched from the back door and asked if this was what I called cooking."],["Six Sacks and a Scare","September. We lifted the maincrop potatoes this week, Tob Wren forking and I picking up behind him. On the second row the leaves were blotched brown and the first potatoes came up soft and foul. Blight. Tob cut the tops off every row and burned them at the far end of the plot.\n\nI did not sleep well. A Midwinter Supper for forty without potatoes is a wager lost.\n\nBut we lost only that one row. The rest came up clean and dry, and we filled six sacks, which now stand in the dark of the Copper Kettle's cellar with their necks tied. The old apple tree by the wall gave four baskets as well. I wrapped each sound apple in a twist of paper and laid them in trays, not touching. Mr. Ost came down to count the sacks. He counted twice and went away quiet."],["A Cat for the Cellar","October. Tob Wren and I lifted the carrots and packed them in boxes of dry sand in the Copper Kettle's cellar, like putting children to bed. Nine squashes sit on the shelf above my range, hardening their skins. The onions hang in ropes from the scullery beam and knock the kitchen maids' caps off.\n\nI found a carrot box dug into and two apples gnawed. Mice. I will not be beaten at Midwinter by something that weighs less than an egg.\n\nSo the inn's cat, Mrs. Biggins, has been promoted. She is large, grey and not ambitious, but she now sleeps on the potato sacks with a saucer of milk for wages, and nothing has been touched since. I also caught Mr. Ost in the cellar with a candle, lifting lids. He claimed to be looking for a barrel. The barrels are on the other side."],["Frost on the Parsnips","November. First hard frost on the kitchen plot, and Tob Wren was pleased for once. Frost turns a parsnip sweet, he says. We have left them in the ground since March, and I dug one to try. It was as long as my forearm. Roasted, it tasted of honey and chestnuts. I ate it alone at the kitchen table and told nobody.\n\nWest of the twine the winter ground stands ready: forty-two leeks thick as broom handles, a row of sprouts like knobbly walking sticks, eleven red cabbages hard as cannonballs, kale past counting.\n\nI have drafted the bill of fare for the Midwinter Supper three times. I counted the leeks, then the potatoes sack by sack, then the jars. On paper there is enough. A cook does not trust paper. Mr. Ost of the Copper Kettle asked today what size a preserving pan usually is."],["The Midwinter Supper","December. It is done, and I write this at midnight with my feet in a basin of hot water. Forty guests sat down at the Copper Kettle to the Midwinter Supper, and every vegetable and fruit before them came from the plot behind the inn.\n\nLeek and potato soup. Parsnips roasted in dripping, carrots glazed, red cabbage stewed with apple, sprouts in browned butter, squash baked whole. Pickled shallots and beetroot down the middle of each table. Apple pudding, and blackcurrant jam with the cheese. Tob Wren sat by the fire and had first helping of everything. He said, “Good parsnips.”\n\nMr. Ost stood up at the end and paid his wager: a copper preserving pan, wide as a cartwheel, with my name stamped on the handle. I have hung it where he must pass it daily. A seed list came in this morning's post."]],[["Casting Off at Ember Mouth","The ketch Kittiwake left Ember Mouth on the second of September, and I was aboard her. My name is Wren Halloran. I am fourteen, and this journal was a present from my mother, who would rather I had been apprenticed to a draper.\n\nMy aunt is Captain Bess Halloran, and she has sailed the Salt Road every autumn for twenty years: north along the Grey Coast, across the open water called the Wide, to the island of Farholm, where the salt pans are. Emberwick cannot cure its fish or its bacon without that salt. We carry wool and candles out, and bring three hundred sacks home.\n\nThe crew is Aunt Bess, the mate Mr. Odd, the cook Dunstan, and me, signed on as ship's girl. I waved until the harbour wall was a grey line. Then Mr. Odd handed me a bucket and a scrubbing brush."],["The First Night Watch","Second day out from Ember Mouth, and I have been sick over the lee rail four times. Dunstan the cook says the lee side is the correct side and that I show promise. He gave me ginger tea and told me to watch the horizon, which stays still when nothing else will.\n\nThe Kittiwake keeps watches of four hours. Mine is with Mr. Odd, the mate, who is tall, bald and silent as a post. At midnight he woke me by tapping my boot. I stood beside him at the tiller in my aunt's old oilskin while the Grey Coast slid by, a darker dark to larboard.\n\nHe showed me the compass in its lit box and how the card swings. He said eleven words in four hours. By the end of it I was no longer sick, and the ship's cat, Biscuit, was asleep in my lap."],["Between the Sister Stacks","Third day on the Salt Road. The Kittiwake came up to the Sister Stacks, seven pillars of black rock standing off the Grey Coast like the stumps of a giant's fence. The tide runs between them fast and pale. Most skippers go round outside and lose half a day.\n\nMy aunt, Captain Bess Halloran, goes through the middle, between the third Sister and the fourth. She told me so at breakfast, with a mouth full of porridge.\n\nShe took the tiller. Mr. Odd stood in the bows and held up one hand or the other. I was told to sit on the hatch, hold the cat and keep quiet, and I managed two of the three. The rock went by so close I could see the limpets. Then we were out, in flat green water, and Dunstan the cook brought up tea as if nothing had happened."],["Fog and the Lead Line","Fifth day out. We woke inside a cloud. I could not see the bowsprit of the Kittiwake from the tiller. Somewhere to larboard was the Grey Coast, with all its rocks. Nobody could say how far.\n\nSo my aunt, Captain Halloran, set me to the lead line. It is a long cord with a lump of lead on the end and bits of leather and rag tied in at each fathom, so you can tell the depth by feel. A hollow in the bottom of the lead is filled with tallow, and brings up a sample of the sea floor.\n\nI called nine fathoms, then eight, then seven. The tallow came up with grey sand, then broken shell. “Shell means the Dunnock bank,” said Mr. Odd, the mate. “We are where we should be.” I have never been so proud of a handful of grit."],["Wool for Dunnock Bay","Sixth day. The fog lifted at noon like a lid, and there was Dunnock Bay half a mile off: a curve of shingle, a stone quay and thirty houses, every one with nets drying on the fence. It is the only harbour on the Salt Road between Ember Mouth and the open sea.\n\nThe Kittiwake lay alongside for a day. We landed forty bales of Lowland wool, and I kept the tally while my aunt, Captain Halloran, argued prices with the quay-master. They have argued every September for twenty years. Both enjoy it.\n\nDunstan the cook took me ashore for fresh bread and a sack of onions. A woman at the bakehouse asked whether I was Bess Halloran's niece, and gave me a currant bun for nothing when I said yes. It seems my aunt is somebody along this coast. She has not mentioned it."],["Across the Wide","Eighth day. North of Dunnock Bay the Grey Coast bends away west and the Salt Road leaves it. Ahead lies the Wide: two days of open water with no land in sight, and the island of Farholm on the far side.\n\nThis morning my aunt, Captain Halloran, put me at the tiller of the Kittiwake. Not beside it. At it. “North by east,” she said, “and mind her, she pulls.” Then she went below, or pretended to.\n\nThe ketch does pull. She leans on the tiller like a dog on a lead, and the trick is to give a little and take a little and never fight. I held her for an hour, with the compass card steady and my arms shaking. Six dolphins came and rode under the bows. Mr. Odd said they come for good steering. I think that was a joke, his first."],["The White Island","Tenth day out of Ember Mouth. We raised Farholm at dawn, and I thought at first it was snow. The island is low and treeless, and all along its south shore the salt lies heaped in white hills beside the pans.\n\nThe pans are shallow ponds floored with clay, hundreds of them, divided by little walls. The tide lets the sea in through a gate. Sun and wind do the rest, all summer, until the water is gone and the salt is left in a crust. The salt-makers rake it up with wooden rakes as broad as a door.\n\nMy aunt, Captain Halloran, was met on the jetty by the salt-master, an old man called Imre with a face like a walnut. He looked the Kittiwake over, then me. “Can she carry a sack?” he said. I said I could carry two. That was not true."],["Three Hundred Sacks","Twelfth day. We have been two days loading at Farholm, and I can report that a sack of salt weighs about the same as I do. I carried one, forty times, down a plank into the hold of the Kittiwake, and tonight my shoulders feel as though they belong to somebody else.\n\nMr. Odd, the mate, stows them. Every sack must sit tight against its neighbour, on boards, clear of the damp. Wet salt is spoiled salt, and three hundred sacks is Emberwick's whole winter. My aunt counts each one into her book. Imre the salt-master counts each one into his. At the end the two books agreed, and they shook hands as though surprised.\n\nThen the salt-makers gave us supper on the shore: flat bread, smoked fish and a soup of seaweed that Dunstan the cook asked the recipe for. I fell asleep sitting up."],["Becalmed on the Wide","Sixteenth day. The Kittiwake left Farholm with three hundred sacks of salt in her and a fair wind. The wind lasted one day. Since then we have lain on the Wide for three days without moving, on a sea like a pewter plate. A piece of orange peel I dropped at breakfast was still alongside at supper.\n\nNobody is worried except me. My aunt, Captain Halloran, mends a sail. Mr. Odd splices rope and has taught me three knots: the bowline, the clove hitch and the rolling hitch. Dunstan the cook is teaching Biscuit the cat to shake hands.\n\nI asked my aunt how she could bear to sit so still. She said that on the Salt Road you get the weather you are given, and the only thing a sailor can hurry is herself. Then she handed me the other end of the sail."],["The Gale and the Pump","Eighteenth day. The wind we wanted came back, and brought all its relations. By dusk it was blowing a gale from the north-east, and the Kittiwake was running south before it under a scrap of sail, with green water coming over the bows.\n\nMy aunt, Captain Halloran, and Mr. Odd took the tiller together. Dunstan the cook and I had the pump. You heave its long iron handle up and down, and the sea you have taken in goes out again in spurts. Three hundred sacks of salt lay under our feet. If the water in the hold reached the boards they sit on, the cargo was lost.\n\nWe pumped by turns all night, two hundred strokes each, counting aloud. I was frightened for the first hour. After that I was too tired, and only counted. At dawn the hold was dry and the wind was easing."],["The Ember Light","Twentieth day. After the gale the sea ran in long grey hills, and the Kittiwake climbed each one slowly, heavy with her salt. We had been blown far to the south. Nobody was sure how far. My aunt, Captain Halloran, took a sight of the sun at noon and frowned at her sums.\n\nAt dusk she sent me up the mainmast, because I am the lightest. I went up with my heart knocking, hooked an arm round the mast, and looked west.\n\nThere was a light. It flashed twice, then stayed dark while I counted ten, then flashed twice again. I shouted it down exactly so. “Two and ten,” my aunt called up. “That is the Ember light. We are a day from home.” Mr. Odd said I had sharp eyes. Dunstan the cook said he had known where we were all along, by the smell."],["Salt on the Quay","Twenty-first day, and the last. The Kittiwake crossed the bar at Ember Mouth on the afternoon flood and came alongside the quay she left three weeks ago. Half of Emberwick seemed to be waiting. The fish-curers had their carts lined up before our ropes were fast.\n\nWe swung the salt ashore, three hundred sacks, and my aunt, Captain Halloran, counted them out as carefully as she counted them in. Not one was wet. Mr. Odd allowed himself to look pleased. My mother was on the quay in her good hat, and held me at arm's length to see how brown I was.\n\nBefore I went ashore my aunt opened the ship's book. Beside my name she crossed out ship's girl and wrote ordinary seaman, and the date. “The Salt Road goes again next September,” she said. I have nine pages left in this journal. I shall need another."]],[["Outward Bound for Lemons","Logbook of the schooner Heron, of Ember Mouth. Josiah Marl, master. Day 1. Sailed on the morning tide, wind north-west, fresh. Crew: Mrs. Agnes Tully, mate; Ham Biddle, cook; Pike and Old Sorrel, hands. One hen, named Admiral, for eggs. Our commission is from the Glasshouse Society of Emberwick, who have built a glasshouse and now want something to put in it. We are to sail south to the Lantern Isles and bring home forty young lemon trees, alive. I have carried coal, slates and a church organ. I have never carried an orchard.\n\nDay 3. Wind steady. Made ninety miles by the log line. The last of the Lowlands shore is astern. The Society's secretary supplied a pamphlet on the care of lemon trees. It runs to thirty pages. I have read four. Admiral has laid no egg. The cook says she is settling in."],["Fair Wind South","Logbook of the schooner Heron, Josiah Marl, master, bound south for the Lantern Isles to fetch lemon trees. Day 8. Wind north, fair. Ran a hundred and ten miles noon to noon, our best yet. Flying fish seen by Pike, who was not believed until one landed on deck. Ham Biddle fried it. Tasted like herring with ambitions.\n\nDay 9. Warm enough to go without a coat. Mrs. Tully, the mate, has the hands building racks along the deck: forty slots, each to hold a tree in its tub, with a canvas screen to keep off spray. She has read the whole pamphlet. She informs me that lemon trees dislike salt, wind, cold, drought and being moved. I asked what they do like. She said the Lantern Isles.\n\nDay 10. Bread from the Emberwick bakehouse is disappearing faster than five people eat. Suspect rats. Admiral: no egg."],["A Stowaway in the Bread","Logbook of the schooner Heron, Josiah Marl, master, southbound for the Lantern Isles. Day 15. The rat has been found. It is four feet six inches high and answers to Tam Reedy, aged eleven, nephew to the baker at Emberwick. He came aboard in the bread locker and has lived since behind the spare sails, on loaves and rainwater. Old Sorrel discovered him by sitting on him.\n\nDay 16. Too far south to turn back. Have entered him in the book as cabin boy, without wages, and written a letter for his aunt to go by the first ship we meet. He says he wished to see a lemon growing. I said most people manage to wish it from the shore.\n\nDay 17. The boy has scrubbed the deck twice unasked and feeds the hen. Admiral laid her first egg, at his feet. The cook is offended."],["Flat Calm","Logbook of the schooner Heron, Josiah Marl, master, bound for the Lantern Isles with five crew, one stowaway and a hen. Day 23. No wind. Sea like glass. Made four miles, all of it sideways. Day 24. No wind. Rigged an awning. Mrs. Tully, the mate, set the hands to scraping and painting, on the principle that idle sailors start to sing.\n\nDay 25. No wind. Tam Reedy, the boy, asked whether we were lost. I showed him the chart, the noon sight and my sum, and how the three agree. He asked to try the sum. He got it wrong by two hundred miles, which put us up a mountain. He tried again and got it right. I did not learn it so quickly.\n\nDay 26. A breath from the north-east at sunset. Admiral laid two eggs, the calm suiting her better than it suits me."],["Squall and a Sprung Plank","Logbook of the schooner Heron, Josiah Marl, master, bound south for the Lantern Isles. Day 32. Black squall from the west. Shortened sail in time, barely. Rain so thick that Pike, at the bows, could not be seen from the wheel. Caught nine buckets of fresh water off the awning.\n\nDay 33. Ham Biddle, the cook, reports his galley floor wet. A plank has sprung forward, below the waterline. She is taking a bucket an hour. Old Sorrel went into the forepeak with oakum, a mallet and the boy Tam Reedy to hold the lantern, since the boy is the only one who fits.\n\nDay 34. Leak stopped. Sorrel says the boy held the light steady for three hours and did not once complain of the smell. Have amended the book: Reedy, cabin boy, with wages, one penny a day from this date. Admiral: one egg."],["The Lantern Isles","Logbook of the schooner Heron, of Ember Mouth. Josiah Marl, master. Day 41. Landfall. Raised the Lantern Isles, four green hills on the sea with cloud sitting on the tallest. We smelled them before we saw them: warm earth and blossom, after forty days of salt and tar. The boy, Tam Reedy, was at the masthead and nearly fell out of it.\n\nDay 42. Anchored in six fathoms off the town of Marivel, in water so clear the anchor could be seen lying on the sand. The harbour-master asked our business. When I said forty lemon trees for a cold grey town in the north, he laughed for some time, then sent for his cousin.\n\nDay 43. All hands ashore. Lemons grow here in every yard, like apples at home, and nobody looks at them. The boy stood under one for a quarter of an hour."],["Forty Trees in Tubs","Logbook of the schooner Heron, Josiah Marl, master, at anchor off Marivel in the Lantern Isles. Day 45. The harbour-master's cousin is a grower, Madam Brin, who has an orchard on the hill. Chose forty lemon trees, three years old, waist high, each in a wooden tub. Price agreed after two hours and a great deal of coffee.\n\nDay 47. She will not release them until we have been taught. All hands attended, the hen excepted. Water at the root, in the evening, fresh and not much. Shade at noon. No salt on the leaf, ever. She made Mrs. Tully, the mate, repeat it. She made me repeat it.\n\nDay 50. Trees carried down by mule and ferried out in the boat, four at a time. All forty stand in their racks on deck. Madam Brin gave the boy, Tam Reedy, a lemon. He will not eat it."],["Homeward with an Orchard","Logbook of the schooner Heron, Josiah Marl, master, homeward bound from the Lantern Isles to Ember Mouth. Day 52. Weighed anchor at dawn, wind south-east, light. Forty lemon trees in tubs on deck behind canvas screens. The schooner looks like a hedge under sail.\n\nDay 54. The trees now govern the ship. Screens are shifted with every change of wind. The noon awning goes over the trees first and the helmsman second. Each tree is watered at dusk by Tam Reedy, the cabin boy, one tin mugful each, and he has numbered the tubs in chalk so that none is missed.\n\nDay 56. Spray came aboard on the starboard side. Mrs. Tully, the mate, had all hands up at two in the morning, wiping leaves with fresh water and rags. Never thought to see Old Sorrel dust a tree. Admiral: one egg, laid in tub nineteen."],["Short of Water","Logbook of the schooner Heron, Josiah Marl, master, homeward bound for Ember Mouth with lemon trees. Day 60. Head winds for a week. Behind our reckoning, and the fresh water is lower than I like: four casks left, and forty trees drinking beside six people and a hen.\n\nDay 61. Two trees dead, numbers seven and thirty-one, scorched by the salt of five nights ago. Thirty-eight remain. Put all hands on short allowance: three pints a day each. The trees keep their full mug. Ham Biddle, the cook, observed that he had never before been ranked below a shrub. Pike gave half his own water to tree number twelve, thinking himself unobserved.\n\nDay 63. Rain at last, a long soft afternoon of it. Filled two casks. The boy, Tam Reedy, stood in it with his mouth open. Full allowance restored. Admiral: no egg, in protest at the damp."],["The Cold Comes Down","Logbook of the schooner Heron, Josiah Marl, master, northbound for Ember Mouth, carrying thirty-eight lemon trees. Day 69. Wind backed north overnight and brought the cold with it. The trees have never met a cold wind in their lives, and showed it by dropping leaves all over my clean deck.\n\nDay 70. Three more dead by morning. Thirty-five remain. Ordered every tub carried below. There are now eleven lemon trees in my cabin, six in the mate's, four in the galley beside the stove, and the rest between decks. I sleep with my feet in foliage. Mrs. Tully says hers rustle.\n\nDay 72. The boy, Tam Reedy, carries a lantern round at night to see that none is in a draught. He has asked whether Emberwick will be too cold for them. I told him about the glasshouse. He had never heard of such a thing."],["A Gale in Home Waters","Logbook of the schooner Heron, Josiah Marl, master, northbound, perhaps eight days from Ember Mouth. Day 79. Glass falling since noon. Sky the colour of slate to the north-west. Doubled the lashings on everything, the lemon trees included.\n\nDay 80. Full gale. Hove to under a storm staysail for fourteen hours, the Heron riding it like the bird she is named for. Below decks, less orderly. A sea struck us on the beam about midnight and the tubs in the galley broke loose. Ham Biddle, the cook, and the boy, Tam Reedy, held them upright with their arms for an hour.\n\nDay 81. Gale blown out. Three trees lost, snapped at the root. Thirty-two remain of the forty. No person hurt, beyond the cook's dignity. Admiral was found in my cabin, roosting in tree number nineteen. She had laid an egg in the tub. I let her stay."],["Thirty-Two and a Blossom","Logbook of the schooner Heron, Josiah Marl, master. Final entries. Day 87. Raised the Grey Coast at dawn. Carried the lemon trees up on deck for the sun, since a ship should come home looking her best. The boy, Tam Reedy, went round with his mug and stopped at tub twelve. One white flower, with a scent that reached the wheel. Pike claims that tree as his own.\n\nDay 88. Crossed the bar at Ember Mouth. The Glasshouse Society was on the quay with a brass band. Landed thirty-two trees of forty, all living, one in blossom. The secretary wept. The baker of Emberwick came aboard, embraced her nephew, and then boxed his ears. Paid off the crew. The boy drew fifty-five pennies and has asked to sign for the next voyage. I have said he must ask his aunt. Admiral goes with the trees. Voyage ends."]],[["Sheet One: Ember Mouth","Sheet One covers Ember Mouth, from the town quay to the bar, at six inches to the mile. I am Isolde Marrin, surveyor to the Harbour Board of Emberwick, and these are the notes that go with my charts. The Board has asked for the whole Grey Coast, Ember Mouth to Cape Lastlight, in twelve sheets. The last survey is forty years old and marks a sandbank where there is now a fish market.\n\nMy vessel is the Plover, a twenty-foot open boat with a brown sail. My assistant is Benno Catt, who is sixteen, rows well and writes a clear figure.\n\nOne matter the Board asked me to settle. Two honest skippers have reported a shoal off Gannet Head, the Kettle Shoal, in two different places. A shoal cannot be in two places. I have promised to find where it is, or prove it is nowhere."],["Sheet Two: The Marsh Shore","Sheet Two runs north from Ember Mouth along the marsh shore: six miles of mud, creek and samphire, with no rock bigger than a loaf. It is the easiest sheet to sail and the hardest to draw, because the edge of the land will not stay put. At high water my boat, the Plover, floats over meadows. At low water she sits on mud a mile from the sea.\n\nBenno Catt and I sounded every creek with a marked pole. The marsh people mark the channels with withies, willow sticks pushed into the mud, and move them when the channels move. My note on the chart says: ask at the eel-catcher's hut.\n\nThe eel-catcher gave us smoked eel and an opinion. A shoal, he said, is always in one place, and the mistake is always in the man. I wrote that in the margin for Gannet Head."],["Sheet Three: The Sister Stacks","Sheet Three shows the Sister Stacks, seven pillars of black rock a mile off the Grey Coast, and the fast pale water between them. I am Isolde Marrin, surveying for the Harbour Board in the open boat Plover, and I do not mind saying this sheet frightened me.\n\nWe could not sound the channels under sail. So Benno Catt rowed, and I swung the lead from the bows, and we worked each gap at slack water, in the twenty minutes when the tide stops to think. Seven Sisters, six channels, nine days. The gap between the third Sister and the fourth carries five fathoms clear. I have marked it, with a warning.\n\nTo fix each rock I measured the angle to it from two places on shore. Where the two lines cross on paper, there is the rock. It is the whole of my trade, that crossing of two lines."],["Sheet Four: Dunnock Bay","Sheet Four is Dunnock Bay, the one good harbour between Ember Mouth and Gannet Head: a curve of shingle, a stone quay, and clean sand to anchor in at four fathoms. After the Sister Stacks it was like drawing a teacup.\n\nBenno Catt and I lodged a week above the bakehouse. The church tower makes a fine mark from seaward and I have sketched it in the margin, as it looks from the south-east, so a stranger may know it. A chart should show what a sailor sees, as well as what a bird would.\n\nI asked on the quay about the Kettle Shoal. Everybody knew of it. Nobody had seen it. An old net-mender told me it shows only at the lowest tides of spring, a patch of brown weed and broken water. She pointed north, towards Gannet Head, with her needle."],["Sheet Five: The Two Reports","Sheet Five takes the Grey Coast north from Dunnock Bay to the foot of Gannet Head, eight miles of low cliff. I am Isolde Marrin, surveyor; my boat is the Plover. Here the puzzle of the Kettle Shoal begins, so I set down both reports as the Harbour Board gave them.\n\nSkipper Maddern, fifteen years ago: struck weed and saw rock under his keel, the white house on Gannet Head bearing west-south-west, about a mile off. Skipper Hobb, two years ago: saw broken water close aboard, the white house bearing west-north-west, about a mile off.\n\nThe old chart shows one white house on the Head, Hollin Farm. The Board's clerk ruled both bearings from it and marked both places. They lie three-quarters of a mile apart. Benno Catt, my assistant, says one man was mistaken. Both men, I am told, are careful. That is what troubles me."],["Sheet Six: Maddern's Mark","Sheet Six is the south side of Gannet Head, a great grey shoulder of cliff loud with seabirds. Hollin Farm stands on its seaward slope, the white house of the old chart, though forty winters have weathered it nearer to grey.\n\nHere Benno Catt and I went looking for the Kettle Shoal in the first of its two reported places. Skipper Maddern put it a mile off, with the white house bearing west-south-west. I set the Plover on that line and we sounded a square half a mile each way. It took four days.\n\nFourteen fathoms. Fourteen, fifteen, fourteen. Fine grey sand on the tallow, and never a rock. Whatever Skipper Maddern struck, it is not where the Harbour Board's clerk has drawn it. I wrote on the working sheet: Not here. Benno wanted to add a rude word. I allowed a small one, in pencil."],["Sheet Seven: Hobb's Mark","Sheet Seven covers the water east of Gannet Head, and the second place where the Kettle Shoal is said to lie. Skipper Hobb, two years ago, saw broken water about a mile off, with the white house bearing west-north-west. The Harbour Board's clerk ruled that line from Hollin Farm, the only white house on the old chart.\n\nI am Isolde Marrin, surveyor, and I gave Hobb's mark five days in the open boat Plover. Sixteen fathoms, sand and small shell. A grey seal came up each morning to watch the lead go down. Benno Catt, my assistant, leaned out to see it and went in head first. He came up still holding the pencil.\n\nSo neither place is right, and two careful men cannot both have dreamed it. The autumn gales are starting. We run for Pennywell, north of the Head, to winter there and think."],["Sheet Eight: Winter at Pennywell","Sheet Eight is the harbour of Pennywell, three miles north of Gannet Head, where my boat, the Plover, lay under a tarpaulin from November to March. I am Isolde Marrin, surveyor to the Harbour Board. A surveyor's winter is spent indoors, turning pencil soundings into ink. I had a table by the window of the Anchor inn, and Benno Catt ground the ink.\n\nEvery evening I laid out the two reports of the Kettle Shoal. Maddern: the white house west-south-west. Hobb: the white house west-north-west. Each says about a mile, but no sailor can judge distance by eye, so I struck that out.\n\nWhat is left is two lines ruled from one house, spreading apart like the legs of a pair of compasses. Two lines from one point never meet again. Yet both men saw the same shoal. There is my difficulty, drawn plain."],["Sheet Nine: Two White Houses","Sheet Nine is the north side of Gannet Head. In the first week of April, Benno Catt and I took the Plover out of Pennywell and stood south along the cliff, and Benno said, “Which white house is ours, miss?”\n\nThere were two. Hollin Farm, far off on the southern slope, weathered almost grey. And much nearer, bright as a tooth, a low cottage that is on no chart at all. We landed and climbed up. It belongs to the Widow Arkle. She limewashed it twelve years ago, she told us, so that her son could find the cove. By my measure it stands a mile and a half north of Hollin Farm.\n\nTwelve years. Maddern made his report fifteen years ago, when Hollin was the only white house and still white. Hobb made his two years ago. I think I know which house Hobb saw."],["Sheet Ten: The Kettle Shoal","Sheet Ten is the smallest of the twelve and gave me the most pleasure. I am Isolde Marrin, surveyor, and this is how the Kettle Shoal was found.\n\nI ruled Skipper Maddern's bearing from Hollin Farm. But I ruled Skipper Hobb's from the Widow Arkle's cottage, a mile and a half away, the white house he truly saw. Two lines from two points. They cross at one place, two miles off Gannet Head: twice as far out as either man guessed.\n\nBenno Catt and I took the Plover there on the lowest tide of April. At the crossing the lead dropped fourteen fathoms, then eight, then stopped at nine feet with weed on it. All round us the water turned brown and began to break. The shoal is two hundred yards long, round, with one spur running out like a spout. Benno cheered. I took forty soundings first."],["Sheet Eleven: The Long Strand","Sheet Eleven carries the Grey Coast north from Pennywell along the Long Strand, fourteen miles of pale sand with dunes behind and nothing on it but terns. I am Isolde Marrin, surveyor to the Harbour Board, in the open boat Plover, with Benno Catt at the oars.\n\nThe Strand shelves so gently that a mile out we still had only two fathoms. I have written Shoal water, keep off along the whole length of it. The one mark is a wrecked hull, half buried, which the terns use as a town hall.\n\nWe sounded through May with the wind behind us. Benno now takes the angles himself and I check one in five. He has not been wrong since the Sister Stacks. Each evening we could see a little more of the cape ahead, and after dark the light on it, turning."],["Sheet Twelve: Cape Lastlight","Sheet Twelve is Cape Lastlight, where the Grey Coast ends in a spine of rock with a lighthouse on its last knuckle. Beyond it my commission stops. Benno Catt and I sounded round the cape in three calm days of June. The keeper waved from the gallery and lowered us a can of tea on a rope.\n\nIn July I laid all twelve sheets on the long table of the Harbour Board in Emberwick. The Kettle Shoal is on Sheet Ten, in one place only, with a note in red: Two white houses on Gannet Head. Be sure which you mean. Skippers Maddern and Hobb both came to look. Each was pleased to learn that he had been right.\n\nA fair copy goes to Emberwick Library. There is a small rock off the Long Strand that had no name. It is now Catt's Tooth. Benno pretends not to mind."]],[["A Hundred and Twelve Steps","Cape Lastlight, January. Dear Posy, you asked me to tell you everything, so here is the first of it. Your uncle is now assistant keeper of the Lastlight, at the very end of the Grey Coast. The lighthouse stands on a rock. At low tide you can walk to it from the cape along a causeway of flat stones; at high tide we are an island, and I wave at the mainland like a king.\n\nThere are a hundred and twelve steps from the door to the lamp. I have counted them every day for a week, in case one goes missing. At the top is the great lens, taller than I am. It turns and sends one long flash every fifteen seconds.\n\nThe senior keeper is Mr. Gant. He has been here nineteen years and has so far said good morning twice. Your loving uncle, Ambrose."],["Mister Buttons","Cape Lastlight, February. Dear Posy, thank you for your drawing of the lighthouse. You have given it a chimney and a cat, neither of which we possess, but I have pinned it up in the lamp room all the same, where the light goes through it every fifteen seconds.\n\nYou asked whether I am lonely. Not very. Mr. Gant, the senior keeper, plays draughts with me and wins. And I have made a friend. A grey seal hauls out on the flat rock below the kitchen window each afternoon and lies there like a sack of flour with whiskers. He has two round spots on his chest, so I call him Mister Buttons. I read him your letter. He yawned, but he yawns at everything.\n\nThe supply boat comes once a fortnight from Pennywell with oil, flour and the post. Write often. Your loving uncle, Ambrose."],["Polishing the Glass","Cape Lastlight, March. Dear Posy, you want to know what a lighthouse keeper does all day, since the light only shines at night. The answer is that he polishes.\n\nEach morning Mr. Gant and I climb the hundred and twelve steps and draw the curtains round the lens, so the sun cannot shine through it backwards and scorch the place. Then we clean every ring of glass, trim the wicks, fill the oil and wind the clockwork that turns the lens. It has a weight on a chain, like a grandfather clock as tall as a house. My winding arm is getting to be a different shape from the other.\n\nMr. Gant inspects the glass with his nose an inch off it. Yesterday he found one thumbprint. This morning he said, “That'll do.” I believe that is his highest praise. Your loving uncle, Ambrose."],["The Fog Bell","Cape Lastlight, April. Dear Posy, we have had three days of fog so thick I could not see the sea from the gallery of the lighthouse. In fog a light is no use to anyone. The ships cannot see us, so they must hear us instead.\n\nWe have a bell for that, as big as a washtub, hung on the seaward side. A hammer strikes it once every half minute, driven by its own clockwork. For three days and nights it went dong, and we counted thirty, and it went dong. Mr. Gant, the senior keeper, sleeps through it. I have not yet learned how.\n\nOn the third evening a fishing boat came creeping past the rock, close in, steering by the sound. A man in the bows lifted his hat to the bell. I lifted mine back, though he could not see me. Your loving uncle, Ambrose."],["A Tern Called Pin","Cape Lastlight, May. Dear Posy, the terns are back, and the lighthouse rock is loud with them. They are white, with black caps and sharp red beaks, and they scold me every time I hang out the washing.\n\nOn Tuesday morning I found one on the gallery under the lamp. Birds fly at the light sometimes, and this one had struck the rail and hurt its wing. It could not fly. It could peck, however, and did. I carried it down all hundred and twelve steps in my hat.\n\nIt lives in a tea chest in the kitchen now and eats slivers of fish. Mr. Gant, the senior keeper, said a lighthouse was no place for pets, and then spent an hour splinting the wing with a matchstick and thread, very gently. I have named the bird Pin, for the beak. Your loving uncle, Ambrose."],["Tea on a Rope","Cape Lastlight, June. Dear Posy, it hardly gets dark at all now. Mr. Gant and I light the lamp at ten and put it out before four.\n\nWe have had visitors. A small open boat with a brown sail spent three calm days sounding the water all round our rock, a lady in the bows swinging a lead line and a boy writing down the numbers. They are making a new chart of the whole Grey Coast. I waved from the gallery and lowered them a can of tea on a rope. So if you ever see the Lastlight on a chart, Posy, your uncle is the dot.\n\nPin the tern is mending. He walks about the kitchen as if he paid the rent. Mister Buttons the seal has a wife now, or possibly a cousin. Your loving uncle, Ambrose."],["Pin Flies","Cape Lastlight, July. Dear Posy, I have news you will find both sad and glad. Pin, the tern I have nursed in the lighthouse kitchen since May, has flown.\n\nMr. Gant took the splint off his wing a week ago. Since then we have carried the tea chest out to the foot of the tower and left the lid off. Pin would hop to the edge, stretch both wings, consider the matter and sit down again. I began to think he had decided to be a hen.\n\nYesterday the wind was light and the other terns were fishing off the causeway. Pin stood on the rim of the chest. Then he simply went, and I lost him among fifty others exactly like him. Mr. Gant said the kitchen would be cleaner. He has not yet moved the tea chest, I notice. Your loving uncle, Ambrose."],["Counting the Ships","Cape Lastlight, August. Dear Posy, you ask whether anything happens at a lighthouse in summer. Everything happens, slowly, a long way off.\n\nWe keep a book of every vessel that passes the cape, and this month I am the one who writes it. On Thursday I logged nine fishing boats, two coal brigs, a schooner, and a small yacht with a yellow sail. Each is a line in the book: the hour, the rig, the heading. Mr. Gant, the senior keeper, has nineteen years of such lines on a shelf. He says nobody has ever asked to read them, and that he would be alarmed if they did.\n\nAt low tide I walk the causeway to the cape and pick mussels. Mister Buttons the seal follows along in the water beside me, like a dog being taken out. I wish you could see him. Your loving uncle, Ambrose."],["A Question from Posy","Cape Lastlight, September. Dear Posy, your mother writes that you would like to come to the lighthouse at Midwinter, and asks whether it is allowed. I put the question to Mr. Gant, the senior keeper, over the draughts board. He thought for the length of three moves.\n\nThen he said that in nineteen years the Lastlight has had no visitor under the age of forty, that the stairs are steep, the wind is rude and the spare room is full of rope. I took that for a no. Next morning I found him clearing the rope out of the spare room.\n\nSo it is settled, if the sea is kind. The supply boat will bring you both from Pennywell on the twentieth of December. Bring your warmest coat and stout boots. The terns have gone south. I looked for Pin among them, but one cannot tell. Your loving uncle, Ambrose."],["The Night the Clockwork Stopped","Cape Lastlight, October. Dear Posy, we have had our first great storm, and I will tell you about it now that it is over and I am dry.\n\nIt blew from the north-east for two nights. Spray went clean over the gallery, and the whole tower hummed like a kettle. On the second night, at one in the morning, the clockwork that turns the lens stopped. A link of the chain had jammed. Without it the lamp burns but does not flash, and a ship cannot tell us from any other light on the coast.\n\nSo Mr. Gant and I turned the lens by hand. One turned while the other counted fifteen by the watch, turn and turn about, until daylight. He told me the whole history of the lighthouse to keep us awake. It was the most he has ever said. Your loving uncle, Ambrose."],["Getting Ready","Cape Lastlight, November. Dear Posy, three weeks until you come, and the lighthouse has never been so tidy.\n\nMr. Gant, the senior keeper, has whitewashed the spare room and put up a shelf at a height he judges right for a person of nine. He asked me whether children still liked barley sugar. I said I believed they did. A tin of it arrived on the next supply boat, addressed to nobody.\n\nI have polished the brass rail on all hundred and twelve steps, so mind you do not slip. Your drawings are still pinned round the lamp room, eleven of them now. Mister Buttons the seal is fat for the winter. I have told him to expect company. You shall have a job while you are here, too. I will not say what. It is at the top of the stairs. Your loving uncle, Ambrose."],["Posy Lights the Lamp","Cape Lastlight, the last night of December. Dear Posy, you have been gone three days and the lighthouse is too quiet. Mr. Gant set out the draughts board for three players before he remembered.\n\nI want it written down, so that you can keep it. On Midwinter Eve you climbed a hundred and twelve steps without stopping. You held the long taper, and at a quarter to four you lit the lamp yourself, and every ship on the Grey Coast steered by a light of your making. Mr. Gant entered it in the book: Lamp lit by Posy, visiting keeper. He has written no other visitor's name there in nineteen years.\n\nMister Buttons misses the herring you threw him. My year here is up tomorrow. I have signed on for another, and your mother says you may come back when the terns do. Your loving uncle, Ambrose."]],[["The Bell That Wasn't There","On the Tuesday before Founders' Day, Emberwick woke late. Nobody could say why until Mrs. Applin the baker looked up from her doorstep and saw daylight through the top of the market cross, where for two hundred years there had been a bell. Old Tabitha, three hundredweight of bronze, was gone.\n\nInspector Hubert Marrow arrived at eight with a notebook, a paper of peppermints and Constable Daisy Pim, who was new and keen. He had heard every joke there was about his surname and vegetables, and he waited patiently while the crowd made them again.\n\n\"Nine days to Founders' Day,\" said the Mayor. \"She must ring it in, Inspector. She always has.\" Marrow looked up at the empty beam. The bell rope lay at the foot of the cross, coiled as neatly as a sleeping cat. He wrote that down first."],["A Neatly Coiled Rope","Inspector Marrow climbed the ladder inside Emberwick's market cross himself, which Constable Pim thought brave for a man of his width. The market bell, Old Tabitha, had been stolen in the night, and he wished to see how.\n\nThe four bolts that had held her to the beam lay in a row on the ledge. They had been unscrewed, not sawn, and somebody had oiled the threads first. The little door at the foot of the cross had been opened with a key and locked again afterwards. Below, the bell rope was coiled and tied off with a tidy hitch.\n\n\"Thieves cut ropes,\" Marrow said, coming down rather faster than he went up. \"They break locks. They do not tidy.\" He offered Pim a peppermint. \"Whoever took her, Constable, was fond of her. Find out who holds a key to that door.\""],["The Baker's Ear","Mrs. Applin the baker rose at four every morning and missed very little. Inspector Marrow, looking into the theft of Emberwick's market bell, called on her while the loaves were still warm, and was given one for his trouble. She had heard no hammering on the night Old Tabitha vanished, and no horse. \"A squeak, perhaps, around midnight. I took it for a gate.\"\n\n\"But I'll tell you what I did hear,\" she said, thumping dough. \"All last week that bell rang wrong. Ever since the hard frost. She used to ring round, like a plum. Last week she rang flat, like a dropped pan. I said so to Mr. Fenn the crier, and he told me my ears wanted syringing.\"\n\nMarrow wrote down flat, and frost, and squeak, and underlined none of them yet. Then he asked what she charged for a second loaf."],["Mr. Lobb's Cart","When three hundredweight of bronze goes missing, a town looks first at its scrap dealer. Mr. Lobb of Tanner's Row knew this, and met Inspector Marrow at his gate with his arms already folded. \"I buy kettles,\" he said. \"I don't buy bells. Who would I sell Old Tabitha to? Everyone from here to the Grey Coast knows her voice.\"\n\nMarrow admired the logic and looked at the cart anyway. Its wheels ran true. In the mud beside Emberwick's market cross, Constable Pim had found a single narrow track that wandered from side to side, the mark of a wheel that wobbled. Mr. Lobb's did not. He had also spent the night at his sister's in Sallowby, where eleven people had watched him lose at dominoes.\n\n\"Then I apologise for the visit,\" said Marrow. Mr. Lobb unfolded his arms and offered tea."],["The Sallowby Theory","Half of Emberwick was certain that Sallowby had taken the market bell. Sallowby had envied Old Tabitha for two centuries, the argument ran, and envy is patient. So Inspector Marrow and Constable Pim walked the four miles over on a damp Thursday to ask.\n\nSallowby was offended, and then delighted to be offended. The churchwarden showed them the village's own bell, new last spring, and rang it for them twice. \"What would we want with yours? Ours is in tune.\"\n\nOn the way home Pim stopped at the crossroads. In the soft verge ran the same wandering wheel track they had found by the market cross. It did not go toward Sallowby at all. It turned north and ended at the first milestone, where a wagon and two horses had stood waiting. Up that road there was nothing for nine miles but sheep, weather and the foundry at Cinderhowe."],["Three Keys","Three keys opened the little door of Emberwick's market cross, and Inspector Marrow, hunting the thief of the market bell, went to see all three.\n\nThe Mayor's hung on a nail in the Guildhall, so rusted to the nail that it took pliers to lift. Miss Dobbs, who wound the town clock, had dropped hers in the river Ember in April and could name four witnesses. The third belonged to Mr. Osric Fenn, town crier and ringer of the bell for thirty-one years. It shone on his belt.\n\nMr. Fenn was grieved and loud. He demanded arrests. He offered five pounds of his own savings as a reward, and blew his nose on a red spotted handkerchief. Walking back, Constable Pim remarked that in all that noise he had never once asked where the bell might be. Marrow had noticed too."],["Turnips and Lozenges","Three boys from Ember Lane came to the police house on Friday with their caps off, pushed from behind by a mother. They had something to confess to Inspector Marrow about the night Emberwick's market bell was stolen.\n\nThey had meant to hang a turnip in Old Tabitha's place, for a joke. But when they crept into the square at midnight the bell was already down, and a handcart was going away up Guildhall Street with a hooded lantern. One wheel squeaked. The person pushing it stopped at the horse trough and wet the axle, and after that it went quietly.\n\n\"We found this where the cart stood,\" said the smallest, and put a sticky brown lozenge on the desk. Horehound, for sore throats. Marrow, a peppermint man, studied it with respect. He confiscated the turnip, which made a good soup."],["The Wobbling Wheel","Constable Daisy Pim found the cart on Saturday. It had taken her two days and most of Emberwick's back yards, and Inspector Marrow, who was investigating the theft of the market bell, said afterwards that it was the best piece of work in the case.\n\nIt stood in the yard behind the Guildhall, a flat handcart used for moving chairs on concert nights. The left wheel leaned on its axle and wobbled when she pushed it. The spokes were caked with red clay, and there is no red clay in Emberwick. It lies up on the Fells road. In the cart bed were two fresh grooves, the width of a bell's lip.\n\nThe shed where the cart lived was kept locked. The caretaker said only four people ever borrowed the key: the Mayor, the clerk, himself, and Mr. Fenn the crier."],["The Foundry at Cinderhowe","Inspector Marrow hired a pony and rode up the Fells road to Cinderhowe alone, following a hunch about Emberwick's missing market bell. The foundry sat in a fold of the hills, smoking gently, like a kettle someone had forgotten.\n\nMrs. Brask the founder was a broad woman in a leather apron. She would not show him the casting floor. \"I don't discuss customers' orders,\" she said. Customers, Marrow noted, not thieves. Through the door behind her he could see a pit, and in the pit a tall clay mould the shape of a bell.\n\nHe asked instead, as a matter of general interest, what happened to a bell rung in a hard frost. \"Cracks, like as not,\" said Mrs. Brask. \"Then she rings flat as a pan. Can't be mended. Has to be melted and cast again.\" He rode home slowly, thinking about a baker's ears."],["Frost in the Ringing Book","The ringing book of Emberwick's market cross lives in a drawer at the Guildhall, and the first rule is pasted inside its cover: the bell shall not be rung in hard frost, but a handbell used instead. Inspector Marrow, five days into the affair of the stolen bell, read it on Sunday afternoon with his boots off.\n\nThe morning of the great frost, a fortnight back, had its entry like every other day. Rang seven, as usual, O. Fenn. But \"as usual\" had been scratched through afterwards in a blacker ink, and the handbell in the drawer wore a year of dust.\n\nOn Monday he met the crier in the square. Mr. Fenn's voice was hoarse and his thumb was bandaged, and along both shirt cuffs ran a faint green stain, the green that old bronze leaves on anything that hugs it. \"Any news, Inspector?\" \"Some,\" said Marrow."],["Marrow Polishes His Boots","Inspector Marrow did his best thinking with a tin of blacking and a brush. On the Tuesday night, a week after the market bell vanished from Emberwick, Constable Pim found him at the police house table with both boots off.\n\n\"Consider our thief,\" he said, working at a toecap. \"He oils the bolts. He coils the rope. He wets a squeaking axle so as not to wake the baker. He drops a throat lozenge. He takes the bell not to a scrap yard but toward a foundry, a week after she began to ring flat.\" He held the boot to the light. \"That is not a thief, Pim. That is a man with a bad conscience and a very sore throat.\"\n\nPim said a name. Marrow nodded, and asked her to invite the Mayor, the baker and the crier to the market cross at dusk tomorrow."],["Old Tabitha Comes Home","At dusk on the eve of Founders' Day a wagon came down the Fells road into Emberwick with something large under a tarpaulin. Inspector Marrow was waiting at the market cross with the Mayor, the baker and Mr. Fenn the town crier, who had gone the colour of candle wax.\n\n\"I rang her in the frost,\" Mr. Fenn said. \"Thirty-one years, and I forgot the rule once. She cracked. I couldn't stand in this square and say so.\" He had spent his savings on having her cast again at Cinderhowe, meaning to hang her back unnoticed.\n\nMarrow charged him with borrowing the Guildhall handcart without leave. The Mayor fined him a shilling and the price of a new wheel. They hung the bell by lantern light, and in the morning she rang round as a plum. Mrs. Applin, at her oven, said that was more like it."]],[["Cocoa Gone Cold","The morning after the Harvest Supper, Mrs. Gantry carried a duster into the vicarage study at Thrushcombe and came out again without it. Mr. Ambrose Thackle, churchwarden and parish treasurer, was sitting in the vicar's armchair with a cup of cocoa gone cold at his elbow. He had been there all night, and he would not be getting up.\n\nMiss Clementine Aske, the vicar's sister, kept the house, the accounts and eleven hives of bees, and was not easily flustered. She shut the study door, sent the garden boy for Dr. Peabody and Sergeant Dunnock, and sat her brother down with tea before telling him.\n\n\"Poor Thackle,\" said the Reverend Barnaby Aske. \"Nobody liked him, you know. I always meant to.\" Clementine was thinking about the cup. Under the smell of chocolate there had been another smell, one she knew from the hedgerows and could not yet place."],["What the Doctor Found","Dr. Peabody was a cautious man who disliked saying anything before lunch. But by noon on the day Mr. Thackle was found in the vicarage study at Thrushcombe, he had said it twice. The churchwarden's cocoa had contained widow's thimble, a purple hedge flower that no cook could mistake for anything else.\n\n\"Then somebody put it there,\" said Sergeant Dunnock, and wrote murder in his notebook, slowly, as if the word might bite. Twenty-three people had sat down to the Harvest Supper. He began a list.\n\nMiss Clementine Aske, the vicar's sister, was already ahead of him. Every guest had drunk cocoa from the same pan and gone home well. Only the study cup was wrong. It had been poured at nine and left on a tray on the hall table, because Mr. Thackle liked to do the supper accounts alone. For ten minutes that tray had stood unwatched."],["A Hymn Sung Flat","Miss Clementine Aske asked everyone at Thrushcombe vicarage the same question: where were you during the last hymn? It was then, at nine on the night of the Harvest Supper, that a cup of cocoa had stood alone in the hall, and by morning it had done for Mr. Thackle.\n\nNearly everybody had been in the parlour, singing. They remembered it, because it had been dreadful. Mr. Vane the organist, who should have been at the piano, had mislaid his spectacles again and gone to hunt for them, so the hymn went up without him and came down in several places at once.\n\nThree people, then, were out of the room. Mr. Vane, peering under cushions. Mrs. Gantry, in the kitchen with the pans. And Farmer Rudge, who had put on his hat at ten to nine and left without a goodnight."],["Farmer Rudge's Temper","Sergeant Dunnock liked Farmer Rudge for the murder at Thrushcombe vicarage. Jed Rudge owed the dead churchwarden two quarters' rent. At the Harvest Supper he had said, before the whole table, that Mr. Thackle would squeeze a turnip and charge it for the juice. Then he had left early, at the very hour the cocoa stood unguarded in the hall.\n\nMiss Clementine Aske, the vicar's sister, walked up to the farm to hear his side. Rudge was in the byre, red in the face and gentle in the hands, with a calf two days old.\n\n\"She came on the night of the supper,\" he said. \"My boy ran down for me at ten to nine. Ask him. Ask the cow.\" The calf was called Harvest. Clementine scratched its ears, believed him, and went home to tell the sergeant, who was sorry to lose so tidy a suspect."],["A Hat on the Pond","Two days after Mr. Thackle was found in the study, Thrushcombe had a second shock. Mr. Leopold Vane, the organist, did not come to play for the evening service. The vicar gave out the psalm unaccompanied, and afterwards the garden boy found a grey felt hat floating on the vicarage pond, with L.V. inked inside the band.\n\nBy supper the village was saying murders, in the plural, and rather enjoying the extra letter. Sergeant Dunnock had the pond dragged. This did not take long, since it is two feet deep in the middle. It gave up a boot, a croquet ball and a large indignant carp.\n\n\"No organist,\" said Miss Clementine Aske, the vicar's sister, watching from the bank. \"Carried off, then,\" said the sergeant. \"Perhaps,\" said Clementine. She was looking at the hat. It had been found the right way up, and the crown was perfectly dry."],["The Second-Best Hat","Mrs. Tibbs let rooms above the saddler's in Thrushcombe, and for nine years the best of them had been Mr. Vane's. Now the organist was missing, his hat had been fished from the vicarage pond, and the village counted him a second victim. Miss Clementine Aske, the vicar's sister, asked to see the room.\n\n\"That hat they found was his second-best,\" said Mrs. Tibbs, opening the wardrobe. \"His best is gone. So are his good boots, his travelling bag and that ticking thing off the piano. His metronome. And he paid me to the end of the month on Saturday, in new coins, him that was always three weeks behind.\"\n\nShe sniffed. \"He'd turned peculiar besides. Wore his gloves indoors ever since the supper, even at breakfast. Said September had got into his fingers.\" Clementine thanked her. A man does not generally pack for his own murder."],["Where Widow's Thimble Grows","Miss Clementine Aske knew every plant in Thrushcombe, since her bees visited most of them. Widow's thimble, the purple flower that had found its way into Mr. Thackle's cocoa at the vicarage, is fussy. It will not grow in a garden. In the whole parish it grows in one place: along the north wall of the churchyard, beside the small door to the organ loft.\n\nShe went to look. A patch as wide as a tea tray had been cut, cleanly, with scissors. Whoever did it had stood on the organ-loft step to reach.\n\nThere is one more thing to know about widow's thimble. Its juice shows nothing at first. By the next morning it comes up violet on the skin, and no soap will shift it for a week. Clementine stood a while, considering who in the village had lately kept his hands covered."],["Mrs. Gantry's Candles","Sergeant Dunnock, having lost Farmer Rudge, turned his notebook toward Mrs. Gantry. She had made the cocoa on the night Mr. Thackle died at Thrushcombe vicarage. She had also quarrelled with him that very week, when the churchwarden docked fourpence from the housekeeping for what he called an extravagance of candles.\n\n\"Fourpence,\" said Mrs. Gantry, with her arms in the sink. \"If I poisoned every man who was mean about candles, Sergeant, there'd be no vestry meetings.\"\n\nMiss Clementine Aske, the vicar's sister, settled it more quietly. Twenty-two people drank from Mrs. Gantry's pan and took no harm, so the pan was innocent, and so was the kitchen. The mischief was done in the hall, to one cup, during the last hymn. And all through that hymn Mrs. Gantry had been heard through the wall, banging pans in a temper. Even the sergeant agreed nobody bangs pans from the hall."],["The Treasurer's Notebook","Mr. Ambrose Thackle had not been loved in Thrushcombe, but he had been exact. In his waistcoat, on the morning he was found in the vicarage study, was a notebook ruled in red, and Sergeant Dunnock let Miss Clementine Aske read it at the kitchen table.\n\nIt held pew rents, coal, the Harvest Supper down to the last halfpenny bun. The final entry was different. \"Organ Fund. Six years of jumble sales and no pipes. Shall count the box on Friday. Have told L.V. so, as is only fair.\"\n\nThe Reverend Barnaby Aske, called in from the garden, remembered something. At the supper Thackle had asked him for the key to the vestry chest, where the Organ Fund box was kept. \"He asked me across the table,\" said the vicar. \"Quite loudly. Mr. Vane was passing the pickles, and dropped the spoon.\""],["Buttons in the Box","The Organ Fund box at Thrushcombe had sat in the vestry chest for six years, growing heavier after every jumble sale. Mr. Thackle, the treasurer, had meant to count it, and had died in the vicarage study the night before he could. So Miss Clementine Aske, the vicar's sister, counted it for him, with Sergeant Dunnock holding the lamp.\n\nThe box was heavy. It rattled handsomely. It contained two hundred and six trouser buttons, iron washers and a flat grey stone. \"Somebody,\" said the sergeant, \"has been at this.\"\n\nOnly two people ever handled the box, Clementine told him: the treasurer who locked it away, and the organist who carried it round at concerts. And the organ still wheezed on its lowest note. She had written to the pipe makers in Emberwick, and their answer lay on the table. No order from Thrushcombe had ever been placed."],["The Carrier's Passenger","Old Mr. Hobb the carrier drives from Thrushcombe to the coast every Monday and talks to nobody but his horse. Miss Clementine Aske, the vicar's sister, brought him a jar of honey and asked whether he had carried anyone on the Monday after the organist disappeared.\n\nHe had. A gentleman had waved him down at the crossroads a mile beyond the village, before light, wrapped to the nose in a muffler. Gloves on. He had ridden all the way to Saltmouth harbour without a word. \"Only his bag talked,\" said Mr. Hobb. \"Went tick, tock, tick, every time we hit a rut. Like a clock that couldn't make up its mind.\"\n\nClementine thought of a metronome missing from a piano, and a hat with a dry crown. She walked straight to Sergeant Dunnock, who heard her out and sent for the fastest horse in the parish."],["Only the One Murder","They found Mr. Leopold Vane at the Ship Inn in Saltmouth, calling himself a traveller in ribbons. He might have passed, but there was a piano and he had not been able to leave it alone. Nobody in ribbons plays like that.\n\nMiss Clementine Aske set it out for Sergeant Dunnock. For six years the organist of Thrushcombe had emptied the Organ Fund and filled the box with buttons. Warned that Mr. Thackle meant to count it, he cut widow's thimble by the loft door, slipped out of the Harvest Supper during the last hymn, and saw to the cup on the hall table. Then he left a hat on the vicarage pond, to be mourned instead of hunted. His fingers, when the gloves came off, were violet.\n\nThrushcombe gave up its plural with regret. The fund began again, and the vicar's sister kept the box."]],[["The Board Said Seven-Forty","Every morning at six Miss Maud Tern chalks the tides on the board outside the Post and Tide Office at Cocklesand, on the Grey Coast. Low water, and the hours when the causeway to Gull Rock is safe. People walk out by that board. She has kept it nineteen years without a slip.\n\nOn a Wednesday in October the cocklers went down at dawn and found Mr. Jasper Crale on the sands below the causeway. He was a visitor, a salvage man from inland, who walked out to the old wreck by Gull Rock every evening. The tide had come round behind him in the dusk, and he had not got back.\n\nCaptain Rudd the harbourmaster read the board with his jaw set. Low water, 7.40. The true time had been 6.40. \"An hour out, Maud,\" he said. \"And a man is dead of it.\""],["A Seven With a Bar","Miss Maud Tern of the Tide Office at Cocklesand did not sleep the night after Mr. Crale was found on the sands. Her board had sent him out to Gull Rock an hour late, the harbourmaster said, and the sea had done the rest.\n\nAt first light she opened her daybook, where each morning she copies what she has chalked. Tuesday: low water 6.40, in her own square hand. Then she went outside with a lamp and looked at the board.\n\nThe 7 of 7.40 had a little bar through its stem. Miss Tern has never barred a seven in her life. Her mother thought it foreign and her schoolmistress thought it showy. Under the figure, when she tilted the lamp, the ghost of a rubbed-out 6 still showed. She had made no slip. Somebody had changed one figure, and left the rest to the tide."],["Two Kinds of Chalk","Constable Ned Lugg of Cocklesand is a slow, fair man who mends nets when he is thinking. Miss Maud Tern brought him to the tide board, the board that had sent Mr. Crale out to Gull Rock an hour too late, and made him put his nose to it.\n\n\"My chalk comes off the cliff,\" she said. \"I cut it myself. It's soft and greyish and it crumbles.\" She drew a line to show him. \"Now look at that seven.\" The false figure was a hard, bright white. Lugg knew it at once. It was the stick chalk kept in a saucer by the skittle board at the Anchor Inn, where half the village scored its Saturday nights.\n\n\"That narrows it to everybody,\" he said. \"Everybody who was in the Anchor,\" said Miss Tern, \"and passed my board before dusk on Tuesday. It's a start, Ned.\""],["Wet Tar at Four","Tom Skerry tarred the post of the tide board at Cocklesand at four o'clock on the Tuesday. By nightfall Mr. Crale had walked out to Gull Rock by a false figure on that board and been caught by the sea. Constable Lugg thought the two facts sat close together.\n\nTom had reason to dislike the man. His grandfather had been mate of the Marigold, wrecked off Gull Rock sixty years ago, and Crale had bought the salvage rights from under the village for eleven pounds. Tom had said things in the Anchor. He was also tarred to the elbows.\n\nMiss Maud Tern of the Tide Office looked at the post instead. At elbow height was a smear in the tar, where somebody had leaned in to reach the board. \"After four, then, while it was wet,\" she said. \"And Tom would never have smudged his own work.\""],["A Clerk With Dry Feet","Mr. Jasper Crale, lost to the tide at Gull Rock, had left one mourner in Cocklesand. Mr. Edwin Larch had been his clerk for fifteen years. He was a pale, tidy man who folded his napkin, and he sat in the parlour of the Anchor Inn like someone waiting for a train.\n\nMiss Maud Tern of the Tide Office, whose board had been tampered with on the evening Crale died, brought the inn's post herself to meet him. She asked whether he had ever walked out to the wreck with his employer.\n\n\"Never once,\" said Mr. Larch. \"I cannot abide wet feet. Mr. Crale went alone, every evening. On Tuesday I was in my room from tea until ten, writing his letters.\" Mrs. Pennant the landlady remarked that Mr. Crale had owed her three weeks' board, and she supposed the sea would not be paying it."],["No Letters in the Bag","In Cocklesand the postmistress and the tide keeper are the same woman, which is how Miss Maud Tern came to know something the constable did not. Mr. Larch, clerk to the drowned Mr. Crale, said he had spent Tuesday evening at the Anchor Inn writing letters. But every letter leaving the village passes through her hands, and since Tuesday the Anchor had posted nothing except the cook's order for seeds.\n\nShe asked Mrs. Pennant about that evening. A supper tray had gone up to Mr. Larch's door at half past five. At eight it was still on the mat.\n\n\"Perhaps he writes slowly,\" said Constable Lugg. \"Perhaps he wasn't there,\" said Miss Tern. Somebody had altered her tide board that afternoon, and a man had died at Gull Rock because of it. She wrote Larch on a slip and put it in the drawer with the stamps."],["The Captain's Sevens","Miss Maud Tern is a fair woman, so she suspected her own harbourmaster too. Captain Rudd had quarrelled with Mr. Crale over harbour dues. He initials the tide board at Cocklesand every week, and knew what an hour's error would do on the Gull Rock causeway. And when Crale was found on the sands, he had been very quick to say the mistake was hers.\n\nThe false figure on her board was a seven with a bar through it. So she asked to see the harbour dues book and read six months of the captain's sums. His sevens were as plain as fence posts.\n\nBesides, from five until nine on the Tuesday he had been chairing the lifeboat committee at the chapel hall, before eleven people and a plate of rock cakes. Miss Tern closed the book. She was relieved, and a little ashamed, and mentioned neither."],["What Crale Wrote Down","They had found a pocket diary on Mr. Jasper Crale, the salvage man caught by the tide at Gull Rock, and Constable Lugg of Cocklesand had dried it on the rack above his stove. On Friday he showed it to Miss Maud Tern, since she read handwriting for a living.\n\nMost of it was sums. Crale had spent a month poking about the bones of the Marigold, which lie in the sand by the rock and show only at low water. The last entry was made on the Monday, the day before he died. \"Under the third rib from the stern. A box, iron-bound. Cannot shift it alone. Tell nobody but L. Bring crowbar when the tide serves.\"\n\n\"L,\" said the constable. \"That's me, Lugg. Only he never told me a thing.\" \"No, Ned,\" said Miss Tern gently. \"I don't think he meant you.\""],["Red Weed and a Receipt","Pip, the boot boy at the Anchor Inn in Cocklesand, is eleven and notices everything below the knee. He told Miss Maud Tern that Mr. Larch's boots, put out on Thursday morning, had been wet through, with red ribbon weed stuck in the welts. That weed grows on Gull Rock and nowhere else on the coast. Yet Mr. Larch, clerk to the drowned Mr. Crale, swore he had never been out there.\n\nHis grey coat had gone to Mrs. Oake the laundress with a black smear on the left elbow that no soap would move. Tar, she said, and him a gentleman.\n\nThen Mrs. Pennant showed Miss Tern the receipt Mr. Larch had written when he settled the bill. 17s. 7d., in a clerk's neat hand. Each seven had a little bar through its stem. Miss Tern put on her hat and went to find the constable."],["Springs on Saturday","\"It's a smear, some seaweed and a way of writing sevens,\" said Constable Lugg. \"A magistrate would laugh at me, Maud.\" He was right, and Miss Tern of the Cocklesand Tide Office knew it. They believed Mr. Larch the clerk had altered her board to drown his employer at Gull Rock. They could not prove it.\n\nSo she took down her tables. The iron-bound box Mr. Crale had found lay under the wreck of the Marigold. The lowest tide of the month, the spring tide, fell on Saturday evening at a quarter past eight. Only then would the third rib stand clear long enough for one man with a crowbar.\n\nOn Saturday morning she chalked the board, true to the minute, where anyone staying at the Anchor might read it. Then she asked Tom Skerry to have his boat ready at seven."],["Waiting on Gull Rock","At seven on Saturday evening Tom Skerry rowed Miss Maud Tern and Constable Lugg round to the seaward side of Gull Rock, a mile off Cocklesand, and they sat down among the red weed to wait. It was cold. The constable had brought a net to mend and could not see to mend it.\n\nThe tide went out and out, further than it goes all month. The causeway lay bare and shining. Near the rock the old ribs of the Marigold came up through the sand one by one, black and crooked, like a comb with teeth missing. Somewhere among them Mr. Crale had found a box, and had not lived to lift it.\n\nA little after eight a lantern left the shore. It came steadily along the causeway, held low. Whoever carried it knew the hour exactly, and had something long and heavy over one shoulder."],["The Tide Keeps Time","It was Mr. Edwin Larch who knelt at the third rib of the Marigold with a crowbar, and Constable Lugg who stood up out of the weed on Gull Rock and wished him good evening. He did not run. There is nowhere to run on a causeway.\n\nHe told it on the walk back to Cocklesand. Fifteen years a clerk, and when Mr. Crale found the box he had promised him nothing from it. So Larch took chalk from the Anchor and turned a six into a seven. \"I only changed one figure,\" he said. \"It was the figure people walk out by,\" said Miss Maud Tern.\n\nThe box, opened at the Tide Office, held forty pewter buttons and a ship's spoon. Captain Rudd apologised, before witnesses. On Sunday at six Miss Tern chalked the tides, low water and safe hours, her sevens as plain as ever."]],[["The Problem Stated","The Locked Room Quarterly exists to examine impossible things calmly. We have forty-one subscribers and, this season, one problem worth all of them.\n\nAt Thistledown House, on the edge of Emberwick, Mr. Lucian Thrupp keeps sixty-one clocks in a room at the top of the stairs. Each Sunday before two he bolts himself in and dozes until four. Last Sunday, he tells us, the Winter Queen, a gold travelling clock no bigger than a plum, was wound and put to bed in her leather case on his table before he shot the bolt. At three he heard her chime, three silver notes going down. At four he opened the case and it was empty.\n\nThe door was bolted on the inside. The window has been painted shut for twenty years. The chimney would not admit a cat. Mr. Thrupp was alone. We invite theories, by post."],["The Secret Passage Theory","Nine subscribers have written to The Locked Room Quarterly proposing a sliding panel. We understand the appeal. The Clock Room at Thistledown House was bolted from within, its owner asleep in his chair, and a gold clock the size of a plum left it all the same.\n\nWe therefore spent a Tuesday with Mr. Tull the builder, tapping. The walls are stone, two feet thick, behind honest plaster. The ceiling is sound. Under the one loose floorboard we found a button, a dead moth and a penny older than Mr. Tull. No panel. No passage.\n\nWe did learn the room's habits. All sixty-one clocks are kept by Mr. Enoch Vesper of Emberwick, who has come at one every Sunday for nineteen years to wind them. He winds the little Winter Queen last, settles her in her case, and is gone before Mr. Thrupp bolts the door."],["The Thread and Bolt Theory","A retired locksmith in Sallowby writes that any bolt may be shot from outside with a bent pin and a length of thread, and that the vanishing of the Winter Queen from Mr. Thrupp's Clock Room is no puzzle at all. The Locked Room Quarterly took him at his word and tried it.\n\nWe tried it for a wet afternoon. The door at Thistledown House fits its frame like a cork in a bottle. No thread passes under it, and the keyhole is blocked on the inside by a brass plate. The bolt itself is as thick as a thumb, and so stiff that Mr. Thrupp uses both hands. We broke four pins and the editor's temper.\n\nA key, several readers suggest. But a key does nothing against a bolt. Mr. Thrupp shot it himself, at five to two, directly after seeing his clock winder to the stair."],["The Gardener at the Window","Several subscribers ask about the window. A gold clock has vanished from the bolted Clock Room at Thistledown House, and a window is a kind of door. The Locked Room Quarterly climbed a ladder to see.\n\nThe window is painted shut. The paint is twenty years old and unbroken all round. The ladder belongs to Dob, the gardener, who spent that Sunday from two until half past three upon it, clipping the wisteria, with his face a foot from the glass.\n\nDob is a truthful man with no imagination, which makes him the perfect witness. He saw Mr. Thrupp asleep in his chair. He saw the little leather case on the table, shut. Nobody came in. Nothing moved but the pendulums. At three he heard a faint chime through the glass. We wrote down exactly what he saw, the case and not the clock, because exactness is our trade."],["The Niece and the Jam","A subscriber who signs herself Suspicious of Cinderhowe asks who gains. The Winter Queen, the little gold clock that vanished from the bolted Clock Room at Thistledown House, is worth more than the roof, and Mr. Thrupp's niece has long wished to open a bookshop.\n\nMiss Sibyl Thrupp received The Locked Room Quarterly with sticky hands. On the Sunday in question she and Mrs. Marl the housekeeper made damson jam from half past one until five. Each swears to the other, and forty-three pots swear to both.\n\n\"I heard her chime at three, from the kitchen stair,\" Miss Sibyl told us. \"I'd know her anywhere. Three notes going down, like somebody small coming downstairs.\" So the Winter Queen was heard at three by her owner, his niece and the gardener. We print this in good faith. We also note that an ear is not an eye."],["The Owner Did It","A gentleman in the Lowlands suggests that Mr. Lucian Thrupp, alone in his bolted Clock Room at Thistledown House, simply pocketed his own gold clock and cried thief. The Locked Room Quarterly has considered this with the seriousness it deserves.\n\nThe Winter Queen was never insured. Mr. Thrupp gains nothing by losing her, and has offered twenty pounds for her return. He sits among his sixty remaining clocks like a man at a party where the one guest he wanted has not come.\n\nHe also let us search. We spent a day in that room on our knees. We looked in the coal scuttle, the cushions and the case of every clock, though to an editor all clockwork looks alike. We found no gold clock the size of a plum. She left the room. We are sure of that much, and of nothing else."],["The Chime Heard Again","The Locked Room Quarterly does not hold with ghosts. We record, however, that on the Monday after the Winter Queen vanished from the Clock Room at Thistledown House, Mrs. Marl the housekeeper was dusting there at three o'clock and heard her chime. Three silver notes, going down. Mrs. Marl left the duster where it fell.\n\nThe little gold clock was not in the room. We had searched it. Emberwick decided by teatime that the house was haunted by a timepiece.\n\nYour editor went up on Tuesday and sat in Mr. Thrupp's chair with the door open and a sandwich. At three, sixty clocks struck in their sixty voices, and under them, quite clearly, came three small notes descending. They did not come from the table. They came from the corner by the door, where the tall clock called Old Parliament stands. We finished the sandwich thoughtfully."],["Mr. Vesper's Sunday","The Locked Room Quarterly must examine the one outsider who entered the Clock Room at Thistledown House on the day the Winter Queen vanished. Mr. Enoch Vesper, clock mender of Emberwick, has wound Mr. Thrupp's sixty-one clocks every Sunday for nineteen years.\n\nHe arrived at one. He wound the tall clocks first and the little gold Winter Queen last, and closed her leather case on the table, with Mr. Thrupp looking on from the armchair. At five to two he was shown to the stair, and the bolt went home behind him.\n\nFrom half past two until four he played the cornet with the Town Band on the bandstand, before two hundred people, several of whom wished he would play more quietly. The Winter Queen chimed in the bolted room at three. If that is so, Mr. Vesper is the one man in Emberwick who cannot have taken her."],["The Maker's Mark","A subscriber asks The Locked Room Quarterly where the Winter Queen came from, before she vanished from Mr. Thrupp's bolted Clock Room at Thistledown House. We ought to have asked sooner.\n\nShe was made in Emberwick sixty years ago. Under her dial, Mr. Thrupp tells us, are the letters E.V. and a small engraved snowflake. Her maker was Elias Vesper, who sold her one hard winter to pay for coal and regretted it until he died. Mr. Thrupp bought her at auction, quite fairly, forty years on.\n\nElias Vesper's grandson is Enoch, who winds the clocks at Thistledown. Every New Year's Day for nineteen years he has asked, hat in hand, to buy his grandfather's clock back at any price he could save. Every year Mr. Thrupp has said no. He mentioned it to us as an amusing habit of the man's. We did not find it amusing."],["Inside Old Parliament","For this number The Locked Room Quarterly brought in an expert. Mrs. Hester Lune mends clocks at Cinderhowe, owes nothing to anybody in Emberwick, and agreed to open the tall clock called Old Parliament, which stands by the door of the Clock Room at Thistledown House. It was from that corner that a chime was heard after the gold Winter Queen had gone.\n\nShe worked for ten minutes with a small screwdriver and then said, \"Well, well.\"\n\nTucked behind the great brass wheels was a thing that had no business there: a steel comb with three teeth, a tiny hammer, and a pin set in the hour wheel to trip it at three o'clock exactly. Three notes, going down. The brass was bright. Mrs. Lune judged it less than a year old, and beautifully made. \"Somebody,\" she said, \"has taught this old fellow to do an impression.\""],["A Challenge to Subscribers","The Locked Room Quarterly now holds every fact in the matter of the Winter Queen, the gold clock that left a bolted room at Thistledown House while her owner slept. Before we print the answer we lay the facts out, and invite subscribers to send theirs first.\n\nOne. Nobody saw the clock itself after five to two. Mr. Thrupp, the gardener and our own search saw only a shut leather case. Two. The chime heard at three did not come from the Winter Queen. It came from a new device hidden in the tall clock. Three. So the chime proves nothing about where she was at three, and an alibi that rests upon it rests on air. Four. She was made by a Vesper.\n\nConsider who closed the case, who winds the tall clock, and who has waited nineteen years. Answers by the first of the month."],["The Solution, With Apologies","Seven subscribers of The Locked Room Quarterly had it right, and so, before our last number was dry, did the culprit. Mr. Enoch Vesper walked up to Thistledown House on Sunday at one and set his cornet case on Mr. Thrupp's table. Inside, wrapped in a duster, was the Winter Queen.\n\nHe had palmed her as he closed her leather case, and carried her out before the bolt was shot. The room was never robbed while locked. The chime at three, built by him into the tall clock over many patient Sundays, was to prove her still there while he played on the bandstand.\n\nMr. Thrupp looked at the little device for a long time. Then he asked what a man owed for nineteen refusals. The Winter Queen now lives in Vesper's shop window, and visits Thistledown every Sunday at one. We apologise to the ghost."]],[["Lamplighter's Song","The day goes down behind the mill,\nthe river keeps the last of it,\nand up the lane and down the hill\nI come to see the lamps are lit.\n\nOne for the baker's shuttered door,\none for the bridge, one for the square,\none for the house where, on the floor,\na child is counting every stair.\n\nI cannot send the dark away.\nI only hold it back a pace,\nso those still walking home can say\nwhich door is theirs, and know the place.\n\nGood night, you windows, one and all.\nMy ladder's on my shoulder now.\nThe small hours come, and they are small\nenough to carry, anyhow."],["Song for the Last Cup","The supper things are washed and dried\nand stood in rows along the shelf.\nOne cup is left. I let it bide\nand pour the last tea for myself.\n\nThe kettle ticks as it grows cold.\nThe fire mutters to the grate.\nWhatever today I was not told\nis news tomorrow. It can wait.\n\nOutside, the lamps come on in rows.\nInside, the clock has things to say,\nbut says them softly, for it knows\nI've heard enough of clocks today.\n\nSo here's to chairs, and sitting down,\nto slippers and a loosened lace,\nto being, in a busy town,\none person in one quiet place."],["Lullaby for a Wakeful Child","Hush now. The stairs have gone to bed.\nThe coats are sleeping on their hooks.\nThe bread is dreaming it is bread.\nThe stories doze inside the books.\n\nThe moon has put her slippers on\nto cross the roofs on quiet rounds.\nYour shoes, with all their running gone,\nlie on their sides like sleeping hounds.\n\nNo, I can't count the stars for you.\nThere's more of them than there is night.\nBut I can count from one to two\nuntil you let go of the light.\n\nOne. And two. And one. And two.\nThe blanket's warm. The window's deep.\nThe whole house holds its breath for you.\nGo on ahead. I'll watch you sleep."],["The House at Ten","The house lets down its shoulders now.\nThe rafters creak like a ship's bow.\n\nThe stair that every foot offends\nsays its one word and makes amends.\n\nThe tap lets fall a single drop,\nconsiders, and decides to stop.\n\nThe hearth gives back, in ticks and sighs,\nthe warmth it borrowed. The clock replies.\n\nA mouse behind the skirting board\ncounts over all it can afford.\n\nAnd we, who say the house is ours,\nare kept by it through the dark hours,\n\nlaid safe away, like folded sheets,\nin rooms above the emptied streets."],["The Cat Goes Out","At eleven the door opens a crack\nand out goes the cat, all whiskers and black,\nwith nothing to carry, no one to tell,\nand a whole town to read by its smell.\n\nAlong the wall top, under the lime,\nshe keeps her appointments and takes her time:\nthe fishmonger's step, the churchyard gate,\nthe barrel where something lived of late.\n\nThe moon is a saucer. The roofs are a stair.\nThe kennelled dog pretends she's not there.\nShe sits on the bridge with her tail in a ring\nand listens to nothing, or everything.\n\nThen home, at nobody's command,\nto wait on the sill with a look that says: and?\nThe night was hers. She won't say where.\nShe sleeps until noon in the warmest chair."],["Midnight Song","Both hands go up. The clock in the square\ncounts twelve out slowly on the air,\nlike someone setting twelve plates down\nfor guests who've long since left the town.\n\nNow it is neither night nor day.\nToday has put its coat away.\nTomorrow stands outside the door\nand has not been this way before.\n\nThe fox stops halfway down the lane.\nThe weathercock forgets the rain.\nFor one held breath, from hill to quay,\nnothing is owed and all is free.\n\nThen the hands part. The fox moves on.\nA minute past, the spell is gone,\nand Tuesday, hat in hand, comes in\nand looks for somewhere to begin."],["For One Who Cannot Sleep","You've turned the pillow. Turned it twice.\nYou've counted sheep, who would not stay.\nYou've given yourself good advice\nand listened to it walk away.\n\nSo get up. Put the kettle on.\nThe kitchen, at this hour, is kind.\nThe lists and letters all are gone\nto bed, and left the jam behind.\n\nSit by the stove. The dark outside\nis only the same fields, unlit.\nThe owl's at work. The tide's the tide.\nThe world goes on, and you are in it.\n\nAnd if sleep comes, then let it come\nthe way a cat comes: when it will.\nAnd if it doesn't, here's a crumb,\na cup, a chair. The night is still."],["The Watchman's Round","Two by the clock, and a clear cold sky.\nI walk my round, my lantern and I.\n\nI try the latch of the baker's yard.\nI nod to the pump, which is standing guard.\n\nThe schoolroom window's open again.\nI shut it on tomorrow's rain.\n\nA light upstairs at number nine:\nthe new baby. All is fine.\n\nA light at the doctor's. A light at the inn,\nwhere somebody's losing who thought he would win.\n\nI know this town by its sleeping sounds,\nits snores and its sighs, on my quiet rounds,\n\nand I call the hour, though none of them hear,\nso the dark may know that someone is here.\n\nTwo by the clock. All's well, all's well.\nAnd what is not well, I do not tell."],["The Smallest Hour","Three is the hour with nothing in it.\nThe moon's gone down behind the hill.\nThe clock takes longer for each minute.\nEven the river's lying still.\n\nThe watchman's gone in to his fire.\nThe baker has an hour yet.\nThe owl has all she could desire.\nThe mouse has what the mouse could get.\n\nNow is the time the old house thinks\nof all the feet upon its stair.\nNow the well, a long way down, drinks\na star, and keeps it, cold, down there.\n\nIf you're awake, you are the one\ncandle the whole dark is about.\nBe easy. It is nearly done.\nThe night is turning inside out."],["The Baker's Song","I'm up before the birds. I rise,\nand so, with luck, will what I make.\nI rub the flour from my eyes\nand poke the oven fire awake.\n\nFlour and water, salt and yeast,\nmy two hands and a wooden board.\nThe window's dark toward the east.\nThe cat comes in to be ignored.\n\nPush and fold, and fold and press.\nThe dough grows smooth beneath my thumb.\nIt's slow work, this, and I confess\nI sing to it to make it come.\n\nBy five the lane will smell of crust,\nand sleepers, turning, half aware,\nwill dream of breakfast, as they must,\nand wake to find it waiting there."],["First Bird","One note, let fall into the dark\nthe way you'd drop a stone to tell\nhow deep it is. No answering spark.\nA wait. And then one more, as well.\n\nWho sings like that before it's light,\nwith no proof that the sun will come?\nA wren, a scrap of brown, a mite\nof faith no bigger than my thumb.\n\nThen others. From the elm, the eaves,\nthe hedge, the thorn, the orchard wall,\nthe dark fills up with song like leaves\ntill there's no room for dark at all.\n\nAnd in the east, a thread of grey,\nas if the singing pulled it through.\nThe lamps look pale. The night gives way.\nThe first bird stops. Its work is through."],["Morning Song","The day comes up behind the mill,\nthe river has the first of it,\nand down the lane and up the hill\nI put out every lamp I lit.\n\nOne by the baker's open door,\nwhere warm bread waits upon the sill.\nOne by the house where, on the floor,\na child's two shoes are lying still.\n\nThe cat is home. The watch is done.\nThe baker's loaves are on the tray.\nThe small hours, counted one by one,\nadd up, as always, to a day.\n\nGood morning, windows, one and all.\nI kept the night. It's yours to keep\nthe day. My ladder's by the wall.\nI'm going home. I'm going to sleep."]],[["My Name","My name is Nell. My name is Sparrow.\nA sparrow is a small brown bird.\nIt is not grand. Its beak is narrow.\nIt only knows one little word.\n\nThe swallow's quick. The hawk is clever.\nThe cuckoo has a famous song.\nThe sparrow has no special feather.\nBut summer birds don't stay for long,\n\nand who is on the windowsill\nand in the yard and on the gate?\nThe sparrow, hopping, cheerful still,\nand never early, never late.\n\nSo I am glad my name is Sparrow.\nI'll have brown feathers and not mind,\nand build a nest in the wheelbarrow,\nand eat the crumbs you leave behind.\n\nNote: Written by Nell Sparrow at eight, in the back of a sum book."],["The School Window","Miss Pike had set us rivers to say,\nthe Ember, from the Fells to sea.\nI knew them all, until halfway\na sparrow came and looked at me.\n\nShe had a straw, and it was long.\nShe tried the gap beneath the eaves.\nShe turned it round. Still it went wrong.\nShe tried it crossways, through the leaves.\n\nShe dropped it, fetched it, and not once\ndid she sit down and say, I can't.\nMiss Pike said, \"Nell, you dreaming dunce,\"\nand stood me by the potted plant.\n\nI did not mind. From where I stood\nI saw her get it in at last.\nI learned the rivers, as I should,\nand one thing more, that no one asked.\n\nNote: From Nell Sparrow's school copybook, aged twelve."],["Leaving the Fells","The carrier's cart was at the gate\nat six. My box was corded tight.\nMy mother said, \"Now don't be late\nfor chapel. Write, and write, and write.\"\n\nMy father looked hard at the horse.\nThe dog ran circles round the wheel.\nI said I would come back, of course.\nI said it twice, to make it real.\n\nThe road went up. I turned to look.\nThe house was small as a dropped glove.\nI had ten shillings and a book\nto write in, and my mother's love\n\npacked in among the bread and shoes.\nA sparrow rode the tailboard down\npast the last gate. And so I chose\nto say I had a friend in town.\n\nNote: Nell Sparrow at seventeen, on the road down to Emberwick."],["Lodgings in Guildhall Street","One room. One window. One hard chair.\nA jug, a hook, a bed that creaks.\nI pay for these, and for the stair,\nwith all the hats I trim in weeks.\n\nBy day I sew on wax and wire,\ncherries no bird would ever take.\nBy night I have a candle's fire,\na pencil, and I stay awake.\n\nNobody knows me in this town.\nSome evenings that is hard to bear,\nand some it is a brand-new gown\nthat only I know how to wear.\n\nThe sparrows found my sill today.\nTown sparrows, sooty, bold as brass.\nThey looked at me as if to say:\nso you're a Sparrow. So. You'll pass.\n\nNote: Written at twenty, in a room above the hat shop."],["The Held Hand","We walked back from the fair by way\nof the river path, which takes too long.\nHe had not very much to say.\nHe whistled half of a slow song.\n\nHe is a joiner. Will's his name.\nHe told me how a joint is made.\nI said that hats were much the same.\nThey're not. He nodded, and we stayed\n\na minute on the bridge. Below,\nthe water carried off the light.\nAnd then, I don't know how, although\nI know which hands, his left, my right,\n\nwere holding. Nothing else at all.\nA sparrow in the alder scolded.\nI've trimmed a hundred hats this fall\nand not one thing so neatly folded.\n\nNote: Nell Sparrow at twenty-three. She married the joiner."],["Two Chairs","He made the table and two chairs.\nI made the curtains and the bread.\nWe have two rooms, a flight of stairs,\na kettle, and a lot ahead.\n\nHis shavings curl along the floor.\nMy pins turn up inside his boots.\nThere's sawdust in the sugar drawer,\nand neither of us much disputes\n\nwhose house it is. It's ours. It's small.\nThe sparrows had the eaves before\nwe came, and pay no rent at all,\nand we have let them stay. What's more,\n\nI think that they have let us stay.\nThey watch us through the windowpane\nat supper, in a landlord's way,\nthen tuck their heads in from the rain.\n\nNote: Written in the first winter of her marriage."],["Cradle Song","Small fist. Small yawn. Small furious face.\nYou came in on a night of rain\nand took up every inch of space,\nand none of it is ours again.\n\nYour father pegged your cradle, oak,\nwithout a nail, and rubbed it smooth.\nI stitched a quilt. Neither one spoke\nof being frightened. That's the truth.\n\nI used to count my lines at night,\nfive beats, or four, and cross them through.\nNow I count breaths, by candlelight,\nand every single one is you.\n\nSleep, little sparrow, small and brown.\nThe eaves are full of sleeping kin.\nThe rain is walking through the town.\nThe door is shut, and you are in.\n\nNote: For her daughter Wren, born that spring."],["Washing Day","Four shirts, two sheets, six pinafores.\nThe copper's lit. The yard is steam.\nA poem tried both kitchen doors\nthis morning, and it would not seem\n\nto hear me say: when they're asleep.\nIt is not that kind of a guest.\nSo here it is, upon a heap\nof washing list, and second-best.\n\nThree children and a husband's sleeves.\nSoap, blue-bag, starch, a line, some pegs.\nThe sparrows queue along the eaves\nand comment on the stockings' legs.\n\nI'm thirty-eight. My hands are red.\nMy verses used to scan and shine.\nNow they are shirts, and they are bread.\nI hang them, flapping, on the line.\n\nNote: Found on the back of a laundry list."],["The Empty Stair","The youngest left today. Her box\nwent bumping down the stairs at eight.\nThe house is very full of clocks.\nI had not noticed that, of late.\n\nI laid five places. Took back three.\nWill looked at them and looked away.\nHe said the gate wants oil. To me\nthat meant what he could not quite say.\n\nI told my mother I would write.\nI did, some weeks. I know now what\nshe did with every other night:\nshe watched the lane, and said she'd not.\n\nThe sparrows' second brood has flown.\nThe old birds sit along the wall,\na little ruffled, on their own,\nnot minding, or not much, at all.\n\nNote: Written the day her youngest left for the coast."],["His Bench","His plane lies where he set it down.\nThe shavings by the vice are dry.\nI sweep the rest of the house, and frown,\nand leave this room, and don't ask why.\n\nHe made the table, forty years\nago, both chairs, the cradle, and\nthe door that never sticks. One hears\na man in what he made by hand.\n\nThere's more of him in every joint\nthan in the likeness on the wall.\nHis chair still holds me. That's the point\nof making things well, after all.\n\nThe sparrows come at four for bread.\nHe fed them. Now they come to me.\nI tell them things he would have said.\nThey tip their heads. They let me be.\n\nNote: Written the winter after Will died."],["Old Sparrow","I'm smaller than I used to be.\nThe top shelf is a foreign land.\nThe stairs and I do not agree.\nMy knitting has got out of hand.\n\nThe children in the lane call me\nOld Sparrow, when they think I'm deaf.\nI'm not. I like it. Let it be.\nIt's what I was at eight, myself.\n\nI wear brown wool. I hop a bit.\nI keep an eye on every gate.\nI have a sunny place to sit,\nand am not early, am not late.\n\nWren's grandchild, who is nearly four,\nasked what a poem's for. I said:\nit's something left outside the door\nfor anyone who wants some bread.\n\nNote: Nell Sparrow at eighty, still in the joiner's house."],["Crumbs on the Sill","I've put the crumbs out on the sill.\nI've banked the fire and wound the clock.\nThe lane is white. The eaves are still.\nI have not turned the kitchen lock.\n\nI wrote my verses on the backs\nof sum books, bills and washing lists.\nA life gets in between the cracks\nof other things, and so persists.\n\nI had a name that suited me.\nI had two chairs, a hand to hold,\na cradle, and a window. See\nhow much that is, now that it's told.\n\nIf you have found this, then you came\nas sparrows do, for what was spread.\nTake what you like. I'd do the same.\nThat's all. It's time I went to bed.\n\nNote: Her last poem, written at eighty-four."]],[["First Frost","This morning the grass\nhad been written on.\nEvery blade signed\nin a white, careful hand.\n\nThe water butt wore a lid\nthin as a watch glass.\nI broke it with one finger\nand felt like a thief.\n\nThe last three apples\nhung on in the top of the tree,\neach with a cap of rime.\nThe robin had opinions.\n\nSo it begins.\nI went in and found my gloves,\none in each pocket\nof last winter's coat,\nstill holding the shape of my hands."],["The Elm Lets Go","All October the elm\ngave its leaves away\nlike a shopkeeper closing down:\nhandfuls, armfuls, everything must go.\n\nNow it stands in its bones.\nYou can see what it was hiding:\nthree old nests,\na kite from some other summer,\nthe whole sky.\n\nThe wind goes through\nand finds nothing to argue with.\n\nI used to think bare trees were sad.\nToday I think\nthey are only resting their arms,\nhaving carried green\nsince April without once putting it down."],["The Woodpile","Ash for a quick fire,\noak for a long one,\napple for the smell,\nand none of it green.\n\nWe stack it under the eaves\nbark side up, the way my father did,\neach log a small roof\nfor the log beneath.\n\nBy dusk there is a wall\nbetween us and January,\nchest-high and smelling of sap.\nThe wren has already moved in.\n\nA woodpile is a kind of sum.\nSo many cold nights,\nso many armfuls,\nand something left over for March."],["First Snow","It came in the night\nwithout knocking.\n\nBy morning it had finished\nthe garden wall,\nrounded the pump,\nand given the scarecrow\na clean white shirt.\n\nNo cart had passed.\nThe lane lay like a page\nnobody had the heart to begin.\n\nThen a cat began it,\nsmall neat commas\nfrom the hedge to the dairy door.\nThen the milk boy.\nThen, shrieking, every child in Emberwick\nwith a tea tray under one arm."],["The Shortest Day","Today the sun\ngot up late, did little,\nand left before four,\nlike a guest who has another party.\n\nWe lit the lamps at three.\nThe hens went to roost\nthinking a week had passed.\nShadows lay down full length\nacross the frozen field\nand did not get up.\n\nStill. This is the turn.\nFrom tonight the dark\ngives back a minute, then two,\nshy as someone returning\na thing they borrowed in June.\nI will count each one."],["The Midwinter Table","Every chair we own,\nand the piano stool,\nand a box with a cushion on it.\n\nThe goose has gone round twice.\nThere are nuts in a blue bowl,\na pudding dark as a wet road,\ncandles in the windows\nso the lane can see us.\n\nGrandfather is asleep\nwith a paper crown over one eye.\nThe children are under the table\nrunning a country of their own.\n\nOutside it is the deep middle of the cold.\nIn here somebody starts a song,\ngets the second verse wrong,\nand all of us follow, gladly, into the mistake."],["New Year on the Bridge","At midnight we walk down to the bridge\nbecause we always have.\n\nThe Ember goes under, black and quick,\ncarrying off the old year\nin pieces of broken moon.\n\nSomeone has brought a bell.\nSomeone has brought a flask.\nThe clock in the square strikes,\nand for twelve strokes nobody says anything.\n\nThen everyone says it at once,\nand shakes hands with gloves on,\nwhich hardly counts,\nso we take them off and do it again.\n\nThe first thing I do this year\nis get cold fingers\nin excellent company."],["Ice on the Millpond","Three days of iron frost\nand the miller walks out\nto the middle, jumps twice,\nand waves his hat.\n\nBy noon the pond is a fairground.\nBoys on barrel staves.\nThe doctor, astonishingly graceful,\nhands behind his back.\nThe schoolmistress going backwards\nfaster than is quite proper.\n\nI have one borrowed skate\nand no talent.\nI go round the edge\nholding the willows by the hand,\nand under my feet, through grey glass,\na slow gold carp looks up at all of us."],["Snowed In","The door opens inward, luckily.\nBehind it stands a second door,\nwhite, with the latch printed in it backwards.\n\nNo post. No bell. No road.\nThe world has shrunk\nto a kitchen, a fire, a cat,\na shelf of jars we filled in August\nwithout knowing it was for today.\n\nWe play draughts with buttons.\nWe read the almanac aloud.\nWe bake, because the oven is the warmest thing we know.\n\nAt dusk I scrape a hole in the window frost\nthe size of one eye.\nBlue snow. One star.\nThe pump wearing a tall white hat,\nand nothing, anywhere, expecting me."],["The Long Month","Nobody writes verses about this part.\nThe feast is eaten.\nThe snow has gone grey at the edges\nlike a tired tablecloth.\n\nMy boots have not been dry since Tuesday.\nThe woodpile is down to its knees.\nThe hens are sulking.\nThe sky comes down each morning\nas far as the chimney and stays.\n\nSo I mend.\nA sock, a strap, the hinge on the gate,\na quarrel with my neighbour over the ditch.\n\nIt is not much.\nBut winter is long in the middle,\nand you get across it\nthe way you cross a ploughed field:\nby the next furrow, and the next."],["Snowdrops Under the Hedge","I was not looking for them.\nI was looking for the other glove.\n\nUnder the hedge, where the drift\nhad pulled back an inch,\nthere they were:\nfive, six, a dozen,\nheads down, like children\nwho have come to the party early\nand are too shy to knock.\n\nGreen on white. White on white.\nEach one no taller than a thimble\nand entirely unafraid of February.\n\nI did not pick them.\nI went and fetched everyone in the house,\nand we stood in the cold\nand were, all of us, extremely pleased."],["The Thaw","It begins as a sound.\nOne drop from the eaves,\nthen another, counting.\n\nBy noon every gutter in Emberwick\nis telling the same long story.\nThe lane runs brown.\nThe pump takes off its hat.\nThe scarecrow gets his old coat back.\n\nOn the Ember the ice goes out\nwith a groan like a great door,\nand the river, which was only waiting,\nshoulders through.\n\nI hang my gloves on the nail by the door,\none over the other,\nstill holding the shape of my hands.\nSomewhere a blackbird\ntries the first two notes of spring, and keeps them."]],[["Ode to the Door Key","O small brass tooth,\nwarm from my pocket,\nwho has travelled all day\namong pennies and string\nand never once complained.\n\nYou know one thing,\nand you know it perfectly.\nOf all the doors in Emberwick\nthere is a single lock\nthat turns for you\nlike a head at its own name.\n\nI have lost you in every coat I own.\nI have found you each time\nin the last place, naturally.\n\nNow do your one trick.\nThere. The click,\nthe give, the smell of home\ncoming out to meet me in the hall."],["Ode to the Doormat","You get the worst of us.\nThe marsh, the lane, the farmyard,\nwhatever the dog has found\nand thought worth bringing home.\n\nNobody thanks a doormat.\nWe arrive, we scuff, we go in\nto be greeted by better furniture.\n\nYet you are the house's first word,\nand the word is: stop.\nLeave the weather outside.\nLeave the long road.\nLeave whatever happened at the market.\n\nBristly, patient, flat as Tuesday,\nyou lie across the threshold\nlike an old dog who has decided\nthat this, exactly this, is his work.\nI wipe my feet twice today, out of respect."],["Ode to the Kettle","Round-bellied, black-bottomed,\ndented where somebody dropped you\nin the year of the flood,\nyou sit on the hob like a small judge.\n\nOther pots are for occasions.\nYou are for everything:\nbad news, good news, wet feet,\nvisitors, no visitors,\nfour in the afternoon.\n\nI love how you begin,\na tick, a mutter,\na rumour of weather inside you,\nthen the long rising note\nthat means: sit down,\nit will all look different in a minute.\n\nAnd it does.\nYou have never once been wrong."],["Ode to the Wooden Spoon","You were pale once, and straight.\nNow you are the colour of gravy\nand lean a little to the left,\nworn to the shape\nof forty years of stirring.\n\nYou have been in every soup,\nevery porridge, every jam,\nthe custard that curdled,\nthe famous Harvest Supper gravy.\nYou have been a drumstick,\na pointer, a sceptre,\nand once, briefly, a boat.\n\nThere are silver spoons in the drawer.\nThey come out at weddings\nand lie about looking handsome.\n\nBut when the pot is hot\nand the moment matters,\nthe hand goes to you\nwithout asking the head."],["Ode to the Kitchen Table","Four legs, one of them short,\nand a folded paper under it\nthat has outlasted two mayors.\n\nOn your back we have rolled pastry,\ndone sums, shelled peas,\nsigned papers, cut out a wedding dress,\nand bathed a baby in a tin basin.\n\nYou are scarred like an old sailor.\nHere, a ring from a hot pan.\nHere, the initials of a boy\nwho is now a grandfather\nand still denies it.\n\nEvery table is an altar of sorts.\nOurs asks for nothing\nbut elbows, crumbs and news,\nand that whoever comes in cold\nshould sit down first and explain afterwards."],["Ode to the Stairs","Thirteen of you,\nand I could climb you asleep,\nand have.\n\nThe third from the bottom complains.\nIt has complained since before I was born.\nEvery child of this house\nlearned to step over it,\ncoming in late,\nand every parent, lying awake,\nlearned to listen for the step that was missed.\n\nYou are worn in the middle\nlike old doorsteps.\nWe have carried up you\ncandles, coal, trays, cradles,\none piano, never again,\nand each other.\n\nGoing up is the day ending.\nComing down is the day begun.\nIn between, good stairs, you hold your breath."],["Ode to the Landing Window","You are the house's eye,\nand you do not blink.\n\nThrough you I have checked the weather\nten thousand mornings\nand been wrong about it most.\n\nYou frame the same things daily:\nthe pear tree, the wall,\na strip of the Ember, the far Fells.\nYet you have never shown me\nthe same picture twice.\n\nRain writes on you.\nFrost draws ferns.\nFlies consult you at length\nabout the way out.\n\nAnd at night, when the lamp is lit,\nyou turn politely into a mirror,\nand show the house to itself:\na stair, a light, a face going up to bed."],["Ode to the Armchair","You have my shape by now.\nAnyone else who sits in you\nfeels like a guest in someone's coat.\n\nYour arms are worn to the weave.\nOne spring has ideas of its own.\nDown your sides, I am certain,\nlive a thimble, three pencils,\na sixpence, and the spectacles\nI accused the cat of taking.\n\nIn you I have read whole winters.\nIn you I have slept through the endings\nof a hundred books.\n\nThrones are for sitting up straight.\nYou are for the other thing:\nthe long outward breath,\nthe boots off,\nthe book open face down on one knee\nwhile the fire does the thinking."],["Ode to the Lamp","All day you are nothing much.\nA glass chimney, a brass belly,\na wick like a tired shoelace.\n\nThen four o'clock comes down the lane\nand I strike a match,\nand you open your one gold eye.\n\nAt once the room has a middle.\nThe corners step back.\nThe table gathers its cloth about it.\nFaces lean in, and are kinder\nthan they looked by day.\n\nYou smoke when I neglect you.\nYou sulk in a draught.\nYou want trimming, filling, polishing,\nlike any small household god.\n\nBut a page read by your light\nstays read.\nAnd a window with you in it\nis how a traveller knows a house from a hill."],["Ode to a Bar of Soap","Yellow, square-shouldered,\nsmelling of nothing but clean,\nyou came into this house\nthe size of a brick.\n\nLook at you now.\nA sliver, a wafer, a new moon\nin a saucer by the basin.\n\nYou are the only thing I know\nthat does its work\nby disappearing.\nEvery pair of hands takes a little.\nThe gardener's, the baker's,\nthe child's who only pretended.\n\nYou have washed off the whole day,\nink, earth, onions, the argument,\nand asked no questions.\n\nTomorrow I shall press what is left of you\nonto the back of the next bar,\nand you will go on, as the good do, unnoticed."],["Ode to the Blanket","Grey wool, blue stripe,\na darn in one corner\nshaped like the island of somewhere.\n\nYou were my grandmother's.\nYou have been a tent,\na cloak, a sail, a stage curtain,\nand a river for tin boats to cross.\nYou have been wrapped round fevers\nand carried to picnics\nand hung out every April\nto be beaten like a carpet and forgiven.\n\nTonight you are only a blanket,\nwhich is the best thing you do.\n\nWeight without heaviness.\nWarmth that asks nothing back.\nI pull you up to my chin,\nand the day, which was large,\nfolds itself to the size of a bed."],["Ode to the Last Candle","Everything else is done.\nThe key is on its nail.\nThe kettle is cold, the spoon is dry,\nthe table is cleared of all but tomorrow.\nThe stairs have said their one word.\n\nNow there is you,\na thumb of wax in a tin saucer,\nholding up a flame\nno bigger than a seed.\n\nBy you I can see the whole of my life:\none room,\none window with the dark in it,\none blanket turned down.\n\nThank you, small light.\nThank you, ordinary things,\nthat stood where I left you all day long.\n\nI lean close. One breath.\nThe house keeps the rest."]],[["The Hay Meadows at Fellfoot","I am Tamsin Arkwright, schoolmistress of Emberwick, and this summer I mean to walk from the foot of the Northern Fells to the top of Cold Cap, writing down every plant I can name. My companion is a donkey called Pudding, who carries the press, the blotting paper and the kettle, and has opinions about all three.\n\nWe begin in the hay meadows at Fellfoot, early in June, before the scythes. Wood cranesbill stands knee-high and violet-blue among the buttercups. Yellow rattle is everywhere; it steals from the roots of the grasses and keeps them short, which is why the flowers have room. Shake a ripe pod and it rattles like a tiny purse.\n\nMy grandfather walked this way forty years ago. His notebook ends with one line: 'Least willow, Cold Cap summit. Smallest tree in the world.' I should like to see it."],["The Beck Side","Second week of June. Pudding the donkey and I have left the Fellfoot meadows and are following Rushy Beck upstream, which is the lazy way to climb the Northern Fells: the water has already found the easiest road.\n\nThe banks are a garden nobody planted. Marsh marigold is nearly over, its gold cups gone to green stars of seed. Water avens hangs its dusky pink heads as if shy. Where the ground squelches there is lady's smock, pale lilac, and brooklime with small blue flowers sitting right in the stream.\n\nI tasted the brooklime, since the old herbals call it a salad. It is bitter enough to make a schoolmistress pull a face. Pudding ate the rest and seemed to think me fussy. I, Tamsin Arkwright, pressed four sheets today. The blotting paper is already running short, and we are barely above the last farm."],["The Gill Wood","Rushy Beck climbs into a steep wooded ravine, which the people of the Northern Fells call a gill. I, Tamsin Arkwright, went in on foot. Pudding the donkey declined and waited at the gate.\n\nIt is cool and green inside, all oak and birch and hazel, with the beck talking loudly below. The bluebells are finished, but wood sorrel still covers the banks, each leaf three folded hearts. The leaves close in rain and at night, like small umbrellas being put away. Higher up, bilberry makes a low thicket hung with pink lantern flowers, and sweet woodruff grows in whorls like tiny green wheels.\n\nI found wild garlic by smell before sight. The whole upper wood reeks of it, pleasantly, as a kitchen does. I counted nineteen flowering plants without leaving the path, and came out with wet knees and a new respect for shade."],["Walls and Intake Fields","Above the gill wood the Northern Fells are parcelled into intake fields, rough pastures taken in from the moor long ago and fenced with drystone walls. No mortar holds them, only patience. Pudding the donkey and I followed one wall for two miles at the end of June.\n\nA wall is a cliff that someone has kindly built at eye level. Stonecrop sits on the capstones with yellow stars. Wall pennywort pokes round green coins from the cracks, and herb robert, red-stemmed, grows wherever a spoonful of soil has blown in. On the shady side, foxgloves stand taller than the donkey.\n\nI watched a bumblebee work a foxglove from the bottom bell upward. The lowest flowers open first, so she always climbs. A shepherd named Ned Tolliver, mending a gap, told me the walls are older than the church. The plants seem to agree."],["The Heather Moor","We are through the last gate. Hagg Moor lies open on every side, and for the first time since Fellfoot there is no wall, no tree and no shade. It is July. I, Tamsin Arkwright, have tied my hat on with string.\n\nThree heathers grow here, and it took me a morning to tell them apart. Ling is the common one, with tiny scale leaves and pale flowers still in bud. Bell heather has fat crimson bells and likes dry banks. Cross-leaved heath is rose pink, with leaves in fours like a cross, and keeps to the wet hollows. Learn which is underfoot and you know whether your boots will stay dry.\n\nBilberries are ripening among them. Pudding the donkey has a purple muzzle and no remorse. Mine is no better, and there is nobody up here to mind."],["Mirk Moss","Beyond Hagg Moor the ground gives up being ground. This is Mirk Moss, a blanket bog a mile across, and I crossed it in mid-July by stepping exactly where Ned Tolliver the shepherd told me to step. Pudding the donkey went round by the cart track, sensibly.\n\nBog moss holds the whole place together, a wet sponge of red and green many feet deep. Cotton grass nods white tufts over it, and is a sedge, neither cotton nor grass. Bog asphodel stands in yellow spikes. On the bare peat I found sundew, no bigger than a penny, its red leaves fringed with sticky drops.\n\nI watched a midge land on one. It did not leave. The leaf folds slowly over its catch and takes a meal the poor soil cannot give it. I felt some sympathy for the plant. The midges have been dining on me all week."],["The Limestone Pavement","High on the Northern Fells, above Mirk Moss, the peat stops and a floor of pale grey rock begins. Whitescar is a limestone pavement: flat blocks, called clints, split by deep cracks, called grikes. From a distance it looks as bare as a scrubbed doorstep.\n\nLook down a grike and it is another matter. Each crack is a narrow, sheltered garden, safe from wind and sheep. Hart's-tongue fern hangs in green straps. Wild thyme spills over the edges. In one grike, an arm's length down, I found bloody cranesbill in full magenta flower, and lily of the valley leaves beside it: woodland plants living in a cellar.\n\nPudding the donkey will not set a hoof on the clints. I went alone, on hands and knees, peering into cracks like a woman who has dropped a ring. It is late July, and I, Tamsin Arkwright, have filled the second press."],["Lantern Tarn","A tarn is a small mountain lake, and Lantern Tarn lies in a hollow under Raven Crag, cold and clear as window glass. Pudding the donkey and I camped beside it for three days at the start of August, the longest we have stopped since Fellfoot.\n\nBogbean grows in the shallows, its three-part leaves held above the water like broad beans that have lost their way. The fringed pink-and-white flowers are over, though I found one late spike. Farther out, water lobelia sends thin stalks up through the surface, each with a few pale lilac flowers nodding over its own reflection. The leaves stay on the bottom in neat rosettes.\n\nTo see those I took off my boots and waded. The cold made my ankles ache within a minute. I sketched standing in the water anyway, and afterwards said some words that I do not teach."],["Scree and Crag","Raven Crag stands over Lantern Tarn, and below it lies a long fan of broken stone. This is scree. It slides when trodden on, so I, Tamsin Arkwright, crossed it slowly in August with a stick, having left the donkey at the tarn.\n\nParsley fern is the plant of the scree: bright green tufts that look just like the kitchen herb and grow where nothing else will hold. On the crag itself, out of reach of sheep, the ledges are crowded. Roseroot hangs in thick grey-green bunches; break the root and it smells of roses. Alpine lady's mantle has leaves edged and backed with silver silk. Mountain sorrel has kidney-shaped leaves, sharp and pleasant to chew.\n\nEverything here is small, tough and close to the rock. I am beginning to resemble the flora. Nine weeks out, and my grandfather's summit is the next thing above me."],["The Wet Ledges","On the north face of Cold Cap, a morning's climb above Raven Crag, water seeps from the rock all summer. It was mid-August when I reached these ledges, and I had to tie Pudding the donkey to a boulder and go on without her.\n\nThe saxifrages live here. Their name means stone-breaker, though they only use cracks the frost has made. Starry saxifrage has white petals, each with two yellow dots, on stalks as thin as thread. Yellow mountain saxifrage makes mats along every trickle. Beside them grows moss campion, a tight green cushion that is no moss at all, studded with pink flowers. A cushion as wide as a dinner plate may be older than I am.\n\nI lay on my front to draw it, with cold water running into one sleeve. A raven watched from above and made a remark. I chose to take it as encouragement."],["The Summit of Cold Cap","The top of Cold Cap is a stony plateau where the wind never quite stops. I, Tamsin Arkwright, came up on the twentieth of August with my grandfather's notebook in my pocket. Pudding the donkey, to my surprise, came too.\n\nAt first I saw only grey woolly moss and stiff sedge. Then I knelt. Flat against the ground, between the stones, lay round shining leaves no bigger than my little fingernail, with a few fluffy seed-heads among them. Least willow. Its trunk and branches creep under the moss, and only the twig tips show. The whole tree would fit under a teacup.\n\nSo he was right, forty years ago, and it is still here. I did not pick any. I drew it life size, which took very little paper, and wrote the date beneath his. Then I sat beside the smallest tree in the world and ate an apple."],["Down Before the Frost","We came down from Cold Cap in the first week of September, Pudding the donkey and I, by the road we went up: ledges, crag, tarn, pavement, bog, moor, walls, gill, beck. Everything had moved on without us. The ling on Hagg Moor was in full purple flower and humming with bees. The foxgloves were brown rattling poles. At Fellfoot the meadows were shorn and green again.\n\nThere was frost on the grass the morning we reached the village. I counted the pressed sheets on the inn table: two hundred and six plants, from wood cranesbill to least willow.\n\nThey go to Emberwick Library with this book. School opens on Monday, and Miss Arkwright must be back at her desk. Pudding has been promised a winter of doing nothing. I have added a line to my grandfather's notebook, under his: 'Still there. Go and see.'"]],[["The Churchyard Wall","My name is Lettice Brambling, of Tanner's Row, Emberwick. On New Year's Day I resolved to find and name one hundred kinds of moss within a day's journey of my own door, and to finish by the year's end. My tools are a hand lens that was my late husband's, a penknife, and a great many paper packets.\n\nJanuary is a good month to begin. Mosses have no dislike of cold, and with the flowers gone there is nothing else to look at. I started at the churchyard wall. Wall screw-moss grows on the mortar in neat green cushions, each leaf ending in a white hair. Its capsules stand up on stalks like tiny candle-snuffers.\n\nUnder the lens a cushion becomes a forest. I stayed until my feet were numb and the verger asked if I had lost something. Nine kinds by teatime."],["Roofs and Cushions","February, and I have been looking up. Every old roof in Emberwick carries moss, and from the lane behind the bakery I can study a whole slope of tiles without a ladder. I am Lettice Brambling, and my neighbours have stopped asking.\n\nThe round grey pincushions are grey-cushioned grimmia. Long white leaf-hairs give them a frosted look. When a cushion dries it turns hoary and tight; a shower later it is green and soft again. The capsules curve back down into the cushion on bent stalks, as if tucking themselves in.\n\nA moss capsule is a seed-box without seeds. It holds spores, fine as flour. Around its mouth is a ring of teeth that open in dry air and close in damp, so the spores go out only on a good day for flying. I made them move by breathing on one. Seventeen kinds now, toward my hundred."],["The Woodland Floor","In March I, Lettice Brambling, took my lens to Coppice Bank, the oak wood above Emberwick mill, before the leaves came and shut out the light.\n\nThe woodland floor is where mosses grow large. Common haircap stands a hand high in dark green stars, each stem like a small fir tree; lie down beside a patch and you are in a pine forest fit for beetles. Swan's-neck thyme-moss carries its capsules on curved red stalks, exactly as named. Over the logs and tree-foots run the feather mosses, branched like ferns and soft as fur.\n\nA moss has no true roots. It holds on by fine threads and drinks through its leaves, straight from rain and mist, which is why a wet wood suits it. I came home with thirty-one kinds in the book and a great deal of the wood on my coat."],["Stones in the Ember","April brought rain, and the river Ember ran high and brown under the mill bridge at Emberwick. When it dropped I went down the bank with my skirts pinned up, a walking stick, and my hand lens on a string.\n\nSome mosses live under water altogether. Greater water-moss grows on the stones in dark tresses a foot long, streaming in the current like hair. Its leaves are folded into keels, in three rows along each stem. On the splashed rocks just above the waterline sit tight dark cushions that are drowned in every flood and seem none the worse.\n\nI wanted a piece from midstream. A boy called Wilf Tarrow, fishing for minnows, waded out and fetched it for a penny, then stayed to look through the lens. He says the cushions are forests and the water-moss is a mermaid. Forty kinds. I, Lettice Brambling, have an assistant."],["The Old Orchard Trunks","May in Emberwick, and the orchard behind Tanner's Row is all blossom. Everyone else looks up at it. Wilf Tarrow and I, Lettice Brambling, look at the trunks.\n\nBark is a country of its own. On the old apple trees, cypress-leaved plait-moss lies in flat green mats, each shoot neatly braided. Wood bristle-moss makes small dark tufts, with capsules half hidden among the leaves and wearing hairy pointed caps. The caps fall off when the spores are ready, like hats at a wedding.\n\nMosses do the tree no harm. They take nothing from it but a place to sit. Wilf, who is nine, asked whether moss always grows on the north side. We walked round six trees with a compass. It grows wherever it is damp, which is often north and just as often not. Fifty-two kinds, and we are past halfway."],["Bog Moss on the Marsh","On Midsummer Eve I, Lettice Brambling, went out on the marsh below Emberwick in borrowed boots, with Wilf Tarrow carrying the basket. We wanted bog moss, and the marsh has more of it than anything else.\n\nBog moss grows in soft hummocks, pale green, ochre and wine red, each colour a different kind. Every stem ends in a shaggy head like a small mop. Most of the plant is empty cells, made for holding water. Wilf squeezed a handful and filled a teacup. At home we weighed a dry tuft, soaked it and weighed it again: twenty times heavier.\n\nIt builds its own ground, growing at the top while the bottom turns slowly to peat. The old people here used it dried to line cradles. Seven kinds of bog moss, and seven more from the ditches, bring the book to sixty-six. My stockings may not recover."],["The Dry Month","Hardly a drop of rain fell on Emberwick in July. The mosses on the churchyard wall shrivelled to grey-brown crusts. I, Lettice Brambling, found two new kinds in the first week and none after, and the count stuck at sixty-eight.\n\nSo I tried an experiment instead. I took a cushion of wall screw-moss, dry as a biscuit, set it on a saucer on the kitchen table, and let one drop of water fall on it. Under the lens the leaves untwisted and spread while I watched. In two minutes the cushion was green. Wilf Tarrow timed it with my husband's watch.\n\nA moss does not die of drought. It stops, like a clock nobody has wound, and starts again when it is wet. I have a packet in my drawer collected in January. We wetted that too, and it opened just the same. I find this cheering, at my age."],["Mr. Dobbin's Lawn","My neighbour in Tanner's Row, Emberwick, Mr. Dobbin, wages war on the moss in his lawn. Every August evening I hear the rake. I, Lettice Brambling, went round to ask for the rakings, and he gave me a look I shall treasure.\n\nHis enemy is springy turf-moss. Each shoot has leaves bent sharply back, so from above it looks like a row of tiny green stars. It likes shade, damp and short grass, and Mr. Dobbin supplies all three by mowing close under his own apple tree. I told him so. He was not grateful.\n\nRaking only helps it. Every broken scrap can grow into a new plant, so he sows it as he goes. In his barrowful Wilf Tarrow and I found five kinds, two of them new to the book. Seventy. Mr. Dobbin has asked, stiffly, to borrow the lens."],["The Rotten Log","September, and Coppice Bank above Emberwick smells of mushrooms. A fallen oak lies near the top path, so soft now that a finger goes into it. I, Lettice Brambling, spent two afternoons on that single log.\n\nThe prize was four-toothed moss, which has a trick. At the tips of some shoots sit small green cups, each holding a few loose grains like eggs in a nest. A raindrop landing in the cup splashes them out, and every grain can grow a new plant. Wilf Tarrow did the raindrop's work with a wet straw and flicked them a full hand-span.\n\nBeside it grow the liverworts, flat and leafy, the mosses' quiet cousins. I am putting them in an appendix; a compendium ought to be generous. One log gave eleven new mosses. Eighty-one in all, with three months left. I begin to think it can be done."],["A Day on the Fells","A day's journey from my door, I said in January, and in October I, Lettice Brambling, used every hour of it. The carrier took Wilf Tarrow and me from Emberwick to Fellfoot at dawn, and we climbed to the edge of Hagg Moor in the Northern Fells.\n\nThe fell mosses are a different company. Woolly fringe-moss covers the boulders in grey fleeces. Under the heather grows glittering wood-moss, the handsomest I have seen: red stems, with fronds set one above another like steps. Each step is one year's growth. Wilf counted seven on a single shoot, so that moss was two years younger than he is.\n\nWe found fourteen new kinds between the last wall and the first cairn. Ninety-five. I slept the whole way home in the cart, I am told, with a packet in each hand."],["Goblin Gold","The quarryman's wife told me of it in spring: a green-gold light at the back of the old slate level on Coppice Bank, which the children of Emberwick call goblin gold. In November, with ninety-eight kinds in the book, I, Lettice Brambling, went to look. Wilf Tarrow, my assistant, came too.\n\nTen paces into the tunnel, where daylight was nearly gone, the floor glowed. It was faint and greenish, like a cat's eye, and it vanished when I moved my head. I carried a thimbleful of earth out to the lens.\n\nLuminous moss. The plant itself is a tiny pale feather. The glow comes from a film of green threads on the soil, whose round cells gather the last scrap of daylight and throw some back. It makes no light; it only wastes none. Ninety-nine. Wilf wanted it to be goblins, and was gracious about it."],["The Hundredth Moss","December in Emberwick, and I, Lettice Brambling, had ninety-nine mosses in the book and eleven days left. I walked the churchyard, the mill bridge and Coppice Bank again, and found only old friends. Wilf Tarrow searched the school roof and was sent down.\n\nOn the last morning of the year I sat on my own doorstep in Tanner's Row to lace my boots. Between the flagstones ran a thin line of silver-green, flattened by twelve months of my own feet. I fetched the lens. Silver-moss: tiny shoots like catkins, pale and shining, the commonest pavement moss there is. I had stepped over it every single day.\n\nSo the hundredth is the nearest. Wilf wrote the label himself. The compendium is bound in green cloth and goes to the library tomorrow, with a hundred packets in a box. Next year he wants lichens. I have asked for a week's rest first."]],[["The Spring at Emberhead","The river Ember begins as a cold trickle under a boulder, high in the Northern Fells, at a place called Emberhead. I am Clemency Oake, and I draw plants for a living. This May my nephew Pip and I set out to follow the Ember from its spring to the sea, drawing every fern on its banks, and every fern ally too.\n\nThe allies came first. In the short turf round the spring grows fir clubmoss, in stiff green fingers a few inches high. It is neither a fir nor a moss. Clubmosses, horsetails and quillworts are the ferns' old relations: like ferns they have no flowers and no seeds, and breed by spores as fine as dust.\n\nPip, who is eleven and carries the press, asked how a plant manages without seeds. I told him he would know by September. He has written that down, in case I forget."],["Along the High Beck","For its first few miles the Ember is only a beck, hopping down the fellside from Emberhead between banks of peat and rock. My nephew Pip and I walked it in the middle of May, when the new fronds were still soft.\n\nTwo ferns own these banks. Lemon-scented fern stands in pale yellow-green shuttlecocks; draw a frond through your fingers and it smells of lemon peel. Hard fern is darker and tougher, and has two sorts of frond on one plant. The low, spreading ones are plain leaves. The tall, thin ones in the middle, like fishbones, carry the spores.\n\nPip rubbed and sniffed every fern for a mile, to be certain, and by evening smelt like a pudding. I, Clemency Oake, drew both kinds with my feet on a stone in the water. The beck is already wide enough that we must choose a side and keep to it."],["Skein Force","Where the Ember leaves the high fells it drops sixty feet into a gorge, and the fall is called Skein Force. The air there is wet all year. My nephew Pip and I climbed down beside it in late May.\n\nOn the dripping rock behind the spray grows the filmy fern. I, Clemency Oake, had wanted to draw it for twenty years. The fronds are an inch or two long, dark green, and so thin that light comes through them as through green tissue paper: one cell thick. Out of the damp a frond shrivels within the hour, so it would never travel to my drawing table.\n\nIt had to be drawn where it grew. I sat in the spray for two hours under an umbrella, with Pip holding the paint box in his hat. The drawing is a little cockled. It is the best thing I have done."],["The Oak Wood and the Jar","Below Skein Force the river Ember runs through Hanging Oak Wood, and in early June the ferns there were still unrolling. Each new frond comes up coiled like the head of a fiddle. My nephew Pip counted forty in one clump of male fern.\n\nMale fern and lady fern grow side by side, and were named by people who thought one sturdy and the other delicate. They are only neighbours. Turn a frond over in late summer and the difference is plain: male fern has round spore-dots, lady fern has curved ones like commas.\n\nPip wanted to see spores grow. In my folder was a frond pressed last autumn, and the paper under it was brown with shed dust. We tipped it on to damp peat in a jam jar and tied muslin over the top. I, Clemency Oake, promised nothing. The jar travels in his coat pocket."],["The Bracken Bank","Out of the wood, the Ember valley opens between steep banks, and every bank is bracken. In mid-June it stood as high as my nephew Pip's chin. I am Clemency Oake, and I had half a mind to leave it out of the book, being so common.\n\nThat would have been unjust. Bracken is the one fern that makes its own thicket. The fronds rise singly from a black stem that creeps underground for yards, so a whole hillside may be one plant. Cut a stalk across near the base and the brown veins make a pattern; Pip sees an oak tree in it, and I see a two-headed bird.\n\nThe farmers here cut it in autumn for winter bedding in the byres. We drew one frond, folded in three to fit the page, and walked on through the rest with our arms up."],["The Mill Bridge Wall","At Emberwick the river passes under the old mill bridge, and my nephew Pip and I stopped a week there at midsummer. I, Clemency Oake, spent most of it leaning over the parapet, to the concern of passers-by.\n\nThree small ferns live in the mortar. Maidenhair spleenwort has a wiry black stalk with round green leaflets in pairs, like beads on a thread. Wall-rue is stubby and grey-green and looks more like a herb than a fern. Rustyback is the neatest: thick scalloped fronds, green above and covered beneath with rust-brown scales. In dry weather it curls up and shows only the rusty side, then opens again after rain.\n\nThey ask for nothing but lime and a crack. Pip's jam jar of spores sat on the inn windowsill all week, and on the last morning the peat had a faint green bloom, like the skin on a pond."],["The Limestone Gorge","South of Emberwick the river Ember has cut a narrow gorge through pale limestone, called the Clatter for the noise the water makes. It is shady and damp even at the start of July, when my nephew Pip and I walked through it.\n\nHart's-tongue fern hangs from every ledge. It is the one fern that a child draws correctly at the first attempt: a plain, glossy green strap, undivided, with a wavy edge. Turn it over and the spores lie in straight brown stripes, slanting out from the midrib like the marks on a ruler. Pip measured the longest frond at twenty-two inches.\n\nHigher in the cracks grows brittle bladder-fern, small and lacy, with stalks that snap at a touch. I, Clemency Oake, snapped three before I learned to draw it without holding it. Each spore-dot wears a tiny pale hood, the bladder of its name."],["Two Odd Ferns","Below the gorge the Ember slows and winds through old water meadows. My nephew Pip and I crossed them in the middle of July, on hands and knees, because the two ferns that live there are easier to kneel on than to see.\n\nAdder's-tongue is a single oval leaf with one green spike standing up inside it. Moonwort is smaller still: a stalk with half-moon leaflets down each side, and a branched spike of spores like a bunch of tiny grapes. Neither unrolls from a coil as other ferns do. Country people say moonwort will open locks and pull the shoes off horses.\n\nWe tested this on the carrier's mare, with his leave. She kept all four shoes and ate the daisies. I, Clemency Oake, record the result for science. Pip found eleven moonworts. I found two, and the heel of my hand found a thistle."],["The Marsh and the Green Hearts","Where the Lowlands sink toward the marsh, the Ember spreads into ditches and reed beds, and the ferns grow large. It was August. My nephew Pip and I went by punt, which he poled with more spirit than direction.\n\nRoyal fern stands on the ditch banks in clumps taller than I am. Its spores are not on the back of the leaves. They sit at the top of the frond in rusty tassels, like a plant in flower. Marsh fern creeps through the wet ground between, sending up thin pale fronds one at a time.\n\nAnd in the jam jar that Pip has carried since the oak wood in June, the green film has become a crowd of flat green hearts, each smaller than a lentil. I, Clemency Oake, explained that these are not ferns yet. A fern grows up in two stages, and this is the first."],["Horsetails on the Dyke","Along the marsh dyke south of the reed beds, the river Ember is edged with horsetails, and in late August my nephew Pip and I walked a mile between them. They are fern allies, and the oddest of the family.\n\nA horsetail is built in joints, like a fishing rod. Each hollow green stem pulls apart with a small pop and fits back together, and at every joint stands a ring of thin branches. Water horsetail grows in the ditch itself. Great horsetail, on the damp bank, reaches Pip's shoulder on ivory-white stems. They feel harsh because they are full of fine grit, and kitchen maids once scoured pewter with them.\n\nTheir ancestors were trees. The coal in the grate is partly made of horsetails and clubmosses as tall as church towers. I, Clemency Oake, find that a steadying thought. Pip took one to pieces and could not rebuild it."],["The Quiet Pool","A mile from the sea the Ember passes a shallow pool on Saltway Common. My nephew Pip and I came to it in September, looking for the last two inland plants on our list. One is a fern and one a fern ally, and nobody would take either for anything but grass.\n\nPillwort, the fern, creeps over the wet mud in thin green threads. Each new leaf unrolls from a tiny coil, and at the base sit hard brown pills the size of peppercorns, which hold the spores. Quillwort, the ally, grows right under the water in stiff dark tufts, like a bundle of quill pens. Pip fetched one with his sleeve rolled up.\n\nI, Clemency Oake, drew them both life size, and both looked like lawn clippings. It cannot be helped. The honest dull ones belong in the book as much as the royal fern does."],["Sea Spleenwort","The river Ember ends at the Grey Coast, sliding out between two low cliffs into the sea. My nephew Pip and I reached it at the end of September, four months and sixty miles from the spring at Emberhead.\n\nOne fern was left. In a cleft of the cliff, wet with salt spray, grows sea spleenwort: glossy, leathery fronds, bright green against black rock. It is the only fern on our list that can bear salt. I, Clemency Oake, drew it at low tide with a wave filling my boots.\n\nThat makes thirty-one ferns and nine allies. And in Pip's jam jar, among the green hearts, one has put up a single true frond, no bigger than a clover leaf. Spore, heart, fern: he has his answer, as promised in May. He carried the jar home on his knees in the cart, and would not let me hold it."]],[["Twelve Circles","My uncle Ambrose left me Latchet Orchard, on the edge of Emberwick, and a map of it drawn on the back of a seed bill. The map shows twelve circles in three rows of four. None has a name. I am Gideon Thrale, a retired schoolmaster, and I know the kings of six countries and not one fruit tree.\n\nI counted in January, in the snow: eleven trees, and in the far corner a mossy stump where the twelfth should be.\n\nMy neighbour, Mrs. Ansty, leaned on the gate and named the first. The leaning tree nearest the lane is an Ember Codlin, a cooking apple older than she is. Its fruit buds are fat and downy, its leaf buds thin and flat. She showed me where to prune: cut out the dead, the diseased and the crossing, and then stop. I wrote 'Codlin' in the first circle."],["Catkins on the Cobnut","February at Latchet Orchard, Emberwick, and one of my uncle's unnamed trees has named itself. In the hedge corner stands a many-stemmed bush hung with yellow catkins, shaking loose a dust of pollen whenever the wind moves. Even I, Gideon Thrale, know a hazel.\n\nMrs. Ansty corrected me kindly. It is a cobnut, a hazel bred for bigger nuts. The catkins are the male flowers. The female ones are harder to find: a fat bud with a tuft of crimson threads at the tip, no bigger than a grain of rice. I needed my reading spectacles. Wind carries the pollen across, and by September each red tuft may be a cluster of nuts, if the squirrels permit.\n\n'Cobnut' goes in the second circle. The twigs she had me save from pruning the old codlin lie in damp sand in the shed. I am told to wait for March."],["Plum Blossom and a Graft","Late March, and the first blossom at Latchet Orchard has opened on a small tree by the south wall: white flowers on bare twigs, before a single leaf. Mrs. Ansty says it is a gage, the sweetest sort of plum, and that it flowers too early for its own good. One hard frost now and there will be no fruit in August. I, Gideon Thrale, have been checking the weathercock hourly.\n\nThis was also grafting week. The twigs saved from the old Ember Codlin came out of their sand, and Mrs. Ansty brought a young rootstock. She cut both on a long slant, fitted one to the other so the green layers under the bark met, and bound them with twine and wax. A new tree, she says, with the old tree's fruit.\n\nIt looks like a stick tied to a stick. 'Gage' is in the third circle."],["The Pear in Flower","The tallest tree in Latchet Orchard stands straight up from the middle row like a church spire, and in April it turned white from top to bottom. Mrs. Ansty, my neighbour here in Emberwick, says it is a pear, and that my uncle's grandfather likely planted it. Pears outlive the people who plant them by several generations.\n\nI, Gideon Thrale, went close to admire it. Pear blossom is handsome, white with dark red anthers, and smells faintly of fish. Nobody had warned me. The flies and beetles enjoy it, and the bees come regardless.\n\nThe bark is the other mark of a pear: grey and cracked into small squares, like the hide of some patient old animal. Apple bark flakes in plates; cherry bark peels in bands. I am learning to read trees with the leaves off and the leaves on. 'Pear' is in the fourth circle."],["The Crab and the Bees","May is apple blossom month at Latchet Orchard, Emberwick. The old Ember Codlin by the lane is pink and white, and so is a smaller, thornier tree in the next row that I, Gideon Thrale, had taken for a poor relation.\n\nMrs. Ansty set me right. It is a crab apple, and my uncle planted it on purpose. An apple tree sets little fruit with its own pollen; it needs a different apple flowering nearby, and bees to carry between them. A crab flowers long and freely, so one crab can serve a whole orchard. Its own fruit is small, hard and sour, and makes the best jelly there is.\n\nI sat under it for an hour. Honeybees, bumblebees and a small ginger bee all went from crab to codlin and back. 'Crab' is in the fifth circle. Against the shed wall, the grafted stick has two leaves."],["The Late Walnut","One tree at Latchet Orchard, Emberwick, stayed bare so long this spring that I, Gideon Thrale, had the saw out. Mrs. Ansty took it from my hand. A walnut, she said, is always last to wake, and it has reason: its young leaves are bronze, tender and easily frosted.\n\nBy June it was in full leaf, the largest tree in the back row. Crush a leaf and it smells sharp and spicy, something like polish. Very little grows beneath a walnut, and people have long sat under one to keep the flies off.\n\nThe nuts are green now, the size of plums. Mrs. Ansty pickles some this week, while a needle still passes through them. They stain the fingers brown for a fortnight; hers are proof. 'Walnut' is in the sixth circle. Meanwhile the apples are dropping their smallest fruit, which I am told is normal."],["Cherries and Blackbirds","July, and the mystery tree by the well at Latchet Orchard has given itself away. Its bark is glossy red-brown, ringed with rough bands like stitching, and for two weeks it has been covered in dark red cherries. I, Gideon Thrale, have had perhaps a pound of them. The blackbirds of Emberwick have had the rest.\n\nI tried a net, and caught myself. I tried a scarecrow in my uncle's hat, and a thrush sat on it. Mrs. Ansty says a tall cherry belongs to the birds and always has; one picks from a ladder at dawn and is thankful.\n\nThere is a kind of bargain in it. The birds carry the stones away and drop them, and that is how wild cherries come to stand in every hedge between here and the river. I wrote 'Cherry' in the seventh circle with a stained thumb, and left a print."],["The Mulberry","The oldest-looking tree at Latchet Orchard, Emberwick, is a low, leaning thing propped on a wooden crutch. Mrs. Ansty calls it the mulberry, and says it is the wisest tree here, because it never opens a leaf until the frosts are truly finished.\n\nIn August it fruits. The berries are like long dark raspberries, and they ripen a few at a time over weeks. They are too soft to carry to market, so one eats them where one stands. The juice is crimson and gets everywhere. I, Gideon Thrale, went to the library with purple cuffs.\n\nMrs. Ansty spreads an old sheet under the boughs and shakes them gently. We filled two bowls and ruined the sheet. 'Mulberry' is in the eighth circle. By the shed, the grafted codlin has a shoot as long as my forearm, and I have stopped calling it a stick."],["Damsons in the Hedge","I had counted the tree in the eastern hedge of Latchet Orchard as part of the hedge. In September it bent under small, oval, blue-black plums with a grey bloom on them, and Mrs. Ansty informed me that I, Gideon Thrale, owned a damson.\n\nA damson is a plum that has never been spoilt by kindness. It is thorny, tough, half wild, and fruits well in years when the pampered gage by the south wall sulks. Eaten raw it puckers the mouth. Cooked with sugar it turns a deep red-purple and tastes richer than any plum I know.\n\nWe picked eighteen pounds in an afternoon. Emberwick smells of jam this week from one end to the other; every kitchen has a pan on. Mrs. Ansty counts the stones on to a plate afterwards, to be sure of them all. 'Damson' is in the ninth circle. Three to go."],["The Russet and the Apple Loft","October is picking month at Latchet Orchard, Emberwick. The old Ember Codlin came down first, by the basketful, for pies. But the tree beside it puzzled me: its apples are small, rough and brown-gold, like fruit knitted from sacking. I, Gideon Thrale, assumed a disease.\n\nMrs. Ansty laughed for some time. It is a russet, and the rough coat is its nature. She cut one. The flesh is cream-coloured, dry and sweet, with a taste like nuts. It is a keeping apple, hard at picking and best after a month in store.\n\nAn apple is ready when it comes away with a lift and a quarter turn. We laid them in my uncle's loft on slatted trays, none touching another, and I am to look them over every week and remove any that turn. The loft already smells wonderful. 'Russet' is in the tenth circle."],["The Medlar After Frost","The last standing tree without a name at Latchet Orchard is small and crooked, with long leaves that turned red and gold this November. Its fruit is odd: brown, round and flat-ended, with a crown of leafy points, like a rosehip grown too large. I, Gideon Thrale, bit one in October and regretted it for an hour.\n\nIt is a medlar, says Mrs. Ansty, and I was too early. Medlars are picked after the first frosts and laid in straw for some weeks until they soften and darken. The old word is bletting. They look ruined, and are then ready.\n\nWe tried the first today in her kitchen in Emberwick. One squeezes the pulp out with a spoon. It tastes like stewed apple with dates. 'Medlar' is in the eleventh circle. That leaves the stump in the corner, and no fruit to name it by."],["The Twelfth Circle","December at Latchet Orchard, Emberwick, a year since I first counted eleven trees and a stump. I, Gideon Thrale, asked Mrs. Ansty what the twelfth had been. An Ember Codlin, she said, twin to the one by the lane. It blew down the winter my uncle Ambrose turned eighty, and he never had the heart to replant.\n\nSo we knew what to do. The graft we made in March, from the old codlin's own twigs, stands waist-high now in its pot. We grubbed out the stump, dug a wide hole, spread the roots, and trod the soil firm. Mrs. Ansty drove the stake. I tied it.\n\nIt will be five years before it bears. I wrote 'Codlin, again' in the twelfth circle, and under the map, in my best schoolroom hand, the names of all twelve. Then we went indoors and ate a russet each from the loft."]],[["The Water Vole at Withy Bridge","My name is Tobias Reedham. I kept the ferry below Emberwick for thirty-one years, and now that there is a bridge I have a punt called Patience and time on my hands. The librarian asked me to write down the beasts of the marsh. So this June I am poling from Withy Bridge to the sea sluice on the Grey Coast, twelve days, a beast a day.\n\nThe first found me before I had cast off. A water vole sat on the bank a yard away, eating a reed held in both paws, like a man with a stick of celery. Blunt nose, small ears, fur the brown of wet bark. Then it dropped in with a plop.\n\nOut in the reeds a bittern was booming, like someone blowing across a jug. I have heard that bird every spring of my life and never once seen it."],["The Grass Snake Swims","Second day out from Withy Bridge, in the punt Patience, on the long ditch called Lazy Lode. I am Tobias Reedham, ferryman retired, writing a bestiary of the marsh one day at a time.\n\nMid-morning something came across the water ahead of me, drawing a neat V, with its head held up like a walking-stick handle. A grass snake, a good three feet long, olive green with a yellow collar. It swims as easily as an eel and hunts frogs and newts along the margins. It has no venom and no wish to meet anyone.\n\nI picked one up years ago, off the ferry landing. It went limp, rolled over, let its tongue hang out and played dead most convincingly. It also made a smell I will not describe. I put it down and it recovered at once. Today I only raised my hat."],["The Heron Stands","Third day in the punt Patience, deep in the marsh now, with Emberwick's church tower gone behind the reeds. I, Tobias Reedham, stopped for my dinner at Alder Carr, where the herons nest.\n\nThere are nine great stick nests in the tops of the alders, used year after year and patched each spring. The young are nearly grown and stand about on the branches looking like disappointed clerks. They clatter their bills when a parent comes in.\n\nThe old birds fish the ditches. A heron's method is to stand. It stands in the shallows, grey and hunched, without moving a feather; then the neck shoots out and there is a fish crosswise in the bill. I timed one against my watch. Twenty-two minutes, one roach. After thirty-one years on a ferry I thought I knew about waiting. I did not."],["Otter at First Light","I woke before four on the fourth morning, moored in the punt Patience under Hollow Bank, in the middle of the marsh. Something was whistling on the water. I, Tobias Reedham, lay still under my blanket and watched over the side.\n\nAn otter and two cubs came down the channel in the grey light. She swam low, with only her flat head and a line of back showing. The cubs bobbed like corks and squeaked. She dived, leaving a chain of bubbles, and came up with a fish, which she ate on a mud bank, chewing with her head on one side.\n\nLater I found their slide, a smooth wet groove down the bank, and a dropping left on a stone as a signpost. It smelt, oddly, of sweet hay. They were gone by sunrise. The bittern boomed as I lit the stove, unseen as ever."],["The Eel Road","On the fifth day of my punt journey through the marsh I tied up at Bess Tarrow's eel house, on the main channel of the Ember. Bess has caught eels here since she was a girl. I am Tobias Reedham, the old ferryman, and she gave me tea in exchange for news.\n\nShe showed me the last of this year's young eels going upstream. They are no longer than a finger and nearly clear as glass, and they climb her wet sluice boards by wriggling. They were hatched far out in the deep ocean, nobody knows quite where.\n\nIn the marsh they turn yellow-brown and grow for ten or twenty years. Then, one wet autumn night, they turn silver, stop eating, and go back down to the sea for good, crossing wet meadows if they must. Bess lets the big silver ones pass. They have, she says, an appointment."],["The Marsh Harrier","Sixth day, halfway between Withy Bridge and the sea, and the reed beds stretch to the edge of sight. I, Tobias Reedham, shipped the pole and let the punt Patience drift, because there was a hawk to watch.\n\nThe marsh harrier flies low and slow over the reeds with its wings held up in a shallow V, tilting like a tired kite. The hen is chocolate brown with a cream cap. The cock is smaller, with grey in his wings and black tips. Their nest is on the ground, somewhere in the thick of the reeds.\n\nAt noon he came in carrying something small. She rose from the reeds to meet him, and he let it fall. She rolled in the air, caught it in her feet and went back down to the young. It took two seconds. I applauded, alone in a punt, and felt no shame."],["The Water Shrew","On the seventh evening I moored the punt Patience at Clear Dyke, a spring-fed ditch in mid-marsh, where one can see the gravel bottom. I am Tobias Reedham, and I was only washing my kettle.\n\nA small black creature ran down the bank, dived, and turned silver. It was a water shrew, no longer than my thumb, black velvet above and white below. Its fur traps air, so under water it shines like a bead of quicksilver. It nosed under stones along the bottom, bobbed up like a cork, and ate a shrimp on a leaf.\n\nA shrew must eat every few hours or starve, so it is always in a hurry. This one hunted, ate, groomed and dived again four times while my kettle boiled. Its bite is said to numb a snail. I kept my fingers in the punt and wished it a good supper."],["The Cuckoo's Lodging","Eighth day in the reed beds of the lower marsh. I, Tobias Reedham, have poled the punt Patience past a hundred reed warblers and seen about six. They are small, plain, brown birds, and they sing from inside the reeds, a steady chattering like two pebbles knocked together.\n\nThe nest is the marvel. It is a deep cup woven round three or four reed stems, a foot above the water, so it sways with the reeds and rises as they grow. I found one by Sallow Reach with four eggs, greenish and blotched.\n\nAnd one larger. A cuckoo had been there. Her chick will hatch first, tip the other eggs over the side, and be fed by the two warblers until it is three times their size and overflowing the cup. I looked for a long while, and decided that a bestiary records, and does not meddle."],["The Harvest Mouse","Ninth day. The marsh is drying toward its seaward edge, and the punt Patience now slides between banks of tall grass and sedge. I am Tobias Reedham, and today I nearly missed the smallest beast in the book.\n\nA grass stem bent that had no reason to bend. Up it went a harvest mouse, the colour of a ripe wheat ear, with a white front. It weighs less than a copper penny. It climbed with its tail wrapped round the stem like a fifth hand, and let go with both forefeet to reach a seed.\n\nNear by hung its nest, the first of the summer and still green: a ball of shredded grass blades the size of a small apple, woven on to living stems, with no door to be seen. The mouse pushes in and the wall closes behind. I have known worse carpentry on ferries."],["Natterjacks in the Dunes","On the tenth night I left the punt Patience at the marsh's edge and walked, because the noise made sleep impossible. Behind the sand dunes of the Grey Coast lie shallow pools, and from them came a churring chorus that carried a mile. I, Tobias Reedham, followed it with a lantern.\n\nNatterjack toads. They are smaller than a common toad, with a thin yellow stripe down the back. Each male sat in the shallows with his throat blown out like a pale bubble, singing. They cannot hop much on those short legs; they run, and one ran over my boot.\n\nThey want pools that dry out by late summer, so that nothing lives there to eat the tadpoles, which must grow up in a hurry. It is a gamble with the weather every year. The whole chorus stopped when I sneezed, and began again when I apologised."],["The Bittern at Last","Eleventh day, in the last reed bed before the sea. For ten days in the punt Patience I, Tobias Reedham, had heard the bittern boom morning and evening, always from somewhere else. I had stopped expecting anything.\n\nAt dusk I was sitting quite still, splicing a rope, when a reed at the channel's edge turned out to have an eye. The bittern stood six yards off, a heron's stout cousin, streaked buff and brown and black exactly like dead reed. Seeing me, it stretched neck and bill straight up and froze. When the wind moved the reeds, it swayed with them.\n\nWe regarded each other for some minutes. Then it lowered its head, walked off between the stems on long green feet, and was gone without a sound. Sixty-three years I have waited for that. I wrote nothing down until morning, in case the pencil scratched."],["The Sluice and the Seals","On the twelfth day the punt Patience bumped against the timbers of the sea sluice, and the marsh was behind me. Beyond the gates the Ember runs out across the sands of the Grey Coast. I am Tobias Reedham, and I had run out of water to pole on.\n\nI climbed the bank. On the bar lay some forty seals, curved like bananas with head and tail both lifted. They are not marsh beasts, but they are neighbours, and they wait off the sluice for the same silver eels that Bess Tarrow lets pass. One scratched its chin with a flipper and looked at me as at a late ferry.\n\nSo the bestiary is done: eleven days of marsh beasts, and the seals for good measure. I shall pole home against the stream, with nothing to write down. That will be a pleasure of its own."]],[["Tracks in the Snow","I am Nell Hathersage, the miller's daughter at Emberwick, and my bedroom is the loft over the grain store. From its window I can see the whole of Coppice Bank. These are my notes on the foxes that live there, begun in January and to be kept for one year.\n\nLast night there was snow, and a fox was barking: three sharp yaps, a wait, then three more. Later came a long hoarse scream that brought Father out with a lantern. It was only a vixen answering. January is when foxes court.\n\nThis morning I followed the tracks. A dog's prints wander. A fox's run in one straight line, each hind foot set exactly in the mark of the front, like a row of stitches. Two lines crossed the mill field side by side and went into the wood together."],["The Vixen Cleans House","February on Coppice Bank, above Emberwick mill. From my loft window I, Nell Hathersage, have now seen both foxes by daylight. The dog fox is big and dark, with a walk as if he owned the wood, so I call him the Squire. The vixen is smaller and redder, with one white forefoot. She is Whitesock.\n\nShe has been digging. There is an old earth under the roots of a fallen beech, and every morning fresh soil has been thrown out in a fan at its mouth, and old bedding dragged clear. She is cleaning house.\n\nI crept close at noon, upwind, and smelt them before I saw anything: a sharp musky smell, like the lion's cage at the travelling fair. Foxes mark their ground with it, on stones and tussocks, as plainly as we paint a name on a gate. I left no mark of my own."],["Born Underground","I, Nell Hathersage, have not seen Whitesock the vixen for twelve days. Her earth is under the fallen beech on Coppice Bank, in plain view of my loft window at Emberwick mill, and she does not come out. It is the middle of March. I think the cubs are born.\n\nThe Squire, her mate, tells me so. Each dusk he trots up the bank with something in his jaws, lays it at the mouth of the earth, and goes away. He never goes in. By morning it is gone.\n\nFather says newborn cubs are blind and deaf, no bigger than moles, with short dark fur. They cannot keep warm alone, so the vixen stays curled round them for the first fortnight and her mate hunts for both. I had not thought of the Squire as dutiful before. He looks thinner, and rather pleased with himself."],["Four at the Door","The seventeenth of April, a warm evening. I was at my loft window over Emberwick mill with Father's spyglass when something small and dark tumbled out of the fox earth on Coppice Bank, sat down, and fell over. Then another. Then two more.\n\nFour cubs. They are not red at all yet, but chocolate brown and woolly, with short blunt faces, small round ears and eyes of a cloudy blue. Their tails are thin as string. Whitesock, their mother, lay above them on the beech trunk with her chin on her white paw.\n\nI, Nell Hathersage, named them within the hour. Bold walked straight to the edge of the bank and looked over. Shy would not leave the doorway. Scrap bit everybody's ears. Tuppence, the smallest, sat exactly where she was put. At a noise from the lane the vixen gave one low cough and all four were gone underground."],["The Playground","By the middle of May the ground outside the fox earth on Coppice Bank is trodden bare as a schoolyard. I, Nell Hathersage, watch it each evening from the mill loft at Emberwick, and it is better than any travelling show.\n\nThe four cubs are turning red from the face backwards, and their muzzles have grown long. Their eyes have changed from blue to amber. They stalk beetles, feathers, and one another's tails. Scrap has an old leather glove, heaven knows whose, which he carries everywhere. Bold pounces on it; Shy steals it when both are busy.\n\nIt is all practice. The crouch, the wriggle, the high jump with stiff forepaws: that is how a grown fox catches a vole in long grass. Whitesock brings them food now, and looks worn and thin. When the cubs mob her she simply steps up on the beech trunk, out of reach."],["Worms and Beetles","June, and the nights are short. The fox cubs of Coppice Bank are out before dark now, so I, Nell Hathersage, can watch them from the loft at Emberwick mill without the spyglass.\n\nWhitesock, their mother, has begun taking them hunting, two at a time. I had imagined rabbits. The first lesson was worms. On damp evenings she leads them down to the mill pasture, where the earthworms lie out on the grass, and shows them how to listen, pinch one in the front teeth, and pull slowly so it does not snap. Tuppence is the best at it. Bold pulls too hard and sits down.\n\nThey eat beetles, crunching them like toast, and moths knocked down with a paw. Father says the hens are far safer than people believe, so long as the door is shut. Half of what a fox eats would fit in a teaspoon."],["The Earth Stands Empty","The fox earth under the fallen beech on Coppice Bank is empty. I, Nell Hathersage, watched it from the mill loft for four evenings in July and saw only a wren. The trodden yard is growing over with grass.\n\nNothing is wrong. Father says a fox family gives up its earth in summer, once the cubs are too big for it. They lie out above ground instead, in bracken or standing corn, wherever it is cool and hidden.\n\nSo I went looking. In the barley at the top of the mill field I found a flattened place the size of a cartwheel, with Scrap's leather glove in the middle. That evening five heads rose out of the corn one after another, ears first. Whitesock is in moult and looks as if moths had been at her. Her four cubs are leggy, three-quarters grown, and sleek as new pennies."],["Dog Days","August at Emberwick mill. The corn is whitening, and the foxes of Coppice Bank are hard to tell apart at a distance. I, Nell Hathersage, have had to learn them again.\n\nThe four cubs are nearly their mother's size. Bold is the biggest, with a white tip to his brush as long as my hand. Scrap has a nick in one ear from some quarrel. Shy is darker, like the Squire her father, and keeps to the hedge. Tuppence is still the smallest and stays close to Whitesock, who has the only white foot in the family.\n\nThey hunt alone now, mostly, and meet at dusk with a great deal of whining and tail-wagging, as dogs do. I watched Bold catch his first vole in the stubble: ears forward, a pause, then straight up in the air and down on his forepaws. He looked as surprised as the vole."],["Blackberry Foxes","September. The hedge along the mill lane at Emberwick is black with fruit, and I, Nell Hathersage, went out with a basket on Saturday to find the lower branches picked already.\n\nIt was the foxes of Coppice Bank. At dusk I watched Tuppence and her mother Whitesock working along the brambles, lifting their lips clear and nipping off the berries one at a time with their front teeth. Their droppings on the path are purple and full of pips. In the orchard they take windfall apples, and Father found Scrap asleep under the damson tree with a round stomach.\n\nA fox in autumn eats what the season offers: fruit, beetles, mice from the stubble. Their new coats are coming in thick and bright for winter. The four cubs are six months old and look like foxes. I can no longer call them cubs with a straight face."],["Bold Goes Away","On the ninth of October I, Nell Hathersage, saw Bold for the last time. He stood at the top of Coppice Bank at first light, looked back once toward Emberwick mill, and went over the ridge toward the Northern Fells.\n\nFather says it is the way of them. In autumn the young foxes leave to find ground of their own, the dog foxes first and farthest; some travel twenty miles. Scrap went a week later, squabbling with the Squire. Shy has moved across the river to the alder wood, where the ferryman's wife has seen her.\n\nIt is quiet at dusk now. I minded more than I expected, and said so. Father pointed out that I would not wish to live in the loft for ever. He is right, though I did not tell him. Tuppence, the smallest of the four, is still here at her mother's heel."],["The One Who Stayed","November, with frost on the mill pasture at Emberwick and the leaves down on Coppice Bank, so that from my loft I, Nell Hathersage, can see farther into the wood than at any time since March.\n\nThree foxes remain: the Squire, Whitesock, and Tuppence, the smallest of this year's four cubs. A young vixen often stays on her mother's ground for a year, Father says, and helps with next spring's litter, carrying food and minding the cubs like an elder sister.\n\nThey are handsome now. The winter coat is deep and thick, red as a beech leaf, with black stockings and a full brush that a fox wraps over its nose when it sleeps. I watched Tuppence bury a mouse beside the gatepost, pushing the soil back with her nose. Foxes hide spare food against a hungry day. Two nights later she came back and dug it up, exactly."],["Snow Again","The last night of December. I, Nell Hathersage, am at the loft window over Emberwick mill with a blanket round me, and it is snowing on Coppice Bank as it did when I began these notes.\n\nA fox barked an hour ago: three yaps, a wait, three more. That is the Squire. From the wood came the long hoarse answer that frightened us all last January and now only makes me smile. Whitesock has been at the old earth under the beech again; there is fresh sand at its mouth.\n\nIn the morning there will be tracks in a straight line across the mill field, and I will know whose they are. One year: two foxes, four cubs, three gone to their own ground and one stayed. I have filled this notebook to the last page, which is as it should be. Father has bought me another."]],[["The Charge Against the Owls","Paper the first, read to the Emberwick Field Club by Miss Verity Holloway, organist of this parish.\n\nAt our February meeting Mr. Crabbe stated that the white owls in the church belfry have been taking his pigeons, nine since Midwinter, and proposed that the louvres be wired shut. I asked the Club to wait. I practise the organ late and have known those owls for eleven years. I have never seen either with a feather in its feet.\n\nThe barn owl is the white owl of our belfry: pale gold above, pure white below, with a heart-shaped face and dark eyes. It hunts at dusk, low over rough grass. I propose a fair trial. An owl coughs up the bones of everything it eats. From now until harvest I shall collect every pellet under the belfry and count what is in them. The owls, meanwhile, remain at liberty."],["On Silent Flight","Paper the second of Miss Verity Holloway, on the barn owls of Emberwick church, whose diet is under inquiry by this Club.\n\nMembers asked how so large a bird can fly unheard. I brought two feathers to the March meeting: a wing feather moulted by the belfry owl, and one from Mr. Crabbe's own pigeons. Swish the pigeon's through the air and it whistles. The owl's makes no sound.\n\nThree things do it. The front edge of an owl's wing feather is toothed like a comb, which breaks up the rush of air. The rear edge is a soft fringe. The whole surface has a velvet pile, so feather slides on feather without a rustle. A vole does not hear the owl coming, and the owl, which hunts by ear, is not deafened by its own wings. The feathers were passed round. Mr. Crabbe stroked the owl's twice."],["What a Pellet Holds","Paper the third of Miss Verity Holloway, organist, being the first evidence in the matter of the belfry owls of Emberwick and Mr. Crabbe's pigeons.\n\nAn owl swallows small prey whole. Some hours later it brings up the fur and bones in a neat pellet, a dark, glossy lump about the size of my thumb, quite dry. They lie in heaps on the belfry floor. The verger has lent me a dustpan.\n\nSoak a pellet in a saucer of warm water and tease it apart with two darning needles. The fur floats away and the bones remain: skulls, jaws, ribs like eyelashes. A vole's teeth are flat with a zigzag pattern. A shrew's are pointed and tipped with red. A mouse's are knobbly. My first twelve pellets held forty-one skulls: twenty-nine voles, nine shrews, three mice. No pigeon. Mr. Crabbe observed that it was early days. It is."],["How an Owl Hears","Paper the fourth of Miss Verity Holloway, read to the Emberwick Field Club in April, while the inquiry into the belfry owls continues.\n\nA barn owl can catch a mouse in complete darkness. It hunts by ear alone, and the heart-shaped face is the instrument. It is a shallow dish of stiff feathers that gathers sound, as a hand does behind the ear, and passes it to ear openings hidden at the edge. These are not level: one sits a little higher than the other. A sound from below reaches them unequally, so the owl knows not only left from right but up from down. It turns its face until both ears agree, and then it is looking straight at the rustle.\n\nAs an organist I admire this greatly. I can tell when the tenors are flat. I cannot tell which tenor in the dark."],["The Tawny in the Yew","Paper the fifth of Miss Verity Holloway. The barn owls of Emberwick belfry are not our only owls, and in fairness to all parties I have examined the neighbours.\n\nA pair of tawny owls lives in the old churchyard yew. The tawny is the brown owl, stout and round-headed, with black eyes, and it is the one that hoots. The famous call is in fact two birds. The hen says 'kee-wick'. The cock answers with a long wavering hoot. Heard together, they have been taken for one voice for centuries.\n\nTawnies nest early. By late April two grey, fluffy owlets were sitting out on a yew bough in daylight, as they do before they can fly. They need no rescuing; the parents are near. I gathered pellets from under the yew: wood mice, voles, a great many beetles, and one frog. They too are cleared of pigeons."],["Five Eggs on the Floor","Paper the sixth of Miss Verity Holloway, organist, reporting from the belfry of Emberwick church in the month of May.\n\nThe barn owls have laid. I climbed the ladder on the third of May, with the verger holding it, and found the hen sitting in the corner behind the tenor bell. There is no nest. She lays on the bare boards, on a soft bed of her own old pellets, trodden flat.\n\nThere are five eggs, white, rounder than a hen's and without gloss. They are laid two or three days apart, and she sits from the first. So they will hatch in the same order, days apart, and the brood will be a staircase of sizes. The cock feeds her on the nest. I counted a larder of six voles laid in a row beside her. Pigeons in the larder: none. Mr. Crabbe was absent with a cold."],["The Little Owl by Day","Paper the seventh of Miss Verity Holloway, read to the Emberwick Field Club in June, the belfry inquiry being now half complete.\n\nMembers who say they have never seen an owl should walk past Latchet Orchard at noon. On the wall, or on the leaning mulberry, sits a little owl. It is no taller than a thrush, grey-brown with white spots, and has fierce yellow eyes under flat white brows, which give it the look of a small cross schoolmaster. When watched it bobs up and down, then flies off low, in dips.\n\nIts pellets are small and glitter. Under the wall I collected thirty, and they are made almost wholly of beetle wing-cases, blue-black and bronze, with earwig pincers and the bristles of worms. This owl could not lift a pigeon's egg. It is discharged without a stain."],["Owlets in the Belfry","Paper the eighth of Miss Verity Holloway, organist of Emberwick, on the family of barn owls above her head.\n\nAll five eggs hatched, between the twenty-fourth of May and the third of June. The eldest owlet is now a fat bag of white down with a long face; the youngest is half its size. They do not hoot. They snore, a long rasping snore for food that goes on all evening and can be heard in the nave. The choir has asked whether it is the bellows.\n\nOn Midsummer Night I sat in the ringing chamber from dusk to dawn with a slate. The cock came in fourteen times, and the hen, who has begun hunting again, five. Nineteen deliveries: sixteen voles, two shrews and a mouse. Each owlet swallows its vole whole, head first, and then looks astonished. Nothing with feathers was brought in."],["The Owl of the Marsh","Paper the ninth of Miss Verity Holloway. In July I left the belfry owls of Emberwick to their parents for a day and walked out along the marsh wall.\n\nThe short-eared owl hunts in daylight, over open ground. I watched one quarter the rough grass for an hour: long, narrow wings, a rowing flight, pale beneath with a dark patch at the bend of the wing. When it turned its head I saw yellow eyes ringed with black, as if it had not slept. It nests on the ground among the sedge.\n\nThe 'ears' are two short tufts of feather, usually laid flat. They have nothing to do with hearing. The true ears are at the sides of the face, as in every owl. It dropped into the grass twice and rose once with a vole. The marsh has no dovecotes, and I did not trouble it for pellets."],["Leaving the Tower","Paper the tenth of Miss Verity Holloway, organist, on the five young barn owls of Emberwick belfry, now nine weeks old and losing their down.\n\nSince the last week of July they have been coming out on to the louvres at dusk, one by one, to exercise. They stand in a row and flap without letting go. They bob and weave their heads to judge a distance. The eldest stares at the weathercock for minutes together.\n\nOn the second of August she flew: a short, silent drop to the nave roof, where she landed in a heap and looked about to see who had noticed. By the tenth all five had made the journey to the yew and back. They still snore for food, and are still fed. I continue to sweep up pellets weekly. The count stands at two hundred and thirty-one. Mr. Crabbe reports a tenth pigeon missing."],["Rain, and Mr. Crabbe's Pigeons","Paper the eleventh of Miss Verity Holloway, read to the Emberwick Field Club in a wet August.\n\nIt rained for six days. This is hard on barn owls. Their soft plumage, so useful for silence, soaks through like a blanket, and rain drowns the small sounds they hunt by. The belfry family sat hunched and hungry.\n\nOn the seventh day the miller sent word. Would Mr. Crabbe kindly fetch his pigeons? Ten of them, wearing his own red rings, have been living under the mill roof, some since Midwinter, growing fat on spilt grain and declining to go home. The miller's daughter, who watches foxes from her window, had counted them weeks ago. So no pigeon has been eaten by anything. Mr. Crabbe collected them in a basket and was very quiet. The pellet count will nonetheless be completed, for the sake of method."],["The Verdict of the Pellets","Twelfth and last paper of Miss Verity Holloway, organist, read to the Emberwick Field Club at its September meeting.\n\nBetween February and harvest I collected two hundred and ninety pellets from beneath the barn owls in the church belfry. They held the remains of nine hundred and eighty-six animals: field voles, 512; common shrews, 241; wood mice, 148; bank voles, 52; pygmy shrews, 21; young rats, 7; frogs, 3; sparrows, 2. Pigeons, none.\n\nOur owls, then, are the best mousers in the parish. Mr. Crabbe has handsomely withdrawn his motion, and has built a nesting box for the tithe barn with his own hands. The five young owls left the tower this month to find barns of their own. The old pair roost behind the tenor bell as before. I heard them go out last night during the final hymn, or rather, I did not hear them."]],[["Setting Out from Highgarth","I am Ada Thornley, and I study animals that walk on their toenails. This October I have leave to travel with Jory Blackthwaite's drove: six score fell cattle, to be walked from Highgarth in the Northern Fells down to the autumn fair at Emberwick, twelve days by the green road. I mean to note every hoofed and horned beast on the way.\n\nThe cattle come first. They are small, black and shaggy, with horns that sweep out and up, a yard from tip to tip. A horn is a sheath, like a fingernail, grown over a core of bone. It is never shed, and grows all the animal's life.\n\nTheir hooves are cloven: two toes, each in its own hard shoe, which spread on soft ground and grip on rock. Jory counted them through the gate with a notched stick. His dog Tansy counted them again, to be sure."],["The Fell Ponies","Second day on the drove road from Highgarth to Emberwick. Our baggage rides on four fell ponies, and I, Ada Thornley, have walked behind them, studying their feet.\n\nA pony does not have a cloven hoof. It stands on one toe to each leg, the middle one, and the hoof is that toe's nail grown thick enough to bear the whole animal. The cattle ahead of us go on two toes; the ponies on one. It is the first thing I look for in any track.\n\nThese ponies are black and brown, deep in the body, with long manes and feathered heels. They have never been shod. The stony fell wears the hoof down as fast as it grows. Jory Blackthwaite, the drover, says a fell pony never puts a foot wrong in a bog. I followed the bay mare's line across Mirk Moss and stayed dry."],["The Hefted Sheep","Third day of Jory Blackthwaite's cattle drove, crossing Hagg Moor in the Northern Fells. There are no walls up here, yet the sheep stay on their own ground. I, Ada Thornley, asked how.\n\nThey are hefted, said Jory. Each ewe learned her patch of fell from her mother, and will teach it to her lamb: where the shelter is, where the first grass comes. Sell the farm and the flock is sold with it, because the sheep know the hill and a new flock would not.\n\nThey are grey-fleeced and white-faced, and both sexes are horned. A ram's horns curl round beside his cheeks in a full turn. Each horn is ringed like a tree, a ridge for every winter when growth slowed. I counted six on an old ram who stood on a rock to watch a hundred and twenty cattle walk through his parlour. He did not approve."],["Wild Goats on Kidstone Scar","On the fourth day the drove road passes under Kidstone Scar, the last crag of the Northern Fells. Jory Blackthwaite the drover pointed with his stick. I, Ada Thornley, saw nothing; then a boulder got up and walked along a ledge no wider than a bookshelf.\n\nWild goats have lived on the scar longer than anyone can say, run off from farms generations ago. There were eleven. They are shaggy, grey and black and piebald, with beards, and the billies carry ridged horns that sweep back like scythes.\n\nA goat's hoof is the secret of the ledge. The outer rim is hard and sharp and bites on rock; the sole inside is soft and grips like a thumb. The two toes work separately. The billy looked down at our cattle picking along the road, and I believe I have never been so thoroughly despised. The smell arrived a minute later."],["The Roaring","Fifth night out from Highgarth with Jory Blackthwaite's cattle, camped at the head of Stag Dale. Nobody slept. From dusk the hillsides roared, a deep, groaning bellow that the cattle answered uneasily. I, Ada Thornley, was delighted.\n\nOctober is the red deer rut. At first light I watched a stag on the far slope, thick-necked and dark from wallowing in peat, with a dozen hinds below him. He roared with his head stretched out and his breath smoking. A second stag came down the ridge. They walked side by side for a while, stiff-legged, each measuring the other. Then the newcomer thought better of it and left.\n\nThat is how most challenges end. The roaring and the walk let each judge the other's size without a fight. The stag I watched had twelve points to his antlers. Jory's only remark was that he wished they would do it by day."],["A Cast Antler","Sixth day, coming down through the birches of Stag Dale behind Jory Blackthwaite's cattle. The dog Tansy brought me something from the bracken, and I, Ada Thornley, carried it the rest of the day: a red deer's antler, four points, grey with weather.\n\nAn antler is not a horn. A horn is a hollow sheath, kept for life. An antler is solid bone, and it falls off every spring. A new one grows through the summer under a skin of fur called velvet, faster than any other bone. By autumn the velvet is rubbed away and the antler is hard and ready for the rut. Only the stags have them.\n\nThis one had tooth marks along the beam. Deer gnaw their own cast antlers, and so do mice, for the lime in them. Nothing on the fell is wasted. Jory says it will make me a good coat peg."],["Shoes for Cattle","Seventh day. The green road from the Northern Fells ends at Garrow's Forge, and from here to Emberwick the way is stony lane. Jory Blackthwaite's cattle have soft feet. So for a day and a half the drove stood in the paddock, and I, Ada Thornley, watched six score cattle being shod.\n\nA cow cannot wear a horseshoe. Her hoof is in two halves that move apart as she walks, so each half has its own iron plate, shaped like a comma: eight to a beast. Mrs. Garrow the smith nails them on while her son steadies the animal, and they finish one in the time it takes me to sharpen a pencil.\n\nNine hundred and sixty plates. The nails go only through the hard outer wall, which has no more feeling than a fingernail. The cattle minded the waiting more than the shoeing. So did Jory."],["The Fallow Deer of Hollin Park","Eighth day of the drove, and the first in the Lowlands. The lane runs for two miles along the paling of Hollin Park, and while Jory Blackthwaite walked his newly shod cattle past, I, Ada Thornley, walked with my nose between the bars.\n\nFallow deer, a herd of sixty under the oaks. They are smaller than the red deer of the fells, and prettier: chestnut dappled with white spots, with a black-edged white rump and a long tail that never stops flicking. Some are nearly black, and one doe was cream all over.\n\nThe bucks' antlers are like no other deer's. Above the first points they broaden into a flat blade, like an open hand with short fingers. It is their rut too. A buck stood under a chestnut making a noise between a groan and a belch, with great seriousness. The does went on eating acorns."],["Pigs Among the Oaks","Ninth day on the road with Jory Blackthwaite's drove. In Thicket Wood, half a day north of Emberwick, we met forty pigs walking the other way, in the charge of a boy with a horn. I, Ada Thornley, asked the cattle to wait.\n\nIn October the Lowland villages turn their pigs into the oak woods to fatten on fallen acorns. They were sandy and black-spotted, lop-eared, and wholly happy. A pig's foot is cloven like a cow's, with two smaller toes behind. No horns: instead the boars grow tusks, which are teeth that never stop growing.\n\nThe snout is the wonder, a tough disc with a nose's sense of smell and a spade's strength. One sow turned over a yard of leaf mould while I found the right page. Too many acorns are bad for ponies and cattle, Jory says, so the pigs do everyone a kindness."],["Oxen at the Plough","Tenth day. The Lowland fields along the lane to Emberwick are being ploughed for winter wheat, and at Long Furlong farm the plough was drawn by four red oxen. Jory Blackthwaite rested his drove at the gate. I, Ada Thornley, went in.\n\nAn ox is slower than a horse, and does not mind. These four leaned into the yoke and walked the furrow at the pace of a man thinking. Their horns are wide and upturned, tipped with brass knobs. The ploughboy called each by name, and each turned at its own.\n\nAt the headland they stood and chewed. Cattle, sheep, goats and deer all swallow their grass half chewed, bring it back up later, and chew it at leisure. It lets them eat fast in the open and digest in safety. The near ox chewed forty-one times, swallowed, and began on the next mouthful. I envied him."],["Reading the Mud","Eleventh evening. Jory Blackthwaite's drove is camped at Stanner Ford on the river Ember, one short day north of Emberwick, and the bank is a page of mud. I, Ada Thornley, read it before the light went.\n\nOur cattle left paired half-moons, with the iron plates showing as a sharp edge. The ponies left single rounds. Between them ran the narrower prints of sheep. A neat little pair no longer than my thumb joint, the toes close together, was a roe deer come to drink at dawn. Deeper, with two extra dots behind each print, a pig.\n\nTwo toes or one: every hoofed beast I have met in eleven days is one or the other. Then Tansy the dog walked across the page, and I shut the notebook. Tomorrow the cattle are sold. I find I know several by face, which Jory warned me against on the first day."],["The October Fair","Twelfth day. Jory Blackthwaite's drove came into Emberwick by the mill bridge: six score black cattle from Highgarth, all present, iron-shod, with the dog Tansy at the rear. I, Ada Thornley, walked in behind them covered in dust.\n\nThe fair filled the green. There were Lowland cattle, penned sheep, ponies trotted up and down, a goat eating a notice. A horner had a stall of combs, spoons and lantern panes, made of cow horn heated soft, pressed flat and scraped thin enough to let light through. I bought a comb.\n\nBy noon Jory had sold the lot to a Lowland grazier with good water meadows, which pleased us both. He goes home tomorrow with the ponies. I have a red deer's antler, a horn comb and a full notebook, which the library has asked to keep. Eight kinds of hoofed beast, by my count, and twelve days well walked."]],[["The Empty Twelfth Plate","My grandfather Fenwick Orrell painted signs on Tallow Lane in Emberwick, and on his free days he painted clouds. His catalogue has twelve plates. Eleven are finished. The twelfth is an empty ruled frame with one pencil line beneath it: Night-shiner. Seen once, midsummer, due north, past midnight. Not believed.\n\nI am Tamsin Orrell, and the shop, the brushes and the catalogue are mine now. This July I have resolved to stand under each of his clouds in turn, a month apiece, and to fill that frame if the sky allows.\n\nPlate one is the easy one, the bread-loaf cloud: flat grey base, white domed top, a whole bakery of them drifting over the Lowlands by noon. They rise where the warm fields breathe upward, and they flatten and vanish by evening. Fair weather. I ate my lunch under forty of them."],["The Anvil over Harrow Fell","August in Emberwick, and plate two of my grandfather's cloud catalogue: the anvil. I am Tamsin Orrell, sign painter of Tallow Lane, working through his eleven painted clouds toward the twelfth he never finished.\n\nThe anvil begins as an ordinary bread-loaf cloud that refuses to stop. By two o'clock it had heaped itself higher than Harrow Fell; by three its top had struck some ceiling in the air and spread flat, exactly like the smith's anvil on Cooper Street. Underneath, it went the colour of a ripe plum.\n\nI counted from flash to thunder, five slow counts to the mile, as Grandfather taught me. Fifteen, then ten, then four. At four I carried my easel indoors. The hail that followed rattled the shop sign and was over in six minutes. His plate shows lilac in the shadow. He was right; I had always thought it fanciful."],["Mare's Tails","September. From the flat roof of the sign shop on Tallow Lane I can see the whole western sky, and this morning it was combed. Long white wisps, each hooked at one end like a hair caught on a brush, streamed in from the Grey Coast far above every other cloud. These are mare's tails, plate three in my grandfather Fenwick Orrell's catalogue, which I am following through the year.\n\nThey are made of ice, he wrote, and they sit so high that the wind up there has nothing to do with the wind down here. Beneath the plate is a rule in his square hand: rain in a day and a half.\n\nI put it to the test with the shop clock. The first drops struck the skylight thirty-one hours later. I painted the tails as he did, with a nearly dry brush dragged once and never touched again."],["A Cloud Sat Down","My grandfather counted fog as a cloud, plate four of his catalogue, on the grounds that a cloud which has sat down is a cloud all the same. So in October I, Tamsin Orrell, left my sign shop in Emberwick before dawn and walked out along the marsh causeway to stand inside one.\n\nThere is nothing to see from within. That is the lesson. The world shrank to twenty paces of wet planks, a heron's complaint, and beads of water gathering on my sleeve and eyelashes. Fog is made of drops too small to fall.\n\nThen I climbed the ladder of the old drainage mill and came out above it. The fog lay flat and white as a lake to the horizon, with the mill's sails and three poplars standing up out of it. By ten the sun had burned it to rags. I drew fast."],["The Slate Sheet","November's cloud is the one nobody loves. Plate five in the catalogue left to me by my grandfather, Fenwick Orrell, is a plain grey rectangle with one Emberwick chimney at the bottom for scale. He called it the slate sheet: a low, even layer with no edges, no towers and no intentions, which sat over Tallow Lane for nine days running.\n\nI nearly skipped it. A sign painter likes a shape. But I had promised myself every plate, so I took my stool to the roof and looked properly.\n\nIt is not one grey. There is a pearl patch where the sun stands behind it, a darker band toward the fells whose tops it had swallowed, and a slow grain like brushed wool. It gives drizzle, never real rain. On the ninth day I mixed eleven greys before I was satisfied, and I felt oddly fond of it."],["The Rain-Bearer","Plate six of my grandfather's cloud catalogue is spotted, and for years I took the spots for mildew. They are rain. He painted the rain-bearer out of doors, and it rained on him.\n\nI am Tamsin Orrell of Tallow Lane, Emberwick, and in December I did the same. The rain-bearer is thicker and darker than the slate sheet of last month, a deep wet ceiling with a ragged underside, and it means a whole day of weather. The lamps in the shop were lit at noon. Shreds of lower cloud hurried beneath it like late clerks.\n\nAt three the rain turned quiet, and I looked up to find it had become snow. I caught flakes on my dark sleeve: six points, every one, no two the same. My own plate has spots on it now. I have decided to leave them."],["The Veil and the Ring","January, clear and bitter, and then on the fourteenth the stars over Emberwick went soft. A thin milky veil had drawn across the whole sky, so fine that the moon shone through it and cast shadows. Around the moon stood a pale ring.\n\nThis is plate seven of the catalogue my grandfather Fenwick Orrell began and I, his granddaughter Tamsin, am finishing. The veil is ice, like mare's tails, but spread into a sheet. The ring is the moonlight bent through it. I measured it from the shop roof: one outstretched hand, thumb to little finger, from the moon to the ring, all the way round.\n\nHis note says: ring round the moon, snow by the second morning. It came by the first evening, four inches of it. The veil is honest, I find, but it does not keep exact appointments."],["Harrow Fell Wears a Hat","For plate eight I had to leave Emberwick. My grandfather's cloud catalogue shows a smooth white lens poised above a mountain, and that cloud, the hat, forms only where a strong wind is made to climb. So in February I rode the carrier's cart north to Fellfoot with my paintbox on my knees.\n\nIt was there when I arrived, sitting a little above the summit of Harrow Fell like a lid lifted off a pot. A gale was blowing. The hat did not move. I watched for two hours and understood at last: the wind pours through it, and the cloud is forever being made at the front edge and unmade at the back.\n\nA shepherd stopped beside my easel. When Harrow wears his hat, he said, tie your own on. Mine was already in the beck. I, Tamsin Orrell, painted bareheaded."],["Fish-Scale Sky","On the Grey Coast they call it a herring sky. My grandfather, Fenwick Orrell, called it fish-scale and made it plate nine of his catalogue of clouds: hundreds of tiny white ripples, very high, laid in rows as neat as the flank of a fish. I am his granddaughter Tamsin, and I saw it in March from the harbour wall at Skerry Point, where I had gone to repaint a chandler's sign.\n\nEach scale is smaller than my little fingernail held at arm's length. That is the test; anything bigger is another cloud altogether.\n\nIt does not last. Within the hour the rows had blurred and thickened. A woman gutting fish beside me said scales mean a change of wind and nothing settled, not long wet and not long dry. I dabbed mine in with the tip of one finger, which is how Grandfather did his. The prints are there."],["The Dairy","Plate ten is the rare one among the finished eleven. My grandfather called it the dairy. In his catalogue of clouds it carries a note: wait behind the storm, face east, be quick.\n\nI am Tamsin Orrell, sign painter of Emberwick, and I waited all April. On the twenty-sixth a thunderstorm crossed the town at teatime and rumbled off toward the Lowlands. I ran up to the shop roof and faced east as instructed.\n\nThe underside of the storm's great shelf had let down pouches, dozens of them, round and soft as a row of udders. The low sun behind me lit each one gold with a bruise-blue shadow. Nothing I have painted on a signboard is as strange. They lasted eleven minutes by the church clock. Nine months of clouds had taught me speed, and I needed all of it. The paint was still wet when they smoothed away."],["The Flock","May over Emberwick, and plate eleven: the flock. It is a sheet of rounded white tufts with blue lanes between, drifting together at a middling height like sheep let out onto a hill. Each tuft is about the width of my thumb at arm's length, far larger than the fish-scales of March. At sunset they are lit from beneath, and the whole flock turns pink at once.\n\nThat completes the eleven clouds my grandfather Fenwick Orrell painted. I have stood under every one. There remains the twelfth frame, empty, and his pencil note: Night-shiner. Seen once, midsummer, due north, past midnight. Not believed.\n\nSo I, Tamsin, have changed my hours. I sleep after dinner and climb to the roof at eleven with a flask and a blanket. The nights are short and never fully dark now. North is a row of chimneys and a pale glow. Nothing yet."],["The Night-Shiner","Twelve nights I sat on the roof of the sign shop on Tallow Lane, facing north over the Emberwick chimneys. On the thirteenth, the twenty-first of June, at ten minutes to one, it came. Low in the north, where the midsummer glow never quite leaves, a web of fine ripples was shining silver-blue. Ordinary clouds are black at that hour. These were bright, because they float so far above all the others that the sun, well below the edge of the world, still reaches them.\n\nMy grandfather saw this once and was not believed. So I ran down and fetched Mr Pell the baker, who was already up with his dough. He stood a long while with flour on his arms and then signed my page as witness.\n\nThe twelfth plate is painted. Under Fenwick Orrell's pencil line his granddaughter Tamsin has added three words: Seen again. Believed."]],[["The Wager at Skerry Point","I am Maren Coldwick, signal-keeper at Skerry Point on the Grey Coast. When a gale is coming I hoist a black canvas cone on the mast so that the eleven boats of the fleet stay in. This September the town council sent me a new barometer, a handsome glass in a brass case, and old Jory Pike laughed at it.\n\nJory fished here for fifty years. He says he can smell a storm, and the gulls tell him the rest. So we have a wager, with Mrs Furze the harbourmaster as judge: a point for each storm called before it strikes, from now until spring. The loser paints the signal mast. The winner eats a herring supper at the loser's expense.\n\nFirst blood to the glass. It fell all Monday; Jory said fair. On Tuesday the wind took every sheet off every washing line in the village."],["Thunder from the Land","Skerry Point, late September. Storm the second of the season came from the wrong direction entirely, and it has cost my barometer its lead.\n\nThe afternoon was hot and still. The glass in its brass case had not moved a hair since breakfast, and I, Maren Coldwick, signal-keeper, told Jory Pike so. He was sitting on an upturned crab pot, sniffing. Thunder by four, he said. The cows on the cliff field were all lying down, the gulls had gone quiet, and the air smelled of pennies.\n\nAt ten to four a cloud like a black cliff came over the hill from inland. Lightning struck the sea twice beyond the harbour bar. Rain fell so hard it bounced knee-high off the quay, and in half an hour the sun was out again. Mrs Furze, who judges our wager, has entered it in the book. One point each. Jory is unbearable."],["The Equinox Gale","The third storm of our wager needed no cleverness from anybody. I am Maren Coldwick, keeper of the signal mast at Skerry Point, matching my new barometer against Jory Pike's nose, and this time both were right.\n\nThe glass began to sink on Thursday noon and kept sinking. Jory came up the path at two to say the gulls were walking about in the turnip field like hens. I hoisted the black cone at three. All eleven boats stayed on their moorings.\n\nIt blew from the south-west for a night and a day. Spray came clean over the harbour wall and salted my windows on the landward side. Old hands call it the equinox gale and expect it yearly, as one expects an aunt. Mrs Furze, our judge, awards a point each. Two all. The mast still wants painting, and neither of us intends to do it."],["Swell without Wind","October at Skerry Point, flat calm and thick fog, and the barometer steady as a church. I am Maren Coldwick, signal-keeper, and I told Jory Pike there was nothing to call.\n\nHe stood on the quay with his head on one side, listening. The sea is breathing long, he said. Sure enough, between the small slaps of the harbour water there came, every quarter minute, a slow heavy heave against the outer wall. A storm was out there, hundreds of miles off, and had sent its swell ahead like a calling card.\n\nNo wind ever reached us. But by evening great smooth hills of water were rolling through the fog, and they smashed nine lobster pots on the skerries. Mrs Furze, who judges our wager, ruled that a storm you can hear counts as a storm. Jory three, the glass two. I have learned to listen for the long breath."],["The Glass Is Avenged","Storm the fifth, the second week of November, and a good day for brass and mercury. I keep the signal mast at Skerry Point on the Grey Coast; my name is Maren Coldwick; and for once Jory Pike was plainly, splendidly wrong.\n\nThe morning was bright. Gulls bobbed on the water like bath toys, which Jory holds to be a certain sign of settled weather. He told the whole quay the boats might go out. But my barometer had dropped three tenths of an inch between supper and breakfast, faster than I had yet seen it fall, and I hoisted the cone anyway.\n\nThe fleet grumbled and stayed. By two o'clock a south-westerly was tearing slates off the net loft. Jory watched from my window with a mug of tea and said the gulls had deceived him personally. Mrs Furze marked the wager book: three all."],["A Cone for Nothing","I am Maren Coldwick, signal-keeper at Skerry Point, and this page is a confession. In late November my barometer fell steeply, just as it had before the last gale. I hoisted the black cone. Eleven boats stayed in harbour on what should have been the best herring day of the month.\n\nNothing came. It rained, gently, straight down. A child could have sailed a washtub to the skerries and back. Jory Pike had said so at dawn: the gulls were far out at sea and his knee was quiet.\n\nI have since learned that the glass falls for rain as well as wind, and that it is the speed of the fall, together with the sky, that tells them apart. Mrs Furze ruled that a false alarm hands the point to the other side. Jory four, glass three. The fleet forgave me by Thursday. Jory has not stopped smiling yet."],["Snow from the North","December on the Grey Coast. The seventh storm of the season came down from the Northern Fells instead of in from the sea, and it came white.\n\nTwo nights earlier Jory Pike had pointed out a ring around the moon, and that afternoon he showed me geese flying south very low and in a hurry. My barometer agreed with him for once, falling slowly and steadily for a day and a half. I, Maren Coldwick, hoisted the cone at Skerry Point with stiff fingers, and we were both right.\n\nThe wind swung north and the snow drove sideways for nine hours. In the morning the boats wore white caps, the mast had a drift up its windward side, and the harbour was skinned with slush that rose and fell like porridge. Our judge, Mrs Furze, gave a point each. Jory five, glass four. We dug out her door together."],["The Glazed Mast","Neither of us called the eighth storm, and I record it for honesty's sake. I am Maren Coldwick, signal-keeper at Skerry Point, and I have a wager running between my barometer and old Jory Pike's weather sense.\n\nOn a still January night rain fell through freezing air and froze where it landed. There was no wind and scarcely a movement of the glass. Jory's gulls gave no sign. We woke to a village dipped in clear ice: every rope, every rung, every blade of grass on the cliff. The signal halyards were as thick as my wrist and the cone was frozen to the mast.\n\nI spent the morning tapping ice off the rigging with a wooden mallet, while Jory held the ladder and offered advice. Twigs tinkled like teaspoons. Mrs Furze awarded no points and slid most of the way home. The score stands at five to four."],["A Gale in the Night","February at Skerry Point. Jory Pike was in bed with a cold, wrapped in three jerseys, and sent word by his granddaughter that the weather would hold. He could not smell anything at all, which I suspect was the trouble.\n\nI am Maren Coldwick, and I keep the signal mast and the barometer. At dusk the glass was dropping so quickly that I could see the difference between one tapping and the next. I hoisted the cone by lantern light and went along the quay knocking on doors.\n\nThe gale arrived at midnight, shrieked for four hours, and left before breakfast. It was short and sharp, as fast-falling storms are. A dinghy was found in the churchyard, unharmed. Mrs Furze brought the wager book to Jory's bedside. Five all. He said it was unsporting to hold a storm while a man had no nose. I took him soup anyway."],["Hail on the Quay","March, and the tenth storm of the wager between my barometer and Jory Pike. I am Maren Coldwick of the signal station at Skerry Point, and I lost this one fairly.\n\nIt was a morning of sunshine and sharp showers. Jory, recovered from his cold, studied a line of tall clouds out to sea, white on top and dark as slate beneath, all leaning the same way. Squall by noon, he said, and hail in it. My glass showed nothing worth a cone.\n\nIt struck at ten to twelve: a wall of wind, one crack of thunder, and hailstones the size of dried peas that drummed on the upturned boats and gathered in the gutters like spilled sugar. Only as it hit did the barometer give a little startled jump. A squall is too small and quick for it. Jory six, glass five, and two weeks to go."],["Gulls on the Water","I am Maren Coldwick, signal-keeper at Skerry Point, and with spring a fortnight off my barometer stood one point behind Jory Pike in our season's wager.\n\nOn the twentieth of March the glass began to fall. Jory would not have it. The gulls were riding the swell beyond the bar, he said, and the sky was clean. I showed him the brass pointer. He showed me the gulls. We stood on the quay disagreeing until Mrs Furze, our judge, told us both to go home.\n\nI hoisted the cone on the strength of the glass alone, and felt lonely doing it. The wind got up after dark and blew a full gale from the west until morning. No boat was out. Six all. Jory came up the path at first light to say so himself, which I thought handsome of him. The glass, I noticed, had not risen again."],["The Great Blow","The last storm of the season at Skerry Point was the worst, and nobody could have missed it. My barometer sank lower than I, Maren Coldwick, had ever seen it, off the bottom of the printed scale. At the same hour Jory Pike arrived breathless to report gulls walking up the village street, inland, in single file.\n\nWe hoisted the cone together, and the drum beneath it, which means stay in and mean it. It blew for two days. The sea came over the wall and into the net loft. When it cleared, all eleven boats were afloat and every soul was at home.\n\nMrs Furze closed the wager book: seven all, a draw. So Jory and I painted the signal mast together, he holding the ladder, and split one herring supper. I read the glass first now. Then I ask Jory."]],[["The Clerk of Frosts","I am Wilmot Garrick, sixteen, and since September I have been bound apprentice to Mistress Edda Thornlow, Frost Clerk of the Lowlands. Her cottage at Hollow Ash holds forty ledgers, one for each winter she has watched. Every orchard and market garden between here and Emberwick plants by them.\n\nThe tables are simple to look at. For each year: the date of the first frost, the date of the last, and the count of frost nights between. The work behind them is a reading at dawn and another at dusk, every day.\n\nShe showed me the instruments. One thermometer hangs in a white slatted box at the height of my chin, to take the air. Another lies on two pegs just above the grass. They will disagree, she said, and when you understand why, you may hold the pen. Until then I carry the lantern and rule the lines."],["Frost on the Grass","The first entry of year forty-one in the Frost Tables was made on the third of October, and I, Wilmot Garrick, apprentice, very nearly missed it.\n\nAt dawn the thermometer in the white box at Hollow Ash stood four degrees above freezing. No frost, I called. Mistress Thornlow, the Frost Clerk, sent me back out to read the one lying over the grass. It showed two degrees below. My boots left dark green prints across the stiff lawn.\n\nThis is a ground frost. On a clear, still night the earth gives up its warmth to the sky, and the coldest air lies in a skin against the grass, while a few feet higher it stays mild. Beans feel it. Apple boughs do not. She entered it in the ground column. Two columns, she said, because a strawberry and a plum live at different heights."],["Dipper's Bottom","On the nineteenth of October the air thermometer at Hollow Ash touched freezing, and the dahlias by the cottage gate turned black in a night. First air frost, year forty-one. Mistress Thornlow, Frost Clerk of the Lowlands, entered it; I, her apprentice Wilmot Garrick, ruled the line.\n\nThen she walked me down to Dipper's Bottom, the dip in the low field, carrying a third thermometer. It read five degrees colder than the garden, two hundred paces away.\n\nCold air is heavy, she explained. On a still night it runs downhill like water and gathers in the hollows, and a hedge across a slope will dam it into a pool. That is why old orchards stand on the hillsides and never in the bottoms. I stood in the hollow and felt the cold lying around my ankles. I had thought all of a field was one weather."],["Hoar on the Gatepost","November at Hollow Ash in the Lowlands, and my third month as apprentice to the Frost Clerk. This morning every gate, thistle and spider's web was furred with white, and Mistress Thornlow handed me her reading glass before I had my coat buttoned.\n\nHoar frost is not frozen dew. It grows. The moisture in the air settles straight onto anything cold as ice, crystal upon crystal, into feathers and needles and tiny fern fronds. Through the glass, a fence rail was a forest.\n\nIt needs three things, and she made me, Wilmot Garrick, recite them: a clear sky, still air, and a long night. Cloud is a blanket; under cloud the ground keeps its warmth. Wind stirs the cold skin away. I measured the longest needles on the gatepost at half an inch and entered the figure in the remarks column myself. My first ink in the tables."],["Rime on the Windward Side","I am Wilmot Garrick, apprentice to Mistress Edda Thornlow, who keeps the Frost Tables for the Lowlands. In December a freezing fog sat over Hollow Ash for three days, and I learned that I had been using one word for two things.\n\nThe white that grew on the hedges was not hoar. Hoar comes on clear nights and coats a twig evenly, all round. This was rime: the fog's own droplets, blown against cold wood and frozen where they struck. It builds only on the side the wind comes from, in rough white combs, each tooth pointing into the breeze.\n\nSo you can read the wind after it has gone, she said, and had me walk the lane with a compass. Every comb pointed north-east. By the third day they were as long as my finger. Rime, north-east, three days, I wrote, and she let the entry stand."],["Ferns on the Glass","Midwinter at Hollow Ash, the Frost Clerk's cottage in the Lowlands. I woke to find my attic window grown over with white ferns, and spent a quarter of an hour admiring them before I, Wilmot Garrick, remembered the dawn reading.\n\nMistress Thornlow says window frost is the warm, damp breath of the house freezing on cold glass, and that it follows the scratches and specks of dust. A clean pane grows plainer ferns. I have not cleaned mine.\n\nThis is the season when the letters come. A nurseryman at Emberwick asks how late he has ever dared set out his beans. A widow with six plum trees asks which wall is safest. She answers each from the forty ledgers and I copy the replies fair. The tables, I begin to see, are forty years of somebody getting up early on behalf of strangers."],["Bearing Ice","January brought eleven frost nights running to Hollow Ash, and Mistress Thornlow, the Frost Clerk, sent me out with an auger. I am her apprentice, Wilmot Garrick, and the tables have a column I had not noticed: ice on the mill pond.\n\nI bored through at the same post each morning. One inch, then two, then three and a half. She will not let the village children skate until it reaches four, clear and dark; white, bubbled ice counts for half. On the day it passed, she hung a blue rag on the pond gate, and by noon the whole school was out.\n\nThe frost goes down into the soil as well. I dug in the bean plot and found the ground hard as brick for the depth of my hand. Beneath that the earth was soft, and a worm was curled up there, waiting it out."],["The Black Frost","I am Wilmot Garrick, apprentice at Hollow Ash, where the Frost Tables of the Lowlands are kept. In February Mistress Thornlow took a cough, and for nine mornings I read the thermometers alone and carried the figures up to her on a slate.\n\nOn the fourth morning I thought the instruments had broken. The air stood eight degrees below freezing, and there was no frost to be seen. No white on the grass, none on the gate. The fields were dark and dry, and a thin wind came steadily from the east.\n\nThat is a black frost, she said from her pillows. The air is too dry to make crystals, so the cold arrives with nothing to show for itself. It is the kind that splits bark and kills the winter greens. Never trust your eyes over the glass. I entered it myself in my best hand."],["The Ground Heaves","March at Hollow Ash in the Lowlands: thaw by day, frost by night, and the Frost Clerk on her feet again. Mistress Thornlow set me, Wilmot Garrick, her apprentice, to walk the garden each morning and write down what had moved.\n\nA good deal had. Wet soil swells as it freezes. Night after night it lifts whatever sits in it, and by day it sinks back, but never quite to where it began. The autumn-sown onions were standing on their roots like stilts. The gatepost leaned a thumb's width further than in February.\n\nIn the bare bed by the well I found the cause in miniature: bundles of fine ice needles, an inch tall, each carrying a crumb of earth on its tip like a hat. I pressed the onions back with my heel, as she showed me. The tables have a remarks column for all of this."],["Blossom and the White Flag","April. The plum orchards of the Lowlands are in flower, and at Hollow Ash the Frost Tables have become the most important books in the district. I am Wilmot Garrick, apprentice to the Frost Clerk, and I now understand what the winter was practice for.\n\nA frost in January harms nobody. A frost on open blossom takes the year's fruit in a night. In forty years, Mistress Thornlow's tables show, the last frost has fallen as early as the ninth of April and as late as the twenty-second of May.\n\nSo each evening we judge the night ahead, and if we fear frost I run a white flag up the pole on the cottage roof. It can be seen from five farms, and they pass the word on. I have raised it twice this month. Both times she decided. Next time, she says, I shall."],["The Night of the Fourteenth","On the evening of the thirteenth of May the sky over Hollow Ash cleared from the north, and the wind dropped to nothing. Mistress Thornlow, Frost Clerk of the Lowlands, sat down in her chair and looked at me. I am Wilmot Garrick, her apprentice. The decision was mine.\n\nI went through it as she taught me. Clear sky: no blanket. Still air: nothing to stir the cold away. At sunset the thermometer was already falling a degree every half hour, and the grass was dry. Frost by three in the morning, I said, and hard in the hollows. I ran up the white flag.\n\nLanterns moved in the orchards all night. The growers lit heaps of damp straw between the rows so that smoke lay over the blossom like a quilt. At dawn the thermometer over the grass read three below. Most of the plums were saved."],["The Line under the Year","The frost of the fourteenth of May was the last. We waited three weeks to be sure, as the rule requires, and on a warm June morning at Hollow Ash Mistress Edda Thornlow opened the Frost Tables at year forty-one and held out the pen to me, Wilmot Garrick, her apprentice.\n\nI wrote the summary. First ground frost, the third of October. First air frost, the nineteenth of October. Last frost, the fourteenth of May. Frost nights, fifty-eight. Remarks: one black frost; white flag raised three times; the plums of the Lowlands mostly saved.\n\nThen I ruled a line under the year, and she had me put my initials beside hers, small, in the corner. The thermometers will disagree again in autumn, and I know why now. She has ordered a new ledger from the binder in Emberwick. There is room on the shelf for forty more."]],[["The Fellman","I am Linnet Garrow, journeyman letter-cutter, and I came to Emberwick this spring a stranger. The town council has given me a round slab of fell slate, to be set in the market square. On it I am to cut a wind-rose with twelve points, and at each point the name of the wind that blows from it. The true name, the clerk said. Whatever people actually call it.\n\nNobody in the council chamber could tell me more than four. So I am to walk the district and ask.\n\nI began with the north, at a shepherd's hut below Harrow Fell. They call the north wind the Fellman there. It comes down off the tops cold, dry and clear, and you can see every wall for ten miles when it blows. A good drying wind, the shepherd said, and a liar about how warm the sun is."],["The Whittler","The second point of my wind-rose lies a little east of north. I am Linnet Garrow, letter-cutter, collecting the names of the twelve winds for a slate that will lie in Emberwick market square, and for this one I climbed to the slate quarry at Cold Rigg in the Northern Fells.\n\nThe quarrymen call it the Whittler. It is a thin wind, they say, never strong, that finds the gap between cuff and glove and pares a man down a shaving at a time. It blows into April and brings small hard snow that rattles like rice.\n\nI felt it myself. It came round the corner of the cutting shed and went straight through two coats. The foreman's wife gave me a hot stone for my pocket and spelled the name for me, though she had never seen it written. Two names in the notebook. Ten to find."],["The Dry Aunt","East by north of Emberwick the land flattens toward the Lowlands, and there, at a farm called Stonecrop, I found the third wind for my rose. I am Linnet Garrow, letter-cutter, hired by the town to carve the names of the twelve winds as local people say them.\n\nThe farmer's mother called this one the Dry Aunt, and would not explain until I had drunk a cup of tea. It comes in spring, she said. It brings no rain. It cracks lips, lifts the seed bed off in dust and puts everyone out of temper, and yet the washing dries in an hour and you cannot fault its housekeeping. Just like an aunt of hers.\n\nBack in Emberwick, in Dunstan Crake's yard, where the slab lies on trestles, I cut the first three names. The slate is sound and takes a clean edge."],["The Miller's Friend","Due east of Emberwick the Lowlands are dotted with windmills, and I supposed the millers would have a name for the east wind. They did. I am Linnet Garrow, letter-cutter, a third of the way round the wind-rose I am making for the market square.\n\nAt Tolley's Mill they call it the Miller's Friend. It is not a strong wind, but it is the steadiest of the twelve. It will blow from one quarter from Monday to Saturday, and he can set his sails once and grind the whole week without touching them. Gusty winds break gear. This one pays the rent.\n\nHe let me stand on the gallery while the sails went round, each one passing with a soft whoop. Up there I noticed the weathercock had worn a bright groove in its spindle, facing east. I wrote the name on a flour bag."],["The Chaff-Wind","I am Linnet Garrow, cutting a twelve-point wind-rose in slate for Emberwick and walking the country to learn what each wind is called. The fifth point lies south of east, and I reached it at harvest, on the threshing floor of a farm near Barley Cross.\n\nThere they wait for the Chaff-Wind. It is a warm, light, dry breeze of late summer afternoons. The women toss the threshed grain up from baskets; the heavy corn falls back, and the wind carries the husks off in a golden smoke. Too little wind and the chaff falls with the grain. Too much and the grain goes over the hedge.\n\nThey put a basket in my hands. I was bad at it and was laughed at kindly. I had chaff in my hair for two days, and found some in the groove of the letter C when I cut it."],["The Honey Wind","A little east of south from Emberwick lie the orchards and bee gardens of Hollow Ash. I went there in September for the sixth name on my wind-rose. I am Linnet Garrow, the letter-cutter carving the twelve winds in slate for the market square.\n\nAn old beekeeper named Aldith told me this one without being asked. The Honey Wind, she said: soft and warm, coming in May and June when the blossom is open. Her bees fly straight up it to the clover as if along a string, and on a Honey Wind day a hive can gain two pounds.\n\nI was too late in the year to meet it, so she sat me among the hives and described it. I notice that the cold winds are named for what they do to people, and the warm ones for what they bring. Six names. Half the rose."],["Mother Mild","Due south. I am Linnet Garrow, letter-cutter, more than half way round the twelve winds whose names I am to carve for Emberwick, and for the south wind I had only to ask a washerwoman at the Ember bridge, who answered as though I had asked the name of her own sister.\n\nMother Mild. It is the wind that ends a frost. It comes up from the south wet and warm, and in the morning the icicles are dripping, the snow has gone grey and soft, and the lanes are running. It rarely blows hard. It simply leans on the cold until the cold gives way.\n\nIt brings low cloud and fine rain, she said, and chilblains ease, and old dogs come out to lie on the step. I cut the name that week in Dunstan Crake's yard, with the first fog of autumn beading on the slate."],["The Marsh Breath","South-south-west of Emberwick the river Ember loses itself in the marsh, and the wind from that quarter is known in the town by its smell before anything else. I am Linnet Garrow; I am collecting the local names of the twelve winds to cut in a slate wind-rose; and this was the eighth.\n\nThe reed cutters call it the Marsh Breath. It is slow and damp and comes mostly at dusk in autumn, smelling of mud, mint and wet reed. It carries the mist inland with it, a field at a time, so that the marsh seems to be walking into town.\n\nA reed cutter named Hob poled me out in his flat boat to feel it rise. The reeds bowed toward Emberwick all together, and my hair went limp. Bad for bread and fiddle strings, he said, good for eels. Eight names. The slab is two-thirds lettered."],["The Wet Shepherd","I am Linnet Garrow, letter-cutter, and in November I walked out to the Grey Coast to ask after the last winds for the rose I am carving for Emberwick market square. The ninth point lies between south-west and west. At Skerry Point they knew it at once.\n\nThe Wet Shepherd, said the net-menders. It blows for days on end from over the sea, mild and strong, and it drives the rain clouds inland ahead of it in long grey flocks, with hardly a gap to dry a shirt in. Most of the district's rain comes on it. The farmers bless it in a dry summer and nobody blesses it in November.\n\nI stood on the harbour wall and watched three flocks go over inside an hour, each trailing its skirt of rain across the water. Then I walked back very wet, with the name safe inside my hat."],["The Greyback","The west wind on the Grey Coast is the one they speak of quietly. I am Linnet Garrow, letter-cutter, tenth name into a wind-rose of twelve for the town of Emberwick, and I heard this one from the signal-keeper at Skerry Point, beside a mast with a black cone flying.\n\nThe Greyback. It is the gale wind, straight off the open sea, and it takes its name from the long grey waves it sends in, each with a back like a whale. When the Greyback blows, the boats stay on their moorings and the spray goes over the harbour wall.\n\nI saw it do so. I spent the afternoon in the net loft with the crews, who mended and told stories and listened to the roof. A name like that should be cut deep, one of them said. I have given it letters a shade deeper than the rest."],["Salt Tom","After the gale came the eleventh wind, as it always does. I am Linnet Garrow, the letter-cutter collecting the names of the twelve winds for Emberwick's market square, and I was still on the Grey Coast when the weather turned.\n\nThe wind swung round north of west and the sky broke open. Now it was all bright blue gaps and sudden hard showers, sun and hail inside the same quarter hour, and the gulls were flung about like scraps of paper. The children at Skerry Point call this wind Salt Tom. He is cold and rough, but he is playing. He steals caps. He leaves salt on the windows three miles inland.\n\nThey held a contest to see whose cap went furthest. Eleven names. But when I asked anyone about the last point, north by west, they shook their heads. That one does not come here, they said."],["The Keyhole Wind","I came back to Emberwick in December with eleven winds and one blank point, north by west. I am Linnet Garrow, letter-cutter. Shepherds, millers, beekeepers and fishermen had each named the wind that mattered to them, and none of them knew the twelfth.\n\nI confessed as much to Dunstan Crake beside the nearly finished rose. He laughed until he had to sit down. That one is ours, he said. It blows down Tallow Lane on winter evenings and whistles in every lock in town. It is the Keyhole Wind.\n\nI had walked the whole district and never asked at home. I cut the name that night with the wind itself fluting in the yard door. The rose was set in the square on the shortest day. People stand on it and turn to face whatever is blowing, and say its name, and I am no longer a stranger here."]],[["January: The Lantern-Bearer","I am Mabyn Thrale, and I ring the night hours from the clock tower in Emberwick. This January a boy of nine named Tob came to live with his aunt in the house below. He does not sleep well in a strange town. His aunt asked whether he might sit with me on the leads until the ten o'clock bell, and I agreed, if he wore two scarves. So I am writing down the sky for him, a month at a time.\n\nJanuary is the grandest month to begin. Due south at nine stands the Lantern-Bearer: two bright shoulders, two bright knees, and three stars in a row for his coat buttons. Follow the buttons down to the Lantern itself, the brightest star we have, which flashes blue, white and red because it hangs so low. Tob said it looked like a signal. I said it was: winter, all clear."],["February: The Nail and the Ladle","February on the clock tower at Emberwick, cold enough to squeak. I am Mabyn Thrale, the night bell-keeper, and my pupil is Tob, aged nine, who cannot sleep and has decided to learn the sky instead.\n\nThis month I turned him round to face north and showed him the Ladle: seven stars, four for the bowl and three for the bent handle. The two stars at the front of the bowl point straight to a modest star standing alone. That is the Nail. It is not bright, but it is the one star that never moves. Everything else wheels round it once a night, like a cloak swung on a peg.\n\nWe tested it. We lined the Ladle up with the weathercock at eight, and by ten it had swung clear, while the Nail sat exactly where we left it. Tob says he likes knowing one thing will stay put."],["March: Four Minutes a Night","I am Mabyn Thrale, who keeps the night bell in Emberwick, and in March my young neighbour Tob came up the tower stairs indignant. The Lantern-Bearer, whom we met in January in the south, had slid over into the west and was setting behind the brewery before bedtime.\n\nNothing is wrong, I told him. Every star rises four minutes earlier each night. It is too little to notice, but it comes to two hours in a month, and in a year it brings the whole sky round to where it began.\n\nSo the winter stars leave by the west door and the spring ones enter by the east. Rising over the Lowlands this month is the Grey Mare, a backward hook of stars for her neck and a blue-white one, the Mare's Eye, at its foot. Tob has begun a notebook. He wrote: four minutes. Check this."],["April: The Shepherd's Crook","April from the clock tower at Emberwick. The evenings are milder, and Tob, the nine-year-old who shares my watch until the ten o'clock bell, has left off his second scarf. I am Mabyn Thrale, bell-keeper, and I am setting down the sky for him month by month.\n\nThe Ladle now hangs high overhead, upside down, as if pouring. Follow the curve of its handle onward, and you come to an orange star, warm as a coal. We call it the Hearthstone. It is the foot of the Shepherd's Crook, a tall kite of fainter stars.\n\nSpring skies are sparse. Tob complained that there was less to see than in January, and he is right; at this season we look out through the thin side of the heavens. But the Hearthstone is the first star to show in the dusk, and he spotted it before I did."],["May: The Ember Crown","I am Mabyn Thrale, night bell-keeper of Emberwick, and this is the fifth month of the sky as I am teaching it to Tob, aged nine, on the leads of the clock tower.\n\nMay gives us a small thing. High in the east, a hand's breadth from the orange Hearthstone, seven faint stars make a half circle, open at the top like a horseshoe. One of the seven is brighter than the rest, a jewel set off-centre. The town has always called it the Ember Crown.\n\nIt is easy to miss, and Tob needed three clear nights to find it without help. On the fourth he found it at once and was cross that he had ever thought it hard. Darkness comes late now. His aunt allows him to stay until the ten o'clock bell only on Fridays, so the Crown is a Friday constellation."],["June: The Gate","June on the Emberwick clock tower, and I, Mabyn Thrale, have very little night to ring the hours through. At midsummer the sky here never turns fully black. A pale glow travels along the northern horizon from sunset to sunrise, and only the stronger stars come out.\n\nThat suits this month's lesson, which is three stars and nothing else. Tob, my nine-year-old fellow watcher, found them himself in the east at half past ten, with special leave from his aunt: three bright stars making a tall, narrow triangle, each belonging to a different figure. We call the triangle the Gate. When it stands in the east at dusk, summer has come through it.\n\nThe highest and bluest of the three is the Weir-light, which passes almost straight overhead. Tob lay flat on the leads to look at it and fell asleep there, which his aunt counts as progress."],["July: The Heron","I am Mabyn Thrale, bell-keeper, writing the July sky for Tob, the boy who keeps the first half of the night watch with me on the tower at Emberwick. The nights have begun to darken, and inside the triangle we call the Gate a shape has come clear.\n\nIt is the Heron. One bright star marks the tail; a long line of fainter ones runs out for the neck, and a shorter line crosses it for the wings. It is flying south with its neck stretched, as herons on the marsh never do, and Tob has complained.\n\nOn the darkest nights there is something more. A faint band of light runs the length of the Heron and on to the southern roofs, like breath on a window. That is the Pale River, and the Heron is following it. In September I shall show it to him properly."],["August: The Harvest Sparks","August, and for once I let Tob stay past the ten o'clock bell. I am Mabyn Thrale, who rings the night hours in Emberwick, and on the twelfth his aunt climbed the clock tower with a blanket and a jug of cocoa.\n\nEvery year in the second week of August the shooting stars come thick. We call them the Harvest Sparks. They may flash anywhere in the sky, but trace each trail backward and they all seem to spring from one spot in the north-east.\n\nThe trick is to lie flat and look at nothing. Tob kept the tally: forty-three in the hour before midnight, one of them bright enough to leave a glowing thread that lasted while he counted four. Each is a speck no bigger than a grain of sand, burning up high in the air. He did not believe me. He wrote it down anyway."],["September: The Pale River","I am Mabyn Thrale, night bell-keeper, and in September I kept a promise made in July to Tob, the nine-year-old who watches with me on the clock tower at Emberwick. We chose a night with no moon. I asked the lamplighter to leave the two lamps in the square until last.\n\nThe Pale River ran from the north-east horizon, straight overhead through the Heron, and down into the south-west: a broad, uneven band of soft light, brighter in some reaches than others, split along part of its length by a dark channel that we call the Ford.\n\nThen I handed him my brass spyglass. Wherever he pointed it along the River, the glow broke up into stars, too faint and too many to count. It is all stars, I said. He was silent for some time, which is rare, and then asked very quietly how far it went."],["October: The Barn Door","October on the tower. The dark comes early enough now that Tob, my young fellow watcher, has a full two hours before the ten o'clock bell sends him to bed. I am Mabyn Thrale, and I ring that bell, in Emberwick, every night of the year.\n\nThe autumn sky has one plain signpost: four stars in a great square, high in the south, with almost nothing inside it. We call it the Barn Door. It is not bright, but once seen it cannot be lost.\n\nFrom one corner two curving lines of stars run off north-eastward. Beside them there is a small smudge like a thumbprint on glass. Tob found it by looking slightly to one side, which is the way to see faint things. That smudge, the almanac-maker tells me, is the farthest thing a human eye can see. Tob has underlined it twice."],["November: The Goslings Return","I am Mabyn Thrale, bell-keeper of Emberwick, and November brought my pupil a surprise I had been saving. Tob was nine when he first climbed the clock tower; he turned ten on the third, and came up the stairs that evening in a new coat.\n\nLow in the east hung a tiny knot of stars, like a pinch of frost. These are the Goslings. Most people count six. Tob counted seven straight off, and with the spyglass he gave up at thirty.\n\nThey are the first of the winter stars, I told him. Wait an hour or two and the rest follow them up, the same figures we watched leave by the west door last spring. He has been cutting two discs of card, one to turn upon the other, with the Nail at the centre pin. He will not yet say what it is for."],["December: The Sky Comes Round","December, the longest nights. I am Mabyn Thrale, and I have rung the night hours from the Emberwick clock tower through a whole year with Tob beside me. He came last January, a boy of nine who could not sleep in a strange town.\n\nOn the shortest day his aunt gave him leave to stay late. At eleven the Lantern-Bearer stood due south: two shoulders, two knees, three buttons, and the Lantern flashing low over the roofs. He stood there at nine last January, and soon will again. Four minutes a night, Tob said, and showed me his card wheel. Turn the top disc to the date and the hour, and a cut-out window shows the sky.\n\nHe has pinned it by the bell rope for whoever watches after us. Then he sat down on the stairs to wait for midnight, and was asleep before the bell."]],[["A Drawing in a Copybook","I am Ottoline Brandreth, schoolmistress at Fellfoot in the Northern Fells. Among my grandmother's things is a child's copybook, and on its last page a drawing in brown ink: the outline of Harrow Fell, and above them a bright head with a long tail swept upward. Beneath it she wrote: The Lantern Comet, drawn by Sabeth Brandreth, aged twelve, from the school wall.\n\nThat was sixty-one years ago. Mr Rudd, the almanac-maker in Emberwick, has gone through the old records and reckons that the comet keeps a circuit of about that length. It is due back, he writes, within the twelvemonth, likeliest in spring.\n\nSo on the first of October I have begun a vigil. I own a short brass telescope with a wide eye, the kind called a comet-sweeper, and the same school wall. Every clear night I shall search, and write here whatever I find."],["How to Sweep","Fellfoot, October. This is the journal of my watch for the Lantern Comet, which my grandmother saw from this village sixty-one years ago. I am Ottoline Brandreth, the schoolmistress, and I am learning the comet-seeker's trade from a book and from cold experience.\n\nA comet new in the sky is a faint round smudge with no tail. It shows first near the sun's place: in the west after dusk, or in the east before dawn. So I sweep those two quarters. I move the telescope slowly level along the horizon, raise it by half its field of view, and come slowly back, like a ploughman.\n\nIt takes an hour each end of the night. I wear two shawls and stand on a hot brick wrapped in flannel. The children have learned that Miss Brandreth yawns on clear mornings, and one of them has started bringing me an apple."],["The First False Alarm","I am Ottoline Brandreth, schoolmistress of Fellfoot, sweeping the sky each clear night for a comet due back after sixty-one years. On the ninth of November, low in the east before dawn, I found a soft round glow in my telescope, and my hands shook so that I lost it twice.\n\nA comet moves. That is the test. From one night to the next it shifts its place among the stars, and a smudge that stays put is something else. I drew the glow with the three small stars beside it, and waited.\n\nTwo nights later it had not stirred by a hair. Nor on the third. It is a fixed thing, a faint cloud that has surely sat in that spot since before the fells were named. I have ruled a fresh page, headed it Not Comets, and entered it as number one. I expect the list to grow."],["The Bearded Star","Cloud has closed the December sky above Fellfoot for sixteen nights. I am Ottoline Brandreth, the schoolmistress, keeping watch for the return of the Lantern Comet; and since I could not look up, I looked back.\n\nThe vestry chest holds the parish book. Sixty-one years ago the curate wrote: a bearded star seen in the west these three weeks, the tail as long as a hay rake; the people much taken with it, and no harm come. Sixty-one years before that, in a crabbed older hand: a great broom star at lambing time.\n\nSo it has come at least twice before, and each time somebody here troubled to write it down. My pupils asked what a comet is. Mr Rudd of Emberwick believes it is a lump of ice and dust that the sun warms into a glowing cloud. I told them that, and that nobody yet knows for certain."],["The Gooseberry","I have found a comet, and it is the wrong one. I am Ottoline Brandreth, schoolmistress at Fellfoot, and since October I have swept the sky for the Lantern Comet my grandmother drew as a girl.\n\nOn the twentieth of January, high in the north, a small greenish blur drifted into my telescope. It was not on my list of Not Comets. The next night it had moved a finger's width against the stars, and the night after, another. No tail; only a round fuzz with a brighter pip in the middle, like a gooseberry held to a candle.\n\nI sent its positions to Mr Rudd, the almanac-maker in Emberwick. He replied by the next carrier: a true comet, new to every record he owns, and travelling the wrong way to be ours. He has entered it under my name. In this journal it shall be the Gooseberry."],["Pins in the Chart","February at Fellfoot. The small comet I found last month, which I call the Gooseberry, is fading. I am Ottoline Brandreth; I teach the village school; and my real quarry, the Lantern Comet of sixty-one years ago, has still not shown itself.\n\nEach clear night I mark the Gooseberry's place on my chart with a pin. There are fourteen pins now. They make a smooth curve across the north, each gap a little shorter than the last as the comet draws away from the sun and slows. It never grew a tail. Some do not.\n\nOne of my pupils, Wren Hollis, who is ten and has the sharpest eyes in the school, held the lantern for me and asked to look. She found the Gooseberry unaided on the last night anyone could. I could not see it myself. I have put her initials by the fifteenth pin."],["The Comet Is Late","Spring has come to the Northern Fells, and the Lantern Comet has not. I am Ottoline Brandreth, schoolmistress of Fellfoot. I have swept the dusk and the dawn on every clear night since October, and my list of Not Comets has reached nineteen.\n\nI wrote to Mr Rudd in Emberwick to ask whether his arithmetic might be at fault. His answer was kind. Sixty-one years is the comet's habit, he says, not its promise. On its long road out and back it passes the great slow planets, and each one tugs it a little, this way or that. It may be half a year early or half a year late, and no almanac can say which.\n\nThen it rained for three weeks. I mended the telescope case, marked forty slates, and slept, I admit, extremely well. The copybook stays open on my desk at my grandmother's drawing."],["Short Nights","I am Ottoline Brandreth, schoolmistress at Fellfoot, nine months into my watch for the Lantern Comet. It is June, and the sky is against me. Dusk lingers until nearly midnight, dawn begins soon after, and a faint comet would drown in the twilight between.\n\nI came near to stopping. It was Wren Hollis, my pupil of ten, who would not hear of it. She now keeps the log on the nights I sleep, and writes Nothing in a round hand with the hour beside it.\n\nTo pass the bright evenings I studied my grandmother's drawing and saw something I had missed. The sun had set to the right of Harrow Fell; the tail streams up and to the left. A comet's tail does not trail behind it like chimney smoke. It points away from the sun, always, whichever way the comet is going. She drew it truly at twelve."],["Not the Same as Sparks","August at Fellfoot, and my whole school lay on the wall to watch the shooting stars that come at harvest. I am Ottoline Brandreth, their teacher, and for ten months I have been waiting for a comet. The children assumed these were what I had been looking for.\n\nThey are not, and I used the evening to say why. A shooting star is over in a breath; it is a speck of dust burning in our own air. A comet is vast and very far away. It does not streak. It hangs in the same part of the sky for weeks, creeping a little each night.\n\nSo nobody need be afraid of blinking, I said. Wren Hollis asked whether the dust might be crumbs a comet had dropped. I did not know. I have written to ask Mr Rudd in Emberwick, and I rather hope she is right."],["A Year on the Wall","On the first of October I had kept my vigil for a full year. I am Ottoline Brandreth, schoolmistress of Fellfoot, and I began sweeping the sky for the Lantern Comet because my grandmother drew it from this same school wall sixty-one years ago.\n\nThe tally for the year: one hundred and forty-one clear nights, twenty-three Not Comets, one small comet nobody asked for, and a pupil, Wren Hollis, who can now find her way about the sky better than I can.\n\nOn the nineteenth, before dawn, I was sweeping low in the east over the ridge of Cold Rigg. A dim round glow slid into view beside a pair of stars I know well. There is no smudge there on my list. I drew its place, and the cloud came over before I had finished. I have told nobody. I must see whether it moves."],["It Moves","I am Ottoline Brandreth, schoolmistress at Fellfoot, and for four mornings I taught sums with half my mind out of the east window. A faint glow I had found before dawn was waiting to be proved, and the sky stayed shut.\n\nOn the twenty-third of October it cleared at five. The glow had left the pair of stars and stood a thumb's width nearer the sunrise. The following dawn it was brighter, with a short stub of light on the side away from the sun. A tail.\n\nI ran to the Hollis farm in my slippers to fetch Wren, who had earned the sight. Then I wrote to Mr Rudd in Emberwick by the carrier. His answer came in two days: the path fits. It is the Lantern Comet, half a year late, and it will pass round the sun into our evening sky."],["The Lantern Comet","By the first week of December the Lantern Comet had rounded the sun and stood in the west after dark, and all of Fellfoot came out to the school wall. I am Ottoline Brandreth, the schoolmistress. I waited fourteen months for this.\n\nIt needed no telescope. The head was a soft bright star beside Harrow Fell, and the tail rose from it up and to the left, longer than my hand held at arm's length. Through the glass there were two tails: one thin, straight and bluish, one broad and curved like a feather.\n\nI drew it on the blank page facing my grandmother's drawing. The two agree in everything but the handwriting. Then I gave the copybook to Wren Hollis, who is ten. She will be seventy-one when it comes again. She has promised to be on the wall, and to bring someone young to hold the lantern."]],[["A Wager at the Heron","I am Ezra Longbarrow, and I keep the Reedwater Ferry where the river Ember crosses the marsh. I have always known the moon the way one knows a neighbour: by sight, without asking her business.\n\nIn January Mr Rudd, the almanac-maker from Emberwick, crossed with me and remarked that no man could foretell the moon without books. I said my eyes were as good as his books. By the time we reached the Heron inn it was a wager. For every full moon of this year I am to write down, beforehand, the day it will fall and the hour it will rise. Ten right, to the day and the half hour, and he prints my table in his almanac and buys the ferry a new bell. Fewer, and I carry him free for life.\n\nMrs Oake at the Heron holds my sealed slips. The first was a guess. I missed by a day."],["Twenty-Nine and a Half","February on the marsh. I am Ezra Longbarrow, ferryman at Reedwater, and I have wagered the almanac-maker that I can foretell this year's full moons with nothing but my eyes and the ferry-house clock. I lost January's. So I started counting.\n\nI cut a notch in the doorpost each night and drew the moon's shape beside it in chalk. From one full moon to the next came to twenty-nine days and about a half. That gave me the right day for February, and I was proud of it.\n\nBut I had the rising wrong by more than an hour. I had supposed the moon kept time like the carrier's cart. She does not. She rises later every night, by three quarters of an hour or so, sometimes less, sometimes a good deal more. Mrs Oake at the Heron opened my slip and shook her head. Two moons gone, none right. I need ten."],["She Rises as He Sets","I am Ezra Longbarrow of the Reedwater Ferry, on the Ember marsh, and I am two full moons down in a wager with Mr Rudd the almanac-maker. In March I found the rule that saved me, and I found it standing in the middle of the river.\n\nIt was the evening of the full moon. I was halfway across with a load of withies when I saw the sun touching the reeds in the west, red and flat, and at the same moment the moon lifting out of the reeds in the east, the same size and nearly the same colour. I stood between them like the pin of a balance.\n\nThe full moon is full because she stands opposite the sun. So she must rise as he sets. I need only know the hour of sunset, and any ferryman knows that. My slip for March was right in both parts. One."],["Ash-Light on the Young Moon","April at Reedwater. I am Ezra Longbarrow, the ferryman, and I am making a year's table of the moon from my own watching, to win a bet. This month's full moon I called right, which makes two. But it is the young moon I want to set down.\n\nThree evenings after new, she hung in the west as a thin bright curl, and the rest of her disc was there too, faintly, the grey of cold ash. I had seen that all my life and never wondered at it.\n\nMy niece Dilly, who is eight, helped me puzzle it out with a lantern and a turnip in the ferry house. The bright curl is sunlight. The grey is light from our own world, shining up at the moon as she shines down on us. It is second-hand sunshine. I have added a column to my table and headed it ash-light."],["Two Moons in May","I am Ezra Longbarrow, ferryman on the Ember marsh, and my wager with Mr Rudd of Emberwick is that I shall foretell ten of this year's full moons. In May I discovered why he smiled when we shook hands.\n\nA full moon came on the second of the month. I counted on twenty-nine days and a half from my doorpost notches and got the thirty-first. I counted again. May has two. Twelve moons take only three hundred and fifty-four days, so the moon's year runs eleven days short of the sun's, and this year a thirteenth full moon squeezes in. Thirteen chances to be wrong, and he never said a word.\n\nI sealed both slips with Mrs Oake at the Heron and got both right. I also marked the tides, which run highest just after full and new; the ferry landing was awash each time. Four right of six. It can still be done."],["The Midnight Moon","June, and a hard lesson at the Reedwater Ferry. I am Ezra Longbarrow, and I have bet the almanac-maker that I can foretell the year's full moons, day and rising, from my own tables. I wrote the thirtieth for this one.\n\nShe rose that evening as round as a cheese, low and honey-coloured over the marsh, and I crossed to the Heron inn feeling sure of myself. Mr Rudd was waiting with his almanac open. By his reckoning the moon did not come exactly full until twenty minutes past midnight, which is the first of July.\n\nMrs Oake, who judges, ruled for the book. A day is a day. That leaves me four right out of seven, with six moons to come, and I can afford no more misses. I have learned that a moon may look full for two nights and be full for only a moment."],["The Ferryman in the Moon","I am Ezra Longbarrow, keeper of the Reedwater Ferry on the Ember, and I must call every remaining full moon of this year correctly to win my wager with Mr Rudd. July's I called to the day and within ten minutes of her rising. Five.\n\nThe summer full moon keeps low. She crept along above the southern reeds all the short night, deep yellow, never climbing higher than the top of my ferry pole, and set again before four. In winter she will ride high overhead. She does the opposite of the sun in everything.\n\nWith her so low and slow I studied her face. It is always the same face; she never turns her back. The grey patches make a man standing with a long pole, plain as paint. My niece Dilly says it is a heron. We have agreed that it is a ferryman, on weekdays."],["Fifty Minutes, More or Less","August on the marsh. I am Ezra Longbarrow, ferryman at Reedwater, and the table of the moon I am keeping to win a wager now fills the back wall of the ferry house. The August full moon was right, day and hour. Six, with four to get and four to come.\n\nThis month I filled in the nights between. Night passengers want to know one thing, whether there will be a moon to cross by, so I timed her rising every evening for a month. On average she is fifty minutes later each night. But the average hides a great deal: I have seen it as little as twenty minutes and as much as an hour and a quarter.\n\nSo the table shows, for each night, a small drawn moon and an hour. A drover studied it for some time and said it was the first almanac he had ever been able to read."],["The Harvest Moon","I am Ezra Longbarrow of the Reedwater Ferry, and September's full moon was the seventh I have foretold correctly for my wager with Mr Rudd the almanac-maker. Three more are needed, and exactly three remain.\n\nThis is the moon the Lowland farmers love, and now I know why. Around the full this month she rose barely twenty minutes later each evening, for five evenings together. No sooner had the sun gone than the moon was up, big and orange, and the reapers worked on by her light. I ferried four harvest wagons across after supper and never lit the lamp.\n\nIt is the season, not kindness. In autumn her path lies low and slanting along the horizon, so a day's travel lifts her little. In spring it is the other way about. I had noticed the long gaps in March and cursed them. Now they have a place in the table."],["The Copper Moon","October at Reedwater, and a night I did not foretell. I am Ezra Longbarrow, ferryman, with eight full moons now called correctly in my wager and two still to win.\n\nMr Rudd came out from Emberwick himself for this one and would not say why. The moon rose at sunset, to the minute of my slip. Mrs Oake marked it right. Then, towards ten, a dark bite appeared on her left edge. It grew. Within the hour the whole moon had gone the colour of an old copper kettle and hung there dim and round, and the stars came out thick around her.\n\nIt is the shadow of our own world, Mr Rudd said, crossing her face. His books had told him the night and the hour years ago. Mine could not have. I took off my hat to the books. He said my table had the rising closer than his."],["High and Cold","I am Ezra Longbarrow, and I keep the ferry at Reedwater on the Ember marsh. For my wager with the almanac-maker I needed November's full moon and December's, both, with no room for a slip. November's is won. Nine.\n\nShe rose at sunset, as she must, a little north of east. But what a different creature from July. By midnight she stood as high as the midsummer sun at noon, and the frozen reeds were lit like a field of glass. The winter full moon takes the high road that the summer sun takes, and stays up fifteen hours.\n\nThere was a ring around her on the second night, wide and pale. I have learned from the marsh folk that it means snow, and I have learned from this year to write such things down. The table has a remarks column now. Dilly is drawing the moons in it; her circles are better than mine."],["Thirteen Moons","The thirteenth full moon of the year fell on the twenty-fourth of December. I am Ezra Longbarrow, ferryman at Reedwater, and my slip, sealed a month before at the Heron inn, said: the twenty-fourth, rising at ten minutes to four, as the sun goes down.\n\nHalf the marsh came to the landing to see. Mr Rudd stood with his watch open. The sun went into the reeds; the moon came out of them; he snapped the watch shut and said eight minutes to. Mrs Oake declared it: ten right of thirteen. The wager was mine.\n\nHe has kept his word. My table will be printed in next year's almanac as Longbarrow's Moon for Travellers, and the new ferry bell came by cart on Tuesday. I rang it and took him across without charge. He protested. I told him it was for the eclipse. My eyes are good, but I mean to buy his book."]],[["Five That Will Not Stay","I am Bryony Wend. Master Corbin Yarrow taught the Emberwick school for forty years, and me among the rest. His eyes are failing now. This October he gave me his notebooks, his brass telescope and an unfinished chart, and asked me to do his looking for him.\n\nHis subject is the wanderers. The fixed stars keep their patterns for ever. Five lights do not. They drift among the stars from week to week, each at its own pace, and always along the same narrow band of sky, which he calls the Wanderers' Road. Another way to know them: stars twinkle, and wanderers shine steady.\n\nHe has names for all five: the Quick One, the Shepherd's Lamp, the Ember, the Alderman and the Slow Miller. And he has a reckoning, forty years in the making. Next November, he says, all five will stand together in the evening sky. He means to be there."],["The Shepherd's Lamp at Dawn","November, Lantern Hill above Emberwick, an hour before sunrise. I am Bryony Wend, and I am observing the five wandering stars on behalf of my old teacher, Master Yarrow, whose sight has gone dim.\n\nThe first on his list needs no finding. The Shepherd's Lamp blazed in the east this morning, far brighter than any star, white and perfectly steady. It threw my shadow faintly on the frost. Shepherds going up to the fells at dawn have set their hours by it time out of mind.\n\nBut it never climbs far. Master Yarrow's notes say the Lamp is tied to the sun as a dog is tied to a cart: it may run ahead or lag behind, never more than a few hours either way. So it is a morning star or an evening star and is never seen at midnight. Already it rises later each week."],["The Ember Rises","I am Bryony Wend of Emberwick, keeping watch on the five wandering stars for Master Corbin Yarrow, who taught me my letters and can no longer see the sky. In December I met the one the town is fondest of.\n\nThe Ember comes up in the east at about nine these nights, and there is no mistaking it. It is the colour of a coal that has been blown on, a steady orange-red among the blue-white winter stars. It stands at present just beneath the little cluster we call the Goslings.\n\nI marked its place on his chart with the date. A week later it had crept a finger's width to the east, and a week after that, another. That is the proper direction; all the wanderers travel eastward along their Road. Master Yarrow had me read the positions aloud twice. Watch it closely next month, he said, and smiled, and would not say why."],["The Ember Stands Still","January on Lantern Hill, with my feet in a sack of straw. I am Bryony Wend, and I am the eyes of old Master Yarrow of Emberwick in the matter of the wandering stars. Last month he told me to watch the red one, the Ember, and now I know why.\n\nIt has been slowing. In December it moved a finger's width eastward each week against the stars. In the first week of January it managed half that. In the second, a hair. And for the last ten nights it has not moved at all; it sits beside the same faint star as though it had forgotten its errand. It is also a good deal brighter.\n\nI reported this to him with some alarm, thinking I had measured badly. He was delighted. Nothing is wrong, he said. It is about to do the thing that puzzled people for a thousand years. Go on marking."],["The Ember Runs Backward","I am Bryony Wend, observing the five wanderers from Lantern Hill for my old schoolmaster, Corbin Yarrow of Emberwick. In February the red wanderer we call the Ember turned round and went the wrong way.\n\nNight by night it slid westward, back past the stars it had passed in December. It rose now at sunset and stood high at midnight, brighter than I had ever seen it.\n\nMaster Yarrow explained it at his kitchen table with two walnuts. All the wanderers circle the sun, he believes, and so do we. We are on the inner track and travel faster. When we overtake the Ember, it seems to drift backward, just as a slow cart seems to slide back along the hedge when the mail coach passes it. It is nearest to us then, which is why it burns so bright. He pushed the walnuts round until I could do it myself."],["The Loop Closed","March. I am Bryony Wend, and for five months I have plotted the wandering stars on a chart begun by Master Yarrow, the old schoolmaster of Emberwick, whose eyes can no longer do it.\n\nThe Ember has finished its trick. Through February it ran backward; in the middle of March it slowed, stood still a second time, and then set off eastward again as though nothing had happened. I joined my dated marks with a pencil line. They make a flattened loop, like a stitch dropped and picked up. It is fading already as we leave it behind, and he says it will linger in the evening sky until autumn, growing dimmer.\n\nThe Shepherd's Lamp, meanwhile, has gone. I looked for it at dawn all month and found only daylight. It is passing behind the sun, he says, and will come out on the other side as an evening star."],["The Quick One","I am Bryony Wend of Emberwick, and in April my old teacher Master Yarrow set me the hardest task on his list of five wandering stars: to catch the Quick One. He saw it himself, he says, only eleven times in forty years.\n\nIt keeps even closer to the sun than the Shepherd's Lamp does. It can be seen for a week or two, and only in twilight, in the half hour when the sky is neither light nor dark. Then it dives back into the glare.\n\nI climbed Lantern Hill on six clear evenings and stared at the place where the sun had set until my eyes watered. On the seventh I had it: a small, pinkish, steady spark above the brewery roofs, there for twenty minutes and gone. I ran down the hill to tell him. He made me describe the colour three times. Twelve, he said."],["The Alderman and His Clerks","June over Emberwick. The nights are short, but the fourth of the wandering stars does not need a dark one. I am Bryony Wend, observing for Master Corbin Yarrow, the old schoolmaster, whose eyes have failed him.\n\nThe Alderman rises as the sun sets this month and stays up all night: a big, calm, cream-coloured light, brighter than anything but the Shepherd's Lamp. It moves along the Wanderers' Road with great dignity, taking twelve years to go round.\n\nThrough the brass telescope it is a small round disc, and beside it, in a straight line, are four tiny stars. Master Yarrow calls them the clerks. I drew them: three on the left and one on the right. The next night, two and two. The night after, only three, the fourth being hidden behind the Alderman himself. They circle him, as he circles the sun. In twelve nights no two drawings matched."],["The Slow Miller","I am Bryony Wend, and since last October I have been the eyes of Master Yarrow of Emberwick, who watched the five wandering stars for forty years. In July I came to the last of them, and his favourite.\n\nThe Slow Miller is a quiet, yellowish light, steady, no brighter than a good star. It rises at dusk this month, low in the south-east. It is the slowest of all: it takes twenty-nine years and a half to grind its way once round the Road. In Master Yarrow's forty years it has gone round once and a third.\n\nThen I put the telescope on it. The Miller wears a ring. It is a thin, tilted hoop standing clear of the round body all the way round, like the brim of a hat seen from below. I must have gasped. From his chair on the hill Master Yarrow said: yes. Everybody does."],["The Lamp Returns","September, and dusk on Lantern Hill. I am Bryony Wend of Emberwick. For most of a year I have followed the five wandering stars for my old teacher, Master Yarrow, and tonight the brightest of them came back.\n\nThe Shepherd's Lamp vanished into the dawn last winter. This evening, twenty minutes after sunset, it was there in the west, low and brilliant above the marsh. It has passed behind the sun and come out on the evening side, exactly as he said it would.\n\nIn the telescope it is not a point. It is a tiny bright shape like the moon a few days short of full. The Lamp shines by borrowed sunlight, as the moon does, and we see more or less of its lit half as it rounds the sun. Master Yarrow nodded when I told him. Four are in the evening sky now, he said. We want only the Quick One."],["Closing Ranks","October again. It is a year since Master Corbin Yarrow gave me his chart of the wandering stars and asked me to be his eyes. I am Bryony Wend, and the Gathering he has been reckoning for forty years is three weeks off.\n\nEach clear dusk I climb Lantern Hill above Emberwick and count. The Shepherd's Lamp stands a little higher in the west each week. Above and to its left is the Ember, small and dim now, a spent coal. Further along the Road is the Slow Miller, and highest in the south the Alderman. Four, strung out like beads. They draw closer together every evening as the sun's glow creeps toward them.\n\nHis sums say the Quick One will rise out of the twilight to join them on the eighteenth of November, with a young moon beside. He has had his thick coat aired. I watch the weather as though it owed me money."],["The Gathering","On the eighteenth of November the sky over Emberwick cleared at four, and I walked Master Corbin Yarrow up Lantern Hill on my arm. I am Bryony Wend. For thirteen months I have watched the wandering stars for him, because he taught school for forty years and his eyes are worn out.\n\nAs the dusk deepened they came out along the Road, and I named them to him from the horizon upward. The Quick One, a pink spark just over the marsh. The Shepherd's Lamp, blazing. A thin new moon. The Ember. The Slow Miller. And the Alderman, highest of all. Five wanderers in one slanting line, as he had reckoned.\n\nHe turned his face to the west for a long time. I can see the Lamp, he said at last, as a sort of smear. And I know where the others are. That will do very well. I marked the date on his chart, and he signed it."]],[["The Spring Below Cold Tarn","I am Barnaby Stile, forty years ferryman at Emberwick. I have carried people across the river Ember all my working life without once asking where it came from. Now the ferry is my nephew's, my knees still serve, and I mean to walk the whole river from its first trickle to the sea.\n\nIt begins, I find, as a wet place in the moss below Cold Tarn, high in the Northern Fells. No banks, no bed. You could stop it with your hat.\n\nI have brought an old fly-box with twelve compartments, and my rule is one stone for each stretch of the walk. The first is a chip of grey granite from the spring itself, speckled black and sharp enough to scratch my thumb. The river has not yet had its way with it. We shall see what eighty miles can do."],["Sharp Slate in Scree Beck","Second day of my walk down the river Ember. Up here in the Fells it is called Scree Beck, and it is narrow enough to step across, though it makes the noise of something far grander.\n\nIts bed is all slate, fallen from the crags above. Every piece is flat, dark and sharp at the corners. I turned over fifty and found not one rounded edge. These stones have only just arrived: frost split them from the crag, perhaps last winter, and the beck has not had time to roll them anywhere.\n\nInto the second compartment of my fly-box goes a slate shaped like an arrowhead. A shepherd asked what I was looking for. \"A round one,\" I said. He told me, kindly, that I was in the wrong valley for that, and gave me half his cheese."],["The White Stone","Where Whin Gill joins it, the young river Ember grows wide enough to need stepping stones. I, Barnaby Stile, late of the Emberwick ferry, crossed them with great dignity and one wet boot.\n\nIn the bank there, a white stripe as broad as my hand runs across the dark rock. It is a vein of quartz, where water once filled a crack and left crystal behind. The beck breaks lumps from it, and because quartz is harder than nearly everything round about, the white lumps last. I followed them for half a mile downstream, their corners only just beginning to blunt.\n\nStone three is milky quartz the size of a walnut. My knife will not scratch it; the blade leaves a grey line of its own steel instead. Of all the stones a river carries, I am told this is the one that goes the distance."],["The Potholes at Hobb's Force","Four days below its spring, the river Ember throws itself off a ledge. Hobb's Force is thirty feet of white water, and I stood in the spray long enough to wet my pipe past lighting.\n\nAt the foot of the fall the rock is drilled with holes, round as cooking pots and some as deep as my arm. I reached into one and brought up a handful of pebbles. Round! Properly round, like marbles. The fall spins them in the hole, day and night, and they grind the pot deeper while it grinds them smooth. A mill with no miller.\n\nSo my fourth compartment holds the first round stone of the walk: a grey-green ball no bigger than a hazelnut. I rolled it across a flat rock for the pleasure of it. It went straight back into the river, and I had to choose another."],["Red Rock at Brackenford","The Fells are behind me now. At Brackenford the river Ember runs under a low red cliff, and the water is the colour of weak tea with a drop of rust in it.\n\nThe cliff is sandstone. Rub it and it comes away on your palm in grains, each grain a tiny stone in its own right, worn round by some river far older than this one. A stone made of older stones: I sat on the bank a good while with that thought. Sandstone pebbles do not last. They round quickly, then crumble, and by the next bend they are sand again.\n\nStone five is a flat red pebble with faint ripples across one face, the very ripples you see on a sandy shallow. I have wrapped it in my spare sock, as it would not survive a journey beside the granite."],["The Gravel Bar at Millbend","Halfway, by my reckoning. At Millbend the river Ember swings wide and leaves a bar of gravel a hundred paces long, and I spent a whole morning on it like a hen in a yard.\n\nThe river sorts its stones. At the head of the bar lie cobbles as big as loaves; at the tail, gravel fine as peas. The flat ones overlap like roof tiles, all leaning into the current, so that the water presses them down rather than lifting them. I counted a hundred pebbles by kind: plenty of granite and white quartz, very little slate. The slate is being ground to mud.\n\nStill, I found one. Stone six is a slate from the Fells, a smooth dark oval now, pleasant as a worry-stone. The shepherd who said I would find no round ones was right about his own valley, at least."],["Flint Under Emberwick Bridge","Home, for one night. The river Ember passes Emberwick under the old bridge, and a little below it my nephew works the ferry I worked for forty years. He rowed me over and I paid my penny like anyone else.\n\nOn the shoal beneath the bridge there is a stone I never noticed in all that time. The brooks from the Lowland chalk bring down flint: lumpy, white-skinned, and black as glass inside where it has broken. Flint does not wear round so much as chip. Every edge is a small, sharp, shell-shaped scar.\n\nStone seven is a flint the shape of a knuckle. Struck against my knife in the dusk it gave a spark, which delighted me more than a man of my years should admit. My sister says the fly-box smells of river and may stay in the porch."],["Fairy Coins from Lode Brook","A day below Emberwick, Lode Brook comes in from the limestone hills and the river Ember gains a new sort of pebble: pale grey, smooth as soap, and cool in the hand.\n\nLook closely and some are full of little rings, each the size of a lentil with a dot in the middle. Children here call them fairy coins. A quarryman on the bank put me right. They are slices of the stems of sea-lilies, creatures kin to the starfish, which lived on a sea floor where these hills now stand. The whole pebble is made of their broken remains.\n\nSo stone eight is older than the river that carries it, and was once alive, more or less. It shows three coins on one side and five on the other. I spent them several times over in my head on the walk to the inn."],["Black Sand at the Old Weir","Below the old weir the river Ember drops what it can no longer carry. I am Barnaby Stile, ferryman retired, and today I became a prospector with a borrowed pie dish.\n\nThe trick is to swirl a scoop of sand under water and let the light grains wash over the rim. What stays behind is heavy and black. The miller's boy lent me a magnet, and half the black grains leapt to it like iron filings, which is very nearly what they are. Among the rest lay specks of deep red, no bigger than pinheads: garnets, washed out of the Fell rocks and far too small for any ring.\n\nNo gold. The miller says there never was. Compartment nine holds a twist of paper with a pinch of black sand and eleven garnets in it. I counted them twice. It is the nearest I shall come to treasure."],["No Stones in the Marsh","Three days now I have followed the river Ember through the marsh, and I have not seen a single stone. The water is brown and slow. I pushed my staff into the bed and it went down a yard without touching anything firm.\n\nAt first I felt cheated. Then the reed-cutter I am lodging with scooped up a handful of the bank and squeezed it. Grey clay, fine as flour. \"That's your stones,\" he said. \"That's where they go.\" He is right. The slate of Scree Beck and the soft parts of the granite have been ground so small that the river carries them as cloud, and here, where it rests, it lets them fall.\n\nFor the tenth compartment I rolled a ball of that clay and dried it by his stove. It is a marble made of mountains. It also left a grey thumbprint on my supper."],["The Brick at Low Staithe","At Low Staithe the river Ember meets the tide. Twice a day it runs backwards, which after eleven stretches of walking downstream I took rather personally.\n\nThere is a hard of pebbles below the landing, and half of them are not stones at all. I found green bottle glass frosted smooth, a blue-patterned crumb of plate, and bricks worn round as buns. The river makes no distinction. Whatever falls in, it tumbles and rounds and carries along with the rest. Among them lay the first cockle shells of the walk, and the air smelled of salt.\n\nNumber eleven is a brick pebble, orange, with a pale streak of mortar still on it. I like to think it came from the old ferry-house chimney, which fell into the Ember in my grandfather's time. If so, it has taken sixty years to come as far as I have in a fortnight."],["Shingle at the River's Mouth","The river Ember ends on the Grey Coast, slipping through a gap in a great bank of shingle and into the sea without any ceremony at all. I had expected to feel triumphant. Mostly I felt I should take my boots off, and did.\n\nThen, among a million pebbles, I picked up a grey one speckled with black. Granite, smooth as an egg. I opened the fly-box and held it beside the sharp chip from the spring below Cold Tarn. The same stone, eighty miles apart.\n\nI laid all twelve on a driftwood plank in their order: chip, blade, white lump, and so on down to clay, brick and egg. That is the river's whole work, set out on a plank. Tomorrow the carrier's cart takes me home to Emberwick. From the ferry bench I shall watch the water go by and know what it is carrying."]],[["Eleven Empty Drawers","My great-uncle Ambrose Quarrender left me his mineral cabinet. It came to the schoolhouse in Emberwick on the carrier's cart, twelve shallow drawers of walnut, and eleven of them were empty. He had sold his crystals one by one in his last winters to pay for coal, and told nobody.\n\nOnly the top drawer was full. In it lay a clear, six-sided point of quartz as long as my finger, and beside it his register: a narrow green book listing twelve specimens, each with its shape, its colour and the place he found it. Reading it was like reading the menu of a dinner already eaten.\n\nI am Isolde Quarrender. I teach forty children their letters and know nothing of stones. But I dislike an empty drawer, and a register is very nearly a map. I intend to fill the cabinet again."],["The Stone That Doubles","The second entry in my great-uncle's register reads: \"Calcite. Clear rhomb. Doubles a line of print. Lode quarry.\" So on Saturday I, Isolde Quarrender, schoolmistress, walked to the limestone quarry above Lode Brook and asked the foreman if I might look through his rubbish.\n\nHe was very civil about it. Calcite fills the cracks in limestone, white and glassy, and when struck it breaks always into the same leaning block, like a brick someone has pushed sideways. Most pieces are cloudy. The foreman's boy found me a clear one the size of a sugar lump.\n\nAt home I laid it on the register and my uncle's thin handwriting appeared twice, one line floating just above the other. I turned the crystal and the second line walked round the first. The children will not believe it on Monday. I scarcely believe it now, and I am looking straight at it."],["Fool's Gold","Tobias Wren, aged nine, came into my schoolroom in Emberwick on Tuesday convinced that he was rich. He had split a roofing slate behind his father's shed and found gold in it: three bright cubes, sharp-cornered, shining like new brass.\n\nI opened my great-uncle's register to the third entry. \"Pyrite. Cubes, brassy, faces finely lined. Common in slate. Not gold.\" Ambrose Quarrender had underlined the last two words, I suspect from experience. We tried the test he gives. Gold leaves a golden streak on unglazed china; Tobias's treasure, rubbed on the back of a saucer, left a streak of greenish black.\n\nHe bore it well. What astonished us both was the shape. Nobody carved those cubes. They grew that way in the dark, square on every side. Tobias has sold me one for sixpence and a mention in the register, and Drawer Three is filled."],["Purple from the Spoil Heap","Entry four in the Quarrender register, which I am refilling drawer by drawer, says: \"Fluorspar. Cubes, violet. Old lead workings, Ravel Fell.\" It is nine miles from Emberwick to Ravel Fell, and I walked them in my second-best boots.\n\nThe old miners threw fluorspar away. It came up with the lead ore and was no use to them, so the waste heaps below the levels are speckled with it, glassy and pale. Most is broken. After two hours on my knees I found a lump with three perfect cubes standing on it, the colour of watered blackcurrant, each edge as long as my thumbnail.\n\nMy great-uncle notes that a cube of fluorspar, tapped just so, splits off its corners and becomes an eight-sided shape like two pyramids joined. I do not intend to try. It took me a whole Saturday to find one with its corners on."],["Swallow-Tails in the Clay","The brickworks in the Lowlands digs its clay from a pit as deep as a church, and the men there keep a shelf of oddities the spade turns up. My great-uncle's register sent me to them. Entry five: \"Gypsum. Clear blades, twinned like a swallow's tail. Clay pits. Soft.\"\n\nSoft is the word. The foreman handed me a crystal as long as my hand, shaped like an arrowhead where two blades had grown together. \"Try your nail on it,\" he said. I did, and my thumbnail left a scratch. A stone I can mark with my own finger!\n\nIt is the same stuff, he told me, that is baked and ground to make plaster. I carried it home to Emberwick wrapped in my scarf. Drawer Five now holds something that looks like ice and will never melt, provided no pupil of mine tests it with a thumb."],["Garnets in the Garden Wall","I have walked many miles this year refilling my great-uncle Ambrose's crystal cabinet, so it was humbling to find entry six in the wall of my own schoolhouse garden.\n\nThe register says: \"Garnet. Twelve-faced, dark red. In glittering grey schist.\" The wall is built of that very rock, carted down from the Fells a century ago. I had taken the lumps in it for rust. They are crystals, dark as old port, each about the size of a pea. Where one has weathered free you can count the faces, every one a neat diamond shape. I made it twelve. So did the three children helping me, eventually.\n\nA loose stone by the gate gave up two. One is in Drawer Six. The other I have kept back, on the advice of a note in my uncle's margin: \"Always keep a spare. Collectors trade.\""],["The Heaviest Drawer","Entry seven of the Quarrender register is a stone I could not have found alone. \"Galena. Lead ore. Cubes, bright grey. Very heavy.\" It lies underground, in the lead veins of the Northern Fells, and schoolmistresses are not encouraged down mines.\n\nSo I wrote to a mine captain. His reply came to Emberwick in a tobacco tin, and inside, in sawdust, lay a cluster of cubes like a handful of tiny silver dice. New-broken galena is as bright as a mirror. It dulls in time to the grey of a rainy sky, and he had cracked this piece fresh for me.\n\nWhat the register cannot convey is the weight. It is no bigger than a plum, and when I passed it round the class every child's hand dropped. \"It's cheating,\" said one. Drawer Seven needed a second nail in its runner."],["What the Fair Brought Back","At the autumn fair in Emberwick a man sells stones from a barrow: polished pebbles, shark's teeth, and rough grey balls he cracks open for a penny. I went looking for entry eight in my great-uncle's register. \"Amethyst. Quartz, violet. Points lining a hollow stone.\"\n\nHe had one: half a grey ball the size of a teacup, and inside it a cave of purple crystals, six-sided, deepest in colour at the tips. I paid three shillings without bargaining, which he seemed to find disappointing.\n\nAt home I turned it over. On the underside was a paper label, brown with age, in a hand like spider thread: \"A.Q. No. 8.\" It is not one like my uncle's. It is my uncle's. It has travelled for years between his coal bill and that barrow, and now it sits in Drawer Eight, where its outline is still pressed in the felt."],["Black Needles in Granite","Nine drawers. I am Isolde Quarrender, and I have nearly refilled my great-uncle's crystal cabinet, but entry nine cost me three visits to the granite quarry on the Fell road before I knew what I was looking at.\n\n\"Tourmaline. Black. Long three-sided prisms, grooved lengthwise.\" I had been stepping over it. In the coarse veins of the granite lie black rods, thin as knitting needles, and I had taken them for coal. Coal, though, is soft and dirties the fingers. These are harder than a knife, and every one is scored along its length like a stick of liquorice. Break one across and the end is a rounded triangle.\n\nThe quarrymen call it \"schorl\" and curse it for spoiling good stone. One of them split out a needle two inches long for me, and would take nothing for it but my opinion of his daughter's spelling."],["A Book with Glass Pages","The tenth entry in my great-uncle's register pleased my class in Emberwick more than all the others together. \"Mica. Six-sided book of leaves. Splits for ever.\"\n\nI found it at the granite quarry, in the same coarse vein that gave me the black tourmaline: a silvery block as wide as a penny and half as thick, with six flat sides. It is a crystal that grows in sheets. Slide a pin under the edge and a leaf lifts off, clear, bendable and thinner than paper. Split that leaf and you have two. The children halved and halved again until the pieces floated on their breath.\n\nOld stoves, I am told, have mica in their doors, because it does not mind the fire. I gave the class the loose leaves and kept the book. Drawer Ten is filled. Two remain, and the register's hand grows shakier toward the end."],["The Dairy Doorstop","Entry eleven nearly defeated me. \"Heavy spar. White crested blades, like a cock's comb. Heavy as its name.\" My great-uncle found his in a mine long since flooded, and I had no notion where to find another.\n\nI offered my spare garnet in trade to anyone who could help. On Thursday little Ada Furlong arrived at the schoolhouse in Emberwick pulling a handcart. In it sat a white lump the size of a loaf, all ridged plates standing on edge. \"Gran says it has held the dairy door open for thirty years,\" she said, \"and you may have it, for it stubs everyone's toes.\"\n\nIt is heavy spar beyond doubt. A stone that looks like chalk has no business weighing so much. It will not fit the drawer, so I chipped off one good crest for the cabinet. The rest holds open the schoolroom door, and stubs toes there instead."],["Grown on the Windowsill","The last entry in my great-uncle Ambrose's register is the shortest. \"Salt. Cubes. Grown on the kitchen windowsill, one winter. The only one I made myself.\"\n\nSo the twelfth crystal could not be found, bought or begged. I stirred kitchen salt into warm water until no more would vanish, hung a thread in the jar, and set it on the schoolhouse sill. Then I waited, which I do badly. In a week the thread was gritty. In five, a cube the size of a pea hung there, square-cornered, and nobody had shaped it.\n\nIt lies in Drawer Twelve, in a pillbox. The cabinet is full again: eleven journeys and one jar. There were blank pages left in the green book. Tonight I ruled a line beneath his last entry and wrote, in my own hand, \"No. 13.\" The rest of the line is empty yet."]],[["Kestrel Level","I am Edmund Rook, captain of Kestrel Level, a lead mine driven into the side of Ravel Fell in the Northern Fells. \"Captain\" is only what miners call the man who must decide where to dig. I have eleven men, two ponies and a boy called Tam, who pushes the ore tubs and asks questions.\n\nOur ore is galena, bright grey and heavy, the stuff lead is smelted from. It lies in a vein we call the Silver Rake: a crack in the limestone, nearly upright, filled long ago with minerals. The level follows it into the hill for four hundred fathoms. Parts were dug before anyone kept records. We call those miners \"the old man\" and find his pick-marks everywhere.\n\nThe owners in Emberwick want a day-book kept of ore and veins. I begin it this first Monday of the year, with the vein three feet wide and good."],["What a Vein Is Made Of","Tam, the tub-boy at Kestrel Level, asked me today why the whole hill is not lead. A fair question. I am the mine's captain and ought to have an answer.\n\nA vein is a healed crack. Ages ago the limestone of Ravel Fell split, and hot waters rising through the split left minerals on its walls, layer upon layer, until it was full. Most of the filling is no use to us. There is white calcite, glassy fluorspar, and heavy spar like lumps of chalk. Miners lump all these together as \"gangue\" and throw them on the heap.\n\nThe galena runs through the middle in ribs: here a thread, there a band as thick as my arm. I held the candle close and showed Tam the layers, like the rings of a cut onion. He said the hill had been mended with treasure. I shall not better that."],["The Dressing Floor","Nothing comes out of Kestrel Level clean. What the ponies draw from the hill is a grey rubble of ore and waste together, and it must be parted on the dressing floor beside the beck before the smelter will look at it.\n\nThe secret is weight. Galena is far heavier than the spar it grows in. So the lumps are broken small with flat hammers, then shaken in a sieve under water. The heavy ore sinks to the bottom of the sieve and the light waste rides on top to be skimmed away. The finest sludge is washed down a sloping trough, where the lead settles first.\n\nIt is cold, wet work, done mostly by the miners' wives and sisters in clogs. In this day-book I, Edmund Rook, captain, record eleven tons of dressed ore for the month, and that Widow Marl can judge a sieve by ear."],["The Vein Pinches","A bad week to write up. At four hundred and six fathoms from the entrance of Kestrel Level, the Silver Rake has begun to pinch.\n\nVeins do this. The crack that holds the ore was never an even width, and where its walls draw together the filling thins. In March we were cutting galena three feet across. Today the vein is a hand's breadth of white spar with a grey thread in it no thicker than a bootlace. The men call it \"nipped\". We drive on through it and hope that it opens again, as it has before.\n\nOld Abel Dunnock, who has worked this hill for fifty years, put his palm flat on the rock and said nothing, which worried me more than talk would have. As captain I have ordered the forehead driven six feet more. Tam has stopped asking questions, and I find I miss them."],["Signs on the Fell Top","With the vein at Kestrel Level pinched to a thread, I left the men driving and spent two days on top of Ravel Fell with my eyes on the turf. A mine captain does half his prospecting above ground.\n\nA vein shows at the surface if you know its manners. Where the Silver Rake runs beneath, there is a line of shallow hollows left by the old man's first diggings, and molehills there throw up crumbs of spar. Better still is the leadwort, a small white flower that thrives on ground too full of lead for grass. Follow the flowers and you follow the vein.\n\nI followed them east for half a mile. Then, above the spot where we are now digging, they stopped dead, and began again forty paces to the north. I have drawn it in the day-book. I do not yet know what it means."],["The Wall of Clay","Today, at four hundred and eight fathoms, Kestrel Level ran into a wall. The thread of ore we have been nursing for weeks ends against a smooth, sloping face of rock smeared with grey clay, and beyond it is nothing but barren shale.\n\nIt is a fault. At some time after our vein was made, Ravel Fell cracked again along this line, and one side of the hill slid past the other. The Silver Rake was cut in two like a ribbon with scissors. Our half stops here. The other half is somewhere beyond the clay, carried up, down or sideways, and nobody can see through rock.\n\nAbel Dunnock held his candle to the wall and whistled. The surface is polished like a table and scored with fine straight grooves. \"The hill wrote down which way it went,\" he said. I, Edmund Rook, captain, intend to learn to read it."],["Reading the Grooves","The question at Kestrel Level is simple to ask. Our vein of lead ore has been cut off by a fault, and the lost half lies to the left of us or to the right. Guess wrong, and we tunnel away from it until the money is gone.\n\nAbel Dunnock and I spent the shift with our noses to the fault wall. The grooves on it run nearly level, so the ground slid sideways, not up or down. Abel stroked the face with his palm: smooth one way, catching the other, like a cat's fur. Rock, he says, feels smooth in the direction the far side travelled. Smooth to the north.\n\nThen I remembered the leadwort on the fell top, which broke off above this spot and began again forty paces northward. Two witnesses agree. I have written in the day-book, as captain: we turn north."],["The Crosscut","A tunnel driven across the grain of the ground to find a vein is called a crosscut, and ours at Kestrel Level now runs northward from the fault where the Silver Rake was lost. I measure it every Saturday with a knotted cord.\n\nIt is slow. The shale is tough and gives us nothing to sell. Two men at the forehead bore holes with hammer and steel, a quarter-turn to each blow, then fire a small charge of powder and wait for the smoke to clear. A good week is five feet. Tam trams the waste out and has learned to tell good air from bad by the candle flame, which leans and yellows when the air grows poor.\n\nEleven weeks, forty-nine feet, not one speck of ore. I am Edmund Rook, captain, and I write the figures down plainly, because the owners in Emberwick can add."],["Water in the Level","It rained on Ravel Fell for nine days, and on the tenth Kestrel Level filled to the knee. Every mine is a drain for the hill above it. Water finds the old man's forgotten workings, gathers there, and comes down on us through cracks we never knew of.\n\nOur level slopes toward its mouth, so that water runs out along a channel under the rails. This week the channel choked with silt and the crosscut became a pond. No one was in any danger. The men cannot bore holes under water, and for six days my company did nothing but dig silt, mend the channel and say what they thought of the weather.\n\nTam floated a candle stub on a chip of wood to watch which way the water wanted to go. It was sensible, so I have entered it in the day-book: \"Drainage surveyed by T., boat.\""],["Green Stain and Black Jack","Sixty-one feet into the crosscut at Kestrel Level, and the rock has changed at last. I write this with my hands still dirty.\n\nFirst came a green stain on the wall, bright as a mallard's head. That is copper showing, and beside it a few brassy specks of copper ore, too little to pay for the candle we saw it by. Then a string of spar carrying dark brown crystals with a resinous shine. The men groaned. It is zinc blende, which miners call \"black jack\", for it looks like lead ore to a hopeful eye and the smelter will not take it.\n\nStill, Abel Dunnock was cheerful. Stains and strings do not wander alone in dead ground, he says. They are the fringe of something. As captain I ought to be cautious in the day-book, so I record only this: the shale is no longer barren."],["One More Month","The owners of Kestrel Level met in Emberwick on Thursday, in the back room of the corn exchange, and I, Edmund Rook, went down from Ravel Fell in a clean collar to explain why their mine had sold no ore since midsummer.\n\nI took the day-book and three things in my pockets: a piece of the last good galena, a chip of the green-stained rock, and the assayer's note on our old ore, which shows a little silver in every ton of lead. I laid them on the table and told the story straight. A vein cut by a fault. A crosscut going north. Seventy feet driven, and signs.\n\nThey are a draper, a miller, two farmers and a clergyman's widow, and the widow asked the best questions. They voted me one more month of wages and powder. Not a week over. I walked home in the dark, counting feet."],["The Vein Regained","On the nineteenth day of our last month, at eighty-three feet in the crosscut, the borer at the forehead of Kestrel Level felt his steel jump into something soft. He shouted. I was there before the echo.\n\nWe cleared the face by hand. White spar first, then a rib of grey so bright that Tam said somebody had left a mirror in the hill. It is the Silver Rake, the vein we lost at the fault, carried north just as the grooves and the leadwort said. By Saturday we had it measured: two feet of solid galena, standing upright and running on eastward.\n\nAbel Dunnock laid his palm on it and this time he spoke. \"There you are,\" he said, as if to a strayed ewe. I have put a lump the size of a plum in my pocket and closed this day-book. The next one starts Monday."]],[["What a Pebble Is","This primer was written on the beach at Tarrow, on the Grey Coast, by me, Dorcas Whin, for my nephew Kit, who came to stay for the summer with one bucket and a great many questions. I have set down twelve lessons, one for each week he was here.\n\nLesson the first: a pebble is a piece of rock that water has rolled until its corners are gone. Smaller than a pea, we call it grit. Bigger than your fist, it is a cobble. Everything in between is a pebble, and a beach like ours holds more of them than there are people in the world.\n\nKit's first question was whether any had holes in. A stone with a hole right through it is lucky, his friend says. I told him yes, somewhere, and that learning the others would pass the time while we looked."],["Wet Stones and Dry Stones","Second lesson from the beach at Tarrow, where my nephew Kit and I are learning pebbles one week at a time.\n\nOn Monday he filled his bucket at the water's edge with the finest stones he had ever seen: deep red, bottle green, black with gold flecks. By supper they sat on the windowsill, dry, and every one had turned the same dusty grey. He thought someone had swapped them.\n\nNobody had. A dry pebble is covered in scratches that scatter the light and hide its colour. Water fills the scratches and the colour shows through, just as a wet pavement is darker than a dry one. So here is the rule: always judge a pebble wet. Carry a jar of water, or do as Kit does and lick your thumb. To get his red stone back, we dropped it in a glass. It sits by his bed, glowing."],["Hard and Soft","Third lesson of the Pebble Primer, set down at Tarrow by Dorcas Whin, aunt, with Kit, nephew, doing the scratching.\n\nStones differ in hardness, and you may sort them with three tools you already own. First, your fingernail: if it scratches the pebble, the stone is very soft, like chalk. Second, a copper coin, which marks limestone but not much else. Third, a steel nail. Flint and quartz laugh at steel; the nail leaves only a silvery line of itself, which rubs off. (An aunt should hold the nail.)\n\nKit lined up his week's pebbles from softest to hardest along the sea wall, eleven in a row, and it took him the whole afternoon. The chalk he could write with. The white quartz at the far end scratched the glass of my jam jar, which I had not planned on. He looked at every one for holes. None yet."],["Three Families of Stone","Fourth lesson, written at the kitchen table in Tarrow because it rained, with a tray of pebbles between my nephew Kit and me.\n\nEvery pebble on the beach belongs to one of three families. The fire-made stones were once melted, deep underground, and cooled into a mass of crystals: granite is one. The laid-down stones were once mud, sand or shells, settled in layers under water and pressed hard: sandstone, limestone and chalk. The changed stones began as either sort and were squeezed and baked inside mountains until they turned into something new, like slate, which was once mud.\n\nHow to tell? Look close. Crystals locked together, speckled all through: fire-made. Grains or layers: laid-down. Stripes, bands or a glittery sheen: changed. Kit sorted forty pebbles into three saucers and got thirty-one right. I got thirty-four, and he checked."],["Granite, the Speckled One","Fifth lesson of the Primer. On the beach at Tarrow my nephew Kit has a favourite kind of pebble, pink and grey and speckled like a thrush's egg, so this week we looked at it properly. It is granite.\n\nGranite is three things mixed, and with a reading-glass you can find all three. The grey, glassy grains are quartz. The pink or cream blocks, which sometimes show a flat shining face, are feldspar. The black flecks that glitter when you tilt the stone are mica. Wet the pebble first.\n\nGranite was molten once and cooled slowly, far underground, which gave the crystals time to grow big enough to see. There is no granite cliff near Tarrow. Kit's pebbles have come a long way, down rivers or along the shore, to end up in his bucket. He approves. He came a long way himself, on the carrier's cart."],["Sandstone and Its Layers","Sixth lesson, from Dorcas Whin's primer for her nephew Kit, written halfway through our summer on the beach at Tarrow.\n\nPick up a pebble that feels rough, like a nutmeg grater. Rub two together and grains fall off. That is sandstone, and it is exactly what it says: sand, laid down by water, then glued and pressed into rock. Under the reading-glass you see the grains packed like sugar in a lump. Often there are stripes, pale and dark, thin as the pages of a shut book. Each stripe is a layer of sand that settled, perhaps in a single flood.\n\nKit found a red pebble with nine stripes and worked out that he was holding nine floods in one hand. So a beach is sand that became stone and is becoming sand again. We sat and listened to the waves doing it. It makes a hissing noise."],["Flint, the Glassy One","Seventh lesson. The cliffs east of Tarrow are white chalk, and from them come the oddest pebbles on our beach. My nephew Kit calls them \"the potatoes\".\n\nThey are flints: knobbly lumps with a white, chalky skin. A whole one is dull. But find one the sea has broken, and inside it is dark, smooth and shiny as a bottle, grey or brown or nearly black. Flint breaks like glass, in curved scoops with sharp edges, so here is the one safety rule of this primer: we never smash flints, and we mind our fingers on fresh ones.\n\nLong ago people chipped flint into knives and arrowheads because of those edges. And two flints tapped together in the dusk will give a spark and a strange burnt smell. Kit and I tried it behind the boat-shed. Many flints have hollows in them, but, he reports, no holes right through."],["The Ring Around the Stone","Eighth lesson of the Pebble Primer, by Dorcas Whin. This one began when Kit ran up the beach at Tarrow holding a grey pebble with a white line all the way round it, like string round a parcel.\n\nThat line is a vein of quartz. Long before the pebble was a pebble, it was part of a bed of rock, and the rock cracked. Water seeped through the crack carrying dissolved quartz, and left it there until the crack was filled with white. Later the rock broke up, the sea rounded the pieces, and here is one with its mended crack still showing.\n\nQuartz is harder than the grey stone, so the white line stands up like a ridge you can feel with your eyes shut. Fishermen's children here call a stone with an unbroken ring a wishing stone. Kit wished at once. I can guess for what."],["Pebbles That Were Alive","Ninth lesson. Some of the pale grey pebbles at Tarrow have shapes in them, and this week my nephew Kit learned to find them: curved white lines like fingernail parings, tiny coils, and patterns like the bottom of a wicker basket.\n\nThey are fossils. The grey stone is limestone, which was once the muddy floor of a warm sea, and the shapes are the shells and corals of creatures that lived there, buried and turned to stone along with the mud. The curved lines are shells seen edge-on. The basket pattern is coral.\n\nThere is a kitchen test for limestone. Put a drop of vinegar on the pebble and watch through a reading-glass: it fizzes, very gently. Nothing else on the beach does that except chalk and seashells, which are made of the same stuff. Kit has vinegared everything in the house, including, I regret to say, the doorstep."],["The Impostors","Tenth lesson, set down at Tarrow by Dorcas Whin for her nephew Kit, who was fooled four times this week and enjoyed it.\n\nNot everything rounded on a beach is a stone. The sea will tumble anything. That frosted green pebble is the bottom of a bottle. The orange one with little holes is brick. The white one with a blue flower on it was a plate. The black one that marks your fingers is coal. And the honey-coloured piece that feels warm and weighs almost nothing may be amber, which is tree resin turned hard, though Kit's proved to be a boiled sweet.\n\nHow to know them: glass shows no grains at all, brick is full of bubbles, pottery has a skin of glaze on one side only. We keep the impostors in their own jar. Kit says it is the prettiest jar we have."],["How the Sea Sorts","Eleventh lesson, from the last full week of my nephew Kit's summer at Tarrow.\n\nWalk from the water's edge to the top of the beach and watch your feet. Down by the waves the pebbles are small and the sand begins. Higher up they grow larger, and at the very top lies the storm ridge, a bank of big stones that only the winter gales can throw so far. The sea sorts by size, and by shape too. Flat pebbles ride the wash like little plates and collect in drifts. Those are the ones for skimming: thin, round, and fitting the crook of your finger. Kit's best is seven bounces, and mine is nine.\n\nHe has searched every drift for a stone with a hole. One week is left. I have told him honestly that some summers do not have one in them."],["The Holed Stone","Twelfth and last lesson of the Pebble Primer. On his final morning at Tarrow my nephew Kit went down to the low-tide rocks, and came back up the beach walking very carefully, as though carrying an egg.\n\nIt was a grey limestone pebble with a hole clean through it, big enough for his little finger. I, Dorcas Whin, who have lived here thirty years, own only two. And here is the lesson: such holes are made by a small shellfish called a piddock, which bores into soft rock and lives in the tunnel. When the rock breaks up and the sea rounds the pieces, some still carry the empty burrow. So Kit's lucky stone was once somebody's house.\n\nHe threaded it on string and tipped the rest of his bucket back for the next learner. That is the primer's last rule: learn all of them, keep one."]],[["Opening the Books","For thirty-eight years I, Bartholomew Quill, kept the accounts of the corn exchange in Emberwick. On retiring I was given a clock and a clean ledger, and I found I could not look at the ledger without wanting to enter something in it.\n\nSo I am keeping accounts of beetles. The rules are these. One line for each kind found in my garden at Tally Cottage or in the lane beside it. Each to be drawn, measured, and let go unharmed. The target, by the last day of December: one hundred kinds. My sister says there cannot be a hundred kinds of beetle in the world. I believe the true figure is rather higher.\n\nJanuary is a poor month to begin. Even so: seven-spot ladybirds asleep in the window frame, a black ground beetle under the water butt, and something small in the log pile. Balance carried forward: three."],["Under Bark and Stone","February at Tally Cottage, and the beetle ledger stands at three. A beetle in winter does not come to you. You must call on it at home, and its home is under something.\n\nI, Bartholomew Quill, have therefore spent the month lifting things and putting them back exactly as they were, which is only manners. Beneath the loose bark of the old pear: a flat brown beetle thin as a pressed leaf. Under the doorstep slab: a violet ground beetle an inch long, black until it turns, when its edges flash purple like shot silk. In the compost: a rove beetle, slender, with wing-cases so short it seems to be wearing a waistcoat and nothing else.\n\nA beetle, I should note for the record, is an insect whose front wings have hardened into a case that covers the back. That is the whole qualification. Total to date: eight."],["The Oil Beetle in the Lane","March. The beetle ledger I keep at Tally Cottage, Emberwick, now holds fourteen entries, and the newest is the strangest thing I have yet drawn.\n\nI met it crossing the lane on the first warm morning: a fat, blue-black creature as long as my thumb joint, lumbering like a small alderman. Its wing-cases are far too short for its body, as though it had outgrown its coat. When I offered it a twig it leaked a bead of yellow oil from its knees. This is an oil beetle. The books say its young climb into flowers and ride away on the backs of solitary bees, to be reared in their nests as uninvited lodgers.\n\nAlso entered this month: two dung beetles, a click beetle that sprang off my palm with a snap, and a ladybird with fourteen spots. I, Bartholomew Quill, counted them twice. Carried forward: fourteen."],["The Pond Column","April, and I have had to rule a new column in the beetle ledger, because a good many beetles turn out to live in water. My pond at Tally Cottage is the size of a tablecloth. It has added nine kinds in a month.\n\nOn the surface spin the whirligigs, black beads that circle one another like clerks at a quarter-day. Each has its eyes divided in two, to watch air and water at once. Below them hunts the great diving beetle, olive-brown and edged with gold, an inch and a quarter long. It rows with fringed back legs and comes up tail-first to tuck a bubble of air beneath its wing-cases.\n\nI caught each in a jam jar, drew it through the glass, and tipped it back. My sister has asked that the jam jars be kept separate from now on. Total entered, by Bartholomew Quill: twenty-three."],["Cockchafers at the Lamp","May. Something struck my study window at Tally Cottage last night with a thump like a thrown acorn. Then another. I, Bartholomew Quill, went out in my slippers with the beetle ledger under my arm.\n\nThey were cockchafers, which country people call maybugs: chestnut-brown, an inch long, dusted with white as if floured, and flying with all the grace of a dropped parcel. Each carries antennae that open into little fans. I counted the leaves of the fan with a lens. Seven means a male; six, a female. They spend three years underground as fat white grubs and then a few weeks in the air, bumping into lamps.\n\nMay is generous. The hawthorn hedge alone gave me a scarlet cardinal beetle, three weevils and a green tortoise beetle that clamps itself flat to a leaf like a limpet. Thirteen kinds entered this month. Carried forward: thirty-six."],["The Midsummer Audit","June, and halfway through the year the beetle ledger of Tally Cottage stands at fifty-four. I passed the halfway mark on the fourteenth, on a hogweed flower, and took a glass of elderflower cordial on the strength of it.\n\nFlowers are where beetles keep their summer. On one flat white head of hogweed I counted: soldier beetles, orange-red, in pairs; a wasp beetle, striped black and yellow and fooling nobody who looks twice, since it has no waist; and the thick-legged flower beetle, bright green, whose male has thighs like a dancer's. On the dog rose sat a rose chafer, a living emerald that hums like a bee when it flies.\n\nAll drawn and let go, as the rules demand. My sister, who doubted there were a hundred kinds of beetle in the world, asked to see the rose chafer. I, Bartholomew Quill, recorded this too."],["A Light on the Bank","July. Entry sixty-one in my beetle ledger was made at eleven at night, by the light of the creature itself.\n\nI was walking the lane beside Tally Cottage when I saw a green spark in the grass of the bank, steady, not flickering. I knelt, at some cost to my trousers. It was a glow-worm, and a glow-worm, I can now state with authority, is no worm. She is a beetle: a wingless female, brown and segmented, who climbs a grass stem and lights the last joints of her tail to call down the winged males. The light is quite cold. I held my hand to it.\n\nI read my pocket watch by her at two inches, and drew her by a candle shaded with my hat, so as not to outshine her. Four more kinds from the lime tree complete the month. I, Bartholomew Quill, carry forward sixty-five."],["Antlers in the Woodpile","August, and the beetle ledger at Tally Cottage has its grandest entry. At dusk a stag beetle flew across my garden, upright in the air and droning like a distant threshing machine, and landed on the woodpile.\n\nHe is over two inches long, the largest beetle in the country, chestnut and black. The antlers are jaws, grown enormous, and he uses them only to wrestle other males off a log. He could not give my finger more than a firm handshake. His grubs live five years or more in rotting oak, which is the best argument I know for an untidy woodpile.\n\nAt the other end of the scale: the acorn weevil, a quarter-inch long, of which nearly half is snout. She drills into green acorns with it. I, Bartholomew Quill, needed the strong lens and three attempts to draw her. Eleven kinds this month. Carried forward: seventy-six."],["The Sexton's Visit","September. A dead shrew lay on my path at Tally Cottage on Monday morning. By Tuesday it had sunk halfway into the ground, and I, Bartholomew Quill, knelt down to see who was responsible.\n\nTwo burying beetles, black with bands of bright orange, were digging the soil from beneath it. They are called sexton beetles, and they work as a pair. Once the shrew is underground they will raise their young beside it, and, unusually for insects, both parents stay to feed the grubs, who beg like nestlings. Nature's undertakers are also devoted nurses. I entered that in the remarks column.\n\nEach beetle carried small tawny mites on its back, passengers hitching a ride to the next burial. The mites do not go in the ledger. They have eight legs, and an accountant must keep his categories clean. Eight kinds entered this month, mostly from fallen pears. Carried forward: eighty-four."],["An Error in the Books","October, and I must report an irregularity in the beetle ledger of Tally Cottage. The total reached ninety-one on the tenth. On the twelfth I struck two entries out.\n\nThe fault lies with a ladybird. I had entered three kinds from the ivy: one red with black spots, one black with red spots, one chequered buff and brown. A borrowed book informs me that all three are the ten-spot ladybird, a single species that cannot settle on a pattern and seldom has ten spots. One beetle, entered three times.\n\nIn thirty-eight years at the corn exchange I, Bartholomew Quill, never let a false figure stand, and I will not begin for the sake of a round hundred. I ruled two red lines and initialled them. My sister says no one would have known. The ladybird would have known. Corrected balance: eighty-nine, with eleven weeks left and the leaves coming down."],["Sifting the Leaves","November. The big handsome beetles have gone from Tally Cottage, and with eighty-nine in the ledger and eleven wanted, I, Bartholomew Quill, have taken up the kitchen sieve.\n\nThe method: shake a handful of leaf litter through the sieve over a white tea-tray, then wait. For a minute nothing happens. Then specks begin to walk. Most beetles, it turns out, are tiny, and I have been striding over them all year. There is a pill beetle that tucks in its legs and becomes a seed. There is a round, glossy thing that feeds on moulds, and a brown one with feathery wings, so small that it sat on a comma in the ledger and left room.\n\nDrawing them takes the strong lens and a steady breath. Seven new kinds in a month I had written off. My sister wants her sieve returned. Carried forward: ninety-six."],["The Hundredth Entry","December, the last week, and the beetle ledger of Tally Cottage stood at ninety-nine. A hard frost had shut the garden like a bank on Sunday. I lifted every stone twice. Nothing.\n\nOn the thirty-first I gave up and went to the pantry for a biscuit to console myself. In the bottom of the tin, among the crumbs, walked a small brown beetle, oval, a tenth of an inch long, perfectly at home. A biscuit beetle. It had been living in my own house all year while I crawled about the lane.\n\nI drew it, measured it, and, as the rules require, let it go, though I own I carried it to the garden shed first. Then I ruled a double line. One hundred kinds, every one alive and at liberty. My sister and I ate the rest of the biscuits, after an inspection. Books closed. Bartholomew Quill."]],[["The Lamp in Needle Lane","I am Lettice Brome, seamstress, of Needle Lane in Emberwick, and I sew late. My work-table stands at the window with the lamp upon it, and for thirty winters I have taken the soft tapping at the glass for sleet.\n\nLast night I looked. A small grey-brown moth was walking up the pane on the outside, in January, with frost on the sill. The carrier's wife, who knows such things, says it is the winter moth, and that only the males fly. The female has mere stubs for wings and climbs the apple trunk on foot to meet him.\n\nMy goddaughter Posy, who is six and afraid of moths, would not come near the window. So I have made a plan. I shall stitch a coverlet of twelve squares, one moth for each month, exactly as they come to my lamp. This one I shall work in plain grey silk."],["The Herald in the Coal Cellar","February in Needle Lane. No moth came to my lamp all month, and I, Lettice Brome, began to fear my coverlet of twelve moths would have a bare square in its first row.\n\nThen I went down for coal. On the cellar wall hung a moth with its wings folded like a roof. The edges were scalloped, as if cut with pinking shears. It was the colour of a rusted hinge, with two orange patches and a fine white line across each wing. The carrier's wife calls it the herald, and says it sleeps in cellars and sheds from autumn until spring, living on nothing.\n\nSo February's moth did not come to my window. I went to it instead. I drew it by candlelight on the back of a bill, and took my coal from the other bin. Its square is rust-brown wool, with two small knots in orange."],["A Moth Like a Butterfly","March. The sallow by the pump in Needle Lane is in yellow catkin, and at dusk it hums with small brown moths come to drink. I have watched them from my sewing window with the lamp turned low.\n\nOne came across to the glass on the ninth. I, Lettice Brome, took it for a butterfly at first, for it sat with its wings pressed together above its back, and no other moth I know does so. They were tawny, with ragged edges like a torn leaf. It is called the early thorn. The carrier's wife says there are a dozen thorn moths in the year and this is the first out.\n\nMy goddaughter Posy looked at my drawing, though not at the window. She said it was praying. I have stitched it in profile for that reason, in fawn silk, which is harder than it sounds."],["Four Eyes at the Glass","April, and something large struck my window in Needle Lane hard enough to rattle the lamp. I looked up from my hemming straight into four eyes.\n\nIt was an emperor moth, as wide across as my palm. She is grey and rose, and on each of her four wings there is a round black eye ringed with yellow, complete even to a white glint, as though it were wet. The carrier's wife says the eyes are there to startle birds. They startled a seamstress. Her kind live on the heath by the marsh, where the caterpillars eat heather.\n\nPosy was with me. She shrieked, hid behind the curtain, and then, since the moth sat still, came out by inches. \"It is looking at me,\" she said. I, Lettice Brome, said it was only looking like looking. This square has taken eleven colours and most of the month."],["White Ermine","May in Needle Lane. The evenings are light now and I light my lamp late, but the moths know the hour, and the fifth square of my coverlet walked onto the glass at ten o'clock.\n\nIt was white, with a scatter of black dots, and a furry white cape round its shoulders. The carrier's wife had the name at once: the white ermine, after the spotted fur that trims a judge's robe. When I tapped the pane it did not fly. It curled its body and showed a flash of yellow beneath, which is a warning, she says, that it tastes foul.\n\nIt is the easiest moth I have stitched: white silk, black seed-stitches, done in two evenings. My goddaughter Posy counted the dots on the drawing and made it thirty-one. She asked whether the real one had as many. I, Lettice Brome, said she might come and count."],["Pink at the Honeysuckle","June. I did not need the lamp for this one. At dusk the honeysuckle under my window in Needle Lane smells strongest, and that is when the elephant hawk-moth comes to it.\n\nNobody warned me a moth could be pink. It is rose and olive green, like a sweet from a jar. It is plump and swift, and it does not settle on the flower. It hangs in the air before it with its wings a blur, and unrolls a tongue as long as its body. The elephant in the name is its caterpillar, which has a snout like a trunk.\n\nPosy watched from my lap with the window open, which three months ago she would not have borne. She held quite still. I, Lettice Brome, have used the pink silk I was saving for a christening gown. The coverlet is half done: six squares, none of them dull."],["The Tiger and the Woolly Bear","July, and the warm nights bring so many moths to my lamp in Needle Lane that I sew with the window shut and choose among them. This month there was no choosing. The garden tiger chose itself.\n\nIts front wings are cream, covered with chocolate-brown blotches like the map of some country all islands. Then it lifts them, and beneath are hind wings of bright orange spotted with blue-black. I gasped aloud. The carrier's wife says the colours tell birds to leave it be.\n\nIts caterpillar is the woolly bear, brown and long-haired, which my goddaughter Posy kept in a box all spring and fed on dock leaves without a notion of what it would become. I, Lettice Brome, showed her my drawing. She looked from the box to the paper and back. \"Mine will do that?\" It spun its cocoon in June. We are both waiting."],["An Old Lady Calls","August in Needle Lane. I found this month's moth behind the parlour curtain when I shook it out: a large dark creature that dropped to the sill and sat there with its wings spread flat.\n\nIt is called the old lady. The wings are brown and grey, in fine wavy bands, like the shawl my grandmother wore to chapel. The carrier's wife says it hides in houses and sheds by day, and comes out at night for sweet things. It cares little for lamps but will come to a smear of jam.\n\nSo an old lady came to call upon an old lady, and I, Lettice Brome, gave her jam on a saucer. My goddaughter Posy thought this the funniest thing that had ever happened. Her woolly bear has hatched: a garden tiger, orange and cream. She opened the box at my window herself and let it go."],["The Crumpled Leaf","September. I, Lettice Brome, swept a dead leaf off my windowsill in Needle Lane, and the leaf opened its wings and flew back.\n\nIt is a moth called the angle shades. At rest it folds its wings lengthwise into creases, so that it looks like a withered leaf, curled at the edges. The colours are olive and pinkish brown, with a darker triangle across the middle. I have sewn pleats all my life and never made one so convincing.\n\nIt set me a difficulty. A moth that looks like a leaf, embroidered, looks like an embroidered leaf. I unpicked the square twice. In the end I stitched it sitting upon a leaf of green felt, so the eye can tell them apart. My goddaughter Posy found it in four seconds. She is seven now, and has begun a moth list of her own, in pencil, with eleven names."],["The Marvel of the Day","October. The oak at the end of Needle Lane is turning, and from it, on the first cold night, came the moth I count the most beautiful of my year at this window.\n\nIt is green. Not leaf-green, but the pale, silvery green of the lichen on old bark, marbled with black and white. It has a foreign name, merveille du jour, which the carrier's wife tells me means \"marvel of the day\". It lives on oaks. Set on a lichened branch it would vanish entirely; on my window glass, with the lamp behind, it shone like a brooch.\n\nI, Lettice Brome, had no silk of that colour. I mixed two threads in one needle, sea-green and grey, and the effect is nearly right. Ten squares are done. Posy has laid them out on the floor in their order and announced that the coverlet wants only two more moths."],["Feathers in the Fog","November in Needle Lane, and fog most nights. I, Lettice Brome, sat nine evenings at my sewing window without a moth, and trimmed the lamp so often that I wore out a wick.\n\nOn the tenth evening one came out of the murk and clung to the frame. It was the colour of a fox, with two dark lines across each wing. Its antennae were what held me. They were feathered on both sides, like two tiny ferns. The carrier's wife calls it the feathered thorn. The male wears those plumes, she says, to scent a female through a quarter-mile of fog, though he cannot see his own feet.\n\nI worked the feathers in single strands of brown silk. Eleven squares. My goddaughter Posy asks every day whether December has a moth at all. I have told her the truth, which is that I do not know."],["The December Moth","December. The answer came on a mild, wet night a week before midwinter. My goddaughter Posy was staying in Needle Lane, and it was she who saw it first and called me from the stairs.\n\nA moth sat on the glass of my sewing window, stout and furry as a bumblebee in an overcoat. It was charcoal-dark, with a cream line curving across each wing. The carrier's wife says it is named the December moth, and that it never eats: it has no mouth to speak of, and lives its few weeks on what it stored as a caterpillar.\n\nPosy held the lamp steady while I drew. She was not afraid, and has not been for months. I, Lettice Brome, stitched the last square in black wool. The coverlet has twelve moths, one for every month at my window. It is on Posy's bed now. She says they keep watch."]],[["The Winter Cluster","I am Silas Combe, and I keep bees in the orchard at Hollowbank, a mile out of Emberwick on the Lowland road. This book follows one hive, the blue one under the old pear, through a single year. I begin in January, with snow on its roof.\n\nYou would think it empty. Put your ear to the wood, though, and there is a low, steady hum, like a kettle a long way off. Inside, some ten thousand bees hang in a ball upon the combs, with their queen at the centre. They do not sleep. They shiver their wing muscles to make heat, and take turns, the cold ones on the outside working inward.\n\nTheir fuel is the honey they stored in summer. The ball creeps slowly across the comb, eating as it goes. My whole task this month is to leave them alone. It is harder than it sounds."],["First Flights","February at Hollowbank orchard. On the first mild noon of the year the bees of the blue hive came out, a few dozen at a time, flew a short loop in the sunshine and went back in.\n\nI, Silas Combe, had been waiting for it. A bee will not foul her own hive. She holds on through weeks of cold, and on the first warm day she must fly. After that there is work: the dead of the winter are carried out and dropped in the grass, tidy as a housemaid shaking a cloth.\n\nBy the month's end they were on the snowdrops and crocuses under the wall, coming home with orange pellets of pollen packed on their hind legs. Pollen is food for the young. So I know, without opening the lid, that in the warm middle of the cluster the queen has begun to lay."],["Her Majesty","March. On a warm afternoon I, Silas Combe, lifted the lid of the blue hive at Hollowbank for the first time this year, and went looking for one bee among thousands.\n\nI found her on the fourth comb. The queen is longer than her daughters, with a tapering body and short wings, and she walks slowly while everything around her hurries. A ring of workers faces her always, touching and feeding her. I call her Madam. She is in her third spring.\n\nShe is not a ruler. She gives no orders. She is the mother of every bee in the hive, and her life is to lay eggs: one to a cell, a white grain stood on end, many hundreds in a day. Round her the comb was filled in arcs: eggs, then pearly grubs, then cells capped with brown wax. I closed the lid. All is well."],["The House Bees","April, with the plum blossom out at Hollowbank orchard and the blue hive growing by the day. This month I write about the bees a visitor never sees, the young ones who have not yet been outdoors.\n\nA worker bee's life is a ladder of trades. New-hatched, she cleans cells. In a few days she becomes a nurse, feeding the grubs many hundreds of times a day. At about twelve days she can make wax. It comes from her own body in little clear flakes under the belly, which she chews and moulds. With it the builders raise the comb, each cell six-sided and each wall shared with its neighbours, with nothing wasted.\n\nI, Silas Combe, have been a joiner's son and a joiner's customer, and I could not build so true with a rule and square. They do it in the dark, by feel, hanging in chains."],["The Dancers","May, and the apple blossom at Hollowbank orchard is so loud with bees that you hear it from the lane. These are the foragers of the blue hive, the oldest workers, in the last trade of their lives.\n\nA forager flies as far as two miles and visits hundreds of flowers for one load of nectar, which she hands to a house bee at the door. In a whole lifetime she makes about a twelfth of a teaspoon of honey. I think of that whenever I am careless with a spoon.\n\nThe best of it is the dance. A bee who finds good blossom comes home and runs a pattern on the comb, a short line with a waggle, and her sisters crowd to follow. The line gives the direction and the waggle the distance. I, Silas Combe, have watched it through glass and I still take off my hat."],["The Swarm","June. On a hot morning the blue hive at Hollowbank poured bees out of its door like water from a jug. The air above the orchard went dark and roaring. Then, in a quarter of an hour, the whole cloud settled on a bough of the old pear and hung there in a quiet bunch the size of a ham.\n\nA swarm is how one hive becomes two. The colony had grown too crowded. So the old queen, Madam, left with half the bees to seek a new home, and behind her the workers are rearing new queens in long cells like acorns.\n\nSwarming bees are full of honey and gentle. I, Silas Combe, held a straw basket under the bough and gave it one sharp shake. By evening Madam and her followers were walking up a white sheet into a new hive. I have painted it green."],["The Young Queen","July at Hollowbank. The old queen has gone with her swarm to the green hive, and for three weeks the blue hive under the pear tree has had me walking about at night.\n\nA hive without a laying queen is a hive running down. The first new queen to hatch made a thin, high piping sound which I heard with my ear to the wood. Some days later, on a fine afternoon, she flew out alone to mate on the wing with drones from hives all over the parish. Drones are the male bees: big, loud, large-eyed, stingless, and good for that one thing. A swallow might have taken her. A shower might.\n\nOn the twentieth I, Silas Combe, opened the hive and found a comb with eggs in it. She is home, and laying. I sat down on the grass for some time."],["Taking the Honey","August. The lime trees along the Hollowbank lane have finished flowering and the blue hive is heavy. This is the month I take my rent.\n\nThe bees store honey in the upper box. They carry in nectar thin as water, fan it with their wings until it thickens, and then seal each full cell with a lid of white wax. On a still evening I, Silas Combe, lifted out the sealed combs with a little smoke to keep everyone calm. The smoke does no harm. It sends the bees to fill themselves with honey, and a full bee is a mild one.\n\nI took twenty-eight pounds and left them the lower box untouched, forty pounds at the least, for that is their winter and not mine to touch. The kitchen at Hollowbank smells of limes and warm wax, and every wasp in the Lowlands has learned my address."],["The Guards at the Gate","September at Hollowbank orchard, and the wasps have found the blue hive. With the flowers failing, every sweet-toothed thing in the parish would like to rob a bee's larder. This month belongs to the guards.\n\nThey stand at the entrance on four legs with the front pair raised, and they meet each arrival. A bee of the hive carries the hive's own smell and passes. A stranger is challenged; a wasp is seized by two or three and bundled off the board. I, Silas Combe, have watched one guard tow a wasp twice her size to the edge and drop it over.\n\nMy part is small. I narrowed the doorway to the width of two bees, so that a few can hold it against many. The young queen's daughters are doing the guarding now. They have their mother's steadiness, and, I am sorry to say, her temper."],["Ivy Honey and Idle Drones","October. The last feast of the bee year at Hollowbank is the ivy on the orchard wall, whose plain green flowers open when everything else is over. On a sunny noon the whole wall hums, and the blue hive brings home a pale honey that sets hard as candle wax in the comb.\n\nThe drones' summer is ended. They cannot gather food, and the hive cannot feed idlers through the winter, so the workers walk them firmly to the door and will not let them back. It looks harsh. It is only housekeeping, and new drones will be raised in spring.\n\nSomething better is happening inside. The bees born now are winter bees. A summer worker wears herself out in six weeks. These are plumper, do little flying, and will live six months, long enough to carry the queen through to the crocuses. I, Silas Combe, envy them their timing."],["Shutting Up for Winter","November. I, Silas Combe, have done the last of my work on the blue hive at Hollowbank until spring, and a list will serve.\n\nOne: a strip of tin with small holes across the door, to let bees out and keep mice from moving in. A mouse thinks a hive a fine larder. Two: a stone on the roof against the gales. Three: I lifted one side of the hive an inch to judge its weight, and it nearly pulled my arm down, so they have honey enough. Four: I left every crack alone. The bees have sealed them with a brown tree-gum, better than any putty.\n\nFive is a custom. In these parts you tell the bees the news of the household, quietly, at the door. I told them the pear tree cropped well, my daughter is to marry in spring, and the green hive thrives."],["A Candle at Midwinter","December at Hollowbank, the shortest day, and frost on the orchard grass. I went out and put my ear to the blue hive, as I did in January. The same low hum, like a kettle a long way off. They are clustered around their young queen, shivering themselves warm on lime and ivy honey.\n\nSo the year is told. One hive became two. A queen left and a queen was made. Many thousands of bees were born in that box since spring and wore out their wings in the orchard, and hardly one of those now humming was alive when I began this book.\n\nFrom the wax of the August combs I, Silas Combe, have made one candle. It is burning in the kitchen window as I write, and smells of summer. When it is done I shall go to bed. The bees will keep the fire in."]],[["A Hole in the Lawn","I am Oswin Tench, schoolmaster, retired, of the Old Schoolhouse in Emberwick. This March I dug a pond in my back lawn, nine feet by six and two feet deep in the middle, with one shelving side so that anything falling in may walk out again.\n\nI lined it with clay from the marsh, trodden down in my oldest boots, and let the rain fill it. That took eleven days. It is now a brown rectangle of water with nothing in it whatever. No weed, no snail, not so much as a gnat.\n\nMy neighbour Mrs Aldous looked over the wall and said I had made a breeding place for midges and would be sorry. Possibly. But I have put nothing in on purpose. I want to see who comes of their own accord, and in what order, and I shall write them down here for one full year."],["First Across the Water","April. The pond I dug behind the Old Schoolhouse is five weeks old, and on the ninth it received its first visitors. I, Oswin Tench, looked out after breakfast and there were three pond skaters standing on the water.\n\nThey had flown in. Few people know that a pond skater has wings, folded flat along its back. It finds new water by the shine.\n\nOn the surface it is a marvel. The water has a kind of skin, and the skater stands upon it on four long legs whose feet are covered in fine hairs that will not wet. Each foot makes a dimple and does not break through. It rows with the middle pair, steers with the hind pair, and keeps the short front pair free to seize any fly that falls in. It feels the ripples of a struggling insect as a spider feels its web."],["The Rowers Underneath","May. My new pond behind the Old Schoolhouse has grown a green tinge and its second kind of resident. Pond skaters own the top of the water. The underside now belongs to the backswimmer.\n\nIt is a boat-shaped bug half an inch long, and it does everything upside down. It hangs beneath the surface on its back, with two long legs held out like oars, and when a shadow falls it rows downward in quick jerks. Its underside, which faces the sky, is dark. Its back, which faces the deep, is pale. A film of air clings to it, so that it flashes silver as it dives.\n\nI, Oswin Tench, lifted one out in a tea-strainer, and it flipped over and flew away, which neither of us expected. A word of caution for the record: it can prick a finger sharply. I admire it through a jar."],["Mrs Aldous Is Proved Right","June. My neighbour Mrs Aldous warned me in March that a pond would breed gnats, and I, Oswin Tench, must record that she was correct. The pond behind the Old Schoolhouse is full of wrigglers.\n\nThey are gnat larvae, a quarter-inch long. Each hangs head-down from the surface by a breathing tube at its tail, like a comma suspended from the ceiling. Tap the bank and they all let go and wriggle to the bottom, then drift up again. Their mothers laid the eggs in floating rafts, each no bigger than a caraway seed.\n\nI told Mrs Aldous over the wall. She was gracious in victory. I have also noted this: the backswimmers eat them all day, the pond skaters take the adults as they hatch, and four whirligig beetles arrived on Thursday and circle like sharks. A pond feeds itself. We shall see who is sorry."],["Blue Needles","July, and the pond behind the Old Schoolhouse is four months old. I planted nothing, but a water forget-me-not has arrived by itself on some bird's foot, and its stems now matter very much.\n\nAll this week damselflies have come to them. They are thin as darning needles and brighter than any thread: the males sky-blue ringed with black, the females duller green. They fly weakly, like blown thistledown, and at rest they fold their wings together along the back. They arrive in couples, the male holding the female by the neck with the tip of his tail. So joined, they settle on a stem. She curves her body down and slits the stem below the water-line, placing one egg in each cut.\n\nI, Oswin Tench, counted nine pairs on Tuesday. That is a great many eggs entrusted to a pond that was lawn in February. I feel the responsibility."],["The Hawker's Beat","August. A dragonfly has taken possession of my pond behind the Old Schoolhouse. He is a hawker, four inches across the wings, striped apple-green and blue, and he patrols the nine feet of water like a constable on a very short street.\n\nI, Oswin Tench, have timed him: eleven seconds a circuit. He can hover, dart sideways, and fly backwards. His eyes are so large that they meet on top of his head. He hunts by catching gnats in the basket of his bristly legs, and he rattles when he turns. When I stand at the edge he flies up and inspects me, a foot from my nose, then carries on.\n\nMrs Aldous has observed over the wall that there are fewer gnats in her garden this month than in any August she remembers. She said it to her washing, but I believe it was intended for me."],["Sticks That Walk","September. The pond behind the Old Schoolhouse has a carpet of dead leaves now, and I, Oswin Tench, spent an hour watching a small twig on the bottom before I was sure that it was walking.\n\nIt was a caddis larva. It is a soft grub, and for protection it builds a tube of silk and sticks on whatever lies to hand: bits of leaf, lengths of stem, grains of grit, sometimes an unlucky snail shell. Only its head and six legs poke out of the front. It drags the house along and backs into it at a touch. Each kind keeps to its own style, as builders do.\n\nNext summer it will seal the door, change inside, and swim up to hatch into a mothlike fly with hairy wings. I found seven in all. One has built entirely with forget-me-not seeds and is the best-dressed animal in the garden."],["A Beetle from the Sky","October. Something landed on my greenhouse roof at night with a crack, and in the morning I, Oswin Tench, found a large dark beetle on its back in the gutter. I carried it to the pond behind the Old Schoolhouse, where it had surely been aiming. To a beetle flying by moonlight, glass shines like water.\n\nIt is the great diving beetle: an inch and a quarter long, olive-black, trimmed with yellow all round. It swam down at once, rowing both hind legs together. Every few minutes it floats up tail-first and takes a fresh store of air beneath its wing-cases.\n\nThe pond is changing guard. The pond skaters, first to come in April, are leaving. I watched two walk ashore across the mud and into the long grass, where they will sleep the winter under moss. The water looks empty again, but I know better now."],["What the Net Brought Up","November. Leaves from the schoolhouse lime have been falling into my pond, and too many will sour the water. So I, Oswin Tench, have been fishing them out with a kitchen sieve tied to a bean pole, and sorting each load in a white pie dish before it goes on the heap.\n\nIt is slow. Everything alive must be picked out and returned. But it is how I found them. Among the leaves crawled slim, pale brown creatures half an inch long, with six legs, wide-set eyes and three leaf-shaped tails. Damselfly nymphs. The eggs laid in the forget-me-not stems in July have hatched, and the young are living on the bottom, hunting water fleas.\n\nI counted fourteen in six sievefuls and put back every one. There must be hundreds. They will spend the winter down there. Mrs Aldous asked what I was grinning at. I said, \"Tenants.\""],["Under the Ice","January, a hard frost, and the pond behind the Old Schoolhouse has a lid of ice an inch thick. I, Oswin Tench, have been lying on a sack at the edge, looking through it, to the alarm of the postman.\n\nIt is clear as a window. On its underside hang silver bubbles, and among them a backswimmer, upside down as ever, rowing slowly along like a fly on a ceiling. Water is heaviest a little above freezing, so the deepest part of the pond stays just warm enough, and everything alive has gone down there to wait.\n\nOne must never smash pond ice; the shock harms what lies below. Instead I stand a pan of hot water on it until it melts a round hole, for air. Mrs Aldous lent me the pan. She asked whether the damselfly nymphs were all right. I said I believed so."],["One Year of Water","March again. A year ago this week I, Oswin Tench, dug a hole in the lawn of the Old Schoolhouse, and it held brown rainwater and nothing else. This morning I sat beside it with my notebook and called the roll.\n\nPond skaters, who came first and came back on Tuesday, five of them. Backswimmers. Whirligig beetles. Gnats, as foretold. Damselflies, nine pairs, and their nymphs, now nearly an inch long. One hawker dragonfly. Caddis, seven. One great diving beetle, by air. Water fleas beyond counting. Also a forget-me-not, three snails and a frog, none of whom are insects, but one must be civil. I invited none of them. Dig a hole, add rain, and wait: that is the entire method.\n\nMrs Aldous came round by the gate, not the wall, and brought a marsh marigold in a bucket. She thought it would suit the shallow end. It does."],["The First-Born","May. For fourteen months I, Oswin Tench, have watched creatures arrive at the pond behind the Old Schoolhouse. This morning, for the first time, I watched one leave that had never been anywhere else.\n\nAt seven o'clock a damselfly nymph climbed out of the water up a forget-me-not stem, the same plant its egg was laid in last July. It gripped and went still. Then its back split, and something pale and crumpled pulled itself out and hung beside the empty skin. Over an hour the wings unfolded like wet silk drying. The body lengthened. A faint blue came into it.\n\nMrs Aldous watched with me from the bench, holding her tea and not drinking it. After nine it lifted off, weak as thistledown, and went over the wall into her garden. The empty skin is still on the stem. Underneath it, the pond skaters row on."]],[["The Ferry at the Ford","Emberwick counts its years from a rope. In the year the town calls One, a widow named Maud Fenner stretched a tarred line across the river at the shallow place and began pulling travellers over on a flat raft, a penny a head, sheep at four a penny.\n\nShe kept an iron basket of coals burning on the north bank so that carters coming late off the Lowland road could find the crossing in the dark. People said, \"Make for the ember.\" Then, when a dairy and two cottages had gathered round it, they said, \"Make for Emberwick,\" a wick being the old word for a farm that sells its butter. The river took the name as well, in time.\n\nMaud's ferry bell still hangs by the water. It is rung once each New Year, by whoever gets there first."],["Seven Households and a Market","By the Year 12 there were seven households at Emberwick: the Fenners at the ferry, a smith, a dairy, two families of weavers, a cooper, and a man named Hobb who did nothing in particular but was always there.\n\nThat autumn the seven agreed, under the oak on the green, to hold a market every Wednesday. No lord granted it. They simply wrote the rules on a board: honest weights, no selling before the bell, and stray pigs to be returned washed. The board hangs in the Guildhall, and the third rule has never been repealed.\n\nThe first market offered cheese, nails, one barrel and a quantity of opinions. It took in four shillings. The oak came down in a gale long ago, but Wednesday has gone on being market day ever since, in all weathers."],["The First Bridge","For thirty-seven years everyone who came to Emberwick came by Maud Fenner's ferry, and waited their turn. By the Year 38 the Wednesday market had grown so busy that the queue of carts on the south bank reached back to the Lowland road, and the town decided to build a bridge over the Ember.\n\nIt was made of oak, on eleven trestles, by a carpenter named Jory Plank, who was teased about his name every day of the work and answered each time with the same patient nod. Maud Fenner, by then very old, was carried across first in a chair. She said it was quicker but less sociable.\n\nThe ferry raft was hauled up the bank and turned over to make the roof of a hen house. Nothing in Emberwick is thrown away if it can keep off rain."],["A Mayor by Show of Hats","Until the Year 52 Emberwick was governed by whoever shouted longest at the Wednesday market. This worked while there were seven households. With ninety it produced sore throats and two different rules about where to tie a horse.\n\nSo the townsfolk met on the green to choose one person to do the deciding. There were no ballot papers, paper being dear. Each voter threw a hat toward the candidate preferred, and the hats were counted. Aldous Fenner, grandson of the ferrywoman, received sixty-one hats and one bonnet thrown from a window.\n\nHis chain of office was made from the brass rings of the old ferry rope, and every mayor since has worn it. It is heavy and turns the collar green. Mayors are still chosen on the green in June, though hats are now kept on."],["The Great Damp","The Year 66 is remembered in Emberwick as the Great Damp. It rained for thirty days from the first of March, the snow came off the Northern Fells all at once, and the river Ember rose nine feet in a night.\n\nNobody was lost. The town owes that to old Mayor Fenner, who rowed from door to door in a brewing tub, and to the ferry bell, which was rung until its rope wore through. But the timber bridge went, whole, like a raft. So did the lower street, a great many hen houses, and the smith's anvil, which was later found exactly where it had always been, under two feet of silt.\n\nWhen the water went down, the town moved itself forty yards up the hill, where it has stood ever since. The old cellars are gardens now, and grow excellent rhubarb."],["The Wool Years","Between the Year 90 and the Year 104, Emberwick grew rich, by its own modest measure, on wool. Fell sheep came down the drove road each June, were shorn on the green, and went home looking embarrassed. The fleeces were washed in the Ember, spun in the cottages, and woven in the long sheds still called Weavers' Row.\n\nEmberwick cloth was a thick grey stuff known as wickset. It was not handsome, but rain ran off it, and Lowland carters would wear nothing else.\n\nWith the profits the wool merchants raised the Guildhall in the Year 104, with a carved ram over the door. The ram has lost one horn to weather and one to a boy with a ladder. The trade faded when fashion turned to finer cloth, but the hall remained, and the town has met in it ever since."],["The Slow Clock","In the Year 140 Emberwick built a clock tower on the market square, paid for by a penny on every stall for three years. The clock was made by a travelling clockmaker who left the morning it was finished, which the town later thought significant.\n\nIt has always run seven minutes slow. Clockmakers were sent for, weights were shifted, the pendulum was shortened by the width of a thumbnail, and for a week it would keep proper time. Then it would settle back, as a cat returns to its own chair.\n\nMayor Ezekiel Moss ended the argument by declaring that the clock was right and the sun was early. Emberwick has kept its own time since. Visitors are advised that the Wednesday market opens at eight by the tower, and that the coach will not wait for them."],["The Quarrel with Marrowby","Every town likes to have one war in its history, and Emberwick's lasted from the Year 163 to the Year 171. It was fought against the village of Marrowby, two miles east, over a three-acre goose pasture called Gander Meadow, after the Pennybrook changed its course in a flood and left the meadow on the wrong bank.\n\nThe chronicle must record the facts honestly. Eleven geese were impounded. A declaration was sent with a penny too little paid on it. Two small armies met at a stile, were rained upon, and shared a barn. Signposts were turned in the night. Nobody was hurt, though a drum was sat on.\n\nPeace was signed beneath a pear tree, and the meadow has been shared ever since. The fuller account fills twelve volumes of its own, which is a good deal more shelf than three acres deserve."],["The Long Frost","The frost of the Year 190 began in Emberwick on the ninth of December and did not let go until the first week of March. The river Ember froze so hard that a loaded hay cart was driven across beside the bridge, simply to show it could be done.\n\nMayor Dorcas Bellwether had soup served from the Guildhall every noon and ordered the Wednesday market onto the ice, where it stayed for eleven weeks. There were stalls, skittles, a roasting pit that slowly sank an inch, and a barber who shaved customers in a sledge.\n\nWhen the thaw came the ice went out in one night with a noise like furniture being moved. It cracked the middle arch of the bridge. A line cut in the Guildhall doorpost shows how deep the snow lay that winter, and children are measured against it still."],["Four Thousand Books","Emberwick Library was founded in the Year 214, somewhat by accident. Mrs Isolde Marram, widow of a sea captain from the Grey Coast, left the town her husband's books: four thousand of them, in forty tea chests, delivered to the Guildhall steps on a wet Tuesday without warning.\n\nMayor Septimus Vole, who read slowly and admitted it, said that a town given four thousand books had better become the sort of town that deserved them. The council bought the old wool warehouse on Lantern Street, shelved it with oak from the Fells, and hired a librarian at twelve pounds a year and all the candles she needed.\n\nThe books arrived in no order at all, and it is said that they have never entirely been put right. Each generation of shelvers has done its best, mostly at night."],["The Canal That Stopped","In the Year 240 a company of gentlemen from the Lowlands proposed to dig a canal to Emberwick, and bring it coal, prosperity and bargemen. Mayor Clementine Rudge was doubtful but polite. Shares were sold. A fine iron footbridge was put up in advance, to carry the meadow path over the water when the water arrived.\n\nThe cut reached to within one mile of the town and then met a bed of gravel that drank every drop poured into it. The company dissolved. The bargemen never came.\n\nEmberwick was left with a dry green trench full of primroses, an iron bridge over nothing, and its quiet. Twenty-eight years later Mayor Oake lit the streets with forty lamps instead, which cost less and worked. Most people now agree that the town had a narrow and fortunate escape."],["The Three Hundredth Year","This chronicle closes in the Year 300, three centuries after Maud Fenner stretched her rope across the Ember. The town now has some two thousand people, one bridge of three arches, a clock seven minutes slow, a Wednesday market, a library and a half share in a goose meadow.\n\nOn New Year's morning the whole of Emberwick walked down to the water. The youngest Fenner, a girl of six, was lifted up to ring the ferry bell, and an iron basket of coals was lit on the north bank, as on the first night.\n\nI have been Town Chronicler for thirty-one years, and have tried to set down what happened without making it grander than it was. The pen now passes to my successor. There are blank pages left at the back of the book, and I am glad of that."]],[["The Stream Moves","The Border Wars between the town of Emberwick and the village of Marrowby began with water, in the spring of the Year 163. For as long as anyone had kept records, the boundary between the two parishes had been the Pennybrook, a stream that a child could jump.\n\nThat March the brook flooded, and when it settled it was found to be running forty yards east of where it had been. Stranded on its western side lay Gander Meadow: three acres of grass, one pear tree, and the grazing of all Marrowby's geese. The meadow was now, by the plain rule of the brook, in Emberwick.\n\nEmberwick said a boundary was a boundary. Marrowby said a brook was not entitled to give away other people's property. Both were right, which is the worst way for a quarrel to start."],["The Affair of the Geese","In April of the Year 163, a few weeks after the Pennybrook shifted and left Gander Meadow on the Emberwick side, the geese of Marrowby walked across to graze there as they always had. Geese do not read parish maps.\n\nThe Emberwick pound-keeper, a careful man named Silas Trott, found eleven of them on what was now town grass and did his duty. He drove them two miles to the pound behind the Guildhall, which took him four hours and cost him the seat of his breeches.\n\nThe geese belonged to Mrs Philippa Crake of Marrowby, who arrived the next morning and demanded them back. Trott asked for the usual fine of a penny a head. Mrs Crake said she would sooner eat her hat. The geese stayed, were fed at the town's expense, and grew noticeably stout."],["A Declaration by Post","By May of the Year 163 the dispute over Gander Meadow had reached the council chamber of Emberwick, where Mayor Horatio Pim felt that something official ought to be done. He drafted a Declaration of Stern Displeasure against the village of Marrowby, in three paragraphs, with a seal.\n\nIt went by the carrier on a Monday. Unhappily the clerk had paid a penny too little, and the rule was that the receiver made up the difference. Marrowby declined. The village elders said they would not pay a penny to be insulted, and that if Emberwick wanted a war it could afford the postage.\n\nThe letter went back to the carrier's office and lay there. For three weeks, strictly speaking, both sides were at peace and did not know it. In the end Mayor Pim walked over and read his copy aloud through Mrs Crake's gate."],["The Mustering","War having been declared in the summer of the Year 163, at least out loud, Emberwick and Marrowby each raised an army to settle the matter of Gander Meadow.\n\nEmberwick's force was the Town Volunteers: fourteen men, one drum, and a pike borrowed from above the Guildhall fireplace, under Captain Barnaby Oddfellow, a candle-maker with a carrying voice. They drilled on the green on Thursdays, since Wednesday was market day and nobody could be spared.\n\nMarrowby mustered nine, but had a brass band of six besides, and gave the command to Mrs Philippa Crake, owner of the impounded geese, with the rank of Colonel. She drilled her troops in the meadow lane on Thursdays too. For some weeks the two armies could hear one another practising across the fields, and each privately thought the other sounded rather good."],["The Battle of the Stile","The only battle of the Border Wars took place on the fourteenth of August in the Year 163, at the stile where the footpath from Emberwick enters Gander Meadow. Fourteen Volunteers under Captain Oddfellow arrived from the west. Nine men of Marrowby and a brass band, under Colonel Crake, arrived from the east.\n\nThe stile admits one person at a time. Neither commander would climb it first, since whoever did would be standing alone in front of an army. They discussed this for an hour.\n\nThen it rained, heavily. Both armies ran for Farmer Dunnock's barn, which was the nearest roof, and stayed there until dark. Cards were played. The band obliged with a few tunes. A drum was sat on and never quite recovered. Marrowby won ninepence, and has counted the battle a victory ever since."],["The Signpost Campaign","Through the autumn of the Year 163 the war between Emberwick and Marrowby over Gander Meadow was carried on entirely at night, and entirely by carpentry.\n\nIt began when someone from Marrowby pulled up the new Emberwick boundary post and replanted it forty yards west. Emberwick moved it back, and for good measure turned the finger-post at the crossroads so that \"Marrowby\" pointed into the marsh. Marrowby answered by repainting the Emberwick milestone to read two hundred miles.\n\nNobody was ever caught, though both blacksmiths did brisk business in nails. The chief sufferer was a travelling pedlar named Fitch, who followed the signs faithfully for a whole day and arrived, at dusk, back where he had started. He sold both places lanterns at a high price, and declared himself satisfied."],["The Pear Embargo","In the second year of the Border Wars, the Year 164, the village of Marrowby tried to win Gander Meadow by trade. It forbade the sale of pears, perry and pear jam to anyone from Emberwick. Marrowby pears were the best in the district, and the blow was felt.\n\nEmberwick replied by refusing to sell Marrowby any candles, the town having the only chandler for ten miles, who was also its Captain of Volunteers. Marrowby went to bed early that winter, and Emberwick ate its cheese plain.\n\nNeither embargo was well kept. Aunts walked between the two places with remarkably heavy baskets. A Marrowby man was seen buying candles disguised in his wife's bonnet, and was served without comment. By spring the trade in secret pears was larger than the open trade had ever been."],["The Surveyor's Opinion","By the Year 165 Emberwick and Marrowby had quarrelled over Gander Meadow for two years, and agreed on one thing only: to hire a surveyor and abide by what he found. They sent to the Lowlands for Mr Ambrose Lint, who came with a brass chain, a boy and a very small dog.\n\nHe measured for six days. Then he reported to both councils together, in Farmer Dunnock's barn. The meadow, he said, was three acres and eleven perches. Emberwick's map was wrong. Marrowby's map was also wrong, in a different direction. The oldest deed he could find described the land only as \"the goose's\".\n\nHe charged each side his full fee and left by the morning coach. The two councils walked home separately, having learned what expert advice so often teaches, and the war went on."],["The Winter Truce","The winter of the Year 167 was a hard one, and it interrupted the war between Emberwick and Marrowby in its fifth year. In January the Marrowby mill pond froze to the bottom, the wheel stood still, and the village had grain but no flour.\n\nCaptain Oddfellow of the Emberwick Volunteers heard of it on a Monday. On Tuesday twelve sacks of flour went east on a sledge, with a note saying they were a loan between enemies and need not be mentioned. Colonel Crake sent back a crate of pears, the first to cross openly in three years, and a note saying nothing had been received.\n\nHostilities were then suspended for skating. Both armies used the frozen pond, and the Marrowby band played. The truce lasted until the thaw, and everyone was sorry to see it end."],["The Wedding Problem","In the Year 169, six years into the Border Wars, the drummer of the Emberwick Volunteers asked to marry the daughter of the Marrowby bandmaster. His name was Ned Sallow, hers was Tabitha Horn, and they had met in Farmer Dunnock's barn during the Battle of the Stile, sheltering from the rain.\n\nNobody objected to the match. The difficulty was the place. Emberwick would not have its drummer married in enemy country, and Marrowby felt the same about its bandmaster's daughter.\n\nTabitha settled it. She said the one piece of ground that both sides claimed must belong to both, and they were married in Gander Meadow under the pear tree, on a fine June morning. Fourteen Volunteers stood on one side and the band on the other. Eleven very fat geese were let out of the pound for the day."],["The Stream Moves Back","What the Pennybrook began, it also did its best to finish. In the spring of the Year 170, seven years after it first shifted and set Emberwick and Marrowby quarrelling over Gander Meadow, the brook flooded again.\n\nThis time it did not choose a side. When the water fell, it was running straight through the middle of the meadow, an acre and a half to the west and an acre and a half to the east, as neatly as if it had been ruled. The pear tree stood on a small island of its own, with water on both sides.\n\nBoth councils came to look. They stood on their own banks for some time. Then Mayor Pim of Emberwick remarked that he supposed that was that, and Colonel Crake said she had been thinking much the same."],["The Treaty of the Pear Tree","The Border Wars ended on Midsummer Day in the Year 171, when Emberwick and Marrowby signed the Treaty of the Pear Tree on a table carried over the water into Gander Meadow for the purpose. It has three clauses. The meadow belongs to both parishes. Geese of either may graze it. Nobody is to mention the signposts.\n\nThe eleven impounded geese were returned to Mrs Crake after eight years, with their descendants, numbering fifty-four. She waived the question of the fine, and Emberwick waived the bill for feed.\n\nEach Midsummer since, the two places have held a tug of war across the Pennybrook, and the losers are pulled in. The original Declaration of Stern Displeasure is still at the carrier's office in Emberwick, unopened, with a penny owing on it. No one has offered to pay."]],[["Aldous Fenner, the First","Aldous Fenner became the first Mayor of Emberwick in the Year 52, chosen by a show of hats on the green. He was grandson to Maud Fenner, who founded the ferry, and had inherited her broad shoulders and her habit of counting everything twice.\n\nHe was mayor for fourteen years and made three laws: carts keep to the left on the bridge, the market bell is rung at eight, and nobody empties a bucket from an upper window without calling out first. All three stand.\n\nHis great hour came at the end. In the flood of the Year 66 he went from door to door in a brewer's tub, at the age of seventy, lifting neighbours from their windows. He retired the following week, saying he had done enough rowing for one family, and kept bees until he died."],["Gwendolen Ashby Moves the Town","Gwendolen Ashby was elected the second Mayor of Emberwick in the Year 66, a week after the great flood, while the lower street was still under silt. She was a builder's widow who ran the yard herself, and she is said to have accepted the chain with a trowel in her other hand.\n\nHer decision was simple and unpopular: the town would move forty yards up the hill. She paced out the new market square herself and marked the corners with four of her own bricks, which are there yet. Those who complained were lent a shovel.\n\nIn fourteen years she saw every household rehoused above the flood line and a new bridge raised in stone. She never made a speech longer than a minute. Asked for her secret, she said that mortar sets whether or not you talk to it."],["Osric Tulley, the Sleeping Mayor","Osric Tulley was Mayor of Emberwick from the Year 80 to the Year 101, the third to wear the chain, and for most of that time he was asleep. He was a miller, stout and amiable, and the warmth of the council chamber overcame him within minutes of every meeting.\n\nThe council learned to manage. Motions were read out, a pause was left, and if the mayor did not object he was held to have agreed. In twenty-one years he objected once, to a draught.\n\nHistorians have been unkind to him, but the facts are awkward for them. Under Tulley the wool trade arrived, the population doubled and the rates were never raised. It may be that a town sometimes needs a mayor who will leave it alone. His portrait in the Guildhall shows him with his eyes shut."],["Marigold Thrupp and the Guildhall","Marigold Thrupp, fourth Mayor of Emberwick, served from the Year 101 to the Year 109. She was a wool merchant who had begun with one borrowed ewe, and she could tell the weight of a fleece by lifting a corner of it.\n\nHer monument is the Guildhall, finished in the Year 104 and paid for entirely by the wool merchants, herself the first and largest giver. She checked the mason's accounts every Friday and found, over three years, a total error of fourpence, in the town's favour, which she returned.\n\nShe disliked waste to a remarkable degree. The Guildhall has one window fewer on the north side than on the south because, she said, nobody needs to look at the north. On leaving office she handed over a surplus of four pounds, a spare roof slate and a list of who still owed what."],["Bartholomew Crane, Lover of Ceremony","Bartholomew Crane was the fifth Mayor of Emberwick and held office from the Year 109 to the Year 131. He was a tailor, and he believed that a town was not properly a town until it had processions.\n\nIn twenty-two years he gave Emberwick nearly all its customs. He invented the Beating of the Bounds, in which the council walks the parish edge each May and taps the boundary stones with willow wands. He designed the mayor's three-cornered hat, the beadle's blue coat, and a march for two fifes that nobody has managed to forget.\n\nHe also proposed a town motto in an ancient tongue, but since no one could agree on what it meant, it was quietly dropped. Crane retired at eighty with a procession of his own devising. It took four hours to pass the Guildhall and was much enjoyed, chiefly by him."],["Ezekiel Moss and the Clock","Ezekiel Moss, sixth Mayor of Emberwick, served from the Year 131 to the Year 148 and is remembered for one sentence. He was a corn dealer, a calm man with large hands, who disliked arguments because they made people late.\n\nIn the Year 140 the town built its clock tower, and the clock at once began to run seven minutes slow. For two years the council debated it. Clockmakers came and went. The baker opened by the sun and the school by the tower, and children arrived at lessons having already eaten their dinners.\n\nMoss rose at last and said, \"The clock is right, and the sun is early.\" It was put to the vote and carried. He governed peacefully for six years more, and was never once late for anything, by the only clock that mattered."],["Lettice Underbough, Planter of Limes","Lettice Underbough was the seventh Mayor of Emberwick, from the Year 148 to the Year 160. She kept a seed shop on Lantern Street and was known to judge people by the state of their window boxes.\n\nHer twelve years were the quietest in the town's record. What she did was plant: forty lime trees along the road to the bridge, a row of pears behind the Guildhall, and a horse chestnut on the green to replace the old market oak, which had blown down before her grandmother was born.\n\nShe was mocked for it at the time, trees being slow and the rates not. But she planted every one herself, with a spade that now hangs in the council chamber. The limes meet overhead today. In July the whole avenue hums with bees and smells of honey as far as the water."],["Horatio Pim, the War Mayor","Horatio Pim, eighth Mayor of Emberwick, served from the Year 160 to the Year 172, and so had the misfortune to be in office for the whole of the Border Wars with Marrowby. He was a stationer by trade and loved a well-drafted document above most things.\n\nIt was Pim who wrote the Declaration of Stern Displeasure in the Year 163, and Pim whose clerk paid a penny too little to send it. It is said he could never afterwards pass the carrier's office without crossing the street.\n\nYet he also wrote the Treaty of the Pear Tree, in the Year 171, and it is a model of its kind: three clauses, no long words, and room at the bottom for everyone to sign. He retired the next year and took up fishing in the Pennybrook, from whichever bank he pleased."],["Dorcas Bellwether in the Frost","Dorcas Bellwether was the ninth Mayor of Emberwick, from the Year 172 to the Year 195. She kept the Ferry Bell inn, and had the innkeeper's gift of knowing at a glance who was cold, who was hungry and who was about to cause trouble.\n\nShe was sixty-eight when the Long Frost came in the Year 190, and it was the making of her name. For eleven weeks she had soup served from the Guildhall at noon, begged coal from every cellar that had it, and sent the Volunteers to knock each morning on the doors of the old. When the river froze solid she moved the market onto it, saying people needed something to look at.\n\nNot one household went without a fire that winter. She served five years more, and when she stepped down the town gave her a silver ladle. She used it."],["Septimus Vole and the Library","Septimus Vole, tenth Mayor of Emberwick, held office from the Year 195 to the Year 223. He was a carter's son who had left school at nine, and he read with one finger under the line, moving his lips, all his life.\n\nIn the Year 214 forty tea chests of books arrived on the Guildhall steps, the bequest of a sea captain's widow. Half the council wished to sell them. Vole would not hear of it. He said he had wanted books as a boy the way other boys wanted apples, and that no child in Emberwick should want them so again.\n\nHe found the warehouse on Lantern Street, bargained for the oak, and carried the first chest in himself. In his last years he was seen in the reading room most evenings, working slowly through a history of bridges, one finger under the line."],["Clementine Rudge, Thirty-Six Years","Clementine Rudge was the eleventh Mayor of Emberwick and the longest in office, from the Year 223 to the Year 259. She was the schoolmistress, and for thirty-six years she governed the council much as she had governed the lower form, with patience and a small brass bell.\n\nHer hardest test was the canal scheme of the Year 240. Gentlemen from the Lowlands promised barges and fortunes. Rudge did the sums on a slate, found that they did not come out, and said so; but the town was eager, and she let it learn by doing. When the cut ran dry a mile short, she did not say she had told them. She merely had primroses planted along it.\n\nBy the end she had taught half the council their letters. She retired at eighty, and they stood up when she left the room."],["Wilfred Oake and the Lamps","Wilfred Oake, twelfth Mayor of Emberwick, served from the Year 259 to the Year 288. He was an ironmonger, a shy, exact man who walked the streets after dark because, he said, a mayor should know what his town is like when nobody is showing it to him.\n\nWhat it was like was dark. In the Year 268 he had forty lamps set up, from the bridge to the library door, and hired two lamplighters. He lit the first himself, on the third attempt, in a wind. Old people complained that they could no longer see the stars from the market square, and Oake had the three lamps there fitted with shutters.\n\nHere these Lives end. There have been mayors since, but they are living, and may borrow this book. It is kinder, and safer, to let them wait their turn."]],[["Before There Were Bridges","The history of bridges at Emberwick must begin with their absence. Before the town had a name, the river Ember was crossed at a shallow place where the bed is gravel and the water, in a dry summer, comes to a tall man's knee. Drovers waded it. Sheep were persuaded.\n\nIn the year the town calls One, Maud Fenner improved on wading with a rope ferry: a flat raft hauled hand over hand along a tarred line. It carried six people, or one cart, or twenty sheep if they were tired.\n\nA ferry is a kind of bridge that moves, and has the same purpose, which is to save people from getting wet. For thirty-seven years it was all Emberwick had. On a low day in August you can still see the gravel of the ford from the present bridge, pale under the water."],["Maud's Bridge","The first true bridge at Emberwick was built in the Year 38 and named for the ferrywoman, Maud Fenner, who had carried the town across the Ember for a generation. It was the work of a carpenter named Jory Plank and took one summer.\n\nIt stood on eleven oak trestles driven into the gravel of the ford, with a deck of split logs laid crossways and a handrail on the upstream side only, timber being short. It was nine feet wide. Two carts could not pass, and the rule was that the one going downhill gave way.\n\nIt creaked. People called this \"Maud talking\". On frosty mornings the deck was strewn with ashes from the ferry coals, a duty that fell to the youngest Fenner. The bridge served twenty-eight years, which for unpainted oak in running water is an honourable life."],["The Bridge That Left","In the flood of the Year 66, known in Emberwick as the Great Damp, the river Ember rose nine feet in a night and Maud's Bridge, the town's first, went away. It did not break. The eleven trestles lifted out of the gravel together and the whole thing floated off downstream, deck uppermost, like a very long raft.\n\nWitnesses said a cat was sitting on it. This cannot now be proved.\n\nThe bridge was found three days later, two miles down, lying across a pasture as if it had meant to cross something there. The farmer claimed it by right of arrival and made the deck into a barn floor. Bridge Barn still stands, and the log planks are worn pale by two centuries of boots. The town was left with a ford, a rowing boat and a strong wish to build in stone."],["Three Arches of Stone","The stone bridge at Emberwick was begun in the Year 67 and opened in the Year 71, under Mayor Gwendolen Ashby, after the flood that carried off its timber forerunner. It has three arches and is the same bridge the town uses now.\n\nThe mason was Hulda Garth, brought down from the Northern Fells with her own quarrymen. She pointed their upstream ends like the bows of boats to part the floodwater, and made the middle arch the widest, at thirty feet. The stone is a grey grit that goes almost silver in rain.\n\nShe was asked how long it would last. She said she had built it for the worst night she could imagine, and then a little more. On the keystone, downstream side, she cut a small ferry bell. You have to lean over to see it."],["The Egg Toll","From its opening in the Year 71 the stone bridge at Emberwick charged a toll, to pay back what the building had cost. A toll house the size of a cupboard stood at the town end, and in it sat a keeper with a ledger.\n\nThe charge was a halfpenny for a person, a penny for a cart, and nothing for anyone carrying a baby. Coin was scarce in the district, so the rule allowed payment in kind: one egg was taken as a halfpenny. On market days the keeper might take two hundred eggs before noon.\n\nThe ledgers survive. They record forty-nine years of eggs, a few buttons, and one entry reading \"a song, badly\". The debt was cleared in the Year 120 and the toll was ended. The last keeper, it is written, never willingly ate an omelette again."],["The Packhorse Bridge","During the wool years, Emberwick cloth went east to market on the backs of ponies, and in the Year 98 the wool merchants paid for a bridge over the Pennybrook on the Marrowby road, so that the pack trains need not ford it.\n\nIt is a single steep arch, humped like a cat, and only five feet wide. The parapets are no higher than a man's knee. That was deliberate: a loaded pony is widest at the pack, and low walls let the bales swing clear on either side. Twenty ponies crossed nose to tail, with a bell on the leader.\n\nThe wool trade is gone, and carts take the newer road. But the packhorse bridge is still there under its ivy, and is still the quickest way to Marrowby on foot. Walkers should know that there is nothing whatever to hold on to."],["The Clapper in the Fells","The oldest bridge in the district is not at Emberwick at all. It lies a long day's walk north, where the drove road crosses the young river Ember in the Northern Fells: five great slabs of stone laid flat on four piles of boulders. Such a bridge is called a clapper.\n\nNobody knows who built it. The shepherds say a giant set it down so as not to wet her stockings, which is as good an account as any. It enters the written record only in the Year 133, when a spate tipped the middle slab into the river and Emberwick sent twelve men and two oxen to put it back.\n\nIt took them nine days. The slab is said to weigh four tons. They left their initials underneath, where only trout can read them, and went home with a new respect for the giant."],["The Marsh Causeway","West of Emberwick, downstream, the land sinks into marsh, and for most of the town's history the only way across was to go round. In the Year 155 the council resolved to build a bridge over ground too soft to hold one.\n\nThe answer was a causeway that floats. Bundles of hazel and alder, each as thick as a barrel, were laid crossways on the peat in a line a mile long, with oak planks pegged over all. It gives underfoot like a mattress. Herons use the handrail.\n\nIt also sinks, about an inch a year, and has done from the start. The remedy is to lay new bundles on the old, so that the causeway of today rests on seven or eight earlier ones, going down into the dark. It is, in its way, the deepest bridge in the district."],["The Bridge of Two Halves","When Emberwick and Marrowby made peace in the Year 171, after eight years of quarrelling over Gander Meadow, they agreed to build a footbridge across the Pennybrook beside the pear tree, as a sign of friendship. Each parish would build half. They would meet in the middle.\n\nEach parish also, it turned out, used its own carpenter, its own measuring rod and its own idea of level. On the day the halves were joined, the Marrowby side was found to be eleven inches higher than the Emberwick side.\n\nThere was a silence. Then old Captain Oddfellow observed that a step was not the same as a gap, and a single oak tread was fitted between. It is there now. Walkers cross from one parish to the other by going up or down one stair, and most do not know why."],["Ice Against the Arches","The hardest test of Emberwick's stone bridge came in the Year 190, at the end of the Long Frost, when it was a hundred and nineteen years old. The river Ember had been frozen for eleven weeks. Then, in one night of warm rain, the ice broke.\n\nIt came down in slabs the size of barn doors and piled against the piers as high as the parapet. The bridgewarden watched from the bank with a lantern and could do nothing. Near dawn there was a sound like a gate slamming, and a crack ran up through the middle arch.\n\nThe arch held. In summer it was stitched with nine iron cramps, set in lead, which can be counted from a boat. Hulda Garth, its mason, had built for the worst night she could imagine and a little more. This was the little more."],["The Dry Bridge","The strangest bridge near Emberwick crosses no water at all. It was put up in the Year 241, during the canal scheme, to carry the meadow footpath over a canal that was confidently expected within the year.\n\nIt is made of cast iron, in a single shallow arch of forty feet, with a railing of iron bulrushes and the company's initials at the centre. It arrived in pieces on six wagons and was bolted together in a week. It was, everyone agreed, the handsomest thing the canal produced.\n\nThe canal itself stopped a mile short of the town, when the cut would not hold water. So the Dry Bridge spans a grassy trench full of primroses, and has done for nearly sixty years. Children roll down the banks beneath it. It is painted green every seventh spring, and has never had the smallest trouble with damp."],["All the Bridges in a Day","To close this history, in the summer of the Year 300, I walked to every bridge in it that one day allows. I am Bridgewarden of Emberwick, as my mother was, and thought it time I inspected the whole inheritance.\n\nI started on the Dry Bridge at dawn, went springily over the marsh causeway, and looked in at Bridge Barn, where the floor is still Maud's deck. Then I turned east, crossed the packhorse bridge holding on to nothing, and took the one step up at Gander Meadow. The clapper in the Fells was too far, and must take my word.\n\nAt sunset I stood on the middle arch of the stone bridge. The ford showed pale below. I leaned over to see Hulda Garth's little bell on the keystone, and nearly lost my hat. Everything was sound. All of it is still carrying people over, dry."]],[["Setting Out from the Bridge","My name is Perpetua Dunn, land surveyor, and this atlas is the record of one season's work. In May the council of Emberwick engaged me to map the Lowlands, from the south end of the town bridge to the sea wall, in twelve sheets. No proper map of that country existed. There was only a sketch in the Guildhall on which the largest feature was a gravy stain.\n\nI set out on the twelfth with my apprentice, Tam, who is fourteen and can already draw a straighter line than I can; a pony named Bracket; a plane table; a brass chain; and forty red flags.\n\nThe first sheet shows very little: the bridge, the Lowland road, and four miles of pasture. But every map must start from one fixed point. Ours is a cross I cut in the last stone of the parapet."],["The Baseline at Larkacre","A map of the Lowlands is built from triangles, and the triangles are built from one line measured on the ground with all the care a surveyor has. I am Perpetua Dunn, and in the second week of my survey I measured that line across Larkacre pasture, five miles south of Emberwick.\n\nIt runs one mile exactly: eighty lengths of the brass chain, laid end to end, each checked twice. My apprentice Tam held the forward end. I held the rear and kept the book. We began at dawn, before the heat could stretch the brass, and finished at four.\n\nWe lost an hour to a brown cow, which ate the red flag marking the far end and had to be followed until we were sure where it had been. Tam has drawn her on Sheet Two, very small, in the corner. I have let her stay."],["The Village of Three Names","Sheet Three of this atlas covers the country around a village eight miles south of Emberwick, and I, Perpetua Dunn, surveyor, regret that I cannot tell you for certain what the village is called. I asked.\n\nThe miller said it was Nether Sedge and always had been. The woman at the inn said Sedgeby, and that the miller came from elsewhere. The children simply call it the Village, and looked at me kindly, as at someone who had not known the name of the sun.\n\nA map must say something. My apprentice Tam suggested we write both names, the larger for the one most people used. So we sat by the pump for a morning and counted. Sedgeby won by eleven voices, Nether Sedge is in smaller letters beneath, and the pump is marked, at the children's request, with a dot."],["The Windmill Triangle","In flat country a surveyor needs tall things to sight on, and the Lowlands south of Emberwick offer only windmills. On Sheet Four I, Perpetua Dunn, fixed the whole middle district from three of them: Sedge Mill, Cobb's Mill and the white mill at Farthing Green.\n\nThe method is simple. From each mill you measure the angles to the other two, and since you already know one distance, the rest follows by arithmetic. My apprentice Tam climbed to each cap with the instrument on his back. I stayed below and held the pony.\n\nThe angles of a triangle should add to a hundred and eighty degrees. Ours came to a hundred and eighty and a hair. I spent two days hunting the hair, and found it. Cobb's Mill is a post mill, and the miller had turned the whole building to face the wind between our visits."],["Drains and Dykes","The Lowlands would be a lake if left alone. What keeps them pasture is ditches, hundreds of miles of them, and on Sheet Five of this atlas I, Perpetua Dunn, had to draw every one within a ten-mile square south of Emberwick.\n\nI could not have done it without Corwen, the dyke-reeve, who is eighty and has cleaned those drains since boyhood. He walked ahead of us with a long hooked pole, naming them as he went: Long Lode, Crooked Lode, Widow's Drain, and the Sulk, which runs backwards in a west wind. My apprentice Tam wrote the names. I measured.\n\nCorwen had never seen a map. When I showed him the finished sheet, with its blue lines, he studied it a long while. Then he put one finger on a ditch I had drawn straight, and said it had a kink in it. He was right."],["The Field with No Owner","Every old sketch of the Lowlands leaves one place blank, about twelve miles south of Emberwick, and marks it only with the word \"doubtful\". I am Perpetua Dunn, surveyor, and in July I went with my apprentice Tam to remove the doubt. It is Sheet Six of this atlas.\n\nWe found a broad level field of forty acres, with good grass and no hedge, no gate and no cattle. The farms on every side stop short of it. Nobody claims it.\n\nThe reason came from a woman cutting rushes at its edge. In summer, she said, it is a field. From November to March it is a lake, two feet deep, with swans. No one wants to own a thing that is only there half the year. I have drawn it in both colours, green hatched with blue, and written in its local name: Sometimes."],["Sounding Hollow Mere","Hollow Mere is the one large water in the Lowlands, a mile and a quarter long, fifteen miles south of Emberwick. To put it on Sheet Seven I, Perpetua Dunn, needed its shape and its depth, and so for four days my survey went by boat.\n\nThe shape was easy: we walked the shore with the chain. The depth was patient work. A fisherman called Ham rowed us back and forth while my apprentice Tam let down a lead weight on a knotted cord and called out the knots. I wrote down two hundred and six soundings.\n\nThe mere is shallow, with one deep place near the middle, nineteen feet, where the water comes up cold. Ham says a great bell lies there. We sounded the spot four times and brought up only weed, but I have marked it with a small bell just the same."],["The Great Flat","Sheet Eight of this atlas is nearly empty, and I wish to say that this is accurate. I am Perpetua Dunn, surveyor. In August my apprentice Tam and I crossed the Great Flat, the middle of the Lowlands, twenty miles south of Emberwick, where for a whole day's walk there is no house, no mill and no rise of ground higher than a molehill.\n\nA surveyor needs things to measure between. Here there was grass, and one thorn tree, bent by the wind into the shape of a person running. We measured to the thorn from four directions simply for the comfort of it.\n\nTam was troubled by so much white paper. In the end he drew the thorn tree, and then, along the top margin, a small careful row of the clouds as they stood that afternoon. It is against all practice. I signed the sheet anyway."],["Tower in the Fog","In September the weather broke over the Lowlands, and Sheet Nine of this atlas was made mostly indoors. I am Perpetua Dunn, surveyor; my apprentice Tam and I were then twenty-five miles south of Emberwick, at the hamlet of Withy End.\n\nWe needed one last sight, to the old watch tower on Rook Knoll, the only landmark in the southern district. For six days it stood in fog. We lodged with a basket-maker, and while we waited I inked the summer's pencil work at her kitchen table and Tam lettered names, his tongue between his teeth.\n\nOn the seventh morning the fog lifted for perhaps ten minutes. Tam ran out with the instrument in his socks. He had the angle read and written down before the tower faded again, and came in soaked to the knee and extremely pleased. The basket-maker gave him the largest egg."],["The Sea Wall","The Lowlands end at a bank of clay twelve feet high, and beyond it is the sea. I am Perpetua Dunn, surveyor, and on the second of October, thirty-one miles and nearly five months from Emberwick bridge, my apprentice Tam and I led the pony up onto the sea wall and looked over.\n\nBehind us lay everything we had measured: green, ruled with ditches, dotted with mills. In front was grey water to the edge of the sky. Bracket, who had never seen the sea, stood with her ears forward and would not be led down for half an hour.\n\nWe chained along the top of the wall for two days, with the tide coming and going at our elbow. Past the last inked line, Sheet Ten is left bare, which is as near as I can come to drawing the sea."],["Closing the Traverse","A survey is proved by coming home. I am Perpetua Dunn, and through the summer my apprentice Tam and I had carried our measurements south across the Lowlands from a cross cut on Emberwick bridge. In October we carried them north again by a different road, mapping as we went. That is Sheet Eleven.\n\nThe test is this. If every angle and every chain length has been true, the figures will lead you back to your starting mark and say that you are standing on it. If not, they will say you are standing somewhere else, and by how much.\n\nWe reached the bridge on the twentieth, at dusk. I worked the sums twice by lantern on the parapet. After seventy miles of measuring, the figures placed the cross four feet from where it is. Tam asked if that was good. I told him it would do."],["The Atlas Bound","The Atlas of the Lowlands was finished at Emberwick in the last week of November. I, Perpetua Dunn, surveyor, inked the twelfth sheet myself. It is the index: the whole country from the bridge to the sea wall on one page, with the other eleven sheets shown as numbered squares.\n\nThe council had the sheets bound in green cloth and placed in the town library, where anyone may consult them. On the first day the dyke-reeve, Corwen, came in his best coat to see that the kink was still in his ditch. It was.\n\nTam's name is on the title page beside mine. He has earned it, and the brown cow and the clouds besides. The pony has gone to grass at Larkacre, in sight of our baseline. As for me, I notice that the council's sketch of the Northern Fells is mostly stain."]],[["The Mouth at Greywater","This is the survey of the river Ember from the sea to its source, made by me, Ottoline Pike, in one spring. I began where the river ends, on the Grey Coast, at the harbour of Greywater, on the first of April.\n\nA river's mouth is the hardest part to draw, because it will not stay still. At high water the Ember here is half a mile wide. Six hours later it is a brown thread between two mudbanks, and the fishing boats sit on their sides like tired dogs.\n\nI have hired a flat-bottomed boat, the Moorhen, and a boatman named Gil Tansy, who speaks about once an hour and is always right. We spent three days sounding the bar with a pole. I marked the channel at low water and at high, in two inks, and set my first peg on the harbour wall. Mile nought."],["Where the Salt Ends","Four miles above its mouth at Greywater, the river Ember stops being sea. I am Ottoline Pike, surveying the river from the coast to its source, and I wished to mark on my chart the exact place where the tide gives up.\n\nGil Tansy, my boatman, said the eel-catchers know it as the Heron Stakes, a row of old posts across a bend. To be sure, we moored the Moorhen there for a full day. Every hour I tasted the water, which is not in any manual, and tied a ribbon to a stake at the level it had reached.\n\nThe flood tide crept up to the stakes, lifted the boat eleven inches, paused, and went back. Below that bend the water was brackish at noon. Above it, sweet all day. I have drawn a line across the river there and written \"tide ends,\" with the date."],["The Marsh Reaches","Between the tide's end and the town of Emberwick the river Ember crosses the marsh, and takes nineteen miles to cover what a crow would do in eight. I am Ottoline Pike, river surveyor, and those nineteen miles cost me twelve days.\n\nThe river here is not one stream but several, plaited together among the reeds. Channels part, wander, and join again. Some end in a pool full of lilies. My boatman, Gil Tansy, poled the Moorhen up each in turn while I took bearings, and more than once we rounded a bend to find our own marker ribbon waiting for us.\n\nI have drawn the main channel in a firm line and the others in fine ones. A bittern boomed the whole time, somewhere to the left. We never saw it. It is on the chart all the same, as a small question mark with a beak."],["Under Emberwick Bridge","Twenty-three miles from the sea the river Ember passes under the three stone arches of Emberwick bridge. I am Ottoline Pike, surveying the river from mouth to source, and here I stopped a week to measure not where the water goes but how much of it there is.\n\nThe method needs a known width, a known depth and a known speed. Width and depth I took from the bridge with a weighted line, arch by arch. For speed, my boatman Gil Tansy dropped painted corks from the upstream parapet while I stood below with a watch and timed them through.\n\nA crowd gathered. Small boys began betting on the corks. By my reckoning the Ember carries past Emberwick, on an ordinary April day, enough water to fill the market square knee-deep every minute. The mayor asked me not to put it that way."],["The Pennybrook Joins","A mile above Emberwick bridge a small stream enters the river Ember from the east. This is the Pennybrook. I am Ottoline Pike, river surveyor, and since a river is the sum of what flows into it, I left the main stream for three days to follow this one.\n\nIt is only four miles long, and a child could jump it nearly anywhere. Yet the people of Emberwick and Marrowby speak of it with great respect, and showed me a meadow it runs through, with a pear tree, geese, and a footbridge that has a step in the middle. The brook, they say, has moved twice and may again.\n\nMy boatman, Gil Tansy, studied the banks and gave it as his opinion that it will. I have therefore drawn the Pennybrook in pencil, not ink. It is the only pencil line on the whole chart."],["The Weir at Tulley's Mill","Thirty miles from the sea the river Ember meets its first wall. I am Ottoline Pike, surveying the river upstream toward its source, and at Tulley's Mill a stone weir crosses the whole stream, four feet high, with the water sliding over it like bent glass.\n\nThe mill has ground Emberwick's flour for more than two hundred years. The miller let me measure the fall, the mill race and the great wheel, which is sixteen feet across and turns nine times a minute when the sluice is open. In return I was to tell him how deep his pond was. He had always wondered.\n\nA boat cannot climb a weir. My boatman, Gil Tansy, and the miller's two sons carried the Moorhen round on their shoulders, upside down, like a long green beetle. The pond is eleven feet deep by the sluice. The miller had guessed forty."],["The Long Pool","Above Tulley's Mill the river Ember slows and deepens into a reach the anglers call the Long Pool: three miles of dark, quiet water under alders, thirty-three miles from the sea. I am Ottoline Pike, river surveyor, and I took its soundings in the first week of May.\n\nMy boatman, Gil Tansy, let the Moorhen drift while I lowered the lead. The bottom is clean gravel, twelve to fifteen feet down, and so clear that I could see the weight land. Swallows drank beside us. It was the easiest work of the whole survey, and I did not hurry it.\n\nOn the second day a very large pike rose under the lead line, looked at it, and sank away again. Gil spoke unprompted for the first time in a week. He said, \"Relation of yours.\" I have marked the place on the chart with my initial."],["Into the Gorge","Forty miles from the sea the river Ember comes out of the Northern Fells through a gorge, and there a boat can go no farther. I am Ottoline Pike, surveying the river to its source. On the twelfth of May we drew the Moorhen up on the shingle and turned her over, and my boatman, Gil Tansy, became my chainman.\n\nThe gorge is a mile long and sixty feet deep, cut in grey rock, with ferns on every ledge. The river, so slow lower down, is here loud and white and in a hurry. I measured its fall: ninety feet in the mile.\n\nAt the top the valley opens, and the drove road crosses on an old bridge of flat stone slabs. We ate our bread sitting on it. From here the survey goes on foot, and the river, I suspect, will keep getting younger."],["Black Beck and White Beck","Four miles above the gorge, high in the Northern Fells, the river Ember divides in two, and a surveyor must decide which branch keeps the name. I am Ottoline Pike, and I had come forty-five miles from the sea to find a fork with no signpost.\n\nThe shepherds call the left stream Black Beck, for its peaty water, and the right one White Beck, for its stones. I asked them which was the Ember. They shrugged. It had never mattered to the sheep.\n\nThe rule is to follow the greater flow. My chainman, Gil Tansy, and I built a little dam of turf on each and measured how fast a bucket filled. Black Beck filled it in nine seconds. White Beck took fourteen. So the Ember is the dark one, and we turned left. White Beck is on the chart too, fairly drawn, as a tributary."],["Spindle Force","Forty-eight miles from the sea, the stream I have followed as the river Ember falls over a cliff. I am Ottoline Pike, river surveyor, and the waterfall is called Spindle Force: a single thin white thread dropping into a black pool, with a rowan tree leaning over the lip.\n\nA fall must be measured, and there is only one honest way. My chainman, Gil Tansy, held my ankles. I lay on the wet rock at the top and let down the sounding line, the same lead that found the bottom of the harbour at Greywater, until I felt it touch the pool. Seventy-one feet.\n\nWe climbed round by a sheep track. Above the fall the land is open moor, the stream is a stride wide, and the wind smells of nothing but grass. Gil remarked that there could not be much river left. He sounded sorry."],["Lantern Tarn","Fifty miles from the sea, the stream I have surveyed as the river Ember runs out of a small round lake. I am Ottoline Pike. The shepherds call the lake Lantern Tarn, because on still evenings it holds the last light after the hills have gone dark.\n\nI had half hoped it was the source. A tarn makes a tidy beginning, and my boots had worn through. We walked its shore with the chain, six hundred yards around, and my chainman, Gil Tansy, waded in with the pole to take its depth.\n\nBut at the far end, among rushes, a trickle no wider than my hand was running in. Water that runs in has come from somewhere. I closed the book and said we would find it in the morning. We slept in a shepherd's hut, and the tarn kept its light until nearly ten."],["The Spring","The river Ember begins under a grey rock the size of a kitchen table, on a hill called High Wick, fifty-one miles from the sea. I am Ottoline Pike, river surveyor, and I reached it on the twenty-sixth of May with my chainman, Gil Tansy, after eight weeks of following the water uphill.\n\nThe spring comes out of a crack in the rock into a basin of moss no larger than a hat. It is very cold. It fills, spills over one edge, and sets off downhill towards the tarn, the fall, the gorge, the mill, the bridge, the marsh and the sea, as if it knew the way.\n\nI drove in my last peg and wrote the mileage on it. Then we each drank a cupful. Gil said it tasted the same as at Greywater, only without the salt, and that was the survey finished."]],[["Where the Salt Way Began","I am Wilmot Cray, and for forty years I mended the roads of this district with a shovel and a barrow. Now that I am retired I mean to walk a road that needs no mending, because it is not there.\n\nThe Salt Way once ran from the salt pans at Brinepan Cove on the Grey Coast to the market at Emberwick, forty miles inland. My grandmother, Nell Cray, drove a string of nine ponies along it as a girl. I have her waybook: twelve stages, each with a note in pencil, such as \"mind the ford\" and \"good pie\".\n\nI started this morning from the cove. The pans are only square hollows in the turf now, with sea lavender growing in them. But a stone slipway still runs up from the beach, grooved by sledges, and points inland like a finger. I followed it."],["The Hollow Way","The second stage of the old Salt Way, from the Grey Coast towards Emberwick, climbs out of Brinepan Cove through gorse, and I, Wilmot Cray, retired road-mender, found it by falling into it.\n\nA road that carries loaded ponies for two hundred years wears down. Hooves loosen the soil, rain carries it off, and little by little the track sinks until it is a trench with banks above a walker's head. This one is nine feet deep and roofed over with hazel. From the field beside it you would see only a line of bushes.\n\nInside, it is green and still, like a room. The floor is worn rock with a groove down the middle. My grandmother's waybook says of this stage, \"ponies know it.\" I walked a mile and a half without seeing the sky, and came out on the hilltop among larks."],["A Milestone in a Garden","Seven miles in from the Grey Coast, the old Salt Way to Emberwick disappears into the village of Furzeham. I am Wilmot Cray, retired road-mender, walking the lost road by my grandmother's waybook, and for the third stage her note says only, \"count the stones.\"\n\nThere were once milestones. I found one at last in a cottage garden, standing among the beans with a pan of water on top for the birds. The lettering was worn but could be read with a thumb: Emberwick, thirty-three miles.\n\nThe owner, Mrs Applin, said her grandfather had rolled it home in a barrow when the road was given up, thinking it too good to waste. She had not known what the numbers meant. I told her. She said she would look at the sparrows' bath with more respect, and gave me a bag of plums for the journey."],["The Causeway under the Reeds","The fourth stage of the old Salt Way crosses a corner of the marsh, eleven miles from the Grey Coast, and my grandmother's waybook says of it, \"keep to the stakes.\" I am Wilmot Cray, retired road-mender. There are no stakes. There are reeds, taller than my hat.\n\nBut a road-mender knows that stone does not go away. I cut a long ash pole and went forward a step at a time, probing. To either side the pole sank in black mud to my hand. In front, a foot under the water, it struck something flat and hard.\n\nThat was the causeway: slabs laid by the salt carriers, drowned now, but as firm as the day they were set. I walked two miles on a road I could not see, with wet boots and great satisfaction. A heron watched the whole performance and plainly thought me unreliable."],["The Packsaddle Inn","Sixteen miles from the Grey Coast, on the fifth stage of the old Salt Way, my grandmother's waybook has its longest entry: \"The Packsaddle. Good pie. Stable dry. Landlady sharp.\" I am Wilmot Cray, retired road-mender, walking the lost road to Emberwick, and I had looked forward to this one.\n\nThere is no inn. There is a farmhouse, long and low, standing end-on to a field as if expecting somebody. But the farmer's wife, Mrs Dace, showed me the kitchen beams, which have nine iron hooks in a row for nine sets of harness, and a stable with stalls too small for any horse.\n\nShe had always wondered about the hooks. I read her my grandmother's note. She laughed at \"sharp,\" said the pie at least could be managed, and it was: mutton and onion, eaten at the same hearth, some ninety years late."],["The Ford Nobody Uses","Twenty miles from the Grey Coast, halfway along the old Salt Way to Emberwick, a stream called the Withy Water crosses the line of the road. I am Wilmot Cray, retired road-mender, and my grandmother's waybook says of this sixth stage, \"mind the ford. Left of the alder.\"\n\nA ford is the one part of a road that cannot be ploughed up. The alder was gone, but its stump was there, as broad as a cartwheel. I took off my boots and waded in to the left of it. Elsewhere the bed was soft silt. On the line it was cobbles, set tight, each the size of a loaf.\n\nMinnows were holding station above the stones, exactly as if waiting to cross. I stood in the middle a while. Nine ponies had gone through here twice a week, and the stream had kept the place for them."],["The Line of Old Thorns","On its seventh stage the old Salt Way enters the Lowlands, twenty-four miles from the Grey Coast, and there the plough has had it. I am Wilmot Cray, retired road-mender, walking the lost road to Emberwick, and I stood at a gate looking at eighty acres of barley and no track.\n\nMy grandmother's waybook says, \"straight on by the thorns.\" It took me some time to see them: seven old hawthorns in a line across the field, each standing alone in the crop. They were once a roadside hedge. The farmer ploughs round them, without knowing why his grandfather did.\n\nThen the wind moved the barley, and I saw the rest. Between the thorns ran a long stripe where the corn stood shorter and paler, on the stony ground of the old roadbed. I kept to the headland and walked beside my road to the far gate."],["The Toll House in the Field","Twenty-eight miles from the Grey Coast, in the middle of a Lowland pasture, stands an eight-sided house with a window on every side. I am Wilmot Cray, retired road-mender, walking the vanished Salt Way to Emberwick, and I knew what it was at once. No one builds a house that shape unless they need to watch a road both ways.\n\nThe road is gone from its door and sheep graze to the step, but the old man who lives there, Mr Garrity, keeps the toll board on his kitchen wall: a pony laden, one farthing; a pony unladen, nothing; a pig, by agreement.\n\nMy grandmother's waybook says of this stage, \"pay the man, he counts.\" I offered Mr Garrity a farthing for the look of the thing. He took it very gravely, wrote me a ticket, and opened his garden gate as wide as it would go."],["The Crossroads with One Arm","At thirty-one miles from the Grey Coast, deep in the Lowlands, the old Salt Way met three other roads, and my grandmother's waybook marks the spot with a cross and the words, \"do not take the wrong one.\" I am Wilmot Cray, retired road-mender, on the ninth stage of my walk to Emberwick.\n\nThe finger-post is still there, oak, silver with age. It has sockets for four arms and only one remaining, which points down a farm track and says \"Sedgeby.\" The other three ways are ditch, hedge and turnip field.\n\nI sat under it and worked the matter out as a road-mender would. The salt went north-east; and in that direction, across the turnips, I could make out a gap in the far hedge just the width of a laden pony. I thanked my grandmother, and took the right one."],["The Green Lane","For three miles of its tenth stage the old Salt Way is not lost at all. I am Wilmot Cray, retired road-mender, thirty-four miles from the Grey Coast on my walk to Emberwick, and after days of reeds, barley and turnips I stepped through a hedge onto a broad lane of grass, with oaks along it.\n\nNobody has stoned or scraped it in ninety years, and it is in better order than some roads I was paid to keep. The turf is close and springy. The ruts are still there, softened, a pony's width apart.\n\nMy grandmother's waybook says of this stretch, \"sing here, it's easy.\" I met a girl bringing five goats home, who told me it was just called the Lane and went nowhere. I said it went to the sea. She thought that over, and said she would try it on Saturday."],["Why the Road Died","Thirty-seven miles from the Grey Coast, the old Salt Way climbs a low rise, and from the top I, Wilmot Cray, retired road-mender, could see the reason it was abandoned. The reason is another road.\n\nIt lies a mile to the east: the Lowland high road, raised and stoned, with a carrier's cart crawling along it. That road was made in my grandmother's girlhood. A pony carries two hundredweight on a track a yard wide. A cart carries a ton, but needs a real road under it. Once the carts could reach the coast, the pony strings were finished within ten years.\n\nMy grandmother's waybook says of this eleventh stage, \"nearly there.\" Ahead, where the two roads draw together, I could see a clock tower and a line of lime trees. I mended that road for forty years, and found I could not be angry with either road."],["The Last Stage","The old Salt Way ends where Emberwick began, at the ford. I am Wilmot Cray, retired road-mender, and on the ninth day of my walk I came down a grassy bank to the river Ember, forty miles from the salt pans, a little below the stone bridge.\n\nMy grandmother's waybook says of the twelfth stage, \"ford, bell, market, home.\" The gravel showed pale under the water. I could have used the bridge, a hundred yards off. I took my boots off instead, waded over to the knee, and touched the old ferry bell on the far bank, as she must have done.\n\nThe market clock said I was seven minutes early. I have given the waybook to the town library, with my own notes pencilled beside hers. The road is still not there. But it can be followed now, by anyone who cares to borrow the book."]],[["Leaving Greywater","I am Jonas Merrow, chart-maker, and this book is my account of charting the Cinder Isles, which lie off the Grey Coast between ten and thirty miles out. The Harbour Board at Greywater engaged me in June, after a coal brig spent a night aground on an island that her chart said was a mile away.\n\nThe only chart in existence is ninety years old and drawn by nobody knows whom. It shows twelve islands. The fishermen of Greywater say there are eleven, and have said so, with some heat, for as long as anyone can remember.\n\nI am to settle it. I have the cutter Sandpiper, a sextant, a lead line marked in fathoms, and Bess Tarrant, who has skippered these waters for thirty years and regards charts as a thing for people who do not know where they are. We sailed on the morning ebb."],["Hearth Island","The first and largest of the Cinder Isles is Hearth, ten miles off the Grey Coast, and I, Jonas Merrow, chart-maker, began my survey there because it has a harbour, a well and a bakery. It is two miles long and shaped something like a boot.\n\nCharting an island means going round it slowly. Bess Tarrant, my skipper, kept the cutter Sandpiper close offshore while I took angles to the headlands and the ship's boy, Dab, swung the lead and sang out the depths.\n\nThe old chart had Hearth half a mile too far north and the harbour on the wrong side. The islanders, forty households, had never noticed, having no need of a chart to find their own front doors. The baker asked to see his shop on the new one. I drew it in, a very small square, and he gave me a loaf."],["The Two Bellows","North of Hearth in the Cinder Isles lie Great Bellows and Little Bellows, and the old chart draws them as one island. I am Jonas Merrow, chart-maker, sent from Greywater to put these waters right, and I can report that the old chart was half correct.\n\nAt high water they are two: grassy humps a quarter of a mile apart. At low water a bar of shingle rises and joins them, and sheep walk across as if on business. For six hours they are one island, and then for six they are not.\n\nMy skipper, Bess Tarrant, asked how I meant to draw that. I timed the bar through four tides, sitting on Little Bellows with a watch and a flask of tea. It is on the chart as a dotted neck, with the words \"dries three hours either side of low water.\" The sheep already knew."],["Cinder Rock","The fourth of the Cinder Isles gives the group its name and is the least welcoming. Cinder Rock is a black stack sixty feet high, with no beach, no grass and no place to put a foot. I am Jonas Merrow, chart-maker, and I charted it without touching it.\n\nIt belongs to the seabirds. Every ledge was packed with guillemots standing shoulder to shoulder like a crowd at a fair, and the noise reached us half a mile off. So did the smell.\n\nMy skipper, Bess Tarrant, sailed the Sandpiper round it twice at a respectful distance while I took its height by sextant and its position by angles to Hearth and the Bellows. The lead found forty fathoms close in: the rock goes down as steeply as it goes up. I have marked it \"steep-to, birds,\" and that is all a sailor needs to know."],["Kettle Island","Kettle, the fifth of the Cinder Isles, is a ring of low rock around a lagoon, with one gap on the eastern side like a spout. I am Jonas Merrow, chart-maker, and after the bare black stack of Cinder Rock it was a pleasure to anchor in still water.\n\nThe gap is forty yards wide and two fathoms deep at half tide. My skipper, Bess Tarrant, took the Sandpiper through under jib alone, with the boy Dab calling depths from the bow. Inside, the lagoon is as round as a plate and so clear that I charted much of the bottom by looking over the side.\n\nOne person lives on Kettle: a retired sailmaker named Orrin, who keeps bees in nine straw hives. He told me heather honey is the only cargo the island ships. We left with a jar, and his hives are on the chart."],["The Tongs","North Tong and South Tong, the sixth and seventh of the Cinder Isles, lie side by side eighteen miles off the Grey Coast: two long thin ridges with a strait between them no wider than a village street. I am Jonas Merrow, chart-maker, and that strait needed sounding.\n\nThe trouble is the tide. It pours through the gap at six knots, first one way and then the other. My skipper, Bess Tarrant, would not take the cutter Sandpiper near it except at slack water, which lasts about twenty minutes.\n\nSo we waited at anchor, went through with the lead going as fast as the boy Dab could swing it, and came out the far end as the stream turned. We did that six times in three days. I have nine soundings from the strait, and I am prouder of them than of any hundred taken elsewhere."],["Fog on Poker Reef","Not everything in the Cinder Isles is an island. Between the Tongs and the outer group lies Poker Reef, a mile of rock that shows only as a line of broken water, and I, Jonas Merrow, chart-maker, was taking its bearings when the fog came down.\n\nIt arrived in ten minutes and took away the Tongs, the horizon and our own bowsprit. My skipper, Bess Tarrant, anchored at once in eight fathoms and set the boy Dab to ring the bell every minute. There was nothing else to be done. A chart-maker in fog is merely a passenger.\n\nWe lay there nineteen hours. I passed them inking soundings below, by lamp. Bess sat on deck, listening to the reef, and said afterwards she could tell where it was by the sound. When it cleared, the surf was breaking exactly where she had pointed. I marked it there."],["The Keeper of Scuttle","Scuttle, the eighth of the Cinder Isles, is a round green island with a lighthouse on it, twenty-two miles off the Grey Coast. I am Jonas Merrow, chart-maker, and I landed there for fresh water and came away with the most useful thing in my survey.\n\nThe keeper, Mrs Agnes Flint, has tended the light for twenty-six years. Every day she has written down the time and height of high water: nineteen ledgers, in a hand like copperplate. Nobody had asked her to, and nobody had ever come to read them.\n\nA chart without tides is half a chart. With her figures I can say when the Bellows bar dries, when the Tongs run slack, and how deep the Kettle spout is at any hour. I copied for two days. She fed us on scones, and would accept in return only a promise of the finished chart."],["The Grate","The ninth of the Cinder Isles is hardly higher than the sea. The Grate is a low bank of sand and marram grass, twenty-five miles off the Grey Coast, with parallel sandbars lying off its southern shore like the bars of a fireplace. I am Jonas Merrow, chart-maker, and it gave me more trouble than any rock.\n\nRock stays where it is put. Sand does not. Mrs Flint of Scuttle light had warned me that the bars shift with every winter gale, and when I compared my soundings with the old chart, nothing agreed.\n\nTwo hundred seals were hauled out on the outermost bar. They watched the cutter Sandpiper go up and down with the lead. My skipper, Bess Tarrant, said they were laughing. I have drawn the bars as I found them and written across them, \"these move,\" which is the truest thing on the sheet."],["Ash Holm and Spark","The two outermost of the Cinder Isles lie thirty miles off the Grey Coast, at the edge of the old chart. I am Jonas Merrow, chart-maker, and I reached them in the last week of August, the tenth and eleventh islands of my survey.\n\nAsh Holm is a whaleback of rock and turf, where the fishermen of Hearth leave sheep for the summer. Spark, a mile beyond, is the smallest island I have ever charted: forty paces by twenty-five, with a spring, one stunted rowan and a cairn. I walked round it in two minutes and measured it with the boy Dab's fishing line.\n\nThat made eleven. I said so to my skipper, Bess Tarrant, who had told me as much in June. But the old chart shows a twelfth, three miles north-east of Spark, labelled, in faded ink, \"the Ember.\" We set a course for it."],["The Twelfth Island","Three miles north-east of Spark, where a ninety-year-old chart of the Cinder Isles shows a twelfth island called the Ember, there is open sea. I am Jonas Merrow, chart-maker, and I looked at it from the deck of the cutter Sandpiper. My skipper, Bess Tarrant, was kind enough to say nothing.\n\nBut a chart-maker sounds before he believes. The lead had been finding twenty fathoms. It came up at four, then two, then one. There was a shoal under us, a hill of sand almost at the surface. Mrs Flint's ledgers at Scuttle light gave the lowest tide of the year for the next dawn, and we anchored.\n\nAt ten past five the sea drew back and a bank of wet sand rose out of it, pink in the early light. I rowed over and stood on it. It stayed for fifty minutes, and then it went out."],["The Fair Chart","I, Jonas Merrow, chart-maker, drew the fair copy of my chart of the Cinder Isles at Greywater in October, on one sheet of linen-backed paper. It shows eleven islands in firm black line, from Hearth to Spark.\n\nIt also shows a twelfth. The Ember is drawn in dots, with the note, \"sand, dries for one hour at the lowest tides of the year.\" The old chart-maker was right, and so were the fishermen; it depends upon the day. Bess Tarrant, my skipper, looked at it a long time and allowed that a chart might have its uses.\n\nOne copy hangs in the harbour office. One went to Mrs Flint at Scuttle light, as promised, and one to the library at Emberwick. The baker's shop on Hearth is there, a very small square, and so are nine beehives on Kettle. A chart should show what matters."]],[["Counting the Rolls","Tamsin Bunce, the baker's daughter, came to the Emberwick schoolroom on a wet Monday and announced that she was no good at numbers and never would be. Mr. Quillfeather said only, \"Then we shall begin where numbers began,\" and set a basket of bread rolls upon his desk.\n\nHe asked whether there were enough rolls for the class. Tamsin did not count. She walked the room and put one roll before each pupil, and when she had finished, two rolls remained in the basket. \"More rolls than pupils, then,\" said Mr. Quillfeather, \"and you found it out without a single number. That matching, one to one, is the root of all counting. A number is only a name for how far the matching went.\"\n\nThen Tamsin counted the pupils anyway, got eleven, and added the two spare rolls to make thirteen. She was allowed to eat one."],["Odd and Even","In the schoolroom at Emberwick, Mr. Quillfeather had the baker's daughter, Tamsin Bunce, sort buttons into pairs. Some heaps paired off neatly, and those numbers he called even. Others left one lonely button over, and those were odd. Seven was odd, and its spare button rolled under the stove.\n\n\"Now add two odd heaps,\" he said. Tamsin pushed a heap of five against a heap of seven and frowned at the two leftover buttons. Then she slid them together. They made a pair, and twelve was even.\n\n\"Will it always happen?\" she asked. Mr. Quillfeather told her to think rather than try. She thought. Every odd heap is pairs with one left over. Two odd heaps bring two leftovers, and two leftovers are a pair. So odd and odd must make even, whatever the heaps, including heaps too large for all the buttons in town. It was her first proof."],["Squares on the Tray","Tamsin Bunce helped in her father's bakery before school, and one morning she noticed something on the bun tray. One bun made a square, of a sort. Three more buns tucked round its corner made a square of four. Five more round that made nine, and seven more made sixteen.\n\nShe carried the news to Mr. Quillfeather at the Emberwick schoolroom, still slightly floury. He drew it on the board. Each new square is the old one wearing a border shaped like a letter L, and each border needs two more buns than the last. So the odd numbers, added in order, always build squares. One and three are four. Add five for nine, seven for sixteen, nine for twenty-five.\n\n\"Add the first ten odd numbers,\" he said, \"without adding.\" Tamsin shut her eyes. Ten borders make a square ten buns each way. \"One hundred,\" she said, and it was."],["The Staircase Sum","Mr. Quillfeather, schoolmaster of Emberwick, wished for a quiet half hour, so he told his pupil Tamsin Bunce to add every number from one to ten. She was back in a minute with fifty-five, and he suspected guessing.\n\nTamsin showed him. She had built a staircase of sugar lumps: one, then two, then three, up to a column of ten. She built another the same, turned it upside down, and fitted it against the first. The two staircases made a block ten columns wide, each column eleven lumps tall. That is a hundred and ten lumps, and one staircase is half of it.\n\n\"And one to a hundred?\" he asked. A hundred columns, each a hundred and one tall, made ten thousand one hundred. Half was five thousand and fifty. He told her such staircase sums are called triangular numbers, and let her keep the sugar."],["Numbers That Stand Alone","The baker of Emberwick liked his buns set out in tidy rectangles, and his daughter Tamsin had learned which numbers obliged him. Twelve buns would go as two rows of six or three rows of four. But thirteen would do nothing except stand in one long line, and neither would seven or eleven.\n\nAt school she asked Mr. Quillfeather what was wrong with thirteen. \"Nothing is wrong with it,\" he said. \"It is a prime: a number that no smaller number divides, save one. Primes cannot be broken into equal rows.\"\n\nTogether they hunted primes below thirty and found ten: two, three, five, seven, eleven, thirteen, seventeen, nineteen, twenty-three and twenty-nine. Two was the only even one, since every other even number splits into two rows. Tamsin's father took the news badly. A baker's dozen, he said, had always been awkward on the tray."],["The Flour Sieve","To find every prime number up to fifty, Mr. Quillfeather chalked all fifty numbers on the schoolroom floor in Emberwick and handed Tamsin Bunce a damp rag. Primes are numbers that nothing divides except one and themselves, and he said they could be sieved out like lumps from flour.\n\nOne was set aside. Tamsin left two standing and wiped out every second number after it. She left three and wiped every third number, then did the same with five and with seven.\n\n\"Now eleven,\" she said. Mr. Quillfeather shook his head. Apart from eleven itself, any number below fifty that eleven divides has a smaller divider too, and was wiped long ago. The first number needing eleven alone is eleven elevens, a hundred and twenty-one, far off the floor. Fifteen primes stood unwiped, from two up to forty-seven. Tamsin walked along them like stepping stones."],["Breaking Sixty","\"Every number is either a prime or built from primes,\" said Mr. Quillfeather, \"as every loaf is built from flour.\" His pupil Tamsin Bunce, the baker's daughter, was asked to break sixty into pieces that would break no further.\n\nShe began with six times ten. Six broke into two and three; ten broke into two and five. That left two, two, three and five, all primes. Across the Emberwick schoolroom her friend Wilf began differently, with four times fifteen. Four broke into two and two, fifteen into three and five. The very same four primes.\n\nThey tried twelve times five, and three times twenty. Always two, two, three, five. Mr. Quillfeather said it is so for every number there is: break it however you like, and the same primes fall out at the bottom. Tamsin thought it the most reassuring thing she had heard all week."],["The Trick of Nines","Tamsin Bunce kept the tally at her father's bakery in Emberwick, and Mr. Quillfeather taught her a trick for it. To learn whether nine divides a number, add up its digits. If nine divides that small sum, it divides the whole number. For seven hundred and thirty-eight, seven and three and eight make eighteen. Nine goes into eighteen, so it goes into the number, and indeed nine times eighty-two is seven hundred and thirty-eight.\n\nTamsin wanted the reason. He set out coins. Ten is nine with one over. A hundred is ninety-nine with one over. So each ten and each hundred hands nine its share and leaves a single coin behind. Seven hundreds leave seven coins, three tens leave three, and the eight units make eighteen.\n\nThe same trick works for three. That evening she caught an error in the flour bill, and was unbearable until bedtime."],["The Largest Square","A slab of gingerbread came out of the Emberwick bakery oven eighty-four fingers long and thirty-six wide. Tamsin Bunce, the baker's daughter, was told to cut it into equal squares, as large as could be, with no crumb wasted.\n\n\"Let the smaller measure the larger,\" said Mr. Quillfeather at school. Thirty-six goes into eighty-four twice, with twelve left over. Then let the leftover measure the thirty-six: twelve goes three times exactly, and nothing is left. When nothing is left, the last measure is the answer. Twelve divides both numbers, and no larger number does.\n\nSo the squares were twelve fingers each way, seven along the slab and three across, twenty-one in all. Mr. Quillfeather said the method was very old, older than the school and perhaps older than gingerbread. Tamsin tried it on forty and twenty-eight for practice, got four, and was rewarded with a corner piece."],["Halving the Pie","On the last day of term Tamsin Bunce brought an apple pie to the Emberwick schoolroom, and Mr. Quillfeather made a lesson of it before anyone could eat. He cut it in half and gave one half away. He halved what remained and gave away a quarter. Then an eighth, then a sixteenth.\n\n\"How much has gone?\" he asked. Tamsin added: a half, a quarter, an eighth and a sixteenth came to fifteen sixteenths. What remained on the plate was one sixteenth, exactly the size of the last slice given. It had been so at every step.\n\nAnd if he cut for ever? The slices given away would creep toward the whole pie, she said, closer than any crumb you could name, but never past it. Mr. Quillfeather was so pleased that he ate the last sixteenth himself, which the class held against him for some time."],["A Perfect Number","Mr. Quillfeather, the old schoolmaster of Emberwick, kept one piece of arithmetic for pupils he thought ready, and in her last winter at school he gave it to Tamsin Bunce. \"List the numbers that divide six,\" he said, \"leaving out six itself.\" One, two and three. \"Add them.\" Six again.\n\nSuch numbers are called perfect: they equal the sum of their own parts. Tamsin went hunting for the next with a slate and a plate of her father's shortbread. Eight failed, since one, two and four make only seven. Twelve overshot, its parts making sixteen.\n\nAt twenty-eight she stopped. One, two, four, seven and fourteen: twenty-eight exactly. Mr. Quillfeather told her the third is four hundred and ninety-six, and that nobody alive knows whether the list of perfect numbers ever ends. Tamsin liked that best of all. Even the books did not know everything."],["Numbers Without End","On Tamsin Bunce's last afternoon in the Emberwick schoolroom, Mr. Quillfeather asked a last question. \"Do the primes ever run out?\"\n\nShe tried pretending there were only four: two, three, five and seven. Multiply them together and you get two hundred and ten. Add one. Two hundred and eleven leaves one over when divided by two, by three, by five or by seven, so none of them divides it. Either it is a new prime or some new prime divides it. Whatever list you begin with, however long, the same trick finds a prime the list has missed. So no list is ever complete, and the primes go on for ever.\n\nMr. Quillfeather gave her his own slate. Tamsin kept the bakery's accounts and never again said she was no good at numbers. On quiet mornings she still set out the buns in squares."]],[["The Knotted Rope","First entry. I am Wren Ashdown, fourteen, newly apprenticed to Mistress Corrigan the geometer, and this notebook is to hold whatever she teaches me. We have been engaged to lay out the bare courtyard behind Emberwick Library with paving, a round bed and a sundial, all to be ready by midsummer.\n\nToday we set the first corner. She took out a loop of rope with twelve knots at equal spaces. I held one knot, she pegged another three spaces along, and we pulled the loop taut into a triangle with sides of three, four and five spaces.\n\nThe corner between the three and the four is square. It must be, she says, whenever the two short sides, each multiplied by itself, add up to the long side multiplied by itself. Nine and sixteen make twenty-five. The masons checked it with their iron square and looked rather put out."],["Finding the Middle","Notebook of Wren Ashdown, at work on the courtyard behind Emberwick Library. Today Mistress Corrigan wanted the exact middle of the line between our two corner pegs, and she forbade me to measure it. Measuring, she says, is how mistakes get in.\n\nShe gave me a cord and a lump of chalk. I looped the cord on the first peg and swung an arc, keeping the cord a good deal longer than half the distance. Then, with the cord unchanged, I swung an arc from the second peg. The arcs crossed twice, once on each side of our line.\n\nA chalk line through the two crossings cut our line exactly in half, and at a square corner too. Each crossing lies equally far from both pegs, so the line through them can favour neither. I measured afterwards, in secret. She was right to the width of the chalk."],["Three Corners, One Line","Rain today, so no work in the library courtyard. Mistress Corrigan sat me, her apprentice Wren, at a reading-room table in Emberwick Library with scissors and scrap paper, and had me cut triangles: fat ones, thin ones, one like a church spire.\n\nThen she told me to tear the three corners off each and lay them point to point. Every time, the three corners fitted together into a perfectly straight edge. That is two square corners' worth, never more or less.\n\nHer reason is tidy. Rule a line through the top corner, level with the base. The two bottom corners reappear up there, one on either side, and the three sit in a row along the straight line. So if one corner of a triangle is square, the other two must share a single square corner between them. The librarian asked us, kindly, to take our scraps home."],["The Long Diagonal","Wren Ashdown's notebook, kept for Mistress Corrigan the geometer. The library courtyard at Emberwick is pegged out at last: eight paces by fifteen. But four pegs do not prove four square corners.\n\nThe test is the diagonal. In any triangle with a square corner, multiply each short side by itself and add the answers; the total is the long side multiplied by itself. Eight eights are sixty-four. Fifteen fifteens are two hundred and twenty-five. Together, two hundred and eighty-nine, which is seventeen seventeens.\n\nSo each diagonal should measure seventeen paces. We stretched the cord corner to corner both ways. One came out a little short and the other a little long, the sign of a leaning courtyard, so we nudged two pegs until both read seventeen. Our knotted rope of three, four and five obeys the same rule, she says, and so do five, twelve and thirteen."],["Round the Rain Barrel","There is to be a round bed in the middle of the library courtyard, seven paces across, and Mistress Corrigan asked me how much edging stone to order. I am Wren, her apprentice, and I did not know.\n\nShe had me wrap a string once round the rain barrel by the library door, then lay that string across the barrel's top. It went across three times, with a short piece over. We tried a bucket, a cartwheel and the lid of the inkpot. Always three times and a bit, and the bit is close to one seventh of the width.\n\nSo a circle seven paces across is about twenty-two paces round. The true figure is a whisker under, she says, and can never be written out exactly, however many figures one spends on it. We ordered twenty-two paces of edging. The spare whisker can go under the bench."],["Six Steps Round","Wren Ashdown, apprentice geometer, writing by the library lamp in Emberwick. Today I learned why Mistress Corrigan wants six-sided tiles for the courtyard.\n\nDraw a circle with a cord. Then, keeping the cord at the same length, step it round the rim. It lands back on its starting mark after exactly six steps. I did it four times, hoping to catch it out.\n\nJoin the six marks and you have a hexagon. Join each mark to the centre and you have six triangles, and every side of every one is the same cord's length: two spokes of the circle and one step. Equal sides make equal corners, so each corner is a third of a straight line, and six of them meet at the centre and fill the whole turn. Bees, she says, have known this longer than geometers. The potter starts on our tiles tomorrow."],["Which Tiles Lie Flat","I, Wren, had hoped for five-sided tiles in the library courtyard. Mistress Corrigan did not argue. She handed me a dozen clay pentagons and told me to pave the workshop table.\n\nIt cannot be done. Where three pentagons meet, a thin wedge of table shows between them, and a fourth will not squeeze in. The corners explain it. A full turn is three hundred and sixty degrees, and tiles lie flat only if the corners meeting at a point use it up exactly. A pentagon's corner is one hundred and eight. Three make three hundred and twenty-four, leaving thirty-six to spare.\n\nSquares manage, four nineties to a point. Triangles manage with six sixties, and hexagons with three corners of one hundred and twenty. Among shapes with equal sides and equal corners, only those three pave a floor alone. I kept one pentagon as a paperweight."],["The Tower's Shadow","Notebook of Wren Ashdown, apprentice to Mistress Corrigan. Our sundial must stand clear of the shadow of the library clock tower, so we needed the tower's height, and nobody in Emberwick would lend us a ladder that long.\n\nWe used the sun instead. I stood a three-foot stick upright in the courtyard, and its shadow measured two feet. At the same moment Mistress Corrigan paced out the tower's shadow, which ran forty feet along the ground.\n\nSunlight strikes stick and tower at the same slant, so the two make triangles of the same shape, one small and one large. The stick is one and a half times its shadow, so the tower must be one and a half times its own. Sixty feet. The librarian later found the builder's account in a drawer, which says sixty feet and four inches. Mistress Corrigan blames the four inches on the weathervane."],["The Pie Cut Thin","The gardener asked how much ground the round bed in the library courtyard will cover, to order his lavender. Mistress Corrigan passed the question to me, her apprentice Wren, with a paper circle and a knife.\n\nI cut the circle into sixteen thin wedges, like a pie, and laid them side by side, one point up, the next point down. They made something very like a rectangle. Its height was the circle's spoke, from centre to rim. Its length was half the rim, since half the crusts lay along the top and half along the bottom.\n\nOur bed is seven paces across, so the spoke is three and a half, and half the rim is eleven. Three and a half elevens are thirty-eight and a half square paces. The gardener ordered lavender for thirty-eight. The odd half, he says, is where he will stand to admire it."],["A Square Corner Inside","Wren Ashdown's notebook. The round bed in the library courtyard is edged, and today we tested whether it is truly round. Mistress Corrigan drove two pegs at opposite ends of a line through the centre and gave me the mason's iron square.\n\nThe rule: hold the square so its two arms touch the two pegs, and its corner will rest on the circle, anywhere round the rim. One stone sat a thumb's width proud, and the square caught it.\n\nJoin the corner to the centre. That spoke equals the spokes running to the pegs, so it makes two triangles, each with two equal sides and therefore two equal corners. The angle at my iron square is one of each kind added together. So are the two peg corners, between them. Since the big triangle's three corners fill a straight line, the square's corner is half of it."],["North by Shadow","A sundial is useless unless it points true north, and Mistress Corrigan does not trust compass needles. So today, in the courtyard of Emberwick Library, she and I, her apprentice Wren, asked the sun.\n\nWe set a rod upright on a levelled board and drew a circle round its foot. In the morning the rod's shadow was long and reached past the circle. As the sun climbed the shadow shrank, and I marked the spot where its tip crossed the circle. After noon it lengthened again, and I marked where the tip crossed on its way out.\n\nThe sun rises and sinks evenly either side of noon, so those two marks are a matched pair. A line from the rod's foot to the point midway between them runs due north. The compass disagreed by a few degrees. The needle, said Mistress Corrigan, is entitled to its opinion."],["Midsummer Noon","Last entry. I am Wren Ashdown, and since the thaw I have been apprentice to Mistress Corrigan the geometer. Today, midsummer, the courtyard behind Emberwick Library was opened, and half the town came to walk on it.\n\nThey walked on corners squared with a knotted rope, and on six-sided tiles that leave no gaps. In the middle pattern one tile is ringed by six, then twelve, then eighteen: thirty-seven in all. The lavender bed is seven paces across and twenty-two round. At noon the sundial's shadow touched its line just as the tower clock struck twelve. The clock was a minute late.\n\nNobody admired the geometry. They admired the lavender, which is as it should be. Mistress Corrigan gave me the knotted rope to keep. This notebook is full, so I am leaving it in the library, where the work it describes lies just outside the window."]],[["Fox, Goose and Oats","Bram Hollin had worked the rope ferry across the river Ember for thirty-four years, and he was tired of the weather as a subject. So when Juno Marsh, aged ten, began crossing each morning to school in Emberwick, he gave her a puzzle instead.\n\n\"A farmer comes to my ferry with a fox, a goose and a sack of oats. The boat holds him and one other thing. Leave the fox with the goose, and no more goose. Leave the goose with the oats, and no more oats. Get them all across.\"\n\nJuno had it by the far bank. Take the goose over first. Go back and fetch the fox, but carry the goose home again. Leave the goose, take the oats over to the fox, and return a last time for the goose. Seven crossings. The trick, said Bram, is being willing to carry something backwards."],["Four Pints Exactly","Bram Hollin, who works the rope ferry over the river Ember, keeps two tin jugs under the bench for bailing. One holds five pints and one holds three, and neither has a mark on it. One morning he set them before his youngest passenger, Juno Marsh, on her way to school in Emberwick. \"Measure me four pints exactly,\" he said. \"You have the whole river to draw on.\"\n\nJuno got her sleeves wet, but she got it. Fill the five. Pour from it into the three until the three is full, which leaves two pints in the five. Empty the three over the side. Tip those two pints into the three. Fill the five again, and from it top up the three, which has room for just one pint more.\n\nFour pints are left in the big jug. Two fives, less two threes, make four. Bram said arithmetic was drier."],["Fifteen Handshakes","It was market day, and six passengers crowded onto the Ember ferry: a miller, two aunts, a pedlar, a curate and young Juno Marsh, bound for school. Being polite people, each shook hands once with every other. Bram Hollin the ferryman asked Juno how many handshakes that had made.\n\nShe said thirty-six, then thirty. Each of the six had shaken five hands, and six fives are thirty. But Bram pointed out that the miller shaking the curate's hand and the curate shaking the miller's were one handshake, not two. Every shake had been counted from both ends. Half of thirty is fifteen.\n\nJuno distrusted it, so she counted another way. The miller shook five hands. The first aunt shook four new ones, the next person three, then two, then one: fifteen again. When two honest roads reach the same answer, said Bram, you may stop worrying."],["The Light Coin","Somebody had paid Bram Hollin, the Ember ferryman, with a false penny. It looked like the other eight in his tin, but was lighter. He owned a balance with two pans, and he bet his young passenger Juno Marsh that she could not find the false coin in two weighings.\n\nJuno first thought of four coins against four, and saw the trap: if one pan rose she would have four suspects and a single weighing left. So she weighed three against three. If the pans hung level, the light coin was among the three left out. If not, it was in the pan that rose.\n\nEither way, three suspects remained. She weighed one against another. The pan that rose held the culprit, and if they balanced, it was the third. A balance gives three answers, said Juno, so make three heaps. Bram paid up, though not with that penny."],["The Patched Deck","The deck of the Ember ferry is laid in squares, eight by eight, and Bram Hollin tars every other one so that it looks like a draughts board. Two squares at opposite corners are cut away for the mooring posts, which leaves sixty-two. Bram had thirty-one boards, each the size of two squares side by side, and he asked his schoolgirl passenger Juno Marsh how to cover the deck.\n\nShe drew decks on her slate for three crossings and always had two squares left over. On the fourth she stopped drawing and looked at the tar.\n\nEach board must cover one tarred square and one plain. But opposite corners of a draughts board are the same colour. Both missing squares were tarred, leaving thirty tarred and thirty-two plain. Thirty-one boards need thirty-one of each. It cannot be done. Bram said a proof like that saves a deal of sawing."],["Socks in the Dark","Bram Hollin rises before dawn to work the Ember ferry, and he dresses without a candle so as not to wake the dog. His drawer holds ten grey socks and ten brown, all loose. \"How many must I pull out,\" he asked Juno Marsh on the morning crossing, \"to be sure of a pair that match?\"\n\nJuno, who is eleven, said eleven. Bram said three. There are only two colours. The first two socks may differ, but the third must agree with one of them. For the same reason, among any thirteen people two must share a birth month.\n\n\"But to be sure of a grey pair?\" asked Juno. Bram began to say three again and stopped. With bad luck he might draw all ten brown socks first. Twelve, then. He allowed that she had caught him, and hauled the rest of the way in thought."],["The Five Bridges of Sedgeby","At Sedgeby, on the marsh, Heron Isle sits in the middle of the river. Two footbridges join the isle to the north bank and two join it to the south. A fifth, the cart bridge, runs from bank to bank. Juno Marsh, whose aunt lives there, asked Bram Hollin the Ember ferryman whether one could stroll over every bridge exactly once and finish where one began.\n\nBram scratched it on the deck with a nail. Passing through a place uses two bridge-ends, one arriving and one leaving. So a round walk needs an even number of bridge-ends everywhere. The isle has four, but each bank has three.\n\nNo round walk, then. An odd place can only be a start or a finish, so begin on one bank and end on the other: over to the isle, back by its twin, across the cart bridge, onto the isle, and off again."],["Three Wrong Labels","Three crates came aboard the Ember ferry from the orchards, chalked APPLES, PEARS, and APPLES AND PEARS. The orchard boy, said Bram Hollin the ferryman, had put every label on the wrong crate. \"You may take one fruit from one crate, without peeking. Then tell me what is in all three.\"\n\nHis passenger Juno Marsh thought as far as mid-river. Then she reached into the crate marked APPLES AND PEARS and drew out an apple.\n\nThat label is wrong, so the crate is not mixed; it holds only apples. The crate marked PEARS cannot hold pears, for its label is wrong too, and it cannot hold the apples, which are found. It must be the mixed one. That leaves pears for the crate marked APPLES. One apple had sorted three crates. Bram let her keep it, and observed that a known lie is nearly as useful as the truth."],["The Snail on the Post","A mooring post stands at the Emberwick landing, ten feet from waterline to top, and one spring morning a snail sat at the bottom. Bram Hollin, the ferryman, made it a puzzle for his passenger Juno Marsh. \"Each day that snail climbs three feet. Each night it slips back two. On which day does it reach the top?\"\n\n\"The tenth,\" said Juno. One foot gained each day, ten feet to go. Bram only hummed, which meant she was wrong, so she walked it through on her fingers.\n\nAfter seven days and seven nights the snail rests at seven feet. On the eighth day it climbs three more and stands on top of the post. It does not slip that night, having arrived. The eighth day. A pattern may hold all the way along, said Bram, and still break at the end. The real snail took most of April."],["Always Five","For once it was Juno Marsh who brought a puzzle aboard the Ember ferry. \"Think of a number,\" she told Bram Hollin, the ferryman. \"Double it. Add ten. Halve what you have. Take away the number you first thought of. You are left with five.\"\n\nBram had thought of seven: fourteen, twenty-four, twelve, five. He tried a hundred and got five. He tried a half, out of spite: one, eleven, five and a half, five. \"It works for those three,\" he said. \"Prove it for the rest.\"\n\nJuno was ready. Picture the secret number as pebbles in a closed bag. Double it: two bags. Add ten loose pebbles. Halve everything: one bag and five pebbles. Take the bag away. Five pebbles lie on the deck, and nobody ever opened the bag. That, said Bram, is the difference between trying and proving. Trying tests one number. Proving settles them all."],["The Square of Fifteen","Bram Hollin chalked a square of nine boxes on the deck of the Ember ferry and gave the chalk to his passenger, Juno Marsh. \"The numbers one to nine, one in each box, so that every row, every column and both slanting lines add up the same.\"\n\nFirst, the total: one to nine make forty-five, shared among three rows, so each line must come to fifteen. Next, the middle box. Four lines pass through it, worth sixty together. Those four lines cover every box once and the middle three times more. Forty-five and three middles make sixty, so the middle is five.\n\nThe rest fell out quickly. Across the top she wrote two, seven, six; in the middle nine, five, one; along the bottom four, three, eight. Bram checked all eight lines with his thumb. His own answer had taken a fortnight, he said, and hers a river's width."],["The Ferryman's Own Puzzle","Juno Marsh was sixteen, and it was her last morning crossing on the Ember ferry before the coach took her to the college in the Lowlands. For six years Bram Hollin had set her puzzles. This time she set him one.\n\n\"You wake every morning on the Emberwick bank and sleep there every night. Forty years on this rope. Without counting, have you crossed the river an odd or an even number of times?\"\n\nBram hauled in silence to mid-river. Then he laughed. Each crossing changes his bank. To end each day where he began, he must cross back for every crossing over, so they go in pairs. Even. Not one of them counted, and yet he was certain. At the landing he gave her an oilcloth notebook holding every puzzle he had asked her, each with her proof. On the cover he had written: Puzzles and Proofs."]],[["A Hundred Tosses","Osric Tallow, the candle-maker of Emberwick, had been asked to run a game stall at the Lantern Fair, to raise money for the library roof. He knew wax, not games. So on the first frosty evening he and his niece Bea sat down by the fire with a penny and a ledger, to learn how chance behaves.\n\nA penny has two faces and no reason to favour either, so the chance of heads is one in two. Bea tossed it a hundred times. Heads came fifty-three times, tails forty-seven.\n\n\"Then it is not one in two,\" said Bea. Osric thought it was, near enough. One in two does not promise fifty exactly, he decided. It promises that over many tosses the tally will lean close to half. They ruled the first table in the ledger, and Bea wrote at the top: Chance speaks of the long run."],["Two Pennies, Four Ways","On the second evening of their study of chance, Bea Tallow and her uncle Osric, the Emberwick candle-maker, tossed two pennies together. Bea had it settled before they began. Three things could happen, two heads, two tails, or one of each, so each must come one time in three.\n\nThe ledger disagreed. In a hundred tosses, two heads came twenty-four times, two tails twenty-seven, and one of each forty-nine. Nearly half, not a third.\n\nOsric found the reason by dabbing candle wax on one penny. Now they could see that waxed-heads with plain-tails and waxed-tails with plain-heads were different happenings that merely looked alike. There are four equal ways for two pennies to fall, and two of the four give one of each. So mixed comes half the time, and two heads only a quarter. Bea wrote under the table: Count the ways, not the names."],["The Honest Die","Osric Tallow, candle-maker of Emberwick, moulded his first die from beeswax. By the fireside it softened, sagged, and began to favour the four. So he and his niece Bea borrowed a bone die from the innkeeper, for an honest die must have six faces with nothing to choose between them.\n\nWith six equal faces, the chance of any one is one in six. Three faces are even, so an even throw has three chances in six, or one in two. Only the five and the six beat four, so that chance is two in six, or one in three. All six faces together make six in six, since the die must land on something.\n\nBea rolled it a hundred and twenty times for the ledger. One in six would be twenty sixes. She got twenty-two, and knew better than to complain. The wax die became a candle."],["The Table of Sums","For two dice, Bea Tallow ruled the largest table yet in the ledger she kept with her uncle Osric, the candle-maker of Emberwick. One die was plain bone and one they had stained with beetroot, to tell them apart. Six faces on the white, six on the red: thirty-six ways to fall, all equally likely.\n\nIn each box she wrote the two faces added together. A total of two appeared once, and so did twelve. But seven ran slantwise across the table, six times: one and six, two and five, three and four, and the same again with the colours changed about.\n\nSo seven comes six times in thirty-six, or one roll in six, and twelve only one roll in thirty-six. Bea had always wished for twelve because it was the biggest. Her uncle observed that she had been backing the rarest horse in the stable."],["At Least One Six","Bea Tallow reasoned thus, in her uncle's candle shop in Emberwick: one die shows a six one time in six, so two dice must show a six two times in six. Her uncle Osric said it sounded right, which made them both suspicious. They opened their ledger at the table of two dice, with its thirty-six boxes.\n\nThe white die's six fills one row of six boxes. The red die's six fills one column of six. But the row and the column cross at double six, and that box must not be counted twice. Eleven boxes hold a six, not twelve.\n\nThey checked it backwards. Each die has five faces that are not a six, and five fives give twenty-five boxes with no six. Thirty-six less twenty-five is eleven again. So the chance of at least one six is eleven in thirty-six: a little under one in three."],["The Penny Forgets","One evening in the candle shop at Emberwick, Bea Tallow tossed five heads in a row. Her uncle Osric entered them in their ledger of chances. \"Tails next,\" said Bea. \"It is due.\"\n\nA run of five heads is rare. Each toss halves the chance, so it goes one in two, then four, eight, sixteen, thirty-two: one chance in thirty-two before you begin. Six heads running is one in sixty-four. But those five were not waiting to happen. They had happened. All that remained was one ordinary toss of one ordinary penny, which has no memory and owes nobody anything. One in two, as ever.\n\nBea tossed. Heads again. Then came tails three times together, which she said proved her point and Osric said proved nothing. He wrote in the margin: The penny forgets. It is the most useful line in the ledger and the hardest to believe."],["Beans in a Bag","Osric Tallow, the Emberwick candle-maker, put three red beans and two white into a cloth bag and held it out to his niece Bea. They were studying chance that winter. Draw one bean: three of the five are red, so the chance of red is three in five.\n\n\"Now draw two together. What chance that both are red?\" Bea named the beans so that she could list every pair. There were ten pairs in all, and only three of them were red with red. Three in ten.\n\nOsric reached it another way. The first bean is red three times in five. But then only two reds remain among four beans, an even chance. Half of three fifths is three tenths. A penny forgets its last toss, Bea wrote in the ledger, but a bag remembers what has been taken out of it. Then they planted the beans."],["Three Pennies","Bea Tallow had a plan for the game stall her uncle Osric was to run at the Lantern Fair in Emberwick. A player tosses three pennies and wins if any shows a head. It sounded generous. Osric opened their ledger of chances to see how generous.\n\nThree pennies can fall in eight equal ways: two for the first, doubled by the second, doubled again by the third. Exactly two heads can happen in three of those ways, since the odd tail may be the first, second or third penny. Three in eight.\n\nBut any head at all was simpler reckoned backwards. Only one way of the eight has no head: tails, tails, tails. So seven players in every eight would win. Osric pictured the prize table empty by noon and the library roof still leaking. Often it is easiest, Bea noted, to count what you do not want."],["What a Game Pays","A fair needs prizes, and prizes cost money. So Osric Tallow, candle-maker of Emberwick, and his niece Bea opened their ledger of chances to see what a game stall keeps.\n\nThey tried a plain game first. The player pays one penny and rolls one die, and a six wins four pennies. Over six plays, on the average, each face turns up once. The stall takes six pennies and pays out four, keeping two. With a prize of six pennies the game would be perfectly fair, and in the long run nobody would gain, the library roof included.\n\nSo an honest stall leans a little its own way, and says so on the board. But Bea disliked the game. One winner in six meant five glum faces for every glad one, and a fair ought to send people home pleased. They wanted a game that paid less, more often."],["Twenty-Three Birthdays","Miss Arkle, the schoolmistress of Emberwick, bet Bea Tallow a currant bun that two of the twenty-three children in her class shared a birthday. A year has three hundred and sixty-five days, and only twenty-three children to scatter over them.\n\nShe lost. Hob the carter's son and Mercy from the mill were both born on the ninth of March. That evening Bea and her uncle Osric, the candle-maker, opened their ledger of chances to see how she had been robbed.\n\nThe mistake was counting children instead of pairs. Each child may match any of the other twenty-two, and twenty-three times twenty-two, halved, makes two hundred and fifty-three pairs. Every pair is a small separate chance of a match. The full sum cost Osric two candles, and it came out a little better than even. Bea paid the bun. Coincidences, she wrote, have more chances than they seem."],["Sevens and Doubles","After a winter of tossing pennies and rolling dice, Bea Tallow and her uncle Osric, the Emberwick candle-maker, designed their game for the Lantern Fair. The player pays a penny and rolls two dice. A seven wins. So does any double. The winner takes two pennies from the stall.\n\nTheir table of two dice gave the chances. Of its thirty-six boxes, six make a seven and six are doubles. No box is both, because a double always adds up to an even number. Twelve winning boxes in thirty-six: one roll in three.\n\nThen the money. In thirty-six plays, on the average, the stall takes thirty-six pennies and pays twelve winners two apiece, twenty-four in all. Twelve pennies stay for the library roof, and every third player walks off pleased. Osric painted the rules on a board, with the chances written underneath for anyone who cared to check."],["The Lantern Fair","The Lantern Fair came to Emberwick on a cold evening, and Osric Tallow's stall stood under a string of his own candles. Roll two dice for a penny; a seven or a double wins two. His niece Bea kept the tally in their ledger of chances.\n\nBy closing time there had been three hundred and sixty plays. One in three should win: a hundred and twenty. In fact a hundred and twenty-seven did. The stall paid out two hundred and fifty-four pennies and kept a hundred and six, not the hundred and twenty the tables foretold.\n\nBea was not troubled. Their first table held fifty-three heads in a hundred tosses. Chance leans; it does not promise. A small boy had won three times running, and the baker had lost nine. The pennies went to the library roof. The ledger went to the library too, and you are holding it."]],[["The Brass Plate","I came to Hollowmere on the first of October, with two crates of books, a new brass plate and old Dr. Pell's bag, which he pressed on me when he retired to the Grey Coast. The village lies where the marsh road meets the Emberwick road: forty houses and a duck pond. Mrs. Ardle keeps the doctor's house and regards me as a temporary arrangement.\n\nFor three days nobody called. The village had had Dr. Pell for forty-one years and saw no reason to be ill for a stranger. I polished the plate. I learned the pony's name, which is Bramble, and her opinion of me, which is low.\n\nOn the fourth day a boy named Davy Fenn knocked, holding out a thumb with a splinter in it. I drew it with a needle and he did not cry. He inspected the splinter, then me, and said he would tell the others I was all right."],["The Blacksmith's Cough","Casebook of Tobias Quince, physician at Hollowmere, late October. Amos Garth the blacksmith came to my door today, sent by his wife and walking like a man to the dentist. He has had a cough for three weeks. It sounds like a dog fox barking in a barrel.\n\nI listened to his chest and found it sound. Then I walked down to see his forge, which told me more than his chest had. He had closed every shutter against the cold, and the smoke hung under the rafters like a second ceiling.\n\nMy advice was open shutters, a walk along the marsh road at noon, and honey in something hot at bedtime. He paid me by mending my gate latch. Three weeks on, the cough is gone. Amos tells everyone it went away by itself. Mrs. Ardle, my housekeeper, says that in this village there is no higher praise."],["Mrs. Tansy's Thursdays","I am Tobias Quince, six weeks the doctor at Hollowmere, and Mrs. Tansy has sent for me five times. First it was her knee, then an ear, then a fluttering, then the knee again. Each time I found nothing wrong and the tea already laid for two.\n\nIt took me until November to make the diagnosis. Her sister moved to the Lowlands in the spring, and the cottage is very quiet. That is not a thing one can write on a slip of paper and hand across a table.\n\nSo I told her that her neighbour Miss Pilbeam, who is growing deaf, needed someone to read aloud to her each Thursday, as exercise for the ears. I told Miss Pilbeam that Mrs. Tansy needed company and must not know it. Both have improved wonderfully. I still call there, but as a guest, and the cake has improved too."],["The Night Ride","Hollowmere, midwinter. At two this morning Jem Fenny hammered on my door: the baby was coming, out at Fenny Farm, three miles along the marsh causeway. I saddled Bramble by lantern light. The pony made her feelings plain, but she went, and the frost rang under her like iron.\n\nI arrived to find Nan Sorrel already there. She has been the village midwife since before I was born, and she had everything in hand. She looked me over, the new young doctor with ice on his eyebrows, and gave me two tasks: keep the kettle full and keep the father out of the way.\n\nI walked Jem round the yard eleven times. At first light his daughter arrived, loud and pink and perfectly well, and her mother with her. They have named her Hester. I did nothing but hold a lantern, and I have seldom been so glad to be anywhere."],["The Schoolmaster Cannot Sleep","Casebook of Dr. Tobias Quince, Hollowmere, January. Mr. Lorimer the schoolmaster came to me grey under the eyes. He cannot sleep. He lies awake counting what might go wrong: the inspector's visit, the schoolroom roof, the Fenn boys and the inkwells. He asked for a sleeping draught.\n\nI gave him three instructions instead. No ledgers after supper. A walk to the duck pond and back before bed, whatever the weather. And his worries were to be written each night on a slate and left downstairs, where they would keep perfectly well until morning.\n\nHe plainly thought it poor value. But a fortnight later he stopped me by the pump to report that he now sleeps until the cock crows. The slate, he says, carries the same three worries most nights, and they look a good deal smaller in chalk. The Fenn boys remain beyond the reach of medicine."],["The Postman's Ankle","February, Hollowmere. Ned Harrow the postman went down on the ice beside the pump and could not get up. A sprain, I found, not a break, but the ankle swelled like a turnip. I bound it and ordered him to keep it up on a stool for a week. He took the sprain bravely. The thought of the letters nearly finished him.\n\nSo for seven days the post went round with the doctor. Bramble the pony and I carried the bag on my rounds, and I learned the parish as no casebook could teach it: which gates stick, which dogs mean it, who hears from the Lowlands every week and who never hears from anyone.\n\nPeople who would not have called me to a sickbed talked for ten minutes over a letter. By the time Ned was walking, I had stood at every door in the village. He says I delivered two letters wrongly. He is right."],["The Speckles","Casebook of Tobias Quince, village doctor at Hollowmere. April brought what the village calls the speckles: pink spots, a mild fever and a mighty itch. It began with the Fenn children and went through the school like a rumour. Nineteen cases in ten days.\n\nThere is little a doctor can do for the speckles except visit. I went from cottage to cottage and said the same things: keep them home, keep them cool and quiet, give them broth, and stop them scratching. The mothers knew all this already. I think my use was to say it would pass, in the voice of someone holding a bag.\n\nIt passed. All nineteen were out of bed within the fortnight. Davy Fenn tried to count his spots and reached sixty-four before he lost his place. The school was shut for ten days, and Mr. Lorimer the schoolmaster told me, in confidence, that he had never felt better."],["Hay for Silas Croft","Hollowmere, June. Silas Croft has put his back out, and he chose the first day of haymaking to do it. I found him bent like a hairpin beside his own gate. A week of rest would mend him, I said. He said the hay would not wait a week, and that I knew less about farming than his dog.\n\nHe was right about the hay. So that evening I went round the village, not as a doctor exactly, and mentioned the matter at seven or eight doors.\n\nNext morning there were eleven people in Croft's meadow with scythes and rakes, the blacksmith among them. The field was cut, turned and carted in three days. Silas watched from a mattress by the gate, shouting directions nobody followed. His back is mended now. Rest is the hardest thing to prescribe to a farmer, and the patient cannot always fill the prescription alone."],["Bramble Goes Lame","July. I am Tobias Quince, doctor at Hollowmere, and my pony Bramble is the patient this month. She picked up a stone on the marsh road and bruised her foot. Amos Garth the blacksmith looked at it, ordered a fortnight's rest in the paddock, and told me not to argue, in very much my own voice.\n\nSo I walked my rounds, six miles on a short day. I expected to fall behind. Instead the village rearranged itself around me. Those who could walk came to the surgery to spare my legs. Children carried my bag in relays from one cottage to the next. The Crofts offered me their donkey, which I declined with thanks, having met it.\n\nMrs. Tansy sent a pie to keep my strength up. Bramble is sound again, and no better tempered. But for a fortnight I was looked after by my own patients, and I have not forgotten it."],["Dr. Pell Comes to Tea","Hollowmere, August. Old Dr. Pell came over from the Grey Coast today, to see what I have done with his village. He was doctor here for forty-one years before me. He studied my brass plate, then his old bag, which I still carry, and then me.\n\nWe walked down the street together and it took an hour, because every door opened. Over tea he asked what I, Tobias Quince, had learned in ten months, and I began to list remedies. He let me finish.\n\n\"What I learned,\" he said, \"is that most people will tell you what is wrong in the first minute, if you let them reach the end of the sentence. Listen first. The bag is mainly for carrying sandwiches.\" Then he told me he had it on good authority that I was all right. The authority was Davy Fenn. He went home on the carrier's cart, looking content."],["Plums at the Harvest Supper","Casebook of Tobias Quince, Hollowmere, September. The harvest supper was held in Croft's barn, and I was put at the top table, between Nan Sorrel the midwife and Mr. Lorimer the schoolmaster. A year ago I would have been lucky to get a stool by the door.\n\nHalfway through the goose, a small hand tugged my sleeve. Davy Fenn was underneath the table, and green. He had a pain in his middle. Under questioning he admitted to plums, and under further questioning to seventeen of them.\n\nI prescribed lying down in the hay, nothing more to eat, and time. By the first dance he was upright again and watching the pudding with interest. Later Amos Garth the blacksmith stood up and proposed the health of the doctor. I looked round for Dr. Pell. It was a moment before I understood that he meant me."],["The Doctor Takes to Bed","October again, and a year since I, Tobias Quince, came to Hollowmere. I marked the occasion by catching a heavy cold on the marsh road. Mrs. Ardle, my housekeeper, ordered me to bed in words I recognised as my own.\n\nThen the village arrived. Mrs. Tansy sent broth. Honey came from Fenny Farm. Amos Garth eased my bedroom shutter so that it would open, for the air, on doctor's orders. Ned Harrow brought the post upstairs. Nan Sorrel inspected me and said I would live, and Davy Fenn stood in the doorway to confirm that I was all right.\n\nI was up in four days. That afternoon I opened the last crate of books, which had stood nailed shut in the hall for a year, in case I should not stay. Mrs. Ardle watched me shelve them. Then she said she supposed she had better order coal for the winter, and for the one after."]],[["January: Honey","I am Philippa Yarrow, and I keep the remedy shop at the sign of the Green Jar in Emberwick. This January I took an apprentice, Colm Reddy, aged thirteen. He arrived hoping for powdered pearls and dragon's teeth. I showed him the shelves: forty jars of honey, dried mint, and a great deal of barley.\n\nHis first customer was the carter, with a throat like a rusty hinge. Colm looked to me for something rare. I had him stir a spoonful of honey into a cup of warm water with a slice of lemon, and send the man home to sip it and keep quiet for a day.\n\nHoney cures nothing, I told Colm afterwards. It soothes, which is a different trade and an honest one. A sore throat mends in its own time, and our business is to make the waiting kinder. He wrote that down, looking cheated. He will come round."],["February: Broth","February at the Green Jar, my remedy shop in Emberwick. The cold weather has half of Tallow Lane in bed, and my apprentice Colm asked which jar on our shelves was best for people too weak to get up. I told him the best remedy for that was not in a jar. It was in the big black pot.\n\nBroth is a chicken, an onion, a carrot and a handful of barley, left to simmer until the kitchen window runs with steam. It asks nothing of a tired body and gives back warmth, salt and the sense of being looked after. I think the last of these does most of the work.\n\nOur pot goes up the lane each winter from house to house, and comes back washed, with a different thank-you in it every time. This week it returned holding six eggs. Colm carried it out twice in the sleet and did not complain, which I have noted in his favour."],["March: Fresh Air","I am Philippa Yarrow of the Green Jar in Emberwick, and in March I prescribe what costs nothing. A young clerk came in this week, pale as a candle, complaining of a heavy head and no appetite. My apprentice Colm reached hopefully for the tonic shelf.\n\nI asked the clerk where he worked. In a back office, it turned out, with the window painted shut and a stove going from eight until six. I sold him nothing. I told him to walk the river path to the ferry and back every noon, and to have that window unstuck by a carpenter.\n\nColm thought we had lost a sale. But sickrooms, offices and bedrooms all go stale, and a stale room makes a dull head. A window open a hand's breadth is a remedy as old as windows. The clerk came back in a fortnight with colour in his face, and bought honey he did not need, out of gratitude."],["April: The Elixir Man","April brought a painted wagon to Emberwick market, and with it Professor Lucian Vesper and his Universal Elixir, in purple bottles. It cured, he announced, coughs, corns, melancholy, baldness and the bad temper of horses. My apprentice Colm stood in the front row with his mouth open. Our own shop, the Green Jar, has never claimed to cure even one of those.\n\nI bought a bottle, for science. Back at my counter I tasted it: sugar water, coloured with something and flavoured with liquorice. Quite harmless. Rather pleasant, in fact.\n\nColm asked why I did not denounce the man in the square. I said the elixir would hurt nothing but purses, and that people forgive a fraud sooner than a scold. But I told him to watch what a remedy promises. The honest ones promise very little. The professor drove on toward the Lowlands, a little ahead of his customers."],["May: Sleep","May, at the Green Jar in Emberwick. The miller's wife came in grey with tiredness. She has not slept properly in a month, since the new mill clock arrived, and she wanted the strongest thing I had. I am Philippa Yarrow, and the strongest thing I have for sleeplessness is a list.\n\nA warm cup of milk at bedtime. A small bag of dried lavender under the pillow, which smells of summer and does no more than that. No accounts after supper. And the clock was to be carried down to the kitchen, where it could tick at the cat.\n\nMy apprentice Colm sewed the lavender bag himself, with stitches like a row of fence posts. Sleep cannot be forced, I told him. It can only be invited, and it comes soonest to a quiet room. The miller's wife called in a week later to say she had slept nine hours. The cat, she says, is looking tired."],["June: Barley Water","Haymaking began this week around Emberwick, and with it my busiest trade of the summer, which is in barley water. I am Philippa Yarrow of the Green Jar, and every June I send my apprentice out to the meadows with two stone jars slung on a yoke.\n\nBarley water is only barley simmered, strained and cooled, with a little lemon and honey to make it welcome. There is no magic in it. But men will mow all day under a hot sun and forget to drink, and by afternoon their heads ache and their tempers go.\n\nColm came back sunburnt and thoughtful. He had found old Hobson dizzy by the hedge and had sat him in the shade with a wet cloth on his neck and a cup in his hand, without being told. In half an hour the old man was himself again. Shade, water and sitting down: Colm is beginning to see how plain most remedies are."],["July: Mint","The day after the Emberwick summer fair is always the same at the Green Jar. By nine o'clock there is a row of sorry faces at my counter, each of whom ate a pie too many, or the toffee apples on top of the pickled eggs. I am Philippa Yarrow, and I keep a large jar of dried mint for exactly this morning.\n\nA pinch of mint in a cup, hot water over it, and five minutes' patience. It smells clean and it settles a stomach that has merely been overworked. I had my apprentice Colm make eleven cups before noon.\n\nHe asked what else we might give them. Nothing, I said. A stomach that has been insulted wants a quiet day and plain food, and it will forgive by evening. The mint is mostly a kindness to sip while it does. Colm drank the twelfth cup himself. He had been at the fair too, and the toffee apples."],["August: Colm's Great Tonic","August is slow at the Green Jar, my remedy shop in Emberwick, and I left my apprentice Colm in charge for an afternoon. He has never forgotten the travelling professor and his Universal Elixir. I came home to a smell like a pond in a heatwave.\n\nColm had made a tonic. His reasoning was that if mint is good, and honey, and barley, and lavender, and ginger, and stewed apple, then all of them boiled together in one pot must be six times as good. The result was grey. It had a skin on it. The shop cat had left by the window.\n\nNobody was harmed, because nobody could be persuaded to taste it, Colm included. I asked what it was meant to cure. \"Everything,\" he said. That, I told him, is the fault in it. A remedy for everything is a remedy for nothing in particular. We poured it on the rhubarb, which has not complained."],["September: Plain Food","September, and the Green Jar smells of apples. I am Philippa Yarrow, remedy-keeper of Emberwick, and this month my apprentice Colm learned what to give people who are nearly well.\n\nOld Mrs. Finch has been three weeks in bed with a chest cold. The cold has gone, but she is weak as a kitten and cannot face her dinner. Her daughter wanted a strengthening tonic. I sent Colm round instead with a bowl of apples stewed soft, and the next day with oat porridge and a spoon of honey in the middle.\n\nGetting better is its own piece of work, I explained, and the body wants plain fuel for it: soft, warm, and little at a time. Appetite comes back like a shy cat, and must not be rushed at with roast beef. On the fifth day Mrs. Finch asked for a boiled egg and complained that the toast was cold. Complaining is an excellent sign."],["October: Warmth","The first frost came to Emberwick this week and brought the usual visitors to the Green Jar: people with chills, stiff knees and cold feet that will not warm. I am Philippa Yarrow, and October is when I fetch the river stones down from the attic.\n\nA smooth stone, warmed by the hearth and wrapped in two thicknesses of flannel, will keep a bed warm for half the night. I lend them out by the dozen. For the rest, a wool shawl, thick stockings, and a cup of hot water with a slice of ginger, which warms from the inside.\n\nMy apprentice Colm wrapped stones all Tuesday. He remarked that we had sold nothing all day and had lent nearly everything. I said that was a fair description of the trade. Then he took a stone round to the old ferryman without being asked, and I began to think the shop would be safe with him."],["November: Company","In November a painted wagon limped into Emberwick with one lamp out. It was Professor Vesper, seller of the Universal Elixir, and he had a streaming cold, a cough and nowhere to stay. His own purple bottles had done nothing for him. I am Philippa Yarrow of the Green Jar, and I put him in my back room.\n\nMy apprentice Colm nursed him with everything this year has taught us: honey for the throat, broth from the black pot, a warm stone at his feet and the window open a hand's breadth. Mostly, though, Colm sat and listened to his stories. The professor has been alone on the road for eleven years.\n\nOn the fourth day he confessed, unasked, that his elixir was sugar water. We said we knew. He was better by the week's end, and I believe the listening did as much as the broth. He now sells honey drops in the market, and calls them only honey drops."],["December: The Universal Tonic","December, the last page of my year at the Green Jar in Emberwick. On Midwinter Eve my apprentice Colm Reddy handed me a sheet of our best paper. He had finally written out his Universal Tonic, and he asked me, Philippa Yarrow, to judge it.\n\nIt read: Honey for the throat. Broth for the weak. Fresh air for the dull head. Sleep, invited and not forced. Water in the heat and warmth in the cold. Plain food, a little at a time. Someone to sit by the bed. And time, which does most of the mending and takes none of the credit.\n\nNot one item needs a purple bottle. I could find nothing to add, so I framed it and hung it over the counter. Then I gave Colm the key to the still-room. He came in January looking for dragon's teeth. He has found something better, and he knows it."]],[["Introducing Mr. Pym","This manual is written for my apprentice, Nell Wicker, who came to my surgery in Tanner's Row, Emberwick, at the age of fifteen, wishing to learn bones. I am Dr. Augustin Farrow. I have set them for thirty years.\n\nOur teacher in these pages is Mr. Pym. He hangs from a hook beside my desk: a full skeleton carved in pearwood by a cabinetmaker who owed me for a mended wrist. Every bone is there to the proper size, two hundred and six in all, counting the six tiny ear bones we keep in a pillbox. Or rather two hundred and five. His left kneecap went missing years ago, and I suspect a dog.\n\nA grown person has two hundred and six bones. A newborn baby has nearer three hundred pieces, many still soft, which knit together as the child grows. We shall take Mr. Pym from the top down. Nell has already shaken his hand."],["The Skull's Seams","Nell Wicker, my apprentice, supposed the skull to be one bone, like a pot. I, Dr. Farrow of Emberwick, lifted down the head of Mr. Pym, our pearwood skeleton, and showed her the seams. A skull is twenty-two bones. Eight make the rounded case that keeps the brain, and fourteen make the face. They lock along wavy joins, like well-cut pieces of a puzzle. Only the lower jaw is free to move.\n\nIn a baby those seams are not yet closed, and there is a soft place on the crown that fills in during the second year. A head must have room to grow.\n\nThis week the cooper's boy stood up under a beam and raised a lump like a hen's egg. His mother feared the worst. I found a sound skull, a clear eye and a boy keen to describe the beam. He was kept quiet for a day and watched, and that was all."],["Hammer, Anvil, Stirrup","The smallest bones of the body live in a pillbox in my desk drawer, and I let my apprentice Nell Wicker tip them into her palm. They belong to Mr. Pym, the pearwood skeleton of my surgery in Emberwick. There are three for each ear, named for their shapes: the hammer, the anvil and the stirrup.\n\nThey sit in a chain behind the eardrum. When a sound sets the drum trembling, the hammer passes the tremble to the anvil, and the anvil to the stirrup, which hands it inward to be heard. The stirrup is the smallest bone we own, no bigger than a grain of rice. The cabinetmaker who carved Mr. Pym said those six cost him more trouble than both legs.\n\nOld Mr. Dabney came in today, quite sure his family had taken to mumbling. Age, not illness; hearing wears as knees do. I, Dr. Farrow, could offer him only an ear trumpet and my sympathy, loudly."],["Seven in the Neck","To teach the spine I, Dr. Farrow, had my apprentice Nell run a finger down the back of Mr. Pym, the pearwood skeleton who hangs in our Emberwick surgery, counting as she went. Seven bones in the neck. Twelve in the back, each carrying a pair of ribs. Five large ones in the loins. That makes twenty-four that move. Below them sits the sacrum, five bones grown into one, and last the little tailbone.\n\nBetween each moving pair lies a cushion of gristle, so the column can bend and take a jolt. The cushions settle through the day; we are all a shade taller at breakfast than at supper. A giraffe, I am told, has seven neck bones too, only longer.\n\nThe weaver came in this week, stiff as his own loom from sitting. His spine was sound. I advised him to stand and stretch whenever he finished a hand's breadth of cloth. Backs are made to move."],["Twelve Pairs of Ribs","My apprentice Nell Wicker asked whether men have one rib fewer than women, as her grandmother holds. I am Dr. Farrow of Emberwick, and I let our pearwood skeleton, Mr. Pym, answer. She counted him: twelve ribs on each side, twenty-four in all. It is the same for everyone.\n\nThe ribs make a cage for the heart and lungs. The upper seven pairs join the breastbone in front. The next three pairs each hang from the rib above. The last two pairs are short and join nothing in front; we call them floating ribs. The whole cage lifts and widens with each breath, which Nell proved with her hands on her own sides.\n\nThe thatcher slid off a roof into a hay cart and bruised three ribs. Nothing was broken. Bruised ribs want only rest and time, and the difficulty is laughing. He begged his friends to stop visiting, since every one arrived with a joke."],["The Collarbone","Run a finger from your throat out to your shoulder and you are tracing the collarbone. I, Dr. Farrow, showed it to my apprentice Nell on Mr. Pym, the pearwood skeleton of our surgery in Emberwick. It is slender and gently curved, like a long letter S, and it works as a strut, holding the shoulder out from the chest so the arm can swing free. Behind it lies the shoulder blade, a flat triangle that glides over the back of the ribs.\n\nBeing a strut, the collarbone takes the shock when anyone lands on an outstretched hand. It is the bone I see broken most often, and children mend it fastest.\n\nThe carrier's daughter came off her pony on Saturday and broke hers cleanly. I put the arm in a sling, and Nell tied the knot. In a few weeks there will be a small firm lump where the bone has knitted, and the girl intends to show it to everyone."],["The Arm and Its Hinge","This page of the manual concerns the arm. I am Dr. Farrow of Tanner's Row, Emberwick, and I taught it to my apprentice Nell by unhooking the arm of Mr. Pym, our pearwood skeleton, and laying it across her knees.\n\nFrom shoulder to elbow there is one stout bone. From elbow to wrist there are two, lying side by side: one on the thumb side and one on the side of the little finger. The second makes the hard point of the elbow. When you turn your palm downward, the thumb-side bone rolls across the other, and Nell turned Mr. Pym's hand over and back until she had seen it.\n\nShe asked for the funny bone. There is none. That tingle comes from a nerve crossing the back of the elbow, close under the skin. The baker's boy came in holding his elbow, certain it was broken. He had struck that nerve on a doorpost. He recovered while describing it."],["Twenty-Seven in a Hand","I asked my apprentice Nell Wicker to guess how many bones are in one hand. She said ten. So I, Dr. Farrow, unhooked the hand of Mr. Pym, the pearwood skeleton of our Emberwick surgery, and we counted. Eight small bones in the wrist, packed in two rows of four like cobbles. Five in the palm. Fourteen in the fingers: three to each finger and two to the thumb. Twenty-seven.\n\nSo many small pieces let the hand cup, grip, pinch and spread. A paw of ten bones could hold a spade, but it could not thread a needle.\n\nJory the fiddler came in with a sprained wrist, four days before the harvest dance, and in despair. Nothing was broken. I bound the wrist and forbade the fiddle for a fortnight. He stood on a barrel at the dance and whistled every tune instead, and Nell says no one stopped dancing."],["The Longest Bone","The thighbone is the longest and strongest bone we have. I, Dr. Farrow, measured Mr. Pym's against my apprentice Nell: in most people it is about a quarter of their whole height. Mr. Pym is the pearwood skeleton who hangs in our surgery in Emberwick, and his thighbones are the only parts of him that needed a second plank.\n\nAt its top the thighbone ends in a ball, set off on a short neck. The ball sits in a deep cup in the hip bone. The two hip bones meet the base of the spine to form a sturdy basin, which carries the weight of everything above.\n\nOld Grandfather Oake's hips have grown stiff with age, and no doctor can make them young. But Nell cut him a blackthorn stick of the proper height, and he now walks to the bridge and back daily to show it off."],["The Missing Kneecap","Mr. Pym, the pearwood skeleton of my surgery in Emberwick, has no left kneecap. My apprentice Nell Wicker noticed it on her first day and has not let the matter rest. I am Dr. Farrow, and I admit it went missing in my time.\n\nThe kneecap is a small rounded shield of bone. It does not join the other bones directly. It rides inside the strong cord that runs from the thigh muscle to the shin, guarding the front of the knee and giving the muscle a better pull. A baby's kneecaps are soft gristle and harden into bone during childhood.\n\nMrs. Lunn the baker has a sore knee from kneeling to her low oven. I advised a cushion and a taller stool. Meanwhile Nell has searched the cupboards, the coal scuttle and the garden bed. No kneecap. She has asked the cabinetmaker for an offcut of pearwood, and will not say why."],["Shin, Ankle and Arch","Below the knee there are two bones again, I told my apprentice Nell Wicker, pointing them out on Mr. Pym, the pearwood skeleton of our Emberwick surgery. The shinbone is the stout one in front and bears the weight. Beside it runs a slender partner, thin as a flute. The knob on the inside of your ankle is the end of the first; the knob on the outside is the end of the second.\n\nThen the foot: twenty-six bones. Seven make the ankle and heel, five the long middle, fourteen the toes. They are built into springy arches. Nell did a sum on the slate. Two hands and two feet hold one hundred and six bones, more than half the body's count.\n\nNed the postman came in with aching feet. I, Dr. Farrow, examined them and then his boots, which were worn flat as planks. I prescribed a cobbler."],["How Bones Mend","This is the last page of the manual I, Dr. Augustin Farrow, have written for my apprentice Nell Wicker, and it holds the best thing I know about bones. They are alive. A broken bone, set straight and kept still, builds a soft bridge across the break, and over some weeks the bridge hardens into new bone. The doctor only holds the ends in place. The bone does the mending.\n\nYesterday Nell stood before Mr. Pym, the pearwood skeleton of our surgery in Emberwick, and named all his bones from the crown to the smallest toe without one mistake. Then she took something from her apron pocket. She had carved a left kneecap from an offcut of pearwood, and it fits him exactly.\n\nSo Mr. Pym has two hundred and six bones at last, and I have a colleague instead of an apprentice. The old kneecap turned up this morning, behind the dresser. We have kept hers."]],[["The First Lantern","I am Clementine Rook, physician of Emberwick, and I begin this book on the eighth of November because of a boy's face. Tam Weller, the ferryman's son, was put to bed this afternoon with cheeks as red as a lamp behind a shade. His grandmother calls it the lantern fever, and says it comes through the town every dozen years or so.\n\nA fever is not the illness itself. It is the body's own fire, lit on purpose against an unwelcome guest, and a sign that the household is defending itself. The doctor's work is mostly to keep the patient comfortable while the fire does its job.\n\nTam was hot, cross and thirsty, and otherwise sound. I ordered bed, water and a quiet room. Then I came home and ruled this book into columns, for I do not think he will be the only one. I mean to set down everything this fever does, from first case to last."],["The Shiver","Casebook of Dr. Clementine Rook, Emberwick, the tenth of November. A second case of the lantern fever today: Mrs. Dunnock, the baker's wife. I record how it begins, for it begins oddly. A fever starts with cold.\n\nShe had been kneading at the warm end of the bakery when she began to shiver. By the time I came she lay under three quilts with her teeth knocking together, declaring the house was freezing. Her forehead was hot as a new loaf. The body is stoking its fire, and while it stokes, the patient feels only the draught.\n\nThe shiver lasts an hour or two and is frightening to watch, though it does no harm. I sat with her until the chattering eased. Her husband hovered with a fourth quilt. Then, just as with young Tam Weller two days ago, the cold fit passed and the red cheeks came up like lamps being lit."],["The Heat","The second day of the lantern fever is the hot one. I am Dr. Clementine Rook of Emberwick, and I set down here what I saw in Tam Weller, the ferryman's boy, who was the first to take it.\n\nHis skin was dry and burning, his eyes very bright, his cheeks two red lamps. He was restless and wanted nothing, then wanted everything. His pulse ran quick as a pony trotting. This is the fire at full blaze, and though it alarms a household, it is the fever doing its work.\n\nI have no wish to put the fire out, only to keep the patient easy beside it. A light sheet in place of blankets. A cool damp cloth on the forehead, changed when it warms. The window open a hand's breadth. Sips of water, often. His grandmother did all of it before I could say it. I sat and counted his breathing, which was steady."],["The Thirst","Emberwick, the fifteenth of November. The lantern fever has reached old Mr. Hobb, caretaker at the school in Lamp Lane, and from him I, Dr. Clementine Rook, have learned about the thirst.\n\nA fevered body dries like washing in a wind. The lips crack, the tongue furs, and the patient thinks of nothing but drink. Water, then, in small sips and often; barley water; weak tea gone cool. I ask each household to keep a covered jug by the bed, and to offer the cup rather than wait to be asked.\n\nFever makes the thirsty particular. Mrs. Dunnock would take water only from her blue cup. Tam Weller asked for water from the middle of the river. Mr. Hobb demanded snow, in a week with none. His wife brought well water in a bowl and told him it was melted. He drank it and pronounced it excellent."],["Fever Dreams","On the third night of the lantern fever the mind wanders a little. I am Dr. Clementine Rook, and I note it here so that no household in Emberwick need be frightened as Miss Ivory's was.\n\nMiss Ivory is the schoolmistress, the most sensible woman in the town. On Tuesday night she informed her sister that the roses on the wallpaper were nodding to her, and that she had nodded back to be polite. Later she set the ceiling a spelling lesson. Her sister sent for me at a run.\n\nI found a hot forehead and a perfectly gentle patient. These fancies belong to the heat and go when it goes, leaving nothing behind. The remedy is a low lamp, a calm voice and a hand to hold. Arguing does no good. By morning Miss Ivory was cooler and remembered none of it. She was mortified about the ceiling, and asked me whether it had spelled well."],["Pins in the Map","Emberwick, the twentieth of November: fourteen cases of the lantern fever. Tonight I, Dr. Clementine Rook, pinned a map of the town to my study wall and pushed in a pin for every sickbed.\n\nThe town already has its explanation. It is the marsh mist, people say, creeping up the river at night. But my pins do not agree. The cottages at the marsh end, which get the mist first and thickest, have no fever at all. Nor do the pins follow the river.\n\nThey gather instead around Lamp Lane, in the middle of the town, like bees about a hive. In Lamp Lane stand the school, the bakery and eleven houses. A fever does not drift about like weather. It is handed from one person to the next, and somewhere in that lane it is being handed on. I do not yet see how. I have left the map where I can look at it."],["The Turning","Every fever has a turning, and with the lantern fever it comes on the third or fourth night. I am Dr. Clementine Rook of Emberwick. I watched it tonight in Mr. Hobb, the school caretaker, and I write it down while the candle lasts.\n\nNear midnight he was at his hottest, dry and muttering. Then, within a quarter of an hour, his forehead grew damp. Soon he was wet through, as if he had been out in rain. The red went out of his cheeks. His breathing slowed and deepened, and he slept: real sleep, the first in three days.\n\nThat is the fire going out, its work finished. All that is wanted is a dry nightshirt, dry sheets and quiet. His wife and I changed the bed around him and he never stirred. Then he began to snore. I know of no pleasanter sound in my profession, and we sat and listened to it for some time."],["Legs Like Wet String","The lantern fever leaves in a night, but it does not give back the patient's strength at once. I am Dr. Clementine Rook of Emberwick, and the hardest part of my month has been keeping the recovered in their chairs.\n\nAfter the fever turns, there is a week of weakness. The head is clear and the appetite is stirring, so the patient feels well, right up until standing. Then the legs go like wet string. It is only the body asking for time to restock the larder, and rest gives it.\n\nNobody believes me. Mrs. Dunnock went back to the bakery on her second day up and was found sitting down in the flour bin. Tam Weller got as far as the ferry landing and was carried home by his father. Miss Ivory tried to mark a pile of slates and woke with chalk on her cheek. All three are now resting under protest, and all three are mending."],["The Tin Cup","The twenty-fourth of November. Tonight I, Dr. Clementine Rook, believe I have found how the lantern fever goes about Emberwick. Twenty-six pins stand in my map, crowded around Lamp Lane, and I went through my casebook asking one question of each.\n\nEvery case is a child at the Lamp Lane school, or lives with one, or works there. Miss Ivory teaches there. Mr. Hobb sweeps there. Mrs. Dunnock, who puzzled me, carries bread to the school each morning, and she tells me she always stops at the pump in the yard for a drink.\n\nAt that pump hangs a single tin cup on a chain. Thirty-one children and anyone passing drink from it, lip after lip, all day. I cannot prove it, but I can act on it. I have asked Miss Ivory that every child bring a cup from home, and wash hands before dinner. The tin cup is now in my desk drawer."],["The Doctor's Own Fever","I write this in bed. On the first of December I, Dr. Clementine Rook, having attended thirty-three cases of the lantern fever in Emberwick, became the thirty-fourth. I am now able to describe it from the inside, which is thorough of me.\n\nThe shiver came first, and I was exactly as cold as my patients said and I had not quite believed. Then the heat. I asked for the window wider and then accused it of staring. My neighbour Mrs. Plover nursed me with sips of water, a cool cloth and no nonsense. On the third night, I am told, I lectured the bedpost on its pulse.\n\nThen the turning: the sweat, the dry sheets, and a sleep like falling into a haystack. Today my head is clear and my legs are string. Mrs. Plover has hidden my boots. I mean to obey my own advice for a week, having learned how it feels to be given it."],["The First Boiled Egg","The last stage of the lantern fever is hunger, and it is the pleasantest to record. I am Dr. Clementine Rook of Emberwick, a fortnight on from my own sickbed, and I remember wanting a boiled egg more than I have ever wanted anything.\n\nAppetite returns in steps. First broth, then toast, then the egg, and then, about the tenth day, the patient is discovered in the pantry. I tell families to let it come at its own pace.\n\nThere is better news than eggs. Since the tin cup at the school pump was taken down, new cases have dwindled: eight in the first week, five in the next, then two, and none at all since the eleventh of December. Mr. Sallow the potter has made thirty-one small cups, each with a child's name painted under the glaze. They hang on pegs in the schoolroom. Tam Weller, the first to fall ill, was the first to drink from his."],["The Lantern Goes Out","Midwinter Eve, and I, Dr. Clementine Rook, close this book. The lantern fever came to Emberwick on the eighth of November with Tam Weller's red cheeks, and the last patient, Mr. Sallow the potter, got out of bed this morning. Forty-one cases in six weeks. Forty-one recoveries. I have counted the columns twice for the pleasure of it.\n\nI have set down all I saw: the shiver, the heat, the thirst, the dreams, the turning, the weakness and the hunger. A fever is a fire that knows its business. My part was water, cool cloths, clean sheets, and noticing a tin cup.\n\nTonight the town walked in the Midwinter procession, every child carrying a lantern, and Tam Weller at the front. I have taken the pins out of my map and rolled it away. The lamps going past my window were the only red faces in Emberwick, and they were made of paper."]],[["A Book Coming Apart","The Statute Book of Emberwick has lost its spine, two corners and most of its dignity. The council has therefore asked me, Hesper Quill, Borough Clerk, to copy all forty-one statutes fresh, and to set beside each a plain account of how it came to be made.\n\nI begin with Statute One: that no goods be sold in the market before the market bell has rung. It dates from the morning a fishmonger from the Grey Coast sold his whole cart of herring to one innkeeper before dawn, and the town breakfasted on porridge and resentment.\n\nOne oddity already. The page for Statute Seventeen is blank. The index, in a hand far older than mine, says only: \"Seventeen. See the mace.\" I have seen the mace. It is a brass stick with a knob on it. I shall look again."],["The Goose Hour","Statute Four, which I, Hesper Quill, Borough Clerk, have this week copied into the new Statute Book, provides that geese may be driven along Tanner's Row only between six and seven in the morning.\n\nThe cause was the wet summer of Mayor Pollard's year. His procession, with band, turned into Tanner's Row from the north at the moment two hundred geese bound for the Lowlands market entered it from the south. Neither party would give way. The geese, being more numerous and less embarrassed, prevailed. The mayor's hat was recovered from the river Ember a week later and is kept in the town hall, in a case.\n\nThe statute has never once been broken, chiefly because geese prefer the early hour. I record it as a rare instance of a law that agrees with those it governs."],["Lanterns on Bridge Street","In recopying the borough's statutes I come to the Ninth, and I, Hesper Quill, Clerk of Emberwick, confess it is my favourite. On any night of marsh fog, each household in Bridge Street shall hang a lit lantern at its door until morning.\n\nIt was made after a carter named Jeb Marlow, feeling his way home, mistook the river for the road. He and the horse were fished out unharmed, though a load of turnips went on to the sea. The street met next evening in the cooper's yard and agreed the rule among themselves. The council merely wrote it down a year later and took the credit.\n\nThat is the usual order of things, I find. The town decides, and the statute arrives afterwards, slightly out of breath. On foggy nights I walk down to count the lanterns, eleven of them, and feel well governed."],["No Whistling by the Weir","Statute Twelve forbids whistling within fifty paces of the weir after dusk. Strangers to Emberwick suppose it a joke. As Borough Clerk, copying the old Statute Book page by page, I can report that it is nothing of the kind.\n\nThe lock-keeper of that day signalled to the night barges by whistle: one long note for wait, two short for come on. A baker's boy, walking home pleased with himself and whistling a jig, delivered what amounted to nine contradictory orders. Two barges met in the cut. Nobody was hurt, but forty sacks of flour went into the water, and the ducks below the weir grew noticeably stout.\n\nThe keeper now uses a lamp, so the reason has gone. The statute stays. I have marked it \"kept for the sake of the story\", which is not a legal category, but ought to be."],["The Brass Loaf","Statute Fourteen: every baker in Emberwick shall keep a loaf-board of the borough's pattern, and no penny loaf shall be smaller than the board. I, Hesper Quill, Borough Clerk, copy it out with the smell of bread at my window.\n\nTwo bakers, Orrin and Padbury, once faced each other across Cheap Street. Each meant to be the cheaper, and managed it by making the loaf a little smaller every week. By midsummer a penny loaf could be lost in a coat pocket. The council had one honest loaf cast in brass and hung it in the town hall.\n\nMrs. Padbury's daughter Nell, who is eight, asked me what a statute was. A promise the whole town makes, I said. She asked who checks. I said that I do. She looked at me as one looks at a very small dog set to guard a very large house."],["The Blank Page","I have reached the page that should hold Statute Seventeen, and it holds nothing. The index of the old Statute Book says, \"Seventeen. See the mace.\" So this morning I, Hesper Quill, Borough Clerk, went and saw the mace.\n\nMr. Corriby, the mace-bearer, lifted it from its case with both hands. It is brass, as long as a walking stick, with a head like a turnip. I read every inch of it through my glass. There is the borough's ember, a date, a dent from the procession of the wet summer, and no statute at all.\n\n\"It rattles a bit,\" said Mr. Corriby, shaking it gently. \"Always has. A loose pin, my father thought.\" I agreed it was probably a pin. In the new book I have left a clean page with the number at the top, which looks either hopeful or foolish."],["Cats of the Corn Exchange","Statute Twenty requires the borough to maintain not fewer than three cats at the Corn Exchange, their wages to be paid in fish each Friday. I, Hesper Quill, Clerk, copying it into the new Statute Book, find it faithfully observed for a hundred and sixty years.\n\nIt followed the Mouse Autumn, when the grain merchants of the Lowlands threatened to take their trade elsewhere. One merchant opened a sample sack before a buyer and three mice climbed out, in no hurry. The council acted within the week, which for the council is a gallop.\n\nThe present officers are Dimity, Burdock and the Chancellor. Their names stand in the ledger between the lamplighter and the man who winds the clock. I took Friday's herring down myself. The Chancellor accepted it without thanks, as is the way of those long in office."],["Washing and the High Street","No person shall hang washing across the High Street on a market day. So runs Statute Twenty-Three, and in copying it I, the Borough Clerk of Emberwick, went looking for the reason, as the council has asked me to do for all forty-one.\n\nThe reason was a bedsheet. The households on either side of the High Street once shared lines strung between their upper windows. Then, one gusty market morning, a double sheet belonging to a Mrs. Arkle came loose and descended upon a string of wool-merchants' ponies. The ponies concluded that the sky had fallen. The damage was mostly to cheeses.\n\nWhat I admire is the wording. The council did not forbid the lines, only the market day. Six days in seven the street still flies its shirts like flags. A law may be exactly as large as the trouble, and no larger."],["The Ferryman's Oath","Each New Year the ferryman of the Ember comes to my office, lays his hand on the Statute Book, and swears not to charge more in rain than in shine. That is Statute Twenty-Eight. This year he swore on the old book, while the fresh copy I am making lay drying beside it.\n\nThe law remembers a ferryman called Dunstan Gaule, who kept two prices and a sharp eye on the clouds. In a downpour the fare doubled. In a thunderstorm it was whatever you had. The council took the view that weather is nobody's merchandise.\n\nThe present ferryman, Jory, asked what I, the Clerk, was frowning at. I showed him the blank page for Statute Seventeen and the old index. \"See the mace,\" he read. \"Well, have you tried looking in it, and not at it?\" I told him a mace is not a cupboard."],["The Lamp in the Window","Statute Thirty-Three concerns the library, and so I, Hesper Quill, Borough Clerk, copied it there, at the long table, to be among its beneficiaries. It orders that a lamp be kept burning in the library window until the last reader has gone home.\n\nThe cause was a schoolmaster, Mr. Evander Loom, who sat down one winter afternoon with a history of the Northern Fells and looked up to find the doors locked, the stove out and the hour past midnight. He spent the night wrapped in the reading-room curtain and finished the book. He bore no grudge, but asked the council for some sign that somebody knew he was there.\n\nSo the lamp is less a light than a promise. While it burns, you are not forgotten. The night shelver trims it still. I finished my page by it, and she waited, and did not hurry me."],["Telling the Bees","The last statute, the Forty-First, directs that the borough's bees be told the name of each new mayor. I, Hesper Quill, Clerk, have now copied it, and with it every law of Emberwick except the Seventeenth, whose page is blank and whose index entry says only, \"See the mace.\"\n\nA mayor was once installed without the almshouse hives being informed, and that summer they swarmed to the next parish. The council thought it cheaper to tell them than to find out.\n\nToday Mr. Corriby brought the mace to my office for its mayor-making polish. He worked at the head with a cloth, turning as he rubbed, and the head turned with him. It came off in his hand like the lid of a jar. We both looked into the hollow. There was no loose pin. There was a small roll of paper, tied with thread."],["Statute Seventeen","The paper that rattled inside the borough mace for generations is the lost Statute Seventeen. I, Hesper Quill, Clerk of Emberwick, have copied it onto its page: \"Once in each generation the Clerk shall read every statute aloud to a child under ten, and shall strike out whichever one the child cannot be made to understand.\"\n\nSo I read all forty-one to Nell Padbury, the baker's daughter, who is eight. She approved the goose hour, the lanterns in the fog and the cats' wages. She stuck fast on Statute Thirty, which forbids any person to be \"unreasonably prominent\" at the autumn fair. I explained it three ways. I could not explain it to myself.\n\nIt is struck out. The new book holds forty statutes, each understood by at least one citizen. I have put the paper back inside the mace. I have grown fond of the rattle."]],[["The Goose in the Dock","The Petty Sessions of Emberwick sit in the Moot Room above the market hall, and there, one Tuesday in October, Magistrate Dorcas Thrale heard the case of Larkin against a goose.\n\nThe goose, Admiral, owned by Mrs. Hobb, was accused of entering Mr. Larkin's garden and eating most of a marrow meant for the Harvest Fair. Mr. Larkin produced the stalk. Mrs. Hobb produced the goose, which was a mistake. Admiral left his basket, crossed the floor and struck the witness box such a blow that one of its feet snapped off. The usher, Mr. Fenn, restored order and, during the adjournment, the box.\n\nMrs. Hobb is to supply Mr. Larkin with goose eggs until the next fair. When the magistrate reached for her gavel to close the sitting, it was not on the bench. She rapped with her knuckles. The gavel has not been seen since."],["The Leaning Gatepost","At the Emberwick Petty Sessions, before Magistrate Thrale, Simeon Dray, carter, was charged with knocking down the north gatepost of the almshouses with a wagon of coal.\n\nDray's defence was that the post had been leaning into the lane for years and had at last leaned into him. The court adjourned to look: the magistrate, the usher, six almswomen and a dog. The fallen post could say nothing for itself, but its fellow on the south side was found to lean at a most suggestive angle. A plumb line was borrowed from the mason and hung beside it.\n\nIt was held that the post had contributed. Dray is to set both upright, the borough supplying mortar. The court's gavel being still missing since the goose's trial, the magistrate closed with a wooden spoon lent by the caretaker. It made a homely sound, and nobody felt very sentenced."],["The Disputed Pie","The Harvest Fair gave the Emberwick bench its sweetest case. Two pies stood side by side on the judging table, the first-prize ticket fell off in a draught, and both Mrs. Quennell and Mr. Abner Stout swore the winner was theirs.\n\nMagistrate Thrale had the pie brought into the Moot Room, with a lump of dough from the baker. Each claimant was asked to crimp a strip of pastry before the court. Mrs. Quennell pinched hers with a thumb and two fingers. Mr. Stout pressed his with a fork. The edge of the prize pie was plainly forked. Mr. Stout wept a little.\n\nMrs. Quennell took it handsomely and asked for his recipe, which was refused. The pie, being evidence, was then eaten by the court, and this reporter can confirm the judges' opinion. The sitting was closed with a darning mushroom, the gavel being still lost."],["Thirteen at Midnight","On the ninth of November the town clock of Emberwick struck thirteen at midnight and woke all Bridge Street. The sexton, Mr. Obadiah Crane, who has charge of the clock, was brought before Magistrate Thrale for a public nuisance.\n\nEleven witnesses had counted thirteen. A twelfth counted fourteen, but admitted to being asleep for the first. Mr. Crane said he had been in bed, and that the clock strikes by itself, which is the whole merit of a clock. The clockmaker, called as an expert, had climbed the tower and found feathers on the striking hammer. An owl had been sitting on it. When the owl left, the hammer fell once more.\n\nMr. Crane was discharged. The owl was held to be beyond the reach of the court. The magistrate, whose gavel is still missing, closed by rapping a brass door knocker on the bench."],["The Ladder of Nine Houses","Eleven years ago Mr. Hale of Emberwick lent his ladder to a neighbour, to clear a gutter. It has since passed through nine households, and at these Petty Sessions he asked Magistrate Thrale for it back. Mrs. Tibb, who has it now, said she had it honestly from the Pococks.\n\nNine witnesses were called. Each had borrowed the ladder \"only for the afternoon\". The witness box bore all nine within the hour, some of them substantial, without a tremor. The magistrate remarked that the usher, Mr. Fenn, had made a fine job of mending it after the goose broke its foot. Mr. Fenn studied the ceiling.\n\nRuling: the ladder has become the town's by custom. It is to hang at the pump, with a slate for names, and Mr. Hale has first turn. The sitting closed with a cobbler's last. The gavel remains at large."],["Half a Ferry Crossing","Jory, ferryman of the Ember, came before the Emberwick bench to claim twopence from Mr. Lucius Pennyfold, a traveller in buttons. The river being low, the ferry had grounded in midstream, and Mr. Pennyfold had waded the rest. He offered a penny: half the fare for half the crossing.\n\nJory answered that he had waded too, with Mr. Pennyfold's sample case on his head, and that not one button was wet. Magistrate Thrale held that the bargain was to be put on the far bank, and that Mr. Pennyfold had been, though damp to the knee. The fare was owed in full. The ferryman, however, must pay for the drying of the stockings, assessed at twopence.\n\nSo the twopence crossed the bench twice and ended where it began, and both men left satisfied. She closed with a pepper mill, the gavel being still lost, and sneezed."],["The Scarecrow's Sunday Coat","Mr. Dunmore's best coat was found on a scarecrow in Gedge's bean field, and Kit Hatch, the farm boy, aged twelve, was charged before Magistrate Thrale at the Emberwick sessions with taking it.\n\nKit said the coat had been handed to him at the Dunmores' back door, for the scarecrow, with a slice of cake. Mr. Dunmore called this an invention. Mrs. Dunmore was called. She looked at her husband from the witness box and said that the coat was twenty years old and green at the seams, that she had asked him since spring, and that the cake was seed cake.\n\nNo theft was found. The coat is to stay on the scarecrow, which it fits. Mr. Dunmore is to be measured for a new one, by order not of the court but of Mrs. Dunmore. The sitting closed with a rolling pin, the gavel being still absent."],["The Swarm in Dispute","In June a swarm of bees left Miss Pargeter's hive in Emberwick and settled in Mr. Odd's pear tree, two gardens away. Mr. Odd hived them. Miss Pargeter wanted them back, and the matter came before Magistrate Thrale.\n\nThe custom of the borough is old: a swarm stays yours while you follow it, ringing a pan, and never lose sight of it. Miss Pargeter had followed, ringing a pan, but owned that she lost sight of the bees while climbing a wall. Mr. Odd said a wall was a wall. The magistrate said that custom asks for reasonable pursuit and does not require a beekeeper to fly.\n\nThe bees go home to Miss Pargeter. Mr. Odd receives the first comb of honey for his trouble. The court's gavel being still missing, the sitting was closed by striking the pan itself, which was heard in the market."],["The Snow Magistrate","In January the beadle of Emberwick marched five children up to the Moot Room, charged with disrespect to the bench. They had built a snow figure outside the market hall, with a coal frown and a wig of sheep's wool, and it was unmistakably Magistrate Thrale.\n\nThe magistrate went down to view the accused. She walked round it twice. The likeness, she said, was fair, though she did not believe she was so wide. A portrait is no offence. The hat, however, was poor, and they were to find a better. They did. The snow magistrate stood until the thaw.\n\nThe sitting closed with a toy mallet lent by the youngest defendant, Posy Hatch, aged five, who asked for a receipt. She wondered aloud if the court would ever see its own gavel again. The usher, Mr. Fenn, said he had looked everywhere a gavel ought to be."],["The Weathercock's Word","Mr. Silas Gurney, a fisherman of the Grey Coast, sued the borough of Emberwick at the Petty Sessions. Before setting out he had consulted the weathercock on the market hall, which promised a fair south-westerly. He was soaked to the skin by ten o'clock and lost a day's catch.\n\nThe borough pleaded that a weathercock is an ornament and promises nothing. A steeplejack, sent up the roof, reported that the bird was rusted solid and had pointed south-west since the spring before last. Magistrate Thrale held that a thing set on a public roof to tell the wind must tell it or come down.\n\nThe borough is to oil the weathercock. Mr. Gurney is awarded a dry coat from the lost-property cupboard. The gavel being still missing, the magistrate closed with a lead fishing weight lent by the plaintiff. The wind has since been reported as changeable."],["The Admiral Returns","A year after his first trial the goose Admiral was found one morning inside the Moot Room of Emberwick, having climbed the stairs alone. He was sitting beneath the witness box, pecking at one of its feet. His owner, Mrs. Hobb, was summoned for letting him stray.\n\nMagistrate Thrale asked what a goose could want with a witness box. Mr. Aske, the carpenter, who was waiting on another matter, knelt to look. He reported that one leg of the box was two inches short and stood upon \"something that is not a wedge\". The usher, Mr. Fenn, went pink.\n\nThe goose was dismissed with a caution. Mr. Aske is to bring his tools to the next sessions and lift the box. The magistrate closed with her knuckles, as on the day the court's gavel first went missing, and looked for some time at her usher."],["The Matter of the Gavel","For a year the Emberwick bench has been called to order with spoons and door knockers, the gavel having vanished the day a goose broke the foot of the witness box. Today Mr. Aske, the carpenter, lifted the box. Under its short leg lay the gavel.\n\nThe usher, Mr. Fenn, came forward unasked. He had wanted something of the right height, only for the afternoon, and had meant to mention it. Since then every witness in the borough had sworn to tell the truth standing on top of it.\n\nMagistrate Thrale tried the matter on the spot. She found Mr. Fenn guilty of good intentions and sentenced him to buns for the court. The box is to have a proper foot. Then she rapped the gavel, which is worn flat on one side and no longer rolls off the bench. She called it improved by its year of service."]],[["To Whoever Sits Here","I have been magistrate of Emberwick for thirty-one years, and at the Midwinter Sessions I shall stop. The council has not yet told me who follows. So I write to a stranger, and shall leave this book in the bench drawer of the Moot Room, under the spare candles.\n\nNobody gave me a companion when I began. I was handed a key, a gavel and a list of fines, and left to learn the rest by doing it wrong in public. You may be spared a little of that.\n\nFirst counsel, then. Come early on your first morning, before the stove is lit, and sit for five minutes on the public's back bench. Look at the magistrate's chair from there. Notice how far away it is, and how high. That is how you will look to them. Remember it when you are up there feeling ordinary."],["On Hearing People Out","Let them finish. I, Dorcas Thrale, have sat on the Emberwick bench long enough to promise you, my successor, that this is the hardest rule and the most useful.\n\nPeople do not bring their trouble out in order. They begin with the grievance they have rehearsed. The real matter comes last, usually after \"and another thing\". A man once stood before me for twenty minutes complaining of his neighbour's cockerel. I had the poultry by-law open and my finger on the place. Then he said, \"And another thing, since my wife died there is nobody to grumble to when it wakes me.\" There is no by-law for that.\n\nI adjourned and spoke to the neighbour, who now takes him a brown egg most mornings. The cockerel crows as before. Had I ruled at the tenth minute I should have been perfectly correct, and no use at all."],["On the Chair","The magistrate's chair you inherit in the Moot Room of Emberwick was made for a Justice Harbottle, who stood six feet four. I am five feet one. For thirty-one years my feet have not touched the floor during sessions.\n\nIt is a piece of furniture. It makes whoever sits in it look certain, and the danger is that it works on the sitter too. On the first morning I felt I deserved its height, I fined a pedlar for having no licence, though plainly he had been robbed of it. My conscience reversed me by supper, and I repaid him from my own purse.\n\nI keep a cushion on the seat, embroidered by my sister with a goose. It is for comfort. It also reminds me, whenever I sit to judge my neighbours, that I am sitting on a goose. I shall leave it for you."],["On Fines and Mending","You will be handed the borough's list of fines on your first day as Emberwick's magistrate. I, Dorcas Thrale, set this beside it. Fine people in a coin they can find. A fine a poor man cannot pay is only a debt, and a debt brings him back before you.\n\nBetter still, where you can, give a sentence that mends the thing broken. A boy once swung on the handle of the town pump until it snapped. His father could not pay the smith. I ordered the boy to carry water each morning to the six households of Pump Court until the handle was mended, and I asked the smith not to hurry.\n\nBy the end he knew every old woman in the court by name, and one of them later left him her clock. I do not promise that every sentence ends in a clock."],["On Being Unsure","You will give rulings you never feel sure of. I, Dorcas Thrale, magistrate of Emberwick, set mine down for you, my successor, honestly.\n\nTwenty years ago, on a night of hard frost, a girl of ten named Tamsin Reed unbolted the borough pound and let out three stray donkeys. They were cold, she told me, and the pound had no roof. By morning they were in the churchyard, having eaten a wreath. The law was plain. I fined her father two shillings and had her apologise to the pound-keeper.\n\nShe did, clearly. Then she looked at me a long moment, not angrily, but like someone working out a sum. I have wondered ever since what answer she got. I paid for a roof on the pound that spring and told nobody. Whether that was justice or only a conscience with a little money, I leave to you."],["On the Kettle","In the retiring room behind the bench at Emberwick there is a kettle. I, your predecessor, commend it to you as the most useful instrument of justice in the borough, the gavel not excepted.\n\nWhen both parties have said the same thing three times, adjourn for ten minutes. People cannot keep up a fury while waiting for water to boil. The brothers Abel and Enoch Farr once came before me over their late mother's dresser, each ready to see it sawn in half sooner than yield. I put the kettle on and left the door ajar. Before it sang I heard Enoch say the thing would never go up his stairs anyway.\n\nAbel took the dresser and Enoch took the plates that stood on it. Buy your own tea, by the way. The borough's allowance is poor stuff, and a ruling made on weak tea shows it."],["On Witnesses","Three sorts of witness want care, and in thirty-one years on the Emberwick bench I have met them all. Children tell the truth, but not always about the question you asked; ask again, differently. The very old remember the day wonderfully and the year not at all.\n\nBeware most the witness who is sure of the hour. Ask instead what they were doing. \"I had just taken the bread out\" is worth more than \"precisely ten past four\".\n\nA man once swore before me that he saw a cart at nine exactly, by the market clock. So he had. The market clock had stood at nine for a fortnight, waiting for the clockmaker. He was not lying. He had looked at a clock. Most false evidence is of that kind, successor: decent folk trusting stopped clocks. Be gentle with it, and send someone to check the clock."],["On the Statute Book","The Borough Clerk of Emberwick keeps the Statute Book, some forty by-laws. Read it through once, successor, then ask the Clerk how each one came about. Every statute was written by tired people one evening, to stop one particular nuisance happening twice.\n\nThere is a by-law against whistling near the weir after dusk. It dates from when the lock-keeper signalled the barges by whistle. A homesick drover from the Northern Fells was brought before me for whistling there to his dog. The fine is a shilling. The reason for the fine had been dead forty years.\n\nI fined him the shilling, as I must. Then I paid him a shilling to carry a parcel to the bridge. You will be told this is not how the law works. In a town this size it is how it works best: the letter kept, and the purpose kept in mind."],["On Tempers","Anger will come into your court at Emberwick, as it came into mine. Do not raise your voice against it. Lower it. People must grow quiet to hear what is being said about them. And never rule while anyone is standing who ought to sit.\n\nTwo fishwives of the Grey Coast once came before me over a spilled barrow of mackerel, each with a row of cousins in the public seats. The Moot Room sounded like a gull colony. I said, very softly, that I would hear whoever sat down first. Then I had the usher open a window. The smell of roasting chestnuts came up from the market, and somebody's stomach spoke. They agreed to share the loss before noon.\n\nAnger is mostly weather, successor. Wait, and it changes. So does your own. On a morning when you are cross, hear the small cases first."],["On the Third Time","The same faces return. Old Bartle Finch stood before me, I think, fourteen times in my years as magistrate of Emberwick, always for borrowing a boat that was not his to fish the Ember by night. He always brought it back by morning, clean, with a trout left in the bottom by way of rent.\n\nMercy is easy the first time. By the third, successor, you must ask whether you are being merciful or merely tired. Here is my test. Mercy costs you some thought. Laziness costs nothing.\n\nSo at last I thought, and asked him why he owned no boat. He had nowhere to keep one. I spoke to the ferryman, who now lets him a mooring for a trout a month. Bartle has not been before me since. I miss him. That too you will find: some of them you will miss."],["On Clearing the Drawer","The Midwinter Sessions are next week, my last, and I, Dorcas Thrale, have been emptying the bench drawer of thirty-one years. Nine pencils. A marble taken in evidence and never claimed. The receipt for a roof on the borough pound. I leave you the goose cushion, the kettle and this book.\n\nThe council meets tomorrow to name you. The market says an advocate from the Lowlands circuit. I find I mind more than I expected. The quarrels of Emberwick are a kind of garden, and I should like to know who is to have the watering of it.\n\nOne thing more stays in the drawer: a slip of paper reading \"Tamsin Reed. Donkeys, frost, two shillings. Not sure.\" She was ten, and I fined her for a kindness. Every magistrate should leave the next one honest doubt. It keeps the chair from growing any taller."],["The Name on the Door","They have painted my successor's name on the Moot Room door, and I know it. Tamsin Reed. The council chose the advocate from the Lowlands circuit, and she is the girl who let three cold donkeys out of the Emberwick pound twenty years ago, whom I fined two shillings and never forgot.\n\nYou called on me yesterday, so I may write \"you\" and mean somebody. You said that in court that day you were working out how a magistrate could plainly agree with a child and fine her all the same. It took you twenty years and many books, you said, and you have the answer now, mostly. You did not say what it is. Quite right. I had my turn.\n\nSo this companion is handed across a tea table and not left in a drawer. Last counsel: the chair is too high. I have left the cushion."]],[["The Rhubarb Line","I, Septimus Pim, clerk to the Emberwick Petty Sessions, open the record of Tull against Sorrel, which began in April with a fence.\n\nMr. Ambrose Tull, retired barge-master, of Barge Cottage, Hollow Lane, had pulled down the rotten palings between his garden and that of Miss Winifred Sorrel, retired schoolmistress, of Quince Cottage, and put up a fence six feet high. Miss Sorrel said it stood nine inches on her side. In evidence she offered her rhubarb, which now grew, undeniably, in Mr. Tull's garden.\n\nMr. Tull said he had built where the old fence stood. Miss Sorrel said the old fence had wandered. Nobody living, the magistrate found, knew where the line ran. The fence stays until a survey can be had. Mr. Tull is to hand the rhubarb back over it, stalk by stalk. They left by separate doors. I have started a large file."],["The Hen Called Duchess","Second hearing, in June, of Tull against Sorrel, neighbours of Hollow Lane, Emberwick, recorded by me, Septimus Pim, clerk of the court. The complaint was not the fence between them, but what went under it.\n\nMr. Tull's hen, Duchess, has been squeezing beneath the new fence to lay in Miss Sorrel's woodpile. Miss Sorrel has been eating the eggs. Mr. Tull claimed eleven eggs or their value. Miss Sorrel replied that an egg laid on her land, by a trespasser, is rent. She added that no hen could get under the old fence, and whose fence was the new one?\n\nThe magistrate held that the hen is Tull's, the woodpile Sorrel's, and the egg a joint production. Eggs are to be shared alternately, and Mr. Tull is to stop the gap. He has done so, with a brick. Duchess has since been seen going over the top."],["Sunflowers of Unusual Size","Third hearing, in August, between Mr. Ambrose Tull and Miss Winifred Sorrel over the fence dividing their gardens in Hollow Lane, Emberwick. I, Septimus Pim, clerk, record it.\n\nMiss Sorrel had planted seven sunflowers along her side. They now stood twelve feet high and had Mr. Tull's runner beans in shade from two o'clock. A statute limits fences to six feet. Mr. Tull called the sunflowers a fence by other means. Miss Sorrel, formerly a schoolmistress, said a sunflower is a flower, and produced a labelled diagram.\n\nThe magistrate held that a sunflower is not a fence and shade is not trespass. She then observed, having walked out to look, that all seven flowers had turned their faces over the fence towards Mr. Tull, so that he had the best of them and Miss Sorrel the backs. No order was made. Miss Sorrel went home thoughtful."],["The Quince Jelly Question","In October the quarrel of Hollow Lane came a fourth time before the Emberwick bench, and I, Septimus Pim, clerk, took it down. The fence between Mr. Tull and Miss Sorrel stood as before. This time the trouble came over it.\n\nMiss Sorrel's quince tree overhangs Mr. Tull's garden, and its windfalls land there. He had made eleven jars of jelly from them. Miss Sorrel claimed the jelly. Mr. Tull said fruit that falls on his ground is his, and the sugar was certainly his.\n\nThe magistrate tasted the exhibit, twice. She ruled that by old custom fallen fruit belongs where it lands. No law compels a gift, though manners suggest a jar. Mr. Tull set a jar on my table and said the court might do as it liked. The court handed it to Miss Sorrel. She said it wanted more lemon, and took it home."],["Two Coats of Paint","Fifth hearing of Tull against Sorrel, neighbours in Hollow Lane, Emberwick; Septimus Pim, clerk, recording. In a dry week of November Mr. Tull painted his side of the fence barge green. The paint ran through the gaps and striped Miss Sorrel's side. She painted her side white. The white ran through and striped his. The fence now resembled a humbug, and each sued the other for spoiling it.\n\nThe magistrate ruled that each face of a fence belongs to the eye obliged to look at it. Each may paint their own side their own colour. While one paints, the other must stand behind and hold a board across the gaps.\n\nIt took a whole Saturday, I am told, and words passed between the boards, not all hostile. Miss Sorrel informed Mr. Tull, through a knothole, that he had missed a bit. He went back and did it."],["The Surveyor's Chain","In December the long case of Tull against Sorrel, concerning the fence between their gardens in Hollow Lane, at last had its survey. I, Septimus Pim, clerk of the Emberwick court, attended with the file, which now needs string.\n\nMr. Linus Rood, borough surveyor, laid out his chain. Mr. Tull's deed measures forty feet from the lane. Miss Sorrel's measures thirty feet from the brook. But the brook has moved since the deeds were written. By his deed the fence stands correctly. By hers it stands nine inches wrong. Both deeds speak of a mere-stone, a boundary stone between the plots, and nobody can find it. Mr. Rood probed with a rod and found a drain and a horseshoe.\n\nThe magistrate could not choose between two honest deeds and one dishonest brook. The matter stands over until the stone is found. Each told me this proved their case."],["Whose Snow","January brought the seventh hearing between Mr. Tull of Barge Cottage and Miss Sorrel of Quince Cottage, Emberwick, whose gardens share a fence and little else. I, Septimus Pim, clerk, record it. Snow had slid from Mr. Tull's shed roof onto Miss Sorrel's path. She shovelled it back over the fence. He returned it, with interest. By my reckoning the same snow crossed six times.\n\nThe magistrate ruled that snow is nobody's property until it lies in the lane, where it becomes the borough's. Both were ordered to shovel it out to the lane together, under my eye.\n\nI attended in two scarves. The work took twenty minutes. Afterwards the parties stood either side of the fence disputing the right way to hold a shovel, for forty minutes more, neither going indoors. I record this without comment, except that I went home before they did."],["Bonfires and Bed Linen","Eighth hearing, in March, of Tull against Sorrel, the neighbours of Hollow Lane, Emberwick, and their fence; taken down by me, Septimus Pim, clerk. Mr. Tull had burned his prunings on the morning Miss Sorrel hung out her sheets. She washed them again. A pillowcase then blew over the fence and settled in his ashes. He complained that her linen had trespassed. She answered that his smoke had trespassed first.\n\nThe magistrate sent for an almanac and drew up a calendar. Mondays are for washing. Thursdays are for bonfires. A copy was to be pinned to each side of the fence.\n\nMr. Tull objected that he could not see her copy to know whether it matched his. I was made to certify both. It is the first document the two have ever held in common, and I wrote it out rather well."],["The Trowel in Common","In May the neighbours of Hollow Lane, Mr. Tull and Miss Sorrel, came a ninth time before the Emberwick bench. I, Septimus Pim, clerk, expected the fence. It was a trowel with a red handle, which each said the other had kept.\n\nUnder questioning it came out that for eleven years, before Mr. Tull built his six-foot fence, the two had passed tools, seedlings and opinions across the old low palings daily. Nobody knew whose the trowel was, because it had lived on the fence post. The magistrate asked what the quarrel was truly about. Nobody spoke. At last Miss Sorrel said the old fence came up to her elbow, and that she is five feet two. Mr. Tull looked at his boots.\n\nThe trowel is to hang on the fence, in a hole cut so that both may reach it. I begin to understand my file."],["The Leaning Fence","Tenth hearing, in August, of Tull against Sorrel; Septimus Pim, clerk to the Emberwick sessions, recording. The fence between the two gardens in Hollow Lane now leans towards Miss Sorrel under the weight of Mr. Tull's runner beans. She has propped it with clothes poles. He complained that her props push it back upon his beans.\n\nThe magistrate remarked that this fence has appeared before her more often than any person in the borough. She added that a fence held up by two people, each wishing it to fall the other way, is likely to stand for ever. Each is to prop their own side.\n\nA hole was cut in the fence last spring so that both might reach a shared trowel. A good deal now passes through it: runner beans one way, and the other way a slice of something with quince in it."],["The Night of the Gale","In October a gale laid the fence of Hollow Lane flat across both gardens, impartially. Mr. Tull and Miss Sorrel each came to the Emberwick bench to blame the other for not holding it up, and I, Septimus Pim, clerk, opened the file for the eleventh time.\n\nThe evidence overtook them. Digging out the snapped post, Mr. Tull's spade had rung on stone: the lost boundary stone, a foot down, a cross cut in its top. Mr. Rood, the surveyor, measured from it. The true boundary lies four and a half inches from where the fence stood, halfway between the line Mr. Tull swore to and the line Miss Sorrel swore to. Each had been half right for eighteen months.\n\nThe magistrate reserved her ruling and advised both to consider what fence they wanted on that line. It was raining. For once they left by the same door."],["Settled Over the Fence","At the November sessions in Emberwick the case of Tull against Sorrel was called three times and nobody answered. The magistrate sent me, Septimus Pim, clerk, to Hollow Lane to see why.\n\nI found both parties in the gap where the old six-foot fence blew down. They were setting posts along the line of the boundary stone, for a fence no higher than an elbow, with a gate in the middle. Mr. Tull was digging. Miss Sorrel held the post plumb and told him it was not. He said they had no time for court, with frost coming. She said the rhubarb had been moved back, and that he had moved it.\n\nA jar of quince jelly stood on the stone. I have written across the file: settled out of court, over the fence. The magistrate says she will miss them. Duchess the hen uses the gate."]],[["The Rope Hung Up","For forty years I, Ansel Corrow, rang the tenor bell of Emberwick, and this autumn I hung up the rope. My ears are going. The doctor says, kindly, that the world will grow quieter for me each year, and that there is nothing to be done but get used to it.\n\nI find I do not know what quiet is. I have spent my life making the opposite. A ringer thinks of quiet as the dull stretch between peals, as a baker thinks of an empty oven.\n\nSo I mean to study it, as one studies a new neighbour who is plainly going to stay. I shall go where quiet is kept, in houses and on the marsh and up the tower, and write down what I find. I confess I begin a little afraid of it. That seems worth writing down too."],["The House After Guests","My sister and her four grandchildren stayed a week and left this morning on the Lowlands coach. I, Ansel Corrow, who am making a study of quiet in my deaf old age, came back from the coach stop to my first specimen.\n\nThe house was quiet, but not as it had been quiet before they came. The chairs were still pulled out from the table. There was a wooden horse on the stairs and a damp towel on the banister. I stood in the hall and could very nearly hear them, the way you go on feeling a hat after you have taken it off.\n\nSo here is a first finding. Quiet is not empty. It keeps the shape of whatever has just left it, as a bed keeps the shape of the sleeper. I did not push the chairs in until supper."],["Before Snow","I walked out on the Fells road this afternoon under a sky the colour of pewter. I am Ansel Corrow, once bell-ringer of Emberwick, now a student of quiet, since quiet is coming for my ears whether I study it or not.\n\nAbout three o'clock everything stopped. The rooks went down into the elms and said nothing. The wind dropped. A dog at the toll house, which barks at everybody, looked at me and did not bark. Even with good ears I think I should have heard nothing at all.\n\nIt did not feel like nothing. It felt like the moment when a ringer stands with the rope in both hands, before the first pull. Then the snow began, large flakes, in no hurry. Some quiet, I learned, is a kind of waiting, and the waiting is part of the thing waited for."],["The Kettle Holds Its Breath","Here is a thing I never noticed in seventy years of making tea. A kettle on the fire begins by ticking. Then it mutters, then it grumbles and roars. And then, just before it boils, it goes quiet.\n\nI, Ansel Corrow, retired bell-ringer of Emberwick, noticed it only this winter, because my hearing is failing and I have taken to listening hard to the little that is left. I thought the fire had gone out. I bent down to look, and the lid began to dance.\n\nSo the kettle is quietest when it is nearest to its work. I have known ringers like that, and one cat. I had thought of quiet as what happens when nothing does. The kettle says otherwise. Some quiet is everything happening at once, under a lid. I drank the tea with respect."],["The Wrong Sort","I must set down, to be fair, that not all quiet is good. I, Ansel Corrow, have had three weeks of the wrong sort.\n\nHob Tansley and I rang side by side in the Emberwick tower for thirty years. At the mayor's daughter's wedding somebody came in a stroke late and spoiled the peal. I said it was Hob. Hob said it was me. Since then we have passed in the market like two carts in a narrow lane, each looking hard at the opposite hedge.\n\nIt is quiet between us, certainly. But it is the loudest quiet I ever sat in. I hear it through walls. The quiet before a snowfall is a breath drawn in. This is a breath held, and a held breath does nobody good for long. I note it here, and do not yet know what to do about it."],["Forty People Reading","On a wet Thursday I sat for two hours in the reading room of Emberwick Library. I had no book I wanted. I, Ansel Corrow, former bell-ringer, went to look at the quiet there, as part of my study of it.\n\nThere were perhaps forty of us. A schoolboy with an atlas. Two women sharing a book of gardens. The old corn merchant asleep behind a newspaper, which rose and fell. Nobody had ordered silence. Each person was simply keeping quiet for the sake of the others, and the others for them.\n\nI had always thought quiet was something that happens to you. Here it was being made, by forty people at once, like a barn roof going up. When a page turned you could tell how carefully. I went home feeling I had been in good company, though nobody had said a word to me."],["The Marsh at Dawn","I went down to the marsh before sunrise, expecting the deepest quiet in the district. I am Ansel Corrow, a deaf old bell-ringer of Emberwick writing a study of quiet, and I thought the marsh would be my best chapter.\n\nIt was a racket. The reeds hissed. Water clucked under the boardwalk. Something boomed far off, a bittern I suppose, and every duck on the Ember had an opinion. Even my poor ears caught most of it. I stood there feeling cheated.\n\nThen I understood that the quiet thing on the marsh was me. I had stopped walking, stopped muttering, stopped thinking about breakfast. All that noise had been there every morning of my life, and I had been too loud inside to hear it. Quiet is not always found in a place. Sometimes it is a thing the listener does."],["The Stopped Clock","My kitchen clock stopped on Tuesday night, and on Wednesday morning the room felt as if a person had left it. I, Ansel Corrow of Emberwick, had not heard that tick for years. I only heard it stop.\n\nThe sounds we live with become a floor we walk on without looking. Take the floor away and for a moment there is a drop.\n\nI left the clock unwound for a day, on purpose, to learn what was underneath. There was a good deal. The beams of the house settling. A mouse, or the idea of a mouse. My own breath, which I am glad to report continues. I wound the clock at dusk and had to sit closer than last winter to catch it. My hearing goes on going. But I know now that under every sound there is another, smaller one."],["Two Chairs","Hob Tansley came to my door on Sunday with a jar of his pickled walnuts and no speech prepared. We had not spoken for a month, over a spoiled peal at a wedding. I, Ansel Corrow, had a speech prepared, and forgot it.\n\nHe sat in the other chair. I put a log on. Neither of us mentioned the wedding. Neither of us mentioned anything. We sat for an hour, two old ringers with our boots to the fire, and the only remark was Hob's, that the walnuts wanted another month.\n\nIt was the same silence we had kept in Emberwick market all those weeks, with not a word different. Yet that one was a wall and this one was a roof. I cannot find the difference in the quiet itself. It must lie in whether the two people are facing the same way."],["The Bell at Rest","I climbed the tower today, sixty-one steps, slower than I used. I, Ansel Corrow, rang the tenor bell of Emberwick for forty years and never once came up simply to look at her.\n\nShe hangs mouth down in the dusk of the bell chamber, as wide as a cart wheel, perfectly still. It struck me that a bell spends nearly her whole life like this. An hour's ringing on a Sunday, a wedding, a funeral, the New Year. All the rest is hanging quiet in the dark.\n\nAnd that is what makes her worth hearing. A bell that rang all day would be a nuisance, and by the second week nobody would notice it. Her quiet is not time wasted between strokes. It is what the strokes are made out of. I laid my hand flat on her side. Cold, and faintly humming, from the wind."],["What I No Longer Hear","An honest account, from Ansel Corrow, once bell-ringer of Emberwick, a year into going deaf. I have lost the wren in the hedge. I have lost the treble bell entirely. I can no longer hear rain unless it means it.\n\nWhat is left: the tenor bell, which I feel in my chest. Hob's laugh. The kettle's rumble, though not its singing. A door shut by someone in a temper. The clock, if I sit by it.\n\nA year ago I should have written the first list and wept over it. I find I am more interested in the second. Each thing on it has become particular. I used to hear everything and attend to almost none of it. Now I hear little and miss none of that little. I do not call it a fair exchange. It is not nothing."],["Room","I have studied quiet for a year, in my house, on the marsh, in the library and up the bell tower, and I, Ansel Corrow, will say plainly what I think it is. Quiet is room.\n\nIt is the room the guests leave behind them, and the room the snow falls into. It is the room under the kettle's lid, and between two old friends, and around a bell. Nothing in it is missing. Something is being made space for.\n\nSo I am no longer afraid of what is coming to my ears. I am being given more room than most, and had better furnish it with attention. This evening I sat with my hand on the kettle to feel it boil. Across Emberwick the young ringers started up. I got only the tenor, one deep note in eight. It was enough to tell the tune by."]],[["The Oak Log","On the first Thursday of the frost, Osric Thane, retired schoolmaster of Emberwick, carried an oak log the size of a small pig into Bryony Hask's kitchen. They had agreed to share one fire a week through the winter, wood being dear.\n\n\"That is too good for an ordinary night,\" said Bryony, who is a weaver and knows the worth of things. \"We will burn it the evening we two agree about something. Fully agree.\" Osric said that might be never. She said then it would keep.\n\nThey lit sticks instead. \"Is this the same fire we had last year?\" Osric asked. \"Different wood, different flames. A new fire.\" Bryony said it was the same hearth and the same two fools in front of it. A fire is something that happens, like supper, and you may have supper every night. He did not agree. The log stayed in its corner."],["Bryony's Loom","Thursday again, and Osric Thane sat by Bryony Hask's fire in Emberwick, watching her mend a treadle on her loom.\n\n\"Your mother's loom,\" he said. \"Was,\" said Bryony. \"Or is. I've replaced the treadles twice, the beam once and every heddle.\" Osric, who taught school for forty years and likes a thing settled, said it was plainly a different loom, then. There was not a splinter of her mother's left in it.\n\nBryony tied off her cord. \"There has been cloth on this loom every day since before I was born. My mother started a length and I finished it. The loom is not the wood. The loom is the going on.\" Osric said nobody could sit at a going on. She told him to try sitting at a pile of old treadles. The oak log they are saving for the night they agree stayed in its corner."],["A Promise to a Dog","Osric Thane came late to Bryony Hask's fire, with mud to the knee. It was their Thursday evening in Emberwick, and he had walked first to the weir and back, in the dark, with his old dog Bracken.\n\n\"I told him this morning we would go,\" he said. \"Then it rained all day.\" Bryony, turning the heel of a stocking, asked whether the dog would have known. \"Not in the least. He forgets a promise before I finish making it.\" \"Then who were you keeping it for?\"\n\nOsric thought about that through one whole log. A promise has two ends, he said at last, and he was holding one of them whether or not anybody held the other. Bryony said that was only pride with wet boots. A promise nobody remembers is a plan, and plans may change with the weather. Bracken, asleep on the hearth, gave no ruling."],["The Locked Gate","\"The orchard gate on Hollow Lane was locked today,\" said Bryony Hask, shaking snow off her shawl at Osric Thane's hearth. It was his turn to keep their Thursday fire. \"Chain and padlock. No notice.\"\n\nOsric asked what she had done. \"Climbed it. I have walked through that orchard for thirty years. If someone wants me out they can say so. A lock doesn't say no. It doesn't say anything.\"\n\nOsric said a lock says no as clearly as iron can. One does not need it in writing. Bryony replied that it might be keeping pigs in, and not weavers out. A lock is only a fact, and it is we who make it a message. He said that sounded like something a person says from the far side of a gate. In Emberwick they are saving an oak log for the night they agree. It was not tonight."],["The Salt in the Soup","Bryony Hask made leek soup for the Thursday fire, and Osric Thane, her guest and old friend, ate two bowls and said it was very good.\n\n\"It was oversalted,\" said Bryony. \"I knocked the jar. You drank three cups of water.\" Osric admitted it. He had only meant to be kind. A small untruth, between friends, was a sort of cushion.\n\n\"I still have salty soup,\" said Bryony. \"Now I also have a friend whose praise I can't spend, since I don't know which coin is good. Tell me it's salty, and then when you say a thing is fine I shall know it.\" Osric said that by that rule he must also tell her about the blue shawl. She said he might try. He looked at the shawl, and at the weaver who made it, and said the soup was salty. She said that would do for one evening."],["Where the Cold Comes In","Midwinter, and the wind off the Northern Fells found every crack in Osric Thane's cottage. He and Bryony Hask sat almost in the hearth for their Thursday fire. She put her hand to the bottom of the door.\n\n\"The cold is coming in here,\" she said. Osric, a schoolmaster to the bone, said that nothing was coming in. Cold is not a thing. There is only warmth, and the lack of it. What she felt was their heat going out.\n\n\"I have felt it come in,\" said Bryony. \"It came across the floor and took hold of my ankle like a hand. Don't tell me that was a lack.\" Osric said dark was the same: no one pours dark into a room. Bryony said anyone who lay awake at three in the morning knew better. They stuffed the gap with a rolled rug, each for a different reason."],["The Forgotten Name","Osric Thane was low at Thursday's fire. A grown man had greeted him in Emberwick market that morning, as an old pupil, and Osric could not find his name. \"Forty years of children,\" he said to Bryony Hask. \"I have forgotten half of them. If I have lost half my life, am I the man who lived it?\"\n\nBryony counted stitches before answering. \"That was Ned Gurney's boy, Silas. You kept him in for drawing boats in his copybook, and then you pinned the best boat on the wall.\"\n\n\"I don't remember it.\" \"No. He does. I do, now.\" A person is not kept all in one head, she said, like money in one pocket. Bits of Osric were lodged all over the town, in people he had forgotten. He said that was a comfort but not an answer. She said he might have the comfort meanwhile."],["Enough Wood","By February Osric Thane had counted his woodpile. \"Sixty-three logs,\" he told Bryony Hask, at their Thursday fire in his cottage. \"Four a night until the end of March, with some to spare. That is enough.\"\n\nBryony put on a fifth. He winced. \"Enough is not a number,\" she said. \"Enough is when I stop being cold. Some nights that's two logs and some nights seven.\" Osric said that people who burn by feeling end in April sitting in their coats. She said that people who burn by counting sit in their coats all winter and have logs left over for the summer.\n\nHe said a careful man is never caught short. She said he was never caught warm either. Then they both looked at the corner, where the great oak log waits for the night they agree on something. Neither of them had ever counted that one in."],["The Last Slice","There was one slice of ginger cake left on the plate at Bryony Hask's fire, and two people. \"You cut and I choose,\" said Osric Thane. \"That is how fairness is taught in every schoolroom in Emberwick. Two equal halves.\"\n\nBryony held the knife and did not cut. \"You walked to the Grey Coast road and back today. I sat at a loom. Is equal fair?\" Osric said equal was the only fair that could be measured. Once you begin weighing who is hungrier and who deserves, there is no end to it, and the cake goes stale.\n\n\"It ends when both of us think it's right,\" said Bryony. She cut the slice two-thirds and one-third and pushed the larger at him. He said he could not accept it. She said then he must explain why his fairness left him unhappy with more cake. He ate it, frowning."],["The Riveted Cup","Osric Thane dropped Bryony Hask's blue cup on her hearthstone, at their Thursday fire, and it broke in two. He was more upset than she was. The next week he brought it back from the tinker in Emberwick market, joined with three brass rivets.\n\n\"It will never be what it was,\" he said. \"A mended thing is a spoiled thing made the best of.\" Bryony turned it in the firelight. She said she liked it better. Before, it was one of six. Now it was the cup Osric broke the winter they shared a fire, and she would know it in the dark.\n\n\"It leaks a little,\" he said. \"So do you,\" said Bryony, \"and nobody thinks of throwing you out.\" He held that whole is still better than mended, if one is honest. She held that whole is only what a thing is before anything has happened to it."],["What Talking Is For","\"Eleven weeks,\" said Osric Thane, by Bryony Hask's fire, \"and we have not agreed once. Not on fires, looms, dogs, gates, soup, cold, cake or cups.\" He nodded at the corner of her Emberwick kitchen, where the oak log they had saved for the night of agreement sat with the cat asleep on it. \"Talk that settles nothing is a road that goes nowhere.\"\n\n\"I walk to the weir most mornings,\" said Bryony. \"I don't go there to be at the weir. There's nothing at the weir. I go for the going.\"\n\nOsric opened his mouth to say she was right, and saw the log. If he agreed, they must burn it, and the cat would lose her seat, and the winter would be over. He shut his mouth. Bryony saw him do it, and laughed, and said nothing either."],["The Log Burns","The last Thursday of the winter was mild. There were crocuses by Bryony Hask's gate in Emberwick and the door stood open. She and Osric Thane hardly needed a fire, but they lit a small one from habit and sat looking at the oak log in the corner, saved since the first frost for the evening they should fully agree.\n\n\"I have been thinking,\" said Osric, \"that the best of these evenings were the ones when we got nowhere.\" Bryony said she had thought so since November. \"Then we agree,\" said Osric, and looked alarmed.\n\nThey lifted the log on together. It caught slowly and burned until past midnight, and they sat by it with nothing left to settle. At the door he said he would bring another oak log at the first frost. Bryony said he might, and that she expected to find something wrong with it."]],[["Noon: The Charge","My brother-in-law Edwin said on Sunday, meaning no harm, that since I gave up the post office my afternoons must be all alike. I, Lettice Marrow, have been cross about it for two days. Today I mean to be useful about it instead.\n\nI shall examine one afternoon. This one, the tenth of October, in Emberwick, with nothing arranged. I shall watch it from the stroke of noon until I light the lamp, and write down what is in it, a notebook to each half hour if need be. If it is empty, Edwin is right and I shall say so.\n\nThe clock has just struck twelve. Before I begin I have written what I expect: dinner, a letter, a walk to the post, tea. Four things. It looks a thin list, I admit. The cat is on the windowsill and the sun has reached the second flagstone. I begin."],["Bread and Cheese","Half past twelve on the tenth of October, the afternoon I, Lettice Marrow of Emberwick, have set myself to examine. Dinner is bread, cheese and an apple, as it is most days. My brother-in-law would say: there, all alike.\n\nBut I walked to the baker's this morning and I am hungry, and the bread tastes better than it did on Sunday when I was not. So I put a question to my plate. Is the pleasure in the bread or in the hunger?\n\nI ate the first slice quickly and the second slowly. The second was better, though cut from the same loaf. So some of it is neither bread nor hunger, but attention. That makes three things on the plate where I expected one. I have kept the apple for later, to see whether waiting improves it."],["One Plate","One o'clock. I, Lettice Marrow, am examining this single afternoon in my kitchen in Emberwick, and it has brought me to the washing-up, which is one plate, one knife and a cup.\n\nI have always called this a chore, by which I meant time that does not count. Today I watched it. The water was warm. The plate, which was my mother's, has a blue rim worn pale where forty years of thumbs have held it. I dried it and it squeaked.\n\nIt took four minutes. I cannot see what was wrong with those four minutes. They were not waiting for something else. If I throw out every hour I have called a chore, I throw out a third of my life with the dishwater. The plate is clean, and I made it so. That is a small thing to have done by one o'clock, but it is done."],["The Unanswered Letter","Half past one, on the afternoon I have promised to examine honestly. Honesty brings me to the mantelpiece, where a letter from my cousin Dorothea in the Lowlands has stood for three weeks, looking at me.\n\nI want to have answered it. I do not want to answer it. I, Lettice Marrow, sorted the letters of Emberwick for thirty years and never understood this about them: the reply grows heavier each day it is not written, though the paper weighs the same.\n\nSo I sat down and wrote only the first line. \"Dear Dorothea, I am ashamed to be so late.\" And there the weight went. It had all been in that one line. The rest ran on by itself, two sides and a postscript. I think most of what I put off is like that. The task is small. The door to it is stiff."],["The Nap Not Examined","Two o'clock. I must report a gap. I, Lettice Marrow, who set out to examine every minute of this October afternoon in Emberwick, sat down in the chair by the window with the sun on my lap, to rest my eyes for a moment.\n\nThe clock now says a quarter past. The cat has moved from the sill to my knee without my leave. I have no account of those minutes whatever.\n\nThe room went on without me. The sun moved a hand's breadth along the floor. I was the only thing in it not attending. I could pretend I was thinking, and nobody would know. But the record must be honest or it is no use. So: a quarter of an hour, unexamined, and I feel the better for it. Perhaps an afternoon needs one room that is kept locked."],["The Third Door","Half past two, and I am walking down Hollow Lane to the post box by the bridge. I, Lettice Marrow, carried the Emberwick post along this lane for thirty years. Today I am examining my afternoon, so I tried examining the lane.\n\nI set myself a test. Without looking, what colour is the third door? Green, I guessed. It is blue, with a brass fish for a knocker. I must have knocked that fish a thousand times.\n\nWhat we know best, it seems, we see least. I knew the lane so well that I had stopped looking, and had been walking through my own idea of it. So I looked. There is a pear tree trained against the fifth house. There is a boot scraper shaped like a hedgehog. The lane was full of things, and all of them had been there on every one of my alike afternoons."],["The Stranger's Question","Three o'clock by the bridge clock, on the afternoon I am examining. A carter stopped me, a stranger, and asked the way to the Grey Coast road. I, Lettice Marrow, told him: over the bridge, left at the smithy, straight on. He thanked me and drove off.\n\nI had gone twenty paces when I doubted. Left at the smithy, or right? I have known that turning all my life, and the moment someone depended on it, it wobbled.\n\nHe was nobody to me. If he went wrong he would lose half an hour and think poorly of an old woman in Emberwick. And yet I turned and ran, at my age, with my hat in my hand, and caught him at the bridge end to say it again. It was left. It had been left all along. I do not know what I owed him, but my legs knew."],["A Bench by the Ember","Half past three. Having run after a carter, I, Lettice Marrow, sat down on the bench below the bridge to get my breath. I am examining one whole afternoon in Emberwick, and here was a stretch with nothing in it. I decided to do nothing, properly, and see what that is like.\n\nIt cannot be done. I sat still, and found I was watching a yellow leaf come down the river. It turned twice, caught on a stone, thought it over, and went on under the arch. Then I was listening to the weir. Then I was wondering who first put a bench here.\n\nSo doing nothing is only doing something small enough to be overlooked. I sat a quarter of an hour, and it was as full as any other quarter this afternoon. I begin to suspect there is no such thing as an empty one."],["The Child at the Gate","Four o'clock. At my own gate I, Lettice Marrow, met Tam from next door, who is six, swinging on it. I told him I was examining my afternoon. He asked when the afternoon stops.\n\nI said at evening. He asked when that was. I said when it gets dark. He pointed out, fairly, that it gets dark slowly, and wanted to know which minute. I had no minute to give him. The sky over Emberwick was plainly still afternoon. In two hours it would plainly be evening, and I should never catch it changing.\n\nTam thought this a poor answer from a grown person. But I have turned it over since. There is no line, and yet the two sides are different. Most things I am sure of are like that: young and old, enough and too much. I gave him the apple I had saved from dinner."],["The Watched Kettle","Half past four, and tea. I, Lettice Marrow, have been watching my afternoon since noon, as an experiment, and now I am watching the kettle, and it will not boil.\n\nEveryone knows this about kettles. I had not thought what it means. The kettle takes the same time whether I look or not. It is the time that changes: watched, it stretches. And I have watched this whole afternoon, which is why it is only half past four and I feel I have lived a week since dinner.\n\nSo perhaps my afternoons seemed alike, and short, because I never looked at them. I let them boil behind my back. I should not care to watch every kettle of my life. But it is something to know that an afternoon is as long as I choose to make it. The lid has begun to rattle. I shall use the good cup."],["The Light Going","Five o'clock. The sun has left the flagstones and climbed the far wall of my kitchen, where it has turned the whitewash the colour of the inside of a shell. I, Lettice Marrow, have examined this afternoon since noon, and now it is going.\n\nI tried to catch the moment when I should need the lamp. I could read my own writing. I could still read it. Then I found I had been holding the page to the window for some time. The moment had gone by without telling me.\n\nI did not expect to mind. But this was the tenth of October, with a blue door in it, and a carter, and a leaf, and it will not come again. Tomorrow will have other things in it. Whatever my brother-in-law Edwin thinks, my afternoons were never alike. I only failed to tell them apart."],["Lamp-Lighting","I have lit the lamp, and by my own rule the afternoon is over. I, Lettice Marrow, set out at noon to examine one ordinary afternoon in Emberwick, because my brother-in-law Edwin said mine were all the same.\n\nI expected four things: dinner, a letter, a walk, tea. I have filled eleven notebooks, and this is the twelfth. There was bread that changed with attention, a plate, a stiff door, a gap, a blue door, a stranger, a leaf, a child's question, a kettle, a wall the colour of a shell.\n\nSo here is my answer, Edwin. My afternoons look alike from outside, as the books on a library shelf look alike with their covers shut. I shall not examine tomorrow's. One need not read every book to know they are full. I am sending you the twelve notebooks by the morning post, tied with string. The label says: \"Tuesday.\""]],[["The Barley-Sugar Jar","I am Ida Fennimore, and I have taught the lower school at Emberwick for nineteen years without once being asked a thing I could not answer, or at least answer firmly. Then Wren Ashby turned nine.\n\nOn Monday I blew out the candle after the early lesson, and Wren put up her hand. \"Where does the flame go?\" I said it goes out. \"Out where?\" I said it simply stops. She asked how a thing can stop without going anywhere, since when she stops she is still standing there.\n\nEleven faces waited. I said we would come back to it, which is what teachers say. After school I wrote the question on a slip, put it in an empty barley-sugar jar, and put the jar at the back of my desk drawer, meaning to look the answer up. A teacher ought to know. I shut the drawer rather quickly."],["Your Blue and Mine","A second slip has gone into the jar in my desk, where I, Ida Fennimore, schoolmistress of Emberwick, keep the questions I cannot answer. Wren Ashby again. She is nine.\n\nWe were painting bluebells. Wren looked at her neighbour Tam's paper and asked: \"When I see blue, and Tam sees blue, how do we know it's the same?\" I said we both call it blue. She said yes, but suppose what Tam sees is what she would call yellow, and he calls it blue because everyone told him to.\n\nWe matched the bluebell to the sky, to my shawl, to the inkwell. Tam and Wren agreed every time, which proved nothing. Nobody can climb in behind another person's eyes to look. All we have is that we point at the same things and say the same word. Wren thought that was not much. I think it may be nearly everything."],["How Long Is Now","This one came during arithmetic, which is when Wren Ashby does her best thinking about other things. I am her teacher, Ida Fennimore, and I keep a jar in my desk at Emberwick school for her questions that I cannot answer. \"Miss, when you say now, how long is it?\"\n\nI said now is this moment. She asked how long a moment is. A minute is plainly too long; its beginning is already then by the time you reach its end. We listened to the school clock. Even a tick has a front and a back.\n\nWren clapped her hands once and said \"now\", and we agreed that by the time we heard it, it was over. So now is too short to hold anything, and yet everything that ever happens to us happens in it. I wrote the slip. The class clapped for some minutes more, to make sure."],["A Heap of Peas","We were counting dried peas, the lower school and I, Ida Fennimore, on a wet morning in Emberwick. I tipped the bag out on the table and said, \"Here is a heap.\" That was my mistake. Wren Ashby, who is nine and asks questions I cannot answer, took one pea away.\n\n\"Still a heap?\" Yes. She took another. Still a heap. She went on, pea by pea, and the class watched as if at a play. At last there were three peas in a row, and nobody would call that a heap. But nobody could say which pea had done it.\n\nNo single pea makes the difference, and yet the difference gets made. I wrote that on a slip for the jar in my desk. Then we put the peas back, one at a time, to see where the heap began. We could not find that either."],["Before the First Day","\"What was the day before the first day?\" asked Wren Ashby, who is nine. This time I, Ida Fennimore, took the barley-sugar jar of unanswered questions out of my desk and carried it, after school, to Emberwick Library.\n\nI sat at the long table until the lamp was lit in the window. The books were interesting, and did not agree. On the day before the first day, three of them said three different things, and the fourth said the question should not be asked.\n\nNext morning I stood before the class and said, \"I do not know. And the books do not know either.\" I had expected to feel smaller. Instead eleven children leaned forward, as they never do for the rivers of the Lowlands. Wren looked at me as if I had finally said something sensible. I put the jar back in the drawer, but not at the back."],["The Cat's Dream","The school cat at Emberwick is called Inkpot, and she sleeps on the windowsill through every lesson I, Ida Fennimore, have ever given. On Thursday her paws began to twitch. Wren Ashby, aged nine, my most difficult pupil in the best sense, put her hand up.\n\n\"Is she dreaming? What about?\" Mice, said the class. Wren said we didn't know that. It might be fish. It might be us. It might not be pictures at all, since a cat knows the world mostly by smell.\n\nWe cannot ask Inkpot. And Wren went one further: we cannot really ask each other. When Tam says he dreamed of falling, we take his word and imagine our own falling. I said that taking each other's word is what a school is for. The slip went into my jar of questions without answers. Inkpot woke, washed, and told us nothing."],["Where the Hole Goes","On Friday the baker sent ring cakes to Emberwick school, the kind with a hole in the middle, and I, Ida Fennimore, should have known better than to hand one to Wren Ashby. She is nine. I keep a jar of her questions that have no answers.\n\nShe looked at me through it. \"When I eat the cake, where does the hole go?\" The class said it gets eaten. Wren said nobody can eat a hole. You can count holes. You can fall in one. But you cannot take one home without bringing the cake.\n\nI said a hole is perhaps a thing that belongs to something else, like a shadow, or a birthday. Wren considered this and ate the cake in a circle, slowly, watching. At the last bite she said, \"It was there, and I didn't see it go.\" Another slip for the jar."],["Why I Am Me","It was a still afternoon at Emberwick school and I, Ida Fennimore, had set the lower class to copying. Wren Ashby, who is nine, stopped with her pen in the air. I know that look. I keep a jar in my desk for what follows it.\n\n\"Miss. Why am I the one who's me?\" There are twelve of us in the room, she said, each looking out of their own eyes, and she is the one looking out of Wren's. Why isn't she looking out of Tam's, and worrying about Tam's lost boot?\n\nI said if she were, she would be Tam, and asking the same thing. She said yes, that was exactly the trouble. I have explained the tides, and long division, and where the Ember rises. I had nothing. I said, \"I have wondered that since I was about your age.\" I had forgotten it until she asked."],["A New Colour","For a week Wren Ashby has been trying to think of a colour nobody has seen. She is nine, and my pupil at Emberwick school, and when I, Ida Fennimore, told the class that people can imagine anything, she took it as a wager.\n\nMonday's colour turned out to be a sort of green. Tuesday's was purple with the light behind it. On Wednesday she had a real one, she said, but could only tell me what it was not.\n\nBy Friday she was indignant. \"I can't. I can only stir up the ones I've got.\" I tried it myself at home, with my eyes shut, for longer than I shall admit. The mind, it seems, is a paintbox and not a paint factory. Yet somebody must once have seen blue for the first time. It is in the jar where I keep the questions I cannot answer."],["Where the Word Went","On Tuesday Wren Ashby, who is nine, could not remember the word thimble. She was telling the class at Emberwick school about her grandmother's sewing, and stopped. \"The little hat for your finger.\" We all knew it. I, Ida Fennimore, her teacher, held my tongue, because she shook her head at us. She wanted to find it herself.\n\nIt came back at supper, she told me next day, while she was thinking about gravy. \"So where was it?\" It had never left her head. Yet she had looked everywhere in there.\n\nI said perhaps it was like a thing fallen behind a dresser. She asked who moves the dresser. I do not know who moves the dresser. I have lost names and found them all my life and never asked where they spend the afternoon. My jar of questions without answers is nearly full."],["The Jar on the Desk","Wren Ashby stayed behind today to help me, Ida Fennimore, wash the inkwells at Emberwick school. She is nine, and goes up to the big school over the bridge after the summer. Looking for a cloth, she opened my desk drawer and found a barley-sugar jar packed with paper slips.\n\nShe knew her own questions at once. Where the flame goes. Whether her blue is mine. \"You kept them. Why didn't you answer them?\" I said I could not. \"Not any?\" Not one.\n\nShe looked at the jar, and then at my shelf, where the book of answers for arithmetic stands, which is thin. \"Are there more questions than answers, Miss?\" I said I had begun to think so, and that a year ago it would have frightened me. She said it sounded like more to do. I stood the jar on top of the desk, in the light."],["The Lid Left Off","On the last day of term I, Ida Fennimore, wrote a slip of my own for the jar of unanswered questions on my desk at Emberwick school. \"What should a teacher do with a question she cannot answer?\"\n\nI held it over the jar and could not put it in. It has an answer. I have been doing it all year. You keep the question. You say you do not know. You ask it again, with company.\n\nWren Ashby, who filled that jar, left at four for the big school over the bridge. She gave me a slip, folded small: \"Who will ask you things now?\" I stood the jar on the windowsill with the lid off and a pencil on a string. On the first morning of next term, before I had my coat off, there was a slip in it, in large letters. \"Why is the jar full?\""]],[["Sweep Before You Mend","This manual is written by me, Tobias Quill, clockmaker of Cogger’s Lane in Emberwick, for my new apprentice, Nettie Aldery, who arrived this morning with a carpet bag and an alarming number of questions. She is fourteen. She will be here seven years, if the questions do not finish me first.\n\nThe first lesson of horology is the broom. Sweep the workshop every evening, slowly, towards the lamp, and look through the sweepings before they go in the bin. A clock screw is smaller than a barley grain and costs an afternoon to remake. Nettie thought I was making a servant of her. Then she found, in her first dustpan, a click spring I had given up for lost in March.\n\nRule the first: nothing in this shop is rubbish until it has been looked at twice."],["The Names of Things","Nettie Aldery, my apprentice here in Cogger’s Lane, has been given an old kitchen clock to take to pieces. Before she touched a screw I made her draw it, front and back. Then each part came off and was laid on a clean cloth, left to right, in the order it was removed. A clock goes back together in the reverse of the way it came apart, and the cloth remembers what the apprentice will not.\n\nLearn the names. A wheel is large and has teeth. A pinion is small and has leaves. The arbor is the axle they ride on, and a pivot is the fine end of the arbor, turning in its hole in the brass plate.\n\nShe rebuilt the clock by supper with one wheel left over. We consulted the drawing. Tomorrow she will do it again."],["A Drop Is Too Much","A clock is cleaned before it is oiled, and oiled far less than you would think. I, Tobias Quill, have told my apprentice Nettie so about once a week since she came to the shop.\n\nWash every part and dry it. Clean each pivot hole with a sharpened stick of pegwood, twirling it and trimming it until the wood comes out as pale as it went in. Then give each pivot one small drop of oil from the tip of a wire. Put none on the wheel teeth. Oil there gathers dust, and dust in oil is grinding paste.\n\nIn her second month Nettie oiled a mantel clock as generously as she would butter toast. It ran beautifully for a week and then stopped dead, and she spent two days undoing her own kindness. More clocks die of affection than of neglect."],["Respect the Mainspring","Rule the fourth in this manual is the one I shout. Before you loosen anything, let down the power.\n\nA mainspring is a ribbon of steel some feet long, coiled into a barrel no bigger than a jam-pot lid. Wound up, it holds a week of work, and if you part the plates carelessly it will hand you the whole week in half a second. So fit the key to the winding square, take the strain, lift the click with a peg, and let the key turn back through your fingers a little at a time until the spring is slack.\n\nAt fifteen I forgot. There is still a wheel tooth in the ceiling beam of this shop, above the lathe, and I show it to every apprentice. Nettie Aldery is in her second year with me and has not forgotten once. She looks at the beam often."],["Counting Teeth","Nettie Aldery, in her third year at my bench in Emberwick, has discovered that a clock is arithmetic made of brass. Here is the sum I set her.\n\nDivide the teeth of a wheel by the leaves of the pinion it drives. The centre wheel turns once an hour. It has 64 teeth and drives a pinion of 8, so the third wheel turns 8 times. The third wheel has 60 teeth and drives another pinion of 8, so the escape wheel turns seven and a half times for each of those: 60 turns an hour, once a minute. It has 30 teeth, each tooth allows two beats, and so the pendulum beats seconds.\n\nLast week a clock came in with its third wheel missing altogether. Nettie worked out the tooth count on a seed packet. Sixty. A lost wheel can be found with a pencil."],["Only the Length Matters","The rate of a pendulum depends on its length and on nothing else worth mentioning. Lower the bob and the clock goes slower. Raise it and the clock gains. A pendulum that beats seconds is a little over thirty-nine inches long, and one a quarter of that length beats twice as fast.\n\nMy apprentice Nettie Aldery did not believe that the weight made no difference, which is to her credit. She hung a flat iron and a thimble from the shop beam on threads of equal length and set them swinging together. They kept step all afternoon. She has not argued the point since.\n\nUnder every bob is a rating nut. Turn it a little, wait a day, and see what the clock does. Then write what you learned inside the case door, for the next person, who may be you."],["Listening for the Beat","A clock in beat says tick, tock, with the same pause after each. A clock out of beat says tick-tock, and waits, and tick-tock, like a man walking with a stone in his shoe. It may go for an hour so. It will not go for a week.\n\nThe cure is small. Either the case is leaning and wants a slip of wood under one foot, or the crutch, the wire that leads the pendulum, wants bending a hair’s breadth one way. Bend, listen, bend again. Use your ears and not your eyes.\n\nNettie Aldery is four years into her time with me, and her ears are better than mine. She can hear a clock out of beat across the shop while a customer is talking. I have begun asking her opinion. Neither of us has mentioned it."],["Three Broken Pivots","Sooner or later a clock arrives with a part nobody can buy, and then the horologist must make it. This year, her fifth in my shop, Nettie Aldery learned to turn a new pivot.\n\nThe arbor spins in the lathe. You hold a graver, a small steel chisel, on the rest and take off metal in shavings finer than hair until the pivot is straight, round and the right size for its hole. Then it is hardened, tempered until the steel turns blue, and burnished until it shines.\n\nNettie broke three. Each snapped at the last moment, as they do. She did not speak for the rest of the day, and the fourth was perfect. I keep her three in a tin on the shelf, next to a much larger tin of my own. Hands learn by spoiling steel."],["How a Clock Counts","A striking clock must count, and there are two ways to teach it. The older is the count wheel, a disc with notches spaced further and further apart. The clock strikes until a lever drops into the next notch. It works, but it does not know the hour. Move the hands carelessly and it will strike four at six.\n\nThe better way is the rack and snail. The snail is a stepped cam that turns with the hour hand, and the depth of the step decides how many blows are struck. It cannot lose its place.\n\nMy apprentice Nettie, now in her sixth year, was called to the Lamb Inn, where the clock struck nine at noon. It was a count wheel. She let it strike round until it agreed with the hands, charged twopence, and was declared a genius."],["The Counter and the Ticket","No clock walks into the shop alone. It comes with an owner, and this page of the manual is about owners.\n\nWrite a ticket for every job: the name, the fault as the customer tells it, in the customer’s words, and later the fault as you find it. They are seldom the same. Give an honest estimate. If the repair will cost more than the clock, say so plainly, and then do as you are asked. Never call a clock worthless. Somebody’s grandfather wound it every Sunday.\n\nNettie Aldery now keeps my counter in Cogger’s Lane on market days. She told a Lowlands farmer that his stopped clock needed nothing but winding, and showed him how. He left offended. He has since brought us four clocks and a cousin."],["A Clock of Her Own","In the seventh year an apprentice makes a clock. Not mends: makes, from brass sheet and steel rod, every wheel and pinion by her own hand. That is the custom, and Nettie Aldery chose the hardest honest thing, a weight-driven wall clock with a pendulum beating seconds.\n\nEach wheel is cut on the wheel-cutting engine, one tooth at a time, the blank turned on by a dividing plate so the spaces fall even. Then the spokes are sawn and filed out, and the wheels are set at the proper depth with their pinions, neither binding nor loose.\n\nShe worked evenings from harvest to spring and forbade me the back room. I looked once, through the gap in the door. She was testing the depth of two wheels by lamplight, turning them slowly, listening. I went to bed and slept very well."],["Quill and Aldery","Nettie Aldery set her clock going on a wet morning in April, in the back room of my shop in Cogger’s Lane. We let it run a week beside my regulator. It lost four seconds. She gave the rating nut a quarter turn, and in the second week I could measure no error at all.\n\nI signed her papers that evening. Seven years, and she is a clockmaker.\n\nThis manual was written for one reader, and it is hers now, to scribble in and argue with. On Thursday the signwriter came and painted new words above the door: Quill and Aldery. She watched from across the lane, then came in and swept the workshop as she has every night since she was fourteen. There was a screw in the dustpan. She looked at it twice and put it in the tin."]],[["Four Minutes Slow","I am Hester Brannock, keeper of the tower clock above the Market Hall in Emberwick. I have wound it every Monday and Friday for nineteen years, and in that time it has never strayed more than half a minute in a week.\n\nThis spring it has begun to lose. Four minutes one week, nothing the next, three the week after. The baker noticed first, because he opens by it, and he has taken to glaring up at the dial.\n\nA clock that loses steadily is no trouble. You turn the nut under the pendulum and that is that. A clock that loses only now and then is hiding something. I have started this notebook to find out what, and I mean to go through the whole machine, wheel by wheel, until I do."],["Weights and the Going Train","A tower clock is driven by a falling weight. Ours, above Emberwick’s Market Hall, is an iron block as heavy as two men, hung on a wire line that unwinds from a drum over the week. The drum turns the great wheel, and the great wheel passes its turning up through the going train, each wheel moving faster and with less force than the one before.\n\nIf the weight caught on anything in its fall, the force would falter and the clock would lose. So I climbed down the weight shaft with a lantern. I found a jackdaw’s nest on a ledge, a foot clear of the weight, and nothing else. I greased both pulleys and checked the line for kinks.\n\nThe following week the clock lost three minutes. It is not the weight. The jackdaws may stay."],["The Anchor and Its Recoil","The escapement is the part of a clock that lets the power out one tick at a time. In Emberwick’s tower clock, which I keep, it is an anchor: a steel arm shaped like its name, rocking above a wheel of thirty pointed teeth. As the pendulum swings, one end of the anchor lets a tooth go and the other end catches the next. Each escaping tooth gives the pendulum a small push.\n\nWatch closely and you see the wheel pushed backwards a little after every tick. That is the recoil, and it wears the steel. Our anchor’s faces were pitted where the teeth had landed for a century.\n\nI stoned the faces smooth and shifted the anchor so the teeth meet fresh metal. The tick is crisper than I have ever heard it. The clock lost four minutes the following week."],["Thirteen Feet of Pendulum","The pendulum of the Emberwick tower clock is thirteen feet long and takes two full seconds over each swing. Its bob is a lump of cast iron as heavy as a grown sheep. The whole thing hangs by a strip of spring steel no wider than two fingers, from a bracket bolted to the great oak beam that crosses the clock room and carries a good deal else besides.\n\nI am Hester Brannock, the keeper, and this week I examined all of it. The steel strip has no crack. The rating nut is tight. One full turn of that nut alters the clock by about half a minute a day, and nobody has touched it.\n\nBesides, a fault in a pendulum makes a clock wrong all the time. Mine is wrong only some of the time. I am looking in the wrong place."],["Heat, Cold and Iron","Iron grows in the warm. A pendulum rod thirteen feet long, like the one in the Market Hall tower at Emberwick, is a hair longer on a hot afternoon than on a frosty morning, and a longer pendulum swings slower. In a warm spell our clock loses perhaps five seconds a day.\n\nFine clocks cure this with a gridiron pendulum, built of brass and steel rods side by side so that one metal’s stretching lifts the bob as the other’s lowers it. Ours has a plain rod and always has had.\n\nI hung a thermometer in the clock room and wrote down the readings for a month. The slow weeks were not the warm weeks. And five seconds a day is half a minute a week at most, not four minutes. So it is not the weather, though I enjoyed blaming it."],["Out to the Dials","A tower clock shows its face a long way from its works. From the movement in Emberwick’s clock room a long rod runs up to a set of bevel gears, which turn it through a corner and send it out to the two dials, one facing the Market Square and one the river. Behind each dial sits the motion work, the gearing that turns the hour hand once for every twelve turns of the minute hand.\n\nI wondered whether a hand was slipping on its arbor when the wind got up. So I compared both outside dials with the small setting dial on the movement itself, on a slow week.\n\nAll three agreed to the minute, and all three were wrong together. The fault is not in the face of the clock. It is somewhere in its heart, where I have already looked twice."],["The Log Book","I have kept the Emberwick tower clock for nineteen years and never before kept a daily log of it. For six weeks now I have climbed the stair every morning and compared the clock with my own regulator at home, which is good to a second a day.\n\nThe pattern is plain once it is written in columns. From Thursday morning to Wednesday evening the clock keeps excellent time. Every minute it has lost, it has lost between Wednesday evening and Thursday morning. On two Wednesdays out of the six it lost nothing at all.\n\nA clock has no notion of the days of the week. Iron and brass do not know a Wednesday from a Sunday. People do. Something is happening in my tower on Wednesday nights, and I think it has very little to do with gears."],["A Better Escapement","Word of my trouble with the Emberwick clock has reached Mr. Lucius Varden, clockmaker of Nettlesby, who has written me four pages. He blames the old anchor escapement and recommends a deadbeat.\n\nHe explains it well. In a deadbeat the pallets are curved so that the escape wheel, once caught, stands perfectly still until it is released. There is no recoil, less wear, and the pendulum is troubled far less. A clock so fitted may keep time to a few seconds a week.\n\nI do not doubt him. But a poor escapement is poor every day, and my clock is at fault only on Wednesday nights. I also checked the striking train, which has its own weight and its own wheels and cannot rob the going train of anything. I have thanked Mr. Varden and kept his drawing. It is a handsome drawing."],["Dust, Oil and Jackdaws","When in doubt, clean. I took down the going train of the Emberwick tower clock on a Monday, washed every pivot, pegged out every hole, picked a quantity of jackdaw feathers from the frame and oiled the lot afresh. The clock ran as sweetly as I have ever known it. On Wednesday night it lost three and a half minutes.\n\nSo I went back to my log and looked at the two Wednesdays when it lost nothing. One was the week of the Goose Fair. The other was the week the bell ringers took their outing to the Grey Coast.\n\nThe ringers practise in this tower every Wednesday evening, six bells for an hour and a half, except when there is a fair or an outing. And in March, I remember now, the great tenor bell was rehung after thirty years of standing idle."],["A Night in the Tower","On Wednesday I sat up in the clock room of the Market Hall tower with a lantern, a flask of tea and my watch. At seven the ringers began in the chamber below, and the six bells of Emberwick woke over my head.\n\nWhen the tenor was swinging full circle, the lantern on its hook began to sway. I laid my hand on the great oak beam and felt it tremble in time with the bell. The pendulum hangs from that beam. Its even swing shrank and faltered, and every so often a pallet failed to clear its tooth, and the escape wheel waited a beat.\n\nEach missed beat is two seconds. I counted a hundred and twenty of them before the ringing stopped. That is four minutes. My clock was never faulty. It was being rocked like a cradle."],["A Bracket in the Wall","The cure for the Emberwick clock was never to silence the tenor bell, though Ned Fallow, the ringers’ captain, went pale when he thought I might ask. The cure was to hang the pendulum from something the bells cannot move.\n\nAldous Pike the smith forged me an iron bracket two feet deep. We let it into the north wall of the tower with four bolts set in lead. That wall is four feet of stone and has never noticed a bell in its life.\n\nOn Monday I stopped the clock for the first time in nineteen years and chalked an apology on the Market Hall door. I moved the suspension spring and the thirteen-foot pendulum from the oak beam to the new bracket, lengthened the crutch by an inch, set the clock in beat and started it at noon."],["Wednesday, and All’s Well","The first Wednesday after the pendulum was moved, I sat up in the Emberwick clock room again. The tenor bell swung. The lantern swayed and the oak beam trembled as before. The pendulum, hanging now from stone, did not care in the least.\n\nOn Thursday morning the clock agreed with my regulator to within three seconds. In the six weeks since, it has strayed eleven seconds altogether. The baker has stopped glaring. Nobody else has said a word, which is the highest praise a town clock ever gets.\n\nThe ringers have asked me to their supper, and Mr. Varden of Nettlesby still writes about deadbeat escapements. I am closing this notebook and leaving it in the clock room cupboard for the next keeper, with one line underlined. When a clock goes wrong only sometimes, look at what else lives in the building."]],[["A Box Marked Unfinished","An automaton is a clock that has been given something better to do than tell the time. Inside there is a spring for strength, a train of wheels for pace, and a set of cams for memory. That is all, and it is enough to make a brass figure write its name.\n\nI am Barnaby Thistlewood. For twenty years I worked beside Coralie Vane in her shop on Lantern Row, Emberwick. She died last winter and left me her tools, her cat, and a wooden box labelled in her hand: Song thrush. Unfinished.\n\nIn the box are a brass frame the size of a matchbox, a spring barrel, two bellows without their leather, four blank cams, and a small bird’s body in bare copper. There are no drawings. She never wrote anything down. I mean to finish the bird, and to write everything down."],["The Spring and the Fusee","Every automaton needs a motor, and the motor of the song thrush I am finishing for my late teacher, Coralie Vane, is a coiled steel spring in a brass barrel.\n\nSprings have one bad habit. They pull hard when fully wound and feebly when nearly run down. A bird driven so would start its song in a rush and finish in a drawl. The cure is the fusee, a cone-shaped pulley with a spiral groove. A fine chain runs from the barrel to the cone. When the spring is strong it pulls on the narrow end, where it has little leverage. As it weakens, the chain reaches the wide end and the leverage grows. The force comes out even.\n\nCoralie had made the fusee. The chain was missing. I spent nine evenings making one, a hundred and sixty links, each smaller than a poppy seed."],["The Fly Sets the Pace","Wind a spring and let it go, and it will spend itself in one angry whirr. Something must hold the pace. In clocks that is the pendulum. In an automaton, which has to run smoothly rather than in ticks, it is the fly.\n\nThe fly is a little two-bladed fan at the fast end of the wheel train. The faster it spins, the harder the air pushes back, so it settles at a steady speed and holds everything behind it to that speed. Turn the blades flat to the air and the machine slows. Turn them edge on and it hurries.\n\nThe fly in Coralie Vane’s unfinished thrush has brass blades the size of a fingernail. I have set them so the main arbor turns once in twenty seconds. Her cat watches the fly with deep professional interest. I work with the door shut."],["Cams, or Brass Memory","A cam is a disc whose edge has been cut into hills and valleys. A small steel finger, the follower, rests on that edge. As the disc turns the finger rises and falls, and a wire from the finger moves something else: a beak, a tail, a valve. The shape of the edge is the whole instruction. An automaton remembers nothing that has not been filed into brass.\n\nThe song thrush I am building in Emberwick, from the box my teacher Coralie Vane left me, needs four cams on one arbor. One works the bellows, one the pitch of the whistle, one the beak and one the tail.\n\nCoralie left four blanks, perfectly round and perfectly smooth. They remember nothing yet. Before I cut a single valley I must know exactly what a thrush does, and for that I shall have to get up early."],["A Pair of Small Bellows","A mechanical bird needs lungs. In the song thrush I am finishing on Lantern Row they are two wedge-shaped bellows, each about the size of a folded visiting card, made of thin pearwood boards hinged at one end and covered with the finest kid leather.\n\nThey work turn about. As one fills, the other empties, so the wind never stops. Both blow into a third, smaller bellows with a light spring on top, which steadies the pressure. Inside, flaps of leather act as valves and let the air go one way only.\n\nMy teacher Coralie Vane left the boards cut and the leather not. I used hot hide glue, and my first pair leaked at the corners where the leather folds. So did my second. The third held. Squeezed beside the ear, it makes a sound like a mouse sighing."],["The Whistle with a Piston","There is no room inside a small mechanical bird for a row of pipes. The thrush I am completing from Coralie Vane’s box of parts has one whistle only, a brass tube shorter than a matchstick and about as thick as a straw.\n\nThe trick is in its far end, which is closed by a sliding piston. Push the piston in and the column of air shortens, so the note rises. Draw it out and the note falls. One cam slides the piston. Another opens and shuts a tiny valve to chop the wind into separate notes. Because the pitch slides rather than steps, the whistle can swoop and trill as a real bird does.\n\nI tried it by mouth this morning. It gives a full octave and a little over, from a low chirp to a squeak that sent the cat under the dresser."],["Listening in the Orchard","For nine mornings in April I sat in the orchard behind Emberwick mill before it was light, with a notebook on my knee, listening to a song thrush on top of a pear tree.\n\nA thrush sings a short phrase and then says it again, sometimes a third time, before going on to a new one. That habit of repeating is how you tell it from a blackbird. I drew each phrase as a line that climbed and dipped, chose the five I liked best, and gave each one twice.\n\nThen I marked those lines round the edge of a blank cam and began to file. High ground for high notes, valleys for low. You file a stroke, try it against the follower, and file again. The song took eleven days. Brass that has been removed cannot be put back."],["Beak, Tail and Linkages","The copper body my teacher Coralie Vane left in her box has a beak hinged on a pin as fine as a bristle and a tail that rocks on a pivot. Making them move in time with the song has been this month’s work in my Emberwick shop.\n\nFine steel wires run down inside the bird’s hollow legs to the mechanism in the base. Each wire ends in a lever, and each lever rides on a cam. The levers multiply. A rise on the cam no thicker than a playing card opens the beak a full eighth of an inch.\n\nThe beak must open for every note and close in the pauses. The tail flicks once at the end of each phrase. I adjust by putting tiny kinks in the wires. At present the bird is bald, and looks like a kettle with ambitions."],["Feathers, Honestly Come By","Coralie Vane, who taught me the making of automata, had one firm rule about feathers. They must be real, and they must never come from a bird that still needed them.\n\nSo all through late summer I gathered moulted feathers under the orchard hedge at Emberwick mill, and Mrs. Peabody’s speckled hens supplied the pale ones for the breast when they dropped them. Each feather is trimmed and glued on singly, beginning at the tail and working forward, overlapping like slates on a roof so that no join shows. There are about three hundred.\n\nThe breast spots of a song thrush are shaped like small arrowheads. I painted them with a brush of three hairs. Two black glass beads made the eyes. When I had finished, the cat sat and stared at the bird for an hour, which I take as my first review."],["The First Song","Today I put the whole thrush together: spring, fusee, fly, bellows, whistle and four cams in the base, and the feathered bird on its perch above. I gave the key eight turns and let go the catch.\n\nThe fly whirred. The bellows puffed. The beak opened, and out came a wheeze. Then the song, but spoiled. Every phrase began with a gasp of wind and no note, and the beak opened a moment after the sound, like a man laughing late at a joke.\n\nI have checked each part against my notes, here in the shop on Lantern Row, and each part does what it should. Together they do not. A week of adjusting has made things worse, then the same. I have put the bird under a glass dome and gone back to Coralie Vane’s empty box, to see what I missed."],["Written Under the Lining","The box in which Coralie Vane left me her unfinished thrush is lined with green baize. This morning I lifted a corner of it, hunting a dropped screw, and found pencil on the bare wood beneath, in her small square hand. She must breathe before she sings. Bellows a sixteenth ahead.\n\nThere was my fault. I had pinned all four cams to start together, so the whistle opened before the bellows had gathered any wind. A living bird fills its chest first. I turned the bellows cam a sixteenth of a turn ahead of the rest and pinned it again, and I advanced the beak cam a hair so the beak opens just before each note.\n\nIt was one day’s work after a month of despair. In forty years she wrote down one thing, and she hid it where only a man who drops screws would find it."],["A Thrush on Lantern Row","On the first mild morning of March I wound the mechanical thrush, stood it in the open window of the shop on Lantern Row, and let go the catch.\n\nThe bellows filled. The beak opened and the song came out clean: five phrases, each given twice, carrying to the end of the street. The tail flicked. The baker’s boy stopped dead with his tray. At the second winding a real thrush answered from the chimney opposite, and the two of them argued for the rest of the hour.\n\nUnder the base I have engraved two lines. Begun by Coralie Vane. Finished by Barnaby Thistlewood. Her box is back on the shelf with a new label: Song thrush. Finished. Everything in it is now written down, as I promised. The cat has been told, more than once, that the bird is not food."]],[["The Clock That Was Never Wrong","I am Dorcas Wimble, and I mend clocks at the Sign of the Brass Hand in Kettle Row, Emberwick. In my experience the clock is seldom the stubborn party. This book records twelve customers who proved it.\n\nThe first was Mr. Ambrose Tulk, whose longcase clock had never been wrong in forty years and was not wrong now, he said, merely stopped. He had moved it across the parlour to make room for a harmonium. The floor slopes there. A leaning clock ticks unevenly, like a limp, and soon gives up.\n\nI slid two slips of cedar under one foot until the tick and the tock came even. Mr. Tulk said this proved the floor was at fault and not the clock. I agreed, and charged him for the floor. My own shop clock, I notice, is two minutes slow. I must see to that."],["Miss Larch Wants Silence","Miss Evadne Larch came to my shop in Kettle Row three times in one week about a mantel clock in perfect health. Her complaint was that it ticked. More exactly, it ticked at her.\n\nI explained that a clock which does not tick does not go. She was not persuaded. So I walked round to her house and listened, and she was right. The clock stood on a hollow sideboard, and the sideboard was acting as a drum. You could hear the thing from the garden gate.\n\nI cut a square of thick felt and set the clock on it. The tick sank to a murmur. Miss Larch returned next morning to say that now she could not tell whether it was going. We settled on thinner felt. I charged for both pieces, and I consider that I earned it."],["The Twins Who Overwound It","Osbert and Oswald Pottle share their late mother’s eight-day clock and very little else. They carried it into my shop in Emberwick between them, each explaining that the other had overwound it.\n\nThere is no such thing. A clock wound to the top that will not go is not too tight. It is too dirty or too dry to use the power it has. The Pottle clock had not been cleaned in thirty years, and its oil had turned to something like toffee. I washed it, pegged the holes, oiled it properly, and it went.\n\nThe brothers then fell out over whose turn it was to wind. I pinned a card inside the case door with fifty-two Sundays and their initials turn about. They disputed who should go first. I tossed a trouser button. Osbert has demanded a recount."],["Thirteen Cuckoos at Greaves Farm","Hob Greaves farms on the edge of the Lowlands and owns a cuckoo clock that called thirteen at noon and wheezed the rest of the day. He would not let it leave his kitchen, and he would pay only in eggs. So I, Dorcas Wimble, mended it on his table, watched by a hen.\n\nA cuckoo’s voice is two small bellows, each sitting on its own pipe, one for each note. Mice had eaten the cover off one bellows. I re-covered it with thin kid leather. The thirteenth cuckoo was a worn notch in the count wheel, which let the lever skip a place. I filed the notch square again.\n\nI went home with two dozen eggs. Jory, my errand-girl, tells me that people set their watches by our shop clock and are then two minutes late for everything. I know. I shall see to it."],["Mrs. Sibley’s Goose Grease","Mrs. Hepzibah Sibley has oiled her own kitchen clock every Sunday for eleven years, with goose grease, on the principle that what is good for a chest is good for a clock. It arrived at my shop in Kettle Row stopped solid.\n\nGoose grease stiffens in the cold and creeps in the warm. It had crept everywhere and gathered every speck of dust in her kitchen. Washing it out took two days and most of my patience.\n\nThe difficulty was not the clock. Mrs. Sibley was affronted at being asked to stop, and a customer who feels useless will find something worse to do. So I told her the movement wants nothing for three years, but the case wants beeswax every Sunday without fail. She has taken to it with great devotion. That clock now shines like a conker and keeps excellent time."],["The Schoolroom Clock Runs Fast","Mr. Crispin Vole, schoolmaster of Emberwick, declared that his schoolroom clock had gained ten minutes a week ever since I last cleaned it, and that I should put it right for nothing.\n\nI found the movement in good order. The fault was lower down. The rating nut under the pendulum bob had been screwed up, raising the bob, and a shorter pendulum swings faster. There were also small fingerprints in the dust inside the case door, at about the height of a ten-year-old. I set it right. The next week it was fast again.\n\nI fitted a lock to the case door, gave Mr. Vole the key, and blamed vibration. Lessons now end when they should, and I am not well thought of among the under-twelves. Mr. Vole observed, on leaving, that my own shop clock is two minutes slow. So it is."],["Captain Marrin’s Eight Bells","Captain Ysolde Marrin, retired from the Grey Coast packet boats, keeps a ship’s clock on her parlour wall. It strikes as clocks do at sea: one bell at the first half hour of a watch, another added every half hour, up to eight bells, and then round again.\n\nThe clock was not at fault. Her sister, who lives with her, could not tell the time by it and wanted it mended to strike like a decent clock. The Captain forbade me to touch the strike and ordered me to do something.\n\nSo I mended the sister. I wrote out a card in my best hand, showing bells against hours, framed it, and hung it beside the clock. Within a week she was announcing that it had gone six bells and the kettle should be on. I charged for one frame."],["The Alderman’s Estimate","Alderman Josiah Roke brought in the clock from the council committee room, whose mainspring had broken with a bang during a debate about drains. He wanted it back by yesterday and thought I might do the work for nothing, as a civic honour.\n\nI, Dorcas Wimble of Kettle Row, wrote him an estimate in three lines. New mainspring. Cleaning. Honour, no charge. He offered half the total. I offered to fit half a spring.\n\nA mainspring cannot be patched. Once it has snapped you fit a new one of the same height, thickness and strength, or the clock runs badly ever after. He paid in full, slowly, in very small coins. Now whenever he passes my window he consults my shop clock, consults his watch, and shakes his head. It is two minutes slow. He is quite right, which is the annoying part."],["Tamsin Reed Asks Why","Tamsin Reed is nine. She brought me her grandfather’s old alarm clock and four pennies tied in a handkerchief, and she would not leave either. She sat on a stool by my bench in Kettle Row and asked why at every screw.\n\nWhy is the spring blue? Because the steel was heated until it turned that colour, which makes it tough. Why does the little wheel go back and forth? Because a hairspring pulls it home each time. Why is there a button inside?\n\nThere should not have been. A shirt button was wedged between two wheels. Tamsin remembered posting it through the back when she was four, to see whether the clock was hungry. Out it came, and the clock ran. I charged one penny and told her to come back on Saturdays. She is the only customer this year I have invited to return."],["Mr. Hask Improves Things","Mr. Lucian Hask mends his own clocks and then brings them to me, Dorcas Wimble, to be mended back. He stands at my elbow throughout and explains what he did.\n\nThis time he had replaced a broken suspension spring with a strip of corset steel and a click spring with a bent hairpin. A cracked wheel tooth was buried under a blob of solder the size of a pea. All three worked, after a fashion, for a fortnight.\n\nA broken tooth is properly mended by cutting a small slot in the wheel, letting in a slip of new brass, and filing it to match its neighbours. I let him watch. I returned the hairpin in an envelope. He is a nuisance, but he loves clocks, and I have lent him my second-best file. He will be back by spring, and so will the file, I hope."],["The Clock Kept Stopped","Mrs. Nan Orrery was marched into my shop in Kettle Row by her daughter, carrying a mantel clock that had stood at ten past four for six years. The daughter wanted it going. Mrs. Orrery did not.\n\n“Ten past four is when Walter came in for his tea,” she said. “I like it there.”\n\nA clock is a machine and ought to be kept sound. Whether it runs is the owner’s business. I cleaned and oiled the movement so that nothing in it will rust or seize, ran it a full day on my bench to be sure, then let it run down and set the hands back to ten past four. I told the daughter it was in perfect order and resting. She was not pleased. Mrs. Orrery pressed my hand at the door, and I did not charge for the hands."],["The Twelfth Customer","On the last Monday of the year I came down to find a repair ticket tied to my own shop clock, in the handwriting of Jory Sprott, my errand-girl. Customer: Mrs. D. Wimble. Fault: two minutes slow all year. Says she will see to it. Difficult.\n\nI took the clock down. It was clean, in beat and without fault. Then Mr. Tulk came in about his floor and shut the door the way my customers do, and I saw the pendulum shudder. The clock hung on the thin partition beside the door. Every slam had jarred it and cost it a moment. A year of stubborn customers makes a great many slams.\n\nI moved the clock to the chimney breast, which is solid brick, and nailed a leather pad to the door frame. It has kept time since. I paid Jory the price of the repair. She had earned it."]],[["Hannah's Half","My grandmother Hannah Ashby played one air on the fiddle better than anyone in Emberwick, and she only ever played the first half of it. It is in D, slow, three beats to the bar: eight bars that climb from the open D string to a high A and hang there. Then she would stop and lay the fiddle down.\n\n“The second half went over the fells with a man who never brought it back,” she said, and would say no more.\n\nShe left me the fiddle and the half tune. I am Corwin Ashby, and I play for dances and for my supper. This spring I mean to walk the country round with a book of ruled paper, write down whatever airs I am given, and ask everywhere for the rest of Hannah's. A tune that stops in mid-air keeps a man awake."],["The Sluice at Harrowmill","A day's walk up the river Ember from Emberwick stands Harrowmill, where the miller's wife, Bess Tranter, plays a jig that copies the mill. It is in G, six quick beats to the bar. The low strain thumps along on the two bottom strings like the wheel turning. The high strain runs in a stream of short notes, like water let through the sluice.\n\nShe plays it with small bows near the tip and a lift on the first note of every three, so that it bounces. Her father made it while waiting for corn to grind, and always claimed the mill wrote it.\n\nI played her my grandmother's half air and asked if she knew the rest. She did not. But she said the way it hangs on its last note sounded like fell-country playing. They leave notes hanging up there, like washing."],["Old Tam's Gate","On a farm outside Netherby, in the Lowlands, I met Tam Oakes, a ploughman of eighty, who plays one slow air and plays it leaning on the same gate every evening. The tune has taken the gate's name.\n\nIt is in A, but with the G left natural, which gives it a grey, old-fashioned sound. He takes a whole bar to each bow and slides up to the high C rather than stepping there. He had it from a travelling tinker, who said he had it from a dream. I wrote it down by the last of the light.\n\nThen I played him my grandmother Hannah's half tune, the one I am walking the country to finish. He listened twice with his eyes shut. He had heard it once, he said, whole, at a hiring fair sixty years ago. A fell man played it, on a dark fiddle."],["Goose Fair Morning","At Withy Cross, in the Lowlands, the Goose Fair is opened each year by the three Dunnock sisters playing a reel of the same name. It is in D and goes at a gallop.\n\nThe trick of it is in the bow, which rocks between the D string and the A so that an open string is always ringing under the tune. From across the green it sounds remarkably like geese. Before each strong beat there is a cut, a flick of the third finger too quick to count as a note. Two sisters play the tune together. The eldest drops to a drone when her arm tires.\n\nI am Corwin Ashby, collecting airs and looking for the lost half of one. I played it to the sisters between sets. They had never heard it, were too busy to care, and bought me a pie. Not every road has a signpost."],["Low Fen Lights","Silas Wend cuts reeds in the marsh east of Emberwick and plays his fiddle with a wooden clothes peg clipped to the bridge. The peg mutes it to a murmur, so the sound will not carry over the water and put up the wildfowl.\n\nHis one air is in E minor and has no steady beat. Each phrase lasts as long as a slow breath and then waits. He calls it after the pale lights that are seen over the fen on still nights. He taught it to me by ear, a phrase at a time, sitting in his punt. Paper does not last in the marsh.\n\nI played him my grandmother's unfinished air. “That's a tune with the door left open,” he said. He could not close it. But the fell fiddlers come down to the Grey Coast each autumn to sell wool, and I might ask there."],["Herring Lamps","At Skarne Haven on the Grey Coast, Meg Corrie plays on the quay outside the net loft, with the fiddle held low against her arm instead of under her chin, so that she can call to the boats without stopping.\n\nHer tune is named for the lamps the herring boats hang out at night. It is in G and moves long-short, long-short, like a hull on a swell. Under it she keeps the open D string droning, a second voice that never changes its mind.\n\nI am Corwin Ashby of Emberwick, and I had come to ask the fell wool-sellers about the lost half of my grandmother's air. They had been and gone. But Meg knew my eight bars at once. “That's the front of Garth's tune,” she said. “Josiah Garth, of Skerrow in the Fells. He only has the back.”"],["The Net-Mender's Waltz","Three days of rain kept me in the net loft at Skarne Haven, on the Grey Coast, with an old net-mender called Dan Pellow. Dan cannot play a note. He hums.\n\nWhat he hums is a waltz in D, three beats to the bar, with the second beat a shade late. It goes exactly with his hands: pull, knot, pull. He has hummed it at his work for fifty years and never thought it worth a name. I put it on the fiddle for him, and he corrected me by tapping my knee whenever I hurried.\n\nA tune can live a long while in someone with no instrument at all. I wrote it in my book as his. When the rain eased I shouldered my fiddle and turned north for Skerrow, where a man named Garth is said to keep the other half of my grandmother's air."],["Cold Shoulder Pass","The road into the Northern Fells goes over Cold Shoulder Pass, and there is a stone hut at the top where drovers sleep. I shared it with one, Abel Rudd, and his fiddle.\n\nAbel plays a striding air in A that the drovers use to keep cattle moving. For it he retunes the two low strings a note higher, so the fiddle is tuned A, E, A, E. Every open string then rings along with the tune, and the instrument sounds twice its size. Fell tuning, he calls it. He retuned mine without asking.\n\nI told him I was Corwin Ashby, walking to Skerrow to find Josiah Garth and the lost half of an air. He was quiet a while. Old Garth died in the winter, he said, at ninety-one. But Skerrow was a day further on, and I had come too far to turn back."],["Three Sheep Short","I reached Skerrow farm, high in the Northern Fells, on the day of the shearing, and was handed a plate before I could explain myself.\n\nThe shearers' own tune is a jig in D called Three Sheep Short. It has three strains, and the third stops two bars early, on purpose. Every stranger who dances to it takes two more steps and falls over somebody. The whole barn then shouts the count. Six fiddles play it at once, roughly, with a great deal of foot.\n\nWhen I had fallen over twice I asked Josiah Garth's son about his late father's half tune, the answer to my grandmother's. He is a shepherd and does not play. “Father taught that to one person only,” he said. “My girl Ellen.” I asked where Ellen was. He pointed south, the way I had come. “Married a baker.”"],["Garth's Ewe","At Skerrow, in the Northern Fells, the shepherd Thomas Garth could not give me the tune I had walked a month to find. He gave me another, and a story.\n\nThe tune is a slow air in G that his father Josiah made for a favourite ewe. Thomas whistled it and I wrote it down, with the dog howling the long notes. Its phrases all come downhill. They settle where another tune would climb.\n\nThe story is this. As a young man Josiah walked south to a hiring fair and met a girl with a fiddle. They made an air between them, she the first half and he the second. He went home for lambing, meaning to return. Snow shut the pass, and then life got in the way. Her name was Hannah. And his granddaughter Ellen, who has his half, married a baker in Emberwick."],["Coming Down by Skerrow","Walking home from the Northern Fells to Emberwick took me three days, and somewhere on the first of them I began, for the first time in my life, to make an air of my own.\n\nIt is in D and goes at walking pace, because my feet wrote most of it. I can hear where the rest came from. The thump in the low strain is the mill at Harrowmill. The drone is Meg Corrie's from the coast. The last note of each phrase hangs, fell fashion. A tune built from other tunes should say so, and I have put their names in the margin.\n\nMy book now holds eleven airs and half of another. As for the baker's wife, one of Emberwick's bakeries is in Mill Street, two streets from my door. I have bought bread there for six years, from a woman with a northern voice."],["The Whole Air","Ellen Dobbs, who was Ellen Garth, came to the back door of the Mill Street bakery in Emberwick with flour to her elbows. I played her my grandmother Hannah's eight bars and stopped on the high A, where Hannah always stopped.\n\nEllen wiped her hands, took my fiddle and played eight more. They come down from that A by the same steps the first half climbed, turn once, and settle on the open D like a door closing quietly. Her grandfather Josiah called it the Answer. He never had the front.\n\nWe played it whole among the cooling loaves, a half each, then changed about. It is the twelfth air in my book, with two names over it: Hannah Ashby and Josiah Garth. I walked to the Fells and back for a tune that was two streets away. I sleep very well now."]],[["January: The Dumb D","I am Lettice Corran, and for eleven years I have played the organ in the chapel on Tallow Street, Emberwick. It has two keyboards, a row of pedals, eleven stops and one silence. On the principal stop, the D just above the middle of the keyboard has never spoken. Press it and you get a sigh of wind and nothing more.\n\nIn January the chapel is so cold that the ivory keys bite, and I play in mittens with the ends cut off. Cold flattens the pipes as well, so the whole organ sinks a little below the choir, who blame each other.\n\nI have learned to play around the D as one walks round a puddle, borrowing the note from the flute stop or leaving a hole and hoping. Most Sundays nobody notices. I notice."],["February: Dickon at the Bellows","An organ breathes, and ours in the Tallow Street chapel at Emberwick breathes by hand. Behind the case is a long ash handle, and on the end of it is Dickon Pyle, aged twelve, who pumps for a small fee and a seat near the stove.\n\nA lead weight on a string shows him how full the bellows are. If it climbs past the chalk mark the wind is running out, and the whole organ sags in pitch like a tired singer. For a loud piece he must pump like a man bailing a boat, so I knock once on the case to warn him.\n\nThe bellows leather is cracked along the folds. In a quiet passage you can hear it hiss. It wants replacing, and the pipes want a tuner, and the chapel has afforded neither in eleven years. I have put a jar on the vestry shelf."],["March: What the Stops Do","Each stop knob on an organ brings on one rank of pipes, a pipe for every key. On the chapel organ at Emberwick, which I play, the principal is the plain strong voice, a row of open metal pipes. The flute is wooden and stopped with a plug, which makes it soft and round and an octave deeper than its length would suggest. The trumpet has a brass tongue that buzzes. It is kept for weddings and last verses.\n\nDraw several stops and the voices add together. That is all the mystery there is.\n\nMarch is the damp month. The wooden sliders swell, and a stop that came out with one finger in January wants both hands. The trackers, the thin rods that join each key to its pipe, swell too, and the keys feel like pressing into porridge. One plays gently in March, and forgives."],["April: Walking the Bride In","Prudence Hale married the ferryman this month, in the chapel on Tallow Street, and I played her up the aisle.\n\nThe art of a wedding is not the tune but the length. A bride takes about forty seconds to walk our aisle, fewer if she is nervous, and the organist must reach the last chord just as she reaches the front. So I play a march of my own making, built in four-bar pieces, any of which can be repeated or left out. It opens and shuts like a telescope.\n\nThis bride stopped halfway to see to her shoe. I went round the middle piece three times and nobody knew. The march is in D, and its grandest note is the one D that has never sounded on our principal stop. I gave it to the trumpet instead. The ferryman wept. I take no credit."],["May: Giving the Note","The choir of the Tallow Street chapel in Emberwick practises on Thursdays, fourteen voices and one organist, with the windows open and a blackbird outside competing with the trebles.\n\nMy humblest duty is to give the note: one soft chord, so that everyone begins in the same place. After that the secret is to lead by a hair. Organ pipes speak a little late, and sound takes its time crossing a room. An organist who follows the choir drags it slower and slower until the whole company comes to a stop, looking hurt.\n\nThe other May skill is filling in. When the collection outlasts the music, I take the last line of the tune, turn it upside down, wander about on the flute stop, and come home as the plates reach the back. Nobody has ever remarked on it. That is how I know it is going well."],["June: Sour Trumpets","Heat untunes an organ in a particular way. As the air warms, the ordinary pipes rise in pitch. The reed pipes hardly move. So the trumpet stop, which agreed with everything in April, sounds sour against the rest by midsummer. There is no cure but a tuner or a cool day. I leave the trumpet pushed in and let the principal carry the chapel, hole and all.\n\nDickon Pyle pumps the bellows in his shirtsleeves, and in June I pay him partly in ginger beer.\n\nThe jar on the vestry shelf now holds about a third of a tuner. Old Mr. Elias Thrupp, who was organist at Emberwick for forty years before me, is said to have tuned the organ himself. He is also said to have quarrelled with the committee over playing music nobody had asked for. I think I should have liked him."],["July: A Cipher in the Sermon","A cipher is a note that sounds by itself and will not stop, because the little valve under its pipe has stuck open. In July, in the middle of the sermon, a low F sharp on the flute stop began to hoot softly, like an owl with something on its mind.\n\nI pushed the flute stop in, which silenced it, and knocked twice on the case to tell Dickon to stop pumping. Dickon had dozed off, woke at the knock, and pumped harder.\n\nAfterwards I crawled inside the organ with a candle, my first time in eleven years as organist here in Emberwick. A dead moth lay on the valve. While I was there I found the dumb D standing in its row, looking just like its neighbours. I did not dare lift it. Pipe metal is as soft as cheese and dents under a thumb."],["August: Pedals in Stockinged Feet","August is the quiet month at the Tallow Street chapel, with half of Emberwick in the fields, and I use it to practise the pedals.\n\nThe pedals are a keyboard for the feet, a row of long wooden levers that play the deepest pipes, some of them twice the height of a man. One plays in stockinged feet, toe and heel, knees together, finding the notes by feel. Looking down is fatal.\n\nI also taught Dickon Pyle, who pumps the bellows, to pick out a tune on the keys. He is quick. A blower who knows what the music is about to do can pump ahead of it. In return he let me try the handle. It is far harder than it looks, and I lasted one verse. Each of us now thinks rather more of the other."],["September: The Harvest Supper","The harvest supper in the chapel hall at Emberwick was held this year for the organ, which needs a tuner and new bellows leather and has had neither in my eleven years.\n\nI played for the singing: plain strong tunes that everyone knows, on the principal and the flute, with the trumpet added for the last verse now that cooler weather has brought it back into agreement. There were pies, an auction of apples, and a marrow raffled twice by mistake.\n\nWe counted the money in the vestry. With the jar, it comes to the cost of the tuner and the leather, and fourpence over. I wrote that night to Mr. Halloran Pym of Netherby. Dickon, my bellows boy, asked whether a tuner can mend the D that has never spoken. I told him the truth, which is that I do not know."],["October: Mr. Pym Listens","Mr. Halloran Pym, organ tuner, spent three days inside the chapel organ at Emberwick. He sets one rank of pipes true and then tunes every other pipe against it, listening for the beats, the slow wah-wah two notes make when they almost agree. As they come together the beats slow, and when they vanish the pipe is right. He taps a brass cone into a pipe's top to sharpen it, or over the top to flatten it.\n\nOn the second day he reached the D that has been dumb for as long as I, Lettice Corran, have played here. He lifted the pipe out, looked down it, and drew out a tight roll of paper.\n\nIt was music, handwritten. For Midwinter. E. Thrupp. Hidden from the committee. Whoever finds this, play it. He set the pipe back. I pressed the key, and the D spoke."],["November: Learning Mr. Thrupp","The music found rolled inside our dumb organ pipe is three pages long, in the hand of Elias Thrupp, who played at Emberwick before me. I have practised it every evening this month.\n\nIt begins with the D alone, on the principal stop, held for four slow bars: the very note he silenced by hiding the paper there. Then the tune unfolds around it, flute above and pedals walking below, and the D sounds through nearly every bar like a lamp left in a window. The middle is hard. My left hand and my feet must travel in opposite directions.\n\nDickon Pyle practises too. A long held note shows every unevenness in the wind, and he is learning to pump as smoothly as breathing. The new bellows leather does not hiss. The committee has asked what I mean to play at Midwinter. I said, something old."],["December: Midwinter","On Midwinter Eve the chapel in Tallow Street was full, with a candle in every window and snow on the sills. I knocked once on the organ case. Behind it, Dickon Pyle began to pump.\n\nI played the D alone. A few heads lifted. Nobody in Emberwick under sixty had ever heard that note from this organ, though they could not have said what was different. Then Mr. Thrupp's tune came in around it. I smudged one bar in the hard middle and was forgiven. The last page gathers every stop, trumpet and all, and ends as it began, on the single D fading into the roof.\n\nIn the quiet old Mrs. Hale said, quite loudly, “That's Elias.” She had heard him practise it through the wall as a girl. His manuscript hangs framed in the vestry now. The D has other work to do."]],[["The Midwife's Notebook","I am Mabel Tansy, and I have been midwife in Emberwick for thirty years. I have heard more lullabies through bedroom doors than anyone living and never written one down. This year I shall. My daughter Rose expects her first child at Midwinter, and a grandmother ought to arrive with the right song. I mean to collect eleven and choose. The first is from Mrs. Oram, the baker's wife, who sings at four in the morning while the dough proves.\n\nSleep, my crumb, the oven's warm,\nThe loaves are rising slow;\nThe night is only flour and dark,\nAnd morning's in the dough.\n\nShe sings it low, on three notes, in time with her kneading. Babies raised over a bakery, she says, will sleep through anything except silence."],["The Fisher's Rocking Song","At Skarne Haven on the Grey Coast the cradles are rocked by foot. Annet Pellow sits mending nets with both hands, a loop of cord round her ankle, and the cradle swings each time she moves her heel. Her song keeps the same slow pace, rising on the first line and falling on the second, like a boat lifted and set down.\n\nOut goes the tide and in again,\nThe lamps are on the bay;\nThe sea that rocks your father's boat\nWill rock your cares away.\n\nI am Mabel Tansy, midwife of Emberwick, collecting lullabies this year for a grandchild of my own. Annet had this one from her mother, who had it from hers. She says it works best with real waves behind it, and that inland babies must take it on trust."],["The Shepherd's Count","Not every lullaby belongs to a mother. On a farm in the Northern Fells I heard Will Harker, a shepherd with hands like spades, sing his small son to sleep with a counting song. He holds the child against his chest so that the deep notes are felt as much as heard.\n\nOne for the ewe on the fellside steep,\nTwo for the lamb beside her,\nThree for the dog who minds their sleep,\nFour for the sky grown wider.\n\nIt goes on to ten, each number slower and quieter than the last, and Will makes up the later verses fresh every night. He told me, Mabel Tansy, writing it all down in my notebook, that in two years he has never once reached seven with the boy still awake. Eight, nine and ten, he admits, have never been properly finished."],["The Marsh Hush","In the marsh east of Emberwick the reed-cutters' wives hardly sing at all. Their lullaby is half whispered, with a long shh between the lines that sounds like wind going through the reed beds. Nell Wend showed it to me in a cottage on stilts, with her baby in a rush basket.\n\nHush, the reeds are saying hush,\nThe heron's gone to bed;\nThe frog has folded up his song\nAnd laid it by his head.\n\nThere are only two notes in it, a step apart, and you rock on the shh rather than on the words. I am a midwife, and I have collected lullabies all this year for my daughter's coming child. This is the quietest so far. Nell says a loud lullaby is a contradiction, like a hot snowball, and I could not argue with her."],["The Miller's Wheel","Old Mrs. Tranter, the miller's mother at Harrowmill on the river Ember, has sung three generations to sleep with the same song, to the sound of the wheel outside the window.\n\nRound goes the wheel, and the river goes by,\nIt has nowhere to be but the sea;\nAnd you've nowhere to be but here, my eye,\nSo go round to sleep with me.\n\nThe tune is cleverer than it seems. Its last note is also its first, so the song joins end to end like a hoop and can be sung for an hour without a seam. She sways from foot to foot with it, as slowly as the wheel turns. I wrote it down for my collection, which is meant for my daughter Rose, whose baby comes at Midwinter. Mrs. Tranter's advice was free: never be the first to stop."],["The Lamplighter's Verse","Jem Corbel lights the street lamps of Emberwick, and his daughter's bedtime falls just as he must go out with his ladder. So he sings to her from the doorway with his cap already on.\n\nI've lit the lamp at the end of the lane,\nAnd the lamp by the baker's door;\nThere's one in the window to light me home,\nAnd you need watch no more.\n\nIt is a walking tune, steady and plain, and he sings it getting quieter, as if he were already going away down the street. By the last line he is. His wife says the child is asleep before the gate clicks. I am Mabel Tansy, the midwife, and this is the sixth lullaby in my notebook. I am halfway to the eleven I promised myself, and no nearer knowing which I shall sing to my own grandchild."],["A Song Without Words","Old Mrs. Quillet of Kettle Row, Emberwick, has been deaf for twenty years and has raised, by her own count, nine grandchildren through their worst nights. Her lullaby has no words, and she has never heard it.\n\nShe hums three notes down and three notes back up, over and over. With it she pats the baby's back: two soft pats and a rest, two pats and a rest, at the pace of a slow heart. She feels the hum in her own chest and the child feels it through her shoulder.\n\nI, Mabel Tansy, sat and watched a furious infant go quiet in under five minutes. I could not write this one down as a verse, so I have drawn the pats as dots. Half of any lullaby is the hand, I think now. Perhaps rather more than half."],["Tilly Sings to Her Doll","The eighth lullaby in my collection comes from a singer aged five. Tilly Oram, the Emberwick baker's youngest, was putting a rag doll to bed in a flour bin and did not know that the midwife was listening at the door.\n\nGo to sleep, you've had your tea,\nYou've had your wash, you've had your say;\nIf you don't sleep then nor shall we,\nAnd I've got things to do today.\n\nShe sang it firmly, on one note, shaking the doll in time. I recognised every word. It is what a tired mother says, set down faithfully by the one person who hears it nightly. A lullaby does not have to be poetry to be true. I have promised Tilly never to tell her mother where I got it. The doll, I am told, slept perfectly."],["The Song of the Road","A family of travelling tinsmiths camps each spring on the Lowlands road outside Netherby, and the mother, Seffy Lovage, sings in the rhythm of the van. Her lullaby jogs along like a horse at a walk, with a small dip in every line where a wheel might find a rut.\n\nThe road is long, the wheels are round,\nThe horse knows where to go;\nThe stars are nails in a big dark roof,\nAnd home is where we slow.\n\nHer children, she told me, cannot sleep in a house. The bed keeps too still. I am Mabel Tansy, midwife of Emberwick, and I wrote her song into my notebook by the light of their fire. She would take nothing for it. A lullaby is the one thing a traveller can give away, she said, and still have when she leaves."],["A Lullaby for the Old","Lullabies are not only for babies. Mr. Tolliver of Mill Street, Emberwick, is ninety-one and sleeps badly, and his daughter Agnes sits by him at night and sings the song he once sang to her.\n\nThe day was long, you carried it far,\nNow set it by the door;\nThe kettle's off, the gate is barred,\nThere's nothing to do more.\n\nShe sings it very slowly and a little flat, which is how he always sang it, and holds his hand through the last line. He joins in on the word door. I have attended a great many beginnings in thirty years as a midwife. This one I collected at the other end, and I am glad I did. It is the tenth in my notebook. My own grandchild is due in six weeks, and I still have not chosen."],["The Lullaby That Failed","The schoolmaster of Emberwick, Mr. Vole, has twin boys who will sleep to no song whatever. He borrowed my notebook and tried all ten lullabies on them in one evening. They enjoyed the concert and asked for more. What works, he found by accident, is arithmetic, chanted in a flat and patient voice.\n\nSeven sevens are forty-nine,\nEight eights are sixty-four;\nNine nines are eighty-one, and then\nThere isn't any more.\n\nThey seldom last beyond the sixes. So a lullaby, I now think, is anything said kindly and without hurry. That makes eleven in my book, and my daughter Rose's baby is due within the fortnight. I, Mabel Tansy, who set out to choose the right song, have read all eleven through twice tonight and cannot choose at all."],["The Twelfth, Unplanned","My granddaughter was born at four on Midwinter morning, in Emberwick, with snow against the door. I was not the midwife. The young one from Netherby did the work, and I boiled kettles, badly. Then Rose handed me the baby, and I opened my mouth to sing one of my eleven collected lullabies. What came out was none of them.\n\nEleven songs I brought for you,\nAnd every one has flown;\nSo here's a twelfth, made up just now,\nAnd it is yours alone.\n\nI cannot tell you the tune. It was whatever my voice did. She slept. Her name is Ivy. So this notebook holds twelve lullabies after all. Eleven belong to the houses where I heard them, and I have only borrowed those. The twelfth belongs to Ivy. I am leaving the book on Rose's shelf, with blank pages at the back."]],[["A Borrowed March","I am Horatio Bunce, bandmaster of the Emberwick Town Band: four cornets, two horns, a euphonium, two trombones, a bass, two drums, and a fiddle and flute who join us for dances.\n\nFor sixty years this band has marched to the Netherby Quickstep, a tune borrowed from the next town and never returned. It is a sound march, two beats to the bar and a hundred and twenty paces to the minute, with the bass drum falling on every left foot. But it is not ours.\n\nThis January the mayor stopped me outside the Market Hall. Charter Day falls in September, three hundred years since Emberwick was made a town, and she wants us to parade to a march of our own. I said yes before I had thought. I have never written a note that was not someone else's first."],["The Reel Called Kettle Row","A reel is a quick dance tune in two halves of eight bars, each half played twice, and the whole thing repeated until the dancers give in. In the Emberwick Town Band the fiddle and the flute carry the tune. The brass keep underneath, the bass on the beat and the horns answering off it, which bandsmen call the oom and the pah.\n\nOur oldest reel is named for Kettle Row, the tinsmiths' street. It is full of repeated notes, three and four together, like small hammers at work.\n\nI am the bandmaster, and I am supposed to be writing the town a march by September. Last night I finished sixteen bars and played them to Mrs. Bunce on the cornet. She listened kindly. Then she said it was the Netherby Quickstep, upside down. She was right. I have burnt it."],["How Fast Emberwick Walks","Most marches go at a hundred and twenty paces a minute. That suits a flat parade ground. Emberwick's High Street climbs all the way from the river to the Market Hall, and for sixty years the Town Band has reached the top too breathless to hold a note in tune.\n\nSo before writing a bar of the new town march, I took my watch and stood by the pump for a morning, counting feet. The baker climbs the hill at a hundred and four. The lamplighter manages ninety-six. Schoolchildren, late, do a hundred and thirty. Most people, left to themselves, go up at about a hundred and eight.\n\nThat is my first decision as a composer. The Emberwick march will go at a hundred and eight paces a minute, the speed this town really walks. I still have no tune. But I know how fast it is."],["The Miller's Reel","We played for the spring dance at Harrowmill, up the river Ember, where they always ask for the Miller's Reel. It has the rhythm of the mill wheel inside it, two quick notes and a heavy one, over and over, and the dancers stamp on the heavy one until the floor complains.\n\nOur new side drummer is Posy Larkin, who is eleven and took the drum over from her grandfather. She is neat and quick, and she hurries. I have told her to listen to the bass drum as if it were her own breathing.\n\nWalking home I could not get the wheel out of my head, and I stopped fighting it. The march I am writing for Emberwick now has an opening: the side drum alone, three knocks and a rest, twice. It is not a tune yet. It is a knock at the door."],["A Slow March","Once a year, in May, the Emberwick Town Band plays a slow march, when the oldest members of the guilds walk from the bridge to the Market Hall. It goes at about sixty-five paces a minute, and it is the hardest thing we do.\n\nIn a quick march, small faults are gone before anyone hears them. In a slow one, every wobble stands in the open. The long notes use up a player's whole breath, so we stagger our breathing, each taking air at a different moment, and the sound never breaks.\n\nI am Horatio Bunce, bandmaster, and my own march for the town still has no melody. I have a speed and a drumbeat. I have tried forty tunes, and each one turned out to belong to somebody else. Mr. Gant, our euphonium, says a tune cannot be hunted. It can only be overheard."],["The Ferryman's Whistle","I crossed the river Ember on the ferry this week, on band business, and heard my march.\n\nJob Hale the ferryman whistles as he pushes off from the bank. It is always the same five notes: up a step, up a leap, then down three like a man coming downstairs. He does not know he does it. His father whistled it on the same ferry. It belongs to nobody but the river, and I wrote it on my shirt cuff before we reached the far side.\n\nThose five notes are now the opening phrase of the Emberwick march. The rest came within the week, as if it had been waiting. The phrase is answered by itself, lower down, and then climbs to the top of the cornets' reach. Sixteen bars. I played them to Mrs. Bunce. She did not mention Netherby at all."],["Trio, the Quiet Middle","Every proper march has a trio. It is the middle section, softer than the rest, set in a key four notes higher, with a smoother tune. The mellow instruments take it, the horns and the euphonium, while the cornets rest their lips. Afterwards the first tune returns, louder, and seems all the better for the holiday.\n\nI am Horatio Bunce, writing a march for Emberwick's Charter Day, and I wanted a trio that did not march at all. I found it by accident. I played our old reel, Kettle Row, at half its speed, and there was a second tune hiding inside it, calm and broad. It was like finding a walking stick inside an umbrella.\n\nSo the town's march will carry the town's reel in its middle. I doubt whether anyone will notice. The tinsmiths might."],["The Reel of Three Corners","At midsummer the Emberwick Town Band plays for dancing on the green from the back of a hay cart. The favourite is the Reel of Three Corners, danced in sets of three, with the fiddle and flute leading and the brass let loose on the last time through.\n\nDancers always push a reel faster. The band's art is to give way slowly. This year it was Posy Larkin, our eleven-year-old drummer, who held us steady when I did not.\n\nI wish my new march had gone as well. At its first rehearsal the cornets cracked on the top note in bar twelve, every time. Mrs. Ottery, our bass, pointed out that I had given her one note for sixteen bars. She said she would fall asleep and then off the kerb. I have gone home to write it again."],["Writing Out the Parts","A march for fourteen players must be written out fourteen times, and not in the same key. A cornet's written note sounds one step lower than it looks, so its part is set a step higher than the tune really is. One slip of the pen puts a horn wrong for the whole march.\n\nI have mended what the first rehearsal of the Emberwick march showed up. The tune now sits a note lower, to spare the cornets. The bass has a walking part that strides down the scale while the tune climbs. The euphonium has a melody of its own, running underneath.\n\nI copied by lamplight for eleven nights while Mrs. Bunce ruled the lines. On the last night I found the second horn, at bar twenty, playing in another march altogether. It had sounded perfectly well in my head."],["The Rival from Netherby","Mr. Septimus Crake, bandmaster of Netherby, wrote to say he had heard that Emberwick was giving up his town's Quickstep after sixty years, and that he was wounded. A second letter followed. He would bring his band to our Charter Day, so that we might be compared.\n\nSo the Emberwick Town Band has been practising the countermarch, which is how a band turns round in a street without stopping. The front rank wheels about and marches back through the ranks behind, and each rank follows in turn, playing all the while.\n\nWe tried it in Mill Lane. The trombones need a yard of clear air in front of them. The bass drummer cannot see over his drum. There were two collisions and a dented horn. I, Horatio Bunce, have composed a march. I had forgotten we must also walk."],["Rehearsal in the Rain","A week before Charter Day we took the new march up Emberwick High Street in the rain, to see whether it would hold. Posy Larkin gave her three knocks and a rest on the side drum. The cornets came in with the ferryman's five notes. People opened their doors.\n\nAt a hundred and eight paces a minute, the hill did not beat us. For the first time in sixty years the band reached the Market Hall with breath to spare.\n\nOne fault remained. We arrived twelve bars before the march did, and stood playing at the steps like guests who will not leave. A street is not a fixed length of music. So I have borrowed the old signal: two double taps on the bass drum mean finish at the next full close. I, the composer, find that the drum has the last word."],["The Emberwick March","On Charter Day the Netherby band played their old Quickstep down the High Street, very smartly, and then the Emberwick Town Band played the new march up it.\n\nPosy Larkin's three knocks sounded alone in the quiet. Then four cornets turned the corner with five notes, and Job Hale the ferryman, standing on the kerb, looked behind him to see who was whistling. At the trio the tinsmiths of Kettle Row began to nod. By the second time through, the children behind us were marching at exactly a hundred and eight.\n\nTwo double taps, and we finished on the top step of the Market Hall. Mr. Crake of Netherby shook my hand and asked for a copy of the parts. The mayor asked what it was called. It is in the band book now, between the borrowed march and the reels, under its own name."]],[["A Plank and Two Chairs","The Bridge at Little Wend is a play for five players and any stage at all. You will need two chairs, set well apart, and a plank laid between them. That is the bridge. The left chair is the village of Upper Wend, the right chair is Nether Wend, and the floor is a cold brown stream.\n\nScene one. Dusk. Pim the tinker comes in pushing a handcart hung with pans. She stops at the bridge. Three planks are rotten through and the handrail has gone. On each bank stands a painted board, and each board faces the other village. Both say the same thing: MEND YOUR BRIDGE.\n\nPim reads one, walks round, reads the other. She tests a plank with her boot and it gives like wet bread. So she sits down on her cart, lights a stub of candle, and waits for somebody to come and explain."],["The Widow’s Side","Scene two of The Bridge at Little Wend. The stage is two chairs with a broken plank between them, and the left chair is the village of Upper Wend. Pim the tinker sits by her handcart in the last of the light.\n\nEnter, from the left, the Widow Sorrel Agnew with a jar of honey and a look of long practice. The bridge belongs to Nether Wend. So does the mending of it, and so does the blame. Upper Wend has walked four miles round by the ford for forty years and can walk forty more. “All this over a bridge,” says Pim. “Over a kettle,” says the widow, and will say no more tonight.\n\nShe offers a bed in the bee-shed instead, which is warm and hums. The player should carry the candle off slowly, so that the far chair is left in the dark, listening."],["The Miller’s Side","Scene three of The Bridge at Little Wend. Morning. Two chairs and a broken plank; the right-hand chair is the village of Nether Wend, where the mill is.\n\nPim the tinker cannot cross the bridge, so she crosses the stream. The player takes off her boots, hangs them round her neck and walks under the plank. On a small stage this is the best joke in the play. Do not hurry it.\n\nOn the far bank Garrick Ould the miller is waiting, floury to the eyebrows, with his goat Bishop on a string. He has watched the whole crossing and offered no hand. The bridge, he says, is Upper Wend’s to mend. Upper Wend called his mother a thief over a copper kettle and has never said sorry. Then he notices the cart of pans across the water and asks, more quietly, whether she can fix a sieve."],["Forty Years of Kettle","Scene four of The Bridge at Little Wend. The two villages are two chairs, with a broken plank bridge between. Pim the tinker has spent a week mending pots on both banks.\n\nNow the Widow Agnew sits in the left chair and Miller Ould in the right, both facing the audience. Pim stands in the stream between them. They tell her the story in turns, a sentence each, and it is very nearly the same story.\n\nForty years ago a copper kettle was lent across the bridge for a wedding breakfast. It never came back. The widow says the Oulds borrowed it and kept it. The miller says his mother sent it home and the Agnews hid it, for the pleasure of a grievance. After that nobody would mend a plank the other side might walk on. Pim asks who was married. Neither of them can remember."],["One Nail","Scene five of The Bridge at Little Wend. Two chairs are two quarrelling villages; the plank between them is a bridge nobody has mended in forty years. Early morning. Pim the tinker is alone with her handcart.\n\nShe has had enough of wading. She announces to the empty banks that she is not mending anybody’s bridge. She is laying one plank, only the one, so a cart can cross, and then she is going. She finds a board. She has a hammer. She has, after turning out every pan on the cart, no nails at all.\n\nA boy has been watching from behind the left-hand chair. This is Jory, the widow’s grandson, nine years old and bound by oath never to help Nether Wend. He considers the matter. A tinker is not Nether Wend. He empties his pocket into her hand: eleven nails, borrowed from the bee-shed, still warm."],["The Other Pocket","Scene six of The Bridge at Little Wend. The stage is two chairs and a plank: two villages that do not speak, and their broken bridge. Pim the tinker and young Jory of Upper Wend have nailed down one board and run out of wood.\n\nFrom behind the right-hand chair comes a plank, walking. Under it is Wren, the miller’s niece, who has dragged it from the mill woodpile and would like it known that she found it lying about. She is the same height as Jory. They have waved across the water all their lives and never once stood close enough to see the colour of each other’s eyes.\n\nThey meet in the middle of the bridge. Pim keeps hammering and pretends not to look. The children shake hands, very carefully, like ambassadors. Then Wren says, “You’re holding that nail wrong,” and the peace is properly begun."],["Caught in the Middle","Scene seven of The Bridge at Little Wend. Two chairs, two villages, forty years of quarrel; and on the plank between them Pim the tinker and two children, Jory and Wren, mending without leave.\n\nThe Widow Agnew enters left. Miller Ould enters right. They arrive at the same moment, which should look like an accident. Each orders the work stopped. Each then leans in to look at it. The widow says those are Agnew nails in an Ould plank. The miller says it is an Ould plank being ruined by Agnew nails.\n\nPim offers to pull the nails out again and leave everything as it was. Neither will allow it, since that would be giving something back. They are still arguing over how to un-mend a bridge when the sky rumbles. Behind the scenes, shake a sheet of tin. The children look up. The elders do not."],["The Night the Wend Rose","Scene eight of The Bridge at Little Wend. Night, and a storm. For rain, roll dried peas in a sieve; for thunder, shake a tin sheet. The two chairs are the villages of Upper and Nether Wend, the plank is their half-mended bridge, and the stream beneath is rising.\n\nMiller Ould comes out with a lantern, calling for his goat. Bishop has slipped his string. A bleat answers from the middle of the bridge. The goat may be played by a horned head on a stick, or by a patient child. He has walked out as far as the new planks go and will not walk back.\n\nThe miller starts across and stops. On his side three boards are still missing, and the water is loud in the gap. He stands there with his lantern, and across the stream a second lantern comes out of the dark."],["A Rope Across","Scene nine of The Bridge at Little Wend. Storm. The plank between the two chairs is a half-mended bridge over a flooding stream, and in the middle of it stands the miller’s goat, Bishop, stuck. Miller Ould is on the right bank. On the left, with a lantern, is the Widow Agnew, his enemy of forty years.\n\nShe says nothing. She puts down the lantern, unwinds the rope she uses for hauling bee-skeps, and throws one end over the water. He catches it at the second try.\n\nWidow and miller pull the rope taut between them for a handrail. The goat, given a rail to walk beside, strolls across as if it had been his own idea. He goes to the widow’s side and begins on her cabbages. She watches him eat a whole row and does not say a word."],["What Was in the Loft","Scene ten of The Bridge at Little Wend. The morning after the flood. Two chairs for two villages, a plank bridge between; we are at the right-hand chair, in Nether Wend, where the mill has had the stream through its ground floor.\n\nMiller Ould and his niece Wren are carrying wet things up to the loft. A stepladder will do for the loft. Wren climbs, moves a row of meal sacks, and stops. She comes down with something round wrapped in a cloth. It is a copper kettle with a dent in one side.\n\nInside is a slip of paper in the hand of the miller’s mother. “Agnews’ kettle. To go back Tuesday, with thanks, and sorry for the dent.” The miller sits down on the bottom step. Forty years of Tuesdays have gone by. Pim the tinker, at the door, begins quietly polishing a pan."],["The Kettle Goes Home","Scene eleven of The Bridge at Little Wend. Two chairs are two villages that have not spoken for forty years, all over a borrowed kettle. The kettle has turned up in the miller’s own loft.\n\nMiller Ould takes off his boots and wades across with it held high, as Pim the tinker did on her first morning. The Widow Agnew waits at the left-hand chair. He hands it over. She turns it round. “There’s a dent,” she says. “There’s a note about the dent,” says he, and gives her that too. She reads it twice, fills the kettle and sets it on to boil.\n\nNow the bridge. The children, Jory and Wren, call for the last planks, and here the audience must help. The boards start at the back of the hall and are passed forward over every head, hand to hand, until they reach the stage."],["Pim Crosses Over","The last scene of The Bridge at Little Wend. The plank between the two chairs is whole. Upper Wend and Nether Wend stand one at each end of their bridge, and nobody quite likes to go first.\n\nSo Pim the tinker goes. She wheels her handcart over with every pan ringing, the first dry crossing in forty years. They ask her to stay, and she says pots do not mend themselves in other places. They ask her to name the bridge. She leaves that to the children, who have already decided. Jory and Wren hang up a new painted board, one board, facing the road: TUESDAY BRIDGE.\n\nThe widow pours tea from the copper kettle for the miller, the cast and, where the hall allows, the front row. Bishop the goat crosses twice more for the pleasure of it. Leave the plank in place until the audience has gone home."]],[["The Stool Behind the Curtain","Emberwick, the Corn Hall, first week of April. Opening night. I am Wynn Aldous, new prompter to the Hollow Lantern Company, and this is my diary of the season.\n\nA prompter sits on a stool behind the left-hand curtain with the play open on her knee and one candle. When an actor loses a line she gives it, softly enough that the hall cannot hear and loudly enough that the actor can. Our play is The Salt Merchant’s Daughter. Our book has a marbled cover and Mrs. Quillfeather’s pencil in every margin.\n\nTonight I gave seven prompts: four to Tam Birdwhistle, who learns his part by hoping, two to Mrs. Quillfeather, and one to the carpenter, who has a single line. None to old Mr. Pell. He has played the Merchant for thirty years, and he looked at my stool as one looks at a spare umbrella."],["The Causeway","The marsh road to Reedham, mid-April. I am the prompter of the Hollow Lantern Company, and today I lost the end of the play.\n\nOur wagon met a cart of reeds on the causeway, and Gooseberry, who is a horse of firm opinions, would not back up. The wagon tilted. One hamper, two masks and the prompt book went into the ditch. Barley Nock, our carpenter, fished them out with a rake. We dried the book page by page along a hawthorn hedge, like washing.\n\nAt dusk I counted the pages. The last one is gone: the Merchant’s closing speech and everything after it. I asked the company how the play ends. Mr. Pell said, “Grandly.” Mrs. Quillfeather said there was a ledger in it. Tam thought somebody forgave somebody. Nobody can give me the words, and we play Reedham tomorrow."],["Nine Minutes of Salt","Reedham, a barn, late April. I prompt for the Hollow Lantern Company, and since we lost the last page of our prompt book on the marsh road, the play has had no written ending.\n\nTonight Mr. Pell made one up. As the Salt Merchant he stepped forward and spoke for nine minutes by my watch. He covered the nobility of salt, the ingratitude of daughters, the price of herring, and a storm at sea that I am almost sure belongs to another play. The audience sat as people sit through weather. Then he forgave everybody and bowed.\n\nI wrote down what I could catch in the back of the book, under the heading Ending, Reedham. Prompts given: five, none of them for the ending. A prompter cannot prompt what was never written. I sat on my stool with my mouth shut and felt like a lamp with no oil."],["What Saltmere Wanted","Saltmere on the Grey Coast, May. Wynn Aldous, prompter, Hollow Lantern Company. We are still playing The Salt Merchant’s Daughter without its last page, which went missing on the marsh road.\n\nMrs. Quillfeather had cut Mr. Pell’s new closing speech to two minutes. He took it bravely. But Saltmere is a town of fishing families, and when the Merchant began to decide his daughter’s future, a woman at the back called out, “Let the girl keep the boat!” Others agreed. A gull, which had come in through the net loft, appeared to agree as well.\n\nMrs. Quillfeather, as the Daughter, looked at the hall, looked at Mr. Pell, and said, “Father, I shall keep the boat.” It is not in any version. They cheered. I have written it in the margin with the date. Prompts: four, and one whisper to the gull, which did not take it."],["Fog at Gullhaven","Gullhaven, on the Grey Coast, late May. I am the Hollow Lantern Company’s prompter, keeping a book with its last page missing and its margins filling up.\n\nThe sea fog came into the hall with the audience and stayed. From my stool I could not see the far side of the stage, and Tam Birdwhistle could not hear me, so I gave his line twice, the second time rather loudly. A small girl in the front row repeated it for him, louder still. He thanked her as “madam” and went on. She prompted him three more times and was right twice.\n\nThe ending tonight kept the boat, kept two minutes of Mr. Pell, and gained a joke of Tam’s about a herring that I do not think can be stopped now. That makes three endings in the back of the book. Prompts: six of mine, four of hers."],["Rehearsal in a Hayfield","The fell road, Midsummer Day. Wynn Aldous, prompter to the Hollow Lantern Company, writing on an upturned bucket while the horse grazes.\n\nWe stopped in a hayfield to settle the ending of The Salt Merchant’s Daughter, the true one having been lost in April. I read out every version from my margins. Mr. Pell wants his speech back at full length. Tam wants the herring. Dorrie Finch, who keeps the wardrobe, wants whichever is shortest, because the Merchant’s coat is hot. They argued until the shadows moved.\n\nThen Mrs. Quillfeather asked what I thought. Nobody has asked the prompter anything before. I said the best nights were the ones where the Merchant said least. Mr. Pell gave me a long look and then, to my surprise, a humbug from his pocket. We have agreed on nothing, but I am to keep writing down what happens."],["Mr. Pell Dries","Thistledown, in the Northern Fells, July. I am the prompter of the Hollow Lantern Company. We played tonight in a shearing shed that smelled of wool and held every soul for six miles.\n\nMr. Osric Pell has acted the Salt Merchant for thirty years and has never taken a prompt from anyone. Tonight, in the second act, in the middle of the scene with the ledger, he stopped. It was not a pause. I know his pauses. His hand went to his waistcoat, and his eyes went, very slightly, to the left-hand curtain.\n\nI gave him the line: “And every grain of it accounted for.” He took it as if he had only been drawing breath, and the shed never knew. Afterwards he walked past my stool without a word and set a humbug on the open book. Prompts tonight: three. I have underlined one of them."],["Rain at Cold Ashby","Cold Ashby, high in the Fells, August. Wynn Aldous, prompter, Hollow Lantern Company. The hall roof here is more of a suggestion.\n\nIt rained onto my stool all through the last act, and by the curtain the back pages of the prompt book had run blue. Those pages hold every ending we have tried since the true one was lost: Reedham, Saltmere, Gullhaven and the rest. I sat up by one candle at the inn and copied them out fair before they faded.\n\nCopying is a kind of listening. Laid side by side, the endings grew shorter as the summer went on, and better. And I saw the one we have not dared. The Merchant says nothing at all. He looks at his daughter, closes the ledger, and puts it into her hands. I wrote that down too, small, at the foot of the page."],["The Silent Ending","Barleyford in the Lowlands, harvest fair, September. I prompt for the Hollow Lantern Company. Four hundred people came tonight, on benches, barrels and each other.\n\nIn the morning I showed Mrs. Quillfeather the ending I had written small at the foot of a page: no closing speech, only the Salt Merchant handing his ledger to his daughter. She read it and said, “Tonight.” Mr. Pell said he was being robbed of forty lines in his own lifetime. But he did it. He looked at her, shut the book, and held it out. She took it.\n\nFour hundred people were quiet for as long as it takes to count ten. Then the benches came up like a wave. Mr. Pell stood in the noise with his hand on his heart, astonished. Coming off, he told me gruffly that we would keep it one more night, purely to be certain."],["A Schoolmaster Remembers","Mallow Green, in the Lowlands, late September. Wynn Aldous, prompter to the Hollow Lantern Company, on a quiet night: two prompts, both to Tam.\n\nFor a fortnight The Salt Merchant’s Daughter has ended in silence, with the Merchant handing over his ledger. The proper last page was lost in April and nobody misses it. After the play an old schoolmaster came round to the wagon. He had seen our piece thirty years ago, he said, and it did not end like that. There was a long speech, and a wedding.\n\nMr. Pell said there was certainly never a wedding. Mrs. Quillfeather said weddings need extra costumes. Dorrie Finch, of the wardrobe, said, “Over my hamper.” The schoolmaster went away shaking his head. But I lay awake. A prompter’s whole trade is the words as written, and I have spent a season helping to lose them."],["Under the Wagon Seat","The river road home, October. I am the prompter of the Hollow Lantern Company, and I have the missing page in my hand.\n\nThe wagon seat split this morning, and when Barley Nock lifted the board to mend it, there it lay: the last leaf of the prompt book, perfectly dry. It never went into the marsh ditch with the rest. It must have slipped down a crack when the wagon tipped in April, and it has ridden under us all season.\n\nWe read it aloud by lantern. The schoolmaster at Mallow Green was right. The Merchant speaks forty-one lines on duty and thrift, and then there is a wedding to a cousin nobody has met. Nobody said anything for a while. Then Mr. Pell cleared his throat. “I expect I was magnificent in it,” he said, “but I should not care to learn it again.”"],["The Last Night","Emberwick, the Corn Hall, the last Saturday of October. Wynn Aldous, prompter to the Hollow Lantern Company, closing the book on the season.\n\nWe put it to a vote in the dressing room: the ending as first written, found last week under the wagon seat, or the one we made on the road. Five hands went up for ours, Mr. Pell’s first. So tonight the Daughter kept the boat, Tam had his herring, and the Salt Merchant gave up his ledger without a word. Emberwick stood. I gave no prompts at all, the whole evening, for the first time since April.\n\nAfterwards I pasted the old page into the back, under the heading As First Written, and copied our ending fair at the front. Mrs. Quillfeather has asked me back for next year. Mr. Pell left a paper bag on my stool. Humbugs, a full quarter pound."]],[["Two Hampers and a Trunk","Emberwick, April, the week before the tour. I am Dorrie Finch, wardrobe-keeper to the Hollow Lantern Company, and these are my notes, kept so that whoever has the hampers after me will know what is in them and why.\n\nWe have two wicker hampers and a tin trunk. They hold thirty-one costumes for five players, nine masks, a box of moustaches and one crown. Everything must fold, and everything must survive a wet wagon. A costume that cannot be sat on has no place in a touring company.\n\nMrs. Quillfeather wants a new gown for our last night in October, when she plays the Lady of the River. There is no money for cloth. So I shall beg one piece from every town we visit and see what a season makes. Mrs. Tansy the haberdasher has started me off: a yard of blue ribbon, the good sort."],["The Fox’s Ear","Reedham, in the marsh, April. Dorrie Finch, wardrobe-keeper, Hollow Lantern Company. Our wagon tipped on the causeway and the fox mask went into a ditch, so today’s notes are about the fox.\n\nOur masks are paper and paste laid over a clay face, layer on layer, then painted. They weigh almost nothing. They also drink water like a hen. The fox came out with one ear folded flat and his whiskers, which are broom bristles, pointing every way. I have built the ear up again in strips, but in marsh air paste takes three days to dry. He hangs from the wagon hoop meanwhile, looking over the reeds with great interest.\n\nA weaver here gave me half a yard of reed-green linen. It is for the River Lady’s gown, which I am piecing from one scrap out of every town on our tour. That makes two pieces."],["A Coat with Seven Pockets","Saltmere, on the Grey Coast, May. I keep the wardrobe for the Hollow Lantern Company, and the largest thing in it is Mr. Pell’s coat.\n\nFirst, the gown I am making from one scrap begged in every town. A sailmaker on the quay has given me a square of old canvas, brown as strong tea. It will make the hem, where a river is muddiest.\n\nMr. Pell wears the coat as the Salt Merchant. It is plum velvet, older than I am, and I let the seams out a finger’s width every spring without either of us mentioning it. It has seven pockets. One is for his spectacles, one for humbugs, one for the letter he reads in the second act, one for a spare letter, and three he will not explain. An actor’s pockets are his own business. The wardrobe’s business is that nothing rattles."],["A Ghost Made of Muslin","Gullhaven, Grey Coast, late May. Dorrie Finch, wardrobe-keeper to the Hollow Lantern Company, with notes on the ghost.\n\nOur ghost is eight yards of muslin hung from a willow hoop, with Tam Birdwhistle underneath holding the hoop above his head. In a dry hall it floats beautifully. In a Gullhaven sea fog the muslin took up the damp, hung like a wet sheet, and showed the audience a clear outline of Tam’s elbows. Then he trod on the hem. The ghost sat down. I am shortening it by a hand and sewing dried peas into the edge, so it swings clear of his feet.\n\nFor the River Lady’s gown, which I am making from a piece of every town we play, a netmender gave me a hank of fine twine. It will lace the back. That is four pieces now, and no one of them agrees with another."],["Eleven Seconds","The fell road, June. I am Dorrie Finch, who keeps the hampers for the Hollow Lantern Company. A farm we passed gave me a twist of grey wool for the gown I am piecing town by town, which makes five scraps. But today’s notes concern the quick change.\n\nIn our comedy Tam plays a farmer, the farmer’s aunt, a constable and a bishop, sometimes within the same minute. He changes behind a clothes-horse, with me. The rules are these. Hooks, never buttons. Each costume goes on over the last, like the skins of an onion. The hat is handed over last and put on while walking.\n\nOur best time, farmer to aunt, is eleven seconds. Our worst produced a bishop in a bonnet. The audience at Gullhaven liked him so much that he has kept the bonnet in, and now I must find a spare."],["The Crown and the Pie Tin","Thistledown, Northern Fells, July. Dorrie Finch, wardrobe-keeper, Hollow Lantern Company. Notes on the crown, which is no more.\n\nIt was pasteboard covered in gold paper, and it had lasted six seasons. Last night the shearing-shed roof dripped on it for an hour. By morning it was a soft grey ring that would not have crowned a cabbage. We play the king tomorrow. So I borrowed a pie tin from the inn, cut points round the rim with the carpenter’s shears, bent them up and rubbed the whole with a rag. From the third row it looks finer than the old one. From the first row it says Thistledown Arms on the inside.\n\nThe innkeeper’s wife would not take the tin back. She gave me a horn button besides, for the River Lady’s gown that I am making from a scrap of every town. Six pieces, then, and a button."],["The Moon Cracks","Cold Ashby, in the Fells, August. I keep the wardrobe of the Hollow Lantern Company. The schoolmistress here has given a lace cuff for the gown I am piecing from every town, the seventh scrap. But today belongs to the Moon.\n\nShe is the oldest mask in the trunk, round and white with closed eyes, made before I was born by a wardrobe-keeper whose name is pencilled inside: A. Marrin. Last night the cold got into her. This morning there was a crack from the left eye to the chin. I laid a strip of linen along the back, pasted it, and held her by the inn fire until my arm ached.\n\nThe mend shows as a thin curved line beside the mouth. But it gives her the look of someone about to smile, and I am leaving it. Under A. Marrin I have pencilled: mended, D. Finch."],["Washing Day at Barleyford","Barleyford, in the Lowlands, September. Dorrie Finch, wardrobe-keeper to the Hollow Lantern Company. It is harvest weather, and thirty-one costumes have been worn in it.\n\nSo today everything that can be washed went into the river Ember, and everything that cannot was hung in the wind and spoken to firmly. Velvet is brushed, never wetted. Stockings are darned before washing, not after, or the holes travel. By noon the hedge behind the fairground was dressed as a king, a bishop, a ghost and a farmer’s aunt. A goat ate most of a ruff. I made a new one from a flour bag, and it sits better than the old.\n\nFor the River Lady’s gown, which I am building from one scrap of each town on our tour, a harvester’s wife plaited me a band of wheat straw, yellow as butter. Eight pieces. Soon I must stop collecting and start cutting."],["Nothing Matches","Mallow Green, in the Lowlands, September. I am Dorrie Finch, wardrobe-keeper of the Hollow Lantern Company, and today I laid out the River Lady’s gown on a barn floor.\n\nAll season I have begged one scrap from every town: blue ribbon, reed-green linen, sailcloth, net twine, grey wool, a horn button, a lace cuff, a plait of straw, and from Mallow Green a strip of red flannel. Spread on the boards they looked like the inside of a magpie’s nest. I sat down among them and very nearly cried.\n\nBarley Nock came in for a hammer and stood looking. He said the Ember does not match either. It is brown in the marsh, green under the willows and grey where it meets the sea, and nobody calls it a bad river. So I have laid the pieces in the order we travelled, hem to collar, and begun to cut."],["The Villain’s Moustache","The river road home, October. Dorrie Finch, wardrobe-keeper, Hollow Lantern Company. A short note on moustaches, written with a pricked thumb.\n\nWe keep six in a tin box. The best, the villain’s, is black horsehair knotted on gauze. The hair came from the tail of Gooseberry, our wagon horse, who has never forgiven it. A moustache is stuck on with gum, and gum does not care for soup. In the supper scene the villain’s moustache let go and swung like a gate while he threatened the heroine. He finished the speech holding it on with one finger, as if deep in thought. I have told him to do that every night.\n\nThe pricked thumb is from the River Lady’s gown, pieced from a scrap of every town on our tour. It is sewn as far as the waist. I work at it on the wagon, between ruts."],["The Fitting","An inn on the river road, October. I am Dorrie Finch, wardrobe-keeper to the Hollow Lantern Company, and tonight Mrs. Quillfeather tried on the gown.\n\nIt is the River Lady’s, for our last night, and I have made it from a scrap begged in each town of the tour. She stood on a bench while I went round her with a mouthful of pins. The hem dips on the left. One sleeve is a thumb longer than the other. She touched the pieces one at a time and named them: Reedham, Saltmere, Gullhaven, the fell farm, all the way to the red flannel of Mallow Green.\n\nThen she touched the front, where it gapes. It wants five fastenings and I have one horn button. The scrap bag is empty. I told her I would think of something, in the voice of a person who has not."],["What the Gown Was Made Of","Emberwick, the Corn Hall, the last Saturday of October. Dorrie Finch, wardrobe-keeper of the Hollow Lantern Company, with the last note of the season.\n\nAt noon the River Lady’s gown, made of one scrap from every town we played, still lacked four buttons. At one o’clock Mr. Pell knocked on the wardrobe door with a brass button off his own coat. Tam brought one of bone. Barley’s was wooden, whittled that morning. The prompter gave a small pearl one from her glove. With Thistledown’s horn button that makes five. I sewed them on in the order they came.\n\nMrs. Quillfeather wore it tonight. Under the lamps the patches ran together like water over stones, and the uneven hem looked meant. I have folded it into the trunk with a list pinned inside, saying where every piece came from. It lies next to the Moon, who appears to approve."]],[["The Bill","A comedy in one act. The scene is the parlour of the Sleeping Goose, an inn at Mallow Green: one table with a long cloth, one door, one window.\n\nMrs. Hobb, the landlady, has written out a bill for one week’s board. It is for Mr. Quince, the lodger in the back room, who has ink on his cuffs and nothing in his pockets. Hearing her on the stairs, he dives under the table. In comes a stranger in a black coat. Mr. Quince, certain this is a bailiff, answers his questions from beneath the cloth in the character of the table, and declares that no Quince has ever lived here.\n\nThe stranger is a piano tuner who has the wrong village. He leaves, shaken. Mrs. Hobb lifts the cloth and lays the bill on Mr. Quince’s knee. “No hurry,” she says. “I can count.” Curtain."],["Mr. Quince’s Rich Uncle","A comedy in one act, set in the parlour of the Sleeping Goose inn at Mallow Green. Mr. Quince, the lodger, now owes Mrs. Hobb for two weeks.\n\nHe announces that his wealthy uncle is arriving to settle everything. He goes out. A moment later the uncle comes in: Mr. Quince’s height, Mr. Quince’s boots, and a grey beard hooked over the ears. Mrs. Hobb is charmed. She would so like to see uncle and nephew together. So Mr. Quince runs out as one and in as the other, faster and faster, changing hats in the passage, until he enters as the nephew still wearing the beard.\n\nPip the potboy saves him by carrying the beard past the window on a broom. “There goes Uncle,” says Mr. Quince faintly. Mrs. Hobb has laid two dinners. She puts both on the bill."],["The Goose Has the Whistle","A comedy in one act. The scene: the parlour of the Sleeping Goose at Mallow Green, where Mr. Quince the lodger owes three weeks and Duchess the goose goes where she likes.\n\nConstable Dabney calls about a complaint of noise. He sets his whistle on the table, and Duchess picks it up in her beak and will not let go. After that every honk comes out as a shrill blast, and at every blast half the village runs in to see who is being arrested. The constable offers a reward. Mr. Quince chases the goose round the table, out of the window and back.\n\nMrs. Hobb holds out a crust. Duchess drops the whistle into her palm like a lady returning a glove. The reward goes to Mrs. Hobb. Mr. Quince asks whether it might come off his bill, and is told that it might not."],["One Room, Twice Let","A comedy in one act, in the parlour of the Sleeping Goose inn at Mallow Green. Mr. Quince owes Mrs. Hobb four weeks for the back room, and has had an idea.\n\nHe lets the room for the night to a traveller in buttons. An hour later he lets it again to a lady who lectures on thrift. There is one door. Mr. Quince spends the evening passing one guest out by the window as the other comes in by the passage, and serving the same cup of tea to each. At last they meet nose to nose in the doorway.\n\nThey are brother and sister, who quarrelled over a sideboard. They fall on each other’s necks, forgive everything, and go off arm in arm to her house without paying anybody. Mr. Quince sleeps in the parlour chair. Mrs. Hobb adds the chair to the bill."],["The Wrong Trunk","A comedy in one act. The scene is the parlour of the Sleeping Goose at Mallow Green. Mr. Quince, the lodger, is five weeks behind with his board.\n\nThe carrier delivers a trunk marked with a large Q. Mr. Quince opens it and finds four dozen silver spoons. He concludes that a relation has died and done the decent thing. He orders supper for the whole village and promises Pip the potboy a pony. In the middle of the pudding a small furious man arrives. This is Mr. Quill, a traveller in cutlery, and he wants his samples.\n\nConstable Dabney counts the spoons. One is missing. It is found in the nest of Duchess the goose, who gives it up with regret. The trunk leaves. The supper stays, on the bill, in Mrs. Hobb’s neatest hand. Pip is still asking about the pony as the curtain falls."],["Soup for Seven","A comedy in one act, set in the parlour of the Sleeping Goose inn at Mallow Green, where Mr. Quince the lodger owes six weeks.\n\nMrs. Hobb must go to her sister’s. She leaves Mr. Quince in charge for one evening, with a pot of soup for three and a week struck off his bill if nothing goes wrong. Seven members of a walking club arrive. Mr. Quince adds a jug of water for each extra guest and goes on stirring. By the time he ladles it out, the soup is clear enough to read through. He serves it as a broth in the Lowland style, celebrated for its lightness.\n\nThe walkers say they have never felt less weighed down. Mrs. Hobb returns to find them asking for the recipe. She strikes off the week. Then Mr. Quince, bowing, drops the tureen, and she writes it back on."],["The Constable’s Trousers","A comedy in one act. The scene: the parlour of the Sleeping Goose at Mallow Green. Mr. Quince the lodger owes seven weeks, but tonight’s trouble, for once, belongs to someone else.\n\nConstable Dabney has fallen into the duck pond while pursuing Duchess the goose on a point of law. His trousers are drying at the parlour fire, and he sits behind the table wrapped in the cloth. Enter the magistrate’s wife, come for tea. The constable cannot stand to greet her. Mr. Quince explains that the constable has taken a vow. Pip the potboy passes cups across so that nobody need move. All goes well until Duchess walks out of the door with the trousers.\n\nThe constable goes home after dark in Mrs. Hobb’s second-best curtain. Mr. Quince remarks four times that none of this was his doing. Mrs. Hobb agrees, and charges him nothing."],["The Ghost on the Back Stairs","A comedy in one act, in the parlour of the Sleeping Goose inn at Mallow Green. Mr. Quince, the lodger, owes eight weeks. Mrs. Hobb, it turns out, owes the brewery rather more.\n\nMr. Crimp, the brewer’s agent, comes to look the inn over with a view to taking it. Mr. Quince reasons that a new landlord would expect to be paid. So he wraps himself in a bedsheet and moans on the back stairs. Pip the potboy, who is fond of Mrs. Hobb, has had the same thought and the other sheet. The two ghosts meet on the landing, shriek, and fall into the parlour in a heap.\n\nMr. Crimp is delighted. A haunted inn, he says, is worth a third more, and he writes it in his book. He will be back. Mrs. Hobb looks at her two ghosts, then goes to iron the sheets."],["The Inspector of Inns","A comedy in one act. The scene is the parlour of the Sleeping Goose at Mallow Green, where Mr. Quince the lodger is nine weeks in arrears.\n\nWord comes that the county Inspector of Inns is travelling in disguise. That afternoon a pedlar of bootlaces walks in. Everybody knows at once. Mrs. Hobb brings the good plates. Constable Dabney salutes. Mr. Quince gives up his chair, waits at table with a napkin on his arm, and laughs at the pedlar’s only joke three times. The pedlar eats three dinners and is shown to the best bed.\n\nNobody has noticed the quiet woman in the corner, who had bread and cheese and fed her crusts to the goose. On her way out she leaves a card on the table. Mrs. Hobb reads it aloud: “Service uneven. Soup thin. Goose excellent company.” The pedlar is heard snoring overhead."],["A Letter for Mr. Quince","A comedy in one act, set in the parlour of the Sleeping Goose inn at Mallow Green. Mr. Quince has lodged in the back room ten weeks and paid for none of them.\n\nThe post brings him a letter, the first he has ever had here. The parlour decides it is a legacy. Mrs. Hobb pours him the good tea. Constable Dabney calls him sir. Mr. Quince will not open it; he says a sealed letter may hold anything, and an open one only what is in it. At last Pip the potboy reads it out.\n\nA company of travelling players has seen the sample he sent. They will be in Mallow Green in two weeks and wish to see the rest. Nobody in the parlour knows what the rest might be. Mr. Quince takes the letter upstairs. Through the ceiling comes the sound of a pen."],["Going, Going","A comedy in one act. The scene: the parlour of the Sleeping Goose at Mallow Green. Mr. Quince the lodger owes Mrs. Hobb eleven weeks, and Mrs. Hobb’s own debt to the brewery has come due.\n\nMr. Crimp, the brewer’s agent, holds an auction of the inn’s goods in the parlour. Up goes the table. Mr. Quince, who cannot bear to see it leave, bids. Up go the chairs, the good plates, the second-best curtain. He bids on each, against the constable, at one point against himself. Duchess the goose is put up, and he buys her too.\n\nWhen the hammer stops, everything is still in the room and Mr. Quince owns all of it without a penny to his name. Mr. Crimp gives him until Saturday to produce the money. Mr. Quince sits down on his own table. Mrs. Hobb, for once, cannot think what to say."],["The Bill Is Settled","The last comedy, in one act. The parlour of the Sleeping Goose at Mallow Green, on Saturday. Mr. Quince the lodger owes Mrs. Hobb twelve weeks, and owes Mr. Crimp, the brewer’s agent, the price of every stick in the room.\n\nMr. Crimp arrives at noon. So does a wagon of travelling players. Mr. Quince comes down from the back room with a bundle of paper: eleven comedies in one act, each set in this parlour, with a landlady, a constable, a potboy and a goose. He has been writing down each week’s disasters. The players’ manager reads the first page standing, and laughs at the table. She buys the lot. It covers the brewery and the bill, with enough over for Pip’s pony.\n\nMrs. Hobb frames the receipt. Mr. Quince says the set wants a twelfth, sits down at the parlour table, and begins. Curtain."]],[["The Fire Is Lit","Emberwick, the loft above Bunce’s bakery in Cinder Lane, the first of January. I am Ambrose Thimblewick, alchemist, and today I began the Great Work as my late aunt’s book describes it.\n\nThe method is simple, which is what makes it hard. Into a crucible no bigger than a teacup I have put one grey pebble from the river Ember, three drops of rainwater that fell by moonlight, and a pinch of patience, which the book says cannot be bought and must be supplied by the operator. The lid goes on. The crucible sits on a brick ledge over a small fire.\n\nFor twelve months the fire must never go out and the lid must never be lifted. On the last night of the year the pebble will have ripened into gold. I have a sack of coal, a stool and a clock. It is now twenty past nine."],["The Draught","Cinder Lane, Emberwick, February. I am Ambrose Thimblewick, keeping a small fire under a small crucible in the loft above the bakery. If the fire burns for a year without a break, the pebble inside is to become gold.\n\nAt three this morning a draught came under the door like a cold cat and laid the flame flat. I stopped the gap with my second-best stockings and walked up and down until it stood again. Mrs. Bunce, the baker, sleeps beneath my floor until four. She came up to ask whether I was keeping a horse.\n\nI explained the Work. She listened with her arms folded. “A year,” she said. “I cannot get this lane to wait while a loaf cools.” But she looked at the fire for some time before she went down, and at seven there was a warm roll on the top stair."],["A Cat on the Lid","The loft over Bunce’s bakery, Emberwick, March. Ambrose Thimblewick, alchemist. Third month of keeping one fire alight under one covered crucible, in the hope of gold at the year’s end.\n\nThe bakery cat, Crumpet, has found the warmest place in Cinder Lane, and it is the lid of my crucible. She arrives at dusk, turns round twice and settles on it like a tea cosy. I consulted the book. It forbids lifting the lid, letting the fire die, and “undue haste”. It says nothing on the subject of cats.\n\nI have reasoned as follows. A cat is warm, steady and in no hurry, which is more than can be said for me. If patience is truly an ingredient, she is adding to the supply. I let her stay. She has now watched more hours of the Great Work than I have, and with a better temper."],["One Red Coal","Emberwick, Cinder Lane, April. I am Ambrose Thimblewick. Since New Year I have kept a fire under a crucible in my loft, for if it goes out before twelve months are up there will be no gold. Last night I fell asleep.\n\nI woke at dawn with my cheek on the table and my heart in my boots. The fire was alive. Kneeling at the hearth was Tilly Bunce, the baker’s daughter, who is ten. She had come up for the cat and found one red coal and an alchemist snoring. She was feeding it wood shavings, one at a time, the way one feeds a sick sparrow.\n\nI thanked her. She asked sensible questions about the pebble. Then she said she could watch from four until six, after school, if I wanted to sleep like other people. The Work has an assistant."],["The Roster","The loft above the bakery, Cinder Lane, Emberwick, May. Ambrose Thimblewick, alchemist, fifth month of tending a fire that must burn a full year beneath a closed crucible.\n\nI caught a cold and could not get up the stairs with the coal. Tilly Bunce, my assistant, went down the lane knocking on doors. By evening there was a sheet of paper pinned to mine, ruled in pencil. Mr. Okey the cobbler has Tuesday nights, since he does not sleep anyway. Hetty Flagg the mason has Sunday mornings. Mrs. Bunce has put herself down for “before the ovens”. There are seven names, and until this week I knew two of them.\n\nI lay in bed listening to strangers creak across my floor and put coal on my fire, more carefully than I do. It is an odd feeling for a solitary man. I cannot say I dislike it."],["A Knot in the String","Emberwick, the loft in Cinder Lane, midsummer. I am Ambrose Thimblewick, and for six months I and a roster of neighbours have kept a fire going under a small crucible. The lid stays on until the year’s end. Then, the book promises, gold.\n\nHalf-way is the dangerous time. This afternoon the loft was hot as an oven, and I found my hand resting on the lid. One look, I thought. The book would never know. Tilly Bunce, who is ten and watches from four until six, removed my hand, fetched string, and tied the lid down with a great many knots.\n\nShe says it is easier to be patient when there is a knot. That may be the truest sentence in alchemy. Half the lane sat on my stairs this evening, where there is a breeze, eating Mrs. Bunce’s buns. Nobody touched the string."],["What the Cobbler Would Buy","Cinder Lane, Emberwick, July. Ambrose Thimblewick, alchemist. Seven months ago I shut a river pebble in a crucible over a slow fire; in five more it is to be gold. My neighbours share the watching.\n\nOn Tuesday night Mr. Okey the cobbler asked what I would do with the gold. I opened my mouth and found little in it. Mend the roof. New stockings. After that I could not think. I had been so busy wanting gold that I had never asked it any questions.\n\nSo I asked him. He said that he would put a window in the east wall of his shop, to see the morning while he worked. Hetty Flagg wants a donkey. Mrs. Bunce wants one day in bed. Tilly wants the pebble, if it stays a pebble. I have written this on the back of the roster. It is a better list than mine."],["Gold in a Fortnight","The loft above Bunce’s bakery, Emberwick, August. I am Ambrose Thimblewick. With the help of Cinder Lane I am keeping a year-long fire under a crucible, as an old book directs, to turn a pebble into gold. Eight months are done.\n\nA man came through town selling a pamphlet called Gold in a Fortnight. I bought one. I am not proud of it. It promised that no patience whatever was required, and I read it three times on the stairs.\n\nMrs. Bunce took it from me and read the first page. “Fortnight bread is poor bread,” she said. Mr. Okey the cobbler said fortnight boots were worse. Tilly asked who would have Tuesdays if I went over to the quick way. Then she used the pamphlet to light the evening coal. It burned very well, which is the only thing I can report in its favour."],["A Crack in the Ledge","Emberwick, Cinder Lane, September. Ambrose Thimblewick, alchemist, in the ninth month of a twelve-month fire. Over it sits a closed crucible that must not be opened, cooled or, I now add, dropped.\n\nThis morning the brick ledge under the crucible cracked across with a noise like a walnut. The whole thing tipped. I caught it in the tongs and stood there. I could not put it down, for there was nothing level to put it on, and I could not take it off the heat.\n\nTilly, the baker’s girl, ran for Hetty Flagg the mason, who came with a trowel and rebuilt the ledge under my arms while I held the crucible steady. It took two hours. Mr. Okey fed me a bun in pieces. When I set it down at last, the lane, which had gathered on the stairs, gave a cheer. My arms still shake as I write."],["Thursday Suppers","The loft over the bakery, Cinder Lane, Emberwick, October. I am Ambrose Thimblewick. A small crucible has sat over a fire here since New Year, watched in turns by me and my neighbours, and at the year’s end it is to hold gold.\n\nSomewhere in the summer the watching turned into company. On Thursdays now everyone comes up with a dish. Mrs. Bunce brings a pie. Mr. Okey brings his fiddle and plays the two tunes he knows. There are fourteen names on the roster. Tonight old Mr. Larch, who has not left his room in two winters, climbed all my stairs to take his first watch.\n\nHe sat by the fire with Crumpet the cat on his knee and said it was the warmest he had been in years. I used to have one chair. I counted nine, and none of them is mine."],["Doubts at Three O’Clock","Cinder Lane, Emberwick, November. Ambrose Thimblewick, alchemist. Eleven months of fire under a covered crucible, with one to go. I shall write down a thing I have not said aloud.\n\nI think it is a pebble. I think it was a pebble in January and will be a pebble at New Year, a warm one. I sat with that at three this morning and waited to feel wretched. Instead I found I was worrying about something else. When the lid comes off, the watching stops. Nobody will need Tuesdays.\n\nTilly Bunce came up at four. I told her my doubt, since she is my assistant. She thought about it while she put coal on. “We will see in a month,” she said. “And somebody has to keep the fire in anyway. It is winter.” I had not looked at it from that side."],["The Lid Comes Off","Emberwick, the loft above Bunce’s bakery, the last night of December. I am Ambrose Thimblewick. For a year, with all of Cinder Lane taking turns, I have kept a fire under a closed crucible that was to turn a river pebble into gold.\n\nAt midnight the stairs were full. Tilly cut the string. I lifted the lid with the tongs, and inside was a grey pebble. It was the same pebble, perhaps a little rounder. Nobody spoke. Then Mr. Okey asked whose turn it was on Tuesday, and Mrs. Bunce said hers, and Hetty Flagg said the ledge wanted looking at in spring.\n\nSo the roster stays on the door. Tilly took the pebble home in her mitten, and tells me it was still warm at breakfast. Result of the Great Work: no gold. One fire, still in. Fourteen names, and a year ago I knew two."]],[["A Challenge by Carrier","Saltmere, on the Grey Coast, January. To Dr. Ignatius Vole, at Cold Ashby in the Fells. Sir, I have read your piece in the Almanack, which states that all things are at bottom a Spirit. They are not.\n\nI am Perpetua Marrow, alchemist, and I have twenty-three jars to prove that all things are at bottom a Salt. A Salt is what remains when the fuss has gone out of a thing. The old books say the perfect Salt will turn lead to gold. I say it will at least keep.\n\nI enclose a jar by Mr. Tobble’s cart: Salt of Sea Fog. I gathered it by leaving a clean plate on the harbour wall through a grey morning. It is white, fine, and faintly mournful. Examine it honestly, then tell me a smell could do as much. I propose we settle the matter at Emberwick, at Midwinter."],["Dr. Vole Replies","Cold Ashby, in the Northern Fells, February. To Miss Perpetua Marrow, at Saltmere. Madam, your jar of Salt of Sea Fog arrived. So did a week of damp and melancholy in my study, which lifted only when my housekeeper, Mrs. Grice, carried the thing out to the scullery. She says she rather likes it. I do not consult Mrs. Grice on philosophy.\n\nA Salt is what is left when everything interesting has departed. A Spirit, in our trade, is whatever rises: a steam, a scent, a breath. I am Ignatius Vole, and I have caught forty of them. I send one by the carrier, Spirit of First Thaw. I took it by holding a bottle under the eaves at the moment the roof sighed. Uncork it near a window.\n\nEmberwick at Midwinter will suit. Bring your jars. I shall bring the truth, which travels lighter."],["Salt of Old Arguments","Saltmere, Grey Coast, March. To Dr. Vole, at Cold Ashby, from Perpetua Marrow, who holds that everything is at bottom a Salt, against his claim that everything is a Spirit.\n\nSir, I uncorked your Spirit of First Thaw. For one afternoon my kitchen smelled of wet slate and snowdrops, and the cat sat in the window as if expecting someone. It was not unpleasant. I withdraw that. It was a smell, and by supper it had gone, which is my whole point about Spirits.\n\nBy Mr. Tobble’s cart I send something that lasts: Salt of Old Arguments. I scraped it from the inside of a teapot that has stood between two sisters for forty years. It is brown and bitter to the nose, with a warmth afterwards that I cannot account for. Keep the lid on in company. We meet at Emberwick at Midwinter, and I advise you to prepare."],["Spirit of Kettle","Cold Ashby, Northern Fells, April. To Miss Marrow, at Saltmere, from Ignatius Vole, who maintains against her that all things are Spirits and not Salts.\n\nMadam, I set your Salt of Old Arguments on my desk with the lid off, purely as a trial. Within the hour I had written to my brother at Barleyford, to whom I have not written in nine years. I mention this as a result and not as a kindness. He has replied. That is beside the point.\n\nThe carrier brings you Spirit of Kettle. It is the breath a kettle lets go just before it sings, and it took me eleven mornings to catch, since one must not be seen waiting. One question, for my records only. Is your coast as grey as reported? I have never seen the sea. Mrs. Grice, my housekeeper, says it is mostly weather."],["The Empty Jar","Saltmere, on the Grey Coast, May. To Dr. Vole, at Cold Ashby in the Fells, from Perpetua Marrow, keeper of Salts, to the keeper of Spirits.\n\nSir, the coast is grey the way a pigeon is grey, which is to say every colour if you stand still. Your Spirit of Kettle made my cold workroom feel as though someone had just left it to fetch cake. I send Salt of Tears, the laughing kind only, gathered at the fish market while Jem Scully told the story of the eel. I do not bottle the sorrowful kind. It does not keep, and ought not to.\n\nSince you ask for records, here is one. My shelf holds twenty-three full jars and one empty. Its label reads Salt of Contentment. I have looked for that Salt for nineteen years and never found a grain. You need not reply to this part."],["A House with One Chair","Cold Ashby, in the Fells, June. To Miss Marrow, at Saltmere on the Grey Coast, from Dr. Ignatius Vole. We are in dispute, she for Salts, I for Spirits.\n\nMadam, I shall reply to that part. I also keep an empty vessel. It is a small blue bottle marked Spirit of Good Company. I have held it open at fairs, at weddings and once at a sheep sale, and caught nothing. Mrs. Grice, my housekeeper, says it will never rise in a house with one chair. I did not ask her opinion.\n\nI enclose Spirit of Hayfield at Noon, taken yesterday from the top of a wagon. Do not open it if you have work to finish. Your laughing Salt is on my windowsill, and I find I look at it. An empty jar is an honest thing to own. I had thought I was the only one."],["An Accident in the Cart","Saltmere, Grey Coast, July. To Dr. Vole, at Cold Ashby, from Perpetua Marrow. He says all things are Spirits; I say Salts; this letter concerns a mishap between the two.\n\nSir, your bottle of Hayfield at Noon cracked on the fell road. Mr. Tobble the carrier sneezed the whole way down and reports that he felt six years old as far as the marsh. That is not my complaint. The Spirit ran into the box where I was returning your empty jars, and one of them still had a pinch of my Sea Fog Salt in the bottom.\n\nWhen I lifted the lid there was a cloud in the jar. It was the size of a plum, and it was raining. It rained for a minute on the bottom of the jar and then cleared. Neither a Salt nor a Spirit does that alone. I am very much annoyed."],["A Cloud the Size of a Bun","Cold Ashby, Northern Fells, August. To Miss Marrow, at Saltmere, from Ignatius Vole, her opponent in the question of Salts against Spirits.\n\nMadam, I did not believe your cloud. I therefore dropped one pinch of your Salt of Sea Fog into an open bottle of my Spirit of Kettle, to prove you mistaken. A cloud the size of a bun formed above the inkstand. It rained on my notes for a full minute. Then it produced a rainbow no wider than my thumb, and went. I was extremely cross about the notes. I have since done it four more times. Mrs. Grice brings a saucer now.\n\nI must set down, as a man of method, what I observed. Your Salt alone sits in its jar. My Spirit alone drifts off. Together they made weather. I do not yet know what this does to my position. Kindly send more fog."],["A Comfortable Silence","Saltmere, on the Grey Coast, September. To Dr. Vole at Cold Ashby, from Perpetua Marrow. For nine months we have argued by carrier’s cart whether the world is made of Salts, as I say, or of Spirits, as he does.\n\nSir, more fog is enclosed. I too have been trying things. Last night I let three breaths of your Spirit of First Thaw into my jar of Old Arguments. The jar grew warm in my hands. Then a silence came into the kitchen, the comfortable kind that sits between people who have finished quarrelling. It lasted until supper.\n\nI have a proposal, and I make it with dignity. When we meet at Emberwick at Midwinter, let it be less a contest than a joint examination. We might each bring everything. I would bring the empty jar. You need not say yes at once. Say it by the next cart."],["A Second Chair","Cold Ashby, in the Fells, October. To Miss Marrow, at Saltmere, from Dr. Ignatius Vole. Our dispute, Salts against Spirits, was to be settled at Emberwick at Midwinter; she now proposes a joint examination.\n\nMadam, yes. I say it with every reluctance I can muster, which I find is not much. I shall bring all forty Spirits and the blue bottle that has never held anything. I have not left Cold Ashby in eleven years. Mr. Tobble will take me down the fell road in his cart, and has promised to drive as if I were eggs.\n\nI have also bought a second chair. It is for the study, in case of visitors. Mrs. Grice had to sit down in it. I enclose Spirit of Woodsmoke in October, for your own journey. Open it when the coast road turns inland. It is good company on a hill."],["How You Will Know Me","Saltmere, Grey Coast, November. To Dr. Vole, at Cold Ashby in the Northern Fells, from Perpetua Marrow, alchemist. Once his opponent on the matter of Salts and Spirits; now, it seems, his colleague in an examination at Emberwick next month.\n\nSir, I have packed twenty-three jars in straw and the empty one, marked Contentment, in my own shawl. We are to meet at the Kettle and Quill tea-room on Lantern Row at noon on Midwinter Day. You will know me by a brown coat and by the fact that I shall be arguing with the carrier about the straw.\n\nOne thing more, and then I shall seal this quickly. The day Mr. Tobble’s cart comes has become the best day in my month. I have checked my shelf, and I own no Salt that accounts for it. Do not answer that. Bring gloves. Emberwick is cold by the river."],["Marrow and Vole","Emberwick, Midwinter night. To Mrs. Grice, at Cold Ashby, from Dr. Ignatius Vole, who came here to settle with Miss Marrow of Saltmere whether the world is made of Salts or of Spirits.\n\nMrs. Grice, I shall not be home on Thursday. We met at noon and argued for one hour, to be polite. Then she set her empty jar on the table, the one marked Contentment, and I set my blue bottle beside it, and we forgot them and talked. At the third pot of tea she looked down. There were pale grains in the bottom of her jar. My bottle had gone misty. Neither of us will say whose came first.\n\nThe old books promise gold. We have taken a shop on Lantern Row instead. The sign is ordered: Marrow and Vole, Salts and Spirits. Please send the second chair by Mr. Tobble. You were right about it."]],[["Note the First","Barleyford, in the Lowlands, October. I am Fennel Ashdown, apprentice to Master Hob Tolliver, alchemist, whose workshop stands behind the wheelwright’s. Master’s eyes are not what they were, so from today I keep his notes.\n\nAttempt the first of this book. Master set a brass bowl of rainwater on the south sill to gather a whole afternoon of sunlight, and had me stir it with a goose feather each time the clock struck. By evening the water was yellow. Yellow is not gold. Master says the difference between the two is the entire profession.\n\nMrs. Weld the dyer stopped and sniffed. She dipped a corner of her apron, looked at it, and bought the bowlful for two pennies. By market day there was wool on her stall the colour of marigolds. Master put the pennies in an old jar and had me label it. The label says Toward Gold."],["The Shine Off a Penny","Barleyford, November. Fennel Ashdown, apprentice, writing the notes of Master Hob Tolliver, alchemist. He has sought gold for forty years, and has me write down each attempt, since he can no longer read his own hand.\n\nAttempt the second. Master reasoned that a new penny shines like gold, and that the shine must go somewhere. We sat a new penny among old ones in a covered dish and coaxed it. I was not told how one coaxes. Master hummed. After nine days we had a grey paste and a dull penny.\n\nIt was not gold. But I wiped my hands on the door latch, and the latch came up bright as a trumpet. Mr. Tapp from the inn took the whole pot for his horse brasses. You can now see the Barley Mow’s cart-horse coming from the far end of the Lowlands. Three pennies in the jar marked Toward Gold."],["Frost and Candlelight","Barleyford, in the Lowlands, December. I am Fennel Ashdown, and I keep the workshop notes for my master, Hob Tolliver, an alchemist whose sight is failing and whose hopes are not.\n\nAttempt the third. Gold, says Master, is cold to the touch and warm to the eye. So we gathered frost from the water butt before dawn and held it near a candle for as long as it would stand. It would not stand long. What ran off into the dish dried to a thin clear skin, cracked all over like ice on a puddle.\n\nNot gold. But Jessamy Marl the potter had come to borrow a sieve, and she turned the dish to the window. Her new jugs have that same crackle on them. She paid in pennies and a jug, and both stand on the shelf beside the jar marked Toward Gold."],["A Pop and Some Soot","Barleyford, January. Fennel Ashdown, apprentice to Master Hob Tolliver the alchemist, keeping his notes because his eyes cannot. We work behind the wheelwright’s, and the wheelwright has asked that today’s attempt at gold be recorded as his complaint also.\n\nAttempt the fourth. Master wished to catch the gold that shows in a cat’s eyes by firelight. The cat was willing. The crucible was not. There was a pop, no louder than a cork, a smell of burnt toast, and a quantity of soot that went up to the rafters and came down on everything, the cat included. She has not been back.\n\nNo gold. I swept for an hour. But soot on a wet thumb writes, and writes very black. Miss Larkin the schoolmistress has taken a jarful for ink, and says her pupils’ blots have never been so handsome. This note is written in it."],["The Intentions of Snowdrops","Barleyford, in the Lowlands, February. I am Fennel Ashdown. My master, Hob Tolliver, is an alchemist with weak eyes and a strong will, and I write up his attempts at gold, of which this is the fifth.\n\nMaster holds that a snowdrop under the snow is a small white thing intending to be elsewhere, and that such intention might persuade lead to become gold. We filled a tub with earth from where the snowdrops grow thickest and kept it by the stove, speaking to it encouragingly. He did the speaking. In three weeks it was dark and crumbly and smelled of April.\n\nThe lead was unmoved. But old Mr. Hollin put his hand in the tub and would not take it out. He has dug it in along his south wall. He pays in pennies now and peas later, and the jar marked Toward Gold is a third full."],["March Wind in a Bottle","Barleyford, March. Fennel Ashdown, apprentice, keeping the notebook of Master Hob Tolliver, alchemist. He seeks gold; I write down what he finds in its place.\n\nAttempt the sixth. Gold does not tarnish, Master says, because nothing can get a grip on it. Wind is the same. So we held bottles open on the hill above the mill in a March gale, corked them quickly, and shook them all the way home to keep the wind lively. Poured out, it was a grey gum that set in the dish while we were still looking at it. We have not got the spoon back.\n\nIt is not gold. It is, however, extremely firm. Mr. Felloe the wheelwright next door put a thumbful on a split spoke, and has since driven that wheel to Emberwick and back. He says he forgives us the soot of January. Six pennies and a pardon."],["Rainbows in Eggshells","Barleyford, in the Lowlands, April. I am Fennel Ashdown, apprentice. Master Hob Tolliver has spent forty years trying to make gold, and since his eyes dimmed I have kept the record. This is attempt the seventh.\n\nEveryone knows what lies at a rainbow’s foot. Master proposed to save the walk. We set out forty eggshells in the yard to catch April rain while a rainbow stood over the mill, then stirred the shellfuls together with a goose feather and a great deal of hope. Overnight it thickened into a slippery grey cake.\n\nThere was no gold in it. There was, when I washed my hands afterwards, a lather up to my elbows. Mrs. Dabb the laundress heard by noon. She says the cake gets the mud out of a ploughman’s shirt in half the rubbing, and has ordered one a month. Master is rather hurt to be good at soap."],["The Hum of a Hive","Barleyford, May. Fennel Ashdown, apprentice to Master Hob Tolliver, alchemist. He can no longer see to write, so I set down each attempt at gold and what came of it. This is the eighth.\n\nBees make gold every summer and are not secretive about it. Master thought the secret was in the hum. We stood a jar beside Mr. Hollin’s hive with its mouth toward the noise, then stopped it and carried it home. Left by the stove, the jar gave us one pale candle. I cannot explain the wick.\n\nIt is not gold, though when lit it is the right colour at the top. It burns with a low steady sound and will not gutter in a draught. Mrs. Orchard the midwife carries one in her lantern on night calls. She paid eight pennies into the jar marked Toward Gold, and asked for another before autumn."],["The Longest Day, Boiled Down","Barleyford, in the Lowlands, Midsummer. I am Fennel Ashdown, and I keep the notes of my master, Hob Tolliver, who has been failing to make gold since before I was born. Attempt the ninth.\n\nMaster’s reasoning: the longest day holds the most sun, and gold is sun that has learned to sit still. We put a pan in the yard at first light and left the day to settle in it until the last light went. What had gathered came down to a spoonful of thick amber, which we did not taste. That is the first rule of this workshop.\n\nNot gold. Sticky, though. Mr. Pippin of the orchard has painted a ring of it round each plum trunk, and the ants turn back like travellers at a toll gate. The jar marked Toward Gold is heavy now. I hear Master lift it when he thinks I am out."],["A Bead of Almost-Glass","Barleyford, July. Fennel Ashdown, apprentice, writing for Master Hob Tolliver, alchemist. This is the tenth attempt at gold in this notebook, and the first note I have not read back to him in full.\n\nWe set a pan of river sand on the sill to catch the flash of lightning. Afterwards there was one clear hard bead in it. Master held it to the window and asked me whether it was gold. He could not tell. I said no, Master. He nodded, and asked me to read him yesterday’s note, and then to read it again, slower.\n\nA pedlar of spectacles came by and looked at the bead. Almost glass, he said, but not quite. He told me of a spectacle-maker in Emberwick who could give my master his own handwriting back, and he named the price. I counted the jar marked Toward Gold. It is not half enough."],["The Cleanest Failure","Barleyford, in the Lowlands, August. I am Fennel Ashdown, apprentice to Hob Tolliver the alchemist, whose eyes are too dim to read his own notes. This is attempt the eleventh, and a private matter besides.\n\nThe attempt first. Master set an empty crucible in the wheat field to take up the colour of the crop. In the evening it held nothing. He called it the cleanest failure of his life and seemed almost pleased.\n\nThe private matter. I told Mrs. Weld the dyer what a pair of spectacles costs, and how far short our jar fell. I only told her. By Friday the potter, the innkeeper, the schoolmistress, the wheelwright, the laundress, the midwife, old Mr. Hollin and Mr. Pippin had each come by with something in a fist. The jar marked Toward Gold will not close. I walk to Emberwick tomorrow. Master thinks I am visiting an aunt."],["Gold, a Small Quantity","Barleyford, September, harvest week. Fennel Ashdown, apprentice, with the last note in this book. My master, Hob Tolliver, has tried all year to make gold. Each failure was of use to some neighbour, and the neighbours’ pennies went into a jar.\n\nI came back from Emberwick with what the jar bought: a pair of spectacles with gold rims. Half of Barleyford was in the yard. Master put them on and was quiet. Then he took this notebook and read it aloud, from the yellow water to the empty crucible, laughing in the right places. He looked at Mrs. Weld. He looked out at the wheat.\n\nThen he gave the book back and dictated. Attempt the twelfth. Gold obtained at last, a small quantity, worn on the nose. Method: eleven failures and one village. He says it need not be repeated. He has read this line himself, and approves it."]],[["The Heron Is Lit","Reedham, at the edge of the marsh, January. I am Barnaby Withers, distiller. My still-house stands on stilts over the water, and my still is a copper vessel with a long bent neck that we call the Heron.\n\nTo distil is to warm a thing gently until the best of it rises, and then to catch that best as it cools and comes down. I do not make strong drink. I distil months. The old books say that twelve of them, joined, give the Quintessence, which mends all ills and may turn tin to gold.\n\nJanuary went into the pot this morning: frost scraped from the boardwalk, the tick of ice in the reeds, one note from a robin. By dusk I had a thimbleful, clear as glass. My neighbour Abel Coot, the old eel-catcher, sniffed the cork. “Cold iron and peat smoke,” he said. “That is January.”"],["A Thin Month","The still-house at Reedham marsh, February. Barnaby Withers, distiller of months. I am drawing the essence of each month into a small bottle, twelve in all, to join at the year’s end into the Quintessence.\n\nFebruary is a thin month and gives little: rain on rope, grey light, a few hazel catkins. The second running gave nine drops. Abel Coot, my neighbour, came along the boardwalk to try it. His eyes show him only light and dark, but there is nothing amiss with his nose. “Wet doorstep,” he said. “And something just thinking of being green.”\n\nI say the second running because I spoiled the first. I was impatient and built the peat fire too high under the Heron, my copper still, and all that came over was the smell of a scorched kettle. I began again with my hands in my pockets, where they could not meddle."],["Wind in the Neck","Reedham, on the marsh, March. I am Barnaby Withers, and my trade is distilling months: one small bottle from each, drawn off through a copper still called the Heron. Twelve bottles joined should make the Quintessence. This is the third.\n\nMarch will not stay in the pot. I put in a gust from the east dyke, the slap of washing on a line and the mud beginning to wake, and each time I turned my back the lid lifted and the wind went out by the door. The Heron whistled through her neck like a boy calling a dog.\n\nIn the end I sat on the lid. What came down was cloudy and restless, and it will not lie still in the bottle. My neighbour, old Abel Coot, took one sniff and held on to his hat. I have put it on the shelf between February and a brick."],["Seven Kinds of Rain","The still-house on stilts, Reedham marsh, April. Barnaby Withers, distiller. Each month this year I draw the essence of the month into a bottle, hoping the twelve will make the Quintessence the old books promise.\n\nApril is mostly rain, but rain is not one thing. I set out seven cups and caught seven kinds: the fine sort that hangs, the sort that arrives sideways, the warm heavy drops before thunder, the sunlit sort the marsh children call a heron’s wedding, and three I have no names for. Into the Heron, my copper still, they went, with a cuckoo’s first call and the green that is on everything.\n\nThe bottle came out faintly green. I put one drop on a handkerchief and carried it to Abel Coot, the old eel-catcher, whose sight has nearly gone. He held it to his face. “Seven,” he said. I had not told him."],["Too Much May","Reedham, at the marsh edge, May. I am Barnaby Withers, and I distil months, one small bottle from each, through a long-necked copper still named the Heron. There will be twelve by the year’s end, and then I shall join them.\n\nEvery month has needed coaxing. May needed a bucket. I put in hawthorn blossom, the smell of the dyke bank in the sun and the noise of every bird at once, and the Heron ran like a gutter. I fetched a second, then a jug. The still-house smelled so strongly of May that the bees came in and would not be argued with.\n\nI kept one small bottle, as the method requires, and poured the rest off the boardwalk into the marsh, which seemed pleased. Abel Coot, my neighbour, did not need to come and sniff. He called across from his doorstep: “I have it from here, thank you.”"],["June in a Bottle","The still-house at Reedham marsh, June. Barnaby Withers, distiller of months. Five bottles stand on my shelf, and at the year’s end I mean to join twelve into the Quintessence. Today I drew off June.\n\nIt went into the Heron, my copper still, easily: warm boards, the south wind bringing hay from the Lowlands, eel-smoke, and the light that is still in the sky at ten. It came down slow and gold-coloured, the first that has had any colour.\n\nI took it next door to Abel Coot. He was an eel-catcher for sixty years. Now his sight has gone and his knees with it, and he has not been further than his doorstep since the winter. He held June under his nose and told me about a hayfield he worked in at fourteen, and a dog called Button. He kept the bottle a long while. I did not hurry him."],["Thunder Will Not Distil","Reedham marsh, July. I am Barnaby Withers. I keep a copper still called the Heron in a house on stilts, and with her I am bottling the essence of each month this year, for a purpose the old books call the Quintessence.\n\nI wanted the thunderstorm. It was the great event of July, purple from the coast to the Fells, and I had the pot ready with the lid off. Thunder will not distil. The noise went straight through the copper and out again, the lightning stayed outdoors, and the Heron sulked with her neck gone cold.\n\nWhat I did catch was the quarter of an hour before: the hush, the smell of hot dust and of rain not yet arrived, a single swift. It made six heavy drops. I thought it a poor showing until Abel Coot, my neighbour, sniffed the cork and went to shut his window."],["Abel Tests the Shelf","The still-house on stilts, Reedham, August. Barnaby Withers, distiller. I have eight small bottles now, one for each month since January, and four to go before I may join them into the Quintessence.\n\nToday I played a trick on my neighbour, Abel Coot, the old eel-catcher, who can no longer see the marsh he worked all his life. I shuffled the eight bottles and handed them to him unlabelled. He named every one. He had a story for each. February was his mother’s kitchen door. March was a kite. With June he was quiet, and smiled.\n\nThen I did a thing the method does not ask for. From each bottle I drew a few drops into a phial the size of a thimble, corked it, and set the eight phials in a drawer. In case of breakage, I told the Heron. She is only a still, and did not argue."],["Mist and Blackberries","Reedham, at the edge of the marsh, September. I am Barnaby Withers, who distils months through a copper still called the Heron. Nine months are now in bottles, and December will bring the joining of all twelve.\n\nSeptember is the easiest month I have had. It wants to be kept. I put in the morning mist off the dyke, spiders’ webs with the dew on, blackberries and the first evening cool enough for a fire. The Heron hardly needed the peat. What came down was the colour of weak tea and smelled like the inside of a basket.\n\nI filled the bottle, and a small phial besides, for I am keeping a second set and no longer pretend it is in case of breakage. Abel Coot, my old neighbour, had the phial uncorked by his chair all afternoon. He says it is like having the window open without the draught."],["Leaf Smoke","The still-house at Reedham marsh, October. Barnaby Withers, distiller of months. Ten bottles of twelve are filled, toward the Quintessence I mean to make at the year’s end.\n\nOctober was bonfire smoke, apples laid out in a loft and the geese coming over at dusk, calling. It distilled dark and slow. I should have enjoyed it more, but Abel Coot, my neighbour, has been poorly and keeps to his chair. He is the old eel-catcher who names every month by its smell. He sent word that he could not manage the boardwalk, and would I bring October to him.\n\nI did. I uncorked it by his elbow and said nothing. He turned his head toward the window, which was shut. “Geese,” he said. “Going over the east dyke. Late this year.” They were. I left the small bottle on his sill and walked back to the Heron in the dark."],["The Fog Month","Reedham, on the marsh, November. I am Barnaby Withers, and with a copper still called the Heron I am bottling the eleventh month of this year. One more, and I may join the twelve as the old books direct.\n\nNovember nearly beat me. Fog walks into a pot willingly, but three times it came out as plain water, smelling of nothing. I had been taking the fog for the month. The month was what people do about the fog: wet wool by a stove, lamp oil, toast, a door shut quickly. That distilled at once. Abel Coot, my neighbour, sniffed it from his chair and asked who was making toast.\n\nI have read the old books again. Twelve months joined, they say, give the Quintessence, cure for every ill. I note that not one of their authors appears to have lived on a marsh, or to have had a neighbour."],["Twelve Bottles","The still-house on stilts, Reedham marsh, Midwinter. Barnaby Withers, distiller. All year I have bottled the essence of each month, to join the twelve into the Quintessence.\n\nDecember came over this morning: peat smoke, orange peel, cold stars. Then I poured all twelve into the great flask. It went the colour of dyke water and smelled of a Tuesday with nothing in it. A whole year at once, I find, is only weather.\n\nBut there was the second set. I carried twelve phials, in a box with twelve holes, along the boardwalk to Abel Coot, the old eel-catcher, who can no longer see or walk his marsh. He felt along the row and drew the sixth cork. The room had hay in it. He laughed, and sat in June for an hour with the snow coming down outside. The Heron is cold tonight. In January I begin again, two sets."]],[["The Coal Tin","I am Odile Farrow, forty years schoolmistress of Emberwick and now nothing in particular. This winter I mean to sit at twelve hearths and collect a tale from each. My terms: one log from my basket for one tale. I also carry a lidded coal tin, to take a live ember on from each fire and lay it in the next, for luck.\n\nI began at the bakery, the oldest fire in town. Tobias Rudd told of Hesper the candlemaker, who settled here before there was a here. Snow fell for a week and she kept one wick burning in her window throughout. Lost carters steered by it, and stayed. Ember Wick, they called the place, and never got round to a better name.\n\nTobias dropped a coal in my tin. My own parlour grate has been cold since I retired. A fire wants somebody to talk to."],["The Cat’s Stone","Second hearth: Widow Tansley’s on Candle Row, where I carried the baker’s ember in my coal tin and a log of ash in my basket. She has the smallest grate in Emberwick and the largest cat.\n\nHer tale explains the cat. Long ago, she says, a grey cat called Smoke minded a poor family’s fire while they slept. When a coal rolled out, Smoke patted it back with one paw, quick as a wink, and never singed a whisker. The family woke each morning to a house still standing. In thanks they gave her the warmest stone of the hearth, for ever, and her kittens after her.\n\nThat is why no Emberwick cat may be moved from the hearthstone, not for a guest, not for the vicar. I sat on the stool. The cat watched me write it all down, and seemed to find it accurate."],["The River’s Loan","The third hearth is hardly a hearth at all: an iron stove in the ferryman’s hut, down where the Ember runs wide and brown. Corwin Dabb took my log, fed my travelling ember a twist of bark to keep it cheerful, and told me this.\n\nHis great-grandfather kept the same ferry. One flood night the river rose to the doorstep and carried off his lantern, lit, bobbing away like a duck. He shouted after it that he expected it back. People laughed at that for seven years.\n\nOn the seventh spring the lantern was found on the step, upright, still burning, with a little river-weed round the handle. The Ember borrows, Corwin says, but it is an honest river. He showed me the lantern. It is certainly old, and there is weed carved, or grown, about the handle. I did not ask which."],["The Hob Who Miscounted","Fourth hearth, with snow on the lane: the kitchen at Stonecross Mill, where Annis Poole grinds for half the Lowlands. I arrived floury to the knees with my coal tin warm under my shawl.\n\nEvery mill has a hob, Annis told me, and theirs lives in the flour bin and counts the sacks. He counts beautifully up to nineteen. The twentieth sack he always misses, however carefully the miller checks him, and so one sack in every score has no owner. It goes to whoever in the parish needs it most, and the hob is never corrected, for fear of hurting his feelings.\n\nI asked if she had seen him. She said no, but she has seen the books, and they have been one sack out in every twenty for two hundred years. As a teacher of arithmetic I ought to disapprove. I find I do not."],["The Thirteenth Sheep","For the fifth hearth I went up into the Northern Fells on the carrier’s cart, coal tin in my lap, to the bothy where Garrick Thwaite winters his flock. His fire is peat, and smells of old rain.\n\nGarrick keeps twelve ewes in the home fold. At dusk, he says, any shepherd on these fells who counts carefully will count thirteen. The extra one is made of mist. She stands a little apart and has no shadow. Leave her be, and on the night a lamb strays she will be gone from the fold, and by morning back again, with the lamb trotting behind her.\n\nWe went out at dusk and I counted. Twelve. Garrick said I had counted like a schoolmistress, which is to say too briskly. I tried again, slowly, and shall record only that I stopped at thirteen because I ran out of sheep."],["The Kettle That Sang","Midwinter night, and the sixth hearth is the great one at the Brass Kettle, wide enough to seat four. Half of Emberwick was in. I laid my carried ember in the fire, paid my log, and got my tale from the landlady, Oriel Finch, between pourings.\n\nThe inn is named for a kettle that hung here in her grandmother’s day. It sang as it boiled, as kettles do, but this one sang the weather. A high thin note meant frost. A rumbling one meant rain off the Grey Coast. Before snow it hummed like a contented cat, and farmers walked in from three parishes to listen.\n\nA tinker mended its lid one autumn and it never sang true again. It only whistled, like anybody’s. Leave well alone, says Oriel. The kettle still hangs by the chimney, and I took my fresh ember from beneath it."],["Boots the Marsh Kept","The seventh hearth lies out on the marsh, along a causeway of planks, in the reed-cutter’s cottage. Bryony Sedge burns dried reed-roots, which crackle like gossip. I laid in my carried ember, as I do at each house, and asked for a tale.\n\nEverybody who walks the marsh loses one boot to it sooner or later, she said. The mud takes it with a sound like a kiss. Nobody in her family has ever grudged this, because of what happens at midsummer. On the shortest night, every boot lost that year is found standing on the causeway in a row, cleaned, and stuffed with dry moss to keep its shape.\n\nNobody sees who does it. The frogs are suspected. Bryony’s mother once left out a saucer of milk in thanks and found it returned, washed. I lost no boot myself, though the marsh tried twice going home."],["A Knot for Enough","Eighth hearth: a driftwood fire at the Marlows’ cottage on the Grey Coast, burning blue and green from the salt. I had come nine miles with my coal tin, and old Peg Marlow said the ember in it looked seasick.\n\nShe mends nets, and her tale was of a knot. Her grandmother knew a way of tying the last mesh so that a net would hold exactly what a household needed and not a herring more. The extra fish slid through it like water. A neighbour scoffed and tied his own nets tight. He filled his boat so full it would not come over the bar, and had to throw half back.\n\nPeg showed me the knot three times. I can tie a bow and a reef, and I could not follow it. She said that was quite usual, and that it does not work on shopping baskets."],["The Giant’s Nightlight","The ninth hearth is one I know too well: the black stove in the Emberwick schoolroom, which I fed for forty years. Mr Ashby teaches there now. He let me in on a wet afternoon, and the children demanded to know my terms. A log for a tale, I said. They went into a huddle.\n\nTheir tale was made up on the spot, with arguments. A giant lived on the Northern Fells who was afraid of the dark. Every evening he reached up and turned the moon so its bright side faced his bed. Some nights he fell asleep before finishing, which is why the moon is so often left half turned.\n\nI asked if a tale invented on a Tuesday counts as folklore. A girl called Nettie said every tale was Tuesday once. I have written that inside the cover of my notebook, and paid the log."],["The Honest Nail","The tenth hearth roars. It is the forge on Anvil Yard, and Hale Orrin would not stop work for me, so I got my tale in pieces between hammer blows, holding my coal tin well back from the sparks.\n\nWhen the Ember bridge was built, he said, the smith of that day made ten thousand nails for it. One came out crooked. He was going to throw it back in the fire, but the nail spoke up and promised that if it were only given a place it would hold harder than any straight one. So he drove it in under the middle arch.\n\nIn the great flood every straight nail groaned. The crooked one held, and the bridge with it. Hale says nobody knows which nail it is, and that this is the point. He gave me an ember with tongs, like a man serving sugar."],["The Unfinished Book","The eleventh hearth is the reading-room fire at Emberwick Library, where the thaw was dripping past the windows. Mrs Larch, the librarian, took my log, shut the door on a draught, and told the last of my borrowed tales.\n\nThere was once a book on these shelves, she said, whose final page was blank. Readers complained. The book was sent back to its binder twice. At last an old shelver noticed that the page filled itself, a line at a time, whenever a reader sat by the fire afterwards and told somebody else what they had read. It only wanted the story passed on before it would end.\n\nThen Mrs Larch looked at my coal tin. Eleven hearths lit from one another, she said, and the collector’s own grate stone cold. She said it kindly. Librarians notice an unreturned item."],["My Own Hearth","The twelfth hearth is mine, in the parlour on Chapel Lane, cold since the day I retired. This evening, with the first snowdrops out by the step, I swept the grate, laid kindling, and tipped in the ember from my coal tin. It had passed through eleven fires, from the baker’s oven to the library, and it caught at once.\n\nI had told nobody. All the same there came a knock, and then another. Tobias Rudd with a loaf. Widow Tansley, without the cat. The ferryman, the miller, Bryony Sedge in marsh-stained boots, the smith, Mrs Larch, and half the schoolroom. Each one held out a log. Those were the terms, they said. A log for a tale.\n\nSo I told one. It was about a woman who carried a fire round a town all winter before she thought to bring it home. They said it would do."]],[["A Yard of Ribbon","My name is Ambrose Tull, pedlar of ribbons, buttons and thread, and my round takes me once a month up the Ember valley to the village of Twelvetrees. An old woman there, Granny Sorrel Wick, has never bought so much as a pin from me. Instead she struck a bargain: one yard of green ribbon each visit, in exchange for one part of a tale. Twelve parts, she said. I write them down as I get them.\n\nThe first part is short. Before it was Twelvetrees the village was called Nethercombe, and it stood under a bare hill. The old wood had been felled for ship timber in her great-grandmother’s time. After that the wind came through every door without knocking, smoke blew back down the chimneys, and the washing was frequently found in the next parish.\n\nShe took her ribbon and said to come back next month."],["Twelve Seeds","At Twelvetrees, for a second yard of green ribbon, Granny Sorrel Wick gave me the second part of her tale of the Green Children. She shelled peas throughout.\n\nIn windy Nethercombe, under its bare hill, lived Marra Linden, a charcoal-burner’s widow, which is a hard thing to be where there are no trees. One winter she mended her husband’s old coat and found twelve seeds in the lining. An acorn. An ash key. A hazelnut, a rowan berry dried hard, a holly stone, and seven more she could not name.\n\nShe had no use for them and no heart to throw them out. So at the turn of the year she carried them up the hill in her apron, planted them in a ring, and watered each one from the well with her only cup. Then she went home and forgot about them, more or less."],["May-Eve Knocking","I am a pedlar, and Granny Sorrel Wick of Twelvetrees pays me for green ribbon in instalments of story. Third visit, third part.\n\nOn May-eve, she said, somebody knocked at Marra Linden’s door, low down, the way a child knocks. Marra was the widow who had planted twelve seeds on the bare hill. On her step stood twelve children, barefoot, very polite, with hair the pale green of new leaves. They asked for nothing but a drink of water.\n\nShe had one cup. They passed it round, each taking a sip and saying thank you, and it did not run dry. At dusk they bowed and walked back up the hill in a line. Marra followed in the morning. Where she had planted her ring of seeds there stood twelve saplings, a hand high, and the grass between was trodden flat by small feet."],["Children by Day","Fourth part of the tale of the Green Children, as told to me, Ambrose Tull, by Granny Sorrel Wick, for the usual yard of ribbon.\n\nThe rule was soon plain, she said. From sunrise the twelve were children, running about the widow Marra’s cottage. At dusk they climbed the hill and stood as saplings until morning. Rain did not trouble them. They rather leaned into it.\n\nThey had no names, so Marra went by their ways. Oak was slow to answer and never wrong. Hazel was quick and wanted to know everything. Willow could not be kept out of the stream. Rowan wore a string of red beads nobody had given her, Birch tidied whatever stood still, and little Holly had sharp elbows and a kind heart. The other six she named as she learned them. It took the whole summer, Granny says, and no summer was better spent."],["The Village Makes Up Its Mind","Fifth visit to Twelvetrees, fifth yard of green ribbon, fifth part. Granny Sorrel Wick told it while darning.\n\nThe village of Nethercombe did not take at once to twelve green-haired children who lived with the widow Marra Linden by day and turned into saplings at night. The miller’s wife said they could not be natural, since they ate nothing but rain. The parson said he would think about it, and did, for months.\n\nThe children settled the matter themselves. Birch swept the street before breakfast. Willow walked up the dry gully, stamped twice, and found the spring the village had lost sixty years before. Hazel sat at the back of the school and did everyone’s sums. By harvest the miller’s wife was knitting twelve pairs of socks. They were never worn, the children disliking anything between their feet and the ground, but they are in the church chest still."],["Holly’s Winter","It was snowing when I reached Twelvetrees with my pack, and Granny Sorrel Wick made me sit by the fire for the sixth part.\n\nWhen the leaves fell that first autumn, she said, eleven of the Green Children yawned, climbed the hill, and did not come down again. The widow Marra Linden went up every day and found only bare saplings asleep in the cold. She had got used to a full house.\n\nBut one came. Holly, in a dark green coat that shone as if polished, knocked each morning all winter long. She carried the firewood, and when the wind came prowling she stood on the step with her sharp elbows out until it went round. It was a smaller wind already. Twelve saplings are not much of a wood, Granny says, but the wind had noticed them, and was beginning to mind its manners."],["A Year in a Season","Seventh part, seventh yard of ribbon. I am Ambrose Tull, pedlar, and I had the tale of the Green Children from Granny Sorrel Wick of Twelvetrees, who that day was planting beans and talked into the row.\n\nThe children grew, she said, as trees do when nobody is watching. They went up the hill one spring evening no higher than Marra Linden’s elbow. By midsummer Oak had to duck under her lintel. The saplings on the hill were head-high and had begun to touch fingers.\n\nOther things came with them. A wren nested in Hazel. Bluebells appeared, though nobody had planted any. The washing stayed on the line. Smoke went up the chimneys of Nethercombe instead of down, and people took to walking on the hill of an evening, for no reason they could give. Marra said the reason was shade. Nobody under forty had ever stood in any."],["Mr Crake’s Tape","Granny Sorrel Wick keeps count on her fingers: this was the eighth part she gave me, for the eighth yard of green ribbon, at Twelvetrees.\n\nOne autumn, when the Green Children’s wood stood taller than a haystack, a stranger rode into Nethercombe. He was a Mr Crake, timber-factor to the estate, a neat man with a measuring tape and a small black book. He went round each of the twelve trees, wrote down its girth, and raised his hat to the widow Marra Linden.\n\nThe hill, he explained, was timber-ground by an old deed. Whatever grew on it would be felled when it reached a useful size. He was sorry. He would return next harvest-end with men and axes. He was not a cruel man, Granny says. He only believed his book. The children stood very still while he measured them, as children will for a tailor."],["Twelve Godparents","I come to Twelvetrees once a month, and Granny Sorrel Wick trades me her tale for ribbon. This ninth part she told quickly, as though she liked it best.\n\nThe estate meant to fell the Green Children’s wood. It was Hazel, the quick one, who found the answer, in a law book she borrowed from the parson. By Lowland custom no axe may touch a tree that has a name and a living godparent.\n\nSo the village of Nethercombe held a christening. Twelve households climbed the hill. The miller’s wife stood godmother to Birch. The parson took Oak, having finally made up his mind. Each tree was sprinkled from the widow Marra’s one cup, named aloud, and tied round with a green ribbon so that nobody could say afterwards they had not known. That, Granny said, looking at my pack, is why it has to be green."],["Tea with the Factor","Tenth yard of ribbon, tenth part. I, Ambrose Tull, wrote it down at Granny Sorrel Wick’s table in Twelvetrees.\n\nAt harvest-end Mr Crake the timber-factor came back to Nethercombe with his black book. He left the men and axes at the foot of the hill. Twelve trees wore green ribbons. Twelve households stood beside them and recited the custom of godparents, more or less together.\n\nHe was asked in to tea at the widow Marra’s. The Green Children sat round the walls. Nobody spoke until Oak, who was slow, asked Mr Crake what tree he had climbed as a boy. A pear tree, he said, in his aunt’s yard. He was quiet for some time. Then he opened his book and wrote across the page: unsuitable for timber. Before he left he asked, rather shyly, if there were any seeds to spare. They gave him a pear pip."],["Fewer Knocks","Granny Sorrel Wick told me the eleventh part slowly, in her doorway at Twelvetrees, winding my eleventh yard of ribbon round her hand.\n\nAs the wood grew tall, she said, the Green Children came down the hill less often. Trees have a great deal to attend to. First they missed the wet days, then the weekdays. In the end they came only on May-eve, twelve tall people with grey-green hair, to knock low on Marra Linden’s door from habit and pass her one cup round.\n\nMarra grew old among them and was content. The last May-eve she was too stiff to rise, so they let themselves in. After that they came no more as people. But the wood crept down the hill, year by year, until it stood about her cottage on every side. No wind has got into the village since. That was when Nethercombe changed its name."],["The Rowan by the Well","The twelfth yard of green ribbon bought me the end of the tale, and Granny Sorrel Wick of Twelvetrees walked me through the village to tell it.\n\nEver since Marra Linden’s day, she said, each child born here has a tree planted and a godparent to stand by it, and all of them are called green children, whatever colour their hair. She stopped at the well and patted a rowan, old and leaning, with eleven of my ribbons on it. She tied on the twelfth. Hers, planted eighty-one years ago.\n\nThen she gave me an acorn. From Oak himself, she said. A pedlar has no ground of his own, so I planted it by the gate of Emberwick Library, where the librarian lets me water it. It is knee-high this spring. It has not knocked at any door yet. I keep a cup handy all the same."]],[["The Stranger’s Rope","I am Hester Quillfeather, newly appointed keeper of the parish records at Emberwick, and I was raised in a street with paving. The vicar has asked me to write down the harvest customs of the Lowland farms before they are forgotten. I began this morning at Longacre, where they were to cut the first barley.\n\nI had barely stepped through the gate when two reapers came up, smiling, and bound my wrists with a rope of twisted straw. This, I learn, is the Stranger’s Rope. Whoever first enters a harvest field without a tool in hand must pay a forfeit.\n\nMistress Bell Hathersage, who farms Longacre, set mine herself: one day’s honest work in the field, to be paid before the Lodger sits down to supper. I do not yet know who the Lodger is. I have written it down, and they let me keep the rope."],["Whetting the Blades","Second day at Longacre farm, where I am recording the harvest customs for the parish. Nobody may cut a stalk, I find, until the blades have been whetted in the proper manner.\n\nThe oldest reaper and the youngest stand face to face at the field gate. This year they are Jem Purvis, who is seventy-eight, and a boy named Colin, who is eleven and was trying not to grin. Each sharpens the other’s scythe. The old hand gives the young blade its edge and the young hand returns the favour, so that skill goes down the years and strength comes back up them. That is how Jem put it.\n\nThen the first swathe is cut in complete silence, the whole line moving together, so as not to wake the field. I asked what happens if the field wakes. Nobody knew. Nobody has ever been willing to find out."],["The Lark’s Acre","I am Hester Quillfeather, and I follow the reapers at Longacre with a notebook. On the third day the line of scythes stopped dead in the middle of the barley.\n\nA lark had gone up. Jem Purvis walked forward, parted the stalks, and found the nest. He pushed a hazel stick into the ground beside it and tied on a red rag. The reapers then cut round that spot in a wide ring, leaving an island of standing corn about the size of a kitchen table. This is called the Lark’s Acre, though it is nothing like an acre.\n\nThere were four such islands by evening. The grain in them is never harvested. It belongs to the lark, in payment for singing over the field all summer. Mistress Hathersage says the lark has never once sent a bill, which is more than can be said for the wheelwright."],["The Long Loaf","Noon in the harvest field at Longacre, where I am recording the Lowland customs. Dinner is carried out by the children in a covered basket, and the chief of it is the Long Loaf.\n\nIt is baked the length of a scythe blade, with a curve to it, and takes two to carry. No knife may touch it. A blade that has cut bread in the field, they say, grows soft and will want only bread afterwards. So the loaf is broken, passed hand to hand down the line, eldest first, each tearing a piece. With it go cheese, onions, and cold tea from a stone jug that has stood all morning in the river Ember.\n\nI was handed the loaf in my turn like anyone else. I pointed out that my forfeit of a day’s work was still unpaid. They said the loaf does not keep accounts."],["Eight Verses and a Hum","Behind the reapers at Longacre come the binders, tying the cut barley into sheaves, and the binders sing. I have been set to record the harvest customs, so I sat on the bank and attended closely.\n\nThere is one binding song and it has eight verses, because eight sheaves make a stook. A verse lasts as long as it takes a quick pair of hands to gather, twist and tie. The tune is plain and goes up at the end of each line, like a question from a child. By the eighth verse the binder has a stook’s worth at her feet and begins again.\n\nThen there is a ninth verse, which has no words. It is hummed, and it is for the weather. I asked why it has no words. Nan Purvis said the weather does not like to be told things outright. One hums, and hopes."],["Three Knots for Weather","A confession belongs in today’s notes. I am Hester Quillfeather, I record harvest customs, and I have meddled with one.\n\nAt Longacre, when the corn is cut but not yet carried, they hang weather knots on the field gate. Each is a wisp of straw tied in a loop, and each asks for one dry day. The rule is strict: never more than three. To ask for four is greedy, and greed fetches thunder.\n\nOn Tuesday evening there were three knots on the gate. The sky looked doubtful to me. I thought a fourth could do no harm, and I tied one, rather neatly. It rained for two days. The whole farm sat in the barn mending sacks and wondering aloud who could have done such a thing. Nobody looked at me. They did not look at me so carefully that I am certain they all know."],["The Water-Carrier’s Due","The children of Longacre are not idle at harvest. I have been recording the customs of the farm for the parish, and the smallest custom belongs to the smallest worker.\n\nOne child is chosen as water-carrier. This year it is Colin, aged eleven, who walks the field all day with a yoke and two cans, so that no reaper need leave the line. It is hot, dull and heavy work. For it he is owed two things by ancient right, and no farmer has ever dared refuse them.\n\nThe first is the earliest ripe apple in the orchard, whoever spots it. The second is the naming of the scarecrow. At the end of harvest the scarecrow is brought in from the field, and the water-carrier announces its name for the coming year. Last year’s was Lord Turnip. Colin says he has decided, and will not be bribed into telling."],["The Lodger","I now know who the Lodger is. I have wondered since my first day at Longacre, when I was bound with a straw rope and told my forfeit must be paid before the Lodger sits down to supper.\n\nThe Lodger is the last sheaf. When only one armful of barley stands in the last field, the reapers do not scythe it. Mistress Hathersage cuts it herself, with a small sickle, and binds it thicker than the rest. It is then dressed: an old hat, a waistcoat, a neckerchief. This year’s has a pipe.\n\nThe Lodger is carried home in state and lives in the farmhouse kitchen until spring, in the chair nearest the fire. Nobody may be short with it. Its grain is the first sown next year, so that the field, they say, remembers itself. It looks a comfortable sort of person. I still owe my day’s work."],["A Day’s Honest Work","Today I, Hester Quillfeather, keeper of parish records, paid my forfeit. I had owed Longacre farm one day’s honest work since the reapers caught me in their field with the Stranger’s Rope.\n\nIt was the last carrying day. I was given a pitchfork and shown how to lift a sheaf onto the wagon. I lifted perhaps two hundred. Roughly forty arrived where I aimed them. One landed on Jem Purvis, who is seventy-eight and said he had been hit by worse. My stooks, built in the morning, were found to be standing on their heads.\n\nNo one laughed until I did. At noon I was given first tear of the Long Loaf, which I am told is not custom at all, but manners. I have eleven blisters and have counted them twice. Mistress Hathersage inspected my hands, nodded, and said the debt was paid in full."],["The Lantern Load","The last wagon of the harvest left the field at Longacre this evening, and I walked behind it with my notebook. They call it the Lantern Load, because it must not start for home until dusk.\n\nA lantern is hung on the shafts. The horse wears ribbons, and the children ride on top with the Lodger, the dressed last sheaf, propped among them. The custom concerns the gates. Each gate on the way must be opened by someone who has opened no other gate that day, or the luck stays shut in the field.\n\nThere are five gates. Four were soon spoken for. At the farmyard gate everyone looked round and found they had all opened something since breakfast. I had not. I had climbed every stile all day on purpose, having read my own notes. I opened it. The cheer was quite unsuitable for a records office."],["The Gleaners’ Bell","With the fields at Longacre cleared, one harvest custom remains out of doors, and I went to record it. At eight this morning the church bell at Emberwick rang twelve slow strokes. That is the Gleaners’ Bell, and until it sounds no one may set foot on the stubble.\n\nThen the gate is opened, and in they go to gather the fallen ears: widows first, then children, then anyone at all. What they glean is theirs. A quick family may carry home flour for the winter.\n\nI noticed something not in any record. The evening before, Mistress Hathersage’s men had gone over the field with rakes, and they had raked remarkably badly. Whole handfuls lay about. Jem Purvis says a tidy field is a mean field, and the trick is to be careless in the right places. I gleaned a small bag myself. Nobody tied my wrists this time."],["The Lodger Sits Down","Harvest supper was held in the great barn at Longacre last night, and it closes my record of the customs. I came to this farm a stranger in town shoes, and was bound with a straw rope on the first day for it.\n\nThe tables ran the length of the threshing floor. Colin the water-carrier announced the scarecrow’s name, which is Sir Hesitant Crow. Then Mistress Hathersage called for the newest hand to carry in the Lodger. Everyone turned to look at me.\n\nSo I carried the last sheaf, hat, waistcoat, pipe and all, to its chair by the fire, and it sat down to supper. The straw rope has been plaited into its belt. I meant to write these customs down before they were forgotten. I had not expected to end up inside one. Next year I am to bring my own pitchfork."]],[["The Lamplighter’s Book","I am Jory Flint, lamplighter of Emberwick. I have forty-one lamps, a ladder, a pole, and a left knee that tells the weather. Every evening I walk the town at dusk, and I have learned that dusk is the hour when people see things. They will not tell the vicar. They will tell the man with the ladder, because he is out in it too.\n\nSo I have bought this book to write the sightings down, without deciding whether they are true.\n\nI begin with my own. It is March. My last lamp is number forty-one, at the bottom of Marsh Lane, a long cold walk on a bad knee. Three evenings this week I have come round the bend and found it already burning, the glass shut and the wick trimmed. Nobody was about. I said thank you to the lane in general, and went home."],["The Hare That Bows","Reported to me, Jory Flint, lamplighter, at lamp number six by the church, by the sexton, Mr Enoch Bramble, who is not a fanciful man and wished that put on record.\n\nMost evenings in April a grey hare comes over the fields and sits upon the churchyard wall, facing west. It keeps quite still as the light goes. When the first star shows, it bows. The sexton was firm that it is a bow, with the ears laid back and the head brought down, and not a hare scratching itself. Then it goes home across the glebe.\n\nI lit number six slowly for a week to watch. I saw the hare five evenings out of seven. I saw the bow twice, and once I believe it included me. Being on a ladder at the time, I could only nod. I have entered the hare in my book as punctual."],["Good Evening from the Line","Dusk comes later in May, and I light lamp fourteen on Thimble Lane at half past eight. That is where Mrs Pettibone stopped me to report a thing for my book of sightings.\n\nShe leaves her washing out late. On still evenings, she says, when not a leaf is moving, one item on the line will lift and wave. It is always the same one, a blue apron that was her mother’s. It waves three times, as her mother used to from that very door, and hangs quiet again.\n\nI am Jory Flint, the lamplighter, and I know every draught in Emberwick. There is none in that yard. I stood and watched with her. The air was like a held breath, and the apron lifted, once, twice, three times. Mrs Pettibone said good evening to it in an ordinary voice. So I did too. It seemed only civil."],["Two Shadows on the Bridge","The baker’s boy came running to my ladder with something for the book. I am Jory Flint, lamplighter, and I write down what Emberwick sees at dusk. He had crossed the Ember bridge at lighting-up and met the Dusk Folk: long thin people, he said, one walking ahead of him and one behind, who stopped when he stopped.\n\nI took him back and showed him. There is a lamp at each end of the bridge, numbers twenty and twenty-one. Walk between them and each throws your shadow a different way, one before you and one behind. Two shadows, one boy. He was a little disappointed and so was I.\n\nThen he asked why the one in front had waved first, before he lifted his arm. I told him shadows on that bridge are known to be forward. I walked home across it with my hands in my pockets."],["Lights Along the Marsh","June, and hardly worth lighting lamps at all. I am the town lamplighter, and from number forty-one at the foot of Marsh Lane I can see clear across the marsh at dusk.\n\nOn warm evenings small lights come out among the reeds, low down, greenish, about the size of a thimble. I have seen marsh lights all my life. What I had not noticed before is that they travel in a line, evenly spaced, like a procession, and that the one in front goes slowest so the others can keep up.\n\nBryony Sedge, who cuts reeds out there, says it is the marsh counting its herons home. I asked how many herons. She said the same number as lights. I counted nineteen lights. I notice Bryony did not offer to count herons. Lamp forty-one was lit before I reached it. That is nine times now."],["The Long Blue","At midsummer in Emberwick the dusk does not fall so much as linger. The old people call it the Long Blue. I am Jory Flint, lamplighter, and on those evenings I sit on my ladder at lamp twelve with nothing to do until eleven.\n\nIt is said that during the Long Blue the Northern Fells walk down to drink at the river. They are simply nearer than they were.\n\nI do not hold with it, but I am a careful man. From lamp twelve, at noon, the top of Cold Pike sits one hand’s breadth above the mill roof. I measured again at half past ten, in the blue. Two hands, and a thumb. I could make out single sheep. By morning it was back where it belonged. I have put both measurements in the book. A hill is entitled to a drink, the same as anyone."],["The Owl on Lamp Seven","A sighting of my own, and a nightly one. I am the lamplighter of Emberwick, and since the middle of July I have not been allowed to light lamp seven, by the corn exchange, without permission.\n\nA white owl sits on the crossbar. She arrives at dusk as I do. If I set my ladder against the post she looks down at me, and does not shift, and I cannot reach the glass past her. I tried waiting. I tried coughing. On the fourth evening, feeling foolish, I took off my cap and said good evening. She stepped aside at once.\n\nIt has been so ever since. No greeting, no lamp. The corn merchants have started raising their hats to her as well. She does not move for them. She will be civil to the public, but she has business only with the man who brings the light."],["A Door in the Hawthorn","September. Three children of Tanner’s Row came in a body to me, Jory Flint, lamplighter, for my book of things seen at dusk. They had found a door in the hawthorn hedge behind the tannery. It is there only at sunset, they said, tall and golden, and gone by the time they fetch a grown-up.\n\nI am a grown-up, so I went early and waited. They are right. At the back of the hedge is a gap where an old gatepost stood. For six minutes in the middle of September the setting sun comes straight through it and stands a door of light on the grass, threshold and all.\n\nI timed it on three evenings. Then I told the children the hour at which their door opens. I did not tell them it was only the sun. I am not certain that it is only anything."],["What the Fog Brought","Reported to me, Jory Flint, lamplighter, at lamp thirty, by Sam Wherry the carrier, while I was up the ladder and the October fog was down to the rooftops.\n\nHe was driving in along the Grey Coast road at dusk. The sea is nine miles from Emberwick. All the same he heard a harbour bell, close by, slow, and then the creak of a rope. His horse stopped of its own accord. Above the hedge, he says, a ship’s lantern went by, swinging gently, as high as a mast would carry it, and passed inland over the turnips. A voice called out a depth. It sounded pleased with the answer.\n\nI would doubt him, but I lit thirty-one and thirty-two in the same fog, and my coat was wet through afterwards. I licked my sleeve, which a lamplighter may do in private. Salt."],["The Fourth Window","November dusk comes at half past four, and I am out with my ladder before most of Emberwick has had its tea. I am Jory Flint, the lamplighter. This sighting is the miller’s boy’s and afterwards mine.\n\nThe old mill by the river has three windows on the side that faces the lane. Anyone can count them by day: three. The boy swears that at dusk, in winter, there are four, and that the fourth is lit, warm and yellow, with a geranium on the sill.\n\nI have counted on eleven evenings. Nine times, three windows, dark. Twice, four. The fourth sits between the second and the third as if it had always been there, and somebody inside was laying a table. I did not go nearer. One does not stare in at windows, whichever sort they are. I raised my cap and got on with number thirty-five."],["Going Round Backwards","For most of a year, lamp forty-one at the foot of Marsh Lane has been lit before I, the town lamplighter, could reach it. I have counted sixty-three times. In January I decided to catch whoever, or whatever, does it.\n\nI went my round backwards. I started at forty-one, in the last of the light, and it was dark and cold and ordinary. I lit it myself and worked my way up through the town to finish at number one, by the library gate. Number one was burning when I got there, the glass shut and the wick trimmed.\n\nSo it is not a matter of Marsh Lane. Whatever it is lights my last lamp, wherever my last lamp happens to be, so that I need not finish in the dark. I stood under it for some time. Then I said thank you, to the street in general."],["The Apprentice’s Taper","This is my last entry in the book of things seen at dusk. I am Jory Flint, lamplighter, and tonight I saw who lights my final lamp.\n\nI hid behind the pump on Marsh Lane. At dusk a girl came down with a taper tied to a bean pole: Nettie Cooper, aged ten. She has watched my round from her window all winter, and my knee, and thought the last lamp was one too many for it. She wants to be a lamplighter. I have taken her on as apprentice, at sixpence and a ladder of her own.\n\nNettie began in October. The lamp was first lit ahead of me in March. I have written down both facts and do not intend to make them fight. The book is hers now. She saw a hare bow on the churchyard wall at dusk. I have told her to put it in."]],[["Opening the Account","I am Linus Orrery, bookkeeper to Pell and Daughter, corn-factors of Emberwick, and in thirty-one years I have never left a sum unrecorded. It struck me last week that my dreams go entirely unaccounted for. This ledger is to correct that. Each morning I shall enter what the night gave and what it took.\n\nFirst entry. I dreamed of a shop built on the half-landing of a staircase. Its shelves held glass jars, and in each jar was an afternoon: a wet one, a golden one, one with a brass band in it. The shopkeeper was a tall grey heron in an apron. She did not offer to sell me anything. She handed me a bill, folded once. It read: One afternoon. Outstanding.\n\nDebit: one afternoon, owed to persons unknown. Credit: nil. I do not recall incurring it, and I am not a man who forgets a debt."],["An Audit by Pike","Second entry in the dream ledger of Linus Orrery, bookkeeper. Having been billed for one afternoon by a heron in an earlier dream, I slept warily.\n\nLast night my books were audited. The auditor was a pike, a large one, in half-moon spectacles, standing upright at my desk in the counting-house in two inches of clear water. He turned every page with a fin. He checked each column twice. I have never been so nervous and so flattered at once. At last he pronounced. The figures were correct to the farthing. The books were, however, short of weather. Thirty-one years, he said, and not one entry for wind.\n\nCredit: a clean audit. Debit: a remark I cannot stop thinking about. I looked through the real ledgers this morning. He is right. There is no column for it."],["The Sideways Stairs","I keep this ledger of my dreams as I keep the accounts at Pell and Daughter: daily, and in ink. Last night I went looking for the shop on the staircase, where a heron once handed me a bill for one afternoon.\n\nI found the stairs. They went sideways. I climbed along them for some time, holding the banister, which was warm, and came out not at the shop but in our own granary. Every sack on the floor had been untied. In each one, curled on the grain, lay a sleeping cat. There were cats to the rafters, tabby, black, marmalade, all breathing together like a slow sea. I sat down among them. Nobody asked me for anything.\n\nDebit: none. Credit: warmth, one granary’s worth, entered here at an estimate since I had no means of weighing it."],["A Loan of Feathers","Dream ledger, fourth entry. Linus Orrery, bookkeeper, in sound health and good standing, except with a certain heron who says I owe an afternoon.\n\nLast night I borrowed wings. The lender was a goose on the bank of the Ember, very businesslike. Her terms were one feather back per mile flown. I signed with a reed. Then I was up over the town, higher than the weathercock, with my coat-tails streaming and the river laid out below like a line ruled in silver. From there I saw it: a chimney where no house stands, halfway up the air above Stair Lane, smoking gently. The heron’s shop. I flew four miles looking for a door and found none.\n\nDebit: four feathers, paid promptly on landing. Credit: one aerial view, and the knowledge that the shop keeps a fire in. Somebody expects me."],["The Bill Itemised","I am a bookkeeper, and I enter my dreams in this ledger each morning before the counting-house opens. Last night the shop on the staircase came back, with its jars of afternoons and its heron in an apron. I asked, politely, to see my account in full. She took a pencil from behind her head and wrote on the bill beneath the first line. It now reads: One afternoon, blue, with wind. Promised. Not delivered.\n\nPromised, by me. The shop only holds the paper. I asked to whom it was promised. The heron looked at me for a long moment, the way Miss Pell does when I have asked something she thinks I could work out.\n\nDebit: one afternoon, now with particulars. Credit: nil. Note: I have never broken a promise in business. I am less sure about before business."],["The Moon Takes Sugar","Sixth entry in the dream ledger. I, Linus Orrery, bookkeeper, received a caller in my sleep. The moon came to tea. She arrived by the window, which she had to take sideways, and sat in the good chair. She was smaller than expected and took three sugars. We talked of the price of barley, on which she was well informed, having looked down on a great many fields.\n\nThen she asked, quite gently, about the afternoon I owe. Everybody up there knew, she said. It had been outstanding a long while. She only wondered if I had forgotten whom it was for. I said I had. She patted my hand with a cool one and said it would come to me, and that the kite would help.\n\nDebit: three sugars. Credit: one hint. I own no kite. I entered the word anyway and underlined it."],["Inventory of the Attic","I record my dreams here as strictly as I record corn at Pell and Daughter. Last night’s needs two columns. I dreamed I was taking inventory in the attic of the house where I was a boy. One rocking horse, lame. One box of marbles. One kite, red, with a tail of knotted rags, hanging from a nail. When I touched it I woke, and I remembered.\n\nI was fourteen. My sister Dora was nine. We built that kite together, and I promised her that on the first blue windy afternoon we would take it up Lantern Hill. That same week I was offered a stool in the counting-house. I started on the Monday. There has been no afternoon since.\n\nDebit: one afternoon, blue, with wind, owed to my sister these thirty-one years. Credit: I know now what I owe. A bookkeeper can do nothing until he knows that."],["Interest Accrued","Linus Orrery, bookkeeper, eighth entry. I now know that the afternoon I owe was promised to my sister Dora, when we were children, for flying a red kite. Last night I went back to the shop on the staircase to ask what such a debt comes to after thirty-one years.\n\nThe heron took me behind the counter. There were shelves I had not seen, running back farther than the building could allow, and every jar on them was labelled in my own handwriting. My afternoons. All the ones I had not used, put by. Some had gone a little cloudy. She held one to the lamp and said they keep remarkably well. Then she named the interest. It was not more afternoons. It was only that I should enjoy the one I paid.\n\nDebit: unchanged. Credit: stock in hand, far larger than supposed."],["A Letter to Dora","This ledger is for dreams, but today’s entry begins awake. I, Linus Orrery, wrote to my sister Dora, who lives at Saltby on the Grey Coast with her daughter Tilly. I told her I owed her an afternoon with a kite, thirty-one years overdue, and asked when I might pay it. It took four drafts. The first three read like invoices.\n\nThen I slept, and dreamed of the post. My letter was carried by a hedgehog in a peaked cap. He bore it along the coast road in both front paws, very upright, and would not be hurried. At each milestone he stopped, checked the address, and went on. I walked beside him and did not mind the pace.\n\nDebit: one stamp. Credit: a letter safely in the dream post, and, I hope, the real one. The hedgehog would accept no tip."],["Leave of Absence","Dream ledger, tenth entry, kept by Linus Orrery of Pell and Daughter. I am preparing to pay my sister Dora the afternoon of kite-flying I promised her as a boy. In last night’s dream I was mending the red kite. I had no paper, so I patched it with pages torn from the corn ledgers, and the columns of figures made a handsome pattern against the sky. I felt no alarm whatever at tearing them out. That is how I knew I was asleep.\n\nThis morning I bought brown paper, paste, and a ball of string. Then I knocked on Miss Pell’s door and asked for an afternoon off. She put down her pen. In thirty-one years, she said, I had never asked. She had begun to wonder if I was real.\n\nDebit: ninepence for materials. Credit: one afternoon, authorised. It only wants wind."],["Waiting on the Wind","I, Linus Orrery, bookkeeper, have a kite built and an afternoon granted, and am waiting for the right weather to pay a very old debt to my sister. My dreams this week have been waiting too. On Monday I dreamed of a pond without a ripple. On Tuesday, a flag hanging straight down. Last night I climbed to the shop on the staircase to ask whether the afternoon could be sent round. The heron was dusting jars. They do not deliver, she said. I must collect in person, and bring my own wind.\n\nBy the morning post came Dora’s answer. She remembers the kite. She remembers the promise better than I did. She and her daughter Tilly will come on Thursday.\n\nDebit: three nights of flat calm. Credit: a date. The weathercock on the corn exchange swung round to the west at noon. I watched it do so."],["Paid in Full","Final entry. On Thursday afternoon I, Linus Orrery, climbed Lantern Hill above Emberwick with my sister Dora and her daughter Tilly. The sky was blue. The wind came steady out of the west. The kite went up at the second attempt and stayed there two hours, and Dora laughed exactly as she did at nine. Tilly held the string. I did no sums of any kind.\n\nThat night I dreamed of the shop on the staircase. The heron took my bill, the one that read One afternoon, blue, with wind, and stamped it. Paid. Then she counted change into my hand. I had not expected change. It was one evening, in a small jar, to spend as I like.\n\nDebit: nil. Credit: nil. The account is balanced and I am closing the ledger. There is a new book at the office, however. It has a column for wind."]],[["January: Frost on the Step","January. Moon full on the ninth, frost most nights. I am Mina Dray, seamstress, of Thimble Lane in Emberwick, and I walk in my sleep. I have decided to keep an almanac of it, since no one else publishes one.\n\nThis month I walked once. I woke at four in the morning on the step of Rudd’s bakery, barefoot, with my shawl on inside out. The baker was already up. He gave me a hot roll and his wife’s slippers and said the smell of bread has fetched in stranger things than me. A curious matter. Next morning there was a quilt square in my workbasket that I did not make, at least not awake. It shows a brown loaf, done in wool, in stitches finer than my best.\n\nAdvice to sleepwalkers for January: wear socks to bed. You do not know where you will want them."],["February: The Watchman’s Bell","February. Moon full on the seventh, wind from the fells, snow twice. This is the almanac of Mina Dray, seamstress and sleepwalker.\n\nOne walk this month. I woke standing at the door of Emberwick Library with my hand on the latch, at an hour when it is very properly shut. Beside me stood Mr Oswin Budge, the night watchman, holding his lantern low so as not to startle me. He said I had been waiting there ten minutes, quite patiently, like someone early for an appointment. He walked me home and has since hung a small brass bell on my gate. If it rings in the night he will know I am abroad and come along behind. In my workbasket next morning lay a second quilt square I do not remember sewing: a blue door, neatly hemmed.\n\nAdvice for February: befriend the watchman. He knows all the icy places."],["March: Other People’s Washing","March. Gales at the equinox, full moon on the ninth. I am Mina Dray. I sew by day and walk in my sleep by night, and this almanac keeps count.\n\nOne walk. The bell on my gate rang at two, and Mr Budge the watchman followed me to the drying green behind Tanner’s Row. He reports that I took down an entire line of washing, folded every piece, and stacked it in the basket with the pegs on top. Then I woke, holding a stranger’s nightshirt, and was mortified. Mrs Pettibone, whose washing it was, says it has never been folded so well and I am welcome any night. The third quilt square appeared in my workbasket: a white shirt on a green ground, with a tiny peg worked in brown.\n\nAdvice for March: if you must do housework asleep, do someone else’s. They are grateful."],["April: An Umbrella for Two","April. Showers daily, moon full on the seventh, cuckoo heard on the nineteenth. An almanac for sleepwalkers, kept by Mina Dray, seamstress, who is one.\n\nOne walk, a wet one. I woke on the bench under the church lychgate with my umbrella open. I was holding it rather to the left. The reason was a large tabby cat, sitting beside me perfectly dry, who looked up as if I had been slow to wake. Mr Budge the watchman was on my other side, wet through. He said the cat had the better half of the umbrella and he had not liked to ask. Quilt square the fourth, found in my basket and sewn by me asleep: a black umbrella with a striped tail showing beneath it. My sleeping self has a sense of humour I was not aware of.\n\nAdvice for April: carry a larger umbrella."],["May: Under the Blossom","May. Warm nights, moon full on the seventh, apple blossom at its height by the twelfth. I am Mina Dray, seamstress of Emberwick, and this is my almanac of walking asleep.\n\nOne walk, the pleasantest so far. I woke at first light in Hallam’s orchard, sitting against a tree, with petals in my hair and my lap. A blackbird was singing directly overhead. Mr Budge the watchman sat on the wall nearby, eating bread and cheese. He had followed the bell on my gate as usual. He said he could have woken me an hour sooner, but I was smiling, and in thirty years on the night watch he has learned to leave a smile alone. The fifth quilt square came next morning: a bough of pink blossom, every petal a separate stitch.\n\nAdvice for May: if you wake somewhere beautiful, do not hurry home."],["June: Hardly Dark Enough","June. Shortest night on the twenty-first, moon full on the fifth, no rain to speak of. From the almanac of Mina Dray, seamstress and sleepwalker, Thimble Lane.\n\nThere is scarcely enough night in June to walk in. I got no farther than my own back garden. The bell on the gate never rang, so Mr Budge the watchman knew nothing of it. I woke kneeling by the bean row with earth to my elbows. I had weeded the whole bed. Not a bean was disturbed, and every weed lay in a tidy heap on the path. I have never weeded so well awake. It is a little hard to be outdone by oneself. The quilt square I found afterwards, the sixth, shows a bean-vine climbing a pole.\n\nAdvice for June: leave the trowel where you can find it in the dark. You may as well be useful."],["July: Counting on the Bridge","July. Thunder on the third, moon full on the fifth, hay all in. I am Mina Dray, a seamstress who walks in her sleep, and I keep this almanac month by month.\n\nOne walk. I woke on the Ember bridge leaning on the parapet, with Mr Budge the watchman at my elbow as he always is when my gate bell rings. He said I had been counting aloud. Not hurriedly. One, a pause, two, like someone going over a list. I reached twelve, nodded as if satisfied, and woke. Twelve what? There is a seventh quilt square in my workbasket now, a grey bridge with a yellow lamp, sewn in the night like the other six. I laid all seven out on the table and looked at them for a long time.\n\nAdvice for July: when your sleeping self starts counting, pay attention. She may have a plan."],["August: A Message on the Slate","August. Moon full on the third, heavy dews, swallows gathering. The almanac of Mina Dray, sleepwalker, of Thimble Lane, Emberwick.\n\nOne walk, and I left evidence. The schoolhouse door is never locked. Mr Budge the watchman, who follows when the bell on my gate rings, says I went in, took the chalk, and wrote on the big slate in a careful hand. Then I dusted my fingers and let him lead me home. The schoolmaster found it in the morning and sent to ask if it was meant for him. It read: Four more. Nearly done. It was not meant for him. I have seven quilt squares that I sewed asleep, and an eighth came this month: a slate with a stub of chalk. Eight and four are twelve.\n\nAdvice for August: read whatever you write at night. It is the only post you will get from yourself."],["September: Pinning Up the Map","September. Fog in the mornings, moon full on the second, first fire lit on the twentieth. I am Mina Dray, seamstress and sleepwalker, and this month my almanac has more to report than a walk.\n\nThe walk first. I woke in the porch of the Brass Kettle, holding a lantern borrowed from its hook, with Mr Budge the watchman beside me in the fog. Ninth quilt square, by morning: a lantern in grey mist. Then I pinned all nine squares to the wall in the order they came. Bakery, library, drying green, lychgate, orchard, bean row, bridge, school, inn. They share one border and one thread. They are every place I have woken this year. I am sewing a map of my own walks, asleep, and it wants three more squares.\n\nAdvice for September: stand back from your work. You may find you have been making something."],["October: The Needle Left Threaded","October. Harvest moon on the first, leaves down by the twentieth, geese going over. I am Mina Dray, seamstress, and I have learned that I sew a quilt in my sleep, one square for each place I wake.\n\nThis month I tried to catch myself at it. I sat up in my chair with the lamp lit and strong tea. I remember the church clock striking one. Then it was morning. My slippers were full of leaves, and the tenth square lay finished in my lap: the churchyard yew. The needle had been left threaded. Mr Budge the watchman says I went no farther than the yew, and that my walks grow shorter. He also told me he retires at the year’s end. I did not like to ask who will follow the bell then.\n\nAdvice for October: you cannot stay up late enough to meet yourself."],["November: As Far as the Gate","November. First hard frost on the eighth, moon full on the twenty-ninth, dark by five. From the almanac of Mina Dray of Thimble Lane, who walks in her sleep and has sewn ten quilt squares that way since January.\n\nOne walk, the shortest of the year. Mr Budge the night watchman was passing at midnight. I came down my own path, reached the gate, and stopped. I did not open it. I put up my hand and rang the brass bell he hung there, once, like a visitor. Then I turned round and went back to bed. He says he raised his hat, though I could not have seen. Eleventh square, in the morning: a garden gate with a small gold bell. One remains. I have hemmed the others together and left a space.\n\nAdvice for November: it is no defeat to turn back at the gate."],["December: The Quilt Entire","December. Longest night on the twenty-first, snow lying, moon full on the twenty-ninth. I am Mina Dray, seamstress, and this closes my almanac of a year’s sleepwalking.\n\nOn the longest night I did not walk at all. I woke in my own bed at eight, having slept straight through, and the twelfth quilt square lay on the pillow. It shows a bed, with a small figure asleep in it under a patchwork of twelve colours. I sewed it into its place before breakfast. Mr Budge the watchman retired that week. I gave him a scarf I had knitted awake, which is why it is uneven. The quilt is on my bed. I sleep under the map of everywhere I went, and go nowhere.\n\nForecast for next year: sleep, mostly. The bell stays on the gate. I like the sound when friends call."]],[["The Brass Plate","I, Ignatius Plume, late of the umbrella trade, have this day fixed a brass plate to the door of 4 Candle Row, above the cheesemonger. It reads: I. Plume, Interpreter of Night Visions. Hours Ten to Four. I appointed myself. There was no one else to do it.\n\nIn this casebook, and nowhere else, I record a difficulty. To the best of my knowledge I have never had a dream. I go to bed, and then it is morning. I do not consider this a bar to practice. A doctor need not have measles.\n\nCase the first. Mrs Orla Pym, of Tanner’s Row, dreamed that her teeth were piano keys and that a small man in a tailcoat was tuning them. Interpretation: she is to come into music. Fee: fourpence. She has since bought a concertina. Her neighbours have called on me, though not, I think, to offer thanks."],["The Flying Baker","From the casebook of Ignatius Plume, Interpreter of Night Visions, 4 Candle Row, Emberwick. I interpret other people’s dreams, having none of my own to distract me.\n\nCase the second. Tobias Rudd, baker, dreams three nights in seven that he is flying over the town seated in a bread tray. He is not frightened. He is hot. He passes the church weathercock at eye level and it turns to watch him go. Interpretation: flight signifies ambition, and a tray signifies trade. Mr Rudd will rise in his profession. I advised him to order a taller hat in readiness. Fee: one cottage loaf.\n\nAddendum, a week later. Mrs Rudd has opened the bedroom window, which was painted shut, the room being directly over the ovens. The flying has stopped. I maintain that he rose, in a sense. He has kept the hat, and looks well in it."],["A Goose Gives Advice","I am Ignatius Plume, formerly umbrellas, now the only Interpreter of Night Visions in Emberwick. I keep this casebook faithfully, including the cases that go sideways.\n\nCase the third. Abel Haddock, farmer in the Lowlands, dreamed that his grey goose walked into the kitchen, sat in his chair, and advised him in plain words to sell the bottom field. Interpretation: it is well known in my profession that geese in dreams mean the reverse of what they say. I told him on no account to sell. Fee: six eggs.\n\nHe kept the field. In the autumn it came up thick with mushrooms, which he sold at market for a good deal more than barley fetches. He credits me. The goose, he reports, will not look at him. I note for my own guidance that the principle concerning geese I made up on the spot. It appears to be sound."],["Posy’s Blue Door","Casebook of Ignatius Plume, Interpreter of Night Visions, above the cheesemonger in Candle Row. A difficult client today, aged nine.\n\nCase the fourth. Posy Marchbanks paid in advance with a pearl button and sat with her boots not reaching the floor. She says every dream she has ends the same way. Whatever it has been about, a picnic, a pony, her arithmetic, at the end there is a blue door. It is shut. She wakes before she can try it.\n\nI had nothing. I said I must consult my books. She asked how many books I had. I have one, and it concerns the repair of umbrella ribs. She said she would come back. I have put her button in the cashbox, as I do not feel I have earned it. A private note. I, who have never had one dream, should rather like to see that door."],["One Dream Between Two","I am Ignatius Plume. I set up as Interpreter of Night Visions with a brass plate and no qualifications, and today I was required to act as a magistrate.\n\nCase the fifth. The Dabb twins, Corwin and Clem, ferrymen on the river Ember, came in quarrelling. They have had the same dream on the same night. In it one of them rows a boat upstream through stars, while the other sits in the stern eating a pie. Each insists that he was the one with the pie. Interpretation: the dream is held in common, like the ferry. I ruled joint ownership. They are to take turns, one to row and one to eat, changing over at the new moon. Fee: a free crossing.\n\nThey left satisfied, which surprised all three of us. I do not dream, but I can tell when two people want the same pie."],["Late to His Own Birthday","From the casebook kept at 4 Candle Row by Ignatius Plume, self-appointed Interpreter of Night Visions and former mender of umbrellas.\n\nCase the sixth. Mr Silas Tench, collector of the market tolls, dreams that it is his birthday. A party is laid in a marquee, with a cake and a band. He runs toward it across a field, but he has his toll-book under one arm and must stop at every gate to enter it. He arrives as the band is packing up. Somebody has kept him a slice. Interpretation: I said he needed a holiday. Fee: threepence.\n\nI record that this was correct. He took a week at the Grey Coast and has not had the dream since. I had grown so used to being wrong in a helpful direction that being right has left me unsettled. I shall try not to make a habit of it."],["On Not Dreaming","This entry is not a case. I, Ignatius Plume, Interpreter of Night Visions in Emberwick, have spent a week trying to obtain a dream of my own, having never yet had one.\n\nMonday: a quarter-pound of the strongest cheese from the shop downstairs, eaten at bedtime. Result: thirst. Tuesday: slept with my head to the north. Result: a stiff neck. Wednesday: a sprig of marsh mint under the pillow. Result: a pleasant smell. Thursday I simply asked, aloud, politely. I slept nine hours and saw nothing whatever.\n\nPosy Marchbanks, aged nine, called on Friday. She is the child whose dreams all end at a shut blue door. She reports a development: the door now has a knocker, brass, shaped like a closed umbrella. I did not tell her what trade I followed before this one. She looked at the stand in my hall for some time, however."],["The Schoolmaster’s Stairs","Ignatius Plume, Interpreter of Night Visions, 4 Candle Row. I mended umbrellas for thirty years and have interpreted dreams for nearly one. I learn as I go. Case the eighth. Mr Ashby, the schoolmaster, dreams of climbing stairs. They have no top. He carries forty slates and may not put them down.\n\nInterpretation, first attempt: stairs signify a wish to rise in the world. Mr Ashby replied that he lives in a cottage of one floor, likes it, and wishes to rise nowhere. Second attempt: then it is not the stairs that matter. It is that there is no banister. He wants something to hold on to. I advised him to ask the school board for an assistant. Fee: waived, as I had needed two tries.\n\nHe asked. They gave him one. He tells me the stairs now have a handrail, and a landing with a chair on it."],["Night Visions of a Cat","From the casebook of Ignatius Plume, Emberwick’s Interpreter of Night Visions. My practice widens. Case the ninth. Mrs Orla Pym brought in her cat, Admiral, in a basket. He twitches in his sleep. His paws run and his whiskers work. She wished to know what he sees, and whether he is happy.\n\nThe client would not describe his dream, so I was obliged to observe it. I sat by the basket for an hour with my notebook. I recorded eleven twitches of the left forepaw, four of the tail, one small noise like a creaking hinge, and a long sigh.\n\nInterpretation: mice, in the past tense. He is remembering triumphs. I pronounced him a contented animal with a distinguished career behind him. Fee: none asked, though Mrs Pym left a saucer of cream. I have never had a dream myself, and I envied the cat a little."],["Posy Knocks","I am Ignatius Plume, Interpreter of Night Visions, who has never had a dream. Posy Marchbanks, aged nine, came up my stairs today to report on hers, which have always ended at a shut blue door.\n\nLast night she knocked. Nobody answered, so she tried the handle, and it opened. Behind it was a room. It was quite empty, she said, and clean, like a house before the furniture comes. There was one chair. In the chair an old man with white side-whiskers was sound asleep, with nothing going on around him at all. She did not like to wake him.\n\nHer interpretation, offered free: the door was mine. It was shut because nobody was at home to dream. She had been knocking on it from her side for months. I have white side-whiskers. I gave her back her button. I could not speak for a moment."],["The Interpreter Prepares","Casebook of Ignatius Plume, 4 Candle Row. No clients today. I shut at noon to attend to a case of my own.\n\nA child of nine, Posy Marchbanks, has dreamed her way through a blue door into an empty room and found me asleep in it. She believes it is the place where my dreams ought to be. I interpret night visions for a living and have never had one. I am inclined to take her opinion over mine.\n\nPreparations, therefore. I have painted my own front door blue, two coats, so that I shall know it. I have oiled the hinges. I have put a second chair in my bedroom, in case of company, and a plate of ginger biscuits. I am going to bed at eight and leaving the door on the latch. If anyone should knock tonight, from either side, I mean to be in."],["A First Vision","I, Ignatius Plume, Interpreter of Night Visions, have had a dream. It is my first. I set it down this morning before it could get away. There was a knock. I opened a blue door, and Posy Marchbanks stood there holding a pearl button. I asked her in. The room was full of open umbrellas, every one I ever mended, hanging in the air, and it was raining upward from the floor. We had tea under a green one. That was all. It was splendid.\n\nI took it straight to Posy at the school gate for interpretation, being too close to the case myself. She is nine and considered it gravely. It means, she said, that I was asleep, and that she had been right.\n\nFee: one button, which I paid. I have since altered my brass plate. It now reads: I. Plume. Night Visions Listened To."]],[["To Wren, a Year On","Dear Wren of next January, this is you, a year ago, aged fifteen, writing on the first evening of the year. I began last month as apprentice to Mr Osric Vane, bookbinder, of Paste Lane in Emberwick. Mrs Larch at the library keeps a Tomorrow Drawer, where a letter may be left sealed for a twelvemonth. I mean to leave you one every month.\n\nSome questions, so you know I was curious. Can you sew a straight line through a gathering yet? I cannot. Do your hands still smell of paste? Are you taller?\n\nI shall also tell you my dreams, since nobody else wants them. Last night I opened a book and the pages were snow, real snow, quite untrodden. I was afraid to turn one in case I marked it. Be kind when you read this. I am doing my best. Yours, Wren."],["About the Cat","Dear Wren of next February, it is me again, from a year back, in my second month at Mr Vane’s bindery. I must tell you about the cat. You know him, of course, but I want you to remember how it was at the start.\n\nHis name is Bodkin. He is grey, and enormous, and sits on top of the standing press all day with his paws folded, watching me work. When I make a mistake he closes his eyes. Mr Vane says Bodkin has seen eleven apprentices and approved of two.\n\nLast night I dreamed he was as tall as the door. He wore spectacles and asked to inspect my indentures. He read every line, then sat on them. So here is my question. Has Bodkin ever once sat on your lap? I should only like to know it is possible. Yours, with gluey thumbs, Wren."],["Crooked Stitches","Dear Wren of next March, I hope you are well and have forgotten today. I am fifteen and three months into my apprenticeship at the bindery on Paste Lane, and this morning I sewed a hymn book with every gathering askew. The spine looked like a staircase.\n\nMr Vane did not scold. He handed me the little knife and said, unpick it. It took all afternoon. He told me that everything he knows he learned from doing it wrong first, and that he was very learned by my age. My face is still hot. If you remember this, I hope you remember it more coolly than I feel it.\n\nI dreamed afterwards that I was sewing the river Ember to its banks, so it would lie flat. My stitches were perfect. The river thanked me and said it had been meaning to have that done. Yours, Wren."],["The Sea, Unseen","Dear Wren of next April, here is a thing I am ashamed of and will only tell you. I am fifteen, I live nine miles from the Grey Coast, and I have never seen the sea. Gran says there was always something else that wanted doing.\n\nToday at the bindery I repaired a ship’s log. It was stained with salt, and the paper had a ripple that would not press out. Mr Vane says paper remembers water. So this letter is a promise as well as a question. By the time you open it, you will have gone. Tell me I am right.\n\nLast night the sea came to me instead. It walked up Paste Lane, quite low and polite, and waited outside our door. It did not come in. It only wanted to know when I was coming, and I said soon. Yours, landlocked, Wren."],["Gran’s Plum Tree","Dear Wren of next May, this letter is about Gran’s plum tree, because you may need reminding how it stood this year. I am your old self, fifteen, apprentice bookbinder, writing at the kitchen table.\n\nThe tree is as old as I am. Gran planted it the week I was born, and it has never borne one plum. Every spring Gran says it is waiting for a reason. This morning it is white with blossom from top to bottom, for the first time, and she keeps walking out to look. I do not know what reason it has found. Perhaps you do.\n\nI dreamed of the plums last night, already ripe. I bit one. Where the stone should be there was a tiny book, sewn properly, with pages the size of a thumbnail. The print was too small to read, and I woke. Yours, Wren."],["A Blank Book","Dear Wren of next June, I have news, and you know how it ended, which I do not. Six months into my apprenticeship, Mr Vane the bookbinder has set me my proving piece. By the year’s end I am to bind one book entirely by myself: fold, sew, round, back, cover and finish. If it holds, I stay on.\n\nIt is to be a blank book. Nothing in it at all. I asked him what a blank book is for. He said that was the purchaser’s business, and went back to his glue pot. It troubles me, to spend six months making something with nothing in it. Do you know what it is for? You must, by now.\n\nLast night I dreamed of a book that read me. It turned me over, quite gently, and seemed interested, though it skipped some parts. Yours, Wren."],["Gold Leaf and Breath","Dear Wren of next July, you will laugh at this by the time it reaches you. I am fifteen and learning gold tooling at Mr Vane’s bindery, and this week I sneezed.\n\nGold leaf is thinner than anything. You lift it on a brush you have stroked against your cheek, and you must not breathe. I had a whole leaf laid ready over the spine of a prayer book. I felt the sneeze coming from a long way off. The gold went up like a startled bird and came down in small pieces on the cat.\n\nMr Vane laughed. I had never heard it before. It sounds like a drawer opening. Bodkin wore a gilded ear until Thursday and was unbearable. I dreamed of gold moths afterwards, hundreds, settling on my hands and staying exactly where I put them. Yours, sneezing, Wren."],["I Went","Dear Wren of next August, one question you need not answer. I went. I am fifteen, I had never seen the sea, and on Sunday Gran and I rode the carrier’s cart nine miles to the Grey Coast, with sandwiches.\n\nI am writing it down for you tonight in case a year rubs it smooth. It was much bigger than I had allowed for. It was loud. It kept arriving, over and over, and nobody on the beach seemed to think this remarkable. Gran took off her boots, which I have never seen her do out of doors. I brought back a flat grey stone. It is on the bench at the bindery, holding down endpapers. I hope it still is.\n\nI dreamed last night that I bound the tide in quarter leather. It would not stay shut. I woke up pleased about that. Yours, salted, Wren."],["Bodkin’s Verdict","Dear Wren of next September, three pieces of news from your younger self, the apprentice at the bindery on Paste Lane. First, Gran’s plum tree bore fruit. Seven plums. We ate one each on Sunday and gave five away, since she says a first harvest should travel. Second, I sewed all twelve gatherings of my proving piece, the blank book, and every one lies straight. Mr Vane ran his thumb down the spine and said nothing, which from him is praise.\n\nThird. I left the sewn book on the bench and went for the paste. When I came back, Bodkin the cat had climbed down from the press and was sitting on it. Squarely, with his paws tucked. Mr Vane says that is the verdict, and there is no appeal.\n\nI dreamed of nothing I remember. I think I was too content. Yours, approved, Wren."],["What Frightens Me Now","Dear Wren of next October, in my second letter I told you I was afraid of the bindery cat. I am nearly sixteen, and I wish to correct the record: I am now afraid of something else.\n\nMy blank book, the proving piece for my apprenticeship, is almost done. It is rounded and backed and in its boards, and I chose green cloth with a brown leather spine. Soon there will be nothing left to do to it. That is the trouble. While I am making it, it might be good. Once it is finished, it will only be what it is. Is it like this every time? Tell me it wears off.\n\nLast night I dreamed of a long shelf in the library, quite empty, with my name on a small card. I thought it would feel lonely. It felt like a room kept ready. Yours, Wren."],["It’ll Hold","Dear Wren of next December, I owe you a letter for November and you shall not get it. I was finishing my proving piece and had no words to spare. I am your self of a year ago, apprentice bookbinder, and tonight the blank book is done.\n\nMr Vane took it to the window. He examined it through his glass for a quarter of an hour. He held it by one board and shook it. Then he set it down and said, it’ll hold. I am to stay on. Bodkin the cat yawned, which I take as applause.\n\nI still do not know what a blank book is for. Mine sits on the shelf, green and brown, with nothing in it. I dreamed last night that tomorrow knocked at the door, and I knew the knock. This is my last letter. The next move is yours. Wren."],["Tomorrow Writes Back","Dear Wren of last January, it is me. I am sixteen, it is the first evening of the year, and Mrs Larch has just handed me your first letter from the Tomorrow Drawer at the library. You asked me to be kind. You were doing better than you knew.\n\nYour answers. Yes, I sew straight, mostly. I am taller by an inch. I have seen the sea twice, and Gran made plum jam. Bodkin, the bindery cat, is on my lap as I write, which makes the penmanship difficult.\n\nAs for the blank book I bound, I have found what it is for. I have pasted your letter onto its first page. I shall open one each month and add it, and when the eleventh is in, this reply goes last. I dreamed nothing last night. I slept, and when I woke it was tomorrow. Yours, as promised, Wren."]],[["Five Sets of Initials","I am Barnaby Thresh, joiner, of Cooper’s Row in Emberwick, and these are notes kept at my bench in a slow January. The bench is older than I am by a good deal. It is beech, black with handling, and it has stood under this window through four joiners before me.\n\nCrouch down and look beneath the front rail and you will find us all. H.T., who built it. M.T., his daughter Martha. J.O., who was no relation and the best of us. R.T., my father. Then B.T., cut by me at nineteen with more flourish than skill.\n\nThere is room to the right of mine for one more set, perhaps two. I am sixty-seven, with no child and no apprentice. I mention it only because the bench is a thing I look at all day, and the gap has begun to look back."],["The Carter’s Daughter","Notes at the bench, Cooper’s Row, February. Dunstan Addle the carter came in on Tuesday with a load of oak and a daughter. The oak I had ordered. The daughter is Wren, fourteen, who he says is no use with horses because she will stop to look at gateposts. He asked would I, Barnaby Thresh, try her till midsummer. I said the bench had seen worse, which is not certain.\n\nI gave her a saw, a board and a pencilled line. Her cut set off well, lost heart, and wandered away to the left like the Ember going through the Lowlands. She looked at it for some time. Then she asked for another board, without being told.\n\nI have set her to sweeping and to learning the names of things. She sweeps badly. She learns names as if she were collecting them."],["A Door for the Bakery","Barnaby Thresh, joiner, at his bench. This fortnight’s work is a new street door for Mrs. Quince’s bakery in Oven Lane, the old one having swollen and stuck so often that customers took to knocking on the window with pennies.\n\nIt is oak, framed and boarded, and heavy enough to need the two of us to turn it over. My apprentice Wren Addle has been six weeks with me. She cannot yet saw to a line, but I find she can read a board. She ran her thumb down one and said the grain turned back on itself near the end, and it did. Nobody taught her that. I only learned it at thirty.\n\nWe hung the door on Friday. It closes with one finger and a sound like a book shutting. Mrs. Quince paid in coin and a warm loaf, and Wren carried the loaf."],["The Cradle Rockers","A cradle this month, for the miller’s first child, due with the lambs. I am Barnaby Thresh, joiner of Emberwick, and I have made some forty cradles on this old beech bench. My father made mine on it, and I am told I complained of the workmanship from the first night.\n\nThe rockers are the particular part. Too flat and the child is merely in a box. Too round and it goes over like a herring boat. You find the curve by eye, and by rocking the thing empty with one finger until it settles the way a breath does.\n\nWren Addle, my apprentice since February, cut the joints for the foot end. Three I recut. The fourth went together with a push and stayed. She said nothing at all, and swept the entire shop without being asked, corners included."],["Sharpening Day","It rained on Emberwick from Monday to Thursday, so we sharpened. A joiner’s shop is half edges, and I, Barnaby Thresh, have let mine go this spring, for a reason I shall come to.\n\nWren Addle, the apprentice, had every chisel and plane iron out along the bench in order of size, like a choir. She worked the stones the whole of Tuesday. I showed her once how a good edge refuses to catch the light, and by afternoon she was holding irons up to the window and frowning like a jeweller.\n\nThe reason is my hands. In wet weather the knuckles swell and the fine grip goes. I note it here as I would note a split in a board: a fact about the material, to be worked around. Wren sharpened mine too. They have not been so keen since my father had the doing of them."],["The Library Window","Notes at the joiner’s bench, Cooper’s Row, midsummer. The north window of Emberwick Library has let in the rain since before I was apprenticed, and this month the librarian sent for me to make a new sash. I am Barnaby Thresh. I have wanted that job for forty years.\n\nThe frame is plain work. The glazing bars are not: thin as reeds, each with its little moulding, meeting in joints you could hide under a thumbnail. My hands would not hold steady for them. So Wren Addle cut them, my apprentice of five months, with me on a box beside her saying less and less.\n\nEleven bars, and I could fault two. The window went in on Saturday. The librarian paid fairly and added that we might each keep our books a month past the date. Wren asked if the offer could be had in writing."],["Elm for the Ferry Steps","I am Barnaby Thresh, joiner, and for nine days I have been away from my bench, which does not suit me. The landing steps at the Ember ferry had rotted, and the ferryman wanted new ones in elm, since elm will sit in water for a lifetime and only grow more stubborn.\n\nWe worked on the bank, my apprentice Wren Addle and I, with the boards across two trestles and the river going by at our elbows. Nothing was level. The wind turned over my drawings. Wren went in to the knee twice and to the waist once, and came out holding the mallet above her head like a prize.\n\nThe steps are sound and the ferryman is pleased. But I missed the bench as you miss a table at dinnertime. I had not known how much of my straightness I borrow from it."],["A Mistake in Ash","A bad Thursday at the bench in Cooper’s Row. We are making a long ladder in ash for the bell-ringers, and my apprentice Wren Addle cut the last pair of joints on the wrong side of her line. A whole pole wasted, and clean ash is not had every week.\n\nShe put down the saw and went to fetch her coat. I am Barnaby Thresh, fifty years a joiner, and I knew that walk. So I pulled open the bench drawer, the deep one, and took out a small oak wedge, cut badly crooked, with B.T. on it in my father’s pencil.\n\nEvery joiner at this bench has had a first real mistake kept in that drawer. There are five. Martha’s is a door peg. I wrote W.A. on the spoiled end of the ash and put it in with the others. Wren hung her coat up again."],["The Hollow in the Top","There is a hollow in the top of my bench, just left of the vice, where every joiner who has owned it stood to plane. You can lay a straight edge across and slide a penny under. My father meant to flatten it. So did Martha Thresh before him, by all accounts. I, Barnaby Thresh, have meant to since I was thirty.\n\nThis week, with the autumn orders not yet in, I sharpened the long plane and set to. I took three shavings. Then my apprentice Wren Addle, who has been with me since February, asked what I would rest small work in afterwards. I had no answer. Half of what I make sits in that hollow to be held.\n\nI oiled the three pale stripes until they darkened. The bench is not flat. It is the shape of the work done on it, and I shall leave it so."],["Table for the Lantern Inn","The Lantern Inn on the Grey Coast ordered a table to seat twenty fishermen, and my shop in Cooper’s Row is not the length of twenty fishermen. I am Barnaby Thresh, joiner. We built it with the street door open and one end out in the Row, so that for a week the whole of Emberwick stepped round our work and gave an opinion on it.\n\nThe opinions I leave out. The joints are Wren Addle’s. She is my apprentice, nine months at the bench, and she cut all eight where the legs meet the frame while I held the lamp and kept my swollen hands in my apron. They went home with a knock of the mallet apiece.\n\nThe baker’s boy asked who made it. I said, “The shop did.” Wren looked up, then went on planing. A shop, I notice, has become two people."],["A Stool by the Stove","December, Cooper’s Row. I have made myself a stool. It is the first thing I have built for my own use since my tool chest, and it stands by the stove, where a joiner with winter hands can be warm and still see the bench. I am Barnaby Thresh, and I find I can direct a good deal of work from a seated position.\n\nWren Addle now takes the orders at the door. She came in February on trial till midsummer, and midsummer passed without either of us mentioning it. Yesterday she mentioned it. She asked whether she was to stay.\n\nI handed her a board with a pencilled line, as on her first morning, and she sawed it. I laid the rule against the cut and no light came through. I said the bench had waited some time for someone who could do that."],["The Sixth Set","The last of these notes, written from the stool by the stove on the shortest day. I am Barnaby Thresh, joiner of Emberwick, and this morning I gave my apprentice Wren Addle the small gouge my father gave me, and told her where to kneel.\n\nUnder the front rail of the bench are five sets of initials, from old H.T. who built it down to my own. It took her an hour. She cut W.A. to the right of B.T., plainly, with none of the flourish I wasted on mine. There is room beyond hers for one more.\n\nThe bench is hers when I am done with it, and I am in no hurry. She is planing as I write, standing in the hollow where we have all stood. These pages go in the deep drawer with the six mistakes, for whoever comes seventh."]],[["On Wobbles","Every chair has a trouble. I say so as Jessamy Poole, chair-mender, of Rushbottom Lane in Emberwick, who has mended some four thousand chairs in thirty years and never yet made one. This book sets the troubles down in order, beginning with the commonest.\n\nA wobble is rarely what you think. Miss Garnet, the schoolmistress, brought me a chair that rocked corner to corner. She had already trimmed one leg herself, then another to match, and was on her way to owning a footstool. I set the chair on my flat stone. It stood like a judge. The trouble was her schoolroom floor.\n\nSo look under the chair before you look at it. I tell everyone that, from my seat by the shop window, beside a ladder-back armchair with ticket forty-one tied to its arm. That one is not mine. It is waiting to be collected."],["On Creaks","A creak is a joint talking. Somewhere two pieces of wood that were once tight have come loose enough to rub, and the chair is letting you know. I am Jessamy Poole, chair-mender of Emberwick, and I can generally silence one in an afternoon with glue, a wedge and patience.\n\nGenerally. The widow Pargeter of Tanner’s Yard sent down her rocking chair with a split arm and a note pinned to the seat. Mend the arm, it said. Touch the creak and I shall know. Her husband had sat in that rocker forty winters, and the creak on the backward swing was the sound of him thinking.\n\nI mended the arm. I left the creak exactly where it was, and tested it twice to be certain. It is the only time I have charged a customer for a thing I did not do, and she paid it gladly."],["On Loose Rungs","The rungs of a chair are the rails that run between its legs, low down, and they come loose for one reason. Somebody tips back. I am Jessamy Poole, who mends chairs in Rushbottom Lane, and I could keep my shop on that habit alone.\n\nMr. Brill of the Lantern Inn sends me fourteen chairs every spring. They arrive on a cart, loose as a bag of kindling, having spent the winter under fishermen who tell long stories on two legs. I knock each one apart, clean the old glue from the sockets, and put it together again tighter than it was born.\n\nI have told Mr. Brill his customers might try sitting on all four legs. He says a man cannot describe the size of a fish that way. So the cart comes each April, and I am not, truthfully, sorry to see it."],["On Sagging Seats","A rush seat lasts twenty years and then lets you down slowly, which is kinder than most things. You notice one evening that you are sitting in the chair rather than on it. I am Jessamy Poole, chair-mender, of Emberwick, and I have rewoven more seats than I have had hot dinners, since a seat once started cannot be left.\n\nThe rush comes off the marsh in high summer. I cut it myself, dry it in the loft till it smells of hay and river, then damp it and twist it as I weave, round and round the frame until the four sides meet in the middle like the back of an envelope.\n\nThe best seat I ever wove is on the ladder-back armchair in my window, ticket forty-one. A young seaman named Ned Harrow left it and said he would be back by spring. That was eleven springs ago."],["On Woodworm","You will know woodworm by its holes, small and round as if someone had gone over the chair with a pin, and by the pale dust beneath it of a morning. It is the one trouble I, Jessamy Poole, chair-mender of Rushbottom Lane, cannot always mend, and a mender should say so plainly.\n\nThe verger of Emberwick brought me the old vestry chair, in which six generations of clergy had sat to pull on their boots. I pressed my thumb to the back leg and it went in as a thumb goes into cake. I told him the chair was finished. He took it well, for a verger.\n\nBut one spindle of the back was sound, dark and hard as horn. I kept it. A year later it went into a farmhouse chair from the Northern Fells, where it will do another century. Chairs have heirs, if you arrange it."],["On Too Much Paint","Some chairs suffer from kindness. Every few years someone gives them a fresh coat, until the turnings fill up and the chair looks made of dough. I am Jessamy Poole, chair-mender, and scraping such a chair is the nearest my trade comes to reading.\n\nThe Doble family’s kitchen chair came to my shop in Rushbottom Lane wearing seven coats. Under the cream was a green, under the green a blue from old Mrs. Doble’s day, then a brown, a red, and a yellow nobody living would own to. At the bottom, on bare elm, I found two pencilled words in a child’s large letters: Hetty’s chair.\n\nI left that corner unscraped and waxed over it. I did my thinking about it in the evening, in the armchair by my window. It has ticket forty-one on its arm and is not mine to sit in. I sit in it regardless."],["On Grand Chairs","A grand chair is built to be looked at, and it is a mistake to ask more of it. I am Jessamy Poole, chair-mender of Emberwick, and the grandest I ever handled was the Mayor’s chair from the Moot Hall: oak, carved with herons and wheat-sheaves, and gilded wherever gilt would stick.\n\nMrs. Oldacre, the Mayor, had it carried to my shop by four men. Nothing was broken. Its trouble, she said, was that no mayor in a hundred years had sat in it for an hour without thinking of resigning. The seat was flat as a doorstep and the back stood bolt upright.\n\nI eased the back a little, hollowed the seat a little more, and hid a cushion under the velvet. Council meetings now run forty minutes longer. Mrs. Oldacre says the town has not thanked me, and she is not sure she does."],["On Broken Backs","When the back of a chair breaks there is always a story, and it is usually laughter. Mr. Hogg, the Emberwick blacksmith, leaned back at his own table to enjoy a joke about a goose, and the top rail parted with a crack they heard in the street. He brought me the pieces in his apron. I am Jessamy Poole, chair-mender, and I asked for the joke as well.\n\nA break like that I mend by letting in new wood, shaped to fit the old and pinned so the two grip one another. It takes a day. The joint ends up stronger than the rail around it.\n\nIt also shows. I will stain a mend but never hide one. Mr. Hogg’s chair has a pale stripe across its shoulders, and he tells me he points it out to visitors, and then tells the joke again."],["On High Chairs","A child’s high chair has troubles of its own, most of them sticky. I am Jessamy Poole, who mends chairs in Rushbottom Lane, Emberwick, and I see the Tunney family’s high chair about once in three years, between babies.\n\nIt is ash, tall and narrow, with a little tray on a wooden catch. Nine Tunneys have sat in it. The catch wears out because each child discovers, at about ten months, that the tray will come off if hammered with a spoon. I have made that catch four times and now cut two at once.\n\nAlong the front rail there is a row of small tooth marks, some shallow, some very determined. Mrs. Tunney asked once if I could sand them out. I said I could, and didn’t. She can tell you which marks are whose. The deep ones, she says, belong to the quietest child."],["On Chairs Nobody Sits In","You would suppose that the safest chair is the one nobody uses. It is the opposite. I am Jessamy Poole, chair-mender of Emberwick, and half the chairs brought to me from best parlours have simply dried out and come apart from loneliness, like a boat left on the shingle.\n\nA chair needs sitting in. Weight keeps the joints snug, warmth keeps the wood from shrinking, and the daily shifting about is a sort of exercise. Miss Larch of Bell Street kept six good chairs under dust sheets for her visitors. When visitors finally came, two chairs sat down with them.\n\nWhich brings me to a confession. The ladder-back armchair in my window, ticket forty-one, has been sound these eleven years because I sit in it every evening. Its owner, a seaman called Ned Harrow, never came back for it. I call this maintenance. I have not charged for it."],["On Stools","The stool is the one seat with almost no troubles. Three legs will stand steady on any floor in the Lowlands, as every milkmaid knows, and there is no back to break and no arm to split. I am Jessamy Poole, chair-mender of Rushbottom Lane, and I have had perhaps five stools to mend in thirty years. A trade could starve on them.\n\nI have been thinking about stools because I have been thinking about making. I have mended four thousand seats and never built one from the tree. A mender only ever meets a chair in the middle of its life.\n\nAnd a letter came on Tuesday, from the Grey Coast, in a hand I did not know. Ned Harrow is ashore for good, it says, and begs to ask whether a certain armchair, left with me eleven years ago, is by any chance still about."],["On Collecting Your Chair","Ned Harrow came for his chair on a Saturday, eleven years late and grey in the beard. I am Jessamy Poole, chair-mender of Emberwick, and I untied ticket forty-one from the arm of the ladder-back in my window and stood aside.\n\nHe sat. He shifted. He stood up again and looked at the seat, and then at me. A rush seat takes the shape of whoever uses it, and that one had had eleven years of my evenings. “She’s sat to you now,” he said, quite kindly. He paid the old bill, which was ninepence, and would not take the chair.\n\nInstead he ordered a new one, made for him from the start. So there is ash drying in my loft, and for the first time in thirty years I am at the beginning of a chair. Its trouble, when it comes, will at least be my own doing."]],[["January: The Salt Box","January. I am Tansy Wickett, I am eleven, and I have come to live for a year with my great-aunt Lovage, who makes boxes in Thimble Yard in Emberwick. My Da is mate of the ketch Curlew and sailed north on New Year’s Day. He will be gone until winter comes round again.\n\nAunt Lovage has a rule for me. One small box every month, each one given away when it is done. She says twelve boxes will teach me more than a hundred lectures, and that she does not know a hundred lectures anyway.\n\nJanuary’s is a salt box in pine, with a sloping lid, for her own kitchen. I sawed, I planed, I nailed. The lid sits up at one corner like a raised eyebrow. I said it was ruined. Aunt Lovage filled it with salt, hung it by the stove and said, “It holds salt. Box.”"],["February: The Seed Box","February. Box two is for Mr. Fenn, who has the allotment behind the tannery and keeps his seeds in old socks. I am Tansy Wickett, and I am learning box-making from my great-aunt Lovage in Thimble Yard, one box a month.\n\nA seed box wants little rooms inside, one for each kind. I cut the dividers to fit and they did not fit. I cut them again and they fitted somewhere else. Aunt Lovage says wood is not being awkward, it is only being exact, and that I am invited to join it. I got them in on the fourth try and wrote on each in pencil: peas, beans, marigold, and one marked Mystery.\n\nA letter came from Da on the Curlew! They are past the Grey Coast and the cook has burnt the porridge twice. Mr. Fenn says the Mystery room is the one he will fill first."],["March: The Button Box","March, and a button box for Miss Odell the dressmaker, whose buttons live loose in a drawer and escape. I am Tansy Wickett, eleven, staying this year with my great-aunt Lovage the box-maker while my Da is at sea.\n\nThis box has a lid that slides in a groove. Aunt Lovage let me use the little grooving plane, which sings when it is going well. Mine sang, and then I made the lid too thin, so that when Miss Odell tipped the box to admire it the lid shot out and so did two hundred buttons. We hunted them for an hour. One was in the cat’s dish.\n\nI made a second lid on Saturday. It slides if you ask it and stays if you don’t. Miss Odell gave me a button shaped like an anchor, to sew inside my coat for Da’s luck."],["April: The Nesting Box","April. I asked my great-aunt Lovage whether a bird box counts as a box, and she said anything with six sides and a reason counts. So this month’s is for the blue tits in the pear tree in Thimble Yard. I am Tansy Wickett, making one box a month for a year.\n\nIt is rough larch, because birds dislike a planed wall, and the roof leans so the rain runs off. Aunt Lovage bored the round door with a tool like a giant corkscrew, and I smoothed its edge with sandpaper wrapped round my thumb so nobody would catch a feather.\n\nThen nothing happened for nine days. I watched from the window until Aunt said I was wearing a hole in the glass. On the tenth day a tit went in with moss. They are the worst customers we have had. They have not paid, and they complain constantly."],["May: The Tea Caddy","May. This is the first box I am not ashamed of. I am Tansy Wickett, and since January I have made a box a month in my great-aunt Lovage’s workshop, and given each away. This one is for the librarian at Emberwick Library, who keeps her tea in a paper bag.\n\nIt is cherry, my first hard wood, which smells faintly of almonds when you saw it. The corners are mitred, meaning each side leans in to meet the next like heads over a map, and inside there is a second lid to keep the air out.\n\nWhen you lower the top it sinks the last little way by itself, with a sigh. Aunt Lovage closed it four times and said nothing, which from her is a speech. Da’s letter came the same day. The Curlew has seen ice, floating, the colour of the inside of a shell."],["June: The Fly Box","June. No letter from Da this month. I am Tansy Wickett, living this year with my great-aunt Lovage, the box-maker of Thimble Yard, while his ship the Curlew trades in the far north. A letter most months since January, and now none.\n\nAunt Lovage did not say he would be all right. She gave me a piece of pear wood no bigger than a slice of bread and told me old Tolly the ferryman needed a box for his fishing flies, to fit a waistcoat pocket. It has tiny brass hinges, let in flush, and a lining of cork. It took every afternoon I had.\n\nThat, I see now, was the idea. Tolly opened and shut it by the Ember and said it was neater than his flies deserved. He also said letters from the north come the way geese do, none for weeks and then a skyful."],["July: The Pencil Box","July, and Tolly the ferryman was right: two letters from Da came on one morning, tied together with tarred string. The Curlew is loading dried fish at a place called Skerry Sound, where it does not get dark at all. I read them both twice on the workshop step.\n\nI am Tansy Wickett, and I am making a box every month this year with my great-aunt Lovage in Thimble Yard. July’s is a pencil box for my friend Pip Larkin, who goes away to school in the Lowlands in autumn.\n\nIt has my first dovetails. Those are the corner joints shaped like a row of birds’ tails, which lock so the box cannot pull apart. Mine have gaps. Aunt Lovage says they look like my teeth did at seven and will improve the same way. Pip says he will tell the Lowland boys the gaps are for ventilation."],["August: The Letter Box","August. The rule in my great-aunt Lovage’s workshop is that I make one small box a month and give it away. This month I asked to keep one. I am Tansy Wickett, and my Da is a year at sea on the ketch Curlew, and his letters were living under my pillow, getting creased.\n\nAunt Lovage thought about it while she swept. Then she said the box would be for the letters, and the letters were Da’s, and I would only be minding both. That is how she gets round her own rules. She calls it joinery.\n\nSo it is oak, long and shallow, dovetailed with smaller gaps than July’s, with a brass catch that clicks. I lined it with blue paper from the stationer’s. Seven letters fit, lying flat, with room for as many again. I checked that part on purpose. It was the only checking I enjoyed."],["September: The Money Box","September. The bell-ringers of Emberwick are collecting for a new rope, and they asked my great-aunt Lovage for a money box, and she pointed at me with her pencil. I am Tansy Wickett, eleven, her apprentice for this year, a box each month.\n\nA money box is a strange job, because you build a box and then shut it for good. There is a slot in the top and no lid at all. I was pleased with that until Aunt asked how the ringers would ever get their pennies out, short of an axe.\n\nSo the bottom slides, but only if you first push one particular corner peg, which looks like all the others. I am not saying which. The ringers shook the box for ten minutes and gave up, and the Tower Captain had to be told privately. It rattles already. I put the first penny in myself."],["October: The Candle Box","October, with gales. This month’s box went the farthest of any: out along the cliff road to the lighthouse on the Grey Coast, for Mrs. Skerrit the keeper, who wanted her spare candles kept dry. I am Tansy Wickett, and I have made ten boxes this year in my great-aunt Lovage’s workshop in Emberwick.\n\nIt is long and plain, in elm, with a lid that slides and a thumb-notch to push it by. Aunt Lovage walked out with me to deliver it, both of us leaning into the wind like a pair of ladders.\n\nMrs. Skerrit writes down every ship that passes. I asked, not very casually, about a ketch called the Curlew, with my Da aboard. She ran her finger down the page. Not yet, she said, but she would look out specially, and she stood a candle from my box in the seaward window."],["November: The Box Within","November. Great-aunt Lovage has a birthday this month, which she denies. I am Tansy Wickett, in my eleventh month of making boxes in her workshop in Thimble Yard, and I built hers in secret, on the afternoons she goes to market.\n\nIt looks like a plain walnut box for hairpins. But the floor is not the real floor. Press the back edge and it tips, and underneath is a second space, just deep enough for a folded paper. I put one in. It says, Thank you for the year, in my best writing.\n\nAunt found the secret in under a minute. I know because I was watching round the door. She took an hour to mention it, and then she only said the hinge was well hung. A letter has come as well. The Curlew is homeward bound, and Da hopes to be in the Ember before midwinter."],["December: The Sea Chest","December, the twelfth box. I am Tansy Wickett. I came to my great-aunt Lovage in Thimble Yard in January, when my Da sailed north on the Curlew, and I have made a box every month since. The last is a sea chest, though only as long as my arm: oak, dovetailed without one gap, with rope handles I spliced myself.\n\nThe Curlew came up the Ember on the evening tide on the nineteenth. Mrs. Skerrit at the lighthouse sent word ahead, so I was on the quay with the chest in a wheelbarrow. Da is thinner and has a beard. He opened the lid there on the stones.\n\nInside I had put one shaving from each of the other eleven boxes, pine to walnut. He looked at them a long time. He is ashore until spring. Aunt Lovage has already put out the wood for January."]],[["The Nail by the Door","Pinned to the nail by the door of Strake’s boatshed, Saltings Hard, at the mouth of the Ember.\n\nEli. Abel Reedy, the marsh postman, has ordered a skiff to carry the letters round the creeks. It is the first proper order this shed has had since Father died, and it needs both of us, so I propose terms. I work the mornings. You work the afternoons. Whatever must be said goes on this nail. We have not spoken for nine years and I see no reason to spoil it over one boat. I have chalked her lines on the floor. Do not walk on them. Dorcas.\n\nDorcas. Agreed. I walked round your lines and they are fair ones, though she looks wide to me. I have picked out the oak for her keel and stood it by the bench. Your handwriting has not improved in nine years. Eli."],["Keel and Complaints","Two notes from the nail in Strake’s boatshed, where a sister and brother are building a skiff for the marsh postman without speaking to one another.\n\nEli. The keel is laid and it is straight. I sighted along it at first light with the gulls for company. I have set up the stem at the bow end and wedged it true. My mallet was not on its hook this morning. It was on the windowsill. Things have hooks for a reason. Dorcas.\n\nDorcas. The mallet was on the sill because the hook is where you hung your wet oilskin. I fitted the sternpost this afternoon and she has a backbone now, bow to stern. You can stand at one end and see a boat that is not there yet. Father used to say that was the best day. Also you leave shavings in the glue pot. Eli."],["Moulds and Manners","Dorcas. Nailed up in Strake’s shed at the end of my afternoon, since notes are all we exchange. I set the moulds along the keel of the postman’s skiff today, the board shapes that show where her planks will run. She has more belly than your chalk lines gave her. I looked twice. Did you think I would not notice? Eli.\n\nEli. She has more belly because Abel Reedy carries parcels as well as letters, and at midwinter a goose for every farm on the marsh. A narrow boat full of geese is a short story. I widened her amidships and would do it again. You may tell me I am wrong in writing. Bar the doors properly when you leave tonight. The tides are making, and you know why I ask. Dorcas.\n\nDorcas. I know why. I barred them. I always do, square in both brackets. Eli."],["The First Plank","Eli. For the nail by the door, there being no other way we talk. The first plank of Reedy’s skiff is on, the one that lies against the keel. It is larch, long as the shed, and it has to twist like a ribbon between bow and stern. I held one end with clamps, the middle with my knee and the far end with strong language. It took the whole morning. Dorcas.\n\nDorcas. I hung its partner on the other side this afternoon. It took me till dark, and I used your language and some of my own. Father and the two of us used to hang a plank in an hour. Two people could still. I mention it as arithmetic. Eli.\n\nEli. Two people would need a conversation first. I have left you the good clamps, the ones Father marked with a red stripe. Dorcas."],["Steam and Larch","Dorcas. Pinned up at Strake’s shed, Saltings Hard, with my left hand. I lit the steam box at noon. Larch will not take the bend at the skiff’s bow cold, so each plank cooks in the long wooden chest over the boiler until it droops like a wet towel, and then you run. You have about as long as it takes to say the alphabet. I got the plank on. I also got my thumb on the boiler lid. Eli.\n\nEli. Four planks a side now and they lie sweetly; I looked along them at first light. There is a pot of salve on this nail. It is Mother’s, goose fat and marigold. No note goes with it. Dorcas.\n\nDorcas. Found a pot. Used it. Not thanking anyone in particular. The postman’s boat is coming on. Eli."],["About the Petrel","Two notes from the nail in Strake’s boatshed, with the postman’s skiff half planked.\n\nDorcas. Nine years is long enough to say this on paper. The night the flood tide floated Father’s last boat, the Petrel, off her stocks and out to sea, I shut the shed. I dropped the bar across the big doors, square in both brackets, and left by the side door. You were in here after me. I have always supposed you lifted it to look at the tide. Eli.\n\nEli. I have read your note four times. I came back that night for my spectacles and never touched the bar. It was down, I grant you, but you always dropped it short at the seaward end, and I have supposed the water found that out. So each of us has been sure of the other. The seventh plank is ready for you. Dorcas."],["Rivets by Lamplight","Left on the nail, Strake’s boatshed, Saltings Hard. The Strakes have not spoken in nine years and are building a skiff by turns.\n\nEli. The planks must be riveted now, copper nails through each overlap, and it cannot be done alone. One of us holds a heavy iron against the nail head outside the hull while the other hammers the point over on the inside. I have tried it with a sandbag and a broom. Come at six. We need not talk. One knock on the planking means ready, two means next. Dorcas.\n\nDorcas. I came at six, as you know, since you were four feet away with a plank between us. Three hundred rivets by lamplight and not one word. You knock exactly as you used to on my bedroom wall. I had forgotten that. Same time tomorrow. Eli."],["Ribs and Rain","Notes from the nail by the door of Strake’s shed, where Dorcas and Eli Strake are building a skiff for Abel Reedy, the marsh postman.\n\nDorcas. Rain on the roof all day, which is good weather for bending ribs. Thin oak, steamed soft, pressed down into the hull and riveted before it changes its mind. We did eleven together this evening, still not speaking. Reedy called in to look. He said to tell my sister the boat is handsome. Consider yourself told. Eli.\n\nEli. He stood between us for half an hour and carried remarks from one to the other like a man passing plates. On his way out he said he has delivered letters for forty years and never before seen two people post them four feet. I did not laugh until he had gone. The last ribs go in tomorrow. Bring the short hammer. Dorcas."],["Seats and Second Thoughts","On the nail, Strake’s boatshed, Saltings Hard. A sister and brother who share the shed but not a word are fitting out the postman’s skiff.\n\nEli. The seats went in this morning, three of them, each braced to her sides with a knee of crooked oak that grew in that shape in a Lowland hedge. She is stiff as a basket now. I see you have built a locker under the stern seat for the mailbag, with a lid that sheds water and a little rack inside for the letters that must not be bent. It is good work. I have looked at it twice. Dorcas.\n\nDorcas. You looked three times. There are three sets of bootprints in the sawdust. Thank you. I did not think I would write that this year. I have rounded the edges of your seats, for Reedy’s knees. Eli."],["The Rotten Bracket","Two notes from the nail in Strake’s boatshed, Saltings Hard, written the week the doors were readied for the launch.\n\nEli. I took down the old iron brackets that hold the door bar, to swing the doors wide for her. The seaward one came away in my hand. The bolts behind it are rusted to threads and the post is soft as cheese, old rot, older than nine years. A child could have pushed that bar out. A flood tide certainly could. It never held, however it was dropped. It was not you. Dorcas.\n\nDorcas. I went to look, and stood some time. Nor you. I dropped that bar square the night the Petrel went, and you never touched it, and the sea lifted it out regardless. Father fitted that bracket and never would be told about it. Nine years. I will bring new oak for the post tomorrow. Eli."],["Paint and a Name","Dorcas. For the nail in Strake’s shed, in the last week of the postman’s skiff. Second coat is on: grey-green, the colour of the marsh in the morning, with a white line at her rail so Reedy can be seen in a mist. He says the builders are to name her. I thought Petrel, after Father’s boat that was lost from here. Eli.\n\nEli. No. That boat has had nine years of us already. This one was built by two people passing notes on a nail, for a man who carries letters. I have painted a name on a spare board for you to see. It says Reply. Dorcas.\n\nDorcas. Reply. Yes. I will cut the letters into her stern myself. I would like to come in the morning tomorrow, on your tide, if the morning will have me. Eli."],["Launch on the Morning Tide","The last note on the nail in Strake’s boatshed, Saltings Hard, in two hands on a single sheet.\n\nWe launched the Reply this morning on the top of the tide, the two of us on the ropes, and she slid down the hard and sat on the water like a gull. Abel Reedy stepped in with the mailbag, stowed it in its locker, and rowed off up the creek with the first post she ever carried. She does not leak a drop. Dorcas.\n\nFor the record, the first words spoken between the Strakes in nine years were “Mind your end,” and “Mind your own.” After that it came easier. We have talked since about the price of larch, and about Father, and about who is to have the mornings. Shed barred, new bracket holds. Gone to breakfast, both of us. Eli."]],[["What the Basket Holds","I am Marnie Tuck, mender, of number four Thimble Row in Emberwick. I do not make new things. There are dressmakers for that, and good luck to them. I put right what is torn, worn, burnt, outgrown or sat upon.\n\nBy my door stands a willow basket the size of a washtub. The rule is painted on the lid: pin a ticket to it, with your name and what is wrong. People leave things on their way to work and call back on Saturday. I shall keep this book for one year, January to December, and set down what the basket holds each month and whose it was.\n\nIt begins oddly. This morning, under two aprons, I found a fisherman’s jersey, dark blue, with the left elbow torn clean out. No ticket. No name. I have put it at the bottom until somebody owns up."],["The Baker’s Third Pocket","February in the mending basket at Thimble Row, and I, Marnie Tuck, have Osric Fenn’s apron again. Mr. Fenn bakes the town’s bread and keeps his mind on it. Everything else goes in his apron pocket, including, this week, a tray cloth straight from the oven door. That is the third pocket burnt through since harvest.\n\nI have lined the new one with mattress ticking, doubled, and told him it will stand anything short of an actual loaf tin. He looked thoughtful. I should not have mentioned the loaf tin.\n\nThe blue jersey with no ticket is still at the bottom of the basket. Nobody has asked for it. On Tuesday I had a fire, an idle hour, and a hole in an elbow, which is an irritating thing to sit near. I have picked up the stitches around the edge. That is all."],["A Coat Cut Down","March. I am Marnie Tuck, the mender on Thimble Row, and this month the basket by my door was too small for the job. Mrs. Ida Larch carried it in over her arm instead: her late husband’s brown overcoat, to be cut down for their grandson Wilf, who is seven and has gone through the elbows of everything he owns.\n\nShe asked to stay while I marked it with chalk. When I picked up the shears she remembered an errand at the chandler’s. I waited until the bell had stopped swinging before I cut.\n\nIt made a good small coat, with enough hem turned up to see Wilf through two winters. In the inside pocket I found a stub of carpenter’s pencil. I sewed the pocket into the new coat exactly where it had been and put the pencil back. Mrs. Larch did not ask, and I did not say."],["The Constable’s Misfortune","April, and a parcel in my mending basket that was not there at closing time. I am Marnie Tuck of Thimble Row, and I know brown paper tied by a man in a hurry when I see it. Inside: one pair of uniform trousers, split along the seat from stem to stern. The ticket said only “B.T. Urgent. Please.”\n\nConstable Bram Tolley came for them after dark, by the back door. It had been the miller’s gander, he said. It had got into the Corn Hall, and he had bent to reason with it.\n\nI had put in a double seam and a gusset, since a constable must be able to bend. He paid at once and asked whether the nature of the repair need be known. I said a mender is like a doctor. The gander is bound by no such rule, and has been seen looking pleased."],["Names Along the Hem","May brought the Sedge family’s christening gown to my basket at Thimble Row, wrapped in a pillowcase and carried four miles in from the marsh. I am Marnie Tuck. I mend, mostly, but once in a generation I am asked to add.\n\nThe gown is fine lawn, soft as a moth. Around the hem, in white thread on white, runs the name of every baby who has worn it: Josiah, Hepzibah, Tom, another Tom, Ruth. I counted nineteen. The new one is Clover. I worked her in after Ruth, in the same small backstitch, with room left beyond her.\n\nOne name, I noticed, had been unpicked and stitched again. The old needle holes show if you tilt the cloth. It was Katharine once, with a K, and is now Catherine. Somebody felt strongly. I left it exactly as I found it. A mender does not take sides in a hem."],["The Boy at the Window","June, and I must put down something about the blue jersey. I am Marnie Tuck, mender, of Thimble Row, and that jersey has lain at the bottom of my basket since January with its elbow out and no ticket on it. I have been mending it in odd half hours, because nobody else will. The elbow is knitted in again. This week, turning up the hem, I found two letters worked into the rib: E.A. That is a Grey Coast habit, so that a jersey left on a quay goes home to the right man.\n\nI know of no E.A. in Emberwick.\n\nBut there is a boy. Nine or so, hair like a hayrick. Three mornings this month he has stood at my window looking at the basket, not at me. Each time I reached the door, the lane was empty. He is quick. I am patient."],["The Scarecrow’s Sunday Best","July at Thimble Row. Six children from the Lowlands school filled my shop on Monday, all talking, carrying between them a coat that had plainly spent the winter outdoors. I am Marnie Tuck, mender, and I asked for the ticket. The smallest girl produced it. It read: “Old Barnaby. Coat. Everything.”\n\nOld Barnaby stands in Farmer Roke’s bean field with a turnip for a head. Rooks had been sitting on his shoulders quite unafraid, the children said, and it was shaming him.\n\nI do not mend by halves, whoever the customer is. I patched both shoulders in red flannel, which rooks dislike, sewed back the collar, and added four brass buttons from the tin, because a scarecrow with buttons stands straighter. The children paid in broad beans. On Sunday I walked out past the field. Not a rook. Barnaby had his arms out as though about to make a speech."],["Half a Lion","August. The travelling players have set up in the Corn Hall, and on Wednesday their lion came to my shop on Thimble Row. I am Marnie Tuck, and I have mended most things, but not, until now, a lion.\n\nHis tail hung by three threads. The moth had been at his mane. The man inside him, a small mild person called Mr. Hobb, explained that there was a performance at seven and no second lion. So I mended the top half while he sat by my stove in the bottom half, drinking tea, with his furry feet crossed.\n\nI sewed the tail on with waxed button thread and a square of canvas behind, and told him he could be swung by it now if the play required. He said, rather wistfully, that he had only the one line. I went on Friday. He roared it beautifully, and the tail held."],["Fourpence and a Name","September, and the boy came in. I am Marnie Tuck, mender, of Thimble Row, and since January a blue fisherman’s jersey has sat in my basket without a ticket. Now I have the ticket’s worth by word of mouth.\n\nHe is Col Ashby, aged nine. The jersey is his father’s spare. His father is Edrin Ashby, who works the herring boats out of Saltgate on the Grey Coast and comes home at midwinter. Col wore the jersey up the schoolhouse apple tree last winter, to feel what it was like, and came down without the elbow. He had no money, so he left no name.\n\nHe put down fourpence and asked how much of an elbow that would buy. I said the price was fourpence and Saturday mornings, sorting my button tin, until the work was paid off. He shook hands on it like a man selling a horse."],["The Left Sock","October on Thimble Row. I am Marnie Tuck, mender, and on Saturdays I now have an assistant: Col Ashby, aged nine, who is working off the cost of a jersey elbow. He has sorted my button tin into shirt, coat, boot and “interesting”. The interesting pile is the largest.\n\nThis month he asked to learn darning. Mr. Gant the lamplighter had left a pair of grey socks in the basket, and I gave Col the left one, a wooden mushroom, and a needle. He wove over and under for an hour with his tongue between his teeth. The result was sturdy, and about the shape of a small potato.\n\nI showed it to Mr. Gant honestly, beside my own. He turned Col’s over twice and said he would take it; his left foot had never been particular. Col walked home a clear inch taller, by my measure."],["Weather from the Coast","November. A gale came in off the Grey Coast on the ninth and blew for three days, and I, Marnie Tuck, mender, of Thimble Row, had a very quiet boy in my shop that Saturday. Col Ashby’s father is out with the Saltgate herring boats. News from the coast takes four days by the carrier.\n\nI did not tell him not to worry. I got down the blue jersey, his father’s, the one we have been mending all year, and said the cuffs were fraying and I wanted them knitted afresh before midwinter. We unravelled them together. I picked up the stitches, and then I put the needles in his hands.\n\nHe knitted eleven rounds that morning, tight as a drum. On Wednesday the carrier called from his cart that every Saltgate boat was in. Col’s eleven rounds are still in the left cuff. I have not loosened them."],["Paid in Full","December, and the last page of my year. I am Marnie Tuck, mender, of Thimble Row. On the twentieth a broad man in a salt-stained cap came in, with Col Ashby pulling him by the sleeve. Edrin Ashby was home from the herring boats, and had come for his jersey.\n\nHe turned it over in his big hands. He found the new elbow, and the tight left cuff, and asked what he owed. I showed him the account: fourpence, and thirteen Saturday mornings, received with thanks. He looked at his son for some time. Then he said he was glad the mend showed. It meant somebody had minded.\n\nThey went up the lane with the jersey on him and Col tucked under one arm of it. Tonight the basket is empty, for the first time since January. It will not last, and I should be sorry if it did."]],[["In and Out","This is a book of stitches, one to a lesson, by Odile Farrow, who has taught the Thursday sewing class above Varley the chandler’s in Emberwick for twenty-six years. You will need a square of linen, a blunt needle, and patience. I can supply the first two.\n\nLesson one is running stitch. Bring the needle up, go forward the width of a barley grain, and go down. Come up again the same distance on. In and out, each stitch as long as the gap beside it. Everything else in sewing is this stitch with ideas above itself.\n\nTwo pupils began this January. Tansy Wickett is ten and finished her row before I had finished explaining it. Bertram Loach is fifty-three, works the ferry on the Ember, and wants to make a sampler for his mother’s ninetieth birthday in December. His row took the hour. It was straighter than Tansy’s."],["One Step Back","Lesson two, backstitch. I am Odile Farrow, and I teach sewing on Thursdays above the chandler’s in Emberwick. Backstitch is for anything that must be a solid line: a seam, a stem, a letter. Bring the needle up one stitch ahead of where you finished. Go back and down into the end of the last stitch. Come up one stitch ahead again. You travel forward by going backward, which my older pupils say is also how they get through a week.\n\nMy ferryman, Bertram Loach, has hands made for oars. The needle vanishes in them. Each time his thread came out of the eye he looked at it as a man looks for a dropped sixpence in long grass.\n\nTansy Wickett, ten, watched this three times. Then she took his needle, threaded it, and handed it back. He said, “Much obliged, miss.” They have shared a bench since."],["Every Cross the Same Way","Lesson three, from Odile Farrow’s Thursday class above the chandler’s in Emberwick: cross stitch. Linen is woven in a grid, and cross stitch sits on it like a mark on squared paper. Make one slanting stitch, bottom left to top right. Cross it with another, bottom right to top left. The one rule is that every top arm must slant the same way. Break it and the light catches each cross differently, and your work looks ruffled, like a cat stroked backwards.\n\nA sampler begins with its alphabet, so this month the letters went on. Tansy Wickett, ten, reached G in an evening.\n\nBertram Loach, the ferryman, who is stitching a sampler for his mother’s ninetieth birthday, made one capital A. It took three Thursdays. Every arm lies the same way. He carried it round the room to show each of us in turn, and nobody minded in the least."],["The Honest Unpick","Lesson four is not a stitch. I am Odile Farrow, I have taught sewing in Emberwick for twenty-six years, and I say the most useful thing a needle does is go backwards. When a row is wrong, unpick it. Use the eye of the needle, not the point, so that you lift the thread and do not split it. Go one stitch at a time. Never pull.\n\nTansy Wickett, who is ten and quick, does not hold with this. She would rather sew a flower over a mistake and call it a garden.\n\nThen Bertram Loach, my ferryman, found that the whole second line of his alphabet, H to P, sat one thread lower than the first. Nine letters, a month’s work. Then he turned his needle round and began. Tansy watched him as far as the K, sighed like a kettle, and started unpicking her garden."],["Filling the Ferry","Lesson five in this book of stitches is satin stitch, and I, Odile Farrow, warn you that it shows everything. It is for filling a shape with colour. Lay straight stitches side by side, so close no linen shows between, each one right across the shape. Do not pull them tight, or the cloth puckers like a mouth tasting a lemon. Let the thread lie.\n\nBelow his alphabet my ferryman pupil, Bertram Loach, has drawn his own boat, flat-bottomed, with the rope and the pole. He is filling her in brown.\n\nThe Thursday class had opinions. Tansy Wickett, ten, said the real ferry is more green than brown, from weed. Mr. Loach said this was his mother’s birthday sampler and the ferry would be shown as she ought to be. The hull took him all of May, and it lies as flat as the Ember on a still morning."],["A River in Chain","Lesson six, chain stitch. Bring the needle up, then put it back down in the same hole, leaving a loop on the surface. Come up a stitch farther on, inside the loop, and pull gently. Each link is held by the one after it. It makes a soft line, good for rope, vines and water.\n\nI am Odile Farrow, of the Thursday class in Emberwick, and this June my pupil Bertram Loach ran the river Ember in blue chain across his sampler, under his brown ferry. While he worked he told us why he is making it.\n\nHis mother, Agnes Loach, kept the dame school at Ferry End for forty years. Every child stitched a sampler there. Her own she gave away, to a girl leaving for the Grey Coast with nothing to take. She has taught half the town its letters, and has none on her wall."],["Eleven Lost Knots","Lesson seven is the seed knot, and I, Odile Farrow, have seen it reduce grown pupils to silence. Bring the needle up. Wind the thread twice round it. Keep it taut with your other hand and put the needle down beside where it came up, not into the same hole. Draw it through slowly. A firm bead sits on the linen. Use the same hole, and the knot follows the needle through and is never seen again.\n\nBertram Loach, the ferryman, wanted stars above the boat on his mother’s sampler. He lost eleven knots through the cloth in one evening. I counted them by the sound he made.\n\nTansy Wickett, ten, got hers right at the first try and said so, several times. Mr. Loach bore it. Then he asked her to show him where his left hand should be. She did. There are seven stars over the ferry."],["Stitching Between Crossings","Lesson eight is stem stitch, for anything that bends. Work from left to right. Take a small stitch backwards along your line and bring the needle up halfway along the stitch before. Keep the thread always below the needle, never above. The stitches overlap like a twist of cord and will follow any curve.\n\nI am Odile Farrow, and my Thursday class above the chandler’s does not meet in August. So Bertram Loach, who is sewing a sampler for his mother’s ninetieth birthday, took his reeds to work. He stitched them along the river bank on the cloth while sitting beside the real one, between crossings of his ferry.\n\nHis passengers helped. A farmer told him the reeds leaned the wrong way for the wind. A lady from the Lowlands said green was unlucky. Mr. Loach thanked them, charged them a penny each for the crossing, and changed nothing."],["Where the Corners Meet","Lesson nine in Odile Farrow’s book is herringbone, and with it, the border. Rule two faint lines. Take a small stitch on the top line, then a small stitch on the bottom line a little farther on, back and forth, until the long threads cross each other in a row of slanting legs. It looks like a fish’s backbone, or a fence drawn by somebody in a hurry.\n\nA border must go round four sides and meet itself, so count the threads before you start. My ferryman, Bertram Loach, counted. At the last corner of his mother’s sampler the herringbone still arrived half a stitch early.\n\nTansy Wickett, who is ten and finished her own sampler in July, said, “Put a flower on it.” She looked at me, and I am afraid I nodded. Some gaps are not mistakes, only arithmetic. A flower gives a corner something to do."],["A Damp Secret","Lesson ten, blanket stitch, is for edges. Bring the needle up on the edge of the cloth. Go down a little way in, and come out at the edge again with the thread looped behind the needle. Pull, and a bar lies along the rim. Repeat evenly and no edge will fray.\n\nI am Odile Farrow, sewing teacher of Emberwick, and in October my ferryman, Bertram Loach, was edging the secret sampler for his mother’s ninetieth birthday. Then his mother came down to the landing with his dinner. He pushed the sampler under the ferry seat. It stayed there through two crossings and a shower.\n\nHe brought it to class like a man carrying a drowned kitten. So, a second lesson. Lay damp work face down on a towel and press the back with a warm iron. The stitches sink into the towel and come up proud. His did."],["Words for the Middle","Lesson eleven. Every sampler carries some words, and I, Odile Farrow, have stitched and taught a great many that I should not care to live with: verses about duty, and early rising. Choose words you would be glad to read every day for forty years. Space them out on paper first, then work them in backstitch.\n\nBertram Loach, the ferryman, thought about his mother’s birthday sampler for two Thursdays. Then he wrote down what Agnes Loach said at the door of her dame school at Ferry End, every afternoon, to every child: “Mind the step. Come back tomorrow.”\n\nHe stitched it under the river in dark red, with his name and the year beneath. It was Tansy Wickett, ten, who saw that the N in “Mind” faced the wrong way. This was the first Thursday in December, with no time to unpick. I told him to let it be."],["Fastening Off","The last lesson is how to finish. Never end with a knot. Turn the work over, run the needle under four or five stitches, and snip close. The stitches hold it, and the back stays as honest as the front.\n\nI am Odile Farrow, sewing teacher, and on her ninetieth birthday I went with my pupil Bertram Loach, the ferryman, to his mother’s kitchen at Ferry End. Tansy Wickett came to carry the parcel. Agnes Loach put on her spectacles and read the sampler slowly: alphabet, stars, ferry, river, reeds. At the words she stopped. “Bertram,” she said, “your N is backwards. It always was.” Then she asked him to hang it where she could see it from her chair.\n\nIt hangs there now. Tansy begins in January as my assistant. I close this book as I would a seam: under a few stitches, and no knot."]],[["Twelve Iron Beds","On the first Tuesday of February the schoolmaster of Emberwick, Mr. Alder Finch, stood up in the Corn Hall and explained about Brindle House. The old toll house by the bridge was to be made a winter lodging. Twelve children from the Northern Fells, who missed half their schooling whenever snow shut the high roads, would board there from November. The infirmary had given twelve iron beds. There were, as yet, no bedcovers.\n\nMrs. Dorcas Pimm, the draper’s widow, was on her feet before he had sat down. “A quilt for every bed,” she said. “The Tuesday Thimbles will see to it.”\n\nThe hall applauded. Dorcas sat, and only then counted the Tuesday Thimbles on her fingers. Herself. Old Hetty Marlow. The two Miss Vosses, who were not speaking. Ned Sorrel, the saddler’s boy. Five quilters, twelve quilts, nine months. She decided not to do the sum aloud."],["Thirty Years of Dresses","The first of the twelve quilts for Brindle House, where the fell children were to lodge, was begun by Dorcas Pimm on her own kitchen table in Emberwick. She had promised a quilt for every bed. It seemed only fair to start with her own scrap bag.\n\nThe bag held a snippet of every dress she had made in thirty years as the draper’s wife. Out came the green sprig from her sister’s wedding, a mourning grey, a yellow check from a summer she could no longer place. She cut them into oblongs and set them climbing corner to corner across the table, a pattern her mother had called Ember Steps.\n\nIt took all of March and every inch of her yellow thread. She spread it over the kitchen chairs and walked round it twice. Then she chalked a figure on the slate by the door: eleven."],["Hetty’s Long Stitches","The Tuesday Thimbles of Emberwick had promised twelve quilts for the twelve beds at Brindle House, and the second was Hetty Marlow’s. Hetty was eighty-one. She had quilted since she was six and saw little now, so she worked by touch, in plain squares of blue and cream, nine to a block. She called it Nine Windows.\n\nHer stitches were enormous. A beetle could have walked under them. The two Miss Vosses exchanged the only look they had shared all year, and Prudence Voss murmured that perhaps, after Hetty went home, somebody might go over it.\n\nDorcas Pimm said no. She ran her hand across the quilt with her eyes shut. Every stitch stood up under her fingers like a row of small knuckles. “Some child,” she said, “will wake in the dark in a strange house. That child will be able to find this.” Nobody went over it."],["Stitched to Hold a Horse","Ned Sorrel was fifteen, apprenticed to the saddler in Emberwick, and the only one of the Tuesday Thimbles who had joined by accident. He had come to deliver a strap and been handed a needle. Now the circle owed twelve quilts to the twelve beds at Brindle House, and the third was his.\n\nHe had no scrap bag, so he begged flour sacks from the mill and feed sacks from the corn merchant, washed them soft, and pieced them in stripes of white and oat-brown. He sewed as he sewed harness, with two needles and waxed thread, every stitch pulled down hard.\n\nMrs. Dorcas Pimm examined it in May. It was not pretty. She took one end, gave Hetty Marlow the other, and they pulled. Nothing gave. “You could lead a horse with it,” Dorcas said, and Ned went red to the ears, never having had a finer compliment."],["The Matter of the Red","Clemency and Prudence Voss, sisters, of Mill Lane in Emberwick, had not spoken since a disagreement over damson jam eleven years before. They sat at opposite ends of the Tuesday Thimbles’ table, and each undertook one of the twelve quilts promised to Brindle House. Clemency’s was the fourth, all stars. Prudence’s was the fifth, all baskets.\n\nBoth patterns wanted red. There was one bolt of red flannel in the town, on the top shelf of Pimm’s the draper’s, and Dorcas Pimm, who owned the shelf, cut it in half.\n\nIt was not enough. In June Prudence ran short by four basket handles. A paper of red scraps appeared at her place, unaddressed. A week later Clemency wanted three star points, and found a paper at hers. By midsummer both quilts were done, each held some of the other’s red, and the sisters had got as far as “Thank you.”"],["The Notice in the Window","At midsummer Dorcas Pimm looked at the slate by her kitchen door. Five quilts were finished for Brindle House, where twelve children from the Northern Fells would lodge come the snow. Seven beds were bare, and five of her nine months were gone. The Tuesday Thimbles numbered five, and one of them was eighty-one.\n\nDorcas did not care to ask for help. She did it anyway. She put a card in the baker’s window: one square of cloth wanted from every house in Emberwick, a span each way.\n\nThey came in for a fortnight. The baker sent a corner of apron, slightly scorched. The constable sent uniform blue. There was ferry sail, the back of a lamplighter’s glove, and from the library a strip of green book-cloth. Eighty squares made the sixth quilt. The Thimbles called it the Town Quilt, and argued over whose square was whose until dark."],["Wool from the Fells","By July the Tuesday Thimbles of Emberwick had made six of the twelve quilts promised to Brindle House, and had nothing left to fill the seventh. A quilt is a top, a back, and a warm middle, and the town had been combed of old blankets.\n\nThe middle walked in on a Tuesday. Greta Howe, a shepherd’s wife from the Northern Fells, had come eleven miles down the drove road with a pack of washed fleece on her back. Her two children would be boarding at Brindle House. She had heard there were strangers sewing for them, and she did not like to be beholden.\n\nShe stayed three weeks, carded the wool, and pieced the seventh quilt herself, grey and white, in a fell pattern of crossing paths called Sheep Trod. Dorcas Pimm asked which child it was for. Greta said that was the point. She would not know."],["Two Crooked Ones","The eighth and ninth of the twelve quilts for Brindle House were made in August by the schoolchildren of Emberwick, who had heard that fell children were coming to lodge for the winter and had views about what a bed needed.\n\nNed Sorrel, the saddler’s apprentice and the youngest of the Tuesday Thimbles, was put in charge, being fifteen and therefore nearly one of them. He taught running stitch to twenty-three children. He confiscated one frog.\n\nNo two squares came out the same size. The seams wandered. One quilt had a pocket sewn on the front with a marble in it, because a boy named Arlo said you needed somewhere to keep things. The other had a sun with a face. Mrs. Dorcas Pimm looked them over for a minute. Then she took out not one stitch, bound the edges, and chalked a new number on her slate: three."],["Sixty Years Unfinished","In September Hetty Marlow, who was eighty-one and the oldest of the Tuesday Thimbles, asked Ned Sorrel to carry a chest down from her attic. The circle still owed three quilts of the twelve promised to the children’s lodging at Brindle House, and Hetty had remembered something.\n\nUnder the winter curtains lay a quilt top, pieced and never quilted: small pink and brown triangles in rows, like geese going over. Her mother had begun it the year Hetty married, and put it by when her eyes failed. That was sixty years ago. The needle was still in it.\n\nThe Thimbles quilted it in a week, all five, following pencil lines so faint that Clemency Voss had to read them out to her sister. Hetty sewed the last corner by touch. Her stitches were enormous. Her mother’s, beside them, were tiny. Dorcas Pimm said they looked very well together."],["All Night at the Corn Hall","October came in cold, and the Tuesday Thimbles of Emberwick had ten quilts finished and two beds at Brindle House bare. The shepherds were saying snow within the month. Dorcas Pimm stopped trusting to Tuesdays. She borrowed the Corn Hall, set up the frame, and sent Ned Sorrel round the town with a handbell.\n\nThirty-one people came. The baker brought buns at midnight. The constable, who could not sew, held the lamp where he was told. Around the frame sat the Voss sisters side by side, Hetty Marlow, Greta Howe down again from the Fells, and anybody who could find an inch of edge.\n\nThe quilt was small yellow diamonds on dark blue, a pattern called Marsh Lights. They put the last stitch in as the windows turned grey. Dorcas went home and chalked on her slate, with a hand that shook slightly: one. Then she slept until Wednesday."],["The Crumb Quilt","Eleven quilts lay in Dorcas Pimm’s parlour in Emberwick, and not a yard of cloth was left in the town for the twelfth. The fell children were due at Brindle House that week. Dorcas had promised a quilt for every bed, in public, and she sat among the eleven feeling the want of one.\n\nIt was Prudence Voss who looked under the table. Nine months of quilting had left a drift of offcuts there: corners of Dorcas’s dresses, Hetty’s blue and cream, Ned’s flour sack, red flannel, a scrap of constable. Nothing was bigger than a playing card.\n\nThe Tuesday Thimbles sewed them together as they came, with no pattern. Clemency Voss said it was the ugliest thing she had ever helped make, and her sister agreed with her out loud, which had not happened in eleven years. They finished it on the Friday. On the Saturday it snowed."],["The Thirteenth Bed","The twelve children from the Northern Fells reached Brindle House in Emberwick on a carrier’s cart in the first snow of November. Twelve iron beds stood ready, and on each lay a quilt made by the Tuesday Thimbles. Dorcas Pimm stood by the stove, pretending not to watch.\n\nThe children chose. A tall girl took Ember Steps. A boy found the pocket and the marble. Greta Howe’s daughter went straight to Sheep Trod, though nobody had told her. Her brother Pip, aged six and the smallest, took the crumb quilt, and spent the evening finding a bit of every other bed in it.\n\nDorcas was putting on her gloves when she passed the housekeeper’s room off the kitchen. Mrs. Bray’s bed had one grey blanket on it. Dorcas looked at it for some time. Then she sighed, took her gloves off, and felt in her pocket for a needle."]],[["Casting On","Gull Staithe, the Grey Coast, September. Dear Linnet, your letter came by the carrier, only a little damp. You ask if I will teach you to knit, as your gran would have. I will go one better. I am seventy-four, my knee has put me ashore, and my nephew Davy has the Kittiwake now. I shall knit you a gansey, the coast jersey, in dark blue wool, one band of pattern a month, and write how it goes. Come next August and fetch it.\n\nYou shall work too. I enclose a yard of cod line. Most letters will bring a knot. This month, the reef knot: left over right and under, then right over left and under. If it sits lumpy you have made a granny and must begin again.\n\nWeather: first frost on the drying nets, white as sugar. I cast on tonight. Your grandfather, Tam Garrow."],["Your Gran’s Needles","Gull Staithe, October. Dear Linnet, the gansey is begun. A gansey is knitted in the round on five steel needles, with no seams. These needles were your gran’s. Bess wore the ends of them bright. I also use her knitting stick, a piece of applewood that tucks in my belt and steadies the needle, so one hand is free to shoo the cat.\n\nI have done the ribbed hem, and above it, in purl stitches, two letters: L.G., for Linnet Garrow. Every gansey on this coast carries its owner’s letters. Left on the quay, it finds its way home.\n\nYour knot is the figure of eight. Make a loop, pass the end behind the standing line, and bring it back through the loop. It stops a rope running out through a ring. Weather: three gales in a fortnight, boats hauled up, everybody cross. Your grandfather, Tam."],["Isaac’s Rope","Gull Staithe, November. Dear Linnet, this month the first band of pattern went into your gansey, and it is a cable: six stitches crossed over one another every sixth round, so they twist like rope. It is for Isaac Thrale. He is eighty and has kept the ropewalk behind the chapel all his life. He walks backwards down that shed twisting hemp, forty times a day, and has never once looked over his shoulder. Every boat here hangs on his work.\n\nSo your knot is the bowline, the king of them. Make a small loop in the line. Bring the end up through it, round behind the standing part, and back down the loop. It makes an eye that will neither slip nor jam.\n\nWeather: fog for nine days. I knit by the sound of the fog bell and count rounds on my fingers. Your grandfather, Tam."],["Peg’s Diamonds","Gull Staithe, December. Dear Linnet, the second band of your gansey is a row of open diamonds in purl. We call it net mesh, and it is for Peg Cuddy. Peg mends the nets on the quay in all weathers, in mittens with no fingers, and finds a torn mesh faster than you read a line of print. She looked at my knitting and said my tension had gone slack since I came ashore. I have tightened it. One does not argue with Peg.\n\nYour knot is hers: the sheet bend. Make a bight in one cord. Bring the other up through it, round the back of both parts, and tuck it under itself. Every mesh of every net on this coast is that knot.\n\nWeather: snow lying on the shingle, which I have seen only four times. The gulls are walking in it looking offended. Your grandfather, Tam."],["The Night of the Ladder","Gull Staithe, January. Dear Linnet, on the fourteenth it blew from the north-east, and at dusk five boats were in and the Kittiwake was not. She was mine thirty years; my nephew Davy has her now. I sat in the window with your gansey, because hands must do something, and knitted the third band. It is the ladder: two purl rungs, two plain, climbing. It is the harbour steps, where everybody waits.\n\nDavy came in at nine with a split sail and a full hold, grinning like a fool. Afterwards I found I had dropped six stitches and knitted one rung twice. I picked up the six. The doubled rung I have left in. It is a true record.\n\nYour knot: the clove hitch. Two turns round a post, the second crossing the first, the end tucked under the crossing. The Kittiwake is tied so tonight. Your grandfather, Tam."],["A Tide Mark in Blue","Gull Staithe, February. Dear Linnet, you ask why your gansey has other people’s patterns and none of our own. On this coast each family keeps its bands. But your father went inland to be a clerk in Emberwick, and you were born beside a river, not a sea. So I am giving you the whole village instead: the ropemaker’s cable, the net mender’s diamonds, the harbour steps. This month, a band of moss stitch, one plain, one purl, for the shingle we all walk on.\n\nI ran out of wool halfway up. Ada Brock, the spinner at Fell Foot, sent more, and it is a shade darker. There will be a faint line across you, like a tide mark. I rather like it.\n\nKnot: the round turn and two half hitches, for making fast to a ring. Weather: still and cold, the sea like slate. Your grandfather, Tam."],["Nan and the Cliff Path","Gull Staithe, March. Dear Linnet, your knitted square came safe. It is wider at one end, rather like a map of the Lowlands, and I have pinned it above the mantel where Peg Cuddy can admire it. Keep on. My first was worse, and I was older.\n\nThis month’s band on your gansey is the zigzag, plain stitches stepping left and then right. That is the cliff path, for Nan Pellow, our postwoman, who walks it to the coast road and back daily, six miles, in whatever is blowing. Every letter of yours has come along that path in her hands.\n\nYour knot is the fisherman’s knot, for joining two lines. Tie an overhand knot in each end around the other line, and pull. They slide together and lock. Weather: wind. When the gulls sit on the chapel roof facing inland, we take the washing in. Your grandfather, Tam."],["Room to Reach","Gull Staithe, April. Dear Linnet, the body of your gansey has reached the armholes, and here is the cleverest thing in it. Under each arm I am knitting a gusset: a diamond of extra stitches, widening, then narrowing. You would never notice it. Without it a jersey rides up each time you lift your arm; with it you may haul a net or reach for an apple and the hem stays put.\n\nI think of my nephew Davy when I knit them. I watched him from the quay hauling pots hand over hand. A fisherman is mostly reach.\n\nYour knot is the sheepshank, which shortens a rope without cutting it: fold the slack in three and drop a half hitch over each end. In fifty years I have needed it twice, but it is handsome. Weather: showers, and a rainbow on the harbour bar most afternoons. Your grandfather, Tam."],["Hen’s Feet","Gull Staithe, May. Dear Linnet, across the chest of your gansey, where the best band goes, I have put your gran’s. Bess had a pattern of her own: small chevrons, three stitches wide. She called it hen’s feet. She knitted it into every gansey I wore for forty years and never wrote it down.\n\nSo I took the last one she made me out of the chest and sat in the doorway reading it stitch by stitch, with a pencil, like a man copying a letter. It took two evenings. Peg Cuddy, who mends the nets, went by twice and for once said nothing.\n\nIt is in now, twelve rows of it, and I think Bess would pass it. No new knot. Tie the eight you have, ten times each, with your eyes shut. Weather: a warm white sea fret every morning, gone by ten. Your grandfather, Tam."],["The Old Men’s Bench","Gull Staithe, June. Dear Linnet, the shoulders of your gansey are joined, and there is no sewing in it. I hold the front and back needles side by side and knit one stitch from each together, casting off as I go. It leaves a ridge like the keel of an upturned boat. Then the neck, in rib, loose enough to go over your head and plaits.\n\nThese light evenings I knit on the bench outside the net loft with the other old men. Isaac Thrale the ropemaker says the neck is too wide. Old Jory Kell says too narrow. Neither has knitted a stitch, and I have told them so.\n\nNo knot this month, but a habit. Coil your line in even rings, with a twist of the wrist at each turn, and hang it up. Weather: calm for a week, and mackerel in the bay. Your grandfather, Tam."],["Made to Be Mended","Gull Staithe, July. Dear Linnet, the sleeves are done, and so is your gansey. I knitted them downward, shoulder to wrist. Every gansey is made so. Cuffs wear first, and when yours do you snip one thread, unravel to sound wool, and knit the cuff again. A gansey is made expecting to be mended. I think well of anything built that way.\n\nI have made the sleeves long. You are eleven and not finished growing. And above each cuff I have left one band plain, for a reason I will tell you when you come.\n\nIt is washed in rainwater and dried flat on the garden wall, with a stone on each corner and the cat in the middle. Take the carrier to the coast road on the first of August. Nan Pellow will walk you down the cliff path. Weather: thunder out at sea. Your grandfather, Tam."],["The Plain Band","Gull Staithe, August. Dear Linnet, you have been gone two days and the house is very tidy. I did not say enough on the quay, so here it is.\n\nThe gansey fits, with room for two more Augusts. Peg Cuddy looked you over and said “It’ll do,” which from Peg is a speech. You tied the Kittiwake to the ring with a clove hitch, and a second for luck, and Davy pretended not to see. You went out past the bar on a flat sea and hauled a pot, and the hem stayed put.\n\nThe plain band above the cuffs is yours. Every other band is somebody of mine. When you have a pattern of your own, knit it in there. Bess’s needles and her knitting stick are in this parcel. Weather: the first cold dawn, and frost soon on the nets. Cast on. Your grandfather, Tam Garrow."]],[["What Hester Left","When Bram Coddle gave up the Emberwick ferry after forty years, he expected to spend his days sitting down. Then his aunt Hester died, at ninety, and left him her pottery in Kiln Lane: one low shed, one cold kiln, eleven sacks of clay and a kick wheel she had always called Maud.\n\nBram had never made a pot in his life. He had, however, broken one. On the bench lay Hester’s order book, and its final line, in her square hand, read: One porridge bowl for Bram, who broke his. Not yet begun.\n\nHe read it twice, standing among the dust sheets. Then he took off his coat, hung it on the nail where her apron still was, and looked at the wheel. The wheel, it seemed to him, looked back. Neither of them was impressed."],["The First Kick","Bram Coddle, forty years a ferryman, sat down at his late aunt’s kick wheel on a Monday in April, meaning to have a porridge bowl made by dinner time.\n\nThe wheel was a heavy thing: a stone flywheel below, a small oak wheelhead above, and an iron spindle between. Hester had called it Maud. Bram set a lump of clay in the middle, wetted his hands as he had seen her do, and kicked. Maud groaned like a gate. She went round nine times, each turn slower and more grudging than the last, and stopped. Bram kicked harder. She managed eleven turns, shuddered, and stopped again, and the clay slid gently off the wheelhead and into his lap.\n\nHe sat a while with it there. On the ferry, he reflected, when you pushed a thing, it had at least the manners to move."],["Oil and Opinions","Word went down Kiln Lane that Bram Coddle, the old ferryman, could not make his aunt Hester’s kick wheel go round. By Wednesday he had more advice than clay.\n\nThe baker said the floor was out of true. The blacksmith said the spindle wanted replacing and he could do it for nine shillings. Mrs Larkin next door said Hester used to sing to it. Bram tried the cheapest remedy first. He fetched the tub of grease he had used on the ferry winch, and he oiled the spindle top and bottom until it shone like an eel.\n\nThen he kicked. The wheel turned exactly as before: nine sour turns, a shudder, a stop. The only change was the smell, which was now strongly of the river. He did not try singing. A man has his limits, and the window was open."],["Levelling the Floor","The baker had told Bram Coddle that the floor of his late aunt’s pottery sloped, and that this was why her old kick wheel fought him at every turn. Bram, a retired ferryman with time on his hands, set a bowl of water on the wheelhead to see. The water sat as flat as a pond.\n\nHe levelled the floor anyway. He spent two days tapping oak wedges under the wheel’s legs, checking the bowl, and tapping again. When he had finished, it was the most level object in Emberwick. He kicked it. Nine turns, a shudder, a stop.\n\nA small girl was watching from the doorway with a basket of eggs. This was Pim Larkin from next door, aged nine. “Miss Hester’s went round for ages,” she said. “It hummed.” Bram thanked her for the eggs and, with great self-control, for the information."],["Clay on the Beam","By May, Bram Coddle had worked out one way to keep his aunt Hester’s stubborn wheel turning: kick it without stopping, hard, like a man running upstairs. This left him no breath and no attention for the clay, but he had been a ferryman, and ferrymen are used to doing two things at once.\n\nOn the Thursday he got the wheel going at a furious lick. The clay rose under his hands into something very like a bowl. He had time to feel proud. Then the wheel gave its shudder, and the bowl leaned, let go of the wheelhead altogether, and flew. It struck the roof beam above the door and stayed there.\n\nBram looked at it for some time. It was, he decided, the best thing he had made so far. He left it on the beam, where it dried, and where it remains."],["The Wheelwright Replies","After two months of being beaten by his late aunt’s kick wheel, Bram Coddle of Kiln Lane swallowed his pride and wrote to the man who had built it: Osric Thane, wheelwright, of Cragfold in the Northern Fells. He described the nine grudging turns, the shudder, the stop. He enclosed a stamp.\n\nThe answer came in a week, on the back of a bill for cart spokes. “I made that wheel for Hester Coddle fifty years ago and it has never wanted anything but clay. There is nothing wrong with it. I would look to the kicker.”\n\nBram read this aloud to the wheel, with feeling. He had carried half of Emberwick across the Ember, in all weathers, without once being told to look to the ferryman. He put the letter on the fire. Then, being honest, he took it off again and kept it."],["The Ferryman’s Lean","Bram Coddle had stood forty years at a ferry pole, and it had left him with a lean: left shoulder down, weight on the right foot, as if the floor might drift. He never noticed it until he took up his late aunt’s pottery. The clay noticed at once.\n\nEverything he coaxed off her stubborn wheel that June leaned the way he did. There was a dish that sloped like a hat brim. There was a pot with one high side, as though it were listening for something. Pim Larkin from next door, who was nine, named them as they came: the Slipper, the Nosy One, the Boat.\n\nStill, they were pots. They held water, mostly. Bram lined them up on the sill beside Hester’s own straight-backed jugs, where they looked like a family in which the good sense had run out early."],["Hester’s Clogs","Sweeping under the bench one wet morning, Bram Coddle found his aunt Hester’s work clogs. She had been the potter of Kiln Lane for fifty years, and he, a retired ferryman, had been failing to work her kick wheel since April.\n\nThey were plain alder clogs, grey with clay. The odd thing was the wear. The right one might have been bought last week. The left was worn through at the ball of the foot, right through, with a patch of leather tacked over the hole and a second hole coming in the patch.\n\nBram turned them over in his hands. Hester had not limped; she had walked into town every market day until she was eighty-eight. He could make nothing of it. He set the clogs side by side under the wheel, since that was plainly where they lived, and went back to his sweeping."],["Pim Has a Go","It rained for three days in July, and Pim Larkin, aged nine, spent them in Bram Coddle’s pottery, watching the old ferryman lose to his late aunt’s kick wheel. On the third day she asked if she could have a go. Bram, who was tired, said she was welcome to it.\n\nPim’s legs were too short to kick as he did. She wriggled round on the seat, hooked her left foot on the flywheel, and shoved it the only way she could reach, which was backwards.\n\nThe wheel did not groan. It went round, and round, and went on going round, with a low steady hum like a kettle thinking about boiling. Pim took her foot away. The wheel spun on by itself. Bram counted sixty turns before he stopped counting and sat down on a sack of clay."],["Turning Widdershins","The trouble with the stubborn wheel in Kiln Lane had been simple all along, and it took a child of nine to find it. Bram Coddle, retired ferryman, had been kicking it the wrong way round.\n\nHis aunt Hester was left-footed. For fifty years she had driven that wheel with her left foot, and the iron spindle had worn its seat to suit her, as a path wears to suit one walker. Turned her way, it ran like cream. Turned his, it was a ferry hauled against the current, which is a thing Bram felt he ought to have recognised.\n\nHer worn left clog had told him. The wheelwright’s letter had told him. He spent August learning it all again backwards: left foot, hands changed over, the clay coming at him from the other side. It was like being nine himself, and he found he did not mind."],["One Honest Bowl","In September, Bram Coddle sat down at his late aunt’s kick wheel, set it humming with his left foot the way she had, and made a porridge bowl. It took him the morning. It was thick in the wall and not perfectly round, but it stood up straight, which no pot of his had done before.\n\nThe first one cracked across the bottom as it dried. He made it again. The second he glazed brown from Hester’s own bucket and fired in her small kiln, feeding the fire through one long night with Pim Larkin’s father to keep him awake.\n\nWhen the kiln had cooled he lifted the bowl out into the daylight. There was a small thumbprint baked into the foot, where a nine-year-old had picked it up wet to see underneath. Bram considered it and decided the bowl was the better for a signature."],["Entry Closed","On the first cold morning of October, Bram Coddle made porridge and ate it out of a bowl he had thrown himself, on his aunt Hester’s wheel, in her pottery in Kiln Lane. It had taken a retired ferryman six months, and one small neighbour, to get that far. The porridge tasted much as porridge does. He had a second helping regardless.\n\nAfterwards he washed the bowl, took down Hester’s order book and found her last line: One porridge bowl for Bram, who broke his. Not yet begun. He crossed out the last three words and wrote Delivered, with the date.\n\nThen, since the page had room, he added a line of his own beneath it. One mug for Pim Larkin, with a handle. Begun. Overhead, the bowl that flew off in May kept its place on the roof beam. The wheel hummed. He had left it turning."]],[["A Clean Shed","Glaze notebook of Wenna Sorrel, potter, Marsh End, Emberwick. Begun the week old Jory Pender retired to the Grey Coast and left me the pottery, the customers, and a glaze shed that had not been tidied since before I was born.\n\nI have tidied it. Forty years of crusted buckets are scrubbed. The battered copper ladle he stirred with, green as a pond and bent like a question mark, I could not quite throw out, so it hangs on a nail by the door. I stir now with a clean beech paddle.\n\nTest 1. Ember green, the glaze everybody wants, from Jory’s own card: wood ash, river clay, sand, patience. Six bowls dipped and fired. They came out brown. Not a bad brown. The brown of good gravy. But I have forty orders for green, and nobody has ever asked me for gravy."],["Mustard, and the Twins","Wenna Sorrel’s glaze notebook, Marsh End pottery. I am trying to make my old master’s Ember green, the green of the river under the willows, and getting brown.\n\nTest 2. More ash, on the theory that Jory Pender never measured anything and had a heavy hand. I burned applewood prunings from the orchard behind the yard and sieved the ash twice. Result: mustard. A cheerful, loud mustard, the colour of a waistcoat at a wedding. Accident: two jugs toppled together in the kiln and fused at the shoulder for good. I have named them the Twins and put them on the high shelf.\n\nI wrote to Jory at Cockle Stairs to ask what I am doing wrong. His postcard came by return, with a picture of a lighthouse. It says, in full: “Can’t think. It was always green for me. Weather fine.”"],["The Sneeze","Notebook of Wenna Sorrel, who inherited the Marsh End pottery and cannot make its famous green glaze. Test 3 was meant to be a thicker coat, each mug held under for a slow count of five.\n\nOn the count of three I sneezed. It was the ash dust. Six mugs got a double coat on one side, a splash on the other, and a good deal went on my apron. I fired them anyway, since the kiln was going and I dislike waste.\n\nThe thick glaze crawled in the heat. It pulled itself up into beads and ridges, glossy and brown-gold, exactly like the back of a toad. They are the ugliest mugs I have ever made. The landlord of the Heron bought all six before they were cool, and wants six more. I have had to write down how I sneezed. Accident named: Toad."],["Rain in the Bucket","I am Wenna Sorrel, potter at Marsh End, and this notebook records my attempts at Ember green, a glaze my master made for forty years and never once wrote down properly.\n\nTest 4 was decided by the roof. A slate slipped in Tuesday’s gale, and by morning the glaze bucket had taken in a good two inches of rain. I had bowls waiting and no time to mix fresh, so I stirred the puddle in and dipped.\n\nThin glaze makes a thin coat. The bowls came out pale, clear honey, with the red clay glowing through like a hand held up to a candle. Not green. Not within a mile of green. But old Mrs Varley held one up to the window for so long that I gave it to her. Accident named: Weak Tea. The slate is mended. I rather miss the leak."],["The Wrong Ash","Glaze notebook, Marsh End pottery, Wenna Sorrel writing. Still hunting Jory Pender’s Ember green. Still not finding it.\n\nTest 5 was not my idea. I sent the carter’s boy for a sack of applewood ash, and he came back with a sack of something grey and light, which he swore was the same. It was reed ash, from the thatchers burning their trimmings out on the marsh. I found that out after the firing.\n\nThe glaze had gone milky and soft, a blue-grey with no shine to it, the colour of the marsh at six in the morning before anything has decided to move. I stood at the kiln door and said nothing for a while. Accident named: Marsh Morning. I paid the boy a penny extra and told him to make the same mistake every month. He is still thinking it over."],["Slip’s Walk","This is the glaze notebook of Wenna Sorrel of Marsh End. The yard cat here is called Slip. She came with the pottery, like the debts.\n\nTest 6: perhaps the secret of Ember green is the water. Jory Pender always said the colour was the river under the willows, so I carried two pails up from the Ember itself, from under the actual willows, and mixed the glaze with that instead of well water. I glazed a dozen tiles and laid them on a board to dry.\n\nSlip crossed the board in the night. Eleven tiles, one paw each, and a pause on the twelfth to wash. The prints fired in perfectly, pale on dark. The glaze was brown, river or no river. But the schoolmistress wants the whole row for her hearth. Accident named: Slip’s Walk. The cat has asked for nothing."],["Treacle on the Shelf","Wenna Sorrel, Marsh End pottery, glaze notebook. Test 7 of my master’s lost green: the same recipe, fired hotter, in case forty years of Jory Pender’s kiln ran fiercer than he knew.\n\nIt ran fiercer than I knew. I sat down by the firebox at midnight to rest my eyes, and woke at three with the spy-hole white as the moon. I have never moved so quickly. It was too late for the pots.\n\nThe glaze had melted clean off the shoulders and pooled round every foot, thick and dark and glassy. Nine jars were stuck fast to the shelf. I got four off with a chisel and my whole vocabulary. Inside each, where the glaze gathered, is a deep brown pool you could lose a spoon in. Accident named: Treacle. Lesson: a potter may rest her eyes, but only one at a time."],["A Visit to the Coast","I shut the Marsh End pottery for two days and took the carrier’s cart to Cockle Stairs on the Grey Coast, to ask Jory Pender to his face how he made Ember green. Seven tests in this notebook, and I, Wenna Sorrel, have produced every colour but that one.\n\nHe was on a bench in his daughter’s garden with a rug over his knees, pleased to see me, and no help. Ash, clay, sand. No, he never weighed it. No, nothing else went in. “Stir it, mind,” he said. “I stirred it every morning for forty years, whether I was glazing or not. A glaze likes to be stirred.”\n\nTest 8, on my return: stirred each morning for two weeks, a hundred turns of the beech paddle, counted. Brown. A beautifully mixed brown. He gave me a bag of shrimps for the journey, which was more use."],["The Thrush Bowls","Glaze notebook of Wenna Sorrel, potter, Marsh End. The shelf of accidents above my bench is filling: the Twins, Toad, Weak Tea, Marsh Morning, Slip’s Walk, Treacle. The space I left for Ember green, my old master’s glaze, is still a space.\n\nTest 9 was spoiled before it began. The blacksmith came to mend the kiln door, and filed a hinge while standing over my open bucket. I saw the dust go in, fine as pepper. I glazed with it out of pure temper.\n\nEvery speck of iron bloomed in the fire. The bowls came out cream-brown, freckled all over with dark rust, like a thrush’s breast. The blacksmith says he should have a share. I have offered him a bowl. Accident named: Thrush. I notice I no longer mind these as I should. A person could get fond of being wrong."],["The Broken Paddle","Wenna Sorrel’s glaze notebook, Marsh End pottery, in the tenth month of failing to make Jory Pender’s Ember green.\n\nAccident first, for once. The glaze had settled hard as a brick in the bottom of the bucket, and when I leaned on my beech paddle to shift it, the paddle snapped at the neck. I had nothing else to hand. I took Jory’s old copper ladle down off its nail by the door, bent and green as ever, and stirred with that. Being busy with the autumn fair, I left it standing in the bucket all week.\n\nTest 10 was only meant to use up the batch. But the bowls have come out different. There is a breath of green at the rims, faint as the river in March, where the glaze lies thinnest. I had opened a new sack of sand that week. I have ordered three more."],["Two Columns","I am Wenna Sorrel, potter, and for most of a year this notebook has chased one glaze: Ember green, which my old master made at Marsh End for forty years. Last month, by accident, I got a faint green rim. I thought it was the new sand. Test 11: new sand, mended paddle, everything else the same. Brown.\n\nSo I ruled two columns, as my mother did for the housekeeping. Left, the green firing. Right, this one. Ash the same, clay the same, water, sand, heat, all the same. One line was left over, and it was the ladle.\n\nI took it to the window. Outside it is crusted green, but the bowl of it is worn thin as a leaf and scoured bright. Forty years of stirring, and the glaze has been eating it a grain at a time. Jory never added copper. He simply never took it out."],["Ember Green","The last entry in this glaze notebook, kept by Wenna Sorrel at the Marsh End pottery, Emberwick.\n\nTest 12. I stood Jory Pender’s old copper ladle in the bucket and left it there a month, stirring each morning as he did, and going against every tidy instinct I own. Yesterday I opened the kiln. Green. The deep, wet green of the Ember under the willows, pooling darker in the hollows. I sat down on the kiln step. I sent the first bowl to Cockle Stairs, and Jory’s postcard, with a different lighthouse, says: “Told you. Stir it.”\n\nI have added a line to his recipe card: one old ladle, left in. The green has its place on the shelf now, beside Toad and Thrush and Marsh Morning, which I shall go on making. It was the one I looked for. I am not certain it is my favourite."]],[["The Survey Begins","Being a survey of the domestic jugs of the Lowlands, undertaken for the Emberwick Antiquarian Society by Miss Philippa Quince, Honorary Surveyor, with a donkey cart, a measuring tape and two hundred gummed labels. The donkey is called Bursar. He was the Society’s only other volunteer.\n\nWe crossed the Ember at Withy Bridge, where an old woman in a toll-keeper’s coat took my halfpenny and looked at Bursar’s teeth. “Jugs,” she said, when I explained. “You’ll find more jugs than sense down there.” I did not record this as evidence.\n\nJug No. 1, Sedgeholt, the property of Mrs Annet Fairlock. Height eight inches. Brown glaze, sound, one chip to the lip. Present use: milk. Beneath the handle is a small stamped frog, no larger than a fingernail. A potter’s mark, presumably. I note it for completeness."],["Change from a Goose","The Lowlands survey of jugs, conducted by Miss Philippa Quince for the Emberwick Antiquarian Society, reached Low Marrow on a Thursday. Bursar the donkey reached it some minutes later, having stopped for a hedge.\n\nJug No. 4, the Plough inn, Low Marrow. Height eleven inches, capacity three pints. Brown glaze, the handle broad and comfortable. Present use: cider. Under the handle, a small stamped frog. That is the second frog in four jugs.\n\nI asked the landlord, Mr Tobias Henge, where the jug was bought. He said it was not bought. His grandfather had it as change. Change from what, I asked. “From a goose,” said Mr Henge, and went to see to the fire. I have written this down exactly as it was said. A surveyor records. She is not required to understand, though I confess I should prefer to."],["A Left Thumb","I am Miss Philippa Quince, surveying the jugs of the Lowlands for the Emberwick Antiquarian Society, and at Cowslip Ending I was shown into the chapel vestry.\n\nJug No. 9. Height nine inches. Brown glaze, thin at the shoulder. Present use: flowers, on Sundays; the rest of the week it holds the verger’s spectacles. Under the handle, the frog again. That makes five. Whoever makes the frog jugs finishes each handle by pressing a thumb into the clay at its foot. The print is there in every one. I tried my own right thumb in the hollow and it sat wrongly. My left fitted like a key.\n\nSo: one potter, left-handed, with a thumb a little broader than mine. The verger asked whether this was important. I said that in a survey everything is important until proved otherwise. Bursar, outside, was eating the chapel’s hollyhocks."],["The Oldest Jug of All","The jug survey of the Lowlands, by Miss Philippa Quince of the Emberwick Antiquarian Society, came to Nine Sluices. The sluice-keeper, Mr Cuthbert Lode, sent word that he owned the oldest jug in the country and I had better bring a cushion to kneel on.\n\nJug No. 12. Height seven inches. Blue and white. Mr Lode says it is three hundred years old and was dug from the fen by his great-grandfather. On the front, in gold letters, are the words A Present from the Grey Coast. I have entered its age as uncertain.\n\nHis wife keeps the dripping in another jug, which he would not have me measure, as it is only the frog one from the bridge road. I measured it while he fetched his great-grandfather’s spade. Nine inches. Frog. Mrs Lode says it has never dripped, which is more than she will say for the sluice."],["Sparkle in the Clay","A trip back to Emberwick for more labels cost me a second halfpenny at Withy Bridge. The old toll-keeper asked how many jugs I had found. Fourteen, I said. “Is that all,” she said, and let Bursar through. I am Miss Philippa Quince, and this is the Antiquarian Society’s survey of Lowland jugs, resumed at Mallowby.\n\nJug No. 15, the property of the brickmaker, Mr Silas Trow. Height ten inches. Frog under the handle. Present use: none, having lost its base to a frost. That was my luck. In the broken edge the clay shows red, with tiny bright specks that glitter when turned.\n\nMr Trow knows clay as I know catalogues. No pit in the Lowlands has that sparkle, he says. It is river-bank clay, from up the Ember. I have wrapped a fragment in my handkerchief and labelled it."],["The Umbrella Stand","Survey of the jugs of the Lowlands, for the Emberwick Antiquarian Society. Surveyor, Miss Philippa Quince. Transport, one donkey, Bursar, at his own speed.\n\nJug No. 22, Hen’s Causeway, the property of the widow Fogg. Height nineteen inches; capacity, by her account, two gallons and a kitten. It is the largest frog jug yet. Present use: umbrellas. Scratched beneath the frog is a wavy line, like water. I have now seen that line on four.\n\nI have formed a theory. So many jugs, over so many years, cannot be one pair of hands. There was once a Frog Pottery: a manufactory of some size, with a dozen workers, a tall chimney, perhaps a wharf. It has been forgotten, and I shall be the one to find it. Mrs Fogg listened to all this very kindly and asked whether I would like a boiled egg."],["Eggs and a Favour","At Otterditch the lane was under four inches of water and Bursar declined it, so I walked the last half mile with the measuring tape round my neck. I am Miss Philippa Quince, and I survey Lowland jugs for the Emberwick Antiquarian Society.\n\nJug No. 27, the property of Mr Enoch Wherry, eel-catcher. Height eight inches. Frog. It leaks from a crack, and holds nothing, and sits in the middle of his mantelpiece. I asked why he kept it. “It was change,” he said, “from two dozen eggs and a favour.”\n\nThat word again. I heard it at Low Marrow, of a goose. I conclude that Change is an old Lowland name for a fair or market, since lost, at which my Frog Pottery sold its wares. I shall propose this to the Society in a footnote. Mr Wherry said I was welcome to propose what I liked."],["In Eleven Pieces","This survey of the jugs of the Lowlands has, until today, damaged nothing. I, Miss Philippa Quince, Honorary Surveyor to the Emberwick Antiquarian Society, must now record otherwise.\n\nAt Much Wading the Misses Dabney set out their jugs for me on a table in the yard. I had tied Bursar to the gatepost. I had not tied the gate. He walked in backwards, as donkeys will when a cart is behind them, and the cart met the table.\n\nJug No. 31. Height formerly six inches. Yellow glaze, no frog. Condition: eleven pieces. Cause: surveyor’s donkey. I have paid the Misses Dabney one shilling and fourpence, and apologised to each in turn. The frog jug beside it bounced off the cobbles and rolled under the mangle without a mark. Whatever else may be said of the Frog Pottery, it did not skimp on the walls."],["A Rubbing of the Frog","I carried a broken yellow jug to Emberwick to be riveted, which meant Withy Bridge again and a third halfpenny. It was raining. The toll-keeper came out in her old coat, brass buttons to the chin and one odd horn one, with clay under her fingernails. I supposed she had been gardening.\n\nMiss Philippa Quince, surveyor of Lowland jugs to the Antiquarian Society, writing from Tansy Fen. Jug No. 38, the property of the miller. Height nine inches. Here the frog is the crispest I have seen, and I took a rubbing with a soft pencil. It measures five-eighths of an inch across. It sits within a raised rim, and round it runs a border of tiny dots.\n\nIt is not cut like a potter’s seal. It looks pressed from a thing made for another purpose: a coin, or a medal. I have pinned the rubbing inside my hat."],["Last Winter’s Jug","The Antiquarian Society’s survey of Lowland jugs, kept by Miss Philippa Quince, has reached Goslingstead, and has there suffered a blow to its theory.\n\nJug No. 47, the property of Jem Sallow, carter. Height eight inches. Frog; thumbprint, left; clay with the sparkle in it; the wavy line beneath. Every sign of the old Frog Pottery I have been tracing since Sedgeholt. But the glaze is as bright as a new penny. I asked how long he had owned it. “Since last winter,” he said. “The floods.”\n\nLast winter. My lost manufactory, with its chimney and its wharf, is neither lost nor, I begin to fear, a manufactory. Somebody is making these now. Somebody left-handed, near the river, who works when the water is up. I asked Jem Sallow who. He was already halfway down the lane with a load of turnips, and carters do not turn round."],["Everybody Knows","I am Miss Philippa Quince, and for eleven weeks I have surveyed the jugs of the Lowlands for the Emberwick Antiquarian Society. Of fifty-eight jugs measured, thirty-nine bear a small stamped frog. At Eelford I asked the schoolmaster, Mr Lucan Thrip, whether he had any notion who made them.\n\nHe looked at me as one looks at a child who has asked what rain is. “Dorcas Tibb,” he said. “At the toll. Everybody knows.”\n\nIt is quite simple. Lowland people are short of coin and pay the bridge toll in kind: eggs, a goose, a favour. When Mrs Tibb thinks she has had the better of the bargain, she gives a jug as change. Every road into the Lowlands crosses Withy Bridge. I have paid there three times myself. Nobody told me because I never asked. I asked only about the jugs."],["The Sixty-Fourth Jug","The survey of the jugs of the Lowlands, by Miss Philippa Quince of the Emberwick Antiquarian Society, ends where it began, at the toll-house on Withy Bridge.\n\nMrs Dorcas Tibb is eighty-one and left-handed. Behind the toll-house is a shed with a wheel, a kiln the size of a dog kennel, and a heap of sparkling bank clay. She makes jugs in flood winters, when the road is shut and there is nobody to charge. The frog is a brass button off her late husband’s toll coat; the wavy line is the flood. She could not see what there was to survey.\n\nI had no halfpenny left. I paid my fourth toll with a jar of quince jam, and she judged she had the better of it. Jug No. 64. Height eight inches. Brown glaze, frog, sound. Present use: holds the Society’s pencils. Bursar carried it home without incident."]],[["Packing Day","Sunday, Clinker Bank. This is the diary of the Long Kiln for this year’s firing, kept by me, Odo Fenn, aged fifteen, wood-boy. Mistress Oxlade, the firing-master, gave me the book and said: write what happens, not what you feel about it. I will try.\n\nWhat happened. The eleven potters of Emberwick carried their year’s work up the fell road, and we packed all three chambers, two hundred and six pots, on shelves propped with fireclay posts. It took from seven until dusk.\n\nMy gran, Nell Fenn, has a jar in the first chamber, front row, where the flame comes in hardest. She asked for that place. It is as tall as a milk churn and the last pot she will throw, because her hands have finished with clay. She set it down herself and walked home without looking back. I am not writing the rest."],["First Smoke","Monday, 6 a.m. Diary of the Long Kiln at Clinker Bank, kept by Odo Fenn, wood-boy. Lit. Mistress Oxlade, the firing-master, laid a fire the size of a hat in the mouth of the firebox and lit it with a coal I carried up from my gran’s own hearth in a tin. That is the custom when a potter has her last pot in the kiln. I did not drop it.\n\n9 a.m. A small fire only. The pots are damp, and if the heat comes on fast the water in them turns to steam and they burst. So we warm them the way you wake somebody gently. Steam is coming off the chimney, white, smelling of wet brick.\n\nNoon. Six barrows of larch burned. Hob Tarn, who stokes, says the first day is the dull one. He has said so four times. I think he is nervous too."],["Night Watch","Monday night, the Long Kiln, Clinker Bank. Odo Fenn’s diary. The kiln has been alight since six this morning and will burn three days without a rest, so we do not get one either.\n\n10 p.m. The watch is me, the twins Hob and Wick Tarn, who stoke, and Mistress Oxlade, who does not seem to sleep at all. Mr Farl the baker came up with a tray of pies. He has thirty pie dishes in the first chamber and pays his share of the wood in pastry.\n\n1 a.m. Mistress Oxlade showed me the spy-hole. You pull out a clay plug and look straight into the fire. The colour tells the heat: dull red now, like a plum. Cherry by morning, she says, then orange, then yellow, then a white you cannot look at. 3 a.m. A fox came to the edge of the light. Nineteen barrows."],["Orange Heat","Tuesday, noon. I am Odo Fenn, wood-boy at the Long Kiln above Emberwick, and this is its diary for the yearly firing. Second day. The spy-hole shows orange. We stoke every five minutes now: open the firebox door, four splits of larch, shut it, count to three hundred, again. The Tarn twins do it without speaking. I bring the barrows. Forty since Monday.\n\n2 p.m. Lost most of my left eyebrow by looking into the firebox at the wrong moment. Wick Tarn says everyone does it once and showed me his, which never grew back properly.\n\n4 p.m. Mistress Oxlade, the firing-master, told me to sleep on the wood-sacks in the lean-to. I said I was not tired. She said it had not been a question. Slept three hours. Dreamed I was stoking. Woke, and went and did it."],["The Kiln Breathes","Tuesday evening, Clinker Bank. The diary of the Long Kiln, written by Odo Fenn, in his first year as wood-boy.\n\n7 p.m. Yellow heat in the first chamber. The kiln has started to breathe. Each time the twins stoke, it draws in with a low roar, and a flame a yard long stands out of the chimney top and folds back. You can see it from the town. People will be at their windows.\n\n8 p.m. Mistress Oxlade let me have the spy-hole for a slow count of ten. My gran’s jar is in the front row, the last she will ever make. I could see it. It was not brown any longer, or any colour of clay. It was glowing clear through like a lamp, with the fire going round its shoulders like water round a stone. I was told to write what happens. That happened."],["A Noise Inside","Wednesday, 2 a.m., the Long Kiln. Odo Fenn, wood-boy, writing by the firebox light. We have been firing since Monday morning. At ten to two there was a noise inside the first chamber. A slither, then a crack, then something settling. Hob Tarn stopped with a split of larch in his hand. Nobody spoke.\n\nMistress Oxlade pulled the plug and looked in for a long time. Then she let me look. Something pale is leaning across the spy-hole where there was nothing before. I cannot see past it. I cannot see my gran’s jar.\n\n“A prop’s gone,” said the firing-master. “Something has come down on something. We shall know on Monday, when she’s cold.” Then she said, “Stoke,” and they stoked. 2.30 a.m. Mr Farl the baker has gone very quiet. His pie dishes were stacked on the shelf above."],["Nothing To Be Done","Wednesday, dawn. Diary of the Long Kiln at Clinker Bank. I am Odo Fenn, and in the night something fell inside the first chamber, close to where my gran’s last jar stands.\n\nI asked Mistress Oxlade if we could stop and open up. She did not laugh. She said the chamber is at yellow heat, and cold air on two hundred hot pots would crack every one like ice on a pond. Eleven potters’ year is in there. Whatever has happened to the jar has happened already, and stopping would only add the rest to it. “A kiln is like a pudding,” she said. “Open it early and all you learn is that you were right to worry.”\n\n7 a.m. Sixty-eight barrows. I have decided to count wood and not think. Mr Farl the baker is counting too, on his fingers, up to thirty, over and over."],["Test Rings","Wednesday afternoon, third day of the firing at the Long Kiln. Odo Fenn, wood-boy, keeping the diary.\n\n1 p.m. The first chamber is at white heat. Mistress Oxlade hooked a test ring out through the spy-hole on an iron rod: a small loop of glazed clay, put there to be fished for. It came out glowing and went dull, and she dropped it in the bucket and turned it to the light. The glaze had gone smooth and glassy. First chamber is done.\n\nNow we stoke the sides. Each further chamber has its own small ports, and we post thin sticks through them, the length of a walking cane, a handful a minute. The fire climbs the hill from room to room. 5 p.m. Second chamber yellow. It is strange to feed a fire past a room you are worried about. The twins say you get used to it."],["Rain on the Woodpile","Wednesday night, Clinker Bank. Odo Fenn’s diary of the Long Kiln, third night of the firing.\n\n11 p.m. The storm came over the fell all at once. The wind took the tarpaulin off the wood stack and the rain got in before we could catch the corners. Wet wood will not hold a kiln at white heat. For an hour the twins stoked with what was dry in the lean-to while I ran barrows, and Mr Farl went down to his bakery and came back with a cartload of his own oven faggots, which was handsome of him.\n\nMidnight. A lantern on the fell road. My gran, who was told to stay at home, with a can of soup. She sat on an upturned barrow under the eaves and watched us stoke. She did not ask about her jar. I did not tell her about the noise. Good soup."],["Clamming Up","Thursday, 9 a.m. The Long Kiln, Clinker Bank. Diary kept by Odo Fenn, wood-boy. The last test ring came out of the third chamber glassy at ten to nine. Mistress Oxlade, the firing-master, held up her hand and the twins put down their sticks. Seventy-five hours since she lit it. One hundred and twelve barrows of wood, of which I wheeled all but nine.\n\nThen the clamming up. We bricked the firebox mouth and smeared every door, port and crack with wet clay by the bucketful, so the heat goes out of the kiln slowly instead of in a rush. A slab went over the chimney. I plugged the spy-hole myself.\n\n9.40 a.m. It is very quiet. There is nothing left to carry. The kiln ticks to itself as it settles. Whatever is in there is finished now, one way or the other, and sealed up."],["Four Days","Saturday. I am Odo Fenn, wood-boy, and the Long Kiln above Emberwick has been sealed and cooling since Thursday. It cannot be opened until Monday. This diary has nothing to record, so I am recording that.\n\nI walk up to Clinker Bank each evening and lay my hand on the bricks. Thursday I could not keep it there. Friday I could, for a count of five. Today the wall is warm like a loaf.\n\nSomewhere inside is my gran’s last jar, and whatever fell near it in the night on Wednesday. I told her about the noise in the end. She went on peeling a potato. In her first firing, she said, a whole shelf of her bowls came out welded into one lump, and she sold it to the vicar as a garden ornament. He still has it. Sunday. Warm like a cat."],["The Opening","Monday, 8 a.m. The Long Kiln, Clinker Bank. Last entry for this year’s firing, by Odo Fenn, wood-boy. Mistress Oxlade took down the door of the first chamber brick by brick. A prop had slipped. Mr Farl’s pie dishes had slid off their shelf: twenty-four whole, five in pieces, and one that had landed against the shoulder of my gran’s jar and stuck there, glazed on like a limpet.\n\nThe jar is whole. The ash has run down one side in green and gold. My gran walked round it twice. “It’s got a pocket,” she said, and laughed until she had to sit down.\n\nIt stands by her door now, holding walking sticks, with her keys in the pie dish. Mr Farl visits his dish on Sundays. Mistress Oxlade says the diary is mine again next year, and I may put one feeling in it. I am saving it."]],[["A Wet Monday in October","I came to Quill’s Bindery in Needle Lane on a wet Monday in October, with my indentures in one pocket and a herring pie from my mother in the other. I am Robin Ellery, fourteen, from Saltby on the Grey Coast, where my family mends nets. Mrs Quill looked at my hands before she looked at my face. “Knots,” she said. “Good. Wipe your feet.”\n\nThe shop smelled of paste and leather and warm cat. The cat is called Kettle, after a stitch, and she sleeps in the paper drawer. I was given a broom, a stool, and three rules: never touch the gold, never hurry the press, never put a cup on the bench.\n\nHigh on a shelf stood one small brown book, so crooked that it leaned on nothing. I asked whose it was. “Mine,” said Mrs Quill. “My first. Sweep.”"],["Folding With the Bone","My first real work at Quill’s Bindery was folding. A book begins as large flat sheets, and each must be folded in half, and in half again, until it is a little bundle of pages called a section. Mrs Quill gave me two hundred sheets and a bone folder, a smooth blade of bone for pressing a crease down sharp.\n\nPaper, I learned, has a grain, like wood. Fold with it and the sheet lies down quietly. Fold against it and it fights, and the crease comes out cracked and sulky. By noon I could find the grain with my eyes shut, by bending the sheet and feeling which way it gave.\n\nBy evening I had a blister, a hundred and ninety good sections, and ten that Mrs Quill held up to the window one by one without a word. Kettle the cat slept on those."],["The Kettle Stitch","In my second month as apprentice to Mrs Quill, the binder in Needle Lane, I was let near the sewing frame. It is a wooden gallows the width of the bench, with cords strung up it like a harp. The folded sections are sewn to those cords until they are a book.\n\nHere, at last, I knew something. I grew up in Saltby mending herring nets, and a needle is a needle. The stitch that locks each section to the one below is called the kettle stitch, and it is very nearly a net-mender’s hitch done sideways. My fingers found it before my head did.\n\nMrs Quill watched me sew eight sections. She pulled at the thread with one fingernail. It did not give. “Hm,” she said, and went back to her own bench. I have since learned that “Hm” is the top of her scale."],["Paste Has Opinions","Midwinter at Quill’s Bindery, and I, Robin Ellery, apprentice, was promoted to paste. Paste is flour and water cooked in a pan until it turns from milk to something like cold porridge. It sounds like nothing. It has opinions. Look away for the length of a sneeze and it goes to lumps, and Mrs Quill can find a lump in the dark.\n\nMy first panful would have mended a boat. My second ran off the brush like soup. My third was good, and I was so pleased that I carried it across the shop to show her, tripped over Kettle the cat, and pasted the cat, the floor, and a customer’s umbrella.\n\nKettle forgave nobody for two days. Mrs Quill said only that paste comes off everything with warm water, which is the whole point of paste, and that I might now prove it."],["The Night Press","There is a great iron press in the corner of Mrs Quill’s bindery in Needle Lane, with a wheel on top like a ship’s. When a book has been pasted it goes between boards under that wheel and stays the night. That was the second rule I was given as her apprentice: never hurry the press.\n\nIn January I hurried the press. I had made my first whole book, a plain account book for the chandler, and I could not wait until morning to see it. I let it out after supper, by candle. It looked perfect. By breakfast the boards had curled outward like the wings of a startled hen.\n\nMrs Quill turned it over in her hands. She did not scold. She damped it, set it back under the wheel, and made me wind it down myself. “Two nights now,” she said. “It charges interest.”"],["Rounding and Backing","The spine of a book is not flat. I had never noticed until Mrs Quill, the binder I am apprenticed to, handed me a hammer. The sewn book is clamped with its back upward and tapped, gently, from the middle outward, until the back swells into a curve and the pages shoulder over to grip the boards. It is called rounding and backing. It is mostly a matter of not hitting your thumb.\n\nI hit my thumb. I also finished, in February, the first book that was mine from fold to cloth: a small blank notebook in green. It leans. Stood on the bench, it bows a little to the left, like a polite man in a wind.\n\nI expected the stove. Instead Mrs Quill reached up and stood it on the high shelf, beside her own crooked brown first book from forty years ago. They lean toward each other."],["The Paring Knife","Spring came to Needle Lane, and with it leather. Mrs Quill laid a skin of brown goat on the stone and showed me how its edges must be shaved thin as a leaf, so that the leather turns neatly over the boards. This is paring. The knife is kept sharper than anything else in the shop, Mrs Quill included.\n\nA skin costs more than I earn in a quarter. I knew that. I held my breath, leaned in, and pared a hole clean through it the size of a sixpence.\n\nI, Robin Ellery, apprentice, confessed at once, because there is no hiding a hole from a binder. Mrs Quill looked through it at me with one eye. Then she cut the piece free, wrote my name and the date on the back, and put it in the patch drawer. “Everyone’s is in there,” she said. “Mine is bigger.”"],["Gold on the Breath","I had been seven months at Quill’s Bindery before I was allowed to touch the gold. It comes in leaves beaten so thin that they have no weight at all. You lift one on the flat of a knife. If you breathe out, it leaves, and you find it later on the cat.\n\nThe letters are brass, each on its own handle, warmed on the stove and pressed through the gold into the leather. Mrs Quill gave me the spine of a town almanac to letter. I held my breath for the whole of EMBERWICK, and every letter sat straight and bright. Then I breathed, and looked. I had picked up the M upside down. The spine read EWBERWICK.\n\n“Well,” said Mrs Quill, “now you have done it once.” She showed me how gold is coaxed off again, which takes an afternoon. Kettle the cat stayed gilded for a week."],["Alone at the Counter","In June Mrs Quill went down to the Lowlands for a day to quarrel with the paper mill, and left me, Robin Ellery, eight months an apprentice, in charge of the bindery in Needle Lane. “Take in work. Promise nothing by Friday,” she said.\n\nThree people came. A boy asked whether we could mend a kite. A lady left a songbook that had lost its back. And old Farmer Dunnock put down a stock book so tired that it sighed, and asked the price of mending it. I looked at the sewing. I said ninepence. It was a two-shilling job. I knew it as the door closed. I spent the afternoon rehearsing my confession to the cat.\n\nMrs Quill heard me out. “Then ninepence it is,” she said. “A binder’s word is part of the binding. You may pay the difference in sweeping.” I am still sweeping."],["The Proof Book Set","On the last Monday of September Mrs Quill cleared the small bench by the window and laid out cream paper, two boards, thread and half a skin of blue goat. “Your proof book,” she said.\n\nEvery apprentice makes one at the end of the first year. I am to bind a blank book alone, from the first fold to the last gold letter, in seven days. She will not help. She will not look. She has turned her own stool to face the wall, which I think is harder for her than for me.\n\nIf it passes, my indentures are signed for six years more and I stay in Needle Lane. If not, I go home to Saltby and the nets. She did not say that part. She only moved the paring knife an inch nearer my hand, and told Kettle the cat to keep off the paper."],["Seven Days Alone","This was the week of my proof book: one blank book bound alone, with Mrs Quill’s back turned, to show what a year at her bench has taught me. I folded on Monday, feeling for the grain. I sewed on Tuesday with the net-mender’s hitch my fingers knew before I came. On Wednesday I rounded the back and spared both thumbs. On Thursday I pared the blue leather, and it held.\n\nOn Friday I pasted the endpapers, and the back one slid. It sits a hair’s breadth askew, and by the time I saw it the paste had taken.\n\nI could have hidden it. A pressed flower would cover the place. I thought about that for a whole cup of tea. Then I put the book under the great press for two full nights, untouched, and on Sunday I lettered the spine with every letter the right way up."],["Signed Where Nobody Looks","A year to the week after I came to Needle Lane, Mrs Quill turned her stool round and took up my proof book. She pulled at the sewing with a fingernail. She found the crooked back endpaper in four seconds, and I said I had left it honest. “Hm,” she said. Then, which has never happened before: “The sewing is better than mine.”\n\nShe signed my indentures for six more years. Then she gave me a bone folder gone yellow and thin at the tip. It was old Mr Tane’s, who taught her at this same bench, and she has folded with it for forty years.\n\nBinders sign where nobody looks. I tooled R.E. inside the back board. The pages were blank, so I have filled them with this account. My leaning green notebook stays on the high shelf. Kettle the cat sleeps on the indentures."]],[["To Whoever Comes After","I am Josiah Tane, binder, of Needle Lane in Emberwick, and I have stood at this bench forty-one years. My knuckles have begun to object. I have no apprentice, so I am writing down what I know for whoever takes the bench after me. That is you. Sit up.\n\nThe trade has three parts and I have put them on the cover: paper, paste, and patience. You may buy the first and cook the second. The third you will have to grow yourself, slowly, like a quince.\n\nI begin because of what came in this morning. The spring flood got into the Town Hall cellar, and the clerk has brought me the Flood Book in a roasting tin. It is the town’s record of every high water on the Ember for a hundred and sixty years, and it is, at present, soup. We shall mend it together as we go."],["Paper Has a Grain","First lesson, from Josiah Tane’s bench in Needle Lane to whoever stands here next. Paper has a grain. The fibres lie mostly one way, as the hairs do on a cat. Bend a sheet both ways and you will feel it yield in one and stiffen in the other. Fold with the grain, always. A book folded against it will never lie open, and will spend its life trying to shut on the reader’s thumb.\n\nThere. That is a third of what I know.\n\nAs to the Flood Book, the town’s drowned river record: I have taken it apart. It came to two hundred and twelve leaves. Each is drying between blotters pegged on strings across the shop, and I go about my own bindery ducking like a man in a laundry. Never light a fire to hurry wet paper. Open a window and go for a walk."],["Paper Remembers Water","You, at my bench: know that paper never forgets a wetting. It dries, but it dries wavy, with a brown tide line to show how far the water came. We call the waviness cockling. You cannot scold it out. You can only damp the leaf again and let it dry flat under a weighted board, and wait.\n\nI, Josiah Tane, have two hundred and twelve such leaves this month. They are the Flood Book of Emberwick, which was caught in the spring flood it existed to record. I find that funnier than the town clerk does.\n\nEach leaf carries a year, and a height taken at the third arch of the bridge, in the hand of whoever was clerk. Some wrote small. One drew a little wave. I have read every page while pressing it. Nobody has read the whole book in a century, and I recommend the job."],["Mending a Tear","A torn page is mended with a strip of thin tissue and a little paste. Here is the secret, and I, Josiah Tane, had to be told it too: tear the tissue, never cut it. A cut edge is a wall. A torn edge is feathered, with a thousand small fibres that hold to the page like burrs to a stocking. Done well, the mend vanishes when the leaf lies flat.\n\nThe Flood Book of Emberwick, which I am rebuilding this year, has thirty-four tears. The worst runs clean through the entry for the Great Flood, parting the feet from the inches. I have put the number back together.\n\nA small face has taken to watching through my window from Needle Lane. It belongs to the chandler’s girl. When I look up, it goes. When I mend, it returns. I have started holding the work where it can see."],["Paste Is Only Flour","Now paste. The young expect a secret, so I, Josiah Tane, binder, will disappoint you at once: paste is flour and water, cooked and stirred until smooth, then strained through muslin. A good cook could make it. A good cook should not, because she will want to add salt.\n\nMake it fresh. In summer it turns within three days, and you will know by the smell, and so will the customer.\n\nHere is why we use so humble a thing when the joiner next door has strong glue. Whatever paste does, clean water gently undoes. Some binder a hundred years on will need to take your book apart, as I am taking apart the town’s Flood Book this year. Paste lets go for him without a fight. That is manners. You are not the last person who will love this book, so do not bind as if you were."],["Thin Coats, Twice","Whoever you are at this bench after me, you will use too much paste. Everybody does. It feels generous. Then the paper drinks it, swells, wrinkles, and squeezes the extra out at the edges, where it finds your sleeve.\n\nThe rule that I, Josiah Tane, was taught with a ruler, and now offer you free: a thin coat, from the middle outward, like spokes from a hub. Let it sink in. Then another thin coat. Two thin coats hold better than one thick, and that is true of paste, paint, and apologies.\n\nThis week I pasted a narrow strip of new paper down the fold of every leaf of the Flood Book, the river record I am rebuilding, so that there is something sound to sew through. The chandler’s girl was at the window. I wiped my brush with a flourish. I am not too old to show off."],["What the Spine Was Hiding","Binders waste nothing. When you take down an old book, you, my successor, must look inside the spine, because the binder before you will have lined it with whatever scrap lay nearest.\n\nI soaked the old lining off the Flood Book of Emberwick this week. It came away in warm water, as paste always will, and floated up in three strips. One was a baker’s bill for forty loaves. One was half a ballad about a goose. The third was in ink: R. Mott bound this, the wet year, with a cold in his head.\n\nI never heard of R. Mott. He stood at a bench in this town a hundred and sixty years before me, sneezing. I, Josiah Tane, have dried his note and shall put it back, with a strip of my own beneath it. I have not yet decided what to complain of."],["Thread, and Not Pulling","Sewing is where a book becomes one thing instead of many. The folded sections go to the sewing frame, and the thread runs through each fold and round the cords. Now the lesson, successor: do not pull. Draw the thread snug and stop. A book sewn tight as a drum will crack along its joints within the year. It needs a little slack to breathe in, as a marriage does, or a boot.\n\nI, Josiah Tane, sewed the Flood Book of Emberwick this week on five cords, with forty blank leaves added at the end for floods to come.\n\nIt rained on Thursday, and the chandler’s girl came in to drip by my stove. Her name is Hesper. She is nine. She watched me sew for an hour and asked only one question, which was why I did not pull it tight. I nearly dropped the needle."],["The Press Does the Work","We come to patience, the third part of the trade, which cannot be bought. I, Josiah Tane, will start you on it with the press.\n\nWhen a book is pasted and put between boards under the iron wheel, your work stops and the press’s begins. You will want to look. You will think of reasons. Do not. A book let out early curls its boards in the night to spite you. Wind it down, bank the stove, and go home. Nothing a binder does after dark is as useful to the book as his sleeping.\n\nThe Flood Book, our town’s river record, went under the wheel on Monday, and I left it there until Thursday. I spent the days between teaching Hesper, the chandler’s girl, to sweep paper shavings toward the bin and not merely around it. She is paid a penny a week and a look at the gold."],["Leather Will Not Be Hurried","Leather is skin, and has not forgotten it. Damp it and it stretches kindly round a book. Rush it and it sulks, shrinks as it dries, and drags the boards awry. So, you at my bench: pare your edges thin, paste the skin, and then let it rest a few minutes before you lift it. Use that time to wash your hands. Leather shows every thumb.\n\nI, Josiah Tane, covered the Flood Book of Emberwick this week in brown calf, river colour, which seemed only civil. Drawing it over the five cords took a whole morning and all the language I had.\n\nThen it must dry slowly, away from the stove, for days. Hesper, the chandler’s child who sweeps for me, asked each morning if it was ready. On the fourth day she stopped asking and simply went and looked at it. That is the beginning of a binder."],["Lettering in Gold","Gold leaf is the last thing put on a book and the first thing anybody sees, which is unfair to the sewing. The leaf is laid on the leather; a warm brass letter is pressed through it; the gold stays where the heat touched, and the rest brushes away. Breathe out at the wrong moment and you have gilded your eyebrows.\n\nForty-one years I, Josiah Tane, have done this, and this autumn my hands shake. So I lettered the Flood Book of Emberwick one letter a day. FLOOD took a week, and BOOK most of another. I rested the hand and came back. Nobody told me slowness could be used as a tool, like a knife. I am telling you.\n\nThe second O sits a whisker low. Hesper, the chandler’s girl, spotted it from the door. I have left it. A book made by hand should admit to the hand."],["Home to the Town Hall","On the last day of October I, Josiah Tane, carried the Flood Book back to the Town Hall. It left in a roasting tin in spring. It returns with two hundred and twelve mended leaves, forty new ones, and a note in the spine: J. Tane rebound this, the year his hands gave notice. He was helped.\n\nThe clerk had kept this year’s flood height on a slip in his waistcoat. He wrote it on the first clean page, and we both stood looking at it rather longer than a number deserves.\n\nSo that book is done, and so is this one. I shall leave it in the bench drawer. Hesper, the chandler’s girl, asked this morning why paper bends easier one way than the other. I have sent her to wash her hands. You, whoever you are, may begin again at the second chapter; she is about to."]],[["A Trough on a Handcart","Ilse Farrow came into Emberwick in April behind a handcart. On the cart rode a shallow wooden trough, a bundle of combs, eleven stoppered jars, and a hatbox with no hat in it. She was a marbler. She made the swirled papers that binders paste inside the covers of good books, and she rented the old wash-house on Ropewalk, by the river, because marbling wants water and a stone floor.\n\nIn the hatbox was one small book. It had been her grandmother Marta’s, and Marta had marbled its endpapers in this town, fifty years before. They were slate blue and rust and a thread of yellow, and every vein in them ran double, as if the pattern had shivered while it was being made.\n\nIlse had tried for nine years to make that paper. Marta had left no word of how. So Ilse had come to ask the town."],["Stone Pattern, Plain","A marbler floats her colours on a trough of thickened water, called size, and lays a sheet of paper on them. The paper lifts the picture off whole. Nothing is left behind, so no two sheets are ever alike.\n\nIlse Farrow began her first week in the Emberwick wash-house with the oldest pattern there is. She shook drops of colour from a brush of broom straw and let them spread into rings, until the trough looked like the bed of a stream. Stone pattern. She made forty sheets, and the binder in Needle Lane bought them all for ledgers.\n\nIn the evening she mixed blue, rust and yellow, and tried for her grandmother’s lost paper, the one whose veins ran double. The colours sat on the size as bright and hard as boiled sweets. She hung the sheet up to dry anyway. It would do for somebody’s ledger."],["The Herring Comb","In May the harbour-master at Saltby, down on the Grey Coast, sent to Emberwick for endpapers for his new log. Ilse Farrow, the marbler lately come to the wash-house on Ropewalk, made him a combed pattern. She laid her colours in stripes across the trough and pulled a wide-toothed comb from end to end. The stripes bent into rows of small arches, each tucked under the last, like scales. She called it Herring Comb. The harbour-master ordered twenty more.\n\nAfterwards she took her grandmother Marta’s little book from its hatbox and held the old endpapers beside the new. Marta’s pattern had been combed too; she could see the arches. But across them ran that fine doubling, every line with a faint twin beside it.\n\nNo comb did that. Ilse owned eleven combs and had made four more to be sure. She began to think the secret was not a tool."],["Colours From the Dyer","Next door to the wash-house where Ilse Farrow marbled her papers lived Emberwick’s dyer, Mr Osric Madder, whose hands were three colours on any day. He came round in June to complain of her drain, and stayed to look at the trough.\n\nIlse showed him what had brought her to town: her grandmother’s endpapers, blue, rust and yellow. Her own colours came out too loud beside them.\n\nMr Madder put on his spectacles and sniffed the page, which is how dyers read. The blue was woad, he said, any fool had that. The yellow was weld. But the rust was no dye at all. It was red earth, the sort that stains the sheep on the east side of the Northern Fells. “Yours is out of a jar. Hers was dug.” He drew her a map on the back of a bill. He never mentioned the drain again."],["Fell Earth, Ground Fine","It is a day’s walk from Emberwick to the east side of the Northern Fells, and Ilse Farrow, the marbler, made it in June with a trowel and two flour bags. She was after red earth for a colour. She found it in a cutting above a beck, where the sheep were faintly pink.\n\nBack in her wash-house she ground the earth on a slab for three evenings, until it was finer than flour. Floated on the trough, it gave a rust so soft that she laughed aloud. That was the rust in her grandmother Marta’s endpapers, which Ilse had spent nine years trying to match.\n\nShe combed a sheet in blue, rust and yellow. The colours were right. But they lay crisp and still, and Marta’s had seemed to tremble. Ilse named the new pattern Fell Stone and sold it to a shepherd for his tally book."],["What the Water Does","By midsummer Ilse Farrow had her grandmother’s three colours and still not her grandmother’s paper. The old endpapers in the hatbox were soft at every edge, colour melting into colour. Hers, marbled in the Emberwick wash-house, came out sharp as cut tin.\n\nIt was Mr Madder the dyer who said it, leaning in her doorway eating a plum. “What water are you using?” The pump in the yard, she told him. His own dyes, he said, took one way with pump water and another with water from the Ember, and differently again in autumn, when rain brings the marsh peat down and the river runs brown as tea. Old Marta would never have carried pump water with a river at her door.\n\nIlse filled a bucket from the Ember. The colours spread more gently. Only a little; the summer river ran clear. She would have to wait for October."],["The Waiting Patterns","All that summer Ilse Farrow waited for the river to turn brown, and while she waited she worked. The wash-house door on Ropewalk stood open, and the children of Emberwick stood in it.\n\nShe made Gull’s Wing for the schoolmaster, grey feathers drawn out with a single pin. She made Marsh Light, green drops with a yellow heart, for a lady’s album. A boy called Tam, who had watched for a fortnight without blinking, was at last allowed to lay a sheet himself. He lifted it and stood holding his own wet paper as though it might fly off.\n\n“Can you make that one again?” he asked. She could not, she told him, and neither could anybody. That was the trade. Which made it all the stranger, she thought that night, to have spent nine years trying to make her grandmother’s sheet a second time."],["An Old Woman Remembers","Mrs Hartle was ninety-one and had been a laundress, and in August she came down Ropewalk on two sticks to see who had taken her old wash-house. She found Ilse Farrow at the marbling trough, and sat down uninvited on the copper.\n\nShe remembered Marta. Marta Farrow had marbled in that very corner fifty years back, and had cursed the stone barges. They came down the Ember from the quarry then, and when one went by the whole floor hummed, and the water in every tub shook, and Marta’s colours with it. “Then one day she stopped cursing,” Mrs Hartle said, “and took to listening for them. She would stand with the paper in her hands, waiting.”\n\nIlse looked at her trough. Then she asked when the next barge was due, and Mrs Hartle laughed until she had to be given water. The quarry had closed twenty years ago."],["Brown Water in October","The rain came in the first week of October, and the Ember turned the colour of strong tea. Ilse Farrow, the marbler, carried eight buckets of it up to her wash-house on Ropewalk and made her size with river water, as her grandmother Marta must have done.\n\nShe floated the blue, the yellow and the red earth she had dug in the Fells. They opened on the brown water slowly, like sleepers stretching, and their edges ran soft into one another. When she lifted the sheet, she had to sit down. It was nearly her grandmother’s lost paper. It was a cousin of it.\n\nBut Marta’s veins ran double, every one, and Ilse’s ran single. She knew why now. The stone barges that once shook this floor were gone. She lacked only something very heavy going by outside, and that cannot be dug out of a hill."],["Something Heavy Going By","For a fortnight in October the wash-house on Ropewalk was the noisiest building in Emberwick. Ilse Farrow, the marbler, needed her trough to tremble as it had trembled for her grandmother when the stone barges passed, and there were no barges any more.\n\nShe stamped. The colours jumped and tore. Mr Madder the dyer lent his two apprentices to leap off the copper, which made rings like a dropped stone. A boy brought his uncle’s drum. The coal cart was paid sixpence to drive past, and did nothing. Everything was too sharp, or too near. A barge is a long slow weight, and what it sends through the ground is more like a held note than a knock.\n\nIlse thanked everybody and gave the apprentices a sheet of marbled paper each. Then she sat down on the step. She was not exactly beaten, but she was out of ideas."],["The Ten Past Four","Ilse Farrow had stopped trying. That is the only reason she was at her trough in the Ropewalk wash-house at ten past four, combing an ordinary sheet. She had her grandmother’s colours and the brown river water, and had given up on the shiver that made every line of the old paper run double.\n\nEmberwick has a branch railway, and its afternoon goods train crosses the iron bridge over the Ember, slowly, with coal. Ilse had heard it every day since April and never listened.\n\nThe comb was halfway down the trough when a hum came up through the floor. The surface shivered. Every line stood for an instant beside its own twin. She dropped the comb and laid the paper down with both hands. When she lifted it, the veins ran double from edge to edge. She held it by the corners, dripping, long after the train had gone."],["Written Down This Time","There are two goods trains a day over the Ember bridge, so through that winter Ilse Farrow made two sheets a day of the shivered paper. She stood in the wash-house on Ropewalk with the sheet in her hands, listening, as her grandmother Marta once stood listening for barges.\n\nMarta had given her pattern no name, and told nobody how it was done, and it had cost her granddaughter nine years. Ilse called hers the Ten Past Four. She had the binder in Needle Lane make a plain book with the first matched pair as endpapers, and on its first page she wrote the whole method: river water in autumn, earth from the Fells, and wait for something heavy going by.\n\nThen she put it in the hatbox beside her grandmother’s, paid a second year’s rent, and saw at last to the drain the dyer next door had complained of."]],[["Left on the Step","Repair ledger of Dorcas Venn, book mender, Bridge Street, Emberwick. Sign over the door: No Book Refused. First frost of November.\n\nBook: An Alphabet of Animals. Brought by: nobody. It was on the step when I opened, wrapped in a flour sack. Complaint: spine gone, sewing perished, every corner soft as cloth. Wants the letter Q. Pinned inside, a note in thick flat pencil: Please mend. Will pay when I come. Do not throw away the loose bits.\n\nNo name. In nineteen years every book has come attached to a person, who told me rather more than I asked. This one came with only a sack. Someone has read it nearly to pieces; the page for B, a badger, is worn pale by a thumb far larger than a child’s. Treatment: resewn, new cloth spine, loose bits kept. Shelved under Unclaimed, where it is the only book."],["Stuck at Puddings","From the repair ledger of Dorcas Venn, book mender, Bridge Street, Emberwick. December. Book: a household receipt book, handwritten, three generations of ink. Brought by: young Wilf Pargeter, for his grandmother, who made him swear an oath first. Complaint: will not open at Puddings.\n\nCause: treacle, of some age. Eleven pages had become one. I parted them over the steam of the kettle, a leaf an hour, across two days. Under the treacle lay the Midwinter pudding the whole Pargeter family has argued about since the old lady’s mother died. It wants a spoonful of strong tea. Nobody had guessed tea.\n\nMrs Pargeter’s orders, by way of Wilf: mend it but do not clean it; the gravy on page nine was her wedding day. So the stains stay. Paid in pudding. The alphabet book left nameless on my step last month is still on the Unclaimed shelf. I dust it."],["The Verger’s Mouse","Ledger of Dorcas Venn, mender of books, Bridge Street, Emberwick. January, bitter. Book: the bell-ringers’ book from the church tower. Brought by: Mr Crake, verger, holding it at arm’s length. Complaint: mice.\n\nOne mouse, and a tidy one. She had eaten the index and the lower margin of forty pages, taking care to leave the print, and built from the crumbs a nest in the hollow of the spine. The ringers’ changes are all there. Only the white is gone. I told Mr Crake his mouse could evidently read. He did not smile.\n\nTreatment: new margins grafted on in matched paper, new spine. The nest I returned to Mr Crake in a matchbox, it being church property. Still on my Unclaimed shelf: one child’s alphabet, left on the step in November with no name. No mouse would have got at that book. Somebody held it too often."],["Where the Thumb Rests","Repair ledger of Dorcas Venn, book mender, Bridge Street, Emberwick. February. Book: a sea atlas of the Grey Coast, salt-stiff, forty charts. Brought by: Captain Ives Tarrant, retired, who stood while I looked. Complaint: one chart, the ninth, has split along its fold.\n\nIt had not split. It had been worn through. Chart nine shows Saltby and the bar, and there is a hole in the paper just off the harbour mouth, the size and shape of a thumb. He has been coming home on that page for thirty years, with his finger always on the one place.\n\nTreatment: chart lined with thin linen; hole filled with toned paper. I asked whether I should ink the lost soundings back in. He said no, he knew the depth there. The alphabet book somebody left unnamed on my step in November has a thumb-worn page too. I notice thumbs now."],["Twelve Broken Grammars","From the ledger of Dorcas Venn, book mender, Bridge Street, Emberwick. March. Books: twelve copies of the same school grammar. Brought by: Mr Phineas Lark, schoolmaster, in a wheelbarrow. Complaint: all broken, all at once.\n\nAll broken, I found, at the same place. Every one fell open at page forty and went no further. Page forty is the irregular verbs. I laid them in a row for Mr Lark and let him work it out. That is the page at which a child of nine puts the book down hard.\n\nTreatment: twelve spines strengthened. I suggested, advice being free, that he might take page forty more slowly. He paid in full and said he would consider it. A thought, entered for want of anywhere else: the child’s alphabet left on my step in November broke nowhere in particular. It wore out evenly. Somebody went through it steadily, many times."],["Jam, String, and Nell","Repair ledger of Dorcas Venn, book mender, Bridge Street, Emberwick. April. Book: Mr Pudderby’s Snail, a picture book. Brought by: Nell Tibbet, aged seven, alone. Complaint: “I mended it and it got worse.”\n\nShe had. The loose pages were stuck in with plum jam and all tied round with garden string, bow on top. I have seen worse from grown binders. I took out the jam and kept the string as a bookmark. Paid: one bone button.\n\nShe stood on a box to watch, and spied the alphabet book on my Unclaimed shelf, left nameless on the step last November. She read it through and reported that there was no Q. I said I knew. She borrowed my pencil, drew a quail on a slip, more or less, and told me to put it in until the real one came. I have. It looks like a potato with opinions."],["Too Full of Flowers","From the ledger of Dorcas Venn, mender of books, Bridge Street, Emberwick. May. Book: a herbal, with coloured plates. Brought by: Miss Amabel Thrush of Marsh End. Complaint: will not shut.\n\nBetween every pair of pages lay a pressed flower: campion, marsh marigold, a whole flat foxglove. The spine had burst with the strain. I proposed removing them. Miss Thrush looked at me as if I had proposed removing her sister. Her sister Lettice pressed them, each at its own page, over forty summers, and now lies in the churchyard.\n\nTreatment: resewn with a thin stub of paper bound in beside each leaf, to make room at the back for what is in the front. It shuts. Every flower is where Lettice put it. On the Unclaimed shelf, the alphabet left on my step last November waits. It holds no flowers, but I think it was loved as much."],["Thirty Lambings","Repair ledger of Dorcas Venn, book mender, Bridge Street, Emberwick. June. Book: a pocket notebook, black, swollen. Brought by: Old Garth, shepherd, down from the Northern Fells. Complaint: cover came off in the rain.\n\nIt has lived thirty years in one coat pocket. It records every lambing on Garth’s side of the fell: dates, weather, twins, and remarks on particular ewes that I shall not repeat. It smells of sheep. I reached for the sponge and he put his hand flat on the book. New cover. Nothing else. The smell, he said, was how he found it in the dark.\n\nTreatment: new cover in oiled cloth. He checked my sum in pencil, thick and flat, the sort sharpened with a knife. I had seen that line before, on the note with the alphabet book left on my step last November. A working pencil, then, and a working hand."],["The Missing Last Page","From the ledger of Dorcas Venn, book mender, Bridge Street, Emberwick. July. Book: a novel, Miss Pellow of Gull Rock, from the lending shelves of the town library. Brought by: Miss Orla Finch, an assistant there. Complaint: last page missing, and has been for eleven years.\n\nThat has not stopped anyone borrowing it. Tucked inside the back cover I found twenty-three slips, each in a different hand, each supplying an ending. She marries the pilot. She sails away alone. In one, a whale is involved. Miss Finch had not the heart to throw any away.\n\nTreatment: spine relined; all twenty-three slips bound in at the back, with ten blank leaves after them. I asked whether the library had missed a child’s alphabet; one has sat on my Unclaimed shelf since November. Nobody had asked, she said. She added that people who are learning do not always like to ask."],["Two Halves, Two Sisters","Repair ledger of Dorcas Venn, book mender, Bridge Street, Emberwick. September. Book: a family birthday book. Brought by: Mrs Hobday, on Tuesday, who had January to June. And by Mrs Peverel, on Thursday, who had July to December. Complaint, from each: the other half is missing.\n\nThey are sisters. The book came apart during a disagreement forty years ago, and each went home with what she was holding. Neither knew the other had been in. I did not say.\n\nTreatment: resewn as one book, the bill divided in two. I told each it would be ready on Saturday at three. They arrived together, and it was quiet in my shop for a while. Then Mrs Hobday found a birthday entered wrong. My Unclaimed shelf still holds a child’s alphabet left on the step ten months ago. Not every owner comes back within the week, or the decade."],["The Sack Is Recognised","From the ledger of Dorcas Venn, mender of books, Bridge Street, Emberwick. October. Book: the day book of Hallow Mill in the Lowlands. Brought by: Jem Rudge, its carter. Complaint: floury, and the hinges gone.\n\nWhile I looked it over, he looked over my shop, and stopped at the Unclaimed shelf. Beside the child’s alphabet I keep the flour sack it arrived in, eleven months ago, on my step. “That is one of ours,” said Jem. So it was; the mill’s mark was on the seam, and I had never thought to look.\n\nMost Hallow Mill flour goes down the Ember by barge, and the bargeman is Abel Strand, a quiet man who writes his tallies with a carpenter’s pencil. Abel went downriver last November. His barge was sold at Saltby, and he has been working the coast to buy another. Treatment: hinges renewed. Jem will pass the word."],["Q Comes Home","Repair ledger of Dorcas Venn, Bridge Street, Emberwick. First frost of November, a year on. Book: An Alphabet of Animals, left on my step in a flour sack. Called for by: Abel Strand, bargeman, who filled the doorway and took off his hat.\n\nThe book was his daughter Minnie’s. When she was grown she taught him his letters from it, as far as P. Then she married, and he went on alone and stuck at Q. Out of his hatband he took a folded page: Q, a quail. He had carried it all year to practise on. The rest he got from the names painted on boats.\n\nI gave him his bill. He read it aloud, slowly: Alphabet, resewn, new spine, one shilling. Treatment: Q mended in beside the quail a child drew to keep its place. He wanted both. The Unclaimed shelf is empty but for the sack, folded."]],[["A Milestone, Corrected","Sketch the first. Pencil. The milestone on the Fell road, three miles out of Emberwick.\n\nMy name is Corwen Ashby. For forty years I painted the signs of this town: BUTCHER, FERRY, NO HAWKERS, all of it dead level, and gilded where the money ran to it. I retired on Friday. On Saturday my sister gave me this sketchbook and told me to go and draw something that was not a letter. So I am walking up into the Northern Fells for twelve days, a sketch a day, and I have begun with a milestone because it had lettering on it. I am not proud of that.\n\nThe sketch: one stone, which leans. I have drawn it upright. I find I have also corrected the spacing of EMBERWICK, which the mason crowded at the K. The stone does not look like the stone. The lettering is excellent."],["The Gate at Stoneladle","Sketch the second. Pencil. A field gate at Stoneladle Farm, where I lodge at both ends of my twelve days in the Fells.\n\nMrs. Tolley keeps the farm with her granddaughter Marrin, who is eleven, and a dog called Biscuit, who is everywhere. There is a board at the lane end which once said STONELADLE and now says ST N L DL. Forty years a signwriter, I noticed that before I noticed the mountains.\n\nThe sketch: five bars, two posts, one hinge. I ruled the bars against the edge of my pencil tin. Marrin said it was a very good gate, but that theirs sags. She is right. Theirs drops a full hand at the latch end, and a hen was sitting on it. I have drawn the gate the joiner intended. The hen I left out, as she would not hold still."],["Sheep, So Called","Sketch the third. Pencil, much rubbed. Sheep on the rough pasture above Stoneladle Farm.\n\nI am Corwen Ashby, retired signwriter, up in the Northern Fells to learn to draw what I cannot rule. A letter stays where you put it. A sheep does not. I began on a ewe’s head and she turned round. I began on her other end and she walked behind a different sheep. By noon the page held eleven beginnings: an ear, a back, something that might be a knee.\n\nThe sketch, as it stands: a cloud with four legs, and near it a smaller cloud. Marrin Tolley, who is eleven and belongs to the farm, studied it gravely and asked which one was the dog. Neither is the dog. I have written SHEEP underneath in a good plain capital, and that at least nobody could mistake."],["Rain on Hollin Tarn","Sketch the fourth. Pencil and weather. Hollin Tarn, two hours above the farm.\n\nThe tarn is a small dark water in a cup of the fell, with one holly tree on the far bank that has no business growing so high. I sat on my coat and got the far shore in: a long line, steady, the best of the trip. I was forty years a signwriter and I can still pull a line. Then it rained. It did not rain much. It rained in single fat drops, slowly, as if counting. Each one raised a blister on the paper and floated a little of the pencil off sideways. I held my hat over the book and drew under it like a man reading a private letter.\n\nThe sketch: one fine shore, one holly, nineteen pale spots. The spots are more like water than anything I drew."],["Marrin’s Method","Sketch the fifth. Pencil, done fast. Biscuit, the dog at Stoneladle Farm.\n\nMarrin Tolley is eleven and draws on the backs of feed bills. Today she showed me how. You look at the dog, she says, not at the paper, and you do not stop your pencil until the dog is finished or gone. She did Biscuit in about the time it takes to sneeze. It was all one line, with a loop for the tail, and it was him exactly, scratching. I am a signwriter. In forty years I never put down a line I was not looking at. I tried it. My pencil went off the page and across my knee.\n\nThe sketch, third attempt: a scribble with one ear. It is wrong in every measurable way. It is also, I am annoyed to say, more like a dog than my sheep were like sheep."],["The Kettle, Boiling","Sketch the sixth. Pencil. My own boots, at the foot of Kettle Crag.\n\nKettle Crag is the highest of the fells behind Emberwick, and the reason I came this way. I have looked up at it from the town for sixty years and meant to draw its top. The shepherds say the Kettle is always boiling, which is their way of telling you the summit sits in cloud. It sat in cloud today. I waited three hours on a flat rock. In the first hour I was patient. In the second I ate my bread. In the third I drew my boots, because they were there.\n\nThe sketch: two boots, laces undone, the left one fallen over. No ruler came near it. It is the first drawing in this book that looks like the thing. I shall try the Crag again on my way back."],["Jessop Rudd’s Wall","Sketch the seventh. Pencil. A drystone wall below Rooksgate, with the man who is mending it.\n\nJessop Rudd has walled these fells since boyhood, and he let me watch him close a gap. He picks up a stone, turns it once, and it goes where it was always going to go. He does not measure. I said that in my trade, signwriting, we snap a chalk line first. He said in his trade the hill would only laugh. I drew the wall stone by stone, and found I enjoyed it. Stones in a course are like letters in a word: each one different, all sitting on the same line, and the gaps mattering as much as the stones.\n\nThe sketch: fourteen feet of wall, and at the end a hat with a man under it. Rudd said the wall was fair. He did not mention the man."],["Indoors at Hag Beck","Sketch the eighth. Pencil, by candle. The inside of the shepherds’ hut at Hag Beck.\n\nIt blew hard all day, and Jessop Rudd the waller advised me, a town signwriter with a sketchbook, to stop where there was a roof. So I have spent the eighth of my twelve days in a stone hut the size of a cart, with a hearth, a bench, and a kettle that takes forty minutes to think about boiling. I drew what there was. One candle in a bottle. My socks on a string over the fire, steaming. The kettle. The door, bulging when the wind leaned on it. Candlelight is kind to a poor draughtsman: it hides the far corners, and nobody can say what you left out.\n\nThe sketch: a small warm room, mostly shadow. The socks came out well. I had not expected to climb a mountain to learn that."],["Wind at Rooksgate","Sketch the ninth. Charcoal, my fingers being too cold for pencil. A thorn tree on the pass at Rooksgate.\n\nRooksgate is the high gap between two dales, and the wind goes through it like a crowd leaving a hall. There is one thorn tree at the top. It has grown all on one side, leaning out flat, the way a man holds his hat on. I wanted to draw the wind, and you cannot. A signwriter of forty years knows that much: you can only draw what a thing does to its neighbours. So I drew the tree, bent; the grass, combed; and a rook going sideways with great dignity.\n\nThe sketch: black and quick, smudged by my sleeve. The book blew shut on it twice and printed a second, fainter tree on the facing page. I have left that one in. It looks like yesterday’s wind."],["Four Seconds of Summit","Sketch the tenth. Pencil. Kettle Crag again, and what I got of it.\n\nI came back to the flat rock under the Crag, where four days ago I waited three hours and drew my boots. The cloud was on the summit as before. I sat. I am Corwen Ashby, I once lettered FERRY in a gale, and I can wait. After an hour my pencil wanted a point. I bent to find my knife in the bottom of the bag. While I was down there a light came over the page, and when I looked up the cloud was closing again, like a curtain when someone has peeped out. Four seconds, perhaps. I saw a notch, and a tilt to the left.\n\nThe sketch: one notch, one tilt, and a great deal of blank paper. I laughed out loud, by myself, on a mountain. A rook looked round."],["Portrait of a Cloud","Sketch the eleventh. Charcoal and thumb. The cloud on Kettle Crag.\n\nI have spent two days of my twelve waiting for this summit to come out. This morning it struck me that I was sitting in front of a good subject and asking it to move. The cloud is always there. A man who painted signs for forty years ought to know that you letter the shop that is there, not the one you wish were. So I drew the cloud. It has a shape, once you look: heavy at the west end, torn thin at the east, with a bright rim where the sun leans on it. I rubbed it in with my thumb and lifted the rim out with a crust of bread.\n\nThe sketch: a mountain wearing its weather. Nothing in it is ruled, and it is the best page in the book."],["A Sign for Stoneladle","Sketch the twelfth and last. Pencil. The new board at Stoneladle Farm, seen from the road home.\n\nI came down off the Fells last night with eleven sketches and a blister. This morning I asked Mrs. Tolley for the old board that said ST N L DL, and for some paint. I am a signwriter. It took me till dinner. STONELADLE, in a plain capital, and under it, small, Kettle Crag with its cloud on, done freehand with the wrong brush. Marrin, who is eleven and taught me to draw a dog without looking, said the mountain was good. She said the letters were a bit straight. I gave her my second pencil.\n\nThe sketch: a gatepost, a board, a lane going down to Emberwick. The board hangs a little low at one end, like their gate. Twelve days ago I would have gone back and levelled it."]],[["Ice in the Wash","January, Withy Bend. I am Nell Garroway and I keep the ferry here, where the Ember turns for the marsh. I have pulled the Dabchick across on her rope for thirty-four years. This is the year they build the bridge. So I mean to paint the view from the ferry-house window once a month until the bridge is in it and I am out of work.\n\nSo far: the far bank, the big willow, the landing stage, a deal of sky. In the water stand six stakes with red rags on, which is all the bridge there is.\n\nI had the window open. I mixed a grey for the sky and it froze. The wash set into ferns and feathers of ice, as on a pane, and dried that way. I could not have painted frost so well on purpose. January did the sky; I did the stakes."],["A Brown Month","February, at the Withy Bend ferry. I paint this window’s view each month, the year they are bridging the Ember, and this month it is brown. The river is brown and in flood to the third step. The willow is a brown fog of twigs. Mr. Strake, the bridge foreman, came over in the Dabchick with a brown dog and looked at his six stakes, of which the river has taken two.\n\nI own five browns and none was right, so I let them run together wet and tipped the board. They made their own river, with a darker thread down the middle where the current goes.\n\nIt is all one colour, yet you can tell water from mud from tree. One thing is not brown. Low on the far bank I left a row of specks bare. Snowdrops. They are too small to paint, so I painted round them."],["The Wind Takes One","March. I am the ferrywoman at Withy Bend, painting the same reach of the Ember every month while a bridge is built across it. Today the piles went in. A great iron weight on a frame, hauled up by six men and let drop: thump, and the herons leave; thump, and my cups jump on the dresser.\n\nI took the board outdoors. That was a mistake. I had the sky down, pale, with torn clouds, and the willow just yellowing, when the wind lifted the sheet and laid it face down in the river. It sailed past the landing like a small raft. Mr. Strake’s youngest man fished it out with a boathook.\n\nI did March again indoors with the window shut. The first one hangs on the line beside it. The river has rinsed it to a ghost, every colour gone soft, and it is the better."],["Rain Lends a Hand","April on the Ember, at the Withy Bend ferry, where I paint one picture of the same view each month. Four piers stand in the water now, like teeth coming through. The willow is in leaf, a green so new it is nearly yellow, and the far meadow has lambs on it who run for no reason.\n\nI sat in the doorway under the porch. A shower came across the river sideways and put a dozen drops on my wet sky. Each drop pushed the blue away from itself and left a pale ring with a dark edge. In January I would have fretted. In April I am learning to say thank you.\n\nSo there are piers, a willow, lambs as five white gaps, and over them a sky with rain in it. Mr. Strake asked how I had got that effect. I told him I had staff."],["Too Many Greens","May. Nell Garroway, ferrywoman, at the window over Withy Bend, with a bridge half grown outside it. Timber beams lie from pier to pier now. The men walk along them carrying planks, not holding on, whistling. I cannot watch and I cannot look away.\n\nMay is all green and I have one green in the box. The willow is a grey green, the meadow a yellow green, the reeds almost blue, and the river reflects the lot and adds brown. I spent the morning mixing on a saucer: blue with this yellow, blue with that, a touch of red to quieten it. Eleven greens by dinner time. The saucer was prettier than most pictures.\n\nThis month’s sheet is green on green on green, with one small scarlet dot on the bridge. That is the foreman’s neckerchief. I put it in last, and everything else settled down around it."],["Four in the Morning","June, Withy Bend. I keep the ferry on the Ember, and I am painting this one view each month of the year the bridge is built. In June the light is up before anyone. I was at the window by four, in my coat over my nightgown.\n\nAt that hour the river is not a colour at all. It is a strip of sky lying down. Mist stood on it knee high, and out of the mist came the bridge: five piers, the beams, and a handrail along one side only, so that it looked like a comb. The willow hung still.\n\nI used pale pink, pale grey, and bare paper for the mist: the least paint I have ever put on a sheet. At five the first passenger rang the bell on the far bank, and I went down to the Dabchick with pink on my thumb."],["Low Water","July. No rain for five weeks. I am Nell Garroway of the Withy Bend ferry, and my painting of the river this month has very little river in it. The Ember has shrunk to the middle of her bed and left mud flats either side, cracked like an old dish. The Dabchick grounds ten feet short of the stage, and passengers walk the last of it on a plank.\n\nThe bridge men, glad of it, work at the piers in dry boots, and the deck is planked a third of the way over.\n\nI have never needed so little water to paint so little water. I dragged a nearly dry brush across the paper and it skipped, leaving sparkle. That is the river. The mud took every brown I own. The rest is heat: a white sky, a tired willow, and two herons standing in the shallows, thinking it over."],["The Foreman’s Children","August at Withy Bend, where I work the ferry and paint the same window’s worth of the Ember each month. The bridge deck reaches two thirds across. Mr. Strake’s children are here, three of them, and they have discovered my table.\n\nI gave them each a corner of paper and a brush. The eldest painted the bridge finished, with a flag. The middle one painted the Dabchick, very large, with me in her, larger. The smallest painted her piece blue and then, gravely, washed the brush in my tea.\n\nMy own picture has their marks in it. There is a thumbprint in the meadow that I have made into a cow, and a hard edge in the sky where somebody leaned. Below, a bright gap of water between bridge end and bank, and three small figures on the stage, fishing with string. They caught nothing and reported a good day."],["Mist, and the Gap","September. I am the ferrywoman at Withy Bend. All year I have painted one view from one window, and all year a bridge has crept across it. This morning the last gap was closed. I did not see it done. There was mist on the Ember so thick that the far bank had gone, and the hammering came out of whiteness.\n\nSo I painted the mist, which means I painted nearly nothing. I wet the whole sheet and touched in the near landing and the ferry rope going out. Then I let the bridge fade, pier by pier, until the paper was bare.\n\nWhen it lifted at ten, there the bridge was, bank to bank, and Mr. Strake standing in the middle with his hat off. My picture shows a bridge that sets out and does not arrive. It was true at eight o’clock. I shall let it stand."],["Tea for the Willow","October on the Ember. Nell Garroway, at the ferry-house window, with the tenth of my twelve monthly paintings. The bridge is whole but not open: there is a rope across each end, and the men are tarring the handrails. People still ring the bell for the Dabchick. I pull them over in the shadow of the new bridge and we both look up at it.\n\nThe willow has turned. Every leaf is a narrow yellow boat, and they come down and sail off toward the marsh. I ran out of my one orange brown by the second branch. There was cold tea in the pot. I can report that strong tea makes a fair autumn willow, if a pale one, and smells better than paint.\n\nSo October is a gold tree, a black tarred rail, and the river carrying a fleet of leaves past the pair of us."],["From the Upstairs Window","November. The Ember is over her banks and into my kitchen, a hand deep, so this month’s painting of Withy Bend is done from the bedroom window with the board on my knees. I have kept the ferry here thirty-four years, and I move the flour upstairs in October as a matter of course.\n\nFrom up here the view is changed. I can see over the willow. The meadow is a lake with a gate in it. The Dabchick is tied short to the porch and nobody could cross in her. Yet at noon the baker’s cart went over the river, on the bridge, with Mr. Strake walking ahead to lift the rope. Not opened yet; only needed.\n\nI painted grey water to every edge, and one dark firm line across it with a cart on. I see what the thing is for. I mixed that grey quite kindly."],["The First to Walk","December, Withy Bend. Today they opened the bridge, with the town band and a ribbon, and snow lying. I am Nell Garroway. I pulled the ferry here thirty-four years and painted this view every month of my last one. At eleven I took the Dabchick over for the final fare, which was the Mayor, who gave me a shilling and his hand.\n\nThen I walked back across the bridge with my hands in my pockets, the whole way over the Ember. I had never crossed it without a rope in them.\n\nThere is hardly any paint in December. The snow is the paper, left alone. Six strokes for the bridge, a grey thread for the willow, one small figure halfway over. The twelve stand along the mantelpiece, stakes to snow. The Dabchick is mine to keep. I mean to fish from her, and to paint the bridge from underneath."]],[["Mrs. Lark at Four","No. 1. Mrs. Hebe Lark, baker. Oil on board. Taken in payment of one month’s rent.\n\nI am Thea Marchbank, portrait painter. I came to Emberwick on the Monday coach with a commission from the Town Council: one portrait of the Mayor, Mr. Aldous Brimble, in his chain, for the Town Hall stairs. On Tuesday he sent word that he was busy with the drains and would sit next week. I had money for four days. Mrs. Lark, who lets the room over her bakery in Cross Street, said she had always wondered what she looked like to others.\n\nShe would only sit while working. So it is four in the morning, and she is to the elbows in dough, with flour on her forearms like white gloves. She is looking at the bread, not at us. She says the nose is hers and the loaves are too small."],["The Cobbler’s Best Last","No. 2. Mr. Osric Tamp, cobbler. Oil on board. Paid for with one pair of boots, resoled.\n\nStill no Mayor. Mr. Brimble, whose portrait I came to Emberwick to paint, is now occupied with the market tolls. Word has gone along Cross Street that the painter over the bakery can be had for goods. Mr. Tamp arrived with an eye on my boots, having noticed them, he said, from behind, on my way to the pump. He wished to be painted in his Sunday collar. I said the apron or nothing. We settled on both, and he holds his best last, a child’s size, worn pale as a bone by sixty years of small shoes.\n\nThe picture: a narrow man in a narrow shop, and behind him a wall of boots, every pair waiting for someone. He counted them and said I had left out the vicar’s."],["The Lamplighter at Dusk","No. 3. Miss Petra Vane, lamplighter. Oil on canvas. Paid for with a can of lamp oil and a promise to light my street first.\n\nI am a portrait painter, in Emberwick to paint its Mayor, who this week cannot sit because of a dispute about a hedge. Miss Vane cannot sit either. She has forty-one lamps to light before dark, and she walks eleven miles a day with a pole on her shoulder. I painted her by following her. It took nine evenings and tried Mr. Tamp’s new soles.\n\nIt is the corner of Cross Street at dusk, everything blue and going bluer, with one figure reaching up. The only warm colour is the flame at the top of her pole, and the side of her face that it finds. She says it is the first time anyone has looked at her and not at the lamp."],["The Constable, Off Duty","No. 4. Constable Hugo Marl. Oil on board. Paid for with one mended window latch, and a word with the boys who throw things at it.\n\nThe Mayor of Emberwick, whom I am here to paint, sends regrets and mentions the Harvest Committee. Constable Marl was on my step at eight. He then stood to attention for three sittings. I have painted statues with more give in them. Each time I asked him to be easy he squared up further, and his chin rose until I could see chiefly neck. On the fourth afternoon my room was warm from the bakery ovens below. He sat down for a moment and went to sleep.\n\nA large man in a small chair, helmet on his knee, collar undone, at peace. I woke him to ask permission. He looked at it a long while and said his mother would like that one."],["Mr. Fisk Declines a Face","No. 5. The hands of Mr. Barnaby Fisk, fishmonger. Oil on board. Paid for with herring, Fridays, until further notice.\n\nMr. Fisk drives his cart in from the Grey Coast and sells from the market steps. He wanted a portrait for his daughter and did not want his face in it. His face, he said, had frightened better painters than me, and besides, she knew it. So I painted his hands, mending a net across his knees. They are broad, red, scarred white at the knuckles, and they go in and out of the mesh with a needle as neatly as Mrs. Lark plaits a loaf.\n\nI am Thea Marchbank. I came to this town to paint the Mayor, who still cannot sit, and I am beginning not to mind. This one is all hands, net and a brass button. His daughter knew them from across the street."],["Class Two, Not Sitting","No. 6. Class Two of the Ember Lane School, with Miss Adderley. Oil on canvas, the largest I had. Paid for with twenty-three drawings.\n\nFirst, the Mayor. Mr. Brimble, whose portrait is the reason I am in Emberwick, sat for me on Wednesday for eleven minutes. Then a goat got into the council chamber and he was needed. I have one ear and the start of an eyebrow.\n\nClass Two gave me a whole afternoon and no stillness. Twenty-three children on four benches. I gave up on faces and painted what they were doing: a boy under the bench, two girls sharing one secret, a hand up at the back that had been up for some time. It is a room full of weather. In return each child drew the painter. I have twenty-three portraits of myself, most in a large hat, and I do not own a hat."],["Bramble, Who Carries Coal","No. 7. Bramble, a cart horse, the property of Mr. Dunstan Hale, coal merchant. Oil on canvas. Paid for with two sacks of coal.\n\nI have been half a year in Emberwick waiting to paint its Mayor, and I have learned to take sitters as they come. This one came to the bakery door with the coal and looked in at my window, which is on the first floor. He is seventeen hands. Mr. Hale said if I was painting the town I could not leave out the one who carried it.\n\nBramble sat, or rather stood, better than the Constable. He moved once in two hours, to eat a geranium. I have him as a grey head in a doorway, with a white blaze shaped like the Grey Coast on the map. One ear is turned back toward Mr. Hale. The geranium is in, as it was before."],["What the Bell-Ringer Knew","No. 8. Mr. Silas Oake, bell-ringer, aged ninety-one. Oil on board. Paid for in information.\n\nMr. Oake has rung the church bells of Emberwick for seventy-four years and talked for ninety. He sat like a rock and never closed his mouth. I painted it open, as a matter of record: an old man in the belfry window, rope in his fist, mid-sentence, with the roofs of the town behind him.\n\nHe knows every face I have done. He knew Mrs. Lark the baker when she could not see over the counter. And he knew the Mayor, whose portrait I was sent here to paint and cannot get, as a boy of nine. A travelling artist drew Aldous Brimble at the fair with ears like jug handles, and pinned it up for the town to laugh at. He has not sat for a likeness since. It was never the drains."],["The Postmistress Behind Bars","No. 9. Mrs. Ines Quarrel, postmistress. Oil on board. Paid for with stamps, and with my letters going first.\n\nI came to Emberwick to paint the Mayor and have painted eight other subjects while he avoids me. I now know why he does, and have written him a note. Mrs. Quarrel took it over the counter, read the envelope on both sides, and told me he would not answer before Michaelmas.\n\nShe sat where she always sits, behind the brass grille of the post office. The morning sun comes through it and lays stripes down her face. She would not have them moved. Thirty years, she said, and she would not know herself without them. So: gold bars, a grey bun, spectacles on a chain, and one hand flat on a postcard she has certainly read. She asks me to record that she reads only the fronts."],["A Penny a Minute","No. 10. Pip Lark, aged eight, the baker’s son. Oil, on a board no bigger than a slice of bread. Paid for by the painter, at a penny a minute.\n\nPip lives downstairs and has watched my sitters come and go. He asked why nobody had asked him. I said boys do not keep still. He said he could, for money. He managed four minutes. In the first he held his breath. In the second he went red. In the third he let it out and was himself, with a gap in his teeth and jam beside it, and I painted like a thief. In the fourth he asked if it was a shilling yet.\n\nOne small face, all in a hurry: fourpence. The Mayor, whom I was sent to paint last spring, has answered my note. He says: six in the morning, the allotments, tell no one."],["The Mayor Among Beans","No. 11. Mr. Aldous Brimble, Mayor of Emberwick. Oil on canvas. Paid for by the Town Council, eleven months late.\n\nHe was there at six, on the allotments behind the churchyard, in a straw hat, tying up beans. The chain of office hung on a bean pole, out of the soil. I am Thea Marchbank; I have waited since spring to paint this man, and I did not ask him to pose, only to carry on. He talked to the beans, then to me. Somebody drew his ears once and made a joke of them. I said I had no jokes, only paint.\n\nThe picture: a stout man in shirtsleeves in a green row, the sun behind him, shining through his ears and turning them shell pink. They are fine ears. He looked for some time. Then he asked if it could hang where people would see it."],["Twelve Frames, Town Hall","No. 12. The Painter, by Class Two, Ember Lane School. Pencil, chalk, and one in gravy. Twenty-three sheets in one frame. Not for sale.\n\nThe exhibition opened on Saturday on the Town Hall stairs. The Council had ordered one portrait and has twelve. The Mayor hangs at the top, among his beans. Below him come Bramble the horse, the lamplighter, the sleeping Constable, Mr. Fisk’s hands and the rest, down to Mrs. Lark by the door with her arms in dough. Mr. Oake the bell-ringer explained each, with corrections.\n\nI am Thea Marchbank. I came for four days and it has been a year. I painted eleven of these. The twelfth is the town’s opinion of me: a large hat, in twenty-three versions. Mrs. Lark has offered the room at the old rent, in loaves. On Monday I begin No. 13, the stationmaster. I have bought a hat."]],[["Crow Black","My name is Dilly Arkwright and I am ten. My Great-Aunt Sabine has given me her paintbox. It is a black tin with a dent in the lid and twelve pans inside, and she painted from it for fifty years. Her hands shake now, so it is mine. Inside the lid is a chart in her writing, with her own name for every colour. Eleven names. The twelfth pan is empty and the chart says: yours.\n\nThere is a rule. I may not use a colour until I have found it in Emberwick. One colour each Saturday.\n\nThe first is black, and its name is Crow. I thought black would be boring. I sat on the wall by the Corn Exchange and painted the crows on the roof, and when I added water the black went blue at the edges, like their wings. Aunt Sabine said, “There. Now you’ve met.”"],["The White Called Don’t","I am Dilly, and I am learning my Great-Aunt Sabine’s paintbox one colour at a time. She gave it to me because her hands shake. Every pan in the box is worn down, some nearly to the tin. Only the white is perfect, smooth as a new soap. On her chart its name is Don’t.\n\nI asked why. She said in watercolour the white is the paper, and anybody who reaches for the white pan has stopped thinking. Then she sent me to find white in Emberwick and paint it without any.\n\nI found the geese on the green. I painted the grass and the pond, and I went round five goose shapes and left them alone. It is hard, not painting something. My tongue stuck out. Aunt Sabine counted. “Five geese, and not a drop of paint in them. Good. Now leave that pan be for fifty years.”"],["Geranium Red","Third Saturday with Great-Aunt Sabine’s paintbox, which has twelve pans and a rule: I, Dilly Arkwright, must find each colour in Emberwick before I am allowed to paint with it. Today was the red. On her chart it is called Geranium.\n\nThat was easy. Everyone knows where the geraniums are. Mr. Pocock the stationmaster keeps eleven pots along the platform. I sat on the luggage trolley and did all eleven. Red is loud. One brushful, and the whole page turned to look at it.\n\nThe trouble came when the red ran into the green leaves and made mud. I showed Aunt Sabine and said sorry. She said mud is only two colours having an argument, and next time I should let one dry before the other speaks. Mr. Pocock wants the picture for the ticket office. I said it has mud in it. He said so has the station."],["Butter Yellow","Fourth Saturday. I am ten, and my Great-Aunt Sabine is teaching me her paintbox by sending me round Emberwick to find its colours. The yellow pan is called Butter, and Saturday is market day, so I knew where to go.\n\nMrs. Dunn sells butter in Market Square, in pats stamped with a thistle. I asked if I could paint one. She said only if I bought one. So I painted Aunt Sabine’s tea.\n\nYellow is sneaky. On its own it hardly shows. I put it on three times and it looked like wet paper. Then I painted the shadow under the pat, as blue as I dared, and the butter jumped up. Aunt Sabine had the butter on her toast and the picture on her knee. She said, “Yellow needs a neighbour. Most of us do.” That pan is worn down on one side. She must have liked it."],["Duckweed Green","I am Dilly Arkwright. I have my Great-Aunt Sabine’s paintbox, and I have to find each colour in town before I use it. Saturday five was green. Its name is Duckweed, so I went to the mill pond behind Tanner’s Row, where she lives.\n\nDuckweed is the green skin on the pond that looks solid enough to stand on. It is not. I know because of my left boot. The pan matched it exactly, a flat green, like paint already.\n\nBut the pond had other greens that were not in the box: the dark under the alders, the pale new reeds. I mixed in some Butter, then a speck of Crow, and got both. Nobody told me to. Aunt Sabine looked at the picture, then at my boot drying on her fender. She said one green in a tin is plenty for anyone with a yellow and some sense."],["Thursday Blue","Sixth Saturday, and I am halfway through my Great-Aunt Sabine’s paintbox. Six colours found in Emberwick, five to go, and then the twelfth pan, which is empty. Her chart says only: yours. I asked what belongs in it. She said she looked for her twelfth colour for fifty years, all over the county, and never found it. So she left it for me.\n\nToday’s is the light blue. It is called Thursday, after the best sky she ever saw, which was on a Thursday when she was nineteen. The pan is worn to a hole. You can see tin.\n\nI had to find a sky to match, on a Saturday. It rained till four. Then it tore open over the Fells, and I painted it from her back step with my paper on a tray. She held the water jar steady with both hands. That part was hers."],["Bridge Stone","I am Dilly, and I am ten. Every Saturday I take my Great-Aunt Sabine’s paintbox into Emberwick to find one of its colours, and this is the seventh. The colour is a sandy brown. On the chart she calls it Stone.\n\nIt is the town bridge. I held the tin up against the wall of it and the pan disappeared. Then I looked round and it was everywhere: the church, the Corn Exchange steps, the front of our own house. The whole town is made of that pan.\n\nI painted the bridge with its three arches and the Ember going under. It took all afternoon, because people kept stopping. An old man said the middle arch was fatter than that. He was right, so I fattened it. Aunt Sabine says Stone is the colour you use most and notice least. There is one in every box, and in most families."],["Ember Brown","Eighth Saturday with the paintbox. It was my Great-Aunt Sabine’s, and I am finding its twelve colours around Emberwick one Saturday at a time. This week it was the dark brown. Its name is Ember, for the river, not for fires.\n\nThe Ember had run brown all week. I went down the steps with my jar, and found I had forgotten to bring water. So I dipped the jar in the river.\n\nThat is how I found out. The river water was the colour already. I painted the river with the river, hardly touching the pan, and it dried a soft brown with specks in. There is a bit of Emberwick in that picture. Probably a bit of duck. Aunt Sabine laughed until she had to put her tea down. She said she did the same at my age and was cross about it for a week."],["Marmalade Orange","I am Dilly Arkwright, and this is my ninth Saturday finding colours for my Great-Aunt Sabine’s paintbox. She can’t paint because her hands shake, so I go out, and she stays at her window and tells me if I got it right. Today was orange. Her chart calls it Marmalade.\n\nMarmalade is not the jam. Marmalade is the cat at the fishmonger’s. He has been three different cats since Aunt Sabine was young, all orange, all called that.\n\nHe sat on a barrel and let me paint him for as long as he wanted. I did him wet, so the edges went furry. For the stripes I waited, then put darker orange on the damp, and they spread just enough. Then he got down, walked across the painting, and left. There are two orange paw prints on it. Aunt Sabine says he has signed it, and it is worth more."],["Coast Grey","Tenth Saturday. My Great-Aunt Sabine gave me her paintbox, and I must find each colour before I use it. The grey could not be found in Emberwick. Its name is Coast. So Dad took me on the branch line to the Grey Coast, with the tin on my knee.\n\nI thought it would be one grey. It was hundreds. The sea was a green grey and the sky a white grey, and the sand a brown grey with the sky lying in it. A gull was the same grey as its rock until it moved.\n\nI painted all of it with one pan and plenty of water. Dad held the paper down with two shoes. On the tideline I found a pebble that matched the pan, and brought it back. Aunt Sabine has one like it on her windowsill, from the summer she bought the tin. Now there are two."],["Nearly Night","Saturday eleven. I am Dilly, and I have found ten of the colours in my Great-Aunt Sabine’s paintbox. The last one with a name is the dark blue. She calls it Nearly Night. She said I would have to stay out late for it, and she had squared it with Mum.\n\nI sat on the churchyard wall at the top of town. First the Fells went blue, then the roofs, then the air. It is not black. It is deeper than that. I painted the sheet with it and lifted out the chimneys with a dry brush.\n\nThen one window lit, down in Tanner’s Row. It was hers. I could see her in the chair, and her old purple cardigan, faded to a soft colour that is not in the tin. I looked in the box. The twelfth pan was empty, and I knew what was going in it."],["The Twelfth Pan","My Great-Aunt Sabine’s paintbox has twelve pans. Eleven had her names on: Crow, Don’t, Geranium, Butter, Duckweed, Thursday, Stone, Ember, Marmalade, Coast, Nearly Night. The twelfth was empty and marked: yours. I am Dilly Arkwright, and on the twelfth Saturday I filled it.\n\nIt took all morning. Thursday blue and Geranium red made a purple that shouted. A touch of Stone quietened it. A speck of Coast. Then it was right: a faded heathery colour, gone soft with washing. I poured it into the pan and wrote its name on the chart under hers. Cardigan.\n\nShe was asleep in her chair, so I painted her in it. When she woke she looked at the picture, then at her sleeve, and laughed. “Fifty years I hunted that colour all over the county,” she said, “and I was wearing it.” She has asked for the painting. I am keeping the tin."]],[["The Ledger Changes Hands","Entered: one pull-along duck. For: Pip Arkle, aged four. Price: ninepence.\n\nThis ledger belonged to my uncle, Osric Thrupp, who made toys in Candle Lane for forty years under the Sign of the Spinning Top. In the autumn he retired to the Grey Coast to look at boats, and left me the shop, the lathe and this book. I am Marigold Thrupp, a cabinet-maker by training. I can make a drawer that shuts. Children, I find, do not want drawers. Inside the cover he has written one rule: Never sell a toy you have not played with. Down the margins he has inked small circles against some orders, and explained nothing.\n\nMy first duck came off the bench looking like a boot. I pulled it up and down the shop, as the rule requires. Pip’s grandmother winced. Pip named it Boot and carried it out under his arm."],["A Top for Mrs Venn","Entered: one whipping top, beech, with a red stripe. For: Mrs Venn of Ropewalk Cottages, aged eighty-one. Price: fourpence.\n\nI keep the ledger at Thrupp’s toy shop in Candle Lane, which was my uncle Osric’s until he retired and is now, alarmingly, mine. Mrs Venn came in on two sticks and asked for a top like the one she had at seven. It had a red stripe, she said, and it hummed. My first three fell over. The fourth walked off the counter. A top must weigh the same all the way round, and wood is never as even as it looks. The seventh stood up and hummed.\n\nUncle’s rule says a toy must be played with before it is sold, so I whipped it round the shop floor till I was breathless. Mrs Venn watched from the chair, then took the whip and did it properly."],["Nine Skittles and a King","Entered: one set of skittles, nine and a king. For: the three Dray brothers at the ferry house. Price: one shilling and sixpence.\n\nTheir father ordered them. He ferries people over the Ember all day and would like one evening in which nobody shouts. His sons quarrel over whose turn it is. I am Marigold Thrupp, new to toymaking, and I have no cure for brothers. I painted three skittles blue, three green and three yellow, one colour to a boy, and left the king plain. Then I bowled at them along the shop floor, as my uncle’s rule in the front of this ledger demands. The king is hard to knock down, which seems right.\n\nWhile the paint dried I counted the little circles Uncle Osric inked in his margins. More than half his orders have one. Mr Dray’s own boyhood toys have three between them."],["A Farmyard by Instalments","Entered: one farmyard, twelve animals and a gate. For: Nell Sorrel of Mill Lane, aged six, in bed with a cough. Price: two shillings.\n\nNell is to stay indoors till the cough goes, and her mother wanted something to last the month. I make toys at Thrupp’s in Candle Lane, the shop my uncle left me, and I am slow, which for once was useful. The animals arrive one at a time. Every third morning the baker’s boy carries one up on top of the loaves.\n\nThe cow went first, then two sheep that really look like sheep. I walked each along the windowsill first, since the rule in this ledger says I must play with whatever I sell. The pig is a difficulty. It will not look like a pig from the front. Nell sends word that she likes the pig best. She has named it Cushion."],["A Boat for the Ember","Entered: one sailing boat, eleven inches, tarred below the waterline. For: Tam Holloway, the bargeman’s boy, aged nine. Price: one shilling.\n\nTam knows boats. He said, kindly, that my first attempt would turn turtle. It did, twice, in the rain barrel behind the shop. I am Marigold Thrupp, a cabinet-maker by training and a toymaker by inheritance, and nobody had told me about keels. The second boat has lead in its belly, begged from the glazier.\n\nMy uncle’s rule, in the front of this ledger, says never to sell a toy you have not played with. So on Thursday I shut the shop and took the boat down to the Ember on a string. I meant to stay ten minutes, and felt foolish for about that long. Then the wind found the sail. I stayed till dusk, and was late with the Sorrels’ farmyard gate."],["What the Circles Mean","Entered: mending, one wooden drummer, left arm off. For: Bessie Fenn, aged five. Price: twopence.\n\nHer father the cooper carried it in. It had been his own, made at this bench by my uncle Osric Thrupp thirty years ago. I looked it up in the ledger. There was the order in Uncle’s hand, with two inked circles beside it. I asked Mr Fenn what they might mean. He laughed. One for the leg he broke off at six, he supposed, and one for the drumstick the dog ate.\n\nSo that is the secret of the margins. A circle marks a toy that came back broken. I went through the book tonight and there are hundreds. I had thought my uncle a careful workman. I am Marigold Thrupp, six months in his shop, and I have no circles at all. I intend to keep my own margins perfectly clean."],["A Jumping Jack, Privately","Entered: one jumping jack, painted as a sailor. For: a nephew of Mr Lobb, the toll-keeper on the Lowlands road. Price: sixpence, in exact coin.\n\nMr Lobb is the gravest man in Emberwick. He counts carts for a living and has never been seen to hurry. He came into Thrupp’s toy shop, where I, Marigold Thrupp, keep this ledger, looked at every shelf in turn, and said the nephew was a serious child who would want something plain. I made the sailor and pulled its string a good many times, as my uncle’s rule requires. Mr Lobb pulled it once at the counter. Then again. He put it inside his coat rather than in his bag.\n\nThe carrier says Mr Lobb’s one sister has no children. He adds that something now hangs in the toll-house window and waves at the carts. I have entered the nephew anyway."],["The Doll With No Face","Entered: one wooden doll, nine inches, the face left blank. For: Ottilie Marsh, aged seven. Price: one shilling and threepence.\n\nOttilie gave her instructions standing on the box I keep for short customers at Thrupp’s in Candle Lane. A painted doll smiles all day, she said, whatever has happened, and she could not be doing with that. She would decide the face each morning. After a week’s thought I painted the head with the black my uncle used for toy slates, and put a stick of chalk and a scrap of flannel in the box.\n\nThe rule in the front of this ledger says I must play with every toy first. So the doll spent Tuesday surprised, Wednesday cross, and Thursday, when the varnish on her boots would not dry, extremely patient. Ottilie came on Friday and drew one eyebrow up. She has not told me the doll’s name."],["The Horse on the Shelf","Entered: one hobby horse, ash pole, leather ears. For: Corin Pellow at the Mill House, aged five. Price: two shillings.\n\nI carried it up myself, being Marigold Thrupp, toymaker, and also the shop’s only errand boy. In the nursery, on a high shelf, stood a rocking horse, dapple grey, every hair of its tail in place. I knew it from this ledger: my uncle Osric made it for Mrs Pellow when she was small. She had never been allowed to ride it, she said proudly. It was kept for best. It was as good as new.\n\nThere is no circle against that horse in the margin. I had been counting such clean entries as my uncle’s successes. Corin took the hobby horse straight through the yard into the mill stream. I walked back to Candle Lane slowly, and sat a long while looking at the circles."],["A Rattle in a Hurry","Entered: one rattle, sycamore, with dried peas. For: the Garlick baby, not yet named, aged one day. Price: one cottage loaf, still warm.\n\nMr Garlick the baker hammered on the door of Thrupp’s toy shop at seven with flour to his elbows. The baby had come three weeks early and nothing was ready. Could I make a rattle by noon. I am not quick. But a rattle is a small thing: turned in one piece, sanded until a cheek could find no fault with it, and left unpainted, since it will be chewed.\n\nMy uncle’s rule, in the front of this ledger, says to play with every toy before I sell it. I shook the rattle all the way to the bakery, and three dogs and the curate looked round. They are calling her Hester. I ate the loaf at the bench and entered it as paid in full."],["Uncle Osric Inspects","Entered: one whistle, elder wood. For: Mr Osric Thrupp, retired toymaker. Price: refused.\n\nMy uncle came up from the Grey Coast by carrier to see what I had done to his shop. He spent the afternoon playing with the stock. He bowled the skittles, sailed a boat in the rain barrel, and timed a top on the counter by the church clock. Then he sat down with this ledger and read my year, from the duck called Boot onward. On each page he ran his thumb down the margin, where he has hundreds of little circles and I have none. Hm, he said. Early days. I took it for praise, though I am no longer sure it was.\n\nHe asked for a whistle for the road home, a toy with one job. I played it first, badly. He played it away down Candle Lane, and it was a tune."],["The First Circle","Entered: mending, one pull-along duck, one wheel gone. For: Pip Arkle, aged five. Price: nothing.\n\nBoot came home today. It was my first toy at Thrupp’s, a year ago, when Uncle Osric left me his shop and this ledger. It looked like a boot then and looks worse now. The beak has no paint left, and Pip says it has been through every puddle in Candle Lane and once, briefly, down the well. He stood at the bench while I fitted a wheel, then pulled Boot twice round the shop.\n\nI turned to my first page and inked a circle in the margin, the mark my uncle set against hundreds of his own orders. I had its meaning wrong all year. It marks a toy used hard enough to break. Under his rule in the cover I have added my own line: And be glad when it comes back."]],[["The Baker’s Breath","There are twelve winds at Emberwick, and I have made something for each. I am Bellwether Tansy. I have kept the kite shed on Whin Hill for forty-odd years, and I am writing this handbook for my great-nephew Jory, who is eleven and arrived from the Lowlands on Tuesday with one bag and a suspicious expression.\n\nThe first wind is the Baker’s Breath. It comes up from the town at dawn in May, smelling of loaves, too weak to lift a kite. It will turn a pinwheel, though, and that is where everyone begins: a square of paper, four cuts, a pin, a bead, a stick.\n\nJory’s first went to Dulcie Hobb, aged ten months, tied to her pram. She watched it spin all the way down Whin Lane. He did not smile. But he walked beside the pram to the bottom, to see that it kept turning."],["The Marsh Sigh","The second wind of Emberwick is the Marsh Sigh. It comes off the reed beds in the early evening, damp and slow, and you will think there is no wind until you see the bulrushes lean. This handbook, which I, Bellwether Tansy, kite-maker of Whin Hill, am writing for my great-nephew Jory, recommends the lightest thing you can build: a diamond of tissue on two split canes, with a tail of paper bows.\n\nWe made one in yellow for Wren Crake, the eel-catcher’s granddaughter, who is seven and lives where the Sigh blows. Her grandfather says it goes up from the punt like a moth.\n\nJory built another for himself. It rose a yard, he felt the tug, and he hauled it straight back in as if it were a fish. It dived into the reeds. In the Lowlands, he explained, things stay where you put them."],["The Ember Draught","Third in my handbook of the twelve winds of Emberwick comes the Ember Draught. It runs up the river valley from the south, steady as a towed barge, and it is the wind for a long tail. I am Bellwether Tansy, and I was making kites on Whin Hill before your mother was born, Jory, so attend. A tail is not decoration. It keeps the kite’s head up. Too short and the kite spins. Too long and it sulks.\n\nFor the Draught we made a red serpent kite with eighteen feet of tail for the Pascoe twins from the boatyard, who are eight and share everything loudly. One holds the line and one runs with the tail, and they change over at the slipway.\n\nJory’s own serpent got as high as the shed roof. Then he felt it pull and shortened the line, and it came down on the hen-house."],["The Fell Comb","The fourth wind is the Fell Comb, and it is not for beginners. It comes down off the Northern Fells with a parting in it, and it has snapped more spars on Whin Hill than I, Bellwether Tansy, care to count in a handbook meant to encourage my great-nephew. For the Comb you want a box kite: two cells of calico on four sticks, braced corner to corner. It looks like a crate and sits in the sky as if nailed there.\n\nOurs went to no child at all. Mr Prew the schoolmaster wanted one to carry a thermometer up, so that he might learn how cold a cloud is. He paid in full and blushed.\n\nJory held the line with him. When the Comb took the box up, the boy’s knuckles went white and he began winding in. Mr Prew, reading the sky, did not notice. I did."],["The Washday Wind","A kite needs somebody on the end. A whirligig asks only a post and a breeze, so this fifth chapter of my handbook turns to whirligigs and to the Washday Wind: the flapping westerly that arrives over Emberwick on Monday mornings as if it had read the calendar.\n\nI am Bellwether Tansy of the kite shed on Whin Hill. My favourite whirligig is the washerwoman. She bends over a tub, and when the vanes behind her spin, a crank works her arms up and down. The faster the wind, the harder she scrubs.\n\nWe made one for Mrs Suggs the laundress, for her gatepost in Soap Yard. My great-nephew Jory, who is with me for the year, carved the tub and painted the suds. He is easier with whirligigs. They are nailed down. Mrs Suggs says it is the only help she ever had that did not want its dinner."],["The Lull","The sixth wind of Emberwick is no wind at all: the Lull. It sits on the town for days in summer; the weathercock goes to sleep and the washing hangs straight. A handbook of kites must be honest about it.\n\nIn the Lull you mend. You also make the one toy that brings its own weather: a coat button on a loop of thread, wound and pulled until it spins and hums. I, Bellwether Tansy, made six for the Hobb children, who had been promised kite-flying and were becoming difficult.\n\nMy great-nephew Jory spent it laying out his five kites on the shed floor. All five have dived. He asked what was wrong with them. Nothing is, I told him. A kite climbs on a line paid out, and he pulls in at the first tug. He holds on too tight. He did not answer. He wound his button."],["Salt Tom","Salt Tom is the seventh wind in this handbook and the best of the twelve. He blows off the Grey Coast on late summer afternoons, steady from tea until dusk, and a child could fly a barn door in him. I am Bellwether Tansy, kite-maker of Whin Hill, and I wait all year for him.\n\nThis year’s kite was for Captain Marl, who is eighty and gets about the quay in a wheeled chair. He did not want a toy. He wanted to feel something pull on a line again. We built him a sailcloth kite five feet tall and tied the reel to his chair.\n\nMy great-nephew Jory launched it and stepped back. The Captain let line out by the fathom, laughing, until the kite was a dot over the harbour bar. Jory watched the whole reel empty. He was quiet all the way home."],["The Harvest Shove","Eighth of the twelve winds in this handbook is the Harvest Shove. It arrives over the stubble at the end of summer, warm, gusty and bad-mannered, and knocks your hat off from behind. It is a poor kite wind and a fine whirligig wind.\n\nMrs Oake of Lowlands Farm wanted the rooks kept off her seed-corn. I, Bellwether Tansy, and my great-nephew Jory built her a sawyer: a man in a red waistcoat who saws a log whenever the vanes turn, with a clack at every stroke. The crank took Jory four days, and he would not be helped.\n\nWe set him on a pole in her field in a stiff Shove, and he sawed as if paid by the log. By Friday the rooks were sitting on him. Mrs Oake does not mind. They are at least sitting still, she says, and she has grown fond of the clack."],["The Chimney Twist","Not every child has a hill. The ninth wind in my handbook belongs to those who have not: the Chimney Twist, which is whatever is left of any wind once it gets among the roofs of Emberwick. It turns corners. It blows up one side of Tanner’s Yard and down the other.\n\nI am Bellwether Tansy, and from my shed on Whin Hill I had always held that nothing would fly in it. My great-nephew Jory, who has looked down on the town since spring, thought otherwise. He made a kite no bigger than a handkerchief, with a tail of plaited wool, for the four Pym children, to be flown from their attic window on twenty feet of thread.\n\nIt works. It bobs above the chimney pots like a cork. I should record that Jory did not fly it himself. He handed the Pyms the thread and stood well back."],["The Goose Wind","The tenth wind comes in October from the north-east, and you hear it before you feel it, because the geese ride down it from beyond the Fells, calling. In Emberwick we name it for them. This is Bellwether Tansy’s handbook of kites and whirligigs, and the Goose Wind gets a whirligig, because by October my hands are too cold for tissue paper.\n\nOld Mr Sedge the lock-keeper ordered it. He has counted the geese over the Ember every autumn for sixty years and wanted some of his own for the months between. So three wooden geese now fly on a rod above the lock, and when the vanes turn, their wings rise and fall in order, the leader first.\n\nMy great-nephew Jory worked out the cranks. Then he went into the back of the shed with brown paper and paste and shut the door. I am not to look."],["Old Scold","Old Scold is the winter gale, eleventh of Emberwick’s twelve winds, and my handbook’s advice is brief. Fly nothing. Take in every whirligig you can reach, and apologise in spring to those you could not.\n\nShe blew for three days in January. I, Bellwether Tansy, sat in the kite shed on Whin Hill with the door roped shut, and my great-nephew Jory and I mended what the town brought up: Mrs Suggs’s washerwoman, short of an arm, and one of Mr Sedge’s wooden geese, found in a hedge.\n\nOn the third evening Jory showed me what he has built since October. It is a plain diamond of brown parcel paper, with a tail unravelled from his own Lowlands muffler. The spars are true. It is not pretty. He asked when the next kite wind would be. March, I said, with luck. He hung it on the nail by the door."],["The Lark Wind","The twelfth wind of Emberwick, and the last in this handbook, is the Lark Wind. It comes on the first fine March morning, light and rising, and takes a kite up as a lark goes, a little at a time.\n\nMy great-nephew Jory, who came to me from the Lowlands last May, carried his brown paper kite up Whin Hill. I, Bellwether Tansy, held it up and let go. The line tugged and I saw his elbows come in, the old habit. Then he opened his fingers and let the reel run. He let out every yard, and stood holding nothing but the end.\n\nWhen it was wound down he put the kite in my hands. It was made for me, he said. In forty-odd years I have built four thousand kites for other people. Nobody had ever given me one. Here the handbook stops. He knows the rest."]],[["A Commission from Heron House","Heron House, Marsh Lane, Emberwick, the 3rd of March. To Mr S. Wickett, Toymaker, Ropewalk.\n\nSir, I am told you are the person who makes dolls’ houses. I require one. My granddaughter Lettice, who is eight, comes to live with me in September, her parents being engaged to sail for two years with the coastal survey. I have not seen the child since she was in a christening gown. I do not understand children. I do understand houses. The dolls’ house is therefore to be a copy of this one, at one inch to the foot, exact in every room, so that she may learn where things are before she has to find them in the dark.\n\nYou may call on Thursday to measure. Wipe your boots. Expense is not to be considered. It is, however, to be itemised. Yours faithfully, Agatha Pennyfold (Mrs)."],["Mr Wickett Measures Up","From the daybook of Silas Wickett, toymaker, Ropewalk, Emberwick. Thursday the 9th of March. Walked out to Heron House to measure it for Mrs Pennyfold’s dolls’ house, which is to be a copy of the real one for her granddaughter. Wiped boots. Was watched doing it.\n\nFourteen rooms. I counted six in use. The rest are under dust sheets, and the drawing-room shutters have not been opened since the old gentleman died. Mrs Dabney the housekeeper carried the lamp and held the end of the tape. The seventh stair creaks. I asked Mrs Pennyfold whether she wished the creak included. She said exact meant exact.\n\nAlso noted from the lane: a small round window high in the north gable. Could find no room inside to go with it. Did not like to ask. Three hours’ measuring. Offered no tea. Given tea anyway, in the kitchen, by Mrs Dabney."],["The First Account","S. Wickett, Toymaker, Ropewalk, Emberwick. Account rendered to Mrs Pennyfold of Heron House, the 30th of April, for work so far upon one dolls’ house, being a copy of her own house at one inch to the foot. Itemised as instructed.\n\nTo the carcase, in seasoned pine, three storeys, made to lift apart: fourteen shillings. To two hundred and twelve roof slates, cut from the lid of a tea chest: three shillings. To one staircase of nineteen stairs: two shillings. To fitting the seventh stair with a creak: sixpence extra, the creak having taken longer than the stairs. To fourteen doors, hung, all opening: seven shillings. To one front-door knocker in the form of a heron, filed from a brass button: one shilling. To boot-wiping, on four visits: no charge.\n\nRooms furnished to date, six. The other eight are fitted as I found them, under dust sheets, pending instruction."],["The Matter of Dust Sheets","Heron House, Emberwick, the 6th of May. Mr Wickett, I have inspected the dolls’ house in your workshop, and I write to complain. You have put dust sheets in the drawing room. Small muslin ones, over small chairs. You have closed the shutters. You have shown eight of my fourteen rooms shut up and furnished with lumps.\n\nYou will say I asked for an exact copy, and that Heron House is like this. I am aware of what it is like. It is not what I wish an eight-year-old to be given as her home.\n\nYou are to model the rooms as they ought to be. And since you will only come back with your tape and catch me out, I have had the sheets taken off the real drawing room and the shutters opened. The carpet has faded in stripes. Kindly do not copy the stripes. A. Pennyfold."],["A Figure Not Ordered","From the daybook of Silas Wickett, toymaker, Ropewalk, the 2nd of June. Mrs Pennyfold’s dolls’ house, the copy of Heron House for her granddaughter, now has curtains at every window and a kettle the size of a pea. She has been asked twice about dolls and has answered twice. No dolls. The child will bring her own, and a house does not need people standing about in it.\n\nI have made dolls’ houses for thirty years. An empty one is a model: sound joinery, and nobody plays with it.\n\nSo this evening, on my own time, I carved a lady four and a half inches high, in a grey dress, very upright, with her hair in a knot and spectacles of fuse wire. She is not entered in the account. She is in the left-hand drawer of my bench, wrapped in a duster. I expect to be told off eventually."],["A Letter from Lettice","Heron House, the 20th of June. Mr Wickett, I enclose a letter received today from my granddaughter Lettice, for whom the dolls’ house is being made. I have copied it exactly, spelling included.\n\nDear Grandmother, Mother says you are having somthing made for me and I am not to ask what, so I am not asking. I have three questions which are diffrent. One, is there an attic. I have always wanted a room at the very top with a round window, like a ship. Two, have you got a cat. Three, do the stairs make a noise, because ours do and I know which ones. Your loving Lettice.\n\nI can answer the third. As to the others, there is no round window at Heron House, and there has never been a cat. I do not wish her first week to be a list of disappointments. Advise. A. Pennyfold."],["The Window in the Gable","Ropewalk, Emberwick, the 22nd of June. Madam, concerning your granddaughter’s questions and the dolls’ house I am building as a copy of Heron House, I must contradict you on the first.\n\nThere is a round window. It is high in your north gable, and can be seen from the lane by the pollard willow. I drew it in March. I could find no room inside to match and supposed it boarded over, so I held my tongue, and regret it. By my measurements a space nine feet by eleven lies behind it. I have left the model unfinished there. I can give it a blank wall, as the real house has, or a room, as the real house had.\n\nAs to a cat, I can carve one in an evening. A live one is outside my trade, but Mrs Dabney knows of a kitten. Your obedient servant, S. Wickett."],["Behind the Wardrobe","Heron House, Midsummer Day. Mr Wickett, you were right about the round window, and I will say so once.\n\nI went up to the top landing with Mrs Dabney and a lamp. I had not been there in thirty years. At the end of the passage stands a wardrobe, and behind it a door, papered over. It took the gardener and a chisel. Inside is a room nine feet by eleven, and at the far end, under boards, your window. It was my room. I slept there until I was twelve, and could see the river from the bed. I had not forgotten it. I had put a wardrobe in front of it, which I find is not the same.\n\nThe boards are down. You may put a bed in the attic of the dolls’ house. I am having one carried up to the real one. A. Pennyfold."],["Delivered Under Tarpaulin","S. Wickett, Toymaker, Ropewalk. Delivery note and final account, the 28th of August. One dolls’ house, a copy of Heron House at one inch to the foot, for Mrs Pennyfold’s granddaughter. Sent by Garrow’s cart in three storeys, under tarpaulin. Loose furniture packed in a tea tin with sawdust: forty-one pieces. The kettle is the one that looks like a pea.\n\nRooms furnished: the fourteen, and the attic, which makes fifteen. To the attic, with bed, and round window glazed with a watch-glass: four shillings. To one cat, tabby, carved, asleep: no charge. Mrs Dabney tells me a live one of the same pattern has come from the mill, and has been at the drawing-room curtains.\n\nTo assemble: the stairs go in before the roof goes on, as in life. The whole comes to four pounds, eleven shillings and sixpence. It was a pleasure, which I have not itemised."],["Lettice Writes Home","Heron House, the 14th of September. Dear Mother and Father, I hope this finds your ship. I got here after dark in the rain and the house was very big and I did not like it. Grandmother shook my hand.\n\nBut on the hall table there was another house, a dolls’ house, and it was this house, small. Grandmother said I might look. So I walked my fingers in at the door and up the stairs and up again to the top, and there was a room with a round window and a bed and a cat on the bed.\n\nThen I went up the real stairs with my candle, and I knew the way. The seventh stair creaks in both. The room was there. The cat was there, real, called Tabitha, and she bites a bit. I can see the river. I am not frightened now. Your loving Lettice."],["An Order from Miss Lettice","Heron House, the 2nd of November. Dear Mr Wickett, I am the Lettice the dolls’ house is for. Thank you for it. I have ninepence of my own and I want to order a thing, please.\n\nThere is nobody in the little house who looks like Grandmother. I have my two dolls in it, but they are visitors. It needs somebody who lives there. Could you make her. She should have a grey dress and spectacles. She should be sitting down, please, not standing, because Grandmother sits down now, mostly on my bed, to hear what the cat did. Yours truly, Lettice Pennyfold.\n\nReply, written at the foot and returned by the same carrier: Miss Pennyfold, the lady was made in June and has been waiting in my drawer since. She is standing. I have this evening made her a chair. Both enclosed. Keep your ninepence. S. Wickett, Toymaker."],["The Inventory Revised","Heron House, Emberwick, the 3rd of March, a year to the day since I first wrote to you. Mr Wickett, I enclose sixpence for the creak in the seventh stair, which I disputed last spring and now concede, and some news of your dolls’ house.\n\nIt is used daily. The grey lady you made without being asked has been moved from the drawing room to the attic, where she takes tea with the cat. I am expected to do the same on Thursdays, and I do. Lettice has grown an inch. Her parents write that the survey is half done.\n\nWhen you first came with your tape you counted the rooms of this house, and I saw what you wrote down. Fourteen, six in use. I have counted again this morning, and I should like your book corrected. Fifteen rooms. Fifteen in use. Agatha Pennyfold."]],[["A Shilling in Heel Alley","Hattie Lask came to Emberwick at twelve with a carpet bag and one shilling, to spend the summer with her uncle Corwin, the cobbler in Heel Alley. He was a kind man who spoke about four words a day. His shop stood at the wrong end of a lane nobody walked down, and since the new boot shop opened on Market Street the bell over his door had hardly rung.\n\nOn her first wet afternoon Hattie found a box under the bench. It held odd lasts, the wooden feet a cobbler builds a shoe on, and a tangle of old bootlaces.\n\nA child’s last, stood on its heel, is nearly a head. Hattie gave one a leather nose and sheepskin hair, hung it by four laces from two crossed sticks, and walked it along the bench. She called him Mr Welt. Cost: nothing. The shilling stayed in her pocket."],["Dame Crumb","Hattie Lask was making puppets in her uncle’s cobbler’s shop in Heel Alley, out of worn-out shoes and old bootlaces, and she had one shilling to do it with. Her second puppet came through the door on someone else’s feet.\n\nMrs Bunce the baker’s wife brought in a pair of house slippers past all mending. They had been blue. Thirty years of flour had turned them the colour of a bun. Uncle Corwin shook his head over them, and Mrs Bunce sighed and bought new bootlaces instead, which was the day’s only sale.\n\nHattie asked for the slippers. One, turned toe upward and stuffed with wool, made a round floury face with the bow for a cap. It needed cheeks. She spent a penny on red paint, and Dame Crumb was done. Mrs Bunce, shown herself dancing on four bootlaces, laughed until she had to sit down. Elevenpence left."],["The Postman’s Boot","Mr Tibbs the postman walked from Emberwick to the Northern Fells and back twice a week, and his boots showed it. When the left one let in the road, he carried it to Corwin Lask the cobbler in Heel Alley. The cobbler’s niece Hattie, who was building a puppet company on a budget of one shilling, watched the cracked old boot-top come off and asked for it before it reached the floor.\n\nShe cut the tall leather into two long legs, jointed at the knee with eyelets from her uncle’s tin. The tongue made a solemn face. She named him Long Tom. On his laces he could stride the bench in three steps, and he had cost nothing. Elevenpence still in hand.\n\nMr Tibbs came back for his mended boot and watched his old one walking without him. He said it had always wanted to go further than he did."],["A Knot from Uncle Corwin","The fourth puppet nearly ended the company. Hattie Lask, twelve, was making puppets from old shoes in her uncle’s cobbler’s shop in Heel Alley, and Miss Lark, who taught dancing at the Corn Hall, had given her a worn satin slipper, pink, with ribbons. It made a dancer. Hattie spent a penny of her shilling on two jet buttons for eyes and named her Miss Twirl.\n\nBut a dancer must dance, and this one only fell over. Her laces tangled. By supper she was a pink knot, and Hattie was nearly one herself.\n\nUncle Corwin, who had not spoken all day, put down his awl. He undid the tangle and retied each lace to the crossbar with a flat knot Hattie had never seen. Then he threaded one more lace to the dancer’s back, so she could bow. Cobbler’s hitch, he said. Miss Twirl curtsied. Tenpence left."],["The Villain Squeaks","Every play needs a villain, and Hattie Lask could not find one. She was twelve, she was making puppets out of old shoes on a budget of one shilling in her uncle’s cobbler’s shop in Emberwick, and every shoe looked honest. A slipper cannot scowl.\n\nThen Mr Grobe the beadle brought in his boots. They squeaked. They had squeaked through every council meeting for two years, and he could not creep up on so much as a pigeon. Uncle Corwin took off the left sole, found two layers of leather rubbing together, and fitted a new one.\n\nHattie kept the old sole, which cost nothing. Bent double, it made a long sour face with a jaw that opened, and every time the jaw moved, it squeaked. So the villain was called Squeak. He wore a black cloak cut from a boot-bag, and he could never sneak up on anybody."],["The Theatre in the Window","By midsummer Hattie Lask had five puppets made of old shoes and nowhere to put them. Her uncle Corwin’s cobbler’s shop in Heel Alley had one window, where a single pair of mended boots had sat for a month. He moved the boots himself and nodded at the space.\n\nThe stage was the crate the leather came in. Hattie had begun the summer with a shilling and had tenpence left. She spent threepence on red flannel and hemmed it into curtains that drew on a bootlace.\n\nShe rehearsed facing the alley, because the light was better. On Monday three children stopped to watch Long Tom stride and Squeak the villain fail to creep. On Tuesday there were seven, and a dog. On Wednesday a carter left his horse at the corner and came down to look. Nobody had walked down Heel Alley on purpose for a year."],["Ned Pym’s First Shoes","The smallest child who watched the cobbler’s window in Heel Alley was Ned Pym, aged five, who left a nose-print on the glass daily. Behind the glass Hattie Lask, the cobbler’s niece, rehearsed her puppets, each made from somebody’s old shoe.\n\nOne day he came in with his first pair of shoes, outgrown, the toes kicked white. He put them on the bench and said they were to be a dog.\n\nA dog has four legs and a tail, which means many laces. Hattie made the body from one shoe and the head from the other, its tongue hanging out as a tongue should. She spent a penny of her shilling on a buckle for his collar and named him Buckle. He needed more laces than Hattie had hands. So Ned was hired, wages nil, to stand on a stool behind the crate and wag the tail. Sixpence left."],["Rain on the Company","It rained on Emberwick for a week in August, and the roof of the cobbler’s shop in Heel Alley leaked over the window where Hattie Lask kept her theatre. She came down on Thursday to find six puppets, made from other people’s old shoes, sitting in a puddle. Wet leather dries hard and curls as it pleases. Dame Crumb had shrunk. Miss Twirl’s ribbons had run pink into everything.\n\nUncle Corwin said nothing. He fetched the tin of dubbin and two rags, and they sat rubbing grease into small leather people until dinner. Most came round. Mr Welt’s nose stayed curled to one side, which gave him a sly, friendly look, and Hattie decided he was meant to have it.\n\nThat evening she asked at the Corn Hall the price of a dry room with a stage. Five shillings. She had sixpence. The play would be in the window."],["The Boot That Walked","A theatre wants a play. Hattie Lask, who had built six puppets from old shoes in her uncle’s cobbler’s shop in Emberwick, wrote hers on the backs of bills his customers had not paid, of which there were plenty.\n\nIt was called The Boot That Walked to the Sea. Long Tom, the postman’s old boot, loses his other half and walks to the Grey Coast to find it. Dame Crumb feeds him. Miss Twirl points the way. Buckle the dog goes too. Squeak the villain follows to steal his laces, and is always heard coming.\n\nThe sea was Mrs Bunce’s blue shawl, shaken from below. Hattie spent twopence of her last sixpence: a penny on tacks, and a penny on a tin whistle for the gulls. There was no part for a cobbler. She noticed on the last page, and sat tapping the pencil against her teeth. Fourpence left."],["A Puppet of the Cobbler","The seventh puppet was the one Hattie Lask was afraid to show. All summer she had made puppets from Emberwick’s old shoes, on a budget of one shilling, in her uncle Corwin’s cobbler’s shop in Heel Alley. This one she made of him.\n\nHis head was the oldest last in the box. His apron was cut from the pocket of one he had thrown out, and his spectacles were two eyelets. She gave him a stoop. Uncle Corwin was shy, and might not care to be put in a window. She left it on his bench and went to bed.\n\nIn the morning it had changed. On its feet were two boots an inch long, stitched and blacked, laced with waxed thread. He must have sat up half the night. I’ll work him myself, he said. Behind the curtain, mind. Eight words, which for him was a speech."],["Saturday in Heel Alley","On the last Saturday of summer the puppet play was given in the window of Corwin Lask’s cobbler’s shop in Heel Alley. His niece Hattie had spent threepence of her last fourpence on chalk and string, and hung boot-box lids down Market Street reading: PUPPETS. HEEL ALLEY. NOON. FREE.\n\nBy noon the alley was full to the pump. The red curtains drew back on their bootlace. Long Tom strode out; Dame Crumb fed him; Squeak crept and squeaked, and forty children shouted every time. Ned Pym, five, wagged the dog’s tail so hard he fell off his stool.\n\nThen, at the edge of the sea, one of Long Tom’s laces snapped. He sagged. A cobbler puppet in tiny boots bustled on and bent over the walker’s foot, and behind the curtain Uncle Corwin relaced Long Tom in mid-air. It got the biggest cheer of the day. One penny left."],["What a Shoestring Costs","After the puppet play in Heel Alley, Hattie Lask passed round a boot, since a hat seemed wrong for a cobbler’s shop. It came back holding three shillings and fourpence, two buttons and a humbug.\n\nMore came back than money. Half of Emberwick had stood looking into Corwin Lask’s window, and had seen rows of well-mended boots behind the puppets. On Monday the shop bell rang eleven times. Mr Tibbs the postman brought his other boot. Miss Lark brought six pairs of slippers and asked when Squeak would be on again.\n\nBefore she went home she put her last penny on the counter and bought a pair of bootlaces from her uncle, the first thing she had ever paid him for. They were for next year’s puppet. The whole theatre had cost one shilling exactly. A card went up in the window: Boots Mended. Puppets Return Next Summer."]],[["Our First Number","The Branch Line Gazette, January number, being the first. Edited by Mr. P. Nibley, booking clerk at Emberwick, and printed on the parcels office duplicator, which accounts for the purple. Our line is fourteen miles long and has six stations, from Lowland Junction to Grey Coast. Until today it had no newspaper. We mean to appear monthly and to report everything, there being about enough of it.\n\nNOTICE. The Directors announce a silver cup for the Best Kept Station, to be judged in November by the District Inspector. Mr. Tulley, stationmaster at Emberwick, has already been seen measuring his flower bed with ticket string. Mrs. Reed of Marsh Halt says she has seen cups before.\n\nLOST PROPERTY. Found on the 8.15: one umbrella, black, with a handle carved as a duck’s head. It may be claimed at the booking office. The duck looks patient."],["A Question of Geraniums","The Branch Line Gazette, February number. Edited by P. Nibley, booking clerk, Emberwick. Circulation last month: forty-one, counting the copy the goat ate at Withy Bridge.\n\nTHE CUP. With nine months until the Best Kept Station is judged, Mr. Tulley of Emberwick has ordered two hundred geraniums. He intends them to spell the station’s name in red along the bank. Mrs. Reed, porter-in-charge at Marsh Halt, has ordered nothing. She has, however, been seen carrying bean poles toward the signal post, and the signalman has asked us to print that he objects.\n\nWEATHER. Snow on the Fellfoot bank on Tuesday. The 7.05 arrived wearing a white hat and eleven minutes late. LOST PROPERTY. The umbrella with the duck’s-head handle remains unclaimed. We have moved it to a hook by the stove, where the duck seems more comfortable."],["Whitewash at Fellfoot Road","The Branch Line Gazette, March number, from the booking office at Emberwick, where your editor Mr. Nibley sells tickets between paragraphs. LOST PROPERTY. One duck-headed umbrella, still. Also one glove, claimed within the hour by the owner of its fellow.\n\nFELLFOOT ROAD ENTERS. Young Tam Ogle, the only porter at Fellfoot Road, has declared for the Best Kept Station cup. His method is whitewash. In one week he has whitened the platform edge, the coal bunker, the fence, forty-two stones and, by mistake, one side of a sheep that leaned. The sheep’s owner has been spoken to and is said to prefer it.\n\nEMBERWICK. Mr. Tulley’s two hundred geraniums have arrived by goods train and are living in the waiting room until the frosts are over. Passengers are asked not to sit on the ones marked W."],["Shells and a Lifebelt","The Branch Line Gazette, April number. Edited at Emberwick by P. Nibley, booking clerk, with reports from all six stations of our fourteen miles.\n\nFROM GREY COAST. Mr. Josiah Pinch, stationmaster at the seaside end, writes that flowers will not stand his wind, so he is competing for the Best Kept Station cup with shells. He has edged both platforms in cockles and hung a lifebelt, freshly painted, beside the clock. Gulls have removed some of the cockles. He regards this as the judge’s problem.\n\nMARSH HALT. Mrs. Reed’s runner beans are up the signal post to the height of a tall child. The signalman reports that he can still see his signal arm, for now. LOST PROPERTY. We lent the duck-headed umbrella to a lady caught in Thursday’s shower. She returned it dry and said it was not hers either."],["The Planting Out","The Branch Line Gazette, May number. P. Nibley, editor and booking clerk, Emberwick.\n\nA GREAT DAY. On Saturday Mr. Tulley planted out his geraniums on the bank opposite platform one. He used a plumb line, a yardstick and the station cat, who was not helpful. By the arrival of the 4.20 the bank read EMBERWICK in red letters three feet high. The driver sounded his whistle. Several passengers applauded from the windows, and one asked whether it was there to remind us.\n\nLOST PROPERTY. The umbrella. Enquiries have now been made at every station on the line. The duck on the handle keeps its own counsel. A SMALL MATTER. Nothing whatever has been done at Withy Bridge, which has no staff, only a shelter, a lamp and the goat belonging to the mill. We mention it for completeness."],["Ember Ick","The Branch Line Gazette, June number, edited at the Emberwick booking office by Mr. P. Nibley, who saw the whole thing and could do nothing, having a queue.\n\nDISASTER ON THE BANK. On Wednesday the Withy Bridge goat, travelling without a ticket in the guard’s van of the 10.30, alighted at Emberwick and ate the letter W. Mr. Tulley’s geraniums now spell EMBER ICK. The goat was returned by the next train. The W was not. Mr. Tulley has taken it quietly, which worries everyone. Mrs. Reed of Marsh Halt, his chief rival for the Best Kept Station cup, sent six spare geraniums up the line in a bucket, with a note that said only: For the gap.\n\nLOST PROPERTY. One umbrella, duck-headed, five months. One goat, briefly."],["Slugs and Diplomacy","The Branch Line Gazette, July number. Edited by P. Nibley at Emberwick, where the bank once again says EMBERWICK, the W being slightly pinker than its neighbours.\n\nMARSH HALT. Slugs have found Mrs. Reed’s runner beans. She patrols the signal post at dusk with a lantern and a jam jar. Mr. Tulley, remembering six geraniums in a bucket, sent down a sack of soot for the roots and a page of advice in copperplate. The two rivals for the cup were afterwards seen sharing a bench at the Junction, discussing greenfly. Each denies it.\n\nWITHY BRIDGE. A passenger reports poppies along the platform fence. We assume the wind. LOST PROPERTY. The duck-headed umbrella has been dusted. A gentleman claimed it on Friday, looked the duck in the eye, and admitted that his had been a swan."],["Water by the Bucket","The Branch Line Gazette, August number, from your editor Mr. Nibley in the Emberwick booking office, with the window open and a wasp.\n\nDROUGHT. No rain for twenty-six days. The water butts at Emberwick ran dry on the ninth, and Mr. Tulley’s geraniums began to hang their heads. Driver Enoch Pask now stops the engine Heron a yard short of her mark each evening and lets a little water from her tank into a row of buckets. This is against at least two rules. We have not seen it and nor have you.\n\nLOST PROPERTY. Umbrella, duck-headed. Nobody wants an umbrella in August. ELSEWHERE. Fellfoot Road reports its whitewash has never looked whiter. Grey Coast reports sunburnt cockles. At Withy Bridge there are hollyhocks now, and a row of something like peas. The wind there is evidently tidy."],["Beans for Everyone","The Branch Line Gazette, September number. Edited by P. Nibley, booking clerk, Emberwick.\n\nHARVEST. The runner beans on the signal post at Marsh Halt have reached the lamp. Mrs. Reed picked eleven pounds on Monday and sent a bundle up the line by every train, tied to the single-line token so that no signalman could refuse it. Your editor had his with butter. THE CUP. Two months to judging. Mr. Tulley has clipped his geraniums with nail scissors. Young Tam at Fellfoot Road has whitewashed the whitewash. Mr. Pinch at Grey Coast has replaced nineteen cockles and named the gull responsible.\n\nLOST PROPERTY. The duck-headed umbrella enters its ninth month. We have begun saying good morning to it. A PUZZLE. Somebody has painted the Withy Bridge shelter green. The Company says it was not the Company."],["The Gale","The Branch Line Gazette, October number, edited at Emberwick by Mr. P. Nibley and delayed two days by weather.\n\nTHE GALE. On the night of the fourteenth a wind came in off the Grey Coast and went the length of our line. At Grey Coast, Mr. Pinch’s lifebelt was found in a field. At Fellfoot Road the fence lay down. At Marsh Halt the beans, poles and all, left the signal post and have not been seen since. At Emberwick the bank now reads, as nearly as we can make out, E B RW K. With the judging three weeks off, no staffed station on the line is fit to be seen. Mr. Tulley and Mrs. Reed spent Sunday on each other’s platforms with brooms.\n\nLOST PROPERTY. The duck-headed umbrella was lent to the editor during the gale. It held. We think more of it than ever."],["The Inspector’s Special","The Branch Line Gazette, November number. P. Nibley, editor, Emberwick booking office.\n\nJUDGING DAY. The District Inspector, Mr. Corbel, travelled the line on Tuesday in a saloon coach, judging the Best Kept Station. He found Grey Coast short of cockles, Fellfoot Road whitewashed over its damage, Marsh Halt swept bare, and Emberwick spelling its name in the few geraniums the gale had left, eked out with painted flowerpots. He was polite at each.\n\nThen, at Withy Bridge, where trains stop only by request, he requested. He was on the platform nine minutes. Your editor, leaning from a window, saw a green shelter, a clipped hedge, late marigolds in a row, and the goat tethered well away from them. Mr. Corbel wrote for some time. LOST PROPERTY. The umbrella, as before. THE RESULT will appear next month."],["The Cup and the Duck","The Branch Line Gazette, December number, closing our first year. Edited by P. Nibley, booking clerk, Emberwick.\n\nTHE CUP for the Best Kept Station goes to Withy Bridge. The gardener has owned up: Mr. Garrow, the lengthman, who walks our fourteen miles of track each week and has planted that platform out of his coat pockets since spring. He accepted the cup in the Emberwick waiting room and asked if it would hold soup. Mr. Tulley and Mrs. Reed led the cheering, and have since exchanged cuttings.\n\nLOST PROPERTY. On the twentieth a small lady in a grey hat asked for an umbrella with a duck’s-head handle. She left it on the 8.15 in January, she said, quite on purpose, her own hall being damp. She thanked us for the year’s storage. The hook by the stove looks bare. We resume in January."]],[["Twelve Minutes Exactly","My name is Ida Pettigrew, and I keep the refreshment room on platform one at Emberwick. I have two things on the wall behind my counter: a looking-glass, which I seldom consult, and the Company’s timetable, which I know as other people know their prayers.\n\nOurs is a single line. Every train from the Junction must wait at Emberwick for the train from Grey Coast to pass it, and the timetable allows twelve minutes for the purpose. Twelve minutes is exactly long enough for a person to step down, drink one hot cup of tea, eat one currant bun and step up again without running.\n\nI light the urn at six. She is copper, older than I am and slow to forgive, and I call her the Duchess. By the first whistle she is singing. That is my whole trade: a timetable, an urn and twelve minutes."],["The 7.05 and Forty Cups","The first train I serve at my refreshment room in Emberwick station is the 7.05 down, which carries the reed-cutters to the marsh sidings and the quarrymen on to Fellfoot Road. They come through my door all at once, forty men in a twelve-minute wait, and not one of them talkative at that hour.\n\nSo the cups stand ready in rows of ten, milk in first, sugar according to a chart I keep in my head. Mr. Hobb takes three lumps. The Dunn brothers take none and look pained at those who do. I pour down the rows with the big pot in one long stroke, like a woman watering beans, and Albie the junior porter carries two cans along to the driver and fireman.\n\nAt eleven minutes the guard looks in. At twelve the room is empty and warm, and smells of wet coats."],["Buns for the 8.15","The 8.15 up is the school train. I am Mrs. Pettigrew of the Emberwick refreshment room, and from September to July that train brings me eighteen children bound for the grammar school at the Junction, with twelve minutes to wait and a penny each.\n\nA penny buys a currant bun, yesterday’s, split and buttered. I do not sell children tea. They get milk if they have been civil and water if they have not, and they know which they have been before I say so.\n\nCuthbert Lowe is late every morning of his life. I hear his boots on the footbridge as the guard lifts his flag. So his bun goes on the windowsill of the last compartment, in a paper bag, and a hand comes out and takes it as the train moves. I have never once had the penny late."],["A Notice in Purple Ink","In April a notice came to Emberwick station in a long envelope, and Mr. Tulley the stationmaster brought it across to my refreshment room, which told me it was bad before I had read a word.\n\nIt was from the Timetable Office at the Junction. From the first of June, in the interests of efficiency, the wait at Emberwick would be reduced from twelve minutes to four. It was signed L. Fenn, in purple ink, with a flourish under the name like a siding.\n\nFour minutes. I worked it out. A cup of tea poured properly is too hot to drink for three. A person needs one to get down from the carriage and cross the platform. That leaves no minutes at all for the tea to be a pleasure, and none for the bun. I gave Mr. Tulley a cup anyway. He needed it."],["The Four-Minute Experiment","Before the new timetable came in, I tested it. I am Ida Pettigrew, I have kept the refreshment room at Emberwick station for nineteen years, and I do not condemn a thing untried.\n\nAlbie the porter played the passenger. Mr. Tulley held the station clock. On the first trial Albie reached my counter in fifty seconds, was served in twenty, and then stood blowing on his tea until Mr. Tulley called four minutes with the cup still full. On the second I poured beforehand, and he drank it in comfort, stone cold. On the third I gave him half a cup. He said it was like half a handshake. On the fourth he drank it hot regardless, and could taste nothing at dinner.\n\nThe Duchess, my urn, hissed through all of it. I took her meaning."],["Letters to Mr. Fenn","I wrote to the Timetable Office. I am no letter-writer; I keep the refreshment room on Emberwick station, and my hand is better suited to a teapot. But the wait at Emberwick was to be cut from twelve minutes to four, and somebody had to say what four minutes does to a cup of tea.\n\nMy first letter was polite. The answer was a printed card: Your communication has been received. My second enclosed the results of my experiments. Another card. With my third I sent a petition, signed by forty reed-cutters and quarrymen, eighteen schoolchildren, two drivers and a commercial traveller in buttons, all on the back of a bun bag because it was what we had.\n\nThat one was answered by hand. The purple ink said: Madam, trains are not run for the convenience of buns. L. Fenn. I pinned it over the counter, beside the timetable."],["The First of June","On the first of June the new timetable came into force, and the trains were given four minutes at Emberwick instead of twelve. I had the Duchess singing by six as usual, and the cups set out in their rows in my refreshment room on platform one. Habit is hard to argue with.\n\nThe 7.05 drew in. Nobody got down. Forty men looked at me through the carriage windows, and I looked at them over forty cups. Mr. Hobb raised his hand, the way you would to a neighbour across a river. Then the guard blew, four minutes to the tick, and they were gone toward the marsh with nothing hot inside them.\n\nI poured the tea back into the pot, which you should never do, and sat down on the customers’ side of my own counter. It is a draughty side. I had not known."],["The Runaway Trolley","If passengers could no longer come to my tea, I decided, my tea would go to them. The new timetable allowed trains only four minutes at Emberwick, so Albie the porter and I fitted out a luggage barrow as a refreshment trolley: the second-best urn lashed on with parcel string, cups in a drawer from the lost property cupboard, buns under a cloth.\n\nIt answered well for two days. On the third the 11.40 stopped a carriage short, I pushed harder than I meant to, and the barrow found the ramp at the platform end. It went down by itself, steaming, at about the speed of a trotting pony, and came to rest in Mr. Tulley’s geraniums.\n\nWhat vexed me most was this. The train from Grey Coast was late, as it very often is, and the 11.40 stood there nine full minutes with everybody afraid to get out."],["A Man with a Stopwatch","One wet morning in August a thin gentleman got down from the 9.50 at Emberwick, let it go without him, and stood on my platform with a stopwatch in one hand and a notebook in the other. He timed the porter. I believe he timed a pigeon. Then the rain came on harder, and he came into my refreshment room and asked what there was.\n\nThere was tea, I said, and seed cake cut that morning, and any amount of time to enjoy them in, since the Timetable Office had seen to it that I had no customers. I told him about the twelve minutes that had become four. I may have gone on. I showed him the note pinned over my counter.\n\nHe read it twice. Then he took out his own pen to write down the price of seed cake, and the ink was purple."],["Mr. Fenn Misses His Train","Mr. Lucius Fenn of the Timetable Office came to Emberwick three days running, and sat each day at the corner table of my refreshment room with his stopwatch beside his saucer. He had cut our wait from twelve minutes to four. He wished to know, he said, why his trains declined to keep to it.\n\nSo I told him what the old twelve minutes had been for. In them the engine took water. The single-line token changed hands. Parcels went in, hens came out, and the guard’s dog was walked to the lamp post. Forty men got warm. A railway, I said, wants somewhere to draw breath, and his timetable had it holding its nose.\n\nHe wrote all that down. He was on his second slice of seed cake, and still writing, when the 2.15 left without him. I did not point it out. The Duchess whistled a little."],["Fourteen Minutes","The winter timetable reached Emberwick in the last week of September. Mr. Tulley the stationmaster carried it across to my refreshment room at a trot, with his thumb marking the page.\n\nEmberwick: arrive 7.05, depart 7.19. I counted on my fingers like a child. Fourteen minutes. Not the four we had suffered all summer, nor even the old twelve, but fourteen, at every train of the day. At the foot of the column stood a small printed teacup, and beside it the words: Refreshments obtainable.\n\nOn the first Monday the 7.05 drew in and every door opened at once. Forty men came across the platform. Mr. Hobb put down his penny and said, “Three lumps, Mrs. Pettigrew,” as if he had been gone one night and not four months. I poured down the rows in one stroke. My hand was not quite steady, and nobody mentioned it."],["The 9.40 and Goodnight","The last train I serve is the 9.40 down to Grey Coast. By then my refreshment room at Emberwick has only the one lamp lit, and the passengers are few: a nurse, a fiddler going home, sometimes a sailor asleep on his kitbag. They get what is left in the pot and the end of the seed cake, at half price.\n\nMr. Fenn of the Timetable Office travels by it on the first Friday of each month. He says he is observing the fourteen minutes he gave us. He observes them from the corner table, with cake. Last time he left a penny too much, and a timetable with my teacup inked over in purple.\n\nWhen the tail lamp has gone round the curve I let the Duchess cool, rinse the last cup and turn the card in the door. Six o’clock comes early."]],[["Three, Pause, One","Nell Arkwright had been a signalwoman for eleven days when the fog came. It rose off the marsh after midnight and leaned on the windows of Marsh Halt signal box until she could not see her own home signal, forty yards off. She had the stove, the lamp, the levers in their painted row, and the two block bells that spoke to the boxes on either side.\n\nNothing was due until the milk train at ten to five. She made tea.\n\nAt ten past two the Emberwick bell rang. Three beats, a pause, one more. Nell knew her bell codes as she knew her own name; she had been examined in them only that month. This was not among them. She tapped a single beat back, which means I am here, speak. The bell stayed silent. Outside, the fog went on saying nothing at all."],["Damp in the Wires","In the morning Nell Arkwright, night signalwoman at Marsh Halt, told the day man what she had heard: an unknown bell signal out of the fog at ten past two, three beats, a pause and one.\n\nMr. Osric Bream had worked the box for sixteen years and did not care to be told things in it. Damp, he said. Fog got into the wires and the bells rang by themselves. He hung up his coat as though the matter hung there with it.\n\nNell had grown up beside this railway. A damp bell, she knew, gives a single dull clunk, or a stutter. It does not count three, wait, and add one, as neatly as a person laying a table. She wrote the time and the signal in the train register, in pencil, and drew a small square round the entry so that she could find it again."],["Emberwick Heard It Too","The fog held a second night over the marsh. Nell Arkwright sat in Marsh Halt signal box with the train register open at her pencilled note about a bell signal nobody could explain, and watched the clock climb toward two.\n\nAt ten past, the Emberwick bell rang: three, pause, one. She cranked the telephone to Emberwick box, two miles up the line, and Hale Dobbin answered with a yawn in his voice. “Was that you, Hale?” she asked. “Me? I was going to ask you,” he said. “My bell’s just gone the same. Three and a one. I’ve not touched my tapper since the last train.”\n\nSo the signal had rung at both ends of the section at once. Nell put the receiver back. Whoever sent it was not in her box, and not in Hale’s. They were somewhere in the two dark miles between."],["The Lamp That Lit Itself","At first light, with the fog thinning, Nell Arkwright walked down the line from Marsh Halt box to look at her distant signal. Wilf Tansy the lampman had confessed that he had forgotten to fill it two days ago. It ought to have burned dry in the night.\n\nIt was burning. She climbed the ladder. The lamp was full, the glass polished, and the wick had been trimmed straight across, square as a chisel. Wilf trimmed his wicks round, like a thumbnail; he had shown her on her first day and been proud of it.\n\nOn the bottom rung, in the frost, was half a boot print. The heel had an iron plate shaped like a horseshoe, worn right through on one side. Nell set her own boot beside it. Longer than hers, and narrow. She drew the heel in her notebook before the sun could take it."],["A Paper of Peppermints","Nell Arkwright, the new signalwoman at Marsh Halt, had two puzzles from two foggy nights: a bell signal that no rule book listed, and a signal lamp filled and trimmed by somebody who was not the lampman. On the third morning she found a third.\n\nIn fog, small warning caps are clipped to the rail beside the distant signal, so that a driver who cannot see is told by a crack under his wheels. That night she had had nobody to send. Yet there they sat on the rail, three of them, spaced as exactly as buttons on a waistcoat. In the lamp hut the tin was three short, with its lid put back straight. On the shelf lay a twist of paper holding two striped peppermints. The hut smelled of them, and faintly of pipe.\n\nA thief who tidies, thought Nell, and who does my work for me."],["The Old Code Book","Mrs. Reed, who had charge of Marsh Halt station, held that the bell in the fog was the Grey Signalman, a ghost she had invented on the spot and grew fonder of hourly. Mr. Bream the day man still said damp. Nell Arkwright, who worked the box by night and had heard the thing ring three, pause, one, went looking for a third opinion in the stationmaster’s cupboard at Emberwick.\n\nBehind the spare flags she found a bell code book with a brown cover, printed for the old Ember Valley Railway before the present Company bought it. She ran her finger down the column. Three, pause, one: Is line clear for goods train.\n\nInside the cover someone had stamped Withdrawn, and a date twenty-six years gone. Ghosts, she supposed, need not learn codes. Somebody had learned this one long ago, and never unlearned it."],["The Second Hook","Mr. Latchford the linesman came out to Marsh Halt to test the bell wires, because the night signalwoman, Nell Arkwright, kept reporting a signal that rang from nowhere on foggy nights. He found the wires sound. Then he remembered something.\n\nReed Siding box, a mile up toward Emberwick, had been shut three years ago when the reed-cutters’ siding was lifted. They took out the levers. They never took out the bell tapper. It was still in the circuit, to save a journey, and anyone pressing it would ring both neighbours at once.\n\nThe box was kept locked. Nell went to see its key, which hung in the stationmaster’s office at Emberwick, grey with dust. Beside it was a second hook, labelled Reed Siding, No. 2. That hook was empty. In the key book, No. 2 had been signed out long before Nell was born, and never signed in."],["A Warm Stove","Reed Siding signal box stood by itself in the reed beds, a mile from Marsh Halt, with its windows boarded and its name board gone. It had been shut three years. Nell Arkwright walked out to it along the track on her rest day, under a white sky, because a bell signal was coming from it on foggy nights and she meant to know whose finger was on the tapper.\n\nThe steps had been swept. The door was locked, but one board had a knot hole, and she put her eye to it. A stove, with its door ajar and grey ash inside. She laid her palm on the chimney pipe where it came through the wall. Barely warm; but warm.\n\nOn the shelf where the block bells used to stand were a tin mug, a kettle, and a train register lying open, with a pen across it."],["Mrs. Reed Remembers","Nobody knew the people of the marsh as Mrs. Reed did, so Nell Arkwright took her questions to the Marsh Halt booking office. Who had last worked Reed Siding box before it shut? Who would still have its second key, and know a bell code withdrawn twenty-six years ago, and trim a lamp wick square?\n\nMrs. Reed gave up her ghost with regret. “That’s Jethro Vane,” she said. “Forty-one years in that box. They retired him the day they shut it. He lives at Crossing Cottage with his daughter, and she says he’s never slept through a fog in his life. Stands at the window in his nightshirt.” “Does he eat peppermints?” asked Nell. “By the pound,” said Mrs. Reed. “And wears his father’s boots, heel plates and all.”\n\nNell closed her notebook. She had her answer on paper. She found she wanted it from the man himself."],["The Watch at Reed Siding","The fog came back on the Sunday. It was Nell Arkwright’s night off from Marsh Halt box. She put on two coats, filled a flask, and at one in the morning walked up the track to the shut signal box at Reed Siding, where she sat down on the bottom step to wait for whoever came there to ring the bell.\n\nFor an hour there was only dripping. Then a lantern showed along the path from Crossing Cottage, swinging low, and with it came the click of iron heel plates and a tune hummed round a peppermint.\n\nAn old man in a railway greatcoat climbed past her without seeing her, thin and very upright. A key turned. Above her head a stove door clanked, a match scraped, and, clear through the boards, a bell tapper went: three, pause, one. “Mr. Vane,” said Nell, from the stairs. “It’s line clear.”"],["Forty-One Winters","Jethro Vane, three years retired, made tea for two in the closed signal box at Reed Siding and was not at all ashamed. Nell Arkwright, signalwoman at Marsh Halt, sat on the locker and listened while the fog pressed at the boards.\n\nIn his first winter, a goods train had stood lost in fog between two boxes for three hours, because each signalman thought the other had it. Nobody was hurt. The driver’s remarks were remembered for a generation. After that Jethro asked after the 2.05 goods every foggy night for forty-one years, and when they took off the goods train, and then shut the box, he found he could not stop asking.\n\nHe had thought the bell was dead. The lamp and the fog caps he saw to on his way, since Wilf forgot. His register held three years of fogs, each entered: On watch. All passed safe."],["Fogman on Watch","Nell Arkwright might have reported an intruder in the old signal box at Reed Siding. Instead she wrote four pages to the District Inspector, and Mr. Tulley, the stationmaster at Emberwick, read them twice and signed.\n\nThe answer came within the week. Mr. Jethro Vane was appointed fogman for the Marsh Halt section: called out in fog, paid by the night, and trusted with the lamps, the fog caps and the key he had never handed in. Mr. Bream said he had suspected as much. Mrs. Reed said the Grey Signalman would be missed.\n\nThe next fog came in December. Nell sat in her box with the stove ticking. At ten past two the bell rang: three, pause, one. She opened the local instructions, where a new line stood in her own handwriting. Fogman on watch. She tapped back one beat, I am here, and poured her tea."]],[["The 8.55, My Last","Lowland Junction, platform three, Midwinter Eve. I am Ezra Coombe, guard, and this is the 8.55 to Grey Coast, the last train of the night. It is also my last train of any kind. Thirty-eight years on the Emberwick branch end when I hang up my lamp at the far end tonight, and I have told nobody on board, because I would rather do the job properly than be fussed over.\n\nTwo coaches and my van. The engine is Heron, with Enoch Pask driving. Eleven passengers, among them a cello with a man attached, a goose in a basket, a sailor already asleep, and a girl of eight named Posy Larkin with a label tied to her coat button: To Grey Coast. Please see she gets there.\n\nThe main line connection is seven minutes late. We wait for it. We always have. Then I show green, and we go."],["Fog at Marsh Halt","The 8.55 from the Junction, last train of the night to Grey Coast, runs out across the Lowlands in the dark with myself, Ezra Coombe, in the guard’s van. I have ridden this van thirty-eight years. Tonight is the last time, so I am noticing things: how the lamp swings, how the floor drums over the river Ember bridge and then goes quiet on the marsh, as if the train had taken its boots off.\n\nMarsh Halt comes up out of fog. Mrs. Reed is on the platform with a lantern, and nobody gets on or off. She hands up the mailbag and then, without a word, a brick hot from her oven, wrapped in flannel, for my feet.\n\nShe has done that every winter night for twenty years. I say thank you as I always do, as if it were nothing much. The signal drops. We go on."],["The Admiral at Large","Between Marsh Halt and Emberwick the last train to Grey Coast has two miles of straight track and nothing to do, which is when things happen. I, Ezra Coombe, the guard, am checking parcels in my van when Mrs. Skerry’s basket, labelled Live Goose, This Way Up, turns out to be the wrong way up and open.\n\nThe goose is called the Admiral. He is not for eating; he is a present for her sister and has, she says, opinions. He walks the length of the first coach like an inspector. He looks at the sleeping sailor and at the cello. He stops by Posy Larkin, a girl travelling alone, who has been sitting up straight and not crying, and puts his head in her lap.\n\nI leave him there. The Company’s rules on geese are clear. I have obeyed rules for thirty-eight years and have about an hour left."],["Fourteen Minutes at Emberwick","Emberwick, 9.33, seven minutes down. I am Ezra Coombe, guard of the last train to Grey Coast, and the timetable gives us fourteen minutes here, which tonight nobody wastes.\n\nOne lamp is lit in the refreshment room. Mrs. Pettigrew gives the cellist tea, wakes nobody, and sends a cup of warm milk out to the girl with the label on her coat, who drinks it at the carriage door with a goose looking on. Enoch Pask takes water for the engine. Mr. Tulley the stationmaster walks the platform with me, trying doors I have already tried.\n\nAt the van he shakes my hand a moment longer than usual. So he knows it is my last trip. Stationmasters hear everything. He says only, “Snow on the Fells, Ezra. Mind the bank.” Then he steps back, and Mrs. Pettigrew turns down her lamp, and I blow."],["A Lamp at Withy Bridge","Withy Bridge is a request stop: a shelter, a lamp and a goat. In thirty-eight years as guard on this line I, Ezra Coombe, have seen the last train to Grey Coast stop there perhaps a dozen times. Tonight somebody is swinging a lamp on the platform, and Enoch brings the engine to a stand.\n\nIt is old Mr. Garrow the lengthman, with a sack for a cape. He does not want to travel. He has a parcel, warm, tied in a cloth: a mutton pie for his sister at Grey Coast, who has had one from him every Midwinter Eve since they were young. He missed the afternoon train on account of the goat.\n\nHe has no label and no money. I take the pie into the van and write the ticket myself. The first flakes of snow come down through the lamplight as we pull away."],["The Fellfoot Bank","After Withy Bridge the line climbs for two miles to Fellfoot Road, and tonight the last train to Grey Coast has snow on the rails. I am Ezra Coombe, the guard. From my van I can hear Heron working: a slow, heavy beat, then a sudden flurry as her wheels slip, then the beat again.\n\nHalfway up she stops. It is not dangerous, only still. The snow comes past the windows quite silently. Enoch Pask is out with the sand bucket and his fireman with a shovel, and I walk through the two coaches to tell eleven passengers that we shall be a little while and are in no difficulty.\n\nNobody complains. Mrs. Skerry shares out gingerbread. The girl Posy asks whether trains get tired. I tell her yes, on hills, like anybody, and that this one has never yet failed to reach the top."],["Music in the First Coach","We are standing on the Fellfoot bank in the snow, the last train of the night to Grey Coast, while the engine crew sand the rails ahead. I am the guard, Ezra Coombe, and I have nothing to do but keep eleven people cheerful.\n\nMr. Dando does it for me. He takes the cello out of its case, wedges himself across two seats, and plays. It is no tune I know: something slow and brown, like a river in autumn, and then a jig that sets the lamp glasses buzzing. The sailor sleeps through it, smiling. The Admiral, a goose, joins in once and is hushed. Posy Larkin keeps time on the label tied to her coat.\n\nPartway through the jig I feel the couplings tighten. Heron has found her feet. We move off uphill at a walking pace, with music going out of the windows into the dark."],["Wake Me at Fellfoot","Fellfoot Road, the highest station on the branch, under two inches of snow. I am Ezra Coombe, guard of the last train to Grey Coast, now twenty-six minutes late, and I have a sailor to deliver.\n\nHe has slept since the Junction with his head on his kitbag and a card pinned to his jersey: Wake me at Fellfoot Road. I shake his shoulder. He comes up all at once, asking whose watch it is. I tell him it is his mother’s.\n\nShe is on the platform under the one lamp, a small woman in a man’s coat, with Tam the porter holding a lantern beside her. She has met this train three nights running in case his ship was early. The sailor gets down. Neither of them says anything I can hear. He drops the kitbag and picks her up, and Tam looks tactfully at the snow."],["Sheep in the Cutting","Beyond Fellfoot Road the line runs through Gorse Cutting before it drops to the sea. Tonight, with the last train to Grey Coast nearly half an hour late, the cutting is full of sheep. They have come down off the Fells out of the snow, forty or so, and they stand between the rails in the light of the engine lamps looking put out.\n\nI am the guard, Ezra Coombe, and the rule book makes livestock on the line my business. I go forward with my hand lamp. The fireman comes with his shovel. Mr. Dando comes too, uninvited, carrying his cello bow like a crook, and Mrs. Skerry’s goose sends his opinion from a window.\n\nIt takes twelve minutes. The last ewe goes up the bank with a look over her shoulder. I count them out of habit and walk back along the train in my own footprints."],["Down to the Sea","From Gorse Cutting it is downhill all the way. The last train to Grey Coast runs out from under the snow cloud, and from my van I, Ezra Coombe, guard, watch the sky clear from the seaward side, one star and then a great many.\n\nThis is the stretch I shall miss. The engine hardly works; she only holds us back, and the wheels make a long easy sound like something being poured. First you smell the sea, coal smoke and salt together. Then the line swings left round Cockle Point, and there is the harbour light, turning.\n\nIn the first coach Posy Larkin has her nose against the glass. I tell her the red lamp below the lighthouse is the station, and that somebody is standing under it. I cannot see that from here. But I have brought this train in for thirty-eight years, and somebody always is."],["Grey Coast, Forty-One Late","Grey Coast station, end of the line, 11.02 by the platform clock. The last train is forty-one minutes late, and every person who meant to meet it has waited. I am Ezra Coombe, the guard, and I open the doors myself.\n\nMrs. Skerry’s sister receives the Admiral and is hissed at fondly. Mr. Dando carries his cello off toward the harbour, where there is a dance he was to have started. A stout woman with a lantern takes a mutton pie from my hands, sniffs it, and says her brother has overdone the pepper again, which means it is right.\n\nLast comes Posy Larkin, with her label. A tall old lady in a blue shawl reads it aloud, To Grey Coast, please see she gets there, and looks at me. “She got there,” I say. Posy takes her grandmother’s hand, and then, quickly, shakes mine."],["Lamp Out","When the last train has emptied at Grey Coast there is still the guard’s work to do, and tonight I, Ezra Coombe, do it slowly. I walk both coaches. I find a goose feather, gingerbread crumbs and one small red mitten, which I leave on the office door handle for a girl of eight to claim tomorrow.\n\nEnoch Pask has uncoupled Heron and is taking her to the shed. He sounds the whistle as he passes my van, one long and two short, which is in no rule book. Mr. Pinch the stationmaster is waiting at the office door with the journal and a teapot and two cups.\n\nI write it up. The 8.55 from the Junction, arrived 11.02, forty-one minutes late owing to snow and sheep. Eleven passengers. All delivered. Then I sign my name, turn down the wick, and hang the lamp on its hook."]],[["The Carrier’s Cart","I am Tamsin Rudd, piano tuner, of Tallow Lane in Emberwick, and until Tuesday the highest thing I had climbed was the organ loft at the chapel. Then came a letter from Mrs Hallam of Kettledale Head. Her piano had not been tuned in eleven years, her daughter Bess was to be married on Saturday, and would I come.\n\nBy road Kettledale is two days round through the Lowlands. Over the High Pass it is nine miles. I chose the nine miles. I say this so that nobody else is blamed.\n\nThe carrier set me down at Gimmer Gate, the last farm, at seven. The fell postman was waiting there with his bag: Osric Mattock, a lean man the colour of a fence. He looked at my tuning case, then at my boots. “Nine miles,” he said, “and two thousand feet. The feet are the long part.”"],["The First Thousand Feet","From Gimmer Gate the path to the High Pass goes straight up beside Scrat Beck, and it does not apologise. I am a piano tuner, and had imagined a pass as a kind of lane. Osric Mattock, the fell postman, who crosses every Tuesday with the Kettledale bag and comes back on Thursday, walked ahead at the pace of a man following a hearse.\n\nI thought him slow. I passed him twice in the first half hour, and twice sat down on a rock with my heart going like a metronome set too fast, and twice he went by without a word or a change of step. After the second time I fell in behind him and stayed there.\n\nBy nine the farm was a grey button below us, and my tuning case, eleven pounds in Emberwick, weighed nearer twenty. Osric said it would be thirty at the top."],["A Summit That Wasn’t","At half past nine on the morning I crossed the High Pass, I reached the top. There was a cairn on a green shoulder and nothing above it but sky. I put down my tuning case, shook the postman’s hand, and ate both my sandwiches as a celebration.\n\nOsric Mattock let me finish. He is a patient man; he has carried the Kettledale post over this fell every Tuesday for twenty-six years. Then he pointed with his stick. Behind the green shoulder stood the rest of the mountain, grey, steep, and a good deal taller than the part we had done. A thin path wandered up into a notch between two crags.\n\n“That’s the pass,” he said. “This is Hopeful Knott. Everybody eats their dinner here.” I had one apple left for the real dinner hour, and I have thought about those sandwiches a great deal."],["Weather From the West","Above Hopeful Knott, on the long slant up to the High Pass, Osric Mattock stopped for the first time all morning. He is the fell postman, and I was the piano tuner he had agreed to see over to Kettledale. He sniffed. He looked west, where the sky had gone the colour of a pewter plate. “Ten minutes,” he said, and got his cape out.\n\nIt was eight. The rain came up the valley like a curtain being drawn, and then it was simply everywhere: in front, behind, and blowing upward. I had a good town mackintosh. It kept the rain off very well in all the places where the rain was not trying to get in. The rest came down my neck in a steady, exploring trickle.\n\nI asked how long it would last. Osric considered the matter fairly. “It’s Tuesday now,” he said."],["The Peat Hags","Two thirds of the way up to the High Pass the ground gives up being ground. The path crosses a wide black shelf of peat, cut into islands and trenches, and every trench holds water the colour of strong tea. Osric Mattock, the postman, went over it in a zigzag, like a man who knows which stair creaks. I, Tamsin Rudd, piano tuner, followed three steps behind and one step wrong.\n\nMy left leg went in to the knee. It was not dangerous. It was only cold, and extremely firm. Osric came back and took my tuning case from me first, I noticed, and then my arm. The bog let go with a noise like a cork leaving a very large bottle. My boot came too, which he said was lucky.\n\n“It has a pair of mine,” he said. “From my first winter. I expect it’s keeping them for me.”"],["The Shelter Stone","Halfway over the High Pass, by Osric Mattock’s reckoning, there is a boulder the size of a hay cart that leans on another. Underneath is a dry space with room for a postman, a piano tuner, and one sheep who was there first and did not move up.\n\nWe ate. That is, Osric ate oatcake and cold bacon, and I ate an apple, having finished my sandwiches hours too soon on Hopeful Knott. He watched me for a while, then broke the oatcake down the middle and handed half across without remark. It was the best thing I have eaten this year.\n\nI took stock. Hat: wet. Collar: wet. Left leg: peat to the knee. The Kettledale letters, wrapped in oilcloth in his bag, were perfectly dry. I said the post was better looked after than the passenger. “The post can’t walk,” said Osric, and gave the sheep his crust."],["Into the Cloud","Above the shelter stone the High Pass goes into the cloud and does not come out until the other side. I could see my own boots, the postman’s back, and sometimes a cairn: a heap of stones as tall as a child, looming grey out of the grey. There is one every forty paces. Osric Mattock built eleven of them himself.\n\nWe went from cairn to cairn like people crossing a dark room by the furniture. My legs had stopped complaining and begun simply to report: this is a step, this is another.\n\nAt every cairn Osric whistled one low note. I asked why. For Fly, he said, a dog he had for fourteen years; she used to wait at each one. I am a piano tuner. The note was a flat B, every time, exactly the same flat B. I have known pianos less reliable."],["The Top of the Pass","The top of the High Pass is a notch between two crags, about as wide as Tallow Lane at home, with the wind coming through it like a train. I reached it at ten past one behind Osric Mattock, the fell postman, and sat down on the first thing that was not a puddle.\n\nThere is a slate box there, built into the rock, with a flat stone for a lid. Kettledale folk leave their letters in it and Osric collects them: four that day, and a parcel of knitting. He wrote the time in a small book.\n\nI had hoped for a view. There was cloud. Then, for about as long as it takes to say so, it tore open, and I saw a green valley far down, a beck like a dropped thread, and one white farm. “Kettledale Head,” said Osric. “That’s your piano.” Then it shut."],["Downhill Is Not Easier","I had supposed, all the way up the High Pass, that the way down would be a rest. I mention it as a warning. The Kettledale side falls away in a slope of loose slate, and the path goes down it in zigzags, forty-one of them. I counted. Counting was the only part of me still working.\n\nGoing up, it is the lungs that object. Going down it is the knees, then the toes, which arrive at the front of each boot a moment before the rest of you. My tuning case swung round and knocked me behind the legs at every turn, like a child wanting attention.\n\nOsric Mattock, the postman, went down ahead with his hands behind his back, as a man goes down his own stairs. At the bottom he waited. “Now,” he said kindly, “it’s only walking.” It was three miles of only walking."],["Five Stepping Stones","There are five stepping stones over Kettle Beck, a mile above the farm where I was to tune a piano. By then I had crossed the High Pass in rain, bog and cloud, and I looked at those stones with contempt. Osric Mattock, the postman, went over first. He said, without turning round, “Mind the fourth.”\n\nI minded the third very carefully. The fourth turned under me like a loaf in a tin, and I sat down in the beck.\n\nIt was up to my waist and astonishingly cold, and the odd thing is that I laughed, because there was nothing left to get wet. I found I was holding my tuning case above my head in both hands, quite dry. Osric took it and looked it over with approval. “That’s the trade showing,” he said. Then he gave me his arm, for the second time that day."],["Kettledale Head","We came down to Kettledale Head at a quarter to five, the fell postman and I, nine miles and one beck after leaving Gimmer Gate. Mrs Hallam met us in the yard, took Osric Mattock’s letters with one hand and me with the other, and said, “You poor wet thing, you came over the top.”\n\nWithin ten minutes I was in the kitchen in a flannel shirt and a pair of Mr Hallam’s trousers, rolled up five times at the ankle and held with string. My own clothes steamed on the rack over the range. There was mutton broth with barley, and I ate as a tuner should not in front of a client.\n\nThe piano stood in the parlour. I meant to try one note, just the A. I woke at the table an hour later with a blanket round me, and Osric gone on his round."],["The Piano at Last","On Wednesday morning at Kettledale Head I came downstairs sideways, holding the rail. Crossing the High Pass had found muscles I had not been told about. But a tuner works sitting down, and Mrs Hallam’s piano had waited eleven years.\n\nIt was a full half-tone flat, every string, all in fair agreement, like a valley that has kept its own time because nobody came over to correct it. I had it up to pitch by four. Bess Hallam, who is to be married on Saturday, played a polka, and her mother stood in the doorway with flour on her hands and forgot the baking.\n\nOsric Mattock, the fell postman, crosses again tomorrow and I am going with him. Mr Hallam has offered the cart round by the Lowlands. I thanked him. I find I should like to see Hopeful Knott from above, and to mind the fourth stone."]],[["Rules of the Hut","This book belongs to Bield Hut on Scrat Fell and stops here. It is kept in the tin on the shelf, out of the way of mice.\n\nApril 2nd. I am Abel Wren, shepherd to Mr Tolley of Gimmer Gate. I am up here most days at lambing and not many after. Others use the hut and are welcome. I have never known who. So write your name, the weather, and what you burned. Leave kinling as you found it. Latch the door or the ewes get in. There is one spoon. It stays. Weather today: cold, east wind, bright. Lambs so far: fourteen. Burned: four peats.\n\nApril 6th, Sunday. Miss P. Quill, Emberwick, walking. Fine, with larks. Burned nothing; ate a pie. I latched the door. Mr Wren, it is spelled kindling, with a d. I taught school for thirty years and cannot help it. A good hut."],["Kindling, With a D","The book of Bield Hut, Scrat Fell. April 9th. Sleet. Lambs: thirty-one. Burned six peats and the kindling, with a d. Fetched more. A. Wren, shepherd.\n\nApril 13th, Sunday. Showers, then a rainbow with both ends in the valley. I found the kindling box full and beside it a slate with KINDLING chalked on it, correctly. I am answered. I have left a quarter of tea in the tin, as a peace offering. P. Quill, walking from Emberwick.\n\nApril 15th. Dry. Lambs: fifty-two, and twins on the scree, which is a daft place. Tea found. I make it stronger than you would, I expect. A. Wren. April 16. Ned Tolley, age 9. I came up with Abel. I carried a lamb all the way from the gill and it went to sleep. It is not ritten in the rules but I ate my dinner with the spoon."],["A Hole Above the Stove","Bield Hut book, Scrat Fell. May 4th, Sunday. Heavy rain from the west. There is a hole in the roof directly above the stove, which seems to me the one place a hut should not have one. I have set the bucket under it. Burned three peats. Mr Wren, the bucket will want emptying by Tuesday. P. Quill.\n\nMay 6th. Bucket emptied. I know about the hole. It has been there since the gale in March and so have two hundred and ten lambs, and the lambs come first. Lambing done Friday. Slates Saturday. A. Wren, shepherd.\n\nMay 8th. Josiah Hake, pedlar, crossing to Kettledale with ribbons and needles. Slept here dry by sitting in the corner away from the bucket. Burned two peats. Left a card of darning needles for the house, in thanks. The spoon is a good spoon."],["Four New Slates","May 11th, Sunday. Bright and cold. Bield Hut has a whole roof: four new slates, greyer than the old ones, and the bucket hung back on its nail. I sat under them for the pleasure of it. Mr Wren, thank you, on behalf of the public. I have brought up a second cup, since the book shows the hut now has callers. P. Quill, Emberwick.\n\nMay 14th. Fine. Sheep to the high ground. Cup noted. It is a better cup than the hut one, so I have been using it. There is a ring ouzel nesting in the gill forty yards above the door, left side, under the rowan. Go quiet and you will see her. A. Wren, shepherd.\n\nMay 18th, Sunday. I went quiet. I saw her. Four eggs, Mr Wren, blue-green and freckled. I taught thirty years of children about birds out of a book. P. Quill."],["Clipping Week","July 7th. Hot. Clipping at the fold below Bield Hut, six men and Ned. Two hundred and forty ewes shorn by Thursday if it holds dry. Burned nothing; nobody wants a fire. The hut smells of wool grease and will for a month. A. Wren, shepherd. July 8. Ned Tolley, 9 and three quarters. I rolled forty fleeces. My hands are soft now from the grease, like a lady’s, Abel says. I rote this at dinner time.\n\nJuly 13th, Sunday. Warm, hazy. The hut does smell of sheep. I find I do not mind. Ned, it is wrote, with a w, but forty fleeces is a great many and I am impressed. I must report that the spoon is not on its nail. P. Quill, walking from Emberwick.\n\nJuly 15th. Spoon gone. Thirty years it has hung there. I am not accusing the public. A. Wren."],["The Matter of the Spoon","Bield Hut, Scrat Fell: the book in the tin. August 3rd, Sunday. Thundery. No spoon. I stirred with a pencil. P. Quill.\n\nAugust 5th. Two of us from the Grey Coast, Hal and Winnie Sark, walking the tops for a week. Came in out of a downpour that had us wet to the vest. Burned four peats, dried our stockings, and replaced the peats from the stack. We read the whole book while we waited. We did not take the spoon. We feel we ought to say so.\n\nAugust 12th. Josiah Hake, pedlar, coming back from Kettledale. Read of the spoon. Have left a new one, tin, from my pack, no charge. It is not the same, I know. August 14th. New spoon hung up. Obliged to Mr Hake. It is not the same. A. Wren, shepherd."],["Found in a Pocket","September 6. Ned Tolley, nearly 10. I have to rite something. Wite. Write. My mother turned out my clipping jacket for the wash and the hut spoon was in the pocket. I did not steal it. I ate my dinner at the fold in July and put it there to bring back and forgot for eight weeks. I walked up by myself to hang it on the nail. Burned nothing. Weather windy.\n\nSeptember 7th, Sunday. A clear, cold morning; first frost on the rushes. Two spoons on the nail. Ned, you spelled write three ways and the third is right, and you walked two miles uphill to put a thing back. Full marks. P. Quill, in the Bield Hut book.\n\nSeptember 9th. Spoon home. Nobody was accused. The tin one can stay for company. Ned can carry the salt up Thursday, since he knows the way. A. Wren, shepherd."],["The Autumn Gather","October 11th. Bield Hut book, Scrat Fell. Three days gathering the fell for the autumn sales, in mist you could lean on. Gyp found the last nine ewes on Winnock Edge by nose alone. I found the hut the same way, near enough. Slept here two nights. Burned twelve peats and am not sorry. All down now. A. Wren, shepherd.\n\nOctober 12th, Sunday. Still and grey, the bracken gone the colour of a fox. The fell is empty of sheep and looks larger without them. Six months of Sundays, Mr Wren, and I know your handwriting better than my sister’s. I have left a fruit loaf in the tin. It is for you and not for the public. P. Quill.\n\nOctober 14th. Loaf found. Gyp had none of it. I write slower than I talk, Miss Quill, but I am glad of the book. A. W."],["The Stove Laid Ready","November 9th, Sunday. Rain all the way up from Emberwick and sleet for the last half mile. I am sixty-four. I reached Bield Hut wet to the knees, my hat brim running like a gutter, and found the stove laid ready: paper, kindling, three peats, and a match on top. Whoever does this, and I believe I know, has my thanks. Burned the three and laid three more. P. Quill.\n\nNovember 12th. Hard frost. Up to look at the tups. I lay the stove on a Saturday now. It is no trouble. Days are short, Miss Quill; be off the fell by three, and take the cart track, not the gill. A. Wren, shepherd.\n\nNovember 16th, Sunday. Bitter. Left by half past two, by the cart track, as instructed. I have not been told what to do in a long while. It was rather pleasant. P. Quill."],["Snow to the Sill","January 18th. This is the book of Bield Hut on Scrat Fell. I am Abel Wren, shepherd, and I am the only one in it this month. Snow to the window sill. Came up by the sledge track with hay for forty ewes sheltering in the hollow. Dug out the door. Burned eight peats. I read the book through from April while my stockings dried. It took one pipe. I had not thought a hut could have so much said in it.\n\nJanuary 25th. More snow, soft. Fed the ewes. No Sunday walker again, which is right and sensible. The hut is quiet without the handwriting. I have put her cup on the high shelf out of the frost. A. W.\n\nFebruary 2nd. Ned Tolley, 10. I came up on the sledge. Abel let me write the weather. It is drippy."],["Four Leaves Left","March 8th, Sunday. Mild, a wind that smelled of earth. I am back at Bield Hut after ten Sundays kept in Emberwick by the snow, and walked up slower than in autumn. My cup was on the high shelf, wrapped in a sock. I read January. Mr Wren, I missed the hut too. P. Quill.\n\nMarch 11th. Bright. Ewes heavy; lambing in three weeks. I have counted the leaves left in this book and there are four. It was a sixpenny one. I never thought to fill it. A. Wren, shepherd.\n\nMarch 15th, Sunday. Showers. The last page ought not to be wasted on weather. In eleven months we have never once been under this roof on the same day. I can walk on a weekday. Name one. P. Quill. March 17th. Second of April. It is a year to the day. Ned will want to come. A. W."],["The Second of April","April 2nd. The last page of the book of Bield Hut, Scrat Fell, begun a year ago today. All three of us are writing it at the one table, which is a squash. Weather: cold, bright, east wind, the same as the first page. Lambs so far: eleven. Burned five peats and boiled the kettle twice. Miss Quill is a deal taller than her handwriting. She brought a new book, a shilling one, with a red spine. It is in the tin. A. Wren, shepherd.\n\nI was shown twin lambs an hour old, and the gill, where the ouzel is back and building. Mr Wren is exactly like his handwriting. We used both cups. P. Quill, Emberwick.\n\nNed Tolley, 10. I stirred everybodys tea with the old spoon. I spelled kindling for Miss Quill out loud. This book is full now. Write in the next one."]],[["The Least of the Twelve","No. 1. Butter Howe. 2,004 feet. Cairn: knee high, untidy. Weather: fair.\n\nThere is a list pinned up in the Emberwick Library of the twelve tops of the Northern Fells that stand over two thousand feet, lowest first. I am Cuthbert Pym, draper, retired this spring after forty years of measuring other people’s curtains, and I mean to stand on all twelve before the snow. I have a notebook, a tape, a yellow muffler my sister knitted, and twelve white pebbles from the Grey Coast, one for each cairn.\n\nButter Howe is the least of them, by four feet. It took me three hours and I lay down at the top. I measured the cairn, added pebble the first, and saw a slate tucked in on the lee side. Scratched with a nail: H.L., May 9th. Three days ago. I had rather thought I was the only one."],["Stoat Nab in Drizzle","No. 2. Stoat Nab. 2,060 feet. Cairn: waist high, well built, a credit to somebody. Weather: drizzle, of the fine sort that gets through tweed.\n\nSecond of the twelve tops on the library list, which I, C. Pym, late draper of Emberwick, am climbing in order, lowest first. The way up is by a sheep trod through bracken and then a grass slope the angle of a roof. I stopped eleven times. I have decided to record the stops honestly, since nobody will read this but me.\n\nOn top, nothing to see but the inside of a cloud. Cairn measured: thirty-eight inches. White pebble placed. And there on the sheltered side was a slate again, scratched H.L., May 17th. Five days ahead of me. The same hand, the same nail. Somebody else has read that list. I ate my bread and cheese with my back to it."],["Shirtsleeves on Spindle Rigg","No. 3. Spindle Rigg. 2,118 feet. Cairn: tall and thin, like its name; forty-six inches. Weather: hot, without a breath.\n\nI am working through the twelve two-thousand-foot tops of the Northern Fells in the order of the library list, and this was the first done in shirtsleeves. Spindle Rigg is a long narrow ridge that you walk like a plank. The yellow muffler went in the knapsack. The first blister arrived at sixteen hundred feet, right heel, and is dressed with best lint from my own shop.\n\nSlate on the lee side of the cairn: H.L., May 28th. I sat and considered H.L. A tall man, I think, from the time he gains on me. Young. Long in the leg. A Harold, or a Hubert. Probably he does not stop eleven times. Stops today: nine. Pebble placed. A lark sang the whole while, which was civil of it."],["Hat Weather on Hagworm Pike","No. 4. Hagworm Pike. 2,190 feet. Cairn: broad and squat, thirty inches; built by someone who understood wind. Weather: wind.\n\nFourth of my twelve tops, and the first with any rock to it. The name is the old word for an adder. I saw none. They had more sense than to be out. From the shoulder upward the wind came across so hard that I walked at a slant, like a man carrying a wardrobe, and at the top it took my hat clean off.\n\nI, Cuthbert Pym, sixty-one, went after it on hands and knees. It had not gone to Kettledale, as I feared. It had lodged against the far side of the cairn, six inches from a slate scratched H.L., June 6th. I retrieved the one and glared at the other. Pebble placed, and wedged. Stops: I did not count. Standing still was harder than walking."],["Lamb’s Lug, the Wet Way","No. 5. Lamb’s Lug. 2,240 feet. Cairn: a poor heap, twenty inches, half sunk in moss. Weather: bright, after a week of rain, which is the worst of both.\n\nA lug is an ear, and from Emberwick this fell looks like one, folded over. Close to, it is a sponge. I am Cuthbert Pym, retired draper, and this is the fifth of the twelve tops I have set myself. For two miles the ground quaked. I went in over one boot, then the other, and near the top I sat down suddenly in a green place that was not grass. I walked the rest wet from the waist, squelching in two different notes.\n\nSlate at the cairn: H.L., June 15th. Dry-shod, no doubt. I added six stones out of charity and set my white pebble on top. Trousers dried on me by Gimmer Gate. Stops: fourteen, a record."],["Lost on Scrat Fell","No. 6. Scrat Fell. 2,305 feet. Cairn: large, round, shoulder high; fifty-two inches when at last measured. Weather: cloud down to the top wall.\n\nSixth of the twelve tops on the library list, and so the half of my summer’s task. The top of Scrat Fell is flat and as wide as Emberwick market square. In cloud, that is not a help. I, Cuthbert Pym, wandered on it for forty minutes, looking for a cairn the size of a haystack. I passed the same puddle three times and began to greet it.\n\nI found the cairn by walking into it. I am not ashamed to say I patted it. Then round to the lee side, and there was the slate, H.L., June 24th, quite at home. For once I was almost pleased to see the fellow’s initials. Six pebbles placed. Six in the knapsack. Down by compass, slowly."],["Two in One Day","No. 7. Fiddler’s Knott. 2,350 feet. Cairn: neat, square-shouldered, forty inches. Weather: clear and cool, the best day of July.\n\nI have been climbing the twelve high tops of the Northern Fells, lowest first, and at every cairn I find a slate scratched H.L., with a date some days before mine. Today’s said July 8th. Mine was the 12th. I ate a pork pie and looked across at the next on the list, Little Thunnock, a mile off along the ridge, with one dip between.\n\nNot yet noon. A draper does not get to be sixty-one without knowing how to make up a short measure. If I did two tops in a day, I, Cuthbert Pym, should gain on a man who plainly does one. Pebble placed. Stops: seven. I wound my muffler twice round and set off into the dip, which was deeper than it looked."],["Little Thunnock, Late","No. 8. Little Thunnock. 2,410 feet. Cairn: about forty-four inches, measured sitting down. Weather: fine, then evening.\n\nEighth of the twelve tops, and my second in one day, which I do not recommend. The dip between Fiddler’s Knott and this one is five hundred feet down and, I can now state, a good eight hundred up again. I reached the cairn at ten past five with legs like two rolls of wet flannel. Stops: nineteen, the last one four yards from the top.\n\nBut the slate. H.L., July 11th. Yesterday! I, Cuthbert Pym, was four days behind at breakfast and one day behind at tea. I set down pebble the eighth, leaned back on the cairn to enjoy the moment, and woke twenty minutes later with a sheep looking at me. Home by lantern at ten. My sister had kept the stew hot and her remarks short."],["Winnock Edge, Twice","No. 9. Winnock Edge. 2,480 feet. Cairn: on the very lip, forty-one inches. Weather: first, rain like poured gravel; second, grey and still.\n\nI, Cuthbert Pym, retired draper, have nine of the twelve tops, but this one I had to ask twice. The first time I thought I was a day behind H.L., whose slates I find at every cairn, and meant to catch him. At eighteen hundred feet the rain came on so hard I could not see my boots. I stood a minute, then turned round. It was the most sensible thing I have done since retiring. I was in a temper all the way down.\n\nA week later I went back. The slate said July 20th: ten days ahead of me. I sat looking down on Ouzel Tarn and found I did not much mind. A list is not a race. Pebble placed. Stops: eight."],["A Figure on Great Thunnock","No. 10. Tinker’s Chair. 2,560 feet. Cairn: built inside the chair itself, a hollow of rock with two arms; thirty-six inches. Weather: perfect. I use the word once in this book.\n\nTenth of the twelve tops on the library list. The last day of August, heather in bloom the whole way up and the Grey Coast shining like a pin along the edge of everything. I, Cuthbert Pym, sat in the Tinker’s Chair for an hour and ate two apples. Slate present: H.L., August 19th.\n\nAcross the gap stands Great Thunnock, next on the list. Beside its cairn there was a small dark figure. It did not move for some time. Then something flashed, twice, as glass does. On an impulse I cannot defend, I stood and waved my yellow muffler over my head like a signalman. The figure waved back. Pebble placed. Stops: I forgot to count."],["A Message on a Slate","No. 11. Great Thunnock. 2,690 feet. Cairn: a massive beehive, sixty inches; the finest of the lot. Weather: cold wind, high cloud, the first smell of autumn.\n\nEleventh of the twelve high tops, and a hard pull of four hours from Gimmer Gate. I, Cuthbert Pym, am sixty-one and felt every one of the two thousand six hundred and ninety feet. I went round to the lee side of the cairn, as I have all summer, for the slate marked H.L.\n\nIt was a larger slate this time, scratched small: H.L., August 31st. To the gentleman in the yellow muffler who has been one fell behind me since May. I have watched you through my glass. You stop a great deal but you always get there. Ember Head, last Saturday in September, noon. I shall bring cake. I read it three times. Pebble placed, not quite straight."],["Ember Head","No. 12. Ember Head. 2,843 feet. Cairn: seven feet; my tape would not reach the top. Weather: clear, frost in the shadows.\n\nThe last and highest of the twelve tops. A spring comes out of the moss below the summit, no wider than a ribbon, and that is the river Ember. I, Cuthbert Pym, stepped over it and arrived at five minutes to noon.\n\nH.L., whose slates I have found at eleven cairns, was sitting against this one with a spyglass and a cake tin. She is Miss Hetty Lumb, seventy-two, who kept the bakehouse in Oven Lane, and comes up to my shoulder. I had imagined a tall young man. We ate seed cake. She scratched C.P. under H.L. on the last slate, and I set down my twelfth pebble. Stops: twenty-two. She says the library has another list. Nineteen tarns. She has not started it yet."]],[["Where the Cart Stops","The carrier would go no farther than the last gate. He set Nell Garth down on the second of November with her box, pointed up the track, and turned for Emberwick before the light went.\n\nNell was eleven. Her mother had gone to the Lowlands to nurse a sister through the winter, and Nell had been sent to a great-aunt she had met twice. Above her the track climbed to a grey farm with one lit window. Below the farm lay a tarn, dark as a slate. Above that was only fell, and a good deal of that.\n\nGreat-Aunt Dinah came down to meet her, a small square woman in a sacking apron. She looked at Nell’s face, and then, for longer, at her boots. “Those won’t do,” she said. “The ouzel left three weeks back, so it’s winter. Come in. There’s broth.” She carried the box herself."],["The Byre at Six","At High Ouzel, the farm above the tarn, the day began in the dark. Nell Garth had been there one night when her great-aunt Dinah shook her foot through the blanket and said, “Six.” That was the whole of the conversation until the byre.\n\nThe byre was warm, which surprised her. It held a brown cow named Duchess, eleven hens, and an old dog called Captain, who had retired from sheep and now supervised. Nell carried two pails from the trough and slopped most of one down her stockings. She forked hay. She held the lantern while Dinah milked, and Duchess breathed on her hair.\n\nBy seven her arms shook when she lifted her porridge spoon. Dinah watched without comment and added more cream. Nell had found a stub of chalk on the byre sill and made one small mark inside the door. One day of winter done."],["Aunt Dinah’s Clogs","Nell Garth’s town boots lasted four days of her winter at High Ouzel. On the fifth, the left sole came away at the toe and flapped like a tongue across the yard. Her great-aunt Dinah heard it coming.\n\nShe said nothing. She went up to the loft and came down with a pair of clogs, black leather on alder soles, shod with iron like a pony. They had been hers at eleven, she said, and her mother’s before. With two pairs of stockings they fitted. Nell stood up in them and was an inch taller and a good deal louder. On the frozen ruts of the track she rang like a smithy.\n\nDinah tied the town boots together by their laces and hung them on a nail by the door. “For March,” she said. They hung there all winter above the tarn, looking smaller every time Nell passed."],["The Tarn Skins Over","The first ice came to Ouzel Tarn at the end of November. Nell Garth, eleven, who was wintering at the farm above it with her great-aunt Dinah, saw it from the yard: the water had stopped moving. A skin lay over it, clear at the edges and grey in the middle.\n\nShe was down the field before she had thought. Dinah’s voice came after her, not loud. “Not a foot on it till I say. I’ll say.” So Nell stood on the shore and threw a pebble instead. It did not splash. It skipped over the ice, and at every bounce the whole tarn chirped like a bird.\n\nShe threw until her fingers would not close. Dinah came down at last, picked up a flat stone, and sent it clear to the far side, ringing all the way. “Sixty years,” she said. “It never stops being worth doing.”"],["Counting to Sixty","Before the snow, the ewes come down. Great-Aunt Dinah kept sixty small grey fell sheep at High Ouzel, and in the first week of December she, Nell Garth, who was eleven, and the old dog Captain brought them down into the walled field above the tarn.\n\nIt took all day. The wind had ice in it. Nell’s job was to stand in gaps and be a gate, which meant holding out her arms while sheep ran at her and thought better of it. By dusk she could not feel her ears, and her legs wobbled from walking on tussocks since breakfast.\n\nDinah counted them through the gate in fives, moving a pebble from one pocket to the other for every score. Three pebbles. Nell counted too, under her breath, and made it fifty-nine. Dinah pointed with her chin. The sixtieth was behind them, eating hay off the sledge."],["The Shortest Day","On the shortest day the sun cleared the fell above High Ouzel at half past nine and was gone behind it by three. Nell Garth, eleven, had been at her great-aunt Dinah’s farm seven weeks. The chalk marks she kept on the byre door, one for each day of winter, came to forty-seven. She had forgotten two.\n\nDinah did not hold with fuss, but she did hold with the shortest day. She baked a mutton pie and a dark spiced loaf with a crust like a boot. She set a lantern in the window facing the tarn, to show the sun the way back. There was a letter from Nell’s mother in the Lowlands, read twice.\n\nAfter supper they went out. The lantern lay on the black ice in one long yellow stroke. “It turns tonight,” said Dinah. “You’ll not see it for a month. But it turns.”"],["Snowed In","The snow came to High Ouzel in mid January and went on for three days without drawing breath. When it stopped, the yard wall was a soft white rise, the tarn had vanished, and the kitchen window was half blue.\n\nNell Garth, eleven, and her great-aunt Dinah dug a trench to the byre, shoulder deep to Nell, and for a week that trench was the world: house, cow, hens, house. Nobody came up from Emberwick. Nobody could. Dinah seemed to find this restful.\n\nIn the evenings she taught Nell to turn a stocking heel, which took four tries, and they played draughts on a board scratched into the table, with white buttons and black. Dinah won eleven games running. She did not let a child win, she said; it gave them wrong ideas. Nell won the twelfth, properly. Dinah made her hot milk with treacle and said nothing."],["The Ewe Under the Drift","When the January snow settled, Great-Aunt Dinah counted her ewes in the walled field above the tarn and made it fifty-nine, not sixty. Then she whistled for old Captain, two winters retired, and he got up off the hearth as if he had only been waiting.\n\nHe went along the top wall nose down and stopped at a drift as high as Nell Garth’s head. There was a round hole in it, yellow at the rim, with warm air coming out. Dinah dug. Nell, who was eleven and wanted to help, stepped forward and went in to her armpits.\n\nSo it was Nell who met the ewe first, face to face in a little blue cave, chewing, and not at all grateful. The ewe walked out over her and went to the hay. Nell was hauled out by the collar, soaked to the skin, and laughing too much to stand."],["The Tarn Talks","In February the frost came down hard on High Ouzel and stayed nine nights. On the tenth morning Great-Aunt Dinah took the axe to the tarn’s edge, chopped a hole, measured the ice against her hand, and said to Nell Garth, “Now I’ll say.”\n\nNell had waited since November. She went out in her iron-shod clogs, close along the shore where Dinah showed her, and found she could slide twenty yards at a push. She slid until the day went pink. Dinah watched from the bank with folded arms, then walked out and slid past her, upright as a post.\n\nThat night Nell woke. Below, the tarn was talking: a long low boom, then a twang like a fiddle string, running from shore to shore. She was not frightened. Dinah had told her. It was only the ice making room for itself, and she lay and listened."],["The Lid Comes Off","The thaw at High Ouzel began on the first of March with a drip from the byre roof and did not stop for a fortnight. The trench Nell Garth had helped dig in January became a ditch, then a stream. The yard was mud to the ankle, and it came indoors on four paws and two pairs of clogs, however Great-Aunt Dinah stood guard with the broom.\n\nNell was eleven and had liked the snow. She did not like this. Her stockings were never dry. The hens sulked, and the tarn below the farm had gone the grey of old porridge, with water on the ice.\n\nThen a west wind blew all one night, and by morning the ice was gone, every plate of it, and the tarn was moving again, blue and wrinkled. Dinah stood at the door with her tea. “There,” she said. “That’s the lid off.”"],["The First Lamb","The first lamb at High Ouzel was born on the nineteenth of March, at a quarter to five, in the field by the tarn. Nell Garth, eleven, was there because her great-aunt Dinah had shaken her foot through the blanket and said, “Lantern.”\n\nThere was little to do but hold the light steady. Nell used both hands. The ewe managed the rest, and then there was a wet yellowish thing on the grass that sneezed, shook its ears, and in ten minutes stood on four legs that did not agree with each other.\n\nDinah said the first lamb was Nell’s to name. Nell thought of the byre door and her chalk marks, one for each day of winter. She had made none for three weeks. She had forgotten to count. “Chalk,” she said. Dinah looked at the lamb, which was dark as a wet slate, and said that would do."],["The Ouzel Comes Back","On the last Thursday of March a bird sang from the rowan by Ouzel Tarn: three clear notes, a pause, and three again. Nell Garth ran down in her clogs. It was black as a blackbird, with a white bib like a tucked-in napkin. Her great-aunt Dinah came to the wall and listened, eyes shut. “That’s him,” she said. “That’s winter done.”\n\nThe carrier’s cart came to the last gate on Saturday. Nell’s mother had written that the sister in the Lowlands was well again. Dinah took the town boots down from their nail. Nell could not get her heel in.\n\nSo she went home to Emberwick in the clogs, ringing down the track. Dinah carried the box to the gate. “Lambs want marking in June,” she said, looking at the fell. “If you’ve nothing on.” She never did wash Nell’s chalk marks off the byre door."]],[["Keys and One Trunk","I came to the Lamplit Goose on the third of October with one trunk, a headache, and my late aunt Berenice’s keys. The inn stands where the causeway leaves the marsh, two miles below Emberwick: the last roof before the reeds, or the first after them. I am Nell Garroway. Until last month I kept the books for a chandler. I have never drawn a pint.\n\nMy aunt left a letter on the bar. It gave the price of a bed, the name of the cook, and a warning about the goose. The last line was underlined. Light the lamp over the door at dusk, every dusk. Never mind the oil.\n\nI lit it that first evening standing on a chair. It took four matches and a burnt thumb. But it lit. This is the house book, and I mean to keep it honestly."],["The Goose Takes Charge","November, and my second month as landlady of the Lamplit Goose, the marsh-end inn my aunt Berenice left me. I have now met the whole household. Mrs. Brewin cooks, and tells her saucepans more than she tells me. Old Corbin Slate keeps the stable and the fires. The yard belongs to a grey goose named Mrs. Alderman, who inspects every arrival and has so far approved of one tinker and nobody else.\n\nOur first full table was six eel-catchers, wet to the knee. Mrs. Brewin gave them barley broth with leeks, then a bacon pudding, and I carried the plates. I can now manage three. The fourth went into a lap.\n\nThe eel-catcher laughed and ate it from there. At dusk I lit the lamp over the door with two matches. Corbin said my aunt used one, but he said it kindly."],["How the House Was Named","December at the Lamplit Goose. Snow lay on the causeway and nobody came, so Corbin Slate, our ostler, told me how the inn got its name. I am the new landlady, and it seemed a thing I ought to know.\n\nThe first landlord, he says, was a marshman called Jeb Tolliver, who went out after stray sheep and lost himself in a fog a yard thick. He stood still, as marshmen are taught. Then he heard his own goose, shouting from his own doorstep, and he walked home along the noise. Next morning he hung a lantern over the door, so that nobody after him would need a goose.\n\nIt is a good story. I went to trim the lamp and found the oil can in the porch brim full. I have not filled it once. I supposed Mrs. Brewin had, and thought no more of it."],["Fog on the Causeway","January. I have kept the Lamplit Goose, on the marsh causeway, for three months, and last night the house earned its sign. A fog came up off the marsh at four. By six I could not see the pump from the kitchen door. I lit the lamp over the porch and turned the wick up high.\n\nAt nine the Grey Coast coach walked in out of nothing, the driver leading his horses by hand. He had steered by our light for the last mile. Seven passengers: a piano tuner, a draper, twin sisters with a parrot, and a family of three. We have five beds. The twins took the settle and the parrot took the mantelpiece.\n\nMrs. Brewin made one pan of onion soup go round eleven people. This morning I thanked her for keeping the lamp can filled. She said she had never touched it."],["The Oil Question","February, a slow month at the Lamplit Goose, and I spent three evenings with my late aunt’s account books. I was a bookkeeper before I was a landlady. A full oil can that nobody fills is an entry without a receipt, and I cannot leave it alone.\n\nAunt Berenice bought candles, lamp wick, soap, and an alarming quantity of mustard. In twenty-two years of ledgers she did not buy one pint of lamp oil. Yet the lamp over our door has burned every night, and the can in the porch is full this minute.\n\nI asked Corbin Slate, our ostler. He scratched his chin. It had always been full, he said. He had supposed my aunt saw to it. Her letter to me says only: never mind the oil. I mind it a good deal. Mrs. Alderman, the yard goose, knows something, but she is not saying."],["A Watch Kept Badly","March. Somebody fills the oil can in the porch of the Lamplit Goose, and it is not me, the cook, or the ostler. Being landlady, I decided to find out, and on Wednesday night I sat up in the porch with two blankets and a flask of tea.\n\nMrs. Alderman, our yard goose, sat up with me, which I took for loyalty. At midnight the can was a third full. I checked at one and at two. After that I remember the goose settling against my boots, very warm.\n\nI woke at six with a stiff neck. The can was full. There was a smell of horse liniment on the handle, and in the frost by the step lay two wheel marks, already softening. The goose had not made a sound. She shouts at curates and bank clerks, but whoever came, she knew them. It was Thursday morning."],["The Painter Pays in Paint","April at the Lamplit Goose, the inn I keep at the edge of the marsh. A travelling painter, Mr. Lysander Voke, stayed nine nights and discovered on the tenth that he had no money. He offered to repaint our sign instead. I have been landlady six months and know a bad bargain. This was not one.\n\nThe old goose on the board had faded to a pudding. Mr. Voke gave her white feathers, an orange beak, and a brass lantern with a yellow flame in it. Mrs. Alderman, our real goose, stood under the ladder and seemed to think it a fair likeness.\n\nHe told me one thing more. Up the ladder early on Thursday, he had seen a cart stop at our porch and go on towards the coast. He took it for a delivery. So do I, now. But nobody here has ever been sent a bill."],["The Marshmen’s Dinner","May, and the Lamplit Goose held the Marshmen’s Dinner, as it does every spring. Forty reed-cutters and eel-catchers sat down in a room that holds thirty. I am the landlady, and it was my first.\n\nMrs. Brewin sent out pea soup, a great raised pork pie, new potatoes with mint, and a gooseberry fool. My part was the bread. I forgot it. Forty men looked politely at their soup until an old reed-cutter stood up and said that Berenice, my aunt, forgot the bread in her first year too, and the mustard in her second.\n\nThey cheered. I fetched the loaves and was made to sit at the top of the table. Afterwards, with the yard full of lanterns and singing, I went out and lit our own lamp over the door. One match. I looked round for someone to tell, and there was only the goose."],["The Night It Went Out","June. The nights are so short on the marsh that the lamp over the door of the Lamplit Goose hardly seems worth a match. I am the landlady, and I will set down plainly that on the twentieth, tired after a wedding party, I went to bed without lighting it. It was the first dark night that door has had in anyone’s memory.\n\nAt eleven there was a knocking. On the step stood Bram Tully, the Emberwick carrier, a broad, slow man of sixty who has never taken more than a mug of tea in this house. He had his hat in both hands. “Your lamp’s out, missus,” he said. “I thought you might be ill.”\n\nI lit it while he watched. He nodded and went back to his cart. I stood wondering why a carrier should look so shaken over one small flame."],["Counting Thursdays","July at the Lamplit Goose, where I am landlady and, lately, a spy. Somebody has kept the lamp can in our porch filled for years without sending a bill. For four weeks I have dipped that can every morning with a marked stick.\n\nThe figures are plain. The oil goes down a little each night. It is lowest on Wednesday. On Thursday morning it is full. Bram Tully, the carrier, passes our door before dawn on Thursdays, bound for the Grey Coast. He rubs his old mare’s knees with liniment; I have smelled it on him in the yard, and once on the handle of the can. And he alone, of all the road, came knocking in June when the lamp was dark.\n\nSo I know who. A ledger would stop there. I find I want the reason, and that is not in any column."],["What the Carrier Said","August. On Thursday I got up at four, made tea, and waited in the porch of the Lamplit Goose, the marsh inn I keep. At five a cart stopped. Bram Tully, the carrier, climbed down with a stone jar of lamp oil and found the landlady holding out a mug.\n\nHe took it and told me. Fifty-one years ago he was nine, riding the causeway with his father, when the fog came down and a wheel went off the edge. His father could not leave the horse. He sent the boy ahead, towards one yellow light. It was this lamp. Men went back with ropes. A quart of oil a week had seemed little enough.\n\nMy aunt never mentioned it, he said. But there was a bun on the porch shelf of a Thursday. He asked me not to make anything of it. I said I would not."],["A Year of Lamplight","October again. A year ago I came to the Lamplit Goose with one trunk and my aunt Berenice’s keys, and could not draw a pint. I can now carry four plates. Mrs. Brewin has begun telling me things before she tells her saucepans, and Mrs. Alderman, the goose, let a commercial traveller cross the yard unchallenged. Corbin says that is a sign of settled times.\n\nToday I turned to the front of the house book, where the landlords have signed since Jeb Tolliver made his mark. Under Berenice Garroway I wrote Nell Garroway, and blotted it.\n\nThe first fog came in at five. I lit the lamp with one match. It is Wednesday, so before bed I put a currant bun on the porch shelf, under a basin, out of the goose’s reach. The can will be full in the morning. I shall not be minding the oil."]],[["In by Ten","Rule One. Lodgers shall be in by ten o’clock, when the door will be locked.\n\nMr. Aldous Pym painted this on a board the week he inherited the Three Kettles, on Cooper’s Row in Emberwick. His uncle Barnaby had kept the house for forty years without a single rule. There were four lodgers, who had come with the building like the damp: a piano tuner, a retired barge captain, a widow, and a young clerk in the attic. Aldous had been a clerk himself, at a ribbon warehouse, and he believed in notices.\n\nAt ten he locked the front door. At five past he remembered that he was standing on the outside of it, having stepped into the street to see whether the board hung straight. It did. He was admitted through the scullery window by Miss Thessaly Brandt, the piano tuner, who held the sash and said nothing."],["The Midnight Cheese","Rule Two. No cooking shall be done in the rooms.\n\nThe Three Kettles had a kitchen, and its landlord, Mr. Aldous Pym, felt that food should stay in it. The rule was aimed at the second floor back, where Mrs. Odalys Crumb, a widow of cheerful habits, toasted cheese at midnight. On the first night the smell came down the stairs. Mr. Pym went up in his dressing gown with a candle and a prepared remark.\n\nMrs. Crumb opened the door holding a long fork. She explained her method while he waited to speak: thick bread, hard cheese grated fine, a little mustard, a splash of ale, and patience. He came down forty minutes later, having eaten two slices and delivered none of the remark. Next morning he added three words to the board in smaller paint: after eleven o’clock. Even he was not sure what this meant."],["The Hen Signs In","Rule Three. No livestock shall be kept above the ground floor.\n\nMr. Aldous Pym, landlord of the Three Kettles in Emberwick, wrote this after meeting a speckled hen on the second landing. She was called Marigold. She belonged to Mrs. Crumb, the widow in the back room, and had lived upstairs for six years, coming down each morning to lay one egg in the umbrella stand. Mr. Pym pointed to the board.\n\nThe four lodgers gathered in the hall. Captain Fenn, the retired bargeman, observed that the rule said kept. Nobody kept Marigold. She came and went. Miss Brandt, the piano tuner, fetched the visitors’ book from the bar. The entry may still be seen, in Mrs. Crumb’s round hand: Marigold, of this town, one night, renewed daily. Payment, one egg. Mr. Pym had his boiled, and found he could not complain of a guest who settled so promptly."],["Between Four and Six","Rule Four. Musical practice shall be confined to the hours between four and six.\n\nThere was a bassoon in the attic of the Three Kettles. It belonged to Mr. Tobias Wren, a clerk at the corn exchange, who had been learning one tune for eleven months. The landlord, Mr. Aldous Pym, had heard that tune at breakfast, at midnight, and once during a thunderstorm. The lodgers obeyed his rule to the minute.\n\nAt four the bassoon began. At four also Miss Brandt, the piano tuner, sat down to the parlour upright with her tuning fork; and Captain Fenn, shaving for the evening, sang a barge song in a key of his own. By half past a small crowd had gathered on Cooper’s Row, and a boy was selling them apples. Mr. Pym stood with his fingers in his ears and reflected that the noise had not been reduced, only collected."],["Beans at the Window","Rule Five. Nothing shall be grown from the windows of this house.\n\nCaptain Orlo Fenn had worked barges on the river Ember for forty years and retired to the first floor back of the Three Kettles, where he missed having something to tend. So he grew runner beans. They started in a tub in the yard, went up the drainpipe, and by August were looking in at every window. The landlord, Mr. Aldous Pym, woke to find a tendril wound twice round his curtain rod, and painted Rule Five before breakfast.\n\nThe Captain read it and pointed out that the beans grew from the yard. They arrived at the windows. That evening a dish came to Mr. Pym’s table: beans sliced thin, boiled, with butter and pepper. He ate them all. The scarlet flowers stayed at his window until the frost, and he was seen, once, watering the tub."],["Rent in Coin","Rule Six. Rent is due on the first of the month, in coin.\n\nMr. Aldous Pym had kept the Three Kettles for half a year when he sat down with his late uncle’s rent book and found no money in it at all. Uncle Barnaby had taken payment in kind. Miss Brandt tuned the pianos. Captain Fenn mended the roof, the pump, and the back gate. Mrs. Crumb supplied eggs and the Sunday pudding. Young Mr. Wren, in the attic, did the accounts.\n\nAldous had been a clerk. He priced it all fairly, in two columns, and added it twice. By his reckoning the house owed its lodgers eleven shillings a month. He looked at the board for some time. Then he took a small brush and wrote beneath Rule Six, or its honest equivalent, and went up to ask Mr. Wren, rather humbly, whether the accounts might continue."],["The Parlour Moves Back","Rule Seven. Lodgers shall not rearrange the furniture of the public rooms.\n\nIn February Mr. Aldous Pym, landlord of the Three Kettles, spent a week in bed with a cold. His four lodgers ran the house. Mrs. Crumb cooked, Captain Fenn kept the bar, and Miss Brandt carried up broth and the news. When he came down, the parlour had changed. The settle was by the fire. The round table stood in the window. The piano had turned its back to the draught.\n\nIt was, Miss Brandt mentioned, how his uncle Barnaby had it, and Aldous had to admit that the room looked, for once, as if people sat in it. He wrote the rule anyway, on principle, and moved nothing. That morning a letter lay on the hall table. A Mr. Hobart Sleek, grain merchant, wished to buy the building for a warehouse, and named a handsome price."],["A Letter Left Lying","Rule Eight. Lodgers shall not read the landlord’s correspondence.\n\nThe letter lay on the hall table of the Three Kettles for a morning before Mr. Aldous Pym, the landlord, put it in his pocket. It was from Mr. Hobart Sleek, a grain merchant, offering to buy the house for a warehouse. Aldous had come to innkeeping from a ribbon warehouse, with no great love for it, and the price was handsome. He painted Rule Eight that afternoon, which was rather late.\n\nNobody admitted to reading anything. But that week Mrs. Crumb’s Sunday pudding came out flat. Captain Fenn left a slate loose on the roof. Mr. Wren’s bassoon was silent, and Miss Brandt, tuning the parlour piano, played only the slow pieces. Even Marigold the hen laid her egg in the coal scuttle instead of the umbrella stand. Aldous found it there, and stood holding it, feeling unreasonably accused."],["The Perfect Lodgers","Rule Nine. No lodger shall address a prospective purchaser.\n\nMr. Hobart Sleek, grain merchant, came to view the Three Kettles on a Tuesday. The landlord, Mr. Aldous Pym, had dreaded what his four lodgers might say to the man. They said nothing. They kept Rule Nine, and for that one day every other rule on the board as well. No bassoon sounded. No cheese was toasted. The hen stayed in the yard, and at ten that night four doors closed quietly. The house was as silent as a cupboard.\n\nMr. Sleek was delighted. A tidy, empty sort of place, he said; no trouble to clear. He would send the papers. Aldous saw him out and came back into a hall that smelled of nothing. He had spent most of a year asking for exactly this. He sat down on the stairs and did not like it at all."],["Supper on Sundays","Rule Ten. The landlord shall take his supper with the house on Sundays.\n\nMr. Aldous Pym did not write this rule. He found it on the board of the Three Kettles one Sunday morning, in white chalk, in four different hands. He was the landlord, and halfway through selling the building to a grain merchant, and he had not expected orders from his lodgers. He went, however.\n\nThe round table in the parlour was laid for five. Mrs. Crumb had made toasted cheese and a pudding. Captain Fenn brought a jar of his runner beans, salted in autumn. There were six boiled eggs from Marigold the hen, and afterwards Miss Brandt played while Mr. Wren got through his bassoon tune without stopping. Nobody mentioned the sale. Captain Fenn remarked that a barge could be sold, but not its crew. Aldous took more pudding and went to bed very thoughtful."],["A Vote of the House","Rule Eleven. No rule shall be added to this board without a vote of the house.\n\nMr. Aldous Pym painted this one in good black paint, the morning after he wrote to Mr. Hobart Sleek. His letter was short. The price offered for the Three Kettles had been fair. But on inspection the building proved to have four fixtures and a hen which were not his to sell, and he must decline. He posted it before he could think better.\n\nThat evening he called his lodgers into the parlour: Miss Brandt, Captain Fenn, Mrs. Crumb, and young Mr. Wren. He read them the rule and put it to a vote. It passed by five to none. Marigold, under the piano, was not counted. Mrs. Crumb proposed toasted cheese, which was carried too. It was a quarter past eleven, and nobody, the landlord least of all, looked at the board."],["The Board Comes Down","Rule Twelve was never painted. A year to the day after he inherited the Three Kettles, in Emberwick, Mr. Aldous Pym took the board down and laid it on the parlour table. The house voted on his rules, one at a time.\n\nThe hours for music went first, then the rule against beans, then the one about furniture. Rent in coin was struck out to cheers. Mr. Wren painted each one out. At the end a single line was left, amended by Miss Brandt: Be in by ten, or use the scullery window quietly.\n\nThe board went back up with new words at the head. The Three Kettles. A. Pym, Landlord, and Lodgers. At ten that night Aldous stepped into the street to see whether it hung straight. It did. This time Miss Brandt, the piano tuner, was holding the front door open, and he went in by it."]],[["The Bell and Bucket","This Guide is compiled by Mr. Horatio Blench, late of the Excise, at the request of Mr. Tench the printer, for travellers who wish to know where a decent supper may be had within two days’ drive of Emberwick. Twelve houses are described. Each is rated in spoons, from one to five. Five spoons denote a supper without fault. The Compiler does not expect to award them.\n\nThe first house is the Bell and Bucket, in the market square at Emberwick. It is clean, plain, and loud on Thursdays. Supper was mutton and barley stew with carrots, and the heel of a loaf. The Compiler ate it alone at the corner table in eleven minutes, having a pony trap to hire.\n\nVerdict: the stew was hot and the bread was bread. Two spoons. The trap was got from a carrier named Latchet, whose daughter is to drive it."],["The Ploughman’s Rest","Second house: the Ploughman’s Rest, at Harrow Green in the Lowlands, nine miles south of Emberwick. The Compiler, Mr. Blench, came by hired trap. The driver is Posy Latchet, aged fourteen, who talks to the pony, Turnip, and to the Compiler about equally.\n\nThe house is long and low, with one table down its whole length. At harvest every seat is taken by reapers, and the traveller sits where he is put. Supper was boiled bacon with broad beans in a parsley sauce, then a wedge of apple tart that nobody had ordered.\n\nThe bacon was tender, the sauce green and plentiful. Against this, a reaper on the Compiler’s left sang, and one on his right asked what he was writing. Verdict: three spoons, one deducted for noise. The driver ate two helpings and called it the best supper in the world. She has been to very few."],["The Eel and Reed","Third house: the Eel and Reed, on the marsh road, built on posts above water the colour of tea. The traveller from Emberwick should arrive before dusk, as the Compiler of this Guide did not. His driver, the girl Latchet, found the door by the smell of frying.\n\nThe house offers one supper, and it is eel. That night it came as a pie: eel with parsley and onion under a pale crust, and a jug of vinegar to shake over it. There were boiled potatoes. There was no alternative.\n\nMr. Blench has disliked eels on principle for forty years. He now finds that the principle had never tasted one. The flesh was white, mild and rich. He declines to be hurried into enthusiasm by a fish. Verdict: two spoons, and a third in pencil. The driver asked how the crust was made and was given a wink."],["The Saltbox","Fourth house: the Saltbox, at Cockle Staithe on the Grey Coast, a tarred inn so close to the tide that the cellar keeps its own hours. The Compiler, Mr. Blench, reached it in a wind that removed his hat twice. His driver, Posy Latchet, recovered it both times.\n\nSupper was herrings, rolled in oatmeal and fried crisp, with a sharp mustard sauce and brown bread. The fish had been swimming that morning, and the landlady said so three times. They were excellent herrings. The room was full of smoke from a chimney that dislikes east winds, and the Compiler ate with his eyes shut.\n\nVerdict: three spoons. The driver holds that a supper eaten with the eyes shut tastes better, there being nothing else to attend to. The Compiler prints this without endorsing it. She has asked whether she is in the book. She is, a little."],["The Lantern and Net","Fifth house: the Lantern and Net, at Gullsend, the last village on the Grey Coast road before it turns inland for the Fells. It rained the whole way. The Compiler arrived wet through, and his driver, Posy Latchet, wetter, having given the pony her oilskin.\n\nThe supper is called a kettle. White fish, potatoes and leeks are simmered together in milk, with butter and a good deal of pepper, and brought to table in the pot. It is eaten with a spoon and yesterday’s bread. The Compiler had three bowls and felt his boots begin to dry from the inside.\n\nThis was the best plate so far. The bedroom ceiling dripped into a basin all night, in waltz time, and the landlord sings. Verdict: four spoons, the first awarded in these pages. The driver says five, and that the Compiler is mean with spoons. He prefers the word exact."],["The Drover’s Halt","Sixth house: the Drover’s Halt, at the foot of the Northern Fells, where the cattle road from the Lowlands begins to climb. Half a mile short of it the trap shed a wheel pin. The driver, Posy Latchet, cut a new one from hazel and fitted it. The Compiler held the pony, and was told he did it well.\n\nSupper was a suet pudding the size of a hat, of bacon and onion boiled in a cloth, with buttered cabbage. It is plain, heavy and hot, and after a wet road there is much to be said for all three.\n\nFrom this entry the Guide prints two verdicts, the driver having earned an opinion. The Compiler: three spoons; a sound pudding, wanting pepper. The driver: five. The Compiler has explained that five means a supper without fault. She replies that she looked and could not find one."],["The Cloud and Kettle","Seventh house: the Cloud and Kettle, at Rooksgill, the highest inn on the Northern Fells and the halfway point of this Guide. The road up is steep. The Compiler, Mr. Blench, walked the last mile to spare the pony, on the advice of his driver, Posy Latchet, who rode.\n\nSnow had closed the pass and the larder was low. Supper was oatcakes warm from the stone, a white crumbling cheese, heather honey, and tea strong enough to stand a spoon in. It was eaten at the one table, between two shepherds and a dog.\n\nAfterwards the shepherds taught the party a card game called Snip the Wick. The driver won fourpence. The Compiler lost sixpence and did not mind, which he cannot account for. Verdicts. The Compiler: three spoons and a half. He does not deal in halves and will remove it before printing. The driver: five, and fourpence."],["The Mill Tap","Eighth house: the Mill Tap, at Withy Bridge on the upper Ember, a neat stone inn beside a working mill. The Compiler, Mr. Blench, sat down at seven. His driver, Posy Latchet, had taken the pony to the smith with a loose shoe and was gone until nine.\n\nSupper was a river trout fried in butter until the skin crackled, with watercress, new bread, and half a lemon. The fish was fresh, the butter was not burnt, the cress was crisp. The room was quiet. Nobody sang.\n\nThe Compiler has looked for a fault and can name none. By his own rule that is five spoons. He finds he cannot write the figure. Verdict: four, and a blank where the reason should be. The driver had hers cold in the kitchen and says five. She asked what he had talked about at table. Nothing, he told her."],["The Three Barges","Ninth house: the Three Barges, at Lockend, where the river Ember widens and the bargemen tie up. It is a low, warm room with a bench round three sides. The Compiler, Mr. Blench, and his driver, Posy Latchet, were given the end nearest the fire.\n\nSupper was pease pudding, thick and yellow, with slices of boiled ham and sharp pickle. It is the same every night, and the bargemen will not hear of a change.\n\nThe landlady, a Mrs. Hallam, asked to see what the Compiler was writing, and he was unwise enough to show her. She read eight entries standing up. Then she said he had rated a good many plates, and asked when he meant to begin on the suppers. He told her they were the same thing. She said he had better eat a few more. Verdicts. The Compiler: three spoons, somewhat ruffled. The driver: five."],["The Orchard Gate","Tenth house: the Orchard Gate, at Applegarth, a brick inn with pear trees along the front. The Compiler, Mr. Blench, arrived with a cold got on the river and went to bed. He records the supper on the word of his driver, Posy Latchet, who carried it up on a tray and sat on the window seat to describe it.\n\nThere was cold pork with apple sauce. Then an apple dumpling: a whole apple, cored, with a clove and sugar where the core had been, wrapped in pastry and baked, with cream.\n\nThe Compiler could taste nothing. The driver reported that the pastry was short, the apple soft but not fallen, and the cream thin. Four spoons, she said, not five. He wrote it down as given. It is the first figure in this Guide to rest on another palate, and the first time she has found a fault."],["The Halfway Hare","Eleventh house: the Halfway Hare, a roadside inn six miles south of Emberwick. The Compiler and his driver, Posy Latchet, reached it at ten, after a wrong turning that was nobody’s fault and the pony’s idea.\n\nThe larder was shut. The landlord, a Mr. Dobbs, said there was only what the family had, and sat them at the kitchen table with his wife, three children and a grandfather. Supper was bread, beef dripping, pickled onions, and tea from a brown pot that never emptied.\n\nBy the rules at the front of this Guide, that is hardly a supper. The Compiler records that the grandfather told a story about a pig and a bishop, and that he laughed until he spilt his tea, which he has not done since the Excise. Verdicts. The driver: five. The Compiler: four, and he is no longer sure what the missing spoon is for."],["The Bell and Bucket Again","Twelfth and last house: the Bell and Bucket, in the market square at Emberwick, where this Guide began. On his first visit the Compiler, Mr. Blench, gave it two spoons, having eaten alone in eleven minutes. He returned after eleven days on the road with his driver, Posy Latchet. The pony, Turnip, was stabled first, at her insistence.\n\nSupper was mutton and barley stew, and the heel of a loaf. They sat at the same corner table for an hour and a half, arguing over every house in the book.\n\nThe stew has not changed. Verdict: five spoons, from both. The Compiler lets his first entry stand as a warning, and adds one direction for the reader: take somebody. Mr. Tench the printer wants a second book, on breakfasts. The trap is engaged, and the driver has asked for her name on the title page. It will be there."]],[["Six Doors in Fog","I sell spectacles. I carry them in a case with forty-eight velvet nests, and my trade is noticing what other people squint at. My name is Winifred Oake. Tonight the fog has put me down at the Turnabout, the inn at Fourways Cross, where the Coast road cuts the Fells road six miles north of Emberwick.\n\nIt is an odd house. It has a front door on each road and a staircase behind each door, and upstairs one long passage with six rooms in a row. Mrs. Tibbet, the landlady, gives me number two. “Second door along,” she says, and I count and find it.\n\nFive other travellers are fogbound with me. A boy of eleven takes my boots to clean; he has been in the house eight days and carries them as if they might hatch. Supper is leek and potato soup. The fog leans on the windows."],["The Wrong Boots","First morning at the Turnabout, the crossroads inn where fog is keeping six of us. I am Winifred Oake, spectacle-seller, in room two. I open my door for my boots and find a pair that are not mine: black, narrow, a man’s, polished like chestnuts.\n\nAlong the passage other doors open. Mr. Lund, the notary’s clerk in room five, is holding my brown buttoned boots at arm’s length. The fiddler in three, Miss Brack, has enormous sea boots. Captain Dray in four, a retired pilot from the Grey Coast, has her small red-laced ones. Mr. Farrant the seedsman, in one, and Mrs. Vane the honey-seller, in six, have each other’s.\n\nWe meet in the middle in our stockings and trade. It is rather like a dance. Mrs. Tibbet, the landlady, apologises and blames the fog. Nothing is missing, I notice. Every boot has gone to the wrong door."],["Chocolate for the Captain","Second day of fog at the Turnabout, the inn at Fourways Cross. I am Winifred Oake. I sell spectacles, and I am watching this house, because yesterday every pair of boots came back to the wrong door.\n\nThis morning it is the trays. Captain Dray, the old sea pilot in room four, ordered shaving water. Miss Brack, the fiddler in three, ordered chocolate. I am in the passage when both doors open. She is holding a jug and a towel. He is holding a blue pot and looking into it with suspicion.\n\n“I’ve not had this since I was a boy,” he says, and drinks it in his doorway. She tells him to keep it and hands the jug across. By noon he has ordered another pot. Mrs. Tibbet, the landlady, says the fog is getting into everyone’s head. I am not sure fog can carry a tray."],["Supper and Suspicion","There are six of us fogbound at the Turnabout, the inn where the Coast road crosses the Fells road, and by the second evening we have a mystery to go with the mutton. Things go astray upstairs. Boots first, then breakfast trays. I sell spectacles; my name is Oake; I listen.\n\nMr. Farrant, the seedsman, is sure Mrs. Vane, who sells marsh honey, is playing tricks. Mrs. Vane says she has better uses for her mornings. Mr. Lund, the notary’s clerk, believes someone is after his deed box and brings it to table, where it sits beside him like a seventh guest. Captain Dray thinks it a joke. Miss Brack tunes her fiddle and thinks nothing aloud.\n\nSupper is boiled mutton with caper sauce and carrots. Mrs. Tibbet, the landlady, serves it with a red face. The new boots boy, Alfie, brings the bread, and looks at no one."],["The Ledger and the Umbrella","Third morning at the Turnabout, the fogbound inn at Fourways Cross. I am Winifred Oake, the spectacle-seller in room two, and I have begun to wake early so as not to miss anything.\n\nToday it is things sent down and sent back. Mr. Farrant, the seedsman in room one, left his ledger to dry by the kitchen fire. Mrs. Vane, the honey-seller in six, sent her umbrella for a new rib. He opens his door on an umbrella. She opens hers on a ledger and, being a woman who cannot leave a column alone, reads it.\n\nShe walks the length of the passage and tells him that his clover seed is added up wrong on page nine, to his own loss, by four shillings. He is outraged for a minute and grateful for the rest of breakfast. They are talking at ten, about clover, and which kind suits bees."],["A List of Pairs","I am a spectacle-seller, held by fog at the Turnabout inn at Fourways Cross, and I believe in looking at a thing properly. On the third afternoon I take a card and write down everything that has gone astray.\n\nBoots, the first morning: mine in room two changed with Mr. Lund’s in five. Miss Brack’s in three with Captain Dray’s in four. Mr. Farrant’s in one with Mrs. Vane’s in six. Trays, the second morning: three and four again. The ledger and the umbrella: one and six again.\n\nSo nothing is lost, and nothing wanders. Each room has a partner and keeps to it. A joker would vary his joke. A thief would take something. This is neither. It is a mistake, made the same way every time. I pin the card inside my case lid and feel I am looking straight at the answer without my glasses on."],["Footsteps from the West","Night at the Turnabout, the crossroads inn where six travellers have been fogbound for three days and nothing reaches the right bedroom. I am Winifred Oake, spectacle-seller, room two, and tonight I set a trap of a mild sort.\n\nI ask Mrs. Tibbet, the landlady, to have my case of spectacles sent up to number two. I hear her repeat it in the kitchen. I go up, leave my door open a finger’s width, and sit in the dark.\n\nFootsteps come, careful ones. They do not come from the east stair beside room one, which I climbed myself. They come from the other end, by room six, where the second staircase rises from the boot room. They pass one door and stop. A knock. Mr. Lund’s voice, in room five, says thank you. My case has gone to him. I sit and count doors on my fingers, from both ends."],["Two and Five Make Seven","Fourth morning at the Turnabout, and the fog is thinner. Before anyone is up I walk the passage in my stockings. I am Winifred Oake, and I sell spectacles. I should have looked at the doors sooner.\n\nThey have no numbers. Six brown doors. At the head of the east stair, the one from the bar, a board says Rooms One to Six, with a hand pointing. At the head of the west stair, the one from the boot room, there is only a lamp hook.\n\nI start at the west end and count. The first door is Mrs. Vane’s, number six. The second is Mr. Lund’s, five. The third is the Captain’s. Anyone told to find the second door, who came up this way, would knock at five. Two and five. Three and four. One and six. Every pair on my card adds up to seven."],["The Boy Who Counts","The Turnabout at Fourways Cross has two staircases, and I, Winifred Oake, spectacle-seller, know that whoever carries things up the west one is counting its six doors backwards. The question is who.\n\nMrs. Tibbet, the landlady, and Jenny, the maid, come up from the bar by the east stair; I have watched them. The west stair rises from the boot room. One person works down there: Alfie Sedge, the boots boy, eleven years old, from a marsh farm, not a fortnight in his first place.\n\nThis evening I stand at the west stair head, apparently polishing a lens. Alfie comes up with Captain Dray’s sea boots, the Captain being in number four. His lips move. One, two, three, four. He sets them down at Miss Brack’s door, and squares the toes. He has done each task exactly as he was told. Nobody thought to tell him which end to begin."],["What the Muddle Mended","Before I say what I know, I take stock. I am Winifred Oake, spectacle-seller, four days fogbound at the Turnabout, where every boot and tray has gone to the wrong room. I have found the cause. I notice I am in no hurry.\n\nConsider the damage. At supper Mr. Farrant the seedsman and Mrs. Vane the honey-seller agree that he will sow white clover along her dyke, and shake hands over the cheese. Captain Dray takes chocolate twice a day and is teaching Miss Brack a pilot’s song, which she plays back on the fiddle, improved.\n\nMr. Lund, the anxious clerk in room five, has worn a pair of my steel-rimmed samples since breakfast. He tried them when my case went to his door. He has read deeds with a headache for three years, he says, and thought it was the law. I sell him the pair at cost."],["A Picture on Each Door","I tell it in the kitchen of the Turnabout, to Mrs. Tibbet the landlady and Alfie Sedge the boots boy, with the door shut. I am Winifred Oake, the spectacle-seller from room two. I show them my card, and walk two fingers along the table from each end.\n\nAlfie goes scarlet. He was told number four, he says, and he counted four. Mrs. Tibbet laughs until she has to hold the dresser. She has sent that child up the back stair forty times. Nobody is cross. Alfie admits, to his boots, that he is not yet steady on written numbers anyway.\n\nSo we do not paint numbers. That afternoon Captain Dray paints a picture on each door, chosen by its guest: a wheatsheaf, spectacles, a fiddle, an anchor, a quill, a bee. Alfie watches every stroke. Outside, for the first time in five days, I can see the signpost."],["Four Roads Out","The fog is gone. From the yard of the Turnabout, the inn at Fourways Cross, all four roads show clear, and six travellers are making a long business of goodbye. I am Winifred Oake, spectacle-seller, and my case is one pair lighter.\n\nMr. Farrant and Mrs. Vane share a cart towards the marsh, still on clover. Miss Brack takes the Coast road to a wedding, with a pilot’s song in her fiddle case. Captain Dray goes north to meet a grandchild, a tin of chocolate under his arm. Mr. Lund walks south to Emberwick, reading a deed, and does not trip.\n\nMrs. Tibbet says the rooms will keep their pictures. Travellers may ask for the Bee or the Anchor. This morning my boots stood outside the Spectacles, and they were mine. Alfie, the boots boy, had squared the toes. I give him sixpence and take the Fells road."]],[["In Reply to Yours","Emberwick Post Office, the ninth of October. To Mr Aldous Crake, Inspector of Rural Offices, District Postal Board, Lowmarket. Sir, I have your notice. It informs me that this office is under review and may be replaced by a wall box, emptied twice weekly by the Lowmarket carrier. Your reason is that Emberwick handles forty-one letters a week, and the Board requires one hundred.\n\nI have been postmistress here nineteen years and have never handled so few as forty-one letters, not even the week of the flood. I do not say your figure is wrong. I say that I should like to meet whoever counted it.\n\nA wall box cannot sell a stamp, Mr Crake, nor tell a boy that his sevens look like ones. I shall count for myself, beginning Monday, and you shall have my figures. I remain, with respect and some surprise, Winifred Tansy, Postmistress."],["A Week Properly Counted","To Mr Crake, Inspector of Rural Offices, Lowmarket, from Winifred Tansy, Postmistress at Emberwick, whose office your Board proposes to close for want of letters. Sir, I promised you figures and here they are.\n\nI counted every item that crossed this counter from Monday to Saturday. I used dried peas, one into a jar for each letter, because a pea cannot be argued with afterwards. The total is two hundred and twelve letters, nine parcels, and a hat-box for Mrs Peabody, which I have counted as a parcel though it behaved like furniture.\n\nYour figure was forty-one. Mine is two hundred and twelve. One of us has mislaid one hundred and seventy-one letters a week, and I have looked under my counter. The jar is on the shelf should you wish to inspect the peas. I have not eaten any. Yours faithfully, W. Tansy."],["What a Wall Box Cannot","To Mr Crake, Inspector of Rural Offices, Lowmarket, from the Postmistress at Emberwick. Sir, since your Board weighs this office against a wall box, permit me to list what crossed my counter on Thursday that was not a letter.\n\nOld Silas Fenn came in with a letter from his daughter in the Lowlands. His eyes are gone, so I read it to him twice by the stove, the second time slower for the part about the baby. Mrs Peabody collected the spare key to number nine, which I keep with thirteen others on a board behind the scales. A drover asked the road to Stonecrop. A small girl asked how to spell sincerely and was told.\n\nNone of this earned the Board a penny. I mention it because your form has no line for it, and I thought you should know what the line would say. Yours faithfully, Winifred Tansy."],["Concerning the Half-Ounce","Emberwick Post Office, to Mr Crake, Inspector, at the District Postal Board. Sir, I have your reply of two lines, which says my figures are noted. I have noted that they are noted.\n\nSince an inspection may follow, I had better confess something before you find it. The brass letter scale on my counter lacks its half-ounce weight. It rolled between the floorboards eleven years ago and is there still; I hear it when the carrier stamps his boots. In its place I use a horn button from a drover’s coat. I have tested that button against the Lowmarket weights and it is true to a hair.\n\nEvery letter in Emberwick has been weighed honestly against it. I should rather you heard this from me than discovered it and supposed the rest of the office ran on buttons. Yours faithfully, Winifred Tansy, Postmistress."],["An Invitation, With Directions","To Mr Aldous Crake, Inspector of Rural Offices, Lowmarket. Sir, I have now written you four letters about whether Emberwick should keep its post office, and I believe you have never seen it. I write to invite you.\n\nTake the branch train to Emberwick and walk down Ember Street as far as the pump. The office is the one with the red door, which wants painting; I say so before you do. Come on a Tuesday if you can. Tuesday is when the Fell bag and the Marsh bag go out, and the bench by the stove is full by three.\n\nYou need not announce yourself. I would sooner you saw the place as it is than as it looks with the brass polished. There will be tea at four in either case, as there is every day, Board or no Board. Yours faithfully, Winifred Tansy, Postmistress."],["Two Sealed Bags","Emberwick Post Office, the second of December. To Mr Crake, Inspector, Lowmarket. Sir, I have found your missing letters, and they were never lost. Your Board says this office handles forty-one letters a week. My own count is two hundred and twelve.\n\nI asked Barnaby Sedge, the carrier, who has more sense than he lets on. Every letter for the Northern Fells goes from here in one sealed bag, and every letter for the marsh in another. Neither is opened until Lowmarket, where the letters are stamped and counted to Lowmarket’s credit. One hundred and seventy-one a week, Mr Crake. I sort them, bag them and seal them, and your clerk counts them as his own.\n\nWhat remains is Emberwick writing to Emberwick: forty-one. I do not blame the clerk. I only ask that somebody open the bags on paper as well as in fact. Yours, W. Tansy."],["Snow on the Fell Road","To Mr Crake, Inspector of Rural Offices, from Winifred Tansy, Postmistress at Emberwick, the office your Board thinks of replacing with a box on a wall. Sir, this is to report the week of the snow.\n\nOn Tuesday the carrier’s cart could not climb past the second milestone. The Fell bag held forty letters, among them a doctor’s note for Stonecrop and a seed catalogue somebody has waited on since autumn. I put on my brother’s boots and carried it up myself, four miles, to the Drover’s Rest, where the shepherds collect. Tom Pellow, my post boy, minded the counter and sold nine stamps without me.\n\nI do not tell you this to be admired. My feet have had the admiring of it. I tell you because a wall box would have spent that Tuesday under a drift, keeping its letters dry and to itself. Yours faithfully, W. Tansy."],["The Gentleman With the Umbrella","Emberwick Post Office, a Thursday in February. To Mr Aldous Crake, Inspector of Rural Offices. Sir, the stationmaster tells me that the gentleman who sheltered here from the rain on Tuesday, and stayed an hour, was yourself.\n\nThen you have seen the office after all. You sat on the bench by the stove between Silas Fenn and the coal scuttle. You heard me read Mr Fenn his daughter’s letter. You held Mrs Peabody’s wool while she wound it, which was good of you, since she asks everybody and most find they are in a hurry. You carried the Fell bag out to the carrier’s cart without being asked, and you drank two cups of tea.\n\nHad I known, I should have used the good cups. On reflection I am glad I did not know. You saw a Tuesday, sir, and that was all I wanted. Yours faithfully, Winifred Tansy, Postmistress."],["One Umbrella, Returned","To Mr Crake, Inspector of Rural Offices, Lowmarket, from the Postmistress at Emberwick. Sir, you left a brown umbrella under my bench the wet Tuesday you called. I return it by this post. It would not sit on the scale, so I have charged it as a walking stick and paid the postage myself.\n\nI thank you for your letter, which ran to four pages where its predecessors ran to two lines. You ask questions no form of the Board’s has asked me in nineteen years, and I answer them gladly. I keep fourteen spare keys. I read letters aloud to three people and write them for two. Mothers have weighed five babies on the parcel scale this winter; all were found satisfactory.\n\nYou ask last whether I would mind your putting such things before the Board. I would mind very much if you did not. Yours faithfully, Winifred Tansy."],["A Petition, Unrequested","Emberwick Post Office, the first week of March. To Mr Aldous Crake, Inspector, District Postal Board. Sir, you write that the Board meets on the twentieth to decide whether this office stays open. I enclose something I did not ask for and cannot in conscience withhold.\n\nMrs Peabody has got up a petition. It carries ninety-six signatures, eleven crosses duly witnessed, and one paw print, which belongs to the baker’s dog and which I have asked her to explain. She says he comes in every day and has a right to an opinion. Old Mr Fenn signed by feel. The schoolchildren signed in a column, getting larger toward the bottom.\n\nI told her that boards are moved by figures and not by feelings. She said, then count the names, that is a figure. I had no answer, sir, so I send it to you. Yours faithfully, Winifred Tansy, Postmistress."],["An Ordinary Day, Set Down","To Mr Crake, Inspector of Rural Offices, from Winifred Tansy, Postmistress at Emberwick. Sir, the Board met yesterday and I shall not hear its decision for a week. Nothing in this letter needs an answer. I only wish one ordinary day to be on record, whichever way it has gone.\n\nI unlocked at seven. Tom Pellow swept the step. I sold thirty stamps, weighed one goose in a basket, and sorted the Fell bag while the kettle boiled. At three the bench filled. At four there was tea. At six I emptied the box in the wall, and the shutter gave the small clap it has given every evening for nineteen years.\n\nThen I lit the lamp in the window, as I always do, so the carrier can find the door in the dark. It is burning as I write. Yours faithfully, W. Tansy."],["The Board Decides","Emberwick Post Office, the last day of March. To Mr Aldous Crake, Inspector. Sir, your letter came by the morning cart and I read it standing up. The office is to stay. The Fell bag and the Marsh bag are to be counted to Emberwick, all two hundred and twelve letters of us, and the wall box is to go to some village that has asked for one.\n\nI thank the Board for the parcel also. The new half-ounce weight sits on the scale as though it had never been away. The horn button has retired to the shelf beside my jar of peas, with honour. Tom Pellow is painting the door with your tin of red paint, and a good deal of himself.\n\nYour umbrella being home, you will want a fresh excuse to call. I suggest tea. Four o’clock, any Tuesday. Yours sincerely, Winifred Tansy, Postmistress."]],[["Behind the Churchyard Wall","Dear Arlo, you went on the carrier’s cart yesterday at eight. I know it was eight because I was behind the churchyard wall and heard the clock. I saw your mother holding the birdcage and your father counting boxes. I saw you look round at the pump twice. I did not come out.\n\nI am not sending this. I am writing it because Mam says a thing written down stops going round your head. So far she is wrong.\n\nThe day before, at the weir, I said you pushed the Otter off early, and then I said, good, go to Loam End then. I have still got your brass compass in my pocket. The needle sticks at north-east the same as ever. This letter is going in the toffee tin in the hollow willow, where nobody goes but us. Jem Aldery."],["Conkers and Your Desk","Dear Arlo, it is October and you have been at Loam End a month. I am not posting this one either. It is going in the toffee tin in our willow by the weir, on top of the first.\n\nThe big chestnut by the forge has dropped everything at once. I have a conker that has beaten nine others, and nobody to tell who would understand how much that is. You would have soaked yours in vinegar and said you hadn’t.\n\nThey have put a new boy at your desk. His name is Wilf Dabb, he comes from the marsh, and he keeps his pencils in a row. Your initials are still cut under the lid, where Mr Greave has never found them. I looked. I have not said one word to Wilf about them, or about you. I still have your compass. Jem."],["The Dam Goes Out","Dear Arlo, this is the third letter I have written since you moved to the Lowlands, and the third I am not sending. They live in a tin in the hollow willow. You would know the tin. It was your toffee.\n\nThe Ember came up on Sunday night, brown and loud, over the stepping stones. Our dam went. All of it: the flat stone you carried from the ford, the clay we packed in with our heels, the stick with the flag. It took us all August, and the river took it in one night without noticing.\n\nIn the morning the willow was standing in water to its knees but the tin was dry on its ledge. I thought you would want to know about the dam. Then I remembered what I said to you at the weir, and I did not know what you would want. Jem."],["The Sledge Steers Left","Dear Arlo, there is snow on Kiln Hill, and I took the Bathtub up by myself. I am telling you in a letter I shall not post. It goes in the tin in our willow with the others, because of what I said the day before you left for Loam End.\n\nThe sledge we built steers left now. It never did with two. I think you were the weight that kept it straight, sitting behind with your boots dug in. I went into the hedge four times. The Pellow twins laughed, and I said the runner was bent, which it is not.\n\nOn the fifth go I sat further back, where you sat, and it ran true all the way to the gate. So that is how it is done. I walked home with the rope over my shoulder and nobody to take a turn pulling. Jem."],["Where the Needle Points","Dear Arlo, I have your brass compass still, the one you lent me in summer, and I ought to have given it back before you moved away. I did not, and I cannot post it now without a letter, and I cannot send a letter. So I am writing one for the tin in the willow instead.\n\nMr Greave has a map on the schoolroom wall. I measured with my thumb. Loam End is nineteen miles from Emberwick, south and a little west, past the place where the Ember goes wide.\n\nThis afternoon I stood on the bridge and tried to point your compass at you. The needle stuck at north-east, as it always does, and I had to knock it with my knuckle the way you showed me. Then it swung round and pointed down the river. I stayed until my hands were cold. Jem."],["About the Race","Dear Arlo, this is the sixth letter I have written you and hidden in the willow, and it is the one with the truth in it. That may be why I am not sending it.\n\nYou did not push off early. On your last day, when we raced the bark boats from the weir, the Otter beat my Kingfisher by the length of the mill wall, and she won fair. I had put a lead fishing weight under mine for a keel and too tall a mast, and she wallowed. I knew it when I said you cheated. I knew it when I said, good, go to Loam End then.\n\nI was not angry about the race. I was angry that you were going on the cart the next morning, and I could not be angry at a cart. There. It is written down. Mam was half right. Jem."],["Mending the Otter","Dear Arlo, it is March. I found your boat. You moved to the Lowlands in such a hurry of boxes that you left the Otter behind, wedged in the hollow willow above the ledge where I keep these letters, which I write and do not send.\n\nHer mast had snapped. I do not know if it was the winter or if it was like that when you put her there. I cut a hazel twig, peeled it, and whittled the foot to fit. It took three tries. Then I waxed her seams with a candle end the way your father showed us.\n\nShe is better than new and I have not sailed her. I held her over the water at the weir and could not let go. It would not be a race with one boat. She is back on her shelf in the willow, facing the door. Jem."],["Wilf Is All Right","Dear Arlo, I have to tell you something, and since these letters never get posted it is safe to tell. They sit in your old toffee tin in our willow, and you are nineteen miles off at Loam End, not reading them.\n\nWilf Dabb is all right. He is the marsh boy who has your desk. He can whistle through a blade of grass loud enough to turn a horse, and he knows where the eels lie up. Yesterday I showed him the weir and the stepping stones.\n\nI did not show him the willow. He asked what was in the hollow one and I said wasps. It felt wrong all the same, standing there with him, like wearing somebody else’s coat. Then I thought, you will have a Wilf at Loam End by now, and I found I did not mind as much as I expected. Jem."],["As Far as the Pump","Dear Arlo, today I nearly sent you a letter. Not this one. This one is going in the tin in the willow, with eight others I have written since you moved to the Lowlands.\n\nThe one I nearly sent had three lines. It said: I am sorry about the race. I have your compass. I hope you are all right. I put it in my pocket and walked up Ember Street with a penny for the stamp. I got as far as the pump. The post office door was open and I could see Mrs Tansy at the scales.\n\nThen I thought of you opening it, and reading three lines after eight months of nothing, and I turned round. I told Mam I had gone to look at the pump. She said, and how was it. I have folded the short letter inside this one. Jem."],["News of the Heron","Dear Arlo, here is the news from the weir, written in June for a tin in a willow tree, since I still have not posted you one word.\n\nThe heron we watched all last summer has a nest in the alders, and two chicks that look like angry umbrellas. The kingfisher is back on the mill post. The stepping stones are dry to their feet. Mr Greave found your initials under the desk lid at last and only said, hm. Wilf Dabb and I have begun a new dam, lower down, and it leaks.\n\nI read the old letters through today, all nine. They are mostly news. I have been writing to you as if you were away for a week with a cold. I think I began these to say sorry, and I have said nearly everything else instead. The compass is still in my pocket. Jem."],["Thursdays, by Loam End","Dear Arlo, it is July and I found something out. I am writing it down for the tin in the willow, where I keep the letters I have not dared send you since you left Emberwick.\n\nMr Sedge the carrier goes through Loam End. Every other Thursday, with the Lowland parcels. I asked him this morning while he watered the horse at the pump, trying to sound as if I did not care. He said there is a lad at the mill there, thin, with a cap too big, who asks him each time whether the weir at Emberwick is still standing.\n\nI said it was. Mr Sedge said, I know, I tell him so. Then he looked at me a while and said a parcel to Loam End costs fourpence and he leaves at eight. He did not say anything else. He did not need to. Jem."],["The One That Goes","Dear Arlo, your card came this morning. Four lines and a drawing of a boat. You wrote that you were sorry you went off without a word, and you asked if the Otter was all right. You have said sorry first. You always did win races.\n\nSo this is the letter that goes. Under it in the parcel are eleven more, September to July, that I wrote and hid in the willow. Read them in order. The early ones are the worst. Your compass is in there too, wrapped in my sock, still sticking at north-east.\n\nThe Otter is mended and she is staying here, because you will want a boat when you come at harvest. Mam says you can have the truckle bed. I am taking this to Mr Sedge now, at the pump, before eight. I am sorry about the race. You won fair. Your friend, Jem Aldery."]],[["Somebody’s Spectacles","To the Post Office, Emberwick. Dear Mrs Tansy, my name is Pip Hollin, I am nine, and I live at 4 Mill Row. A parcel came for me today on the carrier’s cart. It has my name on the label. It is not my parcel.\n\nMy Gran at Gullsend on the Grey Coast was sending me a blue jersey she knitted and a tin of treacle toffee. Instead there is a pair of spectacles, very strong, and a small metal whistle on a cord. I looked through the spectacles and the kitchen went like the bottom of a pond. I blew the whistle and nothing came out. Then every dog in Mill Row arrived in our yard, and is still there.\n\nSomebody must be wanting these. I think he is old, and has a dog. Please can you find him, and my jersey. Yours truly, Philippa Hollin (Pip)."],["A Jersey for One Arm","To the Postmistress at Emberwick, from Josiah Raike, shepherd, Upper Wethering in the Northern Fells. Madam, forgive the size of my writing. I make it large so that I may read it myself, my spectacles being broken these three weeks.\n\nI sent to the spectacle-maker at Lowmarket for a new pair, and a whistle for my dog Bracken, who is going deaf to the old one. The carrier brought a parcel on Tuesday with my name on. In it is a blue jersey that would fit over one of my arms, and a tin of toffee.\n\nI must own that I ate one piece of the toffee before I understood the parcel was not mine. I held the label to the window to be sure. It is very good toffee. Some child is going cold on my account, and I am counting sheep by guesswork. Kindly advise. J. Raike."],["The Matter of the Ford","From the Post Office, Emberwick. To Miss Pip Hollin of Mill Row and to Mr Josiah Raike of Upper Wethering, the same letter to each, since you have each other’s parcels.\n\nI have looked into it. Last Monday the carrier’s cart crossed the ford with the river high, and two parcels took a wetting. Their labels floated off. The carrier’s lad fished them out, dried them on his knee, and pasted them back. He pasted them back the wrong way round. He is sorry, and he has been spoken to.\n\nSo, Miss Hollin, you hold a shepherd’s spectacles and dog whistle; and you, Mr Raike, a child’s jersey and toffee. Wrap them up again, please, and hand them to Barnaby Sedge the carrier on Thursday. He will bring each to its proper door. I should not blow that whistle again, Pip. W. Tansy, Postmistress."],["Left at Reedholm","Nan Fenwick, you were out at your eel traps, so I have left this under a stone on your step. It is Barnaby Sedge, the carrier. My back axle has gone on the marsh causeway and I am walking the horse home to Emberwick.\n\nIn your shed, out of the wet, are two parcels I dare not leave on the cart. They have been astray once already. One is spectacles and a dog whistle for a shepherd, Josiah Raike, up at Upper Wethering in the Fells. The other is a jersey and toffee for a child, Pip Hollin, Mill Row, Emberwick. They are labelled plain, this time by me.\n\nIf the reed-cutter goes north, give him the shepherd’s. If the punt goes down to the ferry, send the child’s by that. I shall owe you a sack of meal. Keep that goat of yours out of the shed. B. Sedge."],["The Goat Was Thorough","To the Postmistress, Emberwick, from Nan Fenwick, eel-catcher, Reedholm on the marsh. Mrs Tansy, the carrier left two parcels in my shed and a note telling me to keep the goat out. The note I found on Friday. The goat found the shed on Thursday.\n\nDuchess has eaten both labels. She likes paste. She did not touch what was inside. I looked: spectacles and a whistle in one, a little jersey and toffee in the other. A shepherd’s and a child’s, plainly. Sedge’s note had the names, but the rain had been at it, and all I could make out was Wethering and Mill.\n\nSo I have written what I know on each in pencil. Shepherd, the Fells, went north with Dan the reed-cutter. Child, Emberwick, went down by punt to the ferry. It is the best I can do. N. Fenwick."],["Handed Over at the Ferry","To Miss Lark at the schoolhouse, Emberwick, from Corry Mott at the ferry. Miss, the marsh punt brought me a parcel this morning. All it says on it, in pencil, is Child, Emberwick. I have worked the Ember ferry thirty years, and there are a good many of those.\n\nIt is soft, and it rattles at one end like a tin of sweets. I asked the punt man who it was for. He said a goat ate the particulars. I did not ask more.\n\nI cannot go knocking on every door in the town with a boat to mind. But every child in Emberwick passes under your eye sooner or later, so I am sending it up to you by the baker’s boy, who has promised not to shake it. Somewhere there is a small person waiting on a parcel. You will know the look. Respectfully, C. Mott."],["Fourteen Shepherds","From Mrs Hale at the Drover’s Rest, Stonecrop, to whichever shepherd this parcel belongs to. Dan the reed-cutter left it on my bar on his way north. The label says Shepherd, the Fells, in pencil, and nothing more. Fourteen shepherds drink here. I counted.\n\nI opened it to be helpful. Spectacles and a dog whistle. On market night I set the spectacles on the bar and waited to see who squinted at them. Everybody squinted. Three tried them on. Old Tibb said they suited him, and had to be persuaded.\n\nThen somebody remembered a man at Wethering who has been counting his flock by guesswork. So I am sending the parcel up with the parson, who walks that way on Sunday and is honest, though vague. If you are the man, there is a pint waiting here for your trouble. If you are not, pass it on. M. Hale."],["Eleven Socks Darned","To Mr Josiah Raike, Upper Wethering, from Ada Brock, Nether Wethering, at the bottom of your hill. Mr Raike, the parson left a parcel with me on Sunday. He was told Wethering and stopped at the first one he came to, as he does. It is your spectacles and a dog whistle.\n\nI shall be honest with you. I tried the spectacles on, only to see. I have not threaded a needle at the first go in six years. That evening I threaded one straight off, and darned eleven socks, and read the back of the seed packet, and saw that the cat is not as young as I thought.\n\nI am sending them up with my grandson this morning before I think better of it. Tell me the name of your spectacle-maker. And look in on your way down to market. Ada Brock."],["The Lost Property Cupboard","To Mrs Hollin, 4 Mill Row, from Miss Lark at the schoolhouse. Dear Mrs Hollin, Pip is bringing this note home, and a parcel with it, and I think you will want the explanation.\n\nThe ferryman sent me a parcel marked only Child, Emberwick. It spent a week in the lost property cupboard between one boot and a recorder until I remembered it on Friday. I held up what was inside before the whole school. Eleven children claimed the toffee. Only one claimed the jersey.\n\nPip said it was hers because the left sleeve would be longer than the right, as her Gran’s knitting always is. I measured. It is longer by a full two inches. No court could ask for better proof. She has worn it since morning lessons with the cuff turned back, and has shared the toffee fairly, by her own account. Yours, E. Lark."],["Labels to Be Sewn","To the Postmistress at Emberwick, from Mrs Dorothea Hollin, Gullsend, on the Grey Coast. Madam, my granddaughter Pip writes that the jersey I posted in October has reached her at last, by way of a ford, a shepherd, a goat, a punt and a school cupboard. I had begun to think I dreamed knitting it.\n\nI do not blame you. I blame paste. In future I shall sew my labels on, and I advise the Board to consider it.\n\nPip tells me that a shepherd in the Fells, a Mr Raike, had her parcel a week, sent it back whole, and owned up to the single toffee he ate. A man like that should not go without a jersey of his own. Kindly ask him his chest measure and the length of his arm. Both arms, tell him. I shall do my best about the sleeves. D. Hollin."],["A Twist of Fell Wool","To Miss Pip Hollin, Mill Row, Emberwick, from Josiah Raike, shepherd, Upper Wethering. Miss Hollin, you will see my writing has shrunk. My spectacles came up the hill on Monday with a small boy, six weeks late. I counted the flock that same hour. I had guessed two hundred and four. There are two hundred and six, and I have apologised to the pair of them.\n\nThe whistle works. Bracken heard it from the far side of the tarn and came like a thrown stone. I am told the dogs of Mill Row can vouch for it.\n\nI had your jersey a week and one of your toffees for good. To square the account I enclose a twist of wool from my best ewe, for your grandmother. She has sent to ask the length of my arms. I have never been so measured. Your friend, J. Raike."],["No Labels This Time","To Mr Josiah Raike, shepherd, Upper Wethering. Dear Mr Raike, thank you for the wool. Gran says it is the best she ever had and she is knitting your jersey out of it, grey, with a blue band from what was left of mine. So we will match a bit.\n\nI am wearing my jersey now. The left sleeve is too long, the same as always. I am glad you ate a toffee. It means you know what they are like. I have saved you two more.\n\nGran is coming at shearing time, and we are all riding up on Mr Sedge’s cart to bring your jersey ourselves. Nobody is posting it. Mrs Tansy says that is a slight on the service and very wise. Please tell Bracken I shall bring her a bone. The dogs of Mill Row still look at me hopefully. Your friend, Pip Hollin."]],[["Arrived, Sea Present","Maud. Arrived at Cockleby four o’clock, train only nine minutes late, sandwiches eaten by Lowmarket. The sea is here. It is a good deal larger than the picture on the chapel raffle ticket led me to expect, and it does not keep still. They call this the Grey Coast, but I should say pewter, with a polish on it.\n\nMrs Crabb’s boarding house is called Sea View. There is one, if I stand on the bed. Supper was a kipper. You said when I left that I should not put so much as a toe in the water, and that forty years behind a button counter had made me unfit for holidays. I have twelve days to prove you wrong and I have brought the stout shoes. Do not let Mrs Peabody have thread on account. Effie."],["The Promenade, Twice","Maud. Day two at Cockleby. Walked the promenade end to end, twice, to get my bearings. It has forty-one benches, a bandstand with no band, and a man who sells cockles from a barrow and calls everybody captain. I was captain twice.\n\nSat a full hour looking at the sea, which is longer than I have sat looking at anything since the shop opened. The tide went out while I watched. Nobody stopped it. There is a boy called Dob on the sands with a donkey named Admiral, a penny a ride; I declined, with thanks, and Admiral looked relieved.\n\nToe not yet in. I walked down to where the wet sand begins and a wave came at my shoes, so I came back. I am studying the enemy. The grey darning wool, if anyone asks, is behind the hooks and eyes. Effie."],["One Toe, Witnessed","Maud. The toe is in. Left foot, big one, seven o’clock this morning, before anyone was about to see my ankles. The sea at Cockleby is as cold as the pump handle in January. I said a word I shall not write on a postcard that the postman may read.\n\nI was seen, however. Four ladies came down the beach in bathing dresses and went straight in up to the neck, talking the whole while about marmalade. The stoutest, a Mrs Orla Finn, called out, well done, that is how it starts. They swim every morning and go home to one teapot between them, so they call themselves the Early Kettles.\n\nShe has asked me to come again tomorrow. I said I would think about it. I have thought. You owe me an apology regarding toes, and may send it by return. Effie."],["Rain and the Shell Museum","Maud. Rained all day at Cockleby, sideways. Mrs Crabb says it does that. No sea for me, so I went to the Shell Museum, which is one room over the chandler’s, kept by a retired harbourmaster who takes twopence and follows you round.\n\nFour hundred shells in trays, Maud, every one labelled in a hand as neat as yours. Some no bigger than a shirt button and some you could bath a baby in. He showed me how mother-of-pearl is cut. I told him I had sold pearl buttons for forty years and never once thought of them having lived in the sea. He was very much shocked.\n\nI have bought two dozen pearl buttons for the shop, at trade, after some discussion. They are in my stocking drawer. We shall put them in the window when I am home and say they are from the coast. Effie."],["Up to the Knees","Maud. Day five at the coast and I have been in to the knees. Mrs Finn of the Early Kettles, the ladies who swim before breakfast, lent me a bathing dress. It is navy serge and has more cloth in it than my winter coat. I changed in a hut that smelt of wet rope.\n\nThe trick, they tell me, is not to stop and consider. I stopped and considered for some minutes. Then a small wave settled the matter by coming to me. Knees, Maud, both of them, in the open sea. The sand moves under your feet as if someone were pulling a rug gently.\n\nAfterwards, tea from their one teapot and a ginger biscuit. I could not feel my feet until half past nine and did not mind. If the traveller in ribbons calls, do not let him sell you the mauve. Effie."],["The Hat Departs","Maud. Bad news, and you are to bear it calmly. My good straw hat is gone, the one with the cherries. I walked to the end of the pier at Cockleby this afternoon to see the boats, the wind came round the lamp post without any warning, and off it went.\n\nIt did not fall in the sea at once. It bowled along the pier like a hoop, hopped the rail, and flew, Maud, a good deal better than it ever sat. I last saw it heading for the lighthouse with a gull in attendance. A gentleman offered to run after it, but there was only water to run on.\n\nEleven years I had that hat. I am wearing a headscarf of Mrs Crabb’s with anchors on it and feel like a fishwife, though a cheerful one. No bathing today on account of the wind. Six days left. Effie."],["Admiral Finds a Hat","Maud. The hat is found. It blew off the pier yesterday, you recall. This morning the boy Dob came to the door of my boarding house leading his donkey, Admiral, with my straw hat on the donkey’s head, one ear through the brim.\n\nThey found it on the sands at low tide, a mile along, sitting on a rock as if waiting for a cab. It is salt-stained, and the cherries are gone. Dob says the gull had them. I looked at Admiral, and Admiral looked at the sea, and I have my own opinion.\n\nI gave the boy sixpence and the donkey a carrot, which was generous in the circumstances. Then I had a penny ride after all, Maud, to the breakwater and back, holding on with both hands. As for the hat, it has seen more of the coast than I have. Effie."],["Waist Deep, Maud","Maud. Waist deep. I write it large so the postman may know too. Eight days at Cockleby and I have stood in the Grey Coast sea up to the buttons of a borrowed bathing dress, with the Early Kettles round me in a ring like hens round a new pullet.\n\nA wave took a liberty and went over my shoulder. I shrieked. I have not shrieked since the mouse in the ribbon drawer, and that was nothing like so pleasant. Mrs Finn says tomorrow I am to lie back and let the water hold me. I said the water and I had not been introduced long enough.\n\nTea from the one teapot afterwards, and I was given the cup without the chip, which I understand is an honour. Your card came. I accept the apology about the toe, though it was grudging. The mauve ribbon will never sell. Effie."],["Fish Supper on the Wall","Maud. No bathing today, it being Sunday. Instead I must confess that I ate my supper in the street. Fried fish, out of a paper, with my fingers, sitting on the harbour wall at Cockleby with my feet hanging over. You are not to tell the chapel.\n\nThe boats came in while I ate. Seven of them, with brown sails, one behind another round the lighthouse, and the women went down with baskets to meet them. A man threw a rope and a boy caught it without looking. Then the lamps were lit along the quay and came out again upside down in the water.\n\nI sat till my paper was empty and the gulls gave me up. Forty years I have shut the shop at six and gone in to a boiled egg. I did not know evenings came in this size. Three days left. Effie."],["Floating, After a Fashion","Maud. I have floated. Mrs Finn of the Early Kettles stood behind me in the sea at Cockleby this morning with a hand under each shoulder and said, lie back, Miss Brindle, as if into a chair. I said there was no chair. She said that was rather the point.\n\nSo I lay back. My feet came up by themselves, which nobody had warned me of, and there was nothing to look at but sky. One gull. A small cloud shaped like the iron. The water held me as a counter holds a bolt of cloth, without comment. Then she took her hands away and I floated alone for a count of three before I remembered myself and sank.\n\nThree seconds, Maud. I am fifty-eight and I did not know I could be carried. Two days left. I have asked what comes after floating. They say strokes. Effie."],["Six Strokes Counted","Maud. I have swum. Six strokes, counted aloud by four witnesses, along the beach at Cockleby and not out of my depth, since I am not a fool. It was not elegant. Mrs Finn says I swim like a person beating a carpet. But I began in one place and finished in another with no foot on the bottom, and that is swimming.\n\nI swallowed some sea on the fourth stroke. It tastes exactly as you would suppose. The Early Kettles, my bathing ladies, gave three cheers, and the cockle man on the promenade took off his cap, though he may only have been scratching.\n\nEight days ago I put in one toe and thought myself brave. Tell nobody at home; I wish to mention it myself, in passing, at the chapel tea, and watch Mrs Peabody’s face. Home the day after tomorrow. Air my bed. Effie."],["Home Tomorrow, Maud","Maud. Last card from Cockleby. I come home tomorrow by the train that gets in at twenty to six. My box is packed: one straw hat without cherries, two dozen pearl buttons, a paper of shrimps for your tea, and a stoppered jar of the sea, so that you may put your own toe in and tell me how you like it.\n\nI swam ten strokes this morning for a farewell. The Early Kettles gave me the unchipped cup again and a ginger biscuit for the train. Dob brought Admiral to the door of Sea View to say goodbye. I shook hands with one of them.\n\nNow attend. I have paid Mrs Crabb a deposit on two beds for the first fortnight of next June. Two, Maud. The shop can wear a card in the door for once. Bring stout shoes. Put the kettle on at six. Effie."]],[["Wagons on the Lowlands Road","Wren Hallow saw them first, because the bakery on Cross Street has the highest attic window in Emberwick and she had been watching from it since Tuesday. On Thursday, a little after four, the Lowlands road grew a smudge of smoke. Under the smoke came a traction engine painted plum and gold, and behind the engine seven wagons, nose to tail like ducklings.\n\nShe was down three flights and into the shop before the flour settled. Marrable’s are coming, she said. Her father went on shaping loaves. They came every year in the last week of September, he said, and every year she announced it as if it were news.\n\nIt was news. Wren stood on the step with flour to her elbows and counted them in over the Ember bridge. Seven wagons. On the last, under a tarpaulin, something stuck out that looked very like a wooden hoof."],["The Green by Lamplight","Marrable’s Travelling Fair takes one night to build, and most of Emberwick has never seen it done, being asleep. Wren Hallow, eleven, the baker’s daughter, saw it because bakers rise at three. She carried the first tray of rolls across to Goose Green in the dark and stopped dead at the gate.\n\nThe Green was full of lanterns and quiet grumbling. Men walked about with striped poles on their shoulders. A woman in a leather apron, who turned out to be Bess Marrable, stood on the engine and directed everything with a spanner. In the middle, the roundabout was going up like a great umbrella opening, rib by rib.\n\nBess bought all the rolls. Then she looked at Wren, who had not moved, and handed her a mallet. The pegs for the hoopla stall wanted knocking in, she said, and they would not knock themselves."],["Opened by the Mayor","The Goose Fair at Emberwick cannot begin until the Mayor has declared it open, a rule the Mayor is fond of. So at noon on Friday the whole of Marrable’s Travelling Fair stood ready and silent on Goose Green, the roundabout still, the coconuts waiting on their cups, while Mayor Tibbet read a speech.\n\nIt covered the charter of the fair, the price of geese, and the new drain in Mill Lane. Wren Hallow, at the front with her father’s tray of buns, watched a showman fall asleep standing up. Then came the ribbon, and it was found that nobody on the Committee had brought scissors. There was a conference. Wren held up the bakery bread knife.\n\nThe Mayor sawed through the ribbon in eleven strokes. Bess Marrable pulled a lever, the engine whistled, and every stall on the Green shouted at once."],["Eleven Horses and a Pole","The roundabout at Marrable’s Fair is called the Gallopers, and it is the reason most of Emberwick comes to Goose Green in fair week. Wren Hallow, the baker’s daughter, rode it on the Friday evening with the first of her four saved pennies. Her horse was dapple grey with a scarlet saddle, and on a scroll along its neck was painted a name: Candlewade.\n\nThey all had names. Thrushby, Oddmoor, Saltgrave, Kettlebridge. Old Jem Marrable, who took the pennies, told her each horse was carved in a town on the fair’s road and called after it, one a year when he was young and slower since. Wren counted as they went round. Eleven horses. And one brass pole, twisted like barley sugar, going up and down with nothing on it.\n\nShe asked whose that was. Jem said it was nobody’s yet, and let her go round again."],["Three Balls a Penny","On the Saturday of fair week, Wren Hallow, the baker’s daughter, spent two of her three remaining pennies at the coconut shy, and has regretted one of them ever since.\n\nThe shy at Marrable’s Fair is kept by a mournful man named Mr Dabb, who calls out three balls a penny as though announcing bad weather. Wren’s first three balls struck the canvas, the grass, and Mr Dabb’s bucket. Her second three were worse. One coconut rocked, considered the matter, and stayed. Mr Dabb said she had a baker’s arm, good for kneading, and offered no further comment.\n\nShe walked home by the edge of Goose Green. The great limb the lime tree dropped in the March gale still lay there, since the Parish Committee had met six times about it without deciding anything. Old Jem Marrable stood beside it in the dusk, running his thumb along the grain."],["Sixpence and a Ride","Marrable’s Fair does not open on a Sunday. Goose Green was quiet, the stalls shuttered, the eleven horses of the roundabout hanging still under their canvas. So the whole Parish Committee had leisure to gather round a fallen lime limb and be asked a question by an old showman.\n\nJem Marrable wanted to buy it. Lime, he said, was the best carving wood there was, and this piece had a horse in it. The Committee conferred. Mr Pim had always favoured a bench. Mrs Garland favoured firewood for the almshouse. The Mayor favoured a further meeting. Wren Hallow, delivering Sunday rolls from the bakery, heard them settle at last on sixpence and one free ride apiece.\n\nJem paid on the spot. By the time the Committee had finished shaking hands with one another, he had fetched an adze and taken off the first long curl of bark."],["Rain on the Canvas","It rained all Monday. Marrable’s Fair sat on Goose Green under dripping awnings, the straw trodden to porridge, the coconut man playing patience on an upturned bucket. Wren Hallow had one penny left and nowhere dry to stand, so she ducked into the smallest tent, which had a painted sign: Madame Zephyrine Sees All.\n\nMadame Zephyrine was Mrs Marrable in a shawl with coins on it. She was turning the heel of a sock. She said fortunes were a penny, but she would not take a last one, and that she could tell it was a last one by the way it was being held.\n\nSo they drank tea instead, and listened. Behind the tent, under a tarpaulin stretched from the roundabout, came a steady knock and pause, knock and pause. That was Jem, Mrs Marrable said, finding out where the horse’s head was."],["A Horse in the Wood","By Tuesday of fair week the fallen lime limb from Goose Green had a neck. Old Jem Marrable, who carves the horses for his own roundabout, worked at it behind the Gallopers with a mallet and three gouges, and Wren Hallow came after the morning bake to sweep up for him.\n\nShe learned a good deal by sweeping. The horse would be a jumper, legs tucked, ears laid back as if it heard the organ. The head was made separately and pegged on. The shavings smelled of honey and were to go to the bakery for kindling, which Jem called a fair trade for rolls.\n\nAt noon the Parish Committee arrived to inspect their limb. Mr Pim thought the nose long. Mrs Garland wondered if it might smile. Jem thanked them, went on cutting, and changed nothing at all, and they left well pleased with their contribution."],["The Colour of a Crust","A roundabout horse is only timber until it is painted, and on the Wednesday of fair week the question of what colour to paint the new one nearly divided Emberwick. It had been carved on Goose Green by old Jem Marrable from the town’s own lime tree. The town felt entitled to a view.\n\nThe Mayor proposed civic blue. The fishermen up from the Grey Coast said grey, like weather. Somebody’s aunt held out for piebald. Bess Marrable, who mixes the fair’s paint in jam jars, listened with a brush behind her ear and then asked Wren Hallow, who had swept up every shaving and said nothing.\n\nWren thought. Brown, she said. The brown of a loaf when it is just right, with a mane the colour of the inside. Bess looked at the horse, then at the bakery chimney across the Green, and reached for the burnt umber."],["The White of the Eye","On Thursday evening the twelfth horse of Marrable’s roundabout stood on two trestles behind the fair, loaf-brown, with a cream mane and a saddle of green and gold. Wren Hallow, the baker’s daughter, had come every day to watch it made. It had everything now but a name and a look in its eye.\n\nBess Marrable lettered the name on the neck scroll, freehand, in gold with a red shadow: Emberwick. Then old Jem did a thing Wren did not expect. He loaded the smallest brush with white, put it in her hand, and pointed. One dot, he said, high in the dark of the eye. Not the middle. That was the difference between a horse and a hat stand.\n\nHer hand shook. She breathed out, as she did before slashing a loaf, and touched the brush down once. The horse looked at her."],["First Ride","The last night of the Goose Fair is always the best, and this one had a new horse in it. At dusk on Friday four showmen carried the loaf-brown jumper round to the Gallopers and hung it on the brass pole that had gone up and down empty all week. Twelve horses at last, on a roundabout built for twelve.\n\nThen came the matter of who should ride it first. The Mayor felt the office spoke for itself. The Parish Committee produced their free tickets and a point of order. The custom of the fair, said old Jem Marrable, was that a new horse is first ridden by whoever swept up after it.\n\nWren Hallow paid her last penny anyway, on principle. The organ struck up, the lamps blurred into one gold ring, and the horse called Emberwick carried her round above the hats of the whole town."],["The Ring on the Grass","A fair goes faster than it comes. By ten on Saturday morning Marrable’s had folded itself back into seven wagons, and Wren Hallow stood at the gate of Goose Green with a parcel of rolls for the road, which Bess Marrable was for once not allowed to pay for.\n\nThe engine whistled. The wagons swung out over the Ember bridge towards the Lowlands, nose to tail. On the last one, under the tarpaulin, a brown hoof stuck out. Old Jem lifted his hat from the tailboard and called that she should look for them in the last week of September, and bring her sweeping arm.\n\nThen the road was empty. On the Green there was a wide pale ring where the roundabout had stood, and a clean scar on the lime tree where the limb had been. Wren walked once round the ring, slowly, and went home to the ovens."]],[["The Sixty-First Fire","Annals of the Emberwick Bonfire Society, kept by Ambrose Tuck, Recorder, newly elected, there being no other candidate.\n\nFor those who come after: on the last night of October the Society lights a fire on Beacon Head. It does so in memory of the Long Fog, when the herring fleet was fogbound off the Grey Coast and the town burned its own chairs and tables on the headland to bring the boats home. Each household still gives one piece of old furniture to the stack. That is the whole of the custom, and the Committee has forty-one rules concerning it.\n\nSixty-first fire. Weather clear, wind light, westerly. Stack fourteen feet, measured by the Treasurer with a marked pole. Lit at seven. A fire was also observed across the bay on the Nab, where the village of Skerrin keeps the same night. It appeared to us somewhat smaller."],["Mrs Pargeter's Sideboard","Annals of the Emberwick Bonfire Society. Sixty-second fire on Beacon Head, last night of October. Ambrose Tuck, Recorder. Weather cold and still. Stack fifteen feet.\n\nThe Society must record an incident. By custom each household gives one piece of worn-out furniture to the fire. Mr Pargeter of Tanner’s Row gave a sideboard. It emerged at twenty minutes to seven, by way of Mrs Pargeter arriving on the headland at some speed, that the sideboard was neither worn out nor his to give, being her mother’s, and having her best tablecloths still in the left-hand drawer.\n\nSix members took the stack down to the fourth layer and got it out. The fire was lit forty minutes late. The Committee has since passed Rule Forty-Two: no gift accepted unless countersigned by every adult of the household. Mr Pargeter gave a footstool instead, and was not thanked."],["The Wet Year","Annals of the Emberwick Bonfire Society, which each last night of October burns old furniture on Beacon Head in memory of the Long Fog. Sixty-third fire. Ambrose Tuck, Recorder.\n\nIt rained for nine days beforehand and through the evening itself. The stack, thirteen feet and sodden, was offered a light at seven. It declined. It declined again at half past, and at eight, by which hour Skerrin’s fire across the bay was burning clearly on the Nab, and their chapel bell was heard ringing, we thought unnecessarily.\n\nAt ten past nine the Treasurer went down to the Society’s cupboard and returned with eleven years of his own accounts, which he said nobody had ever asked to see. They were dry. The fire caught at nine-forty and burned till two. The Recorder notes that these annals were not offered, and will in future be kept in a different cupboard."],["A Question of Feet","Annals of the Emberwick Bonfire Society. Sixty-fourth fire, Beacon Head, last night of October. Recorder, Ambrose Tuck. Fine and frosty. Stack sixteen feet, our tallest in living memory.\n\nWord then came by the carrier that Skerrin, whose fire stands on the Nab across the bay, was claiming eighteen. The Committee does not concern itself with rivalry. It did, however, send the Treasurer and his marked pole round by the coast road the next morning, purely in the interests of accuracy.\n\nHis report is entered in full. The Skerrin stack had stood on a natural hummock of some four feet, which their Committee had counted in. Their own Treasurer, met on the spot, replied that Beacon Head is ninety feet above the sea and nobody had ever asked us to subtract it. The two gentlemen measured each other’s poles, found a difference of one inch, and parted without settling whose."],["Three Hundred Potatoes","Annals of the Emberwick Bonfire Society, concerning the sixty-fifth fire lit on Beacon Head on the last night of October. Kept by Ambrose Tuck, Recorder. Weather mild. Stack fifteen feet.\n\nThis year a Refreshments Sub-Committee was formed, consisting of Miss Dunnock and whoever stood nearest. Three hundred potatoes were pushed into the edge of the embers at eight o’clock with a rake. At nine it was found that nobody had marked where. A general search by lantern recovered forty-four, of which the Committee ate eleven in the course of confirming they were done.\n\nThe remainder were discovered at first light by the children of the town, who needed no sub-committee and found every one, cold, black, and by their account perfect. Parkin was also served. Rule Forty-Three now requires that potatoes be counted in and counted out. Skerrin, we hear, had sausages."],["The Hedgehog Inspection","Annals of the Emberwick Bonfire Society. Sixty-sixth fire. Ambrose Tuck, Recorder. Dry, with a sharp east wind. Stack fifteen feet and, in the Recorder’s view, the best built yet.\n\nA stack of old furniture standing three weeks on Beacon Head is, to a hedgehog, a furnished house. Miss Dunnock therefore proposed that the whole be searched before lighting, and conducted the search herself on the last afternoon of October with a lantern, a long spoon and a lined basket. The Society records her findings: three hedgehogs, asleep in a chest of drawers; one tabby cat, not asleep, and of the opinion that it lived there; and Mr Pim of this Committee, also asleep, who had sat down in a donated armchair at noon to guard the stack.\n\nAll were removed to safety. The Hedgehog Inspection is now Rule Forty-Four. The fire was lit at seven and burned cleanly."],["The Old Question","Annals of the Emberwick Bonfire Society, sixty-seventh fire. Ambrose Tuck, Recorder. Weather fair. Stack fifteen feet.\n\nBoth this town and the village of Skerrin across the bay light a fire each last night of October, we on Beacon Head and they on the Nab, and each has always held that it was their fire that brought the herring boats home in the Long Fog. This year the Skerrin schoolmaster printed a pamphlet saying so. The Recorder accordingly spent three evenings in Emberwick Library with the harbour book of that year.\n\nThe entry is short. It gives the date, the fog, the number of boats, and then, in the harbourmaster’s hand: all came in on the fires. The Recorder notes the word fires. He takes it for a slip of the pen, the man having been up all night, and has written to the schoolmaster to say the matter is closed."],["Mr Flint's Fountain","Annals of the Emberwick Bonfire Society. Sixty-eighth fire on Beacon Head, last night of October. Kept by Ambrose Tuck, Recorder. Clear, no wind. Stack fourteen feet.\n\nIt was put to the Committee that a plain fire was old-fashioned, and that Mr Flint the chemist could provide a firework. The vote was five to four. Mr Flint arrived with a single item the size of a tea caddy, named by him the Emberwick Fountain, and asked everyone to stand a long way back, which they did.\n\nThe Fountain gave a cough, a quantity of pink smoke, and one green star, which rose about a yard, considered Beacon Head, and went out. There was polite applause. Mr Flint bowed. The fire was then lit in the ordinary manner and was greatly admired, and the Committee has resolved by nine votes to none that the Society’s business is bonfires."],["The Lean Year","Annals of the Emberwick Bonfire Society, which burns old furniture on Beacon Head each last night of October. Sixty-ninth fire. Ambrose Tuck, Recorder.\n\nAfter sixty-eight fires the town has run short of furniture it can spare. This year’s gifts came to a hat stand, two stools and a clothes horse. The Committee looked at them for some time. The Recorder then gave his late aunt’s whatnot, the Treasurer a desk, and Miss Dunnock a chair she said had never liked her. The stack reached nine feet.\n\nTwo days before the night, a wagon came round the coast road from Skerrin, loaded with driftwood, and a card: with the compliments of the Nab. The Committee debated for an hour whether this was kindness or comment. It was accepted by one vote. The Recorder must state that it burned blue and green at the edges, and was the handsomest fire in years."],["Applewood for the Nab","Annals of the Emberwick Bonfire Society. Seventieth fire on Beacon Head. Ambrose Tuck, Recorder. Still and starry. Stack fourteen feet, the town having turned out its attics for the anniversary.\n\nMindful of the driftwood sent us last year by the village of Skerrin, whose own fire burns on the Nab across the bay, the Committee voted to return the courtesy. A cart of applewood from the Lowlands orchards went round by the coast road, with a card. The wording of the card took two meetings. It reads: from Beacon Head.\n\nA joint supper of the two societies was proposed, and debated warmly as to which side of the bay should have it. It was held on neither. But at eight o’clock the wind brought the smell of apple smoke across the water from the Nab, and the Recorder observed several members of this Committee standing quite still, facing it."],["A Hand with the Book","Annals of the Emberwick Bonfire Society, seventy-first fire, Beacon Head, last night of October. Weather kind. The Recorder, Ambrose Tuck, did not measure the stack this year and finds he does not mind.\n\nHe has kept this book eleven years, and the path up the headland has grown steeper in that time, though the Committee assures him it has not. He therefore asked for an assistant. Miss Nell Pargeter of Tanner’s Row was appointed, being the only applicant and the best. She carried the book and the lantern up, and he carried himself.\n\nFor her use he sets down what he knows. Build it loose in the middle. Chairs burn better than tables. Light it on the windward side and then leave it alone. The rest is in the rules, of which there are now forty-six, and she need not learn them all at once."],["Both Fires","Annals of the Emberwick Bonfire Society. Seventy-second fire. Fog came in off the Grey Coast at dusk, the thickest in memory, and the herring boat Second Thoughts was still out.\n\nWe lit at seven on Beacon Head. Skerrin lit on the Nab. Theirs was a soft orange blur across the bay, and ours, they tell us, the same. At nine the boat came in between them, slow and quite safe, late for nothing but supper. Her skipper, Dan Corrie, was puzzled to be asked how. One fire on each shoulder, he said, and you are in the channel. With one alone you go round the bay till morning. His grandfather had it from his.\n\nSo it took both, and always did. The harbour book said fires. The Recorder, Ambrose Tuck, was wrong for five years and is glad of it. This is his last entry. Nell Pargeter has the book."]],[["Last in Line","My name is Odo Fenn. I am a tinsmith, and on the longest night of the year I am the Tail-Lamp of the Emberwick Lantern Parade, which means I walk last. Somebody must. A parade without a back end goes on losing children until morning.\n\nIt is a quarter to five and dark at Fell Gate, at the top of the town. They are gathering. Turnip lanterns, paper stars on sticks, a tin moon I made for the schoolmistress. I have forty candle ends in my left pocket and a tinderbox in my right, and my own lamp, which is plain, with a horn window and a good hood.\n\nTwenty years I have done this. We go down through the town to the Ember bridge, every lantern lit, and I count them out at the top and in at the bottom. Tonight I make it two hundred and six."],["One Flame from the Forge","Every lantern in the Emberwick parade is lit from one flame. That is the rule, and I, Odo Fenn, who walk at the back as Tail-Lamp, am the one who enforces it, mostly by frowning.\n\nThe flame comes from the forge at Fell Gate. The smith brings out a single taper, shielding it with a hand like a shovel, and lights the lantern nearest the door. That one lights two more. Those light four. I stand on the horse trough and watch it spread down the lane, yellow dot by yellow dot, until the dark at the top of the town has gone soft and gold and smells of hot turnip.\n\nIt takes eleven minutes. A boy from Chandler’s Yard tries to use his own match, and I see him, and he knows I see him. He blows it out and queues. The last lantern lit is mine."],["The Owl and the Pike","There are two great lanterns in the Emberwick parade, and they do not get on. I am Odo Fenn, the Tail-Lamp, last in the line, and from the back I have the best view of the quarrel.\n\nFell Gate builds the Owl. She is willow and paper, nine feet tall, with six carriers inside and eyes that glow amber. The Quay builds the Pike, fourteen feet of green tissue on hoops, carried on poles by eight fishermen so that it swims. Each has led the parade. Each holds that it ought to lead it every year. Nobody has written down whose turn it is, on purpose.\n\nTonight they settle it as usual, by standing side by side in the mouth of Stair Lane, which is wide enough for one. The Committee is sent for. I sit down on the horse trough with my lamp between my boots, and wait."],["Order of March","The Lantern Parade Committee meets once a year, in the street, in the dark, on the longest night, to decide who goes first. I have watched it for twenty years from the back, where I walk as Tail-Lamp, and it has never taken less than a quarter of an hour.\n\nThe Owl of Fell Gate and the Pike of the Quay stand jammed shoulder to fin at the top of Stair Lane. Mrs Garland, presiding, proposes alphabetical order. The Quay asks whether that is O for Owl or F for Fell Gate. Mr Pim consults last year’s minutes by the light of a turnip and finds they say only: resolved, after discussion.\n\nTwo hundred lanterns are burning down. In the end a boy of six with a jam jar on a string is asked to choose. He points at the Pike, because it is green. We move off."],["Under the Baker's Sign","We are a third of the way down. I am Odo Fenn, tinsmith, walking last in the Emberwick Lantern Parade with my lamp and my pocket of candle ends, and ahead of me the whole line has stopped again.\n\nIt is the Owl. Fell Gate has built her nine feet tall this year, and the bakery sign on Cross Street hangs at eight and a half. She is wedged under it by the ears. The six carriers inside cannot see out and are being given advice by forty people at once. Mr Hallow the baker leans from his upstairs window in his nightcap and offers to butter her.\n\nIn the end the carriers kneel, all six together, and the Owl bows low to the bakery and shuffles under. One ear is bent. From the Pike’s crew, waiting below, comes a cheer that is not entirely kind."],["The Corner by the Library","The wind lives at the corner by Emberwick Library. Every year it waits there for the Lantern Parade, and every year I, Odo Fenn, who walk last as the Tail-Lamp, come round that corner to find a third of the lanterns out and the children holding them up to me like empty cups.\n\nThis is the work. I kneel with my back to the gust, open the horn window of my lamp, and light them one from another: a paper star, two turnips, a fish on a stick. Eleven candle ends go from my pocket. Somebody inside the library has set a lamp in the long window, and it is a help to see by.\n\nThe last is a jam jar on a string, belonging to a boy of six named Pip Garland. His wick is drowned in wax. I pick it clear with a pin, and it takes."],["Soup in the Square","Halfway down the town the Lantern Parade halts in Market Square for soup. I am the Tail-Lamp, Odo Fenn, and I arrive last, as I am meant to, when the best of the dumplings have gone.\n\nThe square is a bowl of light. Two hundred lanterns are propped on the steps of the cross, hung from the pump, held between knees. The town band plays something slow, with the cornet a little ahead of the rest. The Owl of Fell Gate and the Pike of the Quay have been stood at opposite ends, like relations at a wedding, and each crew is explaining to its neighbours how the other one was built wrong.\n\nPip Garland, who is six and has walked beside me since the library, holds my lamp while I eat. He holds it with both hands and does not look away from it once. I notice that."],["Out of Candles","Below Market Square the Emberwick Lantern Parade goes down Quay Hill, which is steep and faces the sea wind. I walk last, being the Tail-Lamp, and so I am the one who sees the Pike go dark.\n\nIt happens a hoop at a time. The Quay’s green fish has fourteen candles in it, and the long wait at the top of the town has burned them to nothing. Head, then fins, then the tail goes grey, and eight fishermen stand in the road holding fourteen feet of unlit paper. I have been handing out candle ends all night and have nine left. I give them all. It needs five more.\n\nNobody speaks. The belly of the Owl opens, and a Fell Gate carrier climbs out with five of her own candles in his fist. He says the Owl sees well enough in the dark. I say nothing about it."],["Rain at the Bridge Foot","It begins to rain as the head of the Lantern Parade reaches the flat by the river. I am Odo Fenn, the Tail-Lamp, last of all, with a boy of six and his jam-jar lantern walking at my elbow, and we both turn our collars up.\n\nRain is hard on paper. The stars go first, then the paper drum from the school. Tin and turnip and glass do better. Ahead of us the Owl of Fell Gate, who lent five of her candles to her old rival on Quay Hill, is sagging at one wing, and I watch two of the Pike’s fishermen leave their poles, without being asked, and get a shoulder under it.\n\nSo they come to the Ember bridge like that, the Owl held up by the Quay and the Pike lit by Fell Gate, arguing the whole way about whose fault the weather is."],["Wide Enough for One","The Ember bridge is the end of the Lantern Parade, and it is wide enough for one great lantern at a time. I know, because I am the Tail-Lamp and have stood at the back of this argument for twenty years.\n\nTonight there is no argument. The Owl of Fell Gate has a broken wing and the Pike of the Quay is carrying it. They cannot be got apart without dropping somebody. So they go onto the bridge together, sideways, the Owl first by a beak and the Pike wrapped round her like a scarf, and the whole town goes quiet, and then laughs, and then claps.\n\nMrs Garland of the Committee asks me, as the oldest officer present, which of them led. I tell her I was at the back and could not see. It is entered in the minutes that way."],["Counting Them In","On the longest night of the year, Emberwick hangs its lanterns along the parapet of the Ember bridge, so that the river carries the light down to the sea and the dark knows it is expected to turn. I am Odo Fenn, Tail-Lamp of the parade. I count the lanterns out at the top of the town and in at the bottom.\n\nTwo hundred and six went out. I walk the parapet in the rain. Turnips, tin moons, the paper ones gone to lace but still hung up. The Owl and the Pike are propped together at the middle arch, steaming gently. Two hundred and five.\n\nI count again. Then something knocks against my knee. Pip Garland, aged six, is holding up his jam jar, still alight, never once out since the library. He could not reach the parapet. I lift him, and he hangs it himself."],["Putting Out the Lamp","The parade is over, and the Tail-Lamp walks home last. That is me, Odo Fenn, tinsmith, going back up through Emberwick alone at ten o’clock on the longest night, with the rain easing and the bridge behind me still gold from end to end.\n\nThere is work even now. On Quay Hill I find a mitten, and in Market Square a cornet mouthpiece and a child’s scarf. At the corner by the library the wind has dropped. The lamp in the long window is still burning, and I lift my hat to it.\n\nTwenty years is a fair turn. Next winter I will make a second lamp, small, with a horn window and a good hood, for a boy who can keep a jam jar alight in the rain. At my own door on Fell Gate I open my lamp and blow it out. Tomorrow is longer by a minute."]],[["Five White Potatoes","I am Ivy Tarn, of the Lowlands. I have judged flower shows for thirty-one summers, and I was told on the platform at Emberwick that theirs is a friendly one. I have learned to count my pencils when I hear that.\n\nThe tent stands on the water meadow by the Ember. By nine the Secretary, a Mrs Thrupp with a whistle, had cleared out the exhibitors, and I was alone with forty-one classes, a steward, and the smell of trodden grass and onions.\n\nClass One, five white potatoes. People think one judges size. One judges sameness: five potatoes like five brothers, clean-skinned, shallow in the eye. The best dish belonged to a Mr Septimus Cray. I wrote First on the card. The steward breathed in sharply and said Mrs Lobb would have something to say. I asked who Mrs Lobb was. He only looked towards the marrows."],["The Longest Bean","Class Six at the Emberwick Flower Show is for the longest runner bean, and I, Ivy Tarn, the visiting judge, measured nineteen of them before ten o’clock with a steel rule, which I carry in my own bag.\n\nA long bean is a simple thing to judge and a hard thing to judge fairly. Growers hang weights on them. They bind them to laths. One bean on the bench was straight as a curtain rod and still showed the marks of the string. I did not mark it down. The schedule says longest, not most natural, and a judge who invents rules is worse than an exhibitor who bends them.\n\nIt measured twenty-two inches and a quarter. First, to Mrs Hetty Lobb. Mr Cray’s bean, at twenty-two exactly, took Second. The steward told me the two of them have not spoken since a certain marrow, forty years ago."],["Dahlias and an Earwig","Class Twelve, six dahlias, any variety. I am Ivy Tarn, judge for the day at the Emberwick Flower Show, and by half past ten I had reached the cut flowers, where the tent smells less of onion.\n\nThere were seven vases. Six were good. The seventh was the kind of exhibit a judge sees perhaps one year in five: six blooms of a dark red, every petal in its place, the stems dead straight in a plain green vase. I bent to look into the heart of the largest, and an earwig walked out, crossed the petals, and regarded me. I have never marked down for an earwig. He had as much right there as I did.\n\nFirst, without difficulty. The card under the vase said Mrs H. Lobb. I began to see that I would be hearing two names all day, hers and Mr Cray’s, turn and turn about."],["The Heaviest Marrow","There is a class at every show that the town really comes for. At Emberwick it is Class Twenty, the heaviest marrow, and I, Ivy Tarn, who had come from the Lowlands to judge, was warned about it by four separate people before I reached the bench.\n\nMr Septimus Cray and Mrs Hetty Lobb have taken First and Second in it, in one order or the other, for forty years. Their marrows lay side by side on straw like two green pigs. The steward and I lifted each onto the scale. Cray, thirty-eight pounds nine ounces. Lobb, thirty-eight pounds seven.\n\nThere was a third. It was pale, lopsided and enormous, and its card was written in pencil: T. Wicket, aged 11. Forty-one pounds three ounces. I weighed it twice more, because I could see what it was going to cost somebody. Then I wrote First on the pencilled card."],["Not According to Schedule","The hardest thing a show judge writes is three letters. I am Ivy Tarn, and I wrote them at Emberwick on a Saturday in August, at Class Twenty-Four, sweet peas, nine stems.\n\nThe best vase on the bench was not in doubt. Lavender and cream, long in the stem, four flowers to each and still with the dew on. They belonged to a Mr Anselm Pye, who the steward said had shown sweet peas for thirty years and never had a card. I counted the stems for form’s sake. Then I counted again. Ten.\n\nThe schedule says nine. An exhibit with ten is Not According to Schedule and cannot be placed. I did not make the rule and I will not break it. I wrote N.A.S. on his card in small letters and gave First to a decent vase of pink. Then I stood there longer than a judge should."],["The Aspidistra","Class Thirty at the Emberwick Flower Show is for a pot plant, foliage, and it had three entries, which in a class with three prizes takes some of the strain off a judge. I am that judge: Ivy Tarn, thirty-one years at it.\n\nThere was a fern. There was a young palm. And there was an aspidistra in a brown glazed pot, taller than the steward, with a luggage label tied to it that said: Property of the Station Waiting Room. Please Return. He told me it had been shown every year since the show began. It has never won. It is always Third. People, he said, would be upset by anything else.\n\nI looked it over properly all the same. The leaves had been washed, I think with milk. The fern was the better plant and took First. The aspidistra took Third, and seemed to me to expect it."],["Gardens on Plates","I have judged in the Lowlands for thirty-one years, and I, Ivy Tarn, do not hand out prizes to be kind. So when the Secretary of the Emberwick Flower Show told me that in Class Thirty-Six, a miniature garden on a dinner plate, exhibitors under ten, it was the custom to give every child a First, I said that I could not.\n\nThere were fourteen plates. Moss lawns, a pond made of a pocket mirror, a gravel path of lentils. One had a live snail, which during judging left its own garden and began on the next.\n\nI took an hour over them, longer than over the marrows. Then I wrote fourteen cards by hand, each different and each true. Best Pond. Straightest Path. Bravest Use of Moss. Most Generous Snail. Mrs Thrupp read them through twice and said it was irregular. Then she pinned them up herself."],["Twenty-Three Pots of Jam","A flower show judge at a small show is expected to judge the jam as well. I am Ivy Tarn; I was handed a teaspoon at noon in the tent at Emberwick, and shown Class Forty, one pot of soft-fruit jam, twenty-three entries.\n\nOne tastes each. There is no other way. One looks first: the set, the colour against the light, whether the fruit has sunk. Then a little on the spoon, and a sip of water between. By the fifteenth pot, raspberry and strawberry had become the same word. The sixteenth was gooseberry, sharp and green-gold, and woke me up.\n\nFirst to the gooseberry, entered by a Mr S. Cray, whose name I had already met among the potatoes, beans and marrows. I remarked to the steward that the man could evidently cook. He said Mr Cray had lived alone forty years and had had to learn."],["An Objection Is Lodged","At one o’clock, with the judging done and the tent still closed, the Committee of the Emberwick Flower Show met behind the tea urn to hear an objection. I, Ivy Tarn, the judge, was asked to attend. I brought my sandwich.\n\nThe objection was against the winning marrow in Class Twenty, grown by Tom Wicket, aged eleven. It was lodged by Mr Septimus Cray and seconded by Mrs Hetty Lobb, and the Secretary remarked that this was the first thing the two of them had agreed upon in forty years. Their ground was that the marrow had been raised on the compost heap behind the railway station, which was not a garden.\n\nMrs Thrupp read out Rule Three: grown by the exhibitor. Nothing about where. The Committee voted. The objection failed, seven to none, with the objectors abstaining side by side, wearing the same expression."],["The Tent Opens","The public is let into the Emberwick Flower Show at two o’clock, and a judge with any sense is out of the tent by five to. I, Ivy Tarn, stayed. I wanted to see the marrow bench.\n\nIt was hot under the canvas, and the wasps had found the jam. People went straight to the cards, as they always do, and only afterwards looked at the flowers. At the marrows stood a small boy with a First card, and on either side of him the two growers he had beaten, Mr Cray and Mrs Lobb, who had not exchanged a word since long before he was born.\n\nThey were questioning him about feeding. Washing-up water, said Tom Wicket, and the tea leaves from the station. Mr Cray wrote that down. Mrs Lobb asked Mr Cray, quite sharply, for the loan of his pencil. He gave it to her."],["Shillings in Envelopes","Prizes at the Emberwick Flower Show are given out at four, from a trestle by the tent door: a shilling in an envelope for a First, sixpence for a Second, and four silver cups that go home for a year and come back polished. I am Ivy Tarn, who judged it. I was asked to hand them over, and did.\n\nMr Cray came up for his potatoes and his jam. Mrs Lobb came up for her bean and her dahlias. The Station Waiting Room was called for its aspidistra, and a porter collected the threepence. Fourteen children received a shilling each and compared cards all the way back.\n\nThen the Marrow Tankard, pewter, forty years on one of two mantelpieces. Tom Wicket needed both arms for it. Mr Cray and Mrs Lobb led the clapping, slightly out of time, each watching to see that the other did not stop first."],["The Tenth Stem","The last prize at the Emberwick Flower Show is the cup for Best in Show, and I, Ivy Tarn, the judge, gave it to Mrs Hetty Lobb’s six red dahlias. Mr Cray carried the cup to her cart, her arms being full. They were talking about tea leaves.\n\nOne thing remained. Rule Nineteen allows the judge a single card of her own, for any exhibit, in or out of schedule. Mr Anselm Pye’s sweet peas had been ruled out for having ten stems where the class wanted nine. I took out the tenth, stood it in an egg cup, and set my card against it. He read it for a long time.\n\nI caught the six o’clock train. On the seat beside me sat a marrow, a small one, for eating, with a label in two different hands. I have judged friendlier shows. I have not judged a better."]],[["Mr Pym of the Attic","At Number Nine, Candlewick Row, in the town of Emberwick, there lived a ghost named Mr Osbert Pym. He was tall and thin and the colour of a clean window, and he had the best manners on the whole Grey Coast.\n\nMr Pym never said boo. He thought it a rude thing to say to a person one had not been introduced to. He never rattled chains, because of the noise, and he never walked through a door without knocking first, which is harder than it sounds when your knuckles go straight through the wood.\n\nThe house had stood empty for nine winters. Mr Pym kept it nicely. Each evening he drifted from room to room, straightening the pictures and wishing the mice good night. Then he went up to the attic, hung his hat on a moonbeam, and wished for somebody to be polite to."],["The Larkspurs Arrive","One wet Thursday in March a cart stopped outside Number Nine, Candlewick Row, and a family got down from it. There was Mrs Larkspur, who carried a clock. There was Mr Larkspur, who carried everything else. There was Tansy, who was seven, and a baby called Bram, who was mostly blanket.\n\nUp in the attic, Mr Osbert Pym, the ghost of the house, watched through a crack in the floor and was thrown into a dreadful fluster. Company, after nine winters. And nothing dusted.\n\nHe longed to go down and say how do you do. But a ghost cannot simply appear in front of people. It startles them, and startling is bad manners. So Mr Pym stayed where he was, wringing his hat, and listened to the lovely noise of boots on his stairs. Somebody dropped a saucepan. He very nearly called out “Bless you.”"],["A Card in the Frost","Mr Osbert Pym was a ghost, the politest in Emberwick, and for three days he had been sharing his house on Candlewick Row with a family who did not know he was there. This troubled him. It felt like eavesdropping.\n\nA gentleman, he decided, leaves his card. Mr Pym had no cards. He did have cold fingers. So on the third night he floated down to the landing window, breathed on the glass to chill it, and wrote in the frost with one careful fingertip. It took him until dawn, because he kept rubbing out the wobbly letters and beginning again.\n\nWhen he had finished, it said: Mr O. Pym. The Attic. At home on Tuesdays. Please do not trouble to knock. Then he read it over and added, smaller, underneath: I hope the stairs have not been creaking. I have spoken to them."],["Tansy Writes Back","Tansy Larkspur, who was seven, found the writing on the landing window before breakfast. Frost had spelled out a calling card from a Mr O. Pym of the attic, and the sun was already nibbling the edges of it.\n\nTansy was not a girl who screamed. She was a girl who fetched a pencil. She copied the card onto the back of a flour bag, and then she sat on the top stair and wrote an answer in her best joined-up hand. Dear Mr Pym, it said. We are the Larkspurs. I am Tansy. Tuesday is tomorrow. I will come at four o’clock if that is convenient. Do ghosts take tea?\n\nShe pushed it under the attic door. On the other side, a ghost who had not had a letter in a very long time read it eleven times, and then went to look for his better hat."],["Tuesday at Four","At four o’clock on Tuesday, Tansy Larkspur climbed the attic stairs of Number Nine, Candlewick Row, carrying two cups of milk on a tray, because nobody would let her have the teapot.\n\nThere was a small cough from inside the old wardrobe. Then a knock, from the inside, three polite taps. “Come out,” said Tansy, which is not what people usually say to wardrobes. Out came Mr Osbert Pym, hat first. He was so tall that he had to stoop under the rafters, and Tansy could see the wallpaper roses straight through his waistcoat.\n\n“I do apologise for the view,” said the ghost. “I am afraid I am rather see-through. It is not deliberate.” Tansy said she liked the roses better that way. Mr Pym could not drink his milk, but he held the cup for a whole hour and said it was the nicest he had ever held."],["Trouble With Doors","Mr Osbert Pym, the ghost in the attic of the Larkspurs’ house, had one friend now, a girl of seven named Tansy. He decided to be useful to the whole family.\n\nHe began with doors. Whenever a Larkspur came along with full hands, Mr Pym swept the door open and bowed. Nobody but Tansy could see the bow. They saw only a door opening by itself. Mr Larkspur blamed the hinges and oiled every one. Mrs Larkspur blamed the wind and wedged them all with folded paper. Dumpling the cat blamed nobody, but would no longer go upstairs.\n\n“Perhaps,” said Tansy, “you could do something quieter.” So in the evenings Mr Pym went back to straightening the pictures, and at night, when baby Bram grizzled, he hummed an old slow tune by the cradle until the grizzling stopped. Nobody thanked him. He did not mind in the least."],["A Call From Mrs Gale","Not every ghost in Emberwick is a polite one. One blowy night Mrs Henrietta Gale, who haunts the windmill on the marsh road and can be heard doing it from the river, came down the chimney of Number Nine, Candlewick Row, to call on Mr Osbert Pym.\n\nShe looked round his tidy attic and sniffed. All these years in the house, and a family downstairs who slept right through till morning! It was a disgrace to the profession. A ghost should moan. A ghost should clank. She clanked for him, to show how, and dust fell out of the rafters.\n\nMr Pym said that he would try. When she had gone he stood at the top of the stairs, cleared his throat, and called down into the dark: “Woo. If it is not inconvenient.” Nobody woke. Dumpling the cat opened one eye, and shut it again."],["The Polite Thing","Mr Osbert Pym was a ghost who worried, mostly about his manners. In the attic of the Larkspurs’ house on Candlewick Row he found a new worry and could not put it down.\n\nThe doors he had opened had cost Mr Larkspur a whole tin of oil. The cat would not use the stairs. And Mrs Gale, the loud ghost from the windmill, had told him that a house with a family in it was no place for a ghost who could not even moan. Perhaps he was simply in the way.\n\nA guest who is in the way, thought Mr Pym, ought to leave before he is asked. That was the polite thing. So he wrote a note in the frost for Tansy, thanking her for the milk, put on his hat, and went out by the front door. He knocked first, out of habit."],["Rain at the Bandstand","A ghost with no house to haunt is a sorry sight, if you can see him at all. Mr Osbert Pym, late of Number Nine, Candlewick Row, spent his first night away under the bandstand in Emberwick’s river gardens. It rained sideways, and the rain went straight through him, which tickled.\n\nHe tried to make the best of it. He wished the ducks good evening. He straightened the notice about keeping off the grass. He told himself the Larkspurs would be far more comfortable without doors that opened by themselves.\n\nBack on Candlewick Row, though, nobody was comfortable at all. The pictures had gone crooked, every one. The stairs had started creaking again. And Tansy Larkspur, aged seven, stood on the landing in her nightgown reading a goodbye written in frost, with her chin stuck out the way it went when she had decided something."],["The Missing Hum","On the second night after the ghost left Number Nine, Candlewick Row, baby Bram Larkspur would not sleep. Mrs Larkspur walked him up and down. Mr Larkspur sang, which made things worse. It was Tansy who said what was wrong. “He wants the humming.”\n\nHer parents looked at each other. There had been a humming, low and old-fashioned, every night by the cradle. Each had supposed it was the other. So Tansy told them everything: the frost card, the Tuesdays, Mr Osbert Pym and his see-through waistcoat. Mrs Larkspur sat down on the stairs. Then she got up again and found her best writing paper.\n\nThe Larkspur family, said the invitation, request the pleasure of Mr Pym’s company at home, for as long as he likes. Tansy buttoned it inside her coat and went out to look in every polite place she could think of."],["Properly Introduced","Tansy Larkspur found the ghost at the bandstand in the river gardens, sitting on the conductor’s step, wet through in a way that did not show. She held out the invitation. Mr Osbert Pym read it twice, took off his hat, and for a moment could not say anything at all.\n\nThey walked home to Candlewick Row together. At the door of Number Nine he hung back. “I have not been introduced,” he said. So Tansy did it properly. “Mother, Father, this is Mr Pym. Mr Pym, my mother and father, and that is Bram.”\n\nOnce they had been told where to look, they could see him quite well: a tall gentleman the colour of a clean window, bowing. Mrs Larkspur said they had wondered who kept the pictures so straight. Mr Larkspur shook his hand, or shook the cold air where it was, and meant it."],["Boo, By Request","Mr Osbert Pym still lives in the attic of Number Nine, Candlewick Row, Emberwick, and on Tuesdays he comes down to tea. A chair is set for him between Tansy and the baby. He cannot drink his cup, but he says the steam is excellent, and he holds it until it goes cold, which with Mr Pym does not take long.\n\nHe opens no doors now unless asked. He hums to Bram each night, and the pictures have never hung so straight. Dumpling the cat sleeps on the attic stairs.\n\nOn Tansy’s eighth birthday she asked for one thing more. Mr Pym turned quite misty with embarrassment, but a guest does not refuse his hostess. He stood up, smoothed his waistcoat, and said it, once, very softly, with a small bow. “Boo.” Everybody clapped. It was, the Larkspurs agreed, the most polite boo ever heard on the Grey Coast."]],[["The Larder Door","This is the Emberwick Cold Spot Survey, and I am the surveyor, Hector Brindle, aged ten and three-quarters, of 14 Ropewalk. My assistant is my sister Posy, who is five and carries the thermometer because she cried. A cold spot is a place that is colder than it has any business being. Some people say ghosts cause them. I mean to find out, properly, with equipment.\n\nCold Spot No. 1. Place: our larder, bottom left, by the onions. Equipment: Gran’s jam thermometer, one feather (for draughts), one biscuit (in case a ghost is hungry, which would be evidence). The thermometer went down. The feather blew sideways. Nobody touched the biscuit except Posy.\n\nFinding: there is a gap under the larder door you could post a kipper through. Cause: draught. Ghost: none. I have written EXPLAINED in red. It is a start."],["The Third Stair","Emberwick Cold Spot Survey, kept by Hector Brindle, surveyor, with Posy Brindle, assistant (thermometer). We test cold places for draughts and ghosts and write down which.\n\nCold Spot No. 2. Place: Emberwick Library, the main staircase, third stair from the bottom. I found it by accident, going up for a book about volcanoes. One stair was like stepping into a pond. The stairs on either side were ordinary. Posy stood on it and said “Ooh” and would not get off. I held up the feather. It hung straight down. No draught at all, not under the stair, not from the window. The thermometer dropped so far that Miss Plumb the librarian came to see what we were doing and stayed to watch.\n\nFinding: none. Cause: unknown. I have put a question mark, in pencil, in case I need to rub it out. Surveyors do not like question marks."],["Mr Haddow’s Doorway","I am Hector Brindle, and this is my survey of the cold spots of Emberwick, with causes. So far: one draught, one question mark.\n\nCold Spot No. 3. Place: the doorway of Haddow’s fish shop on Quay Street. It is cold enough there to make your knees ache. I did not need the feather. Mr Haddow keeps his fish on a slab of ice as long as a rowing boat, and the cold rolls out of the door like something spilled. He let my sister Posy hold a crab. Finding: ice. Cause: fish. EXPLAINED, in red.\n\nBut I must put down one more thing, because a survey has to be honest. When we had walked on as far as the pump, out of reach of the ice, I felt cold on the back of my neck. The feather hung still. I have not given it a number. Yet."],["The Footbridge","Emberwick Cold Spot Survey. Surveyor: Hector Brindle, ten and three-quarters. Assistant: Posy Brindle, five, in charge of Gran’s jam thermometer.\n\nCold Spot No. 4. Place: the footbridge over the river Ember, in the middle, where you can see the weir. The air comes up off the water, and Gran says it goes through a coat like a needle. The feather blew about properly. EXPLAINED: river.\n\nExcept that it was not cold all over. I am sorry to keep doing this. It was colder on my left side than my right, a patch about the size of an open umbrella, and when I changed places with Posy the cold changed places too and stood on my left again. Posy says it likes that side because that is the side my notebook is on. I told her assistants do not have theories. I wrote it down anyway."],["Three Times Round","I am the surveyor of cold spots in Emberwick, Hector Brindle, and today I did an experiment instead of an entry, because suspicions have to be tested.\n\nThe suspicion: one cold spot is not staying in its place. It has been behind me at the pump and beside me on the footbridge. So I walked three times round the bandstand, with my sister Posy in the middle holding the thermometer.\n\nFirst lap, the cold was at my left elbow. Second lap, I ran, and it fell behind, and caught me up at the steps like somebody out of breath. Third lap, I stopped dead. It bumped into me. It was like being walked into by a pillow that had been kept in a cellar. Finding: Cold Spot No. 5 moves. It follows the surveyor. Posy says it is shy. For once I have let the assistant’s theory stand."],["Frost on the Biscuit","Emberwick Cold Spot Survey, by Hector Brindle with Posy Brindle. Most cold spots stay where they are put. No. 5 follows me about, so today we tried to catalogue it properly, in our back yard at 14 Ropewalk, where nobody would stare.\n\nSize: one open umbrella. Height: up to my shoulder. Temperature: like the inside of a letterbox in January. Feather: hangs still, so no draught. Smell: none, or a bit like snow.\n\nThen Posy held out the ghost biscuit. Nothing ate it. But frost grew across the top while we watched, in a pattern like ferns, and in the middle of the ferns there were letters. They were back to front. I held the biscuit up to Gran’s hand mirror and read them. HELLO. Finding: Cold Spot No. 5 can spell. Posy has named it Brr. I have written GHOST, in red, and underlined it."],["What Brr Wanted","My name is Hector Brindle. I survey the cold spots of Emberwick, and one of them, No. 5, is a ghost called Brr who follows me and writes in frost. Brr is the size of an umbrella and cannot be seen, only felt. My sister Posy says Brr is friendly and a bit sad.\n\nToday we asked questions at the bakery window on Oven Street, which steams up nicely. I asked why a ghost would follow a survey. The frost came slowly. LOST. Then, after Posy said please: NO SPOT.\n\nIt took the whole window to get the rest. Every cold place in the town belongs to something. The larder has its draught. The fish shop has its ice. The footbridge has the river. Brr had been all round Emberwick looking for an empty one. Finding: Cold Spot No. 5 has no address. A survey can fix that."],["Rooms to Let","Emberwick Cold Spot Survey. Surveyor Hector Brindle, assistant Posy Brindle, and one ghost, Brr, who is a cold spot with nowhere to live. This week we went house-hunting.\n\nFirst we tried our larder. Brr stayed ten minutes and wrote ONIONS on the butter dish, with the N backwards. Not suitable. Next, Haddow’s fish shop, which is cold already. But Mr Haddow walked through Brr eleven times while serving one customer, and Brr came out looking thin. I cannot see Brr. Posy told me about the thin.\n\nLast we tried the church porch. There is an old cold spot there already, No. 6, EXPLAINED by flagstones. It did not want a lodger. The frost on the boot scraper just said FULL. Finding: three places tried, none taken. Brr walked home on my left side, close, like a dog told off for something it did not do."],["The Question Mark","I am Hector Brindle of the Emberwick Cold Spot Survey, and I have been stupid, and a surveyor ought to write that down too.\n\nTonight I went back through the notebook. No. 1, the larder: draught. No. 3, the fish shop: ice. No. 4, the footbridge: river. And No. 2, the third stair in the library, with no cause and a question mark in pencil. I asked Brr, our ghost, who was sitting on the end of my bed making the blanket crackle: was that you? The frost came on the window. YES.\n\nBrr had been resting on that stair one afternoon, and I was the first person who ever stopped. Everybody else shivered and went on up. I stood there with a feather and wrote it down. So Brr followed the notebook. Finding: the question mark was a ghost all along. I have not rubbed it out."],["Surveyor in Bed","This page of the Emberwick Cold Spot Survey is written by Posy Brindle, assistant, age five. Hector is telling me the spellings. Hector has a cold. Not a cold spot. A nose one. Gran says he got it standing on bridges.\n\nHe has been in bed four days. Brr, who is our ghost and a cold spot, sat on the end of the bed. Then Gran said there was a draught fit to freeze a kettle and laid the draught sausage along the door. Brr thought the sausage was meant for it. So Brr went and sat out on the landing instead, all night.\n\nI took Brr my mitten. In the morning it had frost on it that said THANK. There was not room for YOU. Finding by me: Brr needs a place of its own where nobody puts a sausage. Hector says that is a good finding."],["A Word With Miss Plumb","I am Hector Brindle, surveyor of cold spots, and today the survey did something instead of only writing things down. We went to Emberwick Library, Posy and I and Brr, our ghost, who is a cold spot without a home.\n\nThe third stair was an ordinary stair. It had been ordinary ever since Brr left it to follow me. I showed Miss Plumb the librarian the whole notebook, No. 1 to No. 6, and the question mark. She read every page, not hurrying. Then she asked whether the ghost was a quiet one, and Posy said it only writes, and Miss Plumb said in that case it was better behaved than most of her borrowers.\n\nShe fetched a card and her good pen. Brr went and stood on the third stair, and the stair went cold as a pond, and the window above it frosted over in ferns."],["Survey Closed","Emberwick Cold Spot Survey, final page. Surveyor: Hector Brindle, eleven next month. Assistant: Posy Brindle, who can now read the thermometer by herself.\n\nCold spots catalogued: six. Explained: four, being draught, ice, river and flagstones. The other two turned out to be the same one. Resident: one. There is a card pinned beside the third stair in Emberwick Library, in Miss Plumb’s writing. It says: Cold Spot No. 2. Resident. Please mind the step, and say good afternoon.\n\nPeople do. We go on Saturdays. Posy hangs her mitten on the banister, and I read out the hottest pages of the volcano book, because Brr, our ghost, likes to be horrified. The window always says something. Last week it said HOME, with the E the right way round. Finding: none needed. Survey closed. Friends: one more than when I started."]],[["First Night, Twenty-Three Doors","Night log of Emberwick Old Hall. Kept by Ned Tarrow, watchman, formerly of the Ember ferry. First night.\n\nNine o’clock. Took over from Miss Vale, the curator, who gave me a lantern, a ring of twenty-three keys and a list of what they fit. The Hall is shut for mending until Midsummer and full of dust sheets. My orders are to walk it every two hours, lock what is open and write down what I find. Eleven o’clock. First round. Twenty-three doors, all sound. Scullery window found unlatched. Latched it.\n\nOne o’clock. Second round. I am certain I left the Long Gallery door open behind me to save my knees coming back. Found it shut and locked. I have the only keys. I counted them. Three o’clock. All quiet. A ferryman is used to being the only one awake. Tonight I am not sure I was."],["The Warm Teapot","Night log, Emberwick Old Hall. Ned Tarrow, watchman. Fourth night. Ten o’clock. I have a chair, a stove and a brown teapot in the porter’s room off the kitchen passage. Before the midnight round I set the kettle on the edge of the stove and left the pot empty, meaning to brew when I got back.\n\nHalf past midnight. Round done, all doors sound, scullery window latched by somebody before I reached it. Came back to the porter’s room. The tea was made. Poured, too, one cup, the right colour, with the spoon laid across the saucer the way they do in big houses. Still hot. Then I drank it, since letting it go cold would not have explained anything.\n\nFour o’clock. Nothing further. I said “Much obliged” to the passage on my way through. I felt a fool. I would have felt worse not saying it."],["The Grey Housekeeper","Night log of the Old Hall above the river Ember. Ned Tarrow, watchman, ninth night. I have been finding doors locked ahead of me and tea poured. Tonight I saw who does it.\n\nTwo o’clock. Top of the back stairs. A woman in grey coming along the passage, with a cap, an apron and a great ring of keys at her waist. The keys swung and made no sound. I could see the lamp bracket through her shoulder. She tried the nursery door, found it fast, and nodded to herself.\n\nThen she saw me. I took my cap off. She looked at my boots, which were muddy, and then at my face, and gave me one short nod, the kind you give a new boy who may or may not do. Then she went through the wall, where I am told there used to be a door."],["Corrections in the Margin","Night log, Emberwick Old Hall, kept by Ned Tarrow. Third week. The Hall has a ghost, a grey housekeeper with a ring of silent keys, and she does my rounds before I can.\n\nTen o’clock. I am not proud of this entry. Started the round an hour early to get ahead of her. Every door on the ground floor already locked. Went up the front stairs two at a time, at sixty-one, and found the Long Gallery done and the lamp trimmed.\n\nMidnight. Came back to the porter’s room to find this book open on the table. My entry for Tuesday reads “all sound”. Beside it, in brown ink and a small slanting hand, is written: Scullery window. Again. And on last night’s page, against my count of the doors: Twenty-four. You forget the coal hatch. I went and looked. There is a coal hatch."],["Forty Years of Keys","Night log of the Old Hall. Ned Tarrow, watchman. I asked Miss Vale, the curator, whether the Hall had ever had a housekeeper who wore grey and missed nothing. She did not laugh. She brought down a book.\n\nSix in the evening. It is a housekeeper’s day-book, kept by a Mrs Annis Thorne, who ran the Hall for forty years in the old family’s time. Every page is in the small slanting hand that has been correcting my log. Linen counted. Lamps trimmed. Each night ends the same way: Doors seen to. Keys given up to Mr Pellow, all sound. Good night.\n\nThe butler took the keys at night, so that the housekeeper could go off duty. But the last page stops at “Doors seen to”. No keys given up. No good night. Ten o’clock. Began my round. I have been thinking about that last page."],["The Gallery Window","Night log, Emberwick Old Hall. Ned Tarrow, watchman. A gale off the Grey Coast tonight. Half past one. I was in the porter’s room when the servants’ bells began to ring. All eleven on the board, though the wires were cut years back. Then one only, over and over: Long Gallery. I ran.\n\nThe big end window had burst its catch and was banging wide, with rain driving in across the floor towards the old family’s portraits. The grey housekeeper, Mrs Thorne, who haunts this house and walks my rounds ahead of me, was there already. She can turn a key, but a wet oak shutter is another matter. Her hands went through it. She looked at me, and for once it was not a look for a new boy.\n\nI put my shoulder to the shutter and dropped the bar. Two o’clock. All sound. Portraits dry."],["Upstairs and Down","Night log of the Old Hall above the river Ember, kept by Ned Tarrow. Seventh week. Since the night of the gale the Hall’s ghost and I have come to an arrangement, though neither of us has mentioned it.\n\nTen o’clock. Mrs Thorne, the grey housekeeper, takes the upper floors. I take the ground floor, the cellars and the coal hatch, which I no longer forget. Midnight. All sound below. I can hear nothing above, but the lamps on the landing are trimmed, so all is sound there too.\n\nTwo o’clock. We meet on the half-landing under the big clock. I have taken to bringing the Emberwick paper and reading her the parts a housekeeper might care for: whose chimney wants sweeping, the price of coal. She stands with her hands folded. At the price of coal she shut her eyes, as people do who remember it cheaper."],["She Never Sits","Night log, Emberwick Old Hall. Ned Tarrow, watchman. I share the watch here with a ghost, Mrs Annis Thorne, housekeeper of the Hall in the old days.\n\nTwo o’clock. Half-landing. She was late, which she never is, and when she came I could see more of the wall through her than usual. She flickered at the edges like a lamp wanting oil. I set the landing chair for her. She looked at it as I might look at a boat with a hole in. Nine weeks, and I have never once seen her sit.\n\nFour o’clock. I have worked it out, slowly, as I do. In her day-book, every night ended with the keys handed to the butler and a good night. On the last page nobody took them. So she has been on duty ever since. Nobody is built to stand a watch that long."],["A Stiff Note","Night log of the Old Hall. Ned Tarrow, watchman. The Hall’s ghost is its old housekeeper, Mrs Thorne, who was never relieved of her keys and so has never gone off duty. Tonight I tried to mend that and made a mess of it.\n\nTwo o’clock. Half-landing. I told her that I was the night watch now, and that she had earned her rest and should take it. I meant it kindly. I said it like a ferryman, which is to say loudly. She drew herself up until her cap was level with the clock. Then she went through the wall.\n\nThree o’clock. A note in this log, in the brown slanting hand: And who would see to the scullery window? Underneath, pressed hard enough to mark the page: Twenty-four doors, Mr Tarrow. You found twenty-three. Four o’clock. She is right. I have not earned the keys yet."],["Earning the Keys","Night log, Emberwick Old Hall, kept by Ned Tarrow. Twelfth week, and a fortnight to Midsummer, when the Hall opens again. Mrs Thorne, the grey housekeeper who haunts it, will not hand over her watch to a man who forgets coal hatches. So I have stopped forgetting.\n\nNine o’clock. The joiner came by daylight at my asking and fitted a new catch to the scullery window, brass, with a pin. Tonight I latched it and stood back. I believe I heard a sniff. It was not a displeased one.\n\nMidnight to four. Walked the whole Hall, top to bottom, all twenty-four doors, saying each one aloud as I tried it. Nursery, sound. Linen room, sound. Coal hatch, sound. She came behind me the whole way and checked every one after me. She found nothing to correct. Five o’clock. No note in the log. From Mrs Thorne that is high praise."],["Midsummer Eve","Night log of Emberwick Old Hall. Ned Tarrow, watchman. Midsummer Eve. The Hall opens to the town tomorrow. Tonight I did what somebody should have done for Mrs Annis Thorne, the housekeeper, whose ghost has stood watch here for longer than anyone can tell me.\n\nTen o’clock. Round done together, twenty-four doors. Then I stood on the half-landing under the clock in my good coat, held out my hand, and said the words from her own day-book. “Doors seen to, Mrs Thorne. I will take the keys now. All sound. Good night.”\n\nShe stood a long moment. Then she unhooked the ring and laid it across my palm. It weighed nothing and was cold as well water, and for the first time those keys made a sound: one small chime, like a spoon on a cup. She said, “Good night, Mr Tarrow.” Then she went upstairs. By the stairs."],["Two Cups","Night log, Emberwick Old Hall, open again since Midsummer. Ned Tarrow, watchman. First night of autumn. I keep the watch alone, twenty-four doors, and I have not missed the coal hatch since spring.\n\nMidnight. All sound. Looked in at the old housekeeper’s room. Miss Vale has left it as it was: a narrow bed, and a chair by the window that looks down the river Ember. The cushion on the chair had a dent in it. The room was not cold. It was warm, as a room is when somebody has sat there all evening with nothing to do.\n\nTwo o’clock. Mrs Thorne, the Hall’s ghost, is off duty, but she has not gone anywhere. Tonight I came back to the porter’s room and the tea was made. Two cups, and a second chair drawn up across from mine. I read out the price of coal."]],[["What a Bump Is For","Lesson the first, from your Great-Aunt Hush, who has haunted the Mill House on the marsh since before it had a chimney, to Flit, smallest ghost in Emberwick, lately arrived on the autumn mist and given the bakery on Oven Street to look after.\n\nSo you have a house of your own, my dear, and you wish to know how to haunt it. We begin with the bump in the night, and with what it is for. It is not for frightening. Any fool can frighten. A bump is company. It tells the one person awake that the dark is not empty and means no harm.\n\nA house that never bumps is a lonely house. People lie awake in it listening to nothing. Your baker, Miss Batch, gets up at three every morning, alone. That is what you are there for. Go gently, and write to me."],["Choosing What to Bump","Lesson the second, Flit, from your Great-Aunt Hush at the Mill House on the marsh. You are a small new ghost with a bakery to haunt, and you have asked what, exactly, one bumps.\n\nNot china. Never china. Not glass, not the clock, not anything with a lid that rolls. A bump should be soft and round and finish quickly. The best things are a floorboard, an empty coal scuttle, the underside of a table and a sack of something. You have flour sacks in that bakery on Oven Street. You are spoiled.\n\nNudge, do not shove. Think of a cat getting down off a bed in the next room: that is the sound we want. Practise on the sack by the back door. And never bump twice running in a night. Once is company. Twice is mice, and then they send for a terrier."],["Ten Past, Never On the Hour","Flit, my dear, lesson the third. Great-Aunt Hush writing, from the airing cupboard of the Mill House on the marsh, where I do my best thinking. Today, for my small pupil at the bakery in Emberwick: when to bump.\n\nNever on the stroke of midnight. It is showy, and everyone is expecting it. Never while a clock is striking, either. A wasted bump is a sad thing.\n\nThe time to bump is ten minutes after somebody wakes. For the first ten minutes they are busy finding their slippers. After that they begin to listen to the quiet. That is your moment. One soft bump, somewhere off to the side, as if the house had turned over in its sleep. Your Miss Batch comes down to her ovens at three. So: ten past three, the flour sack, once. Then leave her be till the loaves are in."],["The Stair That Creaks","Lesson the fourth, from Great-Aunt Hush of the Mill House to Flit, small ghost of Batch’s Bakery on Oven Street. You have your bump. Now we add the creak.\n\nEvery staircase has one stair that creaks, and it is a ghost’s business to know which. In my mill it is the fourth from the top. Go up and down yours tonight, lightly, and learn where it lives.\n\nA creak is a slower thing than a bump. A bump says: someone is here. A creak says: and is going up to bed, the same as you. It is the friendliest sound a house can make, if it comes once and goes upward. A creak coming down the stairs is another matter, and we do not do it. I knew a ghost on the Grey Coast who creaked downward all one winter. The family moved to the Lowlands."],["The Matter of the Cat","Flit, this is your Great-Aunt Hush, writing from the Mill House on the marsh with lesson the fifth. You tell me your bakery on Oven Street has a cat, a large pale one called Semolina, who sits and stares at exactly where you are. Cats can see us. Dogs suspect. Hens have no idea.\n\nYou must make friends with her before you bump again. A cat who dislikes the ghost will puff up like a chimney brush, and then the baker looks where the cat is looking, and the whole business becomes alarming.\n\nSit low. Do not loom. Let her come and walk through you; they like the cool of it in a warm kitchen. Then hold your hand behind her ear, where she will feel a small pleasant chill. After three nights she is your partner, and anything you knock, she will take the blame for."],["On Spilling the Flour","Lesson the sixth. Great-Aunt Hush of the Mill House on the marsh, to Flit, small ghost of the bakery on Oven Street, who writes that all is ruined.\n\nYou bumped the flour bin instead of the flour sack. The lid rolled, as I said lids do. Twenty pounds went over the floor, Semolina the cat ran through it, and Miss Batch came down at three to a kitchen like a snowfield with paw prints going up the wall. You have been hiding in the bread oven ever since.\n\nCome out of the oven, child. In the winter of the long frost, when I was newer than you, I meant to nudge a footstool and brought down a whole dresser of plates. That is how I know about china. The miller’s wife laughed till she sat down. Yours swept up and went on baking. She is worth haunting well."],["The Draught and the Sigh","Lesson the seventh, Flit, from your Great-Aunt Hush of the Mill House on the marsh, to one small ghost in an Emberwick bakery. You have bumps and a creak. Here are two quiet things for the days when a bump is too much.\n\nThe draught first. Pass close by a curtain and let it lift an inch and fall. That is all. It is for summer evenings, and people like it; they say, “Ah, a breath of air at last.”\n\nThe sigh is harder. It is not a moan. Moaning is for windmills and show-offs. A proper sigh is the sound a kettle makes just after it is taken off: short, comfortable, finished. Use it once in a month, when your baker sits down at the end of her morning’s work. If you do it well she will sigh too, without knowing why, and feel better for it."],["A Bump in Time","Flit, my dear. Lesson the eighth, from Great-Aunt Hush at the Mill House. Until now I have taught you bumps for company. There is one other kind. You, at your bakery on Oven Street, may need it any morning.\n\nIt is the warning bump. If your Miss Batch nods off in her chair with loaves in the oven, and you smell the crusts going from gold to brown, you do not wait for ten past anything. You go to the largest empty tin in the place and you bump it properly. Loud. Once. As if a loaf had fallen off a shelf.\n\nShe will start up and say “Mercy, the bread,” and never think of you at all. We are not thanked for this kind. Then be quiet for the rest of the day. A ghost who has been loud once must be twice as soft afterwards."],["Salt on the Doorstep","Lesson the ninth. Great-Aunt Hush of the Mill House writing to Flit, small ghost of Batch’s Bakery in Emberwick, who is downhearted.\n\nYou say that since the flour and the tin, Miss Batch has put a line of salt along the back doorstep. You think she wants you gone. Flit, it means she has noticed somebody is there. She does not yet know what sort of somebody.\n\nSo tell her. From tonight you will bump the same way every morning: two soft, then one softer, on the flour sack, at ten past three. Yes, I said twice is mice. Three, alike each day, is a name. No creak, no sigh. Do it for a fortnight. A thing that happens alike each day stops being a fright and becomes a habit, and people grow fond of habits. And step over the salt, dear. It does nothing, but it is manners."],["The Rolling Pin Answers","Flit! Your Great-Aunt Hush has read your letter twice at the Mill House on the marsh and is as pleased as a ghost of her age can decently be. This is lesson the tenth, and you have written most of it yourself.\n\nFor thirteen mornings you bumped the flour sack at ten past three, two soft and one softer, in your bakery on Oven Street. On the fourteenth, Miss Batch put down her dough, picked up her rolling pin, and knocked on the table. Two, and a little one. Then she said, “Morning, you,” and went on kneading.\n\nThat is as good as a haunting gets, child. Now the rule: when they answer, you do not get clever. No tunes, no long speeches in knocks. Answer once, and let that be the whole conversation. Friends who meet at three in the morning do not need much said."],["Your Aunt Comes to Look","Lesson the eleventh was an examination, Flit, and it is over. I, your Great-Aunt Hush, left my Mill House on the marsh for the first time in sixty winters and came down the river Ember on Tuesday’s fog to see how you haunt your bakery.\n\nI sat unseen in the rafters behind the bread paddles. At three Miss Batch came down. At ten past, you gave the flour sack two soft and one softer, and I could not have told them from my own. She knocked back with the rolling pin. The salt was gone from the step. Semolina the cat slept by the oven door and did not turn an ear.\n\nThen I saw the top shelf. A currant bun on a saucer, steaming. A ghost cannot eat a bun. But somebody thought of you, and that is what the steam is for."],["When Not to Bump","The twelfth and last lesson, from Great-Aunt Hush of the Mill House on the marsh to Flit of Batch’s Bakery in Emberwick, who came to me on the autumn mist not knowing a bump from a bang, and who has passed.\n\nOne thing remains, the hardest: when not to bump at all. There will be mornings when your Miss Batch comes down humming, with the fire drawing well and the cat on the sill. On those mornings a house needs nothing from its ghost. Sit on the flour sack and listen to the bread rise. Being quiet company is the top of our trade, and few ghosts get there.\n\nI am going back to my airing cupboard. Some misty autumn a ghost smaller than you will turn up on your doorstep, wanting to know how it is done. Tell them what I told you. Gently, Flit. Always gently."]]];
